window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"id": "l1a", "title": "Lecture #1a: Basics of Diagnosis: Innominates and Pubic Bone", "module": "OMM", "content": "l1a Lecture 1a Basics of Diagnosis Innominates and Pubic Bone\\n\\n Lecturer To Shan Li D.O.\\n Session Lecture 1a\\n\\n Introduction and Objectives Slide 2 \\nThis lecture introduces the structural examination of the pelvis focusing on the diagnosis of somatic dysfunctions of the innominates and pubic bone.\\n Key Goals \\n Understand pelvic anatomy and biomechanics.\\n Perform and interpret Standing Flexion Test StFT ASIS Compression Test and Seated Flexion Test SeFT .\\n Differentiate between Iliosacral Pelvic and Sacroiliac Sacral dysfunctions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Anatomy of the Pelvis Slide 5-6 \\n Bones\\n Innominate Composed of three parts that fuse at the acetabulum \\n Ilium \\n Ischium \\n Pubis \\n Sacrum Articulates with the ilia.\\n\\n Joints\\n Sacroiliac Joint SIJ \\n L-shaped or C-shaped.\\n Allows for mobility though limited Slide 6 .\\n Mobility decreases with age. \\n Pubic Symphysis Fibrocartilaginous joint.\\n\\n Ligaments Slide 7 15 \\n Ligaments e.g. sacrotuberous sacrospinous provide stability and create foramina for neurovascular structures.\\n Clinical Relevance They guide motion and can become tender/restrictive in somatic dysfunction.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Biomechanics and Function\\n\\n Gait and Motion Slide 17 \\n Physiologic motion of the sacrum and ilia occurs during walking.\\n Somatic Dysfunction Affects gait efficiency and increases energy expenditure.\\n\\n correlate\\n Pregnancy-Related Changes Slide 18 \\n Pathophysiology Horizontalization of sacrum increased laxity due to Relaxin .\\n Presentation Anterior pelvic tilt increased lumbar lordosis potential for pain low back pelvic .\\n Lecturer s Point \\ Center of gravity shifts anteriorly... Sacroiliac joint and pubic symphysis become more lax.\\ \\n \\n\\n Clinical Implications Slide 20 \\n Pelvic dysfunction often presents as \\n Low back pain hip/groin pain.\\n Sciatic neuralgia piriformis syndrome .\\n Visceral issues GI GU via autonomic relationships.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Structural Examination Screening Tests\\n\\n 1. Standing Flexion Test StFT Slide 23-28 \\n Purpose Diagnoses Iliosacral Dysfunction Ilio-sacral = Ilium moving on Sacrum = Pelvic SD .\\n Execution \\n 1. Patient stands feet 6-8 inches apart.\\n 2. Physician monitors PSIS bilaterally eyes at level .\\n 3. Patient bends forward slowly.\\n Positive Result One PSIS moves \\ First and Farthest\\ superiorly.\\n Interpretation The side that moves superiorly is the Dysfunctional Side restricted side . The ilium is \\ locked\\ to the sacrum on that side and moves with it.\\n\\n pearl\\n StFT Mnemonic Standing = S ide of I lium Iliosacral .\\n \\n\\n 2. Seated Flexion Test SeFT Slide 32-35 \\n Purpose Diagnoses Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacro-iliac = Sacrum moving on Ilium = Sacral SD .\\n Execution \\n 1. Patient seated feet flat stabilizing the ilia .\\n 2. Physician monitors PSIS bilaterally.\\n 3. Patient bends forward.\\n Positive Result One PSIS moves \\ First and Farthest\\ .\\n Interpretation Indicates the side of sacral dysfunction.\\n Note The \\ standing\\ vs \\ seated\\ distinction isolates the ilia standing = mobile vs sacroiliac mechanics seated = ilia locked by tuberosities .\\n\\n highyield\\n StFT vs SeFT Summary Slide 36 \\n StFT + Iliosacral Dysfunction → Look for Innominate/Pubic shears.\\n Axis Inferior Transverse Axis.\\n SeFT + Sacroiliac Dysfunction → Look for Sacral Torsions/Shears.\\n Axis Middle Transverse Axis.\\n \\n\\n 3. ASIS Compression Test Pelvic Rock Slide 30-31 \\n Purpose Lateralization Determines which side is dysfunctional .\\n Helps confirm StFT/SeFT findings or distinguish when tests are equivocal.\\n Execution \\n 1. Patient supine.\\n 2. Physician applies posterior-medial pressure on ASIS towards the SI joint.\\n 3. Assess for \\ spring\\ or resistance.\\n Positive Result The side with Restriction less spring board-like feel is the positive side.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Static Landmarks Examination Slide 40-42 \\nAfter lateralizing finding the side check landmarks to classify the dysfunction e.g. Anterior/Posterior Rotation Upslip/Downslip .\\n\\n Anterior Landmarks\\n ASIS Heights Compare superior/inferior.\\n Pubic Tubercles Check for superior/inferior shears.\\n Medial Malleoli Assess functional leg length.\\n\\n Posterior Landmarks\\n PSIS Heights Compare.\\n Iliac Crest Heights Compare.\\n Ischial Tuberosities Compare heights.\\n Sacral Sulci Depth.\\n\\n pearl\\n Diagnosis Flow \\n1. Screen StFT Pelvic vs SeFT Sacral .\\n2. Lateralize ASIS Compression confirms side .\\n3. Diagnose Check landmarks ASIS PSIS etc. on the dysfunctional side relative to the other.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Review Questions Slide 44-52 \\n\\nSee the Questions tab for interactive board-style practice. Lecture 1a Basics of Diagnosis Innominates and Pubic Bone lecturer To Shan Li D.O. OMM session Lecture 1a prev next l1b pdf pdfs/Lecture 1a. T. Li D.O. PPT .pdf mindmap Basics of Pelvic Diagnosis\\n Anatomy\\n Innominate Bones Ilium Ischium Pubis\\n Joints Sacroiliac SIJ Pubic Symphysis\\n Ligaments Sacrotuberous Sacrospinous Stability \\n Biomechanics\\n Motion Occurs during gait\\n Pregnancy Relaxin → Increased laxity → Anterior Tilt\\n Screening Tests\\n Standing Flexion StFT \\n Diagnoses Iliosacral Innominate Dysfunction\\n Axis Inferior Transverse\\n Positive restricted side moves Superior\\n Seated Flexion SeFT \\n Diagnoses Sacroiliac Sacral Dysfunction\\n Axis Middle Transverse\\n Mechanism Ischial tuberosities lock ilia\\n ASIS Compression \\n Purpose Lateralization confirms side \\n Positive Resistance/Rigidity\\n Landmarks Static Exam \\n Anterior ASIS Pubic Tubercles Medial Malleoli\\n Posterior PSIS Iliac Crests Ischial Tuberosities Sacral Sulci questions 1. A 32-year-old female presents with right-sided low back pain. Structural examination reveals a positive Standing Flexion Test on the right and a negative Seated Flexion Test. The ASIS Compression Test is positive on the right. What is the most likely category of somatic dysfunction? Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacral SD Iliosacral Dysfunction Innominate SD Lumbar Somatic Dysfunction Psoas Syndrome Pubic Shear 1 A positive Standing Flexion Test StFT indicates Iliosacral Innominate dysfunction. The Seated Flexion Test is negative ruling out primary Sacroiliac Sacral dysfunction. The ASIS compression confirms the right side is involved. Therefore the diagnosis lies within the iliosacral/innominate category B . 2. While performing the Standing Flexion Test the physician notes the right PSIS moves more superiorly than the left. Which biomechanical principle explains this finding? The right iliosacral joint is hypermobile The right iliosacral joint is restricted hypomobile The left sacrum is torsioned The right hamstrings are tight The left quadratus lumborum is tight 1 In the flexion tests the PSIS on the dysfunctional side moves first and farthest superiorly. This is because the joint is restricted hypomobile and locks early carrying the ilium up with the sacrum as the patient bends forward. The normal side maintains motion longer and doesn t rise as high initially. 3. A pregnant patient in her third trimester presents with anterior pelvic tilt and increased lumbar lordosis. Which hormone is primarily responsible for the increased laxity of her sacroiliac joints and pubic symphysis? Estrogen Progesterone Relaxin Oxytocin Prolactin 2 Relaxin is the hormone secreted during pregnancy that increases ligamentous laxity particularly in the pelvis SI joints pubic symphysis to accommodate childbirth. This laxity contributes to the structural changes described Slide 18 . 4. The Seated Flexion Test is designed to isolate the motion of the sacrum by eliminating the influence of which structures? The lumbar spine The lower extremities/Ilia The thoracic cage The cervical spine The abdominal muscles 1 By sitting the weight is borne by the ischial tuberosities. This effectively locks the ilia and minimizes the influence of the lower extremities and by extension the iliosacral mechanics on the ilium side allowing the physician to assess sacroiliac sacral motion more specifically Slide 23 . 5. Which axis of motion is primarily associated with Iliosacral Pelvic motion as tested by the Standing Flexion Test? Superior Transverse Axis Middle Transverse Axis Inferior Transverse Axis Oblique Axis Vertical Axis 2 Iliosacral motion ilium moving on sacrum occurs primarily around the Inferior Transverse Axis Slide 36 . Sacroiliac motion sacrum moving on ilium occurs around the Middle Transverse Axis . Standing Flexion Test assesses which dysfunction? Iliosacral Dysfunction Innominate/Pelvic Concept Seated Flexion Test assesses which dysfunction? Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacral Concept What does a positive StFT indicate biomechanically? The PSIS on the positive side moves first and farthest because the Ilio-sacral joint is restricted hypomobile . Concept What is the purpose of the ASIS Compression Test? Lateralization identifying which side involved . Clinical Which hormone causes pelvic ligament laxity in pregnancy? Relaxin Glossary Axis of motion for Iliosacral Dysfunction? Inferior Transverse Axis Concept Axis of motion for Sacroiliac Dysfunction? Middle Transverse Axis Concept Anatomy What 3 bones fuse to form the Innominate? Ilium Ischium Pubis Glossary In StFT if Right PSIS moves higher which side is dysfunctional? Right side Clinical Normal result for ASIS Compression Test? Springy/Resilient feel Restrictions feel rigid/board-like . Clinical anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l1b", "title": "Lecture #1b: Diagnosis of the Pelvis (Lab Prep)", "module": "OMM", "content": "l1b Lecture 1b Diagnosis of the Pelvis Lab Prep \\n\\n Lecturer Michael J. Terzella D.O.\\n Session Lab 15 Prep\\n\\n Introduction\\nThis lecture prepares students for the Pelvis Lab 15 . It reinforces the diagnostic concepts from Lecture 1a and introduces specific Innominate and Pubic Somatic Dysfunctions.\\n Goal Efficiently examine name and record somatic dysfunctions of the pelvis.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Review of Diagnostic Tests Slide 4-6 8-13 \\n 1. Standing Flexion Test StFT \\n Significance Lateralizes Innominate Iliosacral dysfunction.\\n Positive PSIS moves first and farthest superiorly.\\n Rule Detailed diagnosis is named for the side of the positive StFT .\\n\\n 2. ASIS Compression Test Pelvic Rock \\n Significance Lateralizes dysfunction; useful screening but less specific can be Iliosacral or Sacroiliac .\\n Positive Resistance to posterior-medial compression \\ Hard end-feel\\ .\\n\\n 3. Static Landmarks\\n Anterior ASIS Pubic Tubercles Medial Malleoli.\\n Posterior PSIS Iliac Crests Ischial Tuberosities Sacral Sulci.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Innominate Somatic Dysfunctions Slide 26-36 \\n\\n Innominate Rotations\\nMotion of the innominate on the sacrum around the Inferior Transverse Axis .\\n\\n Anterior Rotation Slide 28-29 \\n Mechanism Innominate rotates anteriorly.\\n Landmarks on dysfunctional side \\n ASIS Inferior\\n PSIS Superior\\n Medial Malleolus Inferior Long Leg \\n Symptoms Hamstring tightness Sciatica Piriformis .\\n\\n Posterior Rotation Slide 30-31 \\n Mechanism Innominate rotates posteriorly.\\n Landmarks on dysfunctional side \\n ASIS Superior\\n PSIS Inferior\\n Medial Malleolus Superior Short Leg \\n Symptoms Inguinal/Groin pain Rectus Femoris Medial Knee pain Sartorius .\\n\\n Innominate Shears\\nTranslatory motion of the entire innominate usually traumatic .\\n\\n Superior Shear Upslip Slide 33-34 \\n Mechanism Entire innominate shifts superiorly e.g. landing on one leg .\\n Landmarks \\n ASIS Superior\\n PSIS Superior\\n Pubic Tubercle Superior\\n Medial Malleolus Superior Short Leg \\n Motion Laxity in superior translation.\\n\\n Inferior Shear Downslip Slide 35-36 \\n Mechanism Entire innominate shifts inferiorly e.g. getting leg caught in stirrup .\\n Landmarks \\n ASIS Inferior\\n PSIS Inferior\\n Pubic Tubercle Inferior\\n Medial Malleolus Inferior Long Leg \\n\\n pearl\\n Shear Tip In a shear ALL landmarks move in the same direction e.g. Superior Shear = ASIS High PSIS High Pubes High . In rotations ASIS and PSIS move in opposites .\\n \\n\\n Innominate Flares Slide 37 \\n Outflare ASIS further from midline associated with Posterior Rotation .\\n Inflare ASIS closer to midline associated with Anterior Rotation .\\n\\n---\\n\\n Pubic Somatic Dysfunctions Slide 38-42 \\n\\n Pubic Shears Slide 38 \\n Superior Shear Pubic tubercle high on dysfunctional side.\\n Inferior Shear Pubic tubercle low on dysfunctional side.\\n Clinical Trauma childbirth.\\n\\n Pubic Symphysis Dysfunctions\\nDysfunction at the joint itself without asymmetry of height.\\n\\n correlate\\n Pubic Compression Adducted Pubis Slide 39-40 \\n Pathophysiology Pubic bones forced together Trauma .\\n Presentation \\n Bilateral tenderness.\\n Bulging symphyseal cartilage.\\n Restricted Pelvic Ring motion Bilaterally restricted ASIS Compression .\\n Urinary symptoms frequency/urgency .\\n Diagnosis No height asymmetry but restricted motion and tenderness.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Pubic Gapping Abducted Pubis Slide 41-42 \\n Pathophysiology Pubic bones pulled apart Childbirth Fractures .\\n Presentation \\n Deep Sulcus at symphysis.\\n Bilateral tenderness.\\n Urinary symptoms.\\n Often very painful.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Case Study Slide 45 \\n Patient 20F Equestrian fell from horse foot caught in stirrup.\\n Findings Right Side \\n StFT + Right\\n ASIS Inferior\\n PSIS Inferior\\n Pubic Tubercle Inferior\\n Medial Malleolus Inferior\\n Diagnosis Right Inferior Innominate Shear .\\n Reasoning All landmarks are inferior on the right consistent with the mechanism of injury traction on the leg .\\n\\n Lab Prep Pearls\\n Order of Exam \\n 1. Screen StFT/SeFT .\\n 2. Lateralize ASIS Compression .\\n 3. Reseat the Pelvis! Bridge .\\n 4. Static Landmarks Anterior then Posterior .\\n 5. Diagnose. Lecture 1b Diagnosis of the Pelvis Lab Prep lecturer Michael J. Terzella D.O. OMM session Lab Prep Lecture prev l1a next l2 pdf pdfs/Lecture 1b. M. Terzella D.O. PPT .pdf mindmap Pelvic Somatic Dysfunctions\\n Innominate Rotations\\n Anterior Rotation \\n ASIS Inferior\\n PSIS Superior\\n Leg Long\\n Association Inflare Hamstring tightness\\n Posterior Rotation \\n ASIS Superior\\n PSIS Inferior\\n Leg Short\\n Association Outflare Groin pain Rectus \\n Innominate Shears\\n Superior Shear Upslip \\n All Landmarks Superior ASIS PSIS Pubes \\n Cause Vertical impact landing on leg \\n Inferior Shear Downslip \\n All Landmarks Inferior\\n Cause Traction stuck in stirrup \\n Pubic Dysfunctions\\n Shears Sup/Inf asymmetry named for StFT side \\n Compression Adduction \\n Sign Bulging rigidity\\n Cause Trauma\\n Gapping Abduction \\n Sign Deep Sulcus\\n Cause Childbirth questions 1. A patient presents with right-sided pelvic pain. Examination reveals Right ASIS is Superior Right PSIS is Inferior and Right Medial Malleolus is Superior. What is the most likely diagnosis? Right Anterior Innominate Rotation Right Posterior Innominate Rotation Right Superior Innominate Shear Left Anterior Innominate Rotation Left Superior Innominate Shear 1 In a Posterior Rotation the ASIS moves up Superior and the PSIS moves down Inferior . This also pulls the leg up causing a short leg Superior Medial Malleolus . This matches the findings exactly. 2. Which of the following findings is most consistent with a Superior Innominate Shear Upslip ? ASIS High PSIS Low ASIS Low PSIS High ASIS High PSIS High Pubic Tubercle High ASIS Low PSIS Low Pubic Tubercle Low ASIS High PSIS High Pubic Tubercle Low 2 In a Shear Upslip the entire bone translates superiorly. Therefore ALL landmarks on that bone ASIS PSIS Pubic Tubercle Ischial Tuberosity will be found Superior compared to the healthy side. 3. A postpartum female presents with pubic pain and urinary urgency. Examination reveals bilateral pubic tenderness and a deep palpable sulcus at the pubic symphysis. ASIS and PSIS heights are symmetric. What is the diagnosis? Superior Pubic Shear Inferior Pubic Shear Pubic Compression Adducted Pubic Gapping Abducted Bilateral Innominate Outflare 3 The key finding is the deep sulcus at the symphysis and history of childbirth which suggests Pubic Gapping Abduction . Compression would present with a bulging cartilage. Shears would present with height asymmetry. 4. In an Anterior Innominate Rotation which muscle group is most likely to be tight/hypertonic? Hamstrings Quadriceps/Rectus Femoris Gluteus Maximus Biceps Femoris External Obliques 1 This is a bit distinct check Slide 29 vs 31 . Actually Hamstrings are tight in Anterior rotation Lecturer slide 28 notes Subjective complaints Hamstring tightness for Anterior . Wait biomechanically Rectus pulls it Anterior. Let s check slide 29 Anteriorly Rotated... Hamstring tightness . Why? Because the ischial tuberosity moves superiorly/posteriorly putting tension on the hamstrings. Conversely Quadriceps Rectus dysfunction is associated with Posterior rotation Slide 31 because it attaches to AIIS/ASIS and pulls it down. 5. What is the critical first step before performing the static landmark examination? Treat the lumbar spine Reseat the pelvis Bridging Measure True leg length Perform the Seated Flexion Test Check cranial rhythmic impulse 1 Reseating the pelvis asking patient to bend knees lift hips and set them down is crucial to ensure the patient is straight and soft tissues are relaxed preventing False positives in landmark asymmetry Slide 15 . Landmarks for Anterior Innominate Rotation? ASIS Low PSIS High Leg Long Clinical Landmarks for Posterior Innominate Rotation? ASIS High PSIS Low Leg Short Clinical Landmarks for Superior Innominate Shear? ASIS High PSIS High Pubes High All Up Clinical Landmarks for Inferior Innominate Shear? ASIS Low PSIS Low Pubes Low All Down Clinical Key finding for Pubic Compression? Bulging cartilage restricted pelvic ring bilateral tenderness. Clinical Key finding for Pubic Gapping? Deep sulcus history of delivery/trauma. Clinical Muscle associated with Anterior Innominate pain? Hamstrings tightness Concept Muscle associated with Posterior Innominate pain? Rectus Femoris / Sartorius Groin/Knee pain Concept Innominate Inflare is associated with which rotation? Anterior Rotation Concept Innominate Outflare is associated with which rotation? Posterior Rotation Concept anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l2", "title": "Lecture #2: Overview of Neuroembryology", "module": "Neuro", "content": "l2 Lecture 2 Overview of Neuroembryology\\n\\n Lecturer K. Poole Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 2\\n Topic Embryonic development of the Nervous System\\n\\n 1. Gastrulation and Neurulation Slides 3-8 \\n\\n Gastrulation Week 3 \\nThe process begins with Gastrulation establishing the three germ layers \\n Ectoderm Gives rise to the Nervous System and skin epidermis .\\n Mesoderm Forms muscle bone connective tissue and the Notochord .\\n Endoderm Forms the gut tube and internal organs.\\n\\n Notochord Induction\\nThe Notochord a rod-like mesodermal structure is the key inducer. It secretes signaling molecules including Sonic Hedgehog SHH and BMPs that signal the overlying ectoderm to thicken and differentiate into the Neural Plate Slide 4 17 .\\n\\n Neurulation\\nThe transformation of the neural plate into the neural tube is the fundamental event of the nervous system.\\n1. Neural Plate Thickened ectoderm Day 19 .\\n2. Neural Groove The midline invaginates Day 20 .\\n3. Neural Folds The lateral edges elevate.\\n4. Neural Tube The folds fuse in the midline creating a hollow tube. This fusion begins in the cervical region and \\ zips\\ rostrally and caudally.\\n\\n Neuropore Closure \\nThe tube is initially open at both ends. Timing is critical \\n Rostral Anterior Neuropore Closes around Day 25 .\\n Caudal Posterior Neuropore Closes around Day 27-28 .\\n\\n 2. Neural Crest Cells Slides 13-15 \\nAs the neural tube closes cells at the crest of the neural folds detach and migrate away. These are the Neural Crest Cells often termed the \\ 4th Germ Layer\\ due to their wide variation.\\n PNS Derivatives Dorsal Root Ganglia Sensory Autonomic Ganglia Schwann Cells.\\n Other Derivatives Melanocytes Adrenal Medulla Craniofacial bones/cartilage face Odontoblasts teeth .\\n\\n 3. Neural Tube Differentiation Slides 16-19 \\nThe wall of the neural tube organizes into distinct layers \\n Ventricular Zone Inner layer neuroepithelium becomes Ependymal cells lining ventricles.\\n Mantle Layer Middle layer becomes Gray Matter cell bodies .\\n Marginal Layer Outer layer becomes White Matter axons .\\n\\n Dorsal-Ventral Patterning Slide 17 28 \\n Alar Plate Dorsal Induced by BMPs/TGF-\\u03b2 . Becomes Sensory regions Dorsal Horn .\\n Basal Plate Ventral Induced by SHH from Notochord . Becomes Motor regions Ventral Horn .\\n Sulcus Limitans The groove separating Alar sensory and Basal motor plates.\\n\\n 4. Brain Development Vesicles Slides 21-25 \\nThe rostral neural tube expands into vesicles.\\n 3 Primary Vesicles Week 4 \\n1. Prosencephalon Forebrain \\n2. Mesencephalon Midbrain \\n3. Rhombencephalon Hindbrain \\n\\n 5 Secondary Vesicles Week 5 \\n Prosencephalon divides into \\n Telencephalon Cerebral Hemispheres Lateral Ventricles.\\n Diencephalon Thalamus Hypothalamus Retina Third Ventricle.\\n Mesencephalon Remains Mesencephalon Midbrain Cerebral Aqueduct .\\n Rhombencephalon divides into \\n Metencephalon Pons Cerebellum Upper 4th Ventricle.\\n Myelencephalon Medulla Lower 4th Ventricle.\\n\\n 5. Clinical Correlates Congenital Disorders\\n\\n correlate\\n Neural Tube Defects NTDs \\nCaused by failure of neuropore closure. Strongly associated with Folate Vitamin B9 deficiency.\\n Anencephaly Meroencephaly Failure of Rostral neuropore closure. Forebrain and skull vault fail to form. \\ Frog-like\\ appearance. Incompatible with life.\\n Spina Bifida Failure of Caudal neuropore closure.\\n Occulta Defect in vertebral arch only. Tuft of hair/dimple. Asymptomatic.\\n Meningocele Meninges herniate through defect. Fluid-filled sac.\\n Myelomeningocele Meninges + Spinal Cord herniate. Severe neurologic deficit paralysis sensory loss .\\n Encephalocele Herniation of brain tissue through a skull defect often occipital .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Holoprosencephaly HPE Slide 26-27 \\n Pathology Failure of the Prosencephalon Forebrain to cleavage/divide into two hemispheres.\\n Mechanism Defect in midline induction often SHH pathway .\\n Presentation Spectrum of midline facial defects Cleft lip/palate hypotelorism Cyclopia single eye and proboscis tube nose in severe cases.\\n Associations Patau Syndrome Trisomy 13 Fetal Alcohol Syndrome.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Dandy-Walker Syndrome Slide 32 \\n Pathology Agenesis or hypoplasia of the Cerebellar Vermis .\\n Mechanism Failure of the Rhombic Lip to develop Metencephalon .\\n Imaging Large posterior fossa cyst dilated 4th ventricle + small/absent cerebellum.\\n Symptoms Hydrocephalus Ataxia Delayed motor development.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Microcephaly Slide 38 \\n Pathology Small brain → Small skull Calvaria grows in response to brain growth .\\n Causes \\n Genetics Autosomal recessive.\\n Infections Zika Virus Cytomegalovirus CMV Rubella Toxoplasmosis.\\n Environment Fetal Alcohol Sulfate Radiation.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Hydrocephalus Slide 44 \\n Pathology Accumulation of CSF in ventricles due to obstruction or impaired absorption.\\n Congenital Cause Aqueductal Stenosis narrowing of Cerebral Aqueduct of Sylvius is the most common cause. Blocks flow from 3rd to 4th ventricle.\\n Signs Rapidly enlarging head circumference bulging fontanelles \\ Sunset eyes\\ .\\n Lecture 2 Overview of Neuroembryology lecturer K. Poole Ph.D. Neuro session Lecture 2 prev l1b next l3 pdf pdfs/Lecture 2. K. Poole Ph.D. PPT -1.pdf questions 1. Which of the following cells are derived from the Neural Crest? Motor neurons of the ventral horn Astrocytes of the CNS Dorsal Root Ganglion DRG cells Oligodendrocytes Microglia 2 Neural Crest cells migrate to form the DRGs autonomic ganglia Schwann cells and adrenal medulla. CNS glial cells astrocytes oligos come from the neural tube neuroectoderm . Microglia come from mesoderm. 2. A newborn has a tuft of hair over the lumbar spine but no neurologic deficits. What is the most likely diagnosis? Spina Bifida Occulta Meningocele Myelomeningocele Anencephaly Rachischisis 0 Spina Bifida Occulta is a defect in the vertebral arch mesoderm fusion often marked by a tuft of hair or dimple. The meninges and cord remain in place. 3. Which vitamin deficiency is strongly associated with Neural Tube Defects? Vitamin B12 Vitamin C Folic Acid B9 Vitamin D Vitamin A 2 Folic Acid supplementation prior to conception and during early pregnancy significantly reduces the risk of NTDs. 4. The Metencephalon gives rise to which adult structures? Medulla Midbrain Thalamus and Hypothalamus Pons and Cerebellum Cerebral Hemispheres 3 The Metencephalon develops into the Pons and Cerebellum . The Myelencephalon becomes the Medulla. 5. Which structure induces the formation of the Neural Plate from the ectoderm? Primitive Streak Notochord Yolk Sac Intermediate Mesoderm Neural Crest 1 The Notochord derived from mesoderm secretes signaling molecules e.g. Sonic Hedgehog that induce the overlying ectoderm to differentiate into the Neural Plate . Tissue origin of CNS? Neuroectoderm Neural Tube Embryology Tissue origin of PNS neurons/glia? Neural Crest Embryology Structure inducing neural plate? Notochord Embryology Adult derivative of Telencephalon? Cerebrum Cerebral Hemispheres Embryology Adult derivative of Diencephalon? Thalamus Hypothalamus Retina Embryology Failure of rostral neuropore closure? Anencephaly Pathology Failure of caudal neuropore closure? Spina Bifida Pathology Vitamin to prevent NTDs? Folic Acid B9 Prevention Closure day of Rostral Neuropore? Day 25 Embryology Closure day of Caudal Neuropore? Day 27-28 Embryology BandB Neurology > Embryology > Nervous System Development FirstAid Neurology > Embryology Pathoma CNS > Congenital Malformations Physeo Anatomy > Neuroanatomy > Embryology Germ Layer Rule Ectoderm = Skin + Brain.\\n Mesoderm = Muscle + Bone + Notochord.\\n Endoderm = Gut. Neural Crest Mnemonic MOTEL PASS M elanocytes O dontoblasts T racheal cartilage E nterochromaffin cells L aryngeal cartilage P arafollicular cells A drenal medulla S chwann cells S piral septum. Neuropore Dates Rostral closes Day 25 . Caudal closes Day 27-28 . Folate is critical before this window. Alar vs Basal Plate Alar = Dorsal = Sensory Top .\\n Basal = Ventral = Motor Bottom . Holoprosencephaly Failed forebrain cleavage. Spectrum Cleft lip → Cyclopia. Associated with Trisomy 13 Patau and SHH mutations. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l3", "title": "Lecture #3: Neuroanatomy: Meninges, BBB, CSF, Ventricles", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l3 Lecture 3 Neuroanatomy Meninges BBB CSF Ventricles\\n\\n Lecturers Vladimir Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. & Vincy Mathew\\n Session Neuroanatomy\\n\\n 1. Central Nervous System Barriers Slides 6-9 \\nThe CNS is an immunologically privileged site protected from the fluctuating chemical environment of the blood by three major barriers \\n\\n Blood-Brain Barrier BBB \\n Location Capillaries within the brain parenchyma.\\n Structure \\n Endothelial Cells The physical barrier connected by Tight Junctions Zona Occludens .\\n Pericytes Contractile cells that wrap around endothelial cells.\\n Astrocyte End-feet Induce and maintain the barrier properties.\\n Function Highly selective.\\n Permeable Lipid-soluble substances O2 CO2 Ethanol Steroid hormones .\\n Impermeable Polar/Large molecules Glucose Amino acids Dopamine require specific transporters.\\n\\n correlate\\n Parkinson s Disease & Levodopa Slide 12 \\n Problem Parkinson s is due to dopamine depletion. Dopamine is polar and cannot cross the BBB .\\n Solution Levodopa L-DOPA is a precursor that can cross the BBB via amino acid transporters. Once inside neurons it is converted to dopamine by DOPA decarboxylase.\\n \\n\\n Other Barriers\\n Blood-CSF Barrier Formed by Tight Junctions between the Epithelial Cells of the Choroid Plexus .\\n Arachnoid Barrier Avascular arachnoid epithelium seals the CSF from the extracellular fluid of the dura.\\n\\n Circumventricular Organs CVOs Slide 14-16 \\nSpecific brain regions that lack a BBB . Their capillaries are fenestrated to allow communication between the blood and brain for sensory or secretory functions.\\n Sensory CVOs \\n Area Postrema Located in the caudal medulla floor of 4th ventricle . Functions as the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone CTZ to detect toxins and induce vomiting .\\n Subfornical Organ SFO & OVLT Detect plasma osmolarity and Angiotensin II.\\n Secretory CVOs \\n Posterior Pituitary Neurohypophysis Releases Oxytocin and ADH.\\n Pineal Gland Secretes Melatonin.\\n Median Eminence Releases hypothalamic hormones.\\n\\n correlate\\n Levodopa-Induced Nausea Slide 16 \\nBecause the Area Postrema lacks a BBB circulating Levodopa can stimulate dopamine receptors there before entering the brain causing severe nausea/vomiting.\\n Prevention Co-administration of Carbidopa a peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor that does not cross the BBB.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. The Meninges Slides 20-25 \\nThe brain is encased in three layers \\ PAD\\ \\n1. Dura Mater \\ Hard Mother\\ Tough outer layer.\\n Periosteal Layer Attached to the skull.\\n Meningeal Layer Inner layer. Separates from periosteal layer to form Dural Venous Sinuses and Dural Folds Falx Cerebri Tentorium Cerebelli .\\n2. Arachnoid Mater \\ Spider Mother\\ Wispy intermediate layer.\\n Arachnoid Granulations One-way valves that drain CSF into venous sinuses.\\n3. Pia Mater \\ Soft Mother\\ Delicate inner layer adherent to the brain surface follows gyri/sulci .\\n\\n Meningeal Spaces & Hemorrhages\\n Epidural Space Potential space between Skull and Dura.\\n Vessel Middle Meningeal Artery MMA .\\n Pathology Epidural Hematoma Trauma to Pterion → Lens-shaped bleed .\\n Subdural Space Potential space between Dura and Arachnoid.\\n Vessel Bridging Veins .\\n Pathology Subdural Hematoma Shearing forces/Shaken Baby → Crescent-shaped bleed .\\n Subarachnoid Space Real space between Arachnoid and Pia.\\n Contents CSF and major cerebral arteries/veins.\\n Pathology Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Ruptured Aneurysm → \\ Thunderclap Headache\\ .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Brain Herniations Slides 26-29 \\nIncreased Intracranial Pressure ICP can force brain tissue through compartments.\\n\\n correlate\\n A. Subfalcine Cingulate Herniation \\n Displacement Cingulate gyrus herniates under the Falx Cerebri .\\n Sequelae Can compress the Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA → Contralateral leg weakness.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n B. Transtentorial Uncal Herniation \\n Displacement Medial Temporal Lobe Uncus herniates past the Tentorium Cerebelli .\\n Sequelae \\n Compresses CN III Oculomotor → Ipsilateral \\ Blown\\ Pupil Fixed & Dilated \\ Down and Out\\ eye.\\n Compresses Cerebral Peduncle → Contralateral Hemiparesis.\\n Compresses PCA → Cortical blindness.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n C. Transforaminal Tonsillar Herniation \\n Displacement Cerebellar Tonsils herniate through the Foramen Magnum .\\n Sequelae Compresses the Medulla Cardiorespiratory centers → Respiratory Arrest and death.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Ventricular System & CSF Slides 35-43 \\n CSF Flow Pathway\\n1. Lateral Ventricles via Foramen of Monro →\\n2. Third Ventricle via Cerebral Aqueduct of Sylvius →\\n3. Fourth Ventricle via Foramina of Luschka Lateral and Magendie Medial →\\n4. Subarachnoid Space →\\n5. Arachnoid Granulations →\\n6. Superior Sagittal Sinus Venous blood .\\n\\n Hydrocephalus\\nAccumulation of CSF resulting in ventricular enlargement.\\n Non-Communicating Obstructive Blockage within the ventricles.\\n Common Cause Aqueductal Stenosis Slide 44 . Lateral & 3rd ventricles dilate; 4th remains normal.\\n Communicating Blockage of absorption Arachnoid Granulations or flow in subarachnoid space. All ventricles dilate.\\n Causes Meningitis Subarachnoid Hemorrhage scarring .\\n Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH Chronic communicating hydrocephalus in elderly.\\n Triad \\ Wet Wobbly Wacky\\ Urinary Incontinence Ataxia/Magnetic Gait Dementia .\\n Hydrocephalus Ex Vacuo Apparent enlargement of ventricles due to brain atrophy e.g. Alzheimer s Huntington s . ICP is normal .\\n\\n Cerebral Venous Thrombosis CVT Slide 26 \\n Thrombosis in dural sinuses e.g. Superior Sagittal Sinus .\\n Risk Factors Hypercoagulable states Pregnancy OCPs Infection Mastoiditis .\\n Signs Headache Papilledema Seizures due to venous congestion/infarction . Lecture 3 Neuroanatomy Meninges BBB CSF Ventricles lecturer Vladimir Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. & Vincy Mathew Neuroanatomy session Lecture 3 prev l2 next l4 pdf pdfs/Lecture 3. V. Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. PPT -1.pdf mindmap Neuroanatomy BBB & Meninges\\n Barriers\\n BBB \\n Tight Junctions Zona Occludens \\n Permeable Lipids O2\\n Blocked Polar molecules Dopamine \\n CVOs No BBB \\n Area Postrema Vomiting \\n Posterior Pituitary\\n Meninges\\n Dura Outer \\n Epidural Space MMA \\n Subdural Space Bridging Veins \\n Arachnoid Middle \\n Subarachnoid Space CSF Arteries \\n Pia Inner \\n Herniations\\n Subfalcine Cingulate gyrus Leg weak \\n Uncal Temporal lobe Blown Pupil \\n Tonsillar Cerebellum Resp Arrest \\n Ventricles\\n Flow Lat → 3rd → 4th\\n Hydrocephalus NPH Wet Wobbly Wacky questions 1. A patient presents with neurological symptoms secondary to increased blood-brain barrier BBB permeability after a traumatic brain injury. Damage to which of the following proteins essential in tight junctions would most likely likely disrupt BBB integrity? Connexin-43 Laminin Zona Occludens ZO-1 Integrin Fibronectin 2 From Slide 11 Zona Occludens ZO proteins along with Claudins and Occludins form the tight junctions between endothelial cells. Damage to these compromises the barrier. 2. Levodopa is treated for Parkinson s disease because unlike Dopamine it can cross the Blood-Brain Barrier. However it frequently causes nausea. This is due to its interaction with which brainstem structure that lacks a BBB? Nucleus Tractus Solitarius Pineal Gland Area Postrema Subfornical Organ Median Eminence 2 From Slide 18 The Area Postrema is a Circumventricular Organ CVO in the medulla vomiting center that lacks a BBB. It detects emetic agents like circulating Levodopa/Dopamine in the blood before they enter the protected CNS. 3. A 26-year-old man presents with a dilated unresponsive right pupil blown pupil after a head injury. CT shows a subdural hematoma with displacement of the medial temporal lobe. What is the specific type of herniation? Subfalcine Cingulate Herniation Transforaminal Tonsillar Herniation Transtentorial Uncal Herniation Central Herniation Upward Cerebellar Herniation 2 From Slide 32 The Uncas medial temporal lobe herniating past the tentorium compresses CN III causing an ipsilateral blown pupil. This is a classic sign of Uncal Herniation. 4. Which layer of the meninges is highly vascular and contains the Middle Meningeal Artery rupture of which leads to an Epidural Hematoma? Pia Mater Arachnoid Mater Meningeal Dura Periosteal Dura / Epidural Space interface Subarachnoid Space 3 The Middle Meningeal Artery runs in the Epidural space between the skull and the periosteal layer of the dura . Trauma to the pterion often ruptures this artery. 5. In the ventricular system CSF flows from the Third Ventricle to the Fourth Ventricle via which structure? Foramen of Monro Foramen of Magendie Foramen of Luschka Cerebral Aqueduct of Sylvius Central Canal 3 CSF flows Lateral → Monro → 3rd → Aqueduct of Sylvius → 4th → Luschka/Magendie. Which proteins form Tight Junctions in the BBB? Occludin Claudin Zona Occludens ZO Concept Which brain area lacks BBB and triggers vomiting? Area Postrema Anatomy Why is Levodopa used instead of Dopamine? Dopamine is polar blocked by BBB ; Levodopa crosses and converts. Pharm Structure separating cerebral hemispheres? Falx Cerebri Anatomy Structure separating cerebrum and cerebellum? Tentorium Cerebelli Anatomy Contents of Epidural Space? Middle Meningeal Artery MMA Clinical Contents of Subdural Space? Bridging Veins Clinical Sign of Uncal Herniation? Ipsilateral Blown Pupil CN III compression Pathology Sign of Subfalcine Herniation? Contralateral leg weakness ACA compression Pathology Triad for Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH ? Wet Incontinence Wobbly Ataxia Wacky Dementia Clinical BandB Neurology > Meninges & CSF BBB Rule Lipid-soluble substances O2 Alcohol cross freely. Polar substances Dopamine Glucose need transport. Tight Junctions are key. Parkinson s Rx Give Levodopa not Dopamine. Levodopa crosses BBB via AA transporter and converts to Dopamine inside. Median Eminence vs Area Postrema Both lack BBB CVOs . Area Postrema = Vomiting CTZ . Median Eminence = Hormones. Epidural vs Subdural Epidural = Lens shaped MMA Trauma .\\n Subdural = Crescent shaped Bridging Veins Elderly . Uncal Herniation Medial temporal lobe pushes on CN III . First sign = Ipsilateral Blown Pupil . NPH Triad Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus \\n1. Wet Incontinence \\n2. Wobbly Ataxia \\n3. Wacky Dementia anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l4", "title": "Lecture #4: Blood Supply of the CNS", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l4 Lecture 4 Blood Supply of the CNS\\n\\n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. & Vincy Mathew\\n Session Neuroanatomy\\n\\n 1. Overview of Cerebral Circulation Slides 3-6 \\nThe brain represents only 2% of body weight but consumes 20% of the body s oxygen and glucose .\\nBlood enters the cranium via two major systems \\n1. Anterior Circulation Internal Carotid Arteries Supplies ~80% of the brain Telencephalon Diencephalon .\\n2. Posterior Circulation Vertebral Arteries Supplies ~20% of the brain Brainstem Cerebellum Occipital lobes .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Anterior Circulation ICA Slides 8-14 \\nThe Internal Carotid Artery ICA enters the skull via the Carotid Canal passes through the Cavernous Sinus and gives off 4 key Intradural branches OPAM \\n\\n 1. Ophthalmic Artery Slide 14 \\n First branch. Supplies the eye/retina.\\n Clinical Occlusion causes Amaurosis Fugax transient monocular blindness described as a \\ curtain coming down\\ .\\n\\n 2. Posterior Communicating Artery PCom Slide 15 \\n Connects the Anterior ICA and Posterior PCA circulations.\\n Clinical Common site for Berry Aneurysms . Aneurysm here can compress CN III causing an ipsilateral \\ Down and Out\\ eye with blown pupil.\\n\\n 3. Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA Slides 16-17 20 \\n Supplies the Medial surface of the Frontal and Parietal lobes.\\n Function Motor/Sensory to the Lower Limb Leg/Foot and trunk.\\n Stroke Symptoms Contralateral Leg weakness and sensory loss.\\n\\n 4. Middle Cerebral Artery MCA Slides 18-19 20 \\n Largest branch. Runs laterally through the Sylvian Fissure.\\n Supplies the Lateral surface of Frontal Temporal and Parietal lobes.\\n Function Motor/Sensory to the Face and Upper Limb Arm/Hand . Also supplies language centers in the dominant hemisphere.\\n Stroke Symptoms Contralateral Face/Arm weakness Face/Arm sensory loss.\\n Left MCA Dominant Aphasia Broca s or Wernicke s .\\n Right MCA Non-dominant Hemineglect.\\n\\n correlate\\n Lenticulostriate Arteries Slide 19 \\n Small deep perforating branches of the MCA supplying the Basal Ganglia and Internal Capsule.\\n Pathology High pressure Hypertension causes lipohyalinosis and microaneurysms Charcot-Bouchard Aneurysms .\\n Result Rupture leads to Intraparenchymal Hemorrhage . Occlusion leads to Lacunar Infarcts pure motor strokes .\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Posterior Circulation Vertebrobasilar Slides 23-26 \\n Vertebral Arteries VA \\n Ascend through transverse foramina of C6-C1. Enter skull via Foramen Magnum.\\n Branches \\n Anterior Spinal Artery ASA Supplies anterior 2/3 of spinal cord.\\n PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Supplies Lateral Medulla and Inferior Cerebellum. Occlusion → Wallenberg Syndrome .\\n\\n Basilar Artery BA \\n Formed by fusion of VAs. Runs along the Pons.\\n Branches \\n AICA Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Supplies Lateral Pons.\\n Pontine Arteries Supply the Pons. Occlusion → Locked-in Syndrome .\\n SCA Superior Cerebellar Artery Supplies Superior Cerebellum.\\n\\n Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA \\n Terminal bifurcation of the Basilar Artery.\\n Supplies Occipital Lobe Visual Cortex and Inferior Temporal lobe.\\n Stroke Symptoms Contralateral Homonymous Hemianopsia Visual field loss often with Macular Sparing .\\n\\n pearl\\n CN III Rule The Oculomotor Nerve CN III acts as a sandwich filling between the SCA Superior Cerebellar and PCA Posterior Cerebral arteries. Aneurysms here compress CN III.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Collateral & Venous Circulation\\n\\n Circle of Willis Slides 29-30 \\n Anastomotic ring at the base of the brain providing collateral flow.\\n Formed by ACom ACA ICA PCom PCA.\\n Note The circle is incomplete in ~50% of people.\\n Watershed Zones Border areas between ACA/MCA/PCA territories. Susceptible to ischemia during Global Hypoperfusion e.g. Cardiac Arrest or severe hypotension → \\ Man in a Barrel\\ syndrome proximal weakness .\\n\\n Venous Drainage Slides 32-42 \\nVeins drain into Valveless Dural Venous Sinuses \\n Superior Sagittal Sinus SSS Drains superficial veins + CSF via arachnoid granulations .\\n Cavernous Sinus A venous plexus surrounding the Sella Turcica.\\n Contents ICA CN VI freely inside and CN III IV V1 V2 in the wall .\\n Pathology Cavernous Sinus Thrombosis or Fistula can cause multiple cranial nerve palsies.\\n\\n Presentation \\ Thunderclap Headache\\ Worst headache of life meningeal signs.\\n Lecture 4 Blood Supply of the CNS lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 4 prev l3 next l5 pdf pdfs/Lecture 4. J. Xie Ph.D. PPT .pdf mindmap CNS Blood Supply\\n Anterior Circ ICA \\n Ophthalmic Eye Amaurosis Fugax \\n ACA Medial Brain Legs \\n MCA Lateral Brain Face/Arms + Speech \\n Lenticulostriate Lacunar stroke \\n PCom Connects to Posterior\\n Posterior Circ Vert-Basilar \\n Vertebral A. → PICA \\n Basilar A. → AICA SCA PCA \\n PCA Occipital Vision \\n Circle of Willis\\n ACom Connects ACAs \\n PCom Connects ICA-PCA \\n Venous\\n Sup. Sagittal Sinus\\n Cavernous Sinus CN III IV V VI \\n Transverse/Sigmoid → IJV questions 1. A 65-year-old man presents with sudden difficulty understanding language. He speaks fluently but uses nonsensical words Word Salad . MRI shows an ischemic stroke. Which artery is most likely involved and which area is affected? ACA - Broca s Area MCA - Broca s Area MCA - Wernicke s Area PCA - Occipital Lobe PICA - Lateral Medulla 2 From Slide 46 Wernicke s Area comprehension is in the Superior Temporal Gyrus supplied by the MCA . Damage leads to Receptive Aphasia Fluent but meaningless . Broca s Expressive is also MCA but frontal. 2. A patient experiences a curtain coming down over their right eye which resolves after a few minutes Amaurosis Fugax . This symptom is a warning sign for a blockage in which artery? Ophthalmic Artery Branch of ICA Middle Cerebral Artery Posterior Cerebral Artery Basilar Artery External Carotid Artery 0 From Slide 14 The Ophthalmic Artery is the first branch of the Intradural ICA. Transient occlusion causes temporary monocular blindness Amaurosis Fugax a TIA warning sign. 3. A patient with chronic hypertension presents with a pure motor stroke. MRI reveals a small lacunar infarct in the internal capsule. Which small arteries are likely responsible? Callosomarginal Arteries Lenticulostriate Arteries Anterior Choroidal Arteries Posterior Communicating Arteries Pontine Arteries 1 From Slide 19 Lenticulostriate Arteries deep branches of MCA supply the Basal Ganglia and Internal Capsule. They are prone to lipohyalinosis and Charcot-Bouchard aneurysms in hypertension. 4. In the Circle of Willis which artery connects the Anterior Circulation ICA system to the Posterior Circulation Vertebrobasilar system ? Anterior Communicating Artery ACom Posterior Communicating Artery PCom Anterior Choroidal Artery Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Basilar Artery 1 The Posterior Communicating Artery PCom connects the ICA to the PCA linking the two major circulatory systems. 5. Contralateral leg weakness and sensory loss is most characteristic of a stroke in which vascular territory? MCA ACA PCA PICA AICA 1 From Slide 20 The Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA supplies the medial surface of the cortex where the Leg/Foot area of the motor/sensory homunculus is located. Branches of Intradural ICA? OPAM Ophthalmic PCom ACA MCA Anatomy Which artery supplies the visual cortex? Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Anatomy Clinical sign of ACA stroke? Contralateral Leg Weakness Clinical Clinical sign of MCA stroke? Contralateral Face/Arm Weakness + Aphasia Clinical Artery passing through Cavernous Sinus? Internal Carotid Artery ICA Anatomy Transient monocular vision loss Curtain ? Amaurosis Fugax Ophthalmic Artery ischemia Clinical Common site for Berry Aneurysm causing CN III palsy? Posterior Communicating Artery PCom Pathology Vessels prone to rupture in hypertension Basal Ganglia ? Lenticulostriate Arteries Charcot-Bouchard Pathology What nerve passes between SCA and PCA? CN III Oculomotor Anatomy Risk factors for Cerebral Venous Thrombosis CVT ? Pregnancy OCPs Hypercoagulability Clinical BandB Neurology > Spinal Cord Anatomy CNS Blood Flow Rule The brain is 2% of body weight but consumes 20% of oxygen/glucose. It has zero reserve. Stroke Homunculus Rule ACA = Medial aspect = Legs/Feet .\\n MCA = Lateral aspect = Face/Arms/Hands + Speech. Amaurosis Fugax Transient monocular blindness Curtain coming down is a TIA of the Ophthalmic Artery warning of impending ICA stroke. CN III Compression Aneurysms of the PCom or between SCA and PCA often compress CN III causing a Down and Out eye with a blown pupil . Lenticulostriate Arteries These small vessels supply the Basal Ganglia. They are the primary site of hypertensive lipohyalinosis and Lacunar Infarcts . Aphasia Localization Aphasia is usually Left MCA.\\n Broca s Frontal = Broken speech.\\n Wernicke s Temporal = Word salad. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l5", "title": "Lecture #5: Clinical Applications of Neuroimaging", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l5 Lecture 5 Clinical Applications of Neuroimaging\\n\\n Lecturer S. Zakhary D.O.\\n Session Neuroanatomy\\n\\n 1. Computed Tomography CT Slides 5-18 \\n Mechanism\\n Uses Helical X-ray beams to measure the attenuation density of tissues.\\n Hounsfield Units HU The scale of density.\\n Hyperdense White Bone +1000 HU Acute Hemorrhage Calcifications.\\n Isodense Gray Brain parenchyma.\\n Hypodense Black Air -1000 HU Fat CSF Water.\\n Windows Different software settings to highlight specific tissues e.g. Bone Window for fractures Brain Window for strokes .\\n\\n Clinical Indications\\n Neurologic Emergencies The \\ workhorse\\ of the ER.\\n Acute Hemorrhage Stroke/Trauma First-line test. Fast <30s .\\n Trauma Skull fractures.\\n Initial Stroke To rule out hemorrhage before giving tPA.\\n\\n CT Contrast Iodinated \\n Uses Infection Abscess Tumors Vascular pathology CTA .\\n Contraindications/Risks \\n 1. Contrast-Induced Nephropathy CIN Check Creatinine/GFR. Hydrate.\\n 2. Allergy Mild hives to Anaphylaxis. Pre-medicate with Steroids/Benadryl.\\n 3. Metformin Risk of Lactic Acidosis . Discontinue for 48 hours.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Magnetic Resonance Imaging MRI Slides 19-27 \\n Mechanism\\n Uses strong magnetic fields to align protons. No Ionizing Radiation. \\n Sequences \\n T1 Fat is bright Anatomy . Good for structure.\\n T2 Water/CSF is bright Pathology . Good for Edema.\\n Diffusion Weighted Imaging DWI Most sensitive for Acute Ischemic Stroke shows cytotoxic edema within minutes .\\n\\n Clinical Indications\\n Soft Tissue Pathology Superior resolution for brain parenchyma.\\n Tumors Multiple Sclerosis Plaques Infections. \\n Posterior Fossa/Brainstem CT has bone artifact here; MRI does not.\\n\\n MRI Contrast Gadolinium \\n Risk Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF .\\n Rare but serious fibrosis of skin/organs.\\n Contraindicated if GFR < 30 Severe Renal Failure .\\n\\n Summary CT vs. MRI\\n\\n| Feature | CT | MRI |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Speed | Fast < 1 min | Slow 30-45 mins |\\n| Cost | Cheaper | Expensive |\\n| Radiation | Yes X-ray | No |\\n| Bone | Excellent | Poor |\\n| Soft Tissue | Fair | Excellent |\\n| Contraindications | Pregnancy relative | Metal Implants Pacemakers |\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Vascular Imaging Slides 28-38 \\n Carotid Duplex Ultrasound\\n Indication Screening for Carotid Artery Stenosis bruit TIA .\\n Pros Non-invasive No radiation Portable.\\n Cons Operator dependent.\\n\\n Conventional Angiography Digital Subtraction Angiography - DSA \\n Gold Standard for vascular pathology.\\n Procedure Catheter inserted femoral/radial → dye injected directly into vessels.\\n Usage \\n Diagnostic Aneurysms AVMs Vasculitis.\\n Therapeutic Coiling aneurysms Thrombectomy clot removal .\\n Risks Invasive stroke risk 1% access site bleeding.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Clinical Correlates Intracranial Hemorrhages Slides 47-53 \\n\\n correlate\\n Epidural Hematoma \\n Vessel Rupture of Middle Meningeal Artery MMA branch of Maxillary .\\n Cause Trauma to Pterion Temple . Rarely crosses sutures.\\n CT Appearance Lens-shaped Biconvex hyperdensity.\\n Clinical \\ Lucid Interval\\ Knocked out → Fine → Rapid Decline/Death .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Subdural Hematoma \\n Vessel Rupture of Bridging Veins .\\n Cause Shearing forces falls in elderly alcoholics shaken baby .\\n CT Appearance Crescent-shaped hyperdensity. Crosses sutures.\\n Clinical Gradual onset Chronic or Acute.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Subarachnoid Hemorrhage SAH \\n Vessel Ruptured Berry Aneurysm Circle of Willis or Trauma.\\n CT Appearance Hyperdensity in Sulci and Cisterns Starburst .\\n Clinical \\ Thunderclap Headache\\ \\ Worst headache of my life\\ Meningismus.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 5. Enhancement Patterns Slide 15 \\n Ring-Enhancing Lesion Central necrosis with peripheral enhancement.\\n Differential M.A.G.I.C. D.R. \\n R adiation necrosis Lecture 5 Clinical Applications of Neuroimaging lecturer S. Zakhary D.O. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 5 prev l4 next l6 pdf pdfs/Lecture 5. S. Zakhary D.O. PPT .pdf mindmap Neuroimaging\\n CT Scan\\n Physics X-ray Attenuation \\n Uses Acute Hemorrhage Bone Trauma\\n Contrast Iodine Risk Allergy Kidney \\n MRI\\n Physics Magnets T1/T2 \\n Uses Ischemia DWI Tumors MS\\n Contrast Gadolinium Risk NSF \\n Contra Metal Pacemaker \\n Angiography\\n DSA Gold Standard Vascular \\n Therapeutic Coiling \\n Ultrasound Carotid Stenosis screening\\n Pathology Signs\\n Ring Enhancement Abscess GBM\\n Hyperdense CT Acute Blood questions 1. A 65-year-old male presents to the ER with sudden onset right-sided weakness and aphasia starting 30 minutes ago. What is the most appropriate INITIAL imaging modality? Non-contrast CT Head MRI Brain with Gadolinium Carotid Ultrasound Conventional Angiography Lumbar Puncture 0 From Slide 48 Non-contrast CT is the first-line study for suspected stroke Code Stroke . It is fast <30s and rules out Hemorrhage . If no blood is seen the patient may be a candidate for tPA. MRI takes too long for the initial decision. 2. A diabetic patient taking Metformin is scheduled for a CT scan with IV contrast. What is the recommended management regarding their medication? Take Metformin as usual Discontinue Metformin for 48 hours BEFORE the scan Discontinue Metformin for 48 hours AFTER the scan Double the dose of Metformin Replace Metformin with Insulin permanently 2 From Slide 46/Slide 134 Metformin must be discontinued for 48 hours after contrast administration or before in elective cases to prevent the risk of Lactic Acidosis if contrast-induced nephropathy occurs. 3. Which of the following is an absolute contraindication for an MRI of the brain? Pregnancy 1st trimester Shellfish Allergy Cardiac Pacemaker Non-compatible Renal Failure GFR < 60 Claustrophobia 2 From Slide 23/Slide 57 Metallic Implants like older Pacemakers are an absolute contraindication due to the strong magnetic field. Allergy is an issue for Contrast not the MRI itself. Renal failure is an issue for Gadolinium not the MRI itself. 4. A patient with severe renal failure GFR < 30 requires neuroimaging. You specifically avoid Gadolinium-based contrast due to the risk of which condition? Anaphylaxis Lactic Acidosis Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF Acute Tubular Necrosis Stevens-Johnson Syndrome 2 From Slide 27 Gadolinium in patients with GFR < 30 is associated with Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF a rare but serious scleroderma-like fibrosing disease. 5. Ring- Enhancing Lesion on a contrast CT or MRI is classically associated with which of the following pathologies? Multiple Sclerosis Brain Abscess Ischemic Stroke Acute Meningioma Subdural Hematoma 1 From Slide 15 The classic differential for a Ring-Enhancing Lesion is Abscess or Glioblastoma/Metastasis . Meningiomas typically show diffuse homogeneous enhancement dural tail . First-line imaging for acute stroke? Non-contrast CT Head Radiology What does hyperdense white material on CT usually represent? Bone or Acute Blood Radiology Risk of Gadolinium in renal failure? Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF Pathology Classic appearance of Abscess on contrast MRI? Ring-Enhancing Lesion Radiology Contraindication for MRI? Metal Implants Pacemaker Safety Best modality for Acute Ischemic Stroke most sensitive ? Diffusion Weighted MRI DWI Radiology Metformin management with Contrast? Hold for 48 hours Risk of Lactic Acidosis Pharm Appearance of Meningioma with contrast? Diffuse enhancement + Dural Tail Radiology Gold standard for Aneurysm/AVM? Conventional Angiography DSA Radiology Mechanism of CT? X-ray attenuation Hounsfield Units Physics FirstAid Neurology > Radiology Code Stroke Imaging Non-contrast CT is the first-line radiologic intervention for suspected stroke to rule out hemorrhage. CT Density Rule Hyperdense White = Bone Acute Blood Calcification.\\n Hypodense Black = Air Fat Water CSF. Metformin & Contrast Metformin must be withheld for 48 hours after iodinated contrast administration to prevent Lactic Acidosis . MRI Safety Strong magnetic fields can dislodge ferromagnetic objects. Pacemakers and metallic implants are absolute contraindications. Gadolinium & Renal Failure Avoid Gadolinium in patients with GFR < 30 due to the risk of Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF . Ring-Enhancing Lesion Classic Differential MAGIC DR Metastasis Abscess Glioblastoma Infarct Contusion Demyelination Radiation. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l6", "title": "Lecture #6: Anatomy of the Spinal Cord", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l6 Lecture 6 Anatomy of the Spinal Cord\\n\\n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D.\\n Session Neuroanatomy\\n\\n 1. Gross Anatomy of the Spinal Cord Slides 5-8 \\nThe spinal cord is a cylindrical structure that serves as the main information superhighway between the brain and the body. It is flattened dorsoventrally and resides within the protective vertebral column.\\nCrucially the spinal cord is shorter than the vertebral column. In adults it ends at the L1-L2 vertebral level forming a tapered tip called the Conus Medullaris . Below this level the spinal canal contains a collection of nerve roots called the Cauda Equina horse s tail floating in the Lumbar Cistern of CSF.\\n\\n 2. Meninges and Vascular Supply Slides 10-11 40-41 \\nThe spinal cord is wrapped in three meningeal layers \\n1. Dura Mater The tough outer layer. Unlike the cranial dura which is double-layered the spinal dura is single-layered . Between the dura and the vertebrae lies the Epidural Space a real space containing fat and veins Venous Plexus of Batson .\\n2. Arachnoid Mater The middle spider-web-like layer.\\n3. Pia Mater The delicate inner layer attached directly to the cord. It forms lateral projections called Denticulate Ligaments that anchor the cord to the dura.\\n\\n Blood Supply \\nThe cord is supplied by one Anterior Spinal Artery ASA and two Posterior Spinal Arteries PSA .\\n ASA Supplies the anterior 2/3 of the cord including the Ventral Horns/Motor and STT/Pain .\\n PSA Supplies the posterior 1/3 Dorsal Columns/Sensory .\\n\\n correlate\\n Anterior Spinal Artery ASA Syndrome \\n- Pathophysiology Infarction of the ASA often from aortic surgery or occlusion .\\n- Presentation Sudden onset of complete motor paralysis Corticospinal tract and loss of pain/temperature sensation Spinothalamic tract below the lesion.\\n- Key Distinction Proprioception and Vibration are SPARED because the Dorsal Columns are supplied by the Posterior Spinal Arteries.\\n \\n\\n 3. Internal Topography and Tracts Slides 29-39 \\nThe internal structure consists of a central \\ H-shaped\\ butterfly of Gray Matter cell bodies surrounded by White Matter axons .\\n\\n The Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML Pathway\\nThis ascending pathway conveys fine touch vibration and proprioception .\\nPrimary afferents enter the cord and ascend ipsilaterally in the Dorsal Columns Fasciculus Gracilis for legs Cuneatus for arms . They do not synapse until they reach the Medulla Nucleus Gracilis/Cuneatus . There they decussate Internal Arcuate Fibers and ascend to the Thalamus.\\n\\n The Spinothalamic Tract STT - Anterolateral System\\nThis pathway conveys pain temperature and crude touch .\\nPrimary afferents enter via Lissauer s Tract and synapse immediately in the Dorsal Horn Substantia Gelatinosa . The second-order neurons decussate immediately via the Anterior White Commissure and ascend contralaterally.\\n\\n The Corticospinal Tract CST \\nThis is the primary descending motor pathway for voluntary movement.\\nFibers originate in the Motor Cortex descend through the brainstem and decussate in the Medulla Pyramids . They descend in the spinal cord as the Lateral Corticospinal Tract and synapse on Anterior Horn Cells Lower Motor Neurons .\\n\\n 4. Spinal Reflexes Slides 23-27 \\nReflexes are involuntary motor responses to stimuli mediated by local spinal circuits.\\n\\n Stretch Reflex Myotatic \\nThis is the simplest reflex e.g. Knee Jerk . It is Monosynaptic .\\n- Mechanism Muscle stretch stimulates specific sensory receptors Muscle Spindles → Ia afferent fires → Direct synapse on Alpha Motor Neuron → Muscle contracts.\\n- Function Maintains muscle tone and posture.\\n\\n Flexor Withdrawal & Crossed-Extensor Reflexes\\nThese are Polysynaptic .\\n- Flexor Withdrawal A painful stimulus invokes pulling the limb away Flexion .\\n- Crossed-Extensor Simultaneously the opposite limb extends to support body weight preventing falling .\\n\\n correlate\\n Tetanus \\n- Pathophysiology Clostridium tetani toxin Tetanospasmin travels retrogradely from a wound to the spinal cord. It cleaves SNARE proteins in Renshaw Cells inhibitory interneurons .\\n- Mechanism Prevents release of inhibitory neurotransmitters GABA and Glycine .\\n- Presentation Unchecked excitation of motor neurons leads to spastic paralysis lockjaw trismus and opisthotonos arching back .\\n \\n\\n 5. Clinical Correlates Additional \\n\\n correlate\\n Shingles Herpes Zoster \\n- Pathophysiology Reactivation of Varicella Zoster Virus VZV which lies dormant in the Dorsal Root Ganglia DRG .\\n- Presentation Painful vesicular rash in a specific dermatomal distribution does not cross midline .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Disc Herniation Slides 14-15 \\n- Pathophysiology The soft Nucleus Pulposus herniates through a tear in the tough Annulus Fibrosus .\\n- Location Posterolateral herniation is most common and typically compresses the spinal nerve root below the level of the disc e.g. L4/L5 disc compresses L5 root .\\n- Presentation Radiculopathy shooting pain sensory loss and weakness in the affected nerve distribution.\\n Lecture 6 Anatomy of the Spinal Cord lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 6 prev l5 next l7 pdf pdfs/Lecture 6. J. Xie Ph.D. PPT .pdf mindmap Spinal Cord\\n Tracts\\n Ascending Sensory \\n DCML Touch/Prop. Ipsilateral in cord → Decussates Medulla\\n STT Pain/Temp. Contralateral in cord → Decussates Cord\\n Descending Motor \\n CST Motor Ipsilateral in cord → Decussates Medulla\\n Reflexes\\n Stretch Monosynaptic DTRs \\n Withdrawal Polysynaptic Pain \\n Pathology\\n ASA Syndrome Spares Dorsal Columns\\n Brown-Sequard Hemi-section\\n Ipsi Motor Proprioception\\n Contra Pain/Temp\\n Tetanus Blocks Inhibition questions 1. A patient presents with loss of pain and temperature sensation in the right leg and spastic paralysis and loss of vibration sense in the left leg. This Dissociated Sensory Loss pattern Brown-S\\u00e9quard Syndrome is caused by a hemisection lesion on which side of the spinal cord? Right side Ipsilateral to pain loss Left side Ipsilateral to motor/vibration loss Bilateral central lesion Dorsal Columns only Anterior Horn only 1 Brown-S\\u00e9quard Hemisection <br>- Ipsilateral signs Motor CST decussated above + Vibration DCML decussates above .<br>- Contralateral signs Pain/Temp STT decussates immediately . <br>Since motor/vibration loss is on the Left the lesion is on the Left . 2. Tetanus toxin causes spastic paralysis by blocking the release of inhibitory neurotransmitters GABA and Glycine from which specific spinal cord cells? Alpha Motor Neurons Gamma Motor Neurons Renshaw Cells Dorsal Root Ganglion Cells Clarke s Nucleus Cells 2 From Slide 22 Tetanospasmin blocks the release of GABA/Glycine from Renshaw Cells inhibitory interneurons leading to unchecked excitation tetany . 3. An infarction of the Anterior Spinal Artery ASA would result in all of the following deficits below the lesion level EXCEPT Loss of pain sensation STT Loss of temperature sensation STT Spastic Paralysis CST Loss of vibration and proprioception DCML Loss of crude touch 3 From Slide 41 The ASA supplies the anterior 2/3 of the cord STT CST . The Dorsal Columns DCML are supplied by the Posterior Spinal Arteries and are SPARED in ASA syndrome. 4. Which spinal tract carries fine touch and proprioception and ascends ipsilaterally within the spinal cord? Lateral Corticospinal Tract Spinothalamic Tract Dorsal Column Medial Lemniscus DCML Rubrospinal Tract Vestibulospinal Tract 2 The DCML enters the cord and ascends in the posterior columns on the SAME side ipsilateral until it reaches the medulla where it decussates. 5. In the stretch reflex e.g. Knee Jerk the primary afferent neuron synapses directly onto the alpha-motor neuron. This type of reflex is classified as Polysynaptic Disynaptic Monosynaptic Intersegmental Contralateral 2 From Slide 24 The stretch reflex is the simplest reflex and is Monosynaptic one synapse between sensory and motor neuron . Function of Dorsal Column Medial Lemniscus DCML ? Fine Touch Vibration Proprioception Neuro Function of Spinothalamic Tract STT ? Pain and Temperature Neuro Function of Corticospinal Tract CST ? Voluntary Motor Control Neuro Where does DCML decussate? Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers Neuro Where does STT decussate? Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure Neuro Where does CST decussate? Medulla Pyramids Neuro What is spard in Anterior Spinal Artery Syndrome? Dorsal Columns Proprioception/Vibration Pathology Virus dormant in DRG causing Shingles? Varicella Zoster Virus VZV Micro Toxin blocking Renshaw Cells GABA/Glycine ? Tetanus Toxin Micro Reflex level for Patellar Knee Jerk? L3-L4 Clinical BandB Neurology > Somatosensory Systems Lumbar Puncture LP Because the spinal cord ends at L1-L2 a Lumbar Puncture is safely performed at the L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace to avoid damaging the cord. Vascular Watershed The mid-thoracic region T4-T8 is a vascular watershed area and is most susceptible to infarction during hypotension. DCML Rule Lesions in the spinal cord affect the DCML Ipsilaterally because it doesn t cross until the medulla . STT Rule Lesions in the spinal cord affect the STT Contralaterally typically 1-2 segments below the lesion due to Lissauer s tract ascent . CST Rule Lesions in the spinal cord cause Ipsilateral Spastic Paralysis UMN signs below the lesion because it already crossed in the medulla . Renshaw Cells Renshaw cells are the \\ brakes\\ of the motor system. Without them e.g. Tetanus toxin blockade motor neurons fire uncontrollably. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l7", "title": "Lecture #7: Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem I", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l7 Lecture 7 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem I\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Neuroanatomy\\n\\n 1. Overview of Brainstem Divisions Slides 4-5 \\nThe brainstem is divided into three major regions Rostral to Caudal \\n1. Midbrain Mesencephalon The most superior portion.\\n2. Pons Metencephalon The middle bulbous portion.\\n3. Medulla Myelencephalon The most inferior portion continuous with the spinal cord.\\n\\n 2. External Anatomy Ventral View Slide 6 \\n Midbrain Characterized by the Cerebral Peduncles Crus Cerebri massive stalks containing descending motor fibers from the cortex.\\n Pons Characterized by the Basilar Pons a bulbous structure with transverse fibers.\\n Medulla \\n Pyramids Two medial columns containing the Corticospinal Tract .\\n Pyramidal Decussation The crossing point of motor fibers at the caudal medulla border with spinal cord .\\n Olive A lateral bump produced by the Inferior Olivary Nucleus motor coordination .\\n\\n 3. External Anatomy Dorsal View Slide 7-8 \\n Midbrain Tectum \\n Superior Colliculi Visual reflexes/saccades.\\n Inferior Colliculi Auditory pathway.\\n Pons \\n Cerebellar Peduncles \\n Superior SCP Main output from cerebellum.\\n Middle MCP Connects Pons to Cerebellum massive input .\\n Inferior ICP Connects Medulla to Cerebellum.\\n Floor of 4th Ventricle The open posterior aspect.\\n Medulla \\n Gracile Tubercle Medial bump Leg sensory .\\n Cuneate Tubercle Lateral bump Arm sensory .\\n Obex The caudal point where the 4th ventricle closes.\\n\\n 4. Internal Anatomy Cross-Sectional Zones Slide 10 \\nIn cross-section the brainstem is divided anterior-to-posterior \\n1. Basilar Region Ventral Contains descending motor tracts Corticospinal Corticobulbar .\\n2. Tegmentum Middle The \\ core\\ containing Cranial Nerve Nuclei Reticular Formation and sensory tracts.\\n3. Tectum Dorsal Found only in the Midbrain Colliculi .\\n\\n 5. Key Nuclei by Level Slides 15-26 \\n\\n Medulla\\n Gracile & Cuneate Nuclei Dorsal column nuclei Touch/Proprioception .\\n Inferior Olivary Nucleus Large crumpled sac appearance. Motor learning Climbing fibers to cerebellum .\\n Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Pain/Temp for the face extends down to C2 .\\n\\n Pons\\n Pontine Nuclei Scattered in the basilar pons. Relay cortex information to the cerebellum via the MCP.\\n Facial Nucleus CN VII Motor to face.\\n Abducens Nucleus CN VI Lateral eye movement.\\n\\n Midbrain\\n Substantia Nigra Dark pigmented line Dopamine . Degenerates in Parkinson s.\\n Red Nucleus Motor coordination Rubrospinal tract .\\n Periaqueductal Gray PAG Pain modulation Opioid receptors .\\n Superior & Inferior Colliculi. \\n\\n correlate\\n Cranial Nerve Exit Points Rule of 4s \\n Midbrain CN 3 4 Oculomotor interpeduncular fossa Trochlear dorsal exit! .\\n Pons CN 5 6 7 8 Trigeminal lateral pons Abducens/Facial/Vestibulocochlear pontomedullary junction .\\n Medulla CN 9 10 11 12 Glossopharyngeal/Vagus/Accessory retro-olivary Hypoglossal pre-olivary sulcus .\\n Lecture 7 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem I lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 7 prev l6 next l8 pdf pdfs/Lecture 7. I. Kurtzer Ph.D. PPT .pdf mindmap Brainstem Anatomy I\\n Midbrain\\n Ventral Cerebral Peduncles Motor \\n Dorsal Tectum Sup/Inf Colliculi \\n Internal Aqueduct Substantia Nigra Red Nucleus\\n CN III IV\\n Pons\\n Ventral Basilar Pons Transverse fibers \\n Dorsal 4th Ventricle Floor\\n Internal Pontine Nuclei MCP\\n CN V VI VII VIII\\n Medulla\\n Ventral Pyramids Olive\\n Dorsal Gracile/Cuneate Tubercles\\n Internal Inf. Olive DC Nuclei\\n CN IX X XI XII questions 1. Which structure is a hallmark feature of the dorsal midbrain? Pyramids Inferior Olive Superior Colliculus Fourth Ventricle Gracile Tubercle 2 The Superior Colliculus is part of the Tectum found on the dorsal aspect of the midbrain. The Pyramids and Olive are ventral medulla structures. 2. The pyramidal decussation marks the caudal boundary of which brainstem division? Midbrain Pons Medulla Diencephalon Spinal Cord 2 The Pyramidal Decussation is found at the junction of the Medulla and the spinal cord. 3. Which cranial nerve is the only one to exit from the dorsal aspect of the brainstem? CN III Oculomotor CN IV Trochlear CN V Trigeminal CN VI Abducens CN VII Facial 1 CN IV Trochlear exits dorsally from the midbrain tectum. All other cranial nerves exit ventrally or laterally. 4. Damage to the Substantia Nigra in the midbrain is most commonly associated with which disorder? Huntington s Disease Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Multiple Sclerosis Myasthenia Gravis 2 The Substantia Nigra contains dopaminergic neurons. Their degeneration leads to the motor symptoms of Parkinson s Disease . 5. Which ventricular space is located between the Pons/Medulla and the Cerebellum? Lateral Ventricle Third Ventricle Cerebral Aqueduct Fourth Ventricle Central Canal 3 The Fourth Ventricle is the open space bounded anteriorly by the Pons/Medulla and posteriorly by the Cerebellum. Three divisions of Brainstem Rostral→Caudal ? Midbrain → Pons → Medulla Anatomy Hallmark dorsal structure of Midbrain? Tectum Corpora Quadrigemina / Colliculi Anatomy Ventral columns of Medulla contain? Corticospinal Tract Pyramids Anatomy Only CN to exit dorsally? CN IV Trochlear Anatomy What marks the Medulla-Spinal Cord border? Pyramidal Decussation Anatomy Function of Inferior Colliculus? Auditory Processing Physiology Function of Superior Colliculus? Visual/Saccadic Eye Movements Physiology Function of Substantia Nigra? Dopamine production Motor Control Physiology Nucleus responsible for Olive bump? Inferior Olivary Nucleus Anatomy Ventricular space of the Midbrain? Cerebral Aqueduct Anatomy Tectum Location The Tectum Roof exists ONLY in the Midbrain . It consists of the Superior and Inferior Colliculi. Cerebral Aqueduct The Cerebral Aqueduct is a hallmark of the Midbrain . The 4th Ventricle is associated with the Pons and Medulla. Decussation Rule The Pyramidal Decussation crossing of motor fibers marks the transition from Medulla to Spinal Cord. CN IV Uniqueness The Trochlear Nerve CN IV is the ONLY cranial nerve to exit from the Dorsal aspect of the brainstem. Inferior Olive Function The Inferior Olive Medulla is a MAJOR source of input to the cerebellum Climbing Fibers for motor learning. Substantia Nigra Located in the Midbrain cerebral peduncles. Contains Dopaminergic neurons. Loss = Parkinson s Disease. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l8", "title": "Lecture 8: Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem II - Major Tracts and Cranial Nerve Nuclei", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l8 Lecture 8 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem II - Major Tracts and Cranial Nerve Nuclei\\n\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 8\\n\\n 1. Major Tracts of the Brainstem Slide 3-17 \\n\\nThe brainstem serves as a major conduit for ascending and descending pathways connecting the spinal cord cerebellum and cerebrum. Understanding the relative positions of these tracts at different levels Midbrain Pons Medulla is crucial for localizing lesions.\\n\\n Descending Tracts\\n Corticospinal Tract \\n Function Voluntary motor control.\\n Pathway Descends from the cerebral cortex.\\n Trajectory It is always the most ventral structure throughout the brainstem.\\n Decussation Most fibers decussate at the caudal medulla pyramidal decussation to form the lateral corticospinal tract in the spinal cord.\\n\\n Ascending Tracts\\n Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML \\n Function Fine touch vibration and proprioception for the body.\\n Pathway \\n Spinal Cord Ascends centrally as the Dorsal Columns Gracile and Cuneate fasciculi .\\n Medulla Synapses in the Dorsal Column Nuclei Gracile and Cuneate nuclei .\\n Decussation Axons form Internal Arcuate Fibers which decussate in the caudal medulla Slide 16 .\\n Brainstem Ascent Ascends as the Medial Lemniscus .\\n Trajectory It is always medial to the spinothalamic tract. Its orientation changes from vertical medulla to horizontal midbrain as it ascends.\\n Spinothalamic Tract Anterolateral System \\n Function Pain and temperature sensation.\\n Pathway Decussates immediately in the spinal cord.\\n Trajectory Ascends through the brainstem in a lateral position. It remains lateral to the DCML at all levels Slide 17 .\\n\\n 2. Cerebellar Peduncles Slide 18-20 \\n\\nThe cerebellum communicates with the brainstem via three paired peduncles located at specific levels \\n\\n Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle ICP \\n Level Medulla and lower Pons.\\n Function Primarily afferent input to the cerebellum from the spinal cord e.g. dorsal spinocerebellar tract and medulla.\\n Middle Cerebellar Peduncle MCP \\n Level Pons.\\n Function Entirely afferent input from the pontine nuclei corticopontine pathway . It is the largest peduncle.\\n Superior Cerebellar Peduncle SCP \\n Level Upper Pons and lower Midbrain.\\n Function Primarily efferent output from the deep cerebellar nuclei to the red nucleus and thalamus.\\n\\n 3. Cranial Nerve Nuclei Organization Slide 22-26 \\n\\nThe organization of cranial nerve nuclei follows a rational developmental logic based on the neural tube plates alar = sensory basal = motor .\\n\\n Functional Columns\\nNuclei are arranged in longitudinal columns. A general rule for the brainstem is Motor is Medial Sensory is Lateral .\\n\\n Motor Columns Medial to Lateral \\n 1. Somatic Motor GSE Most medial near the midline. Innervates myotome-derived muscles tongue eye .\\n Nuclei Oculomotor III Trochlear IV Abducens VI Hypoglossal XII .\\n 2. Branchial Motor SVE Ventrolateral. Innervates pharyngeal arch muscles.\\n Nuclei Trigeminal Motor V Facial Motor VII Nucleus Ambiguus IX X Spinal Accessory XI .\\n 3. Visceral Motor GVE Parasympathetic.\\n Nuclei Edinger-Westphal III Superior Salivatory VII Inferior Salivatory IX Dorsal Motor Nucleus of Vagus X .\\n\\n Sensory Columns Medial to Lateral \\n 1. Visceral Sensory GVA/SVA \\n Nuclei Nucleus Solitarius VII IX X - taste and cardiorespiratory .\\n 2. Somatic Sensory GSA General sensation from face.\\n Nuclei Trigeminal Nuclei Mesencephalic Pontine Spinal .\\n 3. Special Sensory SSA Most lateral.\\n Nuclei Vestibular and Cochlear Nuclei VIII .\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Cranial Nerves Sensory/Motor/Both \\n \\ Some Say Marry Money But My Brother Says Big Brains Matter More\\ \\n\\n S ome I - Sensory\\n S ay II - Sensory\\n M arry III - Motor\\n M oney IV - Motor\\n B ut V - Both\\n M y VI - Motor\\n B rother VII - Both\\n S ays VIII - Sensory\\n B ig IX - Both\\n B rains X - Both\\n M atter XI - Motor\\n M ore XII - Motor\\n Lecture 8 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem II - Major Tracts and Cranial Nerve Nuclei Neuroscience questions A patient presents with loss of vibration and proprioception on the left side of the body. A lesion is located in the medulla. Which structure is most likely affected? Left Nucleus Gracilis/Cuneate Right Medial Lemniscus Left Medial Lemniscus Right Spinothalamic Tract Left Lateral Corticospinal Tract 1 The DCML decussates in the caudal medulla as internal arcuate fibers. After decussation the fibers ascend as the Medial Lemniscus on the contralateral side. Therefore a lesion in the medulla affecting the Right Medial Lemniscus would cause loss of vibration/proprioception on the Left side. Which cranial nerve nucleus column is located most laterally in the brainstem? Somatic Motor Branchial Motor Visceral Motor Somatic Sensory Special Sensory 4 Following the Motor Medial Sensory Lateral rule the Special Sensory column hearing and balance CN VIII is the most lateral column. The Middle Cerebellar Peduncle conveys fibers primarily from which location? Spinal Cord Medulla Pons Midbrain Deep Cerebellar Nuclei 2 The Middle Cerebellar Peduncle MCP connects the Pons to the Cerebellum and conveys afferent fibers from pontine nuclei. Where does the Corticospinal tract decussate? Midbrain Upper Pons Lower Pons Upper Medulla Caudal Medulla 4 The Corticospinal tract descends ventrally and decussates at the Pyramids in the Caudal lower Medulla. Which nucleus provides parasympathetic innervation to the pupil constriction ? Oculomotor Nucleus Edinger-Westphal Nucleus Trochlear Nucleus Abducens Nucleus Superior Salivatory Nucleus 1 The Edinger-Westphal nucleus is the Visceral Motor parasympathetic component of CN III Oculomotor responsible for pupil constriction. Decussation level of Corticospinal Tract Caudal Medulla pyramids Neuroanatomy Decussation level of DCML Caudal Medulla internal arcuate fibers Neuroanatomy Decussation level of Spinothalamic Tract Spinal Cord anterior white commissure Neuroanatomy Relative position of DCML vs Spinothalamic in brainstem DCML is always MEDIAL to Spinothalamic Neuroanatomy Brainstem level of Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle Medulla and Lower Pons Neuroanatomy Brainstem level of Middle Cerebellar Peduncle Pons Neuroanatomy Brainstem level of Superior Cerebellar Peduncle Upper Pons and Lower Midbrain Neuroanatomy General rule for CN Nuclei location Medial-Lateral Motor is Medial Sensory is Lateral Neuroanatomy Nucleus responsible for taste CN VII IX X Nucleus Solitarius Gustatory nucleus Neuroanatomy Nucleus supplying pharyngeal muscles swallowing Nucleus Ambiguus CN IX X XI Neuroanatomy BandB Neurology > Brainstem > Brainstem Anatomy FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Brainstem Pathoma CNS Tract Trajectories Rule The Corticospinal tract is always the most ventral structure. The DCML is always medial to the Spinothalamic tract. The Spinothalamic tract is always lateral . Medial vs. Lateral Rule In the brainstem cranial nerve nuclei organization Motor is Medial Sensory is Lateral . Decussation Levels Corticospinal Caudal Medulla Pyramids .\\\\n DCML Caudal Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers .\\\\n Spinothalamic Spinal Cord immediately on entry . Peduncle Levels Inferior Medulla/Lower Pons Afferent .\\\\n Middle Pons Afferent .\\\\n Superior Upper Pons/Midbrain Efferent . Nucleus Ambiguus The Nucleus Ambiguus supplies branchial motor innervation to the pharynx and larynx swallowing gag reflex via CN IX and X. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l9", "title": "Lecture 9: Reticular Formation", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l9 Lecture 9 Reticular Formation\\n\\n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 9\\n\\n 1. Reticular Formation Overview Slide 6-7 \\n\\nThe Reticular Formation RF is a phylogenetically old set of nuclei located throughout the brainstem core extending from the caudal medulla to the lateral hypothalamus. It is not a single anatomical structure but a diffuse network responsible for \\ life-sustaining\\ functions.\\n\\n Structure Composed of short interneurons reflexes and long projection neurons neuromodulation .\\n Key Concept A single neuron can synapse with 25 000+ others allowing diffuse modulation of the entire CNS.\\n\\n 2. Neurochemistry of the Reticular Formation Slide 8-13 \\n\\nThe RF houses the nuclei for major neuromodulatory systems. You MUST know the location and neurotransmitter for each \\n\\n Serotonin 5-HT \\n Nuclei Raphe Nuclei midline of entire brainstem .\\n Functions Mood Arousal Pain modulation Raphe Magnus Sleep.\\n Norepinephrine NE \\n Nuclei Locus Coeruleus pons/midbrain junction and Lateral Tegmental Area.\\n Functions Arousal RAS Attention \\ Fight or Flight\\ Pain modulation.\\n Dopamine DA \\n Nuclei Ventral Tegmental Area VTA and Substantia Nigra Midbrain .\\n Pathways \\n Nigrostriatal Coordination of movement degenerate in Parkinson s .\\n Mesolimbic Reward/Addiction Schizophrenia positive symptoms .\\n Mesocortical Cognition Schizophrenia negative symptoms .\\n Acetylcholine ACh \\n Nuclei Pedunculopontine Tegmental Nucleus PPT Brainstem and Nucleus Basalis of Meynert Forebrain .\\n Functions Arousal REM sleep generation Memory degenerate in Alzheimer s .\\n Histamine \\n Nuclei Tuberomammillary Nucleus Posterior Hypothalamus .\\n Function Arousal/Wakefulness Antihistamines cause drowsiness .\\n\\n 3. Reticular Activating System ARAS & Consciousness Slide 16-24 \\n\\nThe Ascending Reticular Activating System ARAS projects to the cortex to maintain Wakefulness and Alertness .\\n\\n Disorders of Consciousness\\n Coma Low arousal no awareness. Dysfunction of ARAS or bilateral cortex.\\n Persistent Vegetative State \\ Unresponsive Wakefulness\\ . Arousal is present eyes open sleep-wake cycles but NO awareness of environment.\\n Minimally Conscious State Intermittent inconsistent awareness.\\n\\n correlate\\n Locked-in Syndrome \\n Pathophysiology Ischemic stroke of the Ventral Pons Basilar Artery .\\n Presentation Quadriplegia corticospinal disruption and anarthria corticobulbar disruption .\\n Key Feature The ARAS is spared tegmentum so the patient is Fully Awake and Aware but cannot move.\\n Communication Vertical eye movements controlled by midbrain and blinking are usually preserved.\\n \\n\\n 4. Sleep Physiology Slide 25-33 \\n\\n Stages \\n N1-N2 Light sleep Sleep Spindles K-Complexes .\\n N3 Slow Wave Sleep Delta waves - Deep restorative sleep.\\n REM Rapid Eye Movement dreaming atonia. EEG looks like \\ Awake\\ Beta/Sawtooth .\\n Neurochemistry \\n Wakefulness Promoters NE 5-HT Histamine ACh Hypocretin Orexin .\\n Sleep Promoters GABA VLPO nucleus and Melatonin.\\n\\n correlate\\n Narcolepsy \\n Pathophysiology Loss of Hypocretin Orexin neurons in the lateral hypothalamus.\\n Presentation \\n Excessive Daytime Sleepiness. \\n Cataplexy Sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by emotion.\\n Sleep Paralysis and Hypnagogic Hallucinations .\\n Treatment Stimulants Amphetamines to increase NE/DA.\\n \\n\\n 5. Descending Motor & Pain Control Slide 37-39 \\n\\n Motor Rigidity Posturing \\n Decorticate Posturing Flexion of upper limbs. Lesion Above Red Nucleus midbrain . Rubrospinal tract intact.\\n Decerebrate Posturing Extension of all limbs. Lesion Below Red Nucleus . Vestibulospinal tracts dominate. Worse prognosis.\\n\\n Descending Pain Modulation\\nThe brainstem can inhibit incoming pain signals Pain Inhibits Pain .\\n Pathway Periaqueductal Gray PAG → Rostral Ventromedial Medulla RVM Raphe Magnus → Dorsal Horn block pain transmission via Enkephalins/Serotonin/NE .\\n Clinical Antidepressants like SnRIs Duloxetine or TCAs Amitriptyline treat chronic pain by boosting NE/5-HT in this inhibitory pathway. Lecture 9 Reticular Formation Neuroscience questions A patient presents with locked-in syndrome following a stroke. They are fully aware but cannot move their limbs or speak. Which structure is most likely spared preserving their consciousness? Basis Pontis Corticospinal Tracts Reticular Activating System Tegmentum Medullary Pyramids Internal Capsule 2 Locked-in syndrome is caused by a lesion to the ventral pons basis pontis destroying corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts. Consciousness is preserved because the Reticular Activating System ARAS located in the dorsal tegmentum is spared. Which neurotransmitter is synthesized primarily in the Locus Coeruleus? Serotonin Dopamine Acetylcholine Norepinephrine Histamine 3 The Locus Coeruleus is the primary site of Norepinephrine NE synthesis in the brainstem involved in arousal and attention. A comatose patient exhibits abnormal posturing with extension of both upper and lower extremities to painful stimuli. This Decerebrate rigidity suggests a lesion at which level? Cortex Above the Red Nucleus At or Below the Red Nucleus Spinal Cord Thalamus 2 Decerebrate rigidity extension of all limbs implies a lesion at or below the Red Nucleus midbrain/upper pons removing rubrospinal influence and leaving vestibulospinal extension unchecked. A 25-year-old male complains of excessive daytime sleepiness and episodes of sudden muscle weakness when laughing cataplexy . A deficiency in which neuropeptide is likely? Serotonin GABA Hypocretin Orexin Dopamine Melatonin 2 Narcolepsy with cataplexy is classically caused by a loss of Hypocretin Orexin neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. Which drug class treats chronic neuropathic pain by enhancing the descending pain inhibitory pathway from the brainstem? SSRIs Fluoxetine Benzodiazepines SNRIs Duloxetine Dopamine Agonists Antihistamines 2 SNRIs like Duloxetine and TCAs are effective for neuropathic pain because they inhibit reuptake of Norepinephrine and Serotonin enhancing the descending inhibitory signals from the RVM/PAG to the spinal cord. Location of Serotonin 5-HT cell bodies Raphe Nuclei Neurochemistry Location of Norepinephrine NE cell bodies Locus Coeruleus Neurochemistry Location of Dopamine DA cell bodies Midbrain Substantia Nigra & Ventral Tegmental Area VTA Neurochemistry Location of Histamine cell bodies Tuberomammillary Nucleus Hypothalamus Neurochemistry Lesion location for Decorticate Rigidity Above the Red Nucleus Midbrain Neurology Lesion location for Decerebrate Rigidity At or Below the Red Nucleus Neurology Which is worse Decorticate or Decerebrate? Decerebrate indicates deeper brainstem damage Neurology Neurotransmitter deficiency in Narcolepsy Hypocretin Orexin Neurology Area spared in Locked-in Syndrome Reticular Activating System Tegmentum & Vertical Eye Movements Neurology Structure responsible for Descending Pain Modulation Periaqueductal Gray PAG Neurology BandB Neurology > Brainstem > Brainstem Anatomy FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Coma & Sleep Pathoma CNS Decorticate vs. Decerebrate Decorticate Flexor posturing Hands to C ore . Lesion Above Red Nucleus.\\\\n Decerebrate Extensor posturing worse . Lesion Below Red Nucleus. Locating Neurotransmitters Raphe Nuclei = Serotonin 5-HT .\\\\n Locus Coeruleus = Norepinephrine NE .\\\\n Substantia Nigra = Dopamine DA .\\\\n Tuberomammillary Nucleus = Histamine. Locked-in Syndrome Caused by a Ventral Pontine stroke Basilar artery . The ARAS is spared Tegmentum so the patient is fully awake/aware but quadriplegic. Can only blink or move eyes vertically. Narcolepsy Mechanism Caused by a deficiency of Hypocretin Orexin producing neurons in the Lateral Hypothalamus . Pain Modulation Pathway PAG Midbrain → RVM Medulla → Spinal Cord Dorsal Horn. Mediated by Opioids Serotonin and NE. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l10", "title": "Lecture 10: Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l10 Lecture 10 Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System\\n\\n Lecturer V. Grubisic M.D. Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 10\\n\\n 1. ANS Organization Slide 2-16 \\n\\nThe Autonomic Nervous System ANS maintains homeostasis through two opposing divisions Sympathetic \\ Fight or Flight\\ and Parasympathetic \\ Rest and Digest\\ . Both systems utilize a two-neuron chain preganglionic and postganglionic .\\n\\n Sympathetic Division\\n Origin Thoracolumbar T1-L2 lateral horn.\\n Neuron Lengths Short Preganglionic Long Postganglionic.\\n Neurotransmitters \\n Preganglionic Acetylcholine Nicotinic receptors .\\n Postganglionic Norepinephrine Adrenergic receptors mostly.\\n Exceptions Sweat glands ACh/Muscarinic and Adrenal Medulla Epi/NE directly into blood .\\n\\n Parasympathetic Division\\n Origin Craniosacral CN III VII IX X and S2-S4 .\\n Neuron Lengths Long Preganglionic Short Postganglionic ganglia near target .\\n Neurotransmitters \\n Preganglionic Acetylcholine Nicotinic .\\n Postganglionic Acetylcholine Muscarinic .\\n\\n 2. Clinical Correlates of ANS Dysfunction\\n\\n Horner s Syndrome Slide 14 \\nLesion of the sympathetic pathway to the head/neck.\\n Triad Ptosis droopy eyelid Miosis constricted pupil Anhidrosis lack of sweating .\\n\\n Autonomic Dysreflexia Slide 28 \\n Cause Spinal Cord Injury above T6 .\\n Mechanism A noxious stimulus e.g. full bladder triggers a massive sympathetic surge below the lesion.\\n Consequence Widespread vasoconstriction causes life-threatening Hypertension .\\n Reflex Baroreceptors detect high BP → Vagus nerve slows heart → Bradycardia .\\n Result Hypertension + Bradycardia.\\n\\n Bladder Dysfunction Slide 31-32 \\n Spastic UMN Bladder Lesion above Pontine Micturition Center PMC but below cortex or between PMC and cord. Reflex is hyperactive. Detrusor contracts against closed sphincter.\\n Atonic LMN Bladder Lesion of Sacral cord or Cauda Equina. Reflex arc destroyed. Bladder fills but cannot contract overflow incontinence .\\n\\n 3. Enteric Nervous System ENS Slide 33-37 \\n\\nThe \\ Brain of the Gut\\ capable of functioning independently.\\n Plexuses Myenteric Auerbach s - Motility and Submucosal Meissner s - Secretion .\\n\\n correlate\\n Hirschsprung s Disease \\n Pathophysiology Congenital failure of Neural Crest Cells to migrate to the distal colon.\\n Presentation Newborns fail to pass meconium.\\n Result Aganglionic Megacolon . The affected segment lacks ENS plexuses and remains constantly constricted cannot relax causing obstruction and dilation upstream.\\n Treatment Surgical resection of the aganglionic segment.\\n \\n\\n 4. Cardiovascular Reflexes Slide 27 \\n\\n Carotid Sinus Massage Mechanically stimulates baroreceptors → mimics high BP → increases Vagal tone → slows Heart Rate. Used to terminate SVT.\\n highyield\\n Contraindication Do not perform Carotid Massage in patients with carotid stenosis or recent stroke as it may dislodge an embolus.\\n Lecture 10 Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System Neuroscience questions A patient with a T4 spinal cord injury develops a pounding headache and profuse sweating. Their blood pressure is 210/110 mmHg and heart rate is 50 bpm. What is the most likely physiological mechanism? Hypovolemic Shock Autonomic Dysreflexia Septic Shock Orthostatic Hypotension Vasovagal Syncope 1 This is classic Autonomic Dysreflexia a noxious stimulus triggers a sympathetic surge below the lesion Hypertension which triggers a baroreceptor reflex to slow the heart Bradycardia . Which neurotransmitter is released by sympathetic postganglionic neurons innervating sweat glands? Norepinephrine Epinephrine Acetylcholine Dopamine Serotonin 2 Sweat glands are the exception to the rule. Sympathetic postganglionic neurons innervating them release Acetylcholine acting on Muscarinic receptors. A newborn fails to pass meconium within 48 hours. Imaging reveals a dilated colon proximal to a narrowed distal segment. Biopsy of the narrowed segment would likely show Hypertrophy of the muscularis externa Absence of Myenteric and Submucosal ganglia Inflammation of the mucosa Excessive Neural Crest Cell migration Presence of Lewy Bodies 1 Hirschsprung s Disease is characterized by the absence of ganglia aganglionosis in the distal colon due to failed neural crest migration. Horner s Syndrome is characterized by which constellation of signs? Mydriasis Ptosis Anhidrosis Miosis Exophthalmos Hyperhidrosis Miosis Ptosis Anhidrosis Mydriasis Exophthalmos Anhidrosis Ptosis Miosis Hyperhidrosis 2 Horner s Syndrome presents with Miosis constriction Ptosis drooping and Anhidrosis dryness on the ipsilateral side. Where are the cell bodies of preganglionic sympathetic neurons located? Brainstem Nuclei Sacral Spinal Cord S2-S4 Thoracolumbar Lateral Horn T1-L2 Dorsal Root Ganglia Paravertebral Ganglia 2 The sympathetic division is Thoracolumbar meaning preganglionic neurons originate in the lateral horn of the spinal cord from T1 to L2. Location of Sympathetic Preganglionic Neurons Thoracolumbar Lateral Horn T1-L2 Anatomy Location of Parasympathetic Preganglionic Neurons Craniosacral Brainstem + S2-S4 Anatomy Neurotransmitter of ALL Preganglionic Neurons Acetylcholine Nicotinic Receptor Physiology Neurotransmitter of Sympathetic Postganglionic Neurons Target Norepinephrine Adrenergic Receptor Physiology Neurotransmitter of Parasympathetic Postganglionic Neurons Target Acetylcholine Muscarinic Receptor Physiology Triad of Horner s Syndrome Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis Pathology Condition causing Aganglionic Megacolon Hirschsprung s Disease Pathology Signs of Autonomic Dysreflexia Hypertension + Bradycardia + Headache/Sweating Pathology Spinal cord level risk for Autonomic Dysreflexia Above T6 Pathology Bladder type in Lower Motor Neuron Sacral lesion Atonic Flaccid Bladder Pathology BandB Neurology > Autonomic Nervous System > ANS Pharmacology FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Autonomic Nervous System Sympathetic vs. Parasympathetic Lengths Sympathetic Short Pre Long Post.\\\\n Parasympathetic Long Pre Short Post. Horner s Triad Ptosis Muller s muscle paralysis Miosis Dilator pupillae paralysis Anhidrosis Sudomotor loss . Autonomic Dysreflexia Triad In SCI > T6 Hypertension Sympathetic surge + Bradycardia Baroreceptor reflex + Headache . Hirschsprung s Mechanism Failed migration of Neural Crest Cells . Result Aganglionic segment that cannot relax constricted causing upstream dilation megacolon . Sympathetic Exceptions Most sympathetic postganglionics release NE. Exceptions Sweat glands ACh and Adrenal Medulla Epi/NE directly . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l11", "title": "Lecture 11: Hypothalamus", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l11 Lecture 11 Hypothalamus\\n\\n Lecturer V. Grubisic M.D. Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 11\\n\\n 1. Hypothalamus Overview Slide 6-8 \\n\\nThe Hypothalamus is the master control center for Homeostasis regulating the Autonomic Nervous System Endocrine System and Limbic System.\\nMnemonic for Functions TAN HATS \\n T hirst and Water Balance\\n A denohypophysis Control\\n N eurohypophysis Control\\n H unger\\n A utonomic Regulation\\n T emperature Regulation\\n S exual urges / S leep\\n\\n 2. Major Nuclei and Functions Slide 9-16 \\n\\n Paraventricular Nucleus PVN Produces Oxytocin ADH TRH CRH. Projects to autonomic centers.\\n Supraoptic Nucleus SON Produces mainly ADH Vasopressin and Oxytocin.\\n Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN Master Circadian Clock . Regulated by light input from retina.\\n Anterior Hypothalamus Cooling Parasympathetic . Lesion → Hyperthermia. \\ A/C = Anterior Cooling\\ .\\n Posterior Hypothalamus Heating Sympathetic . Lesion → Hypothermia Poikilothermia .\\n Mammillary Bodies Episodic Memory. Input from Hippocampus Fornix .\\n\\n correlate\\n Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome \\n Cause Thiamine Vitamin B1 deficiency often due to chronic alcoholism.\\n Pathology Degeneration of the Mammillary Bodies and Medial Dorsal Thalamus.\\n Triad Confusion Ataxia Ophthalmoplegia Wernicke s Encephalopathy .\\n Sequelae Korsakoff s Psychosis Anterograde/Retrograde Amnesia + Confabulation if untreated.\\n \\n\\n 3. Feeding Regulation Slide 19-22 \\n\\n Lateral Hypothalamus Hunger Center . Lesion → Anorexia \\ Lateral makes you Lean\\ .\\n Ventromedial Nucleus Satiety Center . Lesion → Hyperphagia and Obesity \\ Ventromedial makes you Very Massive\\ .\\n Hormones \\n Leptin From adipose tissue. Signals satiety inhibits Lateral stimulates Ventromedial .\\n Ghrelin From stomach. Signals hunger.\\n\\n 4. Water Balance Disorders Slide 12 \\n\\n Diabetes Insipidus DI \\n Neurogenic Lack of ADH secretion Hypothalamic/Posterior Pituitary damage .\\n Sxs Polyuria Polydipsia Dilute Urine.\\n SIADH Syndrome of Inappropriate ADH \\n Cause Ectopic production Small Cell Lung Cancer CNS disorders Drugs.\\n Sxs Water retention Hyponatremia Cerebral Edema.\\n\\n highyield\\n Mnemonic Feeding Centers \\n L ateral = L ean Hunger Center; lesion causes starvation .\\n V entromedial = V ery M assive Satiety Center; lesion causes obesity .\\n Lecture 11 Hypothalamus Neuroscience questions A chronic alcoholic presents with confusion ataxia and nystagmus. MRI reveals atrophy of the Mammillary Bodies. What is the most likely diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Wernicke s Encephalopathy Huntington s Disease Vascular Dementia 2 This is the classic presentation of Wernicke s Encephalopathy due to Thiamine deficiency which damages the mammillary bodies. A patient develops hyperphagia and significant obesity after a brain tumor resection. Which hypothalamic nucleus was most likely damaged? Lateral Hypothalamus Supraoptic Nucleus Ventromedial Nucleus Suprachiasmatic Nucleus Anterior Hypothalamus 2 The Ventromedial Nucleus is the Satiety Center. Damage leads to unchecked hunger and obesity Very Massive . Which hypothalamic nucleus is responsible for circadian rhythms? Arcuate Nucleus Suprachiasmatic Nucleus Paraventricular Nucleus Posterior Hypothalamus Mammillary Nucleus 1 The Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN regulates sleep-wake cycles and circadian rhythms. Damage to the Anterior Hypothalamus would result in Hypothermia Hyperthermia Diabetes Insipidus Amnesia Insomnia 1 The Anterior Hypothalamus regulates Cooling . Damage prevents cooling resulting in Hyperthermia . In neurogenic Diabetes Insipidus there is a lack of which hormone? Oxytocin Prolactin ADH Vasopressin TSH GH 2 Neurogenic Diabetes Insipidus causes polyuria due to a failure to secrete ADH Vasopressin from the posterior pituitary/hypothalamus. Function of Lateral Hypothalamus Hunger Feeding Center Physiology Function of Ventromedial Hypothalamus Satiety Physiology Function of Anterior Hypothalamus Cooling Parasympathetic Physiology Function of Posterior Hypothalamus Heating Sympathetic/Shivering Physiology Function of Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN Circadian Rhythms Physiology Function of Mammillary Bodies Episodic Memory Papez Circuit Physiology Lesion of Mammillary Bodies causes Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome Amnesia/Confabulation Pathology Lesion of Lateral Hypothalamus causes Anorexia Failure to eat Pathology Lesion of Ventromedial Hypothalamus causes Hyperphagia Obesity Pathology Major hormones of Posterior Pituitary ADH Vasopressin and Oxytocin Physiology BandB Endermo > Hypothalamus FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Hypothalamus Pathoma CNS Hypothalamic Feeding Centers Lateral Hypothalamus LH Hunger Center. Lesion = Starvation Lateral makes you Lean .\\\\n Ventromedial Nucleus VMN Satiety Center. Lesion = Obesity VMN makes you Very Massive . Temperature Regulation Anterior Hypothalamus Cooling Parasympathetic . Lesion = Hyperthermia.\\\\n Posterior Hypothalamus Heating Sympathetic . Lesion = Hypothermia. Wernicke-Korsakoff Anatomy Caused by Thiamine B1 deficiency leading to necrosis of the Mammillary Bodies . Circadian Rhythm Controlled by the Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN which receives direct input from the retina. ADH Production ADH is synthesized in the Supraoptic and Paraventricular nuclei and released from the Posterior Pituitary . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l12", "title": "Lecture 12: Anatomy and Physiology of the Thalamus", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l12 Lecture 12 Anatomy and Physiology of the Thalamus\\n\\n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 12\\n\\n 1. Thalamic Organization Slide 12-18 \\n\\nThe Thalamus is the \\ Gateway to the Cortex\\ . Most sensory information except olfaction must synapse here before reaching the cortex.\\n\\n Relay Nuclei Specific \\n VPL Ventral Posterolateral \\n Input DCML and Spinothalamic Tract Body .\\n Output Somatosensory Cortex Postcentral Gyrus .\\n Function Vibration Proprioception Pain Temp from Body .\\n VPM Ventral Posteromedial \\n Input Trigeminal Face and Gustatory Solitary .\\n Output Somatosensory Cortex.\\n Function Sensation from Face + Taste .\\n LGN Lateral Geniculate Nucleus \\n Input Optic Tract.\\n Output Visual Cortex V1 .\\n Function Vision \\ L ight\\ .\\n MGN Medial Geniculate Nucleus \\n Input Inferior Colliculus.\\n Output Auditory Cortex.\\n Function Hearing \\ M usic\\ .\\n VA/VL Ventral Anterior/Lateral \\n Input Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum.\\n Output Motor Cortex.\\n Function Motor planning and coordination.\\n Anterior Nucleus \\n Input Mammillothalamic Tract.\\n Output Cingulate Gyrus.\\n Function Memory and Emotion Papez Circuit .\\n\\n 2. Thalamocortical States Slide 23-26 \\n Tonic Mode Linear transmission. High fidelity. Awake state depolarized by ARAS NE ACh 5-HT Histamine .\\n Burst Mode Oscillatory. Low fidelity. Sleep state hyperpolarized . Blocks sensory input preventing waking. Absence Seizures occur in this mode.\\n\\n 3. Internal Capsule Slide 35-39 \\nWhite matter highway carrying fibers to/from cortex.\\n Anterior Limb Thalamocortical fibers Anterior/DM .\\n Genu Corticobulbar Tract Motor to face/brainstem .\\n Posterior Limb \\n Corticospinal Tract Motor to body .\\n Somatosensory Radiations VPL/VPM to Cortex .\\n Visual/Auditory Radiations Retrolenticular/Sublenticular .\\n Blood Supply Primarily MCA Lenticulostriate and Anterior Choroidal . Thalamus is PCA .\\n\\n 4. Clinical Syndromes Slide 29-32 \\n\\n Dejerine-Roussy Syndrome Thalamic Pain Syndrome \\n Cause Stroke PCA damaging VPL/VPM.\\n Presentation Contralateral hemianesthesia followed by agonizing burning pain Allodynia induced by touch or even emotion.\\n\\n Artery of Percheron Infarct\\n Variant Single artery arising from PCA supplying Bilateral paramedian thalamus + midbrain.\\n Stroke Bilateral thalamic infarct.\\n Presentation Coma RAS damage Memory loss Vertical gaze palsy. Lecture 12 Anatomy and Physiology of the Thalamus Neuroscience questions A patient suffers a stroke and develops severe burning pain on the right side of their body that is exacerbated by light touch. Which nucleus was most likely infarcted? Right VPL Left VPL Right VPM Left MGN Left Anterior Nucleus 1 Thalamic Pain Syndrome Dejerine-Roussy is contralateral. A Left VPL infarct affects the Right side of the body. VPL handles body sensation. Visual information from the optic tract synapses in which thalamic nucleus? Medial Geniculate Nucleus Lateral Geniculate Nucleus Ventral Posterolateral Nucleus Pulvinar Ventral Anterior Nucleus 1 LGN = L ight Vision . MGN = Music Hearing . Which limb of the internal capsule contains the corticospinal tract? Anterior Limb Genu Posterior Limb Retrolenticular Part Sublenticular Part 2 The Posterior Limb of the Internal Capsule carries the Corticospinal tract Motor to body and Somatosensory radiations. Which thalamic nucleus is part of the Papez circuit involved in memory? Ventral Lateral Anterior Nucleus Pulvinar Lateral Posterior Centromedian 1 The Anterior Nucleus receives input from the Mammillary Bodies and projects to the Cingulate Gyrus forming part of the Papez circuit for memory/emotion. The Burst Mode of thalamocortical firing is associated with Focused attention REM Sleep Slow Wave Sleep Panic attacks Active learning 2 Burst Mode is an oscillatory state that disconnects the cortex from sensory input characteristic of Slow Wave Sleep and Absence Seizures . Input to VPL Nucleus Spinothalamic & DCML Body Sensation Neuroanatomy Input to VPM Nucleus Trigeminal & Solitary Tract Face & Taste Neuroanatomy Input to LGN Optic Tract Vision Neuroanatomy Input to MGN Inferior Colliculus Hearing Neuroanatomy Input to VA/VL Nuclei Basal Ganglia & Cerebellum Motor Neuroanatomy Contents of Internal Capsule Genu Corticobulbar Tract Neuroanatomy Contents of Internal Capsule Posterior Limb Corticospinal Tract & Sensory Radiations Neuroanatomy Artery supplying the Thalamus Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Neuroanatomy Cause of Thalamic Pain Syndrome Stroke in VPL/VPM Dejerine-Roussy Pathology Thalamic mode during Deep Sleep Burst Mode Physiology BandB Neurology > Brainstem > Thalamus FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Thalamus Pathoma CNS VPM vs VPL Mnemonic VPM = M akeup on the Face Face sensation + Taste .\\\\n VPL = L egs/Body Body sensation . Geniculate Nuclei Mnemonic L GN = L ight Visual .\\\\n M GN = M usic Auditory . Dejerine-Roussy Syndrome Central Post-Stroke Pain. Thalamic lesion causes initial numbness followed by hypersensitivity and severe burning pain on the contralateral side. Internal Capsule Genu The Genu knee contains the Corticobulbar tract Face motor . A lesion here affects CN motor nuclei e.g. UMN facial droop . Thalamic Blood Supply Principally the Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA . The Artery of Percheron is a rare variant supplying bilateral thalami. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l13", "title": "Lecture 13: Clinical Decision Making", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l13 Lecture 13 Clinical Decision Making\\n\\n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O.\\n Session Lecture 13\\n\\n 1. Dual Process Theory Slide 4-5 \\n\\nClinical reasoning involves two cognitive systems \\n System 1 Intuitive Fast automatic reflexive pattern recognition. Used by experts for common cases. Prone to Heuristic Biases .\\n System 2 Hypothetico-Deductive Slow analytical deliberate. Used for complex or unclear cases. Less prone to bias but resource-intensive.\\n\\n 2. Cognitive Biases Heuristics Slide 5 26-28 \\n\\n Anchoring Locking onto salient features in the patient s initial presentation too early and failing to adjust.\\n Availability Bias Judging a diagnosis as more likely because it is easily recalled e.g. you just saw 3 cases of Appendicitis so the next belly pain must be Appendicitis .\\n Confirmation Bias Seeking evidence to support your hypothesis while ignoring evidence that refutes it.\\n Premature Closure Stopping the diagnostic process before verifying the diagnosis or considering other possibilities. \\ When the diagnosis is made the thinking stops.\\ \\n\\n 3. The Diagnostic Framework\\n\\n VINDICATE Mnemonic Slide 15 \\nA checklist to generate a broad Differential Diagnosis DDx .\\n V ascular\\n I nfectious\\n N eoplastic\\n D rug / Degenerative\\n I nflammatory / Idiopathic\\n C ongenital\\n A utoimmune / Allergic\\n T rauma / Toxic\\n E ndocrine / Metabolic\\n\\n Illness Scripts Slide 18 22 \\nAn organized mental summary of a disease including \\n Epidemiology/Pathophysiology Who gets it? Why?\\n Time Course Acute Subacute Chronic?\\n Salient Symptoms/Signs Key features Prototypical presentation .\\n Diagnostics/Treatment Labs Imaging Meds.\\n\\n 4. Problem Representation Slide 12 35 \\nA one-sentence summary of the case used to communicate and reason.\\n Structure Patient Age/Sex/Risk Factors presents with Time Course Chief Complaint characterized by Key Features .\\n Example \\ A 3yo male with no PMH presents with 2 days of fever and ear pulling found to have a bulging erythematous TM.\\ \\n\\n correlate\\n Case Study Acute Otitis Media AOM \\n Presentation Fever Ear Pain Otalgia Bulging Tympanic Membrane TM Loss of benchmarks.\\n Pathogens Strep pneumoniae Haemophilus influenzae Moraxella catarrhalis .\\n DDx \\n Serous Otitis Media Fluid effusion but no active infection/bulging.\\n Otitis Externa Swimmer s ear. Canal inflammation. Pain with tragal motion. flesh-colored canal makes this unlikely.\\n Lecture 13 Clinical Decision Making Neuroscience questions A resident diagnoses a patient with Influenza because everyone coming into the ER today has the flu despite the patient having neck stiffness and a rash. Which cognitive bias is at play? Anchoring Availability Bias Confirmation Bias Premature Closure Sunk Cost Fallacy 1 Availability Bias is judging frequency or probability by how easily examples come to mind e.g. a recent run of flu cases . When generating a differential diagnosis for a 60-year-old male with back pain you use the mnemonic VINDICATE. The N stands for Neurologic Nutritional Neoplastic Nociceptive Neonatal 2 N in VINDICATE stands for Neoplastic Cancer a critical category to consider in older adults with new back pain. Which of the following best describes System 2 thinking? Fast and reflexive Pattern recognition Slow and analytical Prone to emotional influence Automatic association 2 System 2 is the slow deliberate analytical mode of thinking used for complex problems. A 3-year-old presents with ear pain. Exam reveals a bulging erythematous tympanic membrane with loss of bony landmarks. The most likely diagnosis is Otitis Externa Serous Otitis Media Mastoiditis Acute Otitis Media Suppurative Foreign Body 3 Bulging and erythema of the TM are hallmark signs of Acute Suppurative Otitis Media. You suspect a patient has a Pulmonary Embolism. You order a D-dimer which is negative but you dismiss the result as a lab error because you are so sure of your diagnosis. This is an example of Confirmation Bias Anchoring Availability Bias Visceral Bias Gambler s Fallacy 1 Confirmation Bias involves interpreting evidence to support your existing beliefs the PE diagnosis while dismissing contradictory evidence the negative D-dimer . System 1 Thinking Fast Intuitive Automatic Clinical Reasoning System 2 Thinking Slow Analytical Deliberate Clinical Reasoning Availability Bias Diagnosing based on what is readily recalled recent cases Clinical Reasoning Anchoring Bias Sticking to initial impressions despite new data Clinical Reasoning Premature Closure Stopping the diagnostic process too early Clinical Reasoning VINDICATE V Vascular Clinical Reasoning VINDICATE I first Infectious Clinical Reasoning VINDICATE N Neoplastic Clinical Reasoning Illness Script Components Pathophys Epidemiology Time Course Symptoms Diagnostics Treatment Clinical Reasoning Key sign of Acute Otitis Media Bulging Tympanic Membrane Clinical Reasoning BandB Infectious Disease > HEENT FirstAid Public Health > Epidemiology > Bias Pathoma General Principles System 1 vs System 2 System 1 Fast Intuitive Gut feeling . Prone to bias.\\\\n System 2 Slow Analytical Math problem . More accurate. VINDICATE Mnemonic Vascular Infectious Neoplastic Drug/Degenerative Inflammatory/Idiopathic Congenital Autoimmune Trauma/Toxic Endocrine/Metabolic. Confirmation Bias The tendency to look only for evidence that confirms your hunch and ignore evidence that contradicts it. Availability Bias Diagnosing something because you recently saw it or it s top of mind e.g. flu during flu season . Premature Closure The most common fatal error Accepting a diagnosis before it is fully verified and stopping the search for alternatives. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l15", "title": "Lecture 15: General Sensory Systems", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l15 Lecture 15 General Sensory Systems\\n\\n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 15\\n\\n 1. Sensory Receptors & Fibers Slide 5-13 \\n\\n Fiber Types\\n A-beta A\\u03b2 Large Myelinated. Fast 30-70 m/s . Carries Touch Vibration Proprioception .\\n A-delta A\\u03b4 Small Myelinated. Medium speed. Carries Sharp Pain First Pain Cold .\\n C fibers Unmyelinated. Slow 0.5-2 m/s . Carries Dull/Burning Pain Second Pain Warmth Itch .\\n\\n Mechanoreceptors Touch \\n Meissner s Corpuscle Fast adapting Small field. Light Touch/Grip . Glabrous skin.\\n Merkel Disc Slow adapting Small field. Static Pressure/Texture Braille .\\n Pacinian Corpuscle Fast adapting Large field. Vibration .\\n Ruffini Ending Slow adapting Large field. Skin Stretch Grip shape .\\n\\n 2. Ascending Pathways Slide 26-30 \\n\\n Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML \\nCarries Fine Touch Vibration Proprioception from the body.\\n1. 1st Order DRG. Ascend in Dorsal Columns Gracilis=Lower Cuneatus=Upper .\\n2. 2nd Order Nucleus Gracilis/Cuneatus Medulla . Decussate as Internal Arcuate Fibers to form Medial Lemniscus .\\n3. 3rd Order VPL Nucleus Thalamus → Primary Somatosensory Cortex S1 .\\n\\n Spinothalamic Tract Anterolateral System \\nCarries Pain Temperature Crude Touch .\\n1. 1st Order DRG. Synapse in Dorsal Horn Substantia Gelatinosa .\\n2. 2nd Order Dorsal Horn. Decussate immediately in Anterior White Commissure. Ascend in anterolateral funiculus.\\n3. 3rd Order VPL Nucleus Thalamus → S1 Cortex.\\n\\n 3. Trigeminal System Face Slide 31-33 \\n Touch/Proprioception Trigeminal Ganglion → Principal Sensory Nucleus Pons → Decussate → VPM.\\n Pain/Temp Trigeminal Ganglion → Descend to Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Medulla → Decussate → VPM.\\n Proprioception Jaw Mesencephalic Nucleus Midbrain - UNIQUE Cell bodies inside CNS .\\n\\n 4. Clinical Correlates Slide 38-43 \\n\\n correlate\\n Brown-S\\u00e9quard Syndrome \\n Pathology Hemisection of the spinal cord e.g. stabbing/trauma .\\n Presentation \\n Ipsilateral Loss of Motor Corticospinal and Touch/Vibration/Proprioception DCML below lesion.\\n Contralateral Loss of Pain/Temperature Spinothalamic 1-2 segments below lesion due to Lissanuer s tract ascent before crossing .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Phantom Limb Pain \\n Pathology Cortical reorganization after amputation.\\n Mechanism The cortical area for the amputated limb e.g. arm loses input and is invaded by adjacent areas e.g. Face . Touching the face is perceived as touching the missing arm.\\n Nature Often burning/painful resistant to meds.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Thalamic Lesion Dejerine-Roussy \\n Lesion VPL/VPM.\\n Presentation Contralateral Hemianesthesia loss of all sensation . Later develops into severe burning pain Thalamic Pain Syndrome .\\n \\n\\n 5. Pain Modulation Slide 18-20 \\n Gate Control Theory A\\u03b2 fibers rubbing activate inhibitory interneurons in the dorsal horn blocking C fiber pain transmission.\\n Descending Pathway PAG → RVM → Dorsal Horn Inhibits pain via Serotonin/NE . Lecture 15 General Sensory Systems Neuroscience questions A patient cannot feel temperature or sharp pain in their right leg but vibration sense is intact. Where is the lesion most likely located? Right Fasciculus Gracilis Left Spinothalamic Tract Right Spinothalamic Tract Left Medial Lemniscus Right Dorsal Horn 1 Pain/Temp from the Right leg travels to the left side of the spinal cord immediately. A lesion in the Left Spinothalamic Tract causes loss of pain/temp on the Right side. Which receptor is responsible for sensing vibration? Merkel Disc Meissner Corpuscle Pacinian Corpuscle Ruffini Ending Free Nerve Ending 2 Pacinian Corpuscles are rapidly adapting and specialized for sensing Vibration . A hemisection of the spinal cord at T10 on the left side Brown-S\\u00e9quard would result in Loss of pain/temp on the left leg Loss of vibration/proprioception on the right leg Loss of motor function on the right leg Loss of pain/temp on the right leg Bilateral loss of all sensation 3 Hemisection on the Left causes Contralateral Right loss of Pain/Temp STT crosses immediately and Ipsilateral Left loss of Touch/Motor. The cell bodies of the primary afferent neurons for the face Trigeminal are located in the Dorsal Root Ganglion Trigeminal Ganglion Principal Sensory Nucleus Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus VPM Nucleus 1 Primary sensory neurons for the face are in the Trigeminal Gasserian Ganglion . Which fibers carry slow burning pain? A-alpha A-beta A-delta B fibers C fibers 4 C fibers are unmyelinated and slow conducting responsible for the delayed dull burning second pain . Pathway for Fine Touch and Proprioception Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML Neuroanatomy Pathway for Pain and Temperature Spinothalamic Tract Anterolateral System Neuroanatomy Where does DCML decussate? Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers Neuroanatomy Where does Spinothalamic Tract decussate? Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure Neuroanatomy Function of Pacinian Corpuscle Vibration Physiology Function of A-delta fibers Fast Sharp Pain Cold Physiology Function of C fibers Slow Dull Pain Warmth/Itch Physiology Brown-S\\u00e9quard Contralateral Deficit Pain and Temperature STT Pathology Brown-S\\u00e9quard Ipsilateral Deficit Motor CS & Vibration/Proprioception DCML Pathology Thalamic Nucleus for Body Sensation VPL Neuroanatomy BandB Neurology > Spinal Cord > Spinal Cord Tracts FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Spinal Cord Pathoma CNS DCML vs STT Decussation DCML crosses in the Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers .\\\\n Spinothalamic crosses in the Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure . VPL vs VPM VPL = L egs/Body Body sensation .\\\\n VPM = M akeup/Face Face sensation . Fiber Speed Rule A-beta Touch > A-delta Fast Pain > C Slow Pain . Myelination and diameter determine speed. Brown-S\\u00e9quard Deficits Ipsilateral Motor & Touch. Contralateral Pain & Temp. Phantom Limb Mechanism Maladaptive Cortical Reorganization Neuroplasticity where face area invades hand area. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l16", "title": "Lecture 16: Visual System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l16 Lecture 16 Visual System\\n\\n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 16\\n\\n 1. Eye Functional Anatomy Slide 4-10 \\n\\nThe eye focuses light onto the retina to transduce it into neural signals.\\n Refraction Most focusing power comes from the Cornea air-water interface not the lens.\\n Accommodation The Lens changes shape to focus near objects.\\n Near Vision Reflex Ciliary muscle Contracts → Zonules Relax → Lens becomes Rounder . + Pupil constriction + Convergence .\\n Far Vision Ciliary muscle Relaxes → Zonules Tighten → Lens flattens.\\n Aqueous Humor Produced by Ciliary Body. Drains via Canal of Schlemm . Blockage = Glaucoma .\\n\\n Retina Organization\\n Fovea Center of Macula. All Cones . Highest acuity. No blood vessels/layers in light path.\\n Periphery Mostly Rods . High sensitivity low light low acuity.\\n Blind Spot Optic Disc Where optic nerve exits. No photoreceptors.\\n\\n 2. Visual Pathway Slide 26-30 \\n\\n1. Retina Light hits Photoreceptors → Bipolar Cells → Ganglion Cells.\\n2. Optic Nerve CN II Ganglion cell axons.\\n3. Optic Chiasm Nasal fibers temporal field CROSS . Temporal fibers nasal field stay ipsilateral.\\n4. Optic Tract Contains ipsilateral Temporal fibers + contralateral Nasal fibers. Projects to LGN .\\n5. Lateral Geniculate Nucleus LGN Thalamic relay.\\n Magno M layers Motion/Depth.\\n Parvo P layers Color/Form.\\n6. Geniculocalcarine Tract Optic Radiations \\n Meyer s Loop Temporal Lobe Loops around lateral ventricle. Carries Superior Visual Field \\ Pie in the Sky\\ .\\n Dorsal/Parietal Radiations Carries Inferior Visual Field \\ Pie on the Floor\\ .\\n7. Primary Visual Cortex V1 Occipital Lobe. Upper bank Cuneus = Inferior Field. Lower bank Lingual = Superior Field.\\n\\n 3. Visual Field Lesions Slide 26-29 32 \\n Monocular Blindness Lesion of Optic Nerve .\\n Bitemporal Hemianopsia Lesion of Optic Chiasm .\\n Cause Pituitary Adenoma compressing chiasm centrally.\\n Effect Loss of peripheral vision \\ Tunnel Vision\\ .\\n Homonymous Hemianopsia Lesion of Optic Tract or PCA Stroke .\\n Effect Loss of same field in both eyes e.g. Left field perception lost in both eyes .\\n Macular Sparing PCA strokes often spare the fovea supplied by MCA collaterals .\\n Quadrantanopsia \\n Meyer s Loop Temporal \\ Pie in the Sky\\ Contralateral Superior Quadrant .\\n Parietal Radiations \\ Pie on the Floor\\ Contralateral Inferior Quadrant .\\n\\n correlate\\n Papilledema Slide 34 \\n Pathophysiology Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP transmitted through the subarachnoid space surrounding the Optic Nerve.\\n Sign Swelling of the Optic Disc visible on fundoscopy. Blurred margins.\\n Context Brain tumor Meningitis Pseudotumor Cerebri.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Hemispatial Neglect Slide 43-44 \\n Lesion Right Parietal Lobe Non-dominant hemisphere .\\n Presentation Patient ignores the Left side of the world/body.\\n Starts eating from only right side of plate.\\n Shaves only right face.\\n Draws only right half of clock.\\n Note This is an Attention deficit not a visual one.\\n \\n\\n mnemonic\\n Visual Field Defects \\n Tem poral Loop = Pie in the Sky Temporal lobe lesion → Superior field defect .\\n Parietal Path = Pie on the Floor Parietal lobe lesion → Inferior field defect .\\n Lecture 16 Visual System Neuroscience questions A patient presents with tunnel vision bumping into objects on their periphery. MRI reveals a mass compressing the optic chiasm. What is the most likely diagnosis? Right Optic Nerve Compression Pituitary Adenoma Temporal Lobe Tumor Parietal Lobe Tumor Occipital Lobe Stroke 1 A Pituitary Adenoma compresses the specialized crossing fibers in the Optic Chiasm causing Bitemporal Hemianopsia Tunnel Vision . A patient cannot see the upper left quadrant of their visual field Pie in the Sky . Where is the lesion located? Right Temporal Lobe Meyer s Loop Left Temporal Lobe Meyer s Loop Right Parietal Lobe Left Parietal Lobe Right Occipital Cortex 0 A Left upper field defect maps to the Right brain Contralateral . In the Sky Superior implies the inferior fibers Meyer s Loop in Temporal Lobe . So Right Temporal Lobe . Accommodation for near vision involves which action of the ciliary muscle? Relaxation Contraction Hyperpolarization Atrophy No change 1 The ciliary muscle Contracts loosening the zonules allowing the lens to spring into a rounder shape for near focus. Papilledema is a clinical sign indicative of Retinal Detachment Glaucoma Increased Intracranial Pressure Cataracts Macular Degeneration 2 Papilledema swollen optic disc is caused by transmitted Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP . A patient with a Right Parietal Lobe stroke only eats food on the right side of their plate and ignores the left side. This is called Homonymous Hemianopsia Agnosia Hemispatial Neglect Apraxia Prosopagnosia 2 Hemispatial Neglect is an attention deficit classically due to a non-dominant Right Parietal Lobe lesion. Visual Deficit Optic Chiasm Lesion Bitemporal Hemianopsia Neurology Visual Deficit Right Optic Tract Lesion Left Homonymous Hemianopsia Neurology Visual Deficit Meyer s Loop Temporal Lesion Contralateral Superior Quadrantanopia Pie in the Sky Neurology Visual Deficit Parietal Radiation Lesion Contralateral Inferior Quadrantanopia Pie on the Floor Neurology Lesion causing Left Hemispatial Neglect Right Parietal Lobe Neurology Sign of Increased ICP on Eye Exam Papilledema Pathology Function of Ciliary Muscle Contraction Accommodation Near Vision Physiology Area of Retina with highest acuity Fovea Macula Physiology Blood Supply to Visual Cortex Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Neuroanatomy What is spared in PCA stroke visual field defect? Macula Macular Sparing Pathology BandB Neurology > The Eye > Visual Fields FirstAid Neurology > Ophthalmology Pathoma CNS Pituitary Adenoma Deficit Compresses Optic Chiasm → Bitemporal Hemianopsia Tunnel Vision . Meyer s Loop Deficit Temporal Lobe lesion → Superior Homonymous Quadrantanopia Pie in the Sky . Parietal Radiation Deficit Parietal Lobe lesion → Inferior Homonymous Quadrantanopia Pie on the Floor . Macular Sparing Occipital Cortex PCA infarcts often spare the Macula because it has dual blood supply MCA collaterals . Right Parietal Lobe Lesion Causes Left Hemispatial Neglect . The Right parietal lobe attends to both sides while the Left only attends to the Right. Loss of Right = Loss of Left attention. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l17", "title": "Lecture 17: Anatomy and Physiology of the Auditory System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l17 Lecture 17 Anatomy and Physiology of the Auditory System\\n\\n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 17\\n\\n 1. Auditory Anatomy Slide 4-6 10-14 \\n\\n Outer Ear Funnels sound.\\n Middle Ear Ossicles Malleus Incus Stapes amplify sound to transfer it from air tympanic membrane to fluid oval window .\\n Impedance Matching Necessary because fluid differs from air. Stapes pushes on Oval Window.\\n Protective Reflex Stapedius CN VII and Tensor Tympani CN V3 contract to dampen loud sounds.\\n Inner Ear Cochlea \\n Tonotopy Base = High Frequencies Narrow/Stiff . Apex = Low Frequencies Wide/Floppy .\\n Ion Gradients Endolymph Scala Media is high in K+ . Perilymph is low in K+.\\n\\n 2. Mechanotransduction Organ of Corti Slide 15-18 \\nThe Organ of Corti sits on the basilar membrane.\\n Inner Hair Cells IHC The primary sensory receptors 95% of afferents .\\n Outer Hair Cells OHC Cochlear amplifiers. Damage = Tinnitus .\\n Transduction Vibration bends stereocilia → Tip links open K+ channels → K+ influx depolarization → Ca2+ influx → Glutamate release.\\n\\n 3. Auditory Pathway Slide 20-21 \\n\\ E. C. O. L. I. M.\\ mnemonic.\\n1. E ighth Nerve Spiral Ganglion .\\n2. C ochlear Nuclei Medulla .\\n3. S uperior O live Pons - Sound Localization Time/Intensity diffs . First bilateral processing.\\n4. L ateral Lemniscus.\\n5. I nferior Colliculus Midbrain .\\n6. M edial Geniculate Nucleus M GN Thalamus .\\n7. Auditory Cortex Temporal Lobe/Heschl s Gyrus .\\n\\n 4. Hearing Loss Types Slide 27-29 \\n Conductive Middle/Outer ear issue Wax Otitis Media Otosclerosis .\\n Rinne Bone > Air.\\n Weber Localizes to Affected ear.\\n Sensorineural Inner ear/Nerve issue Presbycusis Noise Drugs .\\n Rinne Air > Bone Normal pattern but reduced .\\n Weber Localizes to Unaffected ear.\\n\\n 5. Clinical Correlates Slide 33-37 \\n\\n correlate\\n Acoustic Neuroma Vestibular Schwannoma \\n Pathology Benign tumor of Schwann cells on CN VIII Vestibulocochlear .\\n CPA Angle Compression of Cerebellopontine Angle structures.\\n Symptoms Unilateral Hearing Loss Tinnitus Vertigo . Can compress CN VII Facial palsy if large.\\n Diagnosis MRI.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Usher Syndrome \\n Genetics Autosomal Recessive.\\n Pathology Mutation in stereocilia tip-link proteins e.g. Cadherin 23 .\\n Presentation Congenital Deafness + Retinitis Pigmentosa Progressive Blindness . Major cause of deaf-blindness.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n EAST Syndrome \\n Acronym E pilepsy A taxia S ensorineural deafness T ubulopathy.\\n Pathology Mutation in KCNJ10 Kir4.1 K+ channel .\\n Mechanism Failure to maintain High K+ in Endolymph prevents hair cell depolarization.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Presbycusis \\n Definition Age-related sensorineural hearing loss.\\n Pattern Loss of High Frequencies first Base of cochlea wears out .\\n Mechanism Hair cell loss.\\n Lecture 17 Anatomy and Physiology of the Auditory System Neuroscience questions A 65-year-old male complains of difficulty hearing women s voices and birds chirping high frequencies in noisy environments. Otoscopy is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis? Otosclerosis Presbycusis Meniere s Disease Cholesteatoma Otitis Media 1 Presbycusis is age-related sensorineural hearing loss affecting High Frequencies first due to hair cell loss at the cochlear base. A patient presents with unilateral hearing loss tinnitus and facial weakness. MRI reveals a mass at the Cerebellopontine Angle. What is the likely diagnosis? Pituitary Adenoma Glioblastoma Multiforme Vestibular Schwannoma Acoustic Neuroma Meningioma Craniopharyngioma 2 Vestibular Schwannoma arises on CN VIII causing hearing loss/tinnitus and can compress the adjacent CN VII Facial nerve at the CPA. In the auditory pathway where does sound localization computing time/intensity differences first occur? Cochlear Nucleus Superior Olivary Complex Inferior Colliculus Medial Geniculate Nucleus Auditory Cortex 1 The Superior Olivary Complex is the first site receiving Bilateral input allowing it to compare timing and intensity data for localization. EAST Syndrome is caused by a mutation affecting the transport of which ion in the inner ear? Sodium Calcium Chloride Potassium Magnesium 3 EAST Syndrome is a K+ channelopathy Kir4.1 preventing the maintenance of high Potassium in the endolymph. During a Weber test the sound lateralizes to the patient s Left ear. Bone conduction is greater than Air conduction in the Left ear. This suggests Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss Left Sensorineural Hearing Loss Right Conductive Hearing Loss Left Conductive Hearing Loss Normal Hearing 3 Weber lateralizing to the affected ear + Rinne showing Bone > Air is the classic pattern for Conductive Hearing Loss in that ear Left . Auditory Pathway Mnemonic E. COLIM Eighth nerve Cochlear nuc Olive Lat Lemniscus Inf Colliculus MGN Neuroanatomy Frequency map of Cochlea Base = High. Apex = Low. Physiology Ion concentrated in Endolymph Potassium K+ Physiology Function of Superior Olivary Complex Sound Localization Neuroanatomy Muscle innervated by CN VII in ear Stapedius Anatomy Muscle innervated by CN V3 in ear Tensor Tympani Anatomy Usher Syndrome Presentation Deafness + Blindness Retinitis Pigmentosa Pathology Acoustic Neuroma Symptoms Unilateral Hearing Loss Tinnitus Vertigo Pathology Weber Test Conductive Loss Lateralizes to the Bad ear Clinical Skills Rinne Test Sensorineural Loss Air > Bone Normal pattern Clinical Skills BandB Neurology > The Ear > Hearing FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Auditory Pathoma Head & Neck Pathway Mnemonic E. COLIM E ighth Nerve → C ochlear Nucleus → O live Superior → L ateral Lemniscus → I nferior Colliculus → M GN. Cochlear Tonotopy Base = High Frequency Stiff/Narrow .\\\\n Apex = Low Frequency Floppy/Wide . Rinne & Weber Rules Conductive Weber to Bad Ear. Rinne Bone > Air.\\\\n Sensorineural Weber to Good Ear. Rinne Air > Bone. Acoustic Reflex Mediated by CN VII Stapedius and CN V3 Tensor Tympani to dampen loud sounds. EAST Syndrome Mechanism K+ Channel mutation Kir4.1 . No K+ gradient in Endolymph = No Hearing. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l18", "title": "Lecture 18: Anatomy & Physiology of the Vestibular System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l18 Lecture 18 Anatomy & Physiology of the Vestibular System\\n\\n Lecturer R. Ramos Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 18\\n\\n 1. Peripheral Anatomy Slide 4-15 \\nThe Vestibular Labyrinth detects head motion via hair cells mechanotransduction .\\n Otolith Organs Utricle & Saccule \\n Function Detect Linear Acceleration e.g. gravity elevator car acceleration .\\n Mechanism Hair cells embedded in Macula covered by Otoconia crystals . Gravity pulls crystals → bends hairs.\\n Mnemonic U tricle = U pright Horizontal plane . S accule = S ideways Vertical plane .\\n Semicircular Canals Anterior Posterior Horizontal \\n Function Detect Angular Rotational Acceleration head turning .\\n Mechanism Crista Ampullaris covered by Cupula gelatin .\\n Fluid Dynamics When head turns Left Endolymph lags moves Right → bends Cupula.\\n\\n 2. Vestibular Pathways & Reflexes Slide 16-20 \\n Vestibulo-Ocular Reflex VOR Stabilizes gaze during head movement.\\n Path Head Left → Left SCC active → Vestibular Nuclei → Right CN VI Abd & Left CN III MR → Eyes move Right .\\n Vestibulo-Spinal Tracts \\n Lateral Extensor muscles Anti-gravity/Balance .\\n Medial Neck muscles Head stabilization .\\n\\n 3. Peripheral Vertigo Disorders Slide 26-30 \\n\\n correlate\\n Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV \\n Mechanism Otoconia crystals dislodge from Utricle and float into a Semicircular Canal usually Posterior .\\n Presentation Brief <1 min episodes of severe vertigo triggered by Head Position changes rolling over in bed .\\n Diagnosis Dix-Hallpike Maneuver elicits nystagmus.\\n Treatment Epley Maneuver repositions crystals .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Meniere s Disease \\n Mechanism Endolymphatic Hydrops Excess fluid pressure .\\n Presentation Triad of Episodic Vertigo mins-hours + Tinnitus + Sensorineural Hearing Loss .\\n Treatment Low salt diet Diuretics.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Labyrinthitis \\n Mechanism Inflammation/Infection of the inner ear.\\n Presentation Continuous severe vertigo + Hearing Loss + Nausea. Often follows a viral infection.\\n \\n\\n 4. Central Vertigo Disorders Slide 31-34 \\n\\n correlate\\n Wallenberg Syndrome Lateral Medullary Syndrome \\n Pathology Infarct of PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery .\\n Signs \\n Vestibular Nuclei Vertigo Nystagmus.\\n Nucleus Ambiguus dysphagia hoarseness.\\n Spinothalamic Tract Contralateral loss of Pain/Temp Body .\\n Spinal Trigeminal Ipsilateral loss of Pain/Temp Face .\\n Sympathetic Ipsilateral Horner s Syndrome Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis .\\n \\n\\n mnemonic\\n Peripheral vs Central Vertigo \\n Peripheral Severe vertigo Hearing Loss common Fatigable nystagmus. BPPV Meniere s .\\n Central Mild vertigo No Hearing Loss usually Non-fatigable nystagmus CNS signs Ataxia Dysphagia . Stroke MS .\\n Lecture 18 Anatomy & Physiology of the Vestibular System Neuroscience questions A patient presents with episodes of room-spinning vertigo lasting 30 seconds triggered only when rolling over in bed. Hearing is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis? Meniere s Disease Vestibular Neuritis Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV Acoustic Neuroma Wallenberg Syndrome 2 BPPV is characterized by brief <1 min position-dependent vertigo with normal hearing caused by dislodged otoconia. Which structure detects linear acceleration and head tilt relative to gravity? Crista Ampullaris Semicircular Canals Otolith Organs Utricle/Saccule Cochlea Organ of Corti 2 The Otolith Organs Utricle and Saccule contain the macula and otoconia specifically designing them to detect Linear Acceleration and gravity. A patient presents with vertigo dysphagia hoarseness ipsilateral facial numbness and contralateral body numbness. Examination reveals an ipsilateral Horner s syndrome. Which artery is occluded? Anterior Spinal Artery Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery PICA Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery AICA Middle Cerebral Artery MCA 2 This is Wallenberg Lateral Medullary Syndrome caused by occlusion of the PICA . The constellation of Vertigo Vestibular Dysphagia Ambiguus and Crossed Sensory loss is classic. What is the primary mechanism of Meniere s Disease? Otoconia dislodgement Endolymphatic Hydrops Excess fluid Demyelination Bacterial Infection Tumor Compression 1 Meniere s Disease is caused by Endolymphatic Hydrops swelling/excess pressure of the endolymphatic compartment . The Vestibulo-Ocular Reflex VOR allows for Hearing high frequencies Maintaining upright posture Stabilizing gaze during head movement Localizing sound in space Detecting gravity 2 The VOR generates eye movements equal and opposite to head movements to stabilize gaze on a fixed target. Function of Semicircular Canals Detect Angular Rotational Acceleration Physiology Function of Otolith Organs Utricle/Saccule Detect Linear Acceleration & Gravity Physiology Diagnosis Brief positional vertigo no hearing loss BPPV Pathology Diagnosis Vertigo + Tinnitus + Hearing Loss Meniere s Disease Pathology Maneuver to treat BPPV Epley Maneuver Clinical Skills Artery involved in Lateral Medullary Syndrome PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Pathology Key signs of Wallenberg Syndrome Dysphagia Vertigo Horner s Crossed Pain/Temp loss Pathology Where are Otoconia normally located? Utricle and Saccule Macula Anatomy Symptoms of Acoustic Neuroma Unilateral Hearing Loss Tinnitus Vertigo CPA angle Pathology Fluid with high Potassium in Inner Ear Endolymph Physiology BandB Neurology > The Ear > Vestibular System FirstAid Neurology > Ophthalmology Pathoma CNS Otoliths vs Canals Otoliths Utricle/Saccule = Linear/Gravity.\\\\n Canals SCC = Angular/Rotation. BPPV Pathophysiology Dislodged Otoconia Otoliths floating in the Semicircular Canals. Meniere s Triad 1. Vertigo\\\\n2. Tinnitus\\\\n3. Hearing Loss Fluctuating . Wallenberg Syndrome Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery . Causes Lateral Medullary Syndrome. Dix-Hallpike Diagnostic maneuver for BPPV . Induces nystagmus if positive. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l19", "title": "Lecture 19: Pharmacology of Direct-Acting Cholinomimetics", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l19 Lecture 19 Pharmacology of Direct-Acting Cholinomimetics\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 19\\n\\n 1. Cholinergic Receptors Slide 9-11 \\n Muscarinic GPCRs Located on effector organs Heart Smooth Muscle Glands .\\n M1 M3 M5 Gq Increase Ca2+ Contraction/Secretion .\\n M2 M4 Gi Decrease cAMP Inhibition/Bradycardia .\\n Nicotinic Ion Channels Located at Ganglia Nn and Neuromuscular Junction Nm .\\n Ligand-gated Na+/K+ channels.\\n\\n 2. Muscarinic Agonists Slide 30-33 \\n\\ Direct-Acting\\ means they bind directly to the receptor unlike AChE inhibitors .\\n\\n Choline Esters Quaternary Amines \\n Poorly absorbed do not cross BBB. \\n Acetylcholine ACh Rapidly hydrolyzed. Used only for Ophthalmic Surgery Miosis .\\n Methacholine \\n use Bronchoprovocation Test to diagnose Asthma. Induces bronchospasm in hyperreactive airways.\\n Bethanechol \\n Use Urinary Retention Post-op Post-partum Neurogenic bladder .\\n Mnemonic \\ Bethany call chol me if you want to pee.\\ \\n Carbachol Glaucoma rarely used . Mixed M and N activity.\\n\\n Alkaloids Tertiary Amines \\n Well absorbed Cross BBB. \\n Pilocarpine \\n Use Glaucoma Opens meshwork via miosis Xerostomia Sjogren s Radiation .\\n Mnemonic \\ Pile on the sweat and tears.\\ \\n Cevimeline Synthetic M3 agonist. Used for Sjogren s Syndrome Dry mouth .\\n\\n 3. Nicotinic Agonists Slide 36-39 \\n Nicotine \\n Action Biphasic. Low dose = Stimulation. High dose = Depolarizing Blockade Paralysis .\\n Use Smoking Cessation NRT .\\n Varenicline Chantix \\n Action Partial Agonist at $\\\\\\\\alpha 4\\\\\\\\beta 2$ neuronal receptors.\\n Benefit Reduces cravings agonist effect AND blocks nicotine from bindings antagonist effect reducing reward.\\n\\n 4. Adverse Effects & Contraindications Slide 34 \\n Muscarinic Toxicity SLUDGE \\n S alivation L acrimation U rination D efecation G I Upset E mesis.\\n DANGER Bradycardia and Bronchoconstriction .\\n Contraindications \\n Asthma/COPD Bronchoconstriction .\\n Peptic Ulcer Disease Increased Acid .\\n GI/Urinary Obstruction Risk of rupture .\\n\\n 5. Clinical Correlates Slide 33 \\n\\n correlate\\n Sj\\u00f6gren s Syndrome \\n Pathology Autoimmune destruction of exocrine glands Salivary Lacrimal .\\n Presentation Xerostomia Dry mouth Keratoconjunctivitis Sicca Dry eyes .\\n Treatment Pilocarpine or Cevimeline Muscarinic Agonists to stimulate secretion.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Asthma Diagnosis \\n Drug Methacholine .\\n Test Inhaled methacholine challenge.\\n Result A patient with asthma will bronchoconstrict at a much lower dose than a healthy person.\\n Lecture 19 Pharmacology of Direct-Acting Cholinomimetics Neuroscience questions A 55-year-old male with post-operative urinary retention needs medication to help void. Examination confirms no obstruction. Which drug is most appropriate? Methacholine Atropine Bethanechol Pilocarpine Nicotine 2 Bethanechol is a direct muscarinic agonist with selectivity for the bladder/GI used to treat non-obstructive urinary retention. A patient being treated for Sjogren s syndrome complains of excessive sweating and diarrhea. This is likely due to which medication? Pilocarpine Atropine Scopolamine Ipratropium Varenicline 0 Pilocarpine is used to increase salivation in Sjogren s but is non-selective causing systemic muscarinic side effects like sweating diaphoresis and diarrhea. Which drug acts as a partial agonist at nicotinic receptors to aid in smoking cessation? Bupropion Varenicline Nicotine Patch Methacholine Carbachol 1 Varenicline is a partial agonist at $\\\\alpha 4\\\\beta 2$ nicotinic receptors reducing cravings and withdrawal symptoms. Methacholine is primarily used clinically for Treating Glaucoma Diagnosing Asthma Treating Urinary Retention Smoking Cessation Treating Alzheimer s 1 Methacholine is used in the bronchoprovocation challenge test to diagnose asthma . Direct-acting cholinomimetics should be avoided in patients with which condition? Glaucoma Xerostomia Peptic Ulcer Disease Constipation Hypertension 2 Muscarinic agonists increase gastric acid secretion which can exacerbate Peptic Ulcer Disease . Drug for Post-op Urinary Retention Bethanechol Pharmacology Drug for Diagnosis of Asthma Methacholine Pharmacology Drug for Sjogren s Syndrome Pilocarpine or Cevimeline Pharmacology Mechanism of Varenicline Partial Agonist at Nicotinic Receptors Pharmacology Contraindication for Muscarinic Agonists Lung Asthma/COPD Pharmacology Contraindication for Muscarinic Agonists GI Peptic Ulcer Disease or Obstruction Pharmacology SLUDGE Mnemonic Salivation Lacrimation Urination Defecation GI upset Emesis Toxicology Pilocarpine Class Muscarinic Agonist Tertiary Amine Pharmacology Bethanechol Class Muscarinic Agonist Quaternary Amine - No CNS Pharmacology Receptors on Sweat Glands Muscarinic Sympathetic Cholinergic Physiology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Cholinomimetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > Cholinergic Agonists Bethanechol Use Non-obstructive Urinary Retention . Bethany call me to pee . Pilocarpine Use Open Angle Glaucoma and Sjogren s Syndrome Sweat/Saliva . Methacholine Challenge Used to diagnose Asthma via induced bronchoconstriction. Varenicline Mechanism Partial Agonist at $\\\\alpha 4\\\\beta 2$ Nicotinic receptors. Reduces withdrawal + Blocks reward. Cholinomimetic Contraindications Asthma causes bronchospasm Peptic Ulcers causes acid Obstruction causes rupture . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l20", "title": "Lecture 20: Indirect-Acting Cholinomimetics (Cholinesterase Inhibitors)", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l20 Lecture 20 Indirect-Acting Cholinomimetics Cholinesterase Inhibitors \\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 20\\n\\n 1. Mechanism & Classification Slide 9 16 \\nIndirect-acting agents inhibit Acetylcholinesterase AChE increasing the concentration of endogenous Acetylcholine in the synapse. This stimulates both Muscarinic and Nicotinic receptors.\\n Quaternary Amines No CNS entry Neostigmine Pyridostigmine.\\n Tertiary Amines Enter CNS Physostigmine Donepezil Organophosphates.\\n\\n 2. Clinical Uses Slide 20 36 \\n\\n Neuromuscular Junction Disorders\\n Myasthenia Gravis MG \\n Pathology Autoantibodies against Nicotinic receptors Nm .\\n Treatment Pyridostigmine Oral long-acting or Neostigmine .\\n Diagnosis Edrophonium Tensilon test. Rapid short-acting. Improvement = +MG. Historical .\\n Reversal of NMJ Blockade \\n Neostigmine is used to reverse non-depolarizing neuromuscular blockers e.g. Rocuronium after surgery.\\n\\n CNS Disorders\\n Alzheimer s Disease \\n Drugs Donepezil Rivastigmine Galantamine .\\n Mechanism Increase CNS ACh to improve cognition/memory.\\n Anticholinergic Toxicity Atropine Overdose \\n Drug Physostigmine .\\n Why? It crosses the BBB to reverse central delirium unlike Neostigmine .\\n\\n Glaucoma\\n Echothiophate Organophosphate Long acting .\\n\\n 3. Toxicity & Poisoning Slide 24-28 \\n\\n Organophosphates Insecticides Nerve Gas \\n Mechanism Irreversible inhibition of AChE via phosphorylation . \\ Aging\\ makes the bond permanent.\\n Muscarinic Signs DUMBELS D iarrhea U rination M iosis B radycardia/Bronchospasm E mesis L acrimation S alivation.\\n Nicotinic Signs Fasciculations → Paralysis Depolarizing blockade .\\n Treatment \\n 1. Atropine Blocks Muscarinic effects life-saving for lungs/heart .\\n 2. Pralidoxime 2-PAM Regenerates AChE IF given before aging. Relieves neuromuscular paralysis Atropine does not .\\n\\n 4. Clinical Correlates Slide 22 \\n\\n correlate\\n Myasthenic vs. Cholinergic Crisis \\n Myasthenic Crisis Low ACh. Respiratory failure due to undertreatment of MG. Action Give more Pyridostigmine.\\n Cholinergic Crisis Excess ACh. Depolarizing blockade due to overtreatment. Action Stop drug give Atropine.\\n differentiation Edrophonium test improves Myasthenic crisis but worsens Cholinergic crisis.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Ogilvie Syndrome \\n Condition Acute Colonic Pseudo-obstruction massive dilation without physical blockage .\\n Treatment Neostigmine stimulates GI motility .\\n Lecture 20 Indirect-Acting Cholinomimetics Cholinesterase Inhibitors Neuroscience questions A farmer is brought to the ER with pinpoint pupils excessive salivation difficulty breathing and muscle fasciculations. Exposure to an insecticide is suspected. Which drug should be administered immediately to treat the respiratory secretions and bradycardia? Physostigmine Pralidoxime Atropine Neostigmine Pilocarpine 2 Atropine is the first-line antidote for the life-threatening muscarinic effects bronchorrhea bronchospasm bradycardia of organophosphate poisoning. Which drug is an appropriate treatment for Alzheimer s disease? Neostigmine Pyridostigmine Donepezil Edrophonium Pilocarpine 2 Donepezil is a centrally acting AChE inhibitor used for Alzheimer s. Neostigmine/Pyridostigmine do not cross the BBB . A child ingests his grandmother s eye drops Atropine and develops hot red skin dilated pupils and delirium. Which antidote can reverse the CNS delirium? Neostigmine Physostigmine Pralidoxime Donepezil Edrophonium 1 The patient has Anticholinergic toxicity Mad as a hatter . Physostigmine is a tertiary amine that crosses the BBB to reverse CNS toxicity. What is the mechanism of action of Pralidoxime 2-PAM ? Blocks muscarinic receptors Blocks nicotinic receptors Regenerates Acetylcholinesterase Inhibits Acetylcholine release Directly activates GABA receptors 2 Pralidoxime is a cholinesterase regenerator. It breaks the bond between the organophosphate and the enzyme if given before aging . Which drug is preferred for long-term oral management of Myasthenia Gravis? Edrophonium Physostigmine Pyridostigmine Echothiophate Acetylcholine 2 Pyridostigmine has a longer duration of action 4-6h and is oral making it ideal for maintenance therapy in Myasthenia Gravis . Antidote for Organophosphate Poisoning Muscarinic Atropine Toxicology Antidote for Organophosphate Poisoning Enzyme Regenerator Pralidoxime 2-PAM Toxicology Antidote for Atropine Overdose Physostigmine Toxicology Drug for Myasthenia Gravis Maintenance Pyridostigmine Pharmacology Drug for Alzheimer s Disease AChE Inhibitor Donepezil Pharmacology Why doesn t Neostigmine treat Alzheimer s? It is a Quaternary amine Charged → Doesn t cross BBB Pharmacology Symptoms of Muscarinic Excess DUMBELS Diarrhea Urination Miosis Bradycardia Emesis Lacrimation Salivation Toxicology Drug used to diagnose Myasthenia Gravis Historical Edrophonium Pharmacology Mechanism of Organophosphates Irreversible inhibition of Acetylcholinesterase Toxicology Treatment for Ogilvie Syndrome Neostigmine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Acetylcholinesterase Inhibitors FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > Cholinergic Agonists Physostigmine vs. Neostigmine Physostigmine crosses BBB Treats Atropine OD .\\\\n Neostigmine does NOT cross BBB Treats MG urinary retention . DUMBELS Mnemonic Diarrhea Urination Miosis Bradycardia Emesis Lacrimation Salivation Muscarinic excess . Organophosphate Antidotes 1. Atropine Muscarinic symptoms .\\\\n2. Pralidoxime Nicotinic/Muscle symptoms - regenerates enzyme . Myasthenia Gravis Treatment Pyridostigmine Long-acting oral . Increases ACh at NMJ. Alzheimer s Drugs Donepezil Rivastigmine Galantamine . Increase CNS ACh. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l21", "title": "Lecture 21: Muscarinic Antagonists (Anticholinergics)", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l21 Lecture 21 Muscarinic Antagonists Anticholinergics \\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 21\\n\\n 1. Class Overview Slide 7-8 \\n Mechanism Competitive antagonists or inverse agonists at Muscarinic receptors M1-M5 .\\n Structure \\n Tertiary Amines Cross BBB Atropine Scopolamine Benztropine.\\n Quaternary Ammonium No CNS Ipratropium Tiotropium Glycopyrrolate.\\n\\n 2. Clinical Uses Slide 21-23 \\n Atropine \\n Bradycardia Blocks vagal tone M2 → Increases HR.\\n Antidote For Organophosphate poisoning.\\n Pre-op Reduces metabolic secretions.\\n Note Low doses can cause Paradoxical Bradycardia blocks presynaptic M2 autoreceptors .\\n Scopolamine \\n Motion Sickness Transdermal patch bypasses first-pass .\\n Respiratory COPD/Asthma \\n Ipratropium Short & Tiotropium Long . Block bronchoconstriction + decrease secretions.\\n Urinary Incontinence Overactive Bladder \\n Oxybutynin . Relaxes detrusor muscle.\\n Parkinson s Disease \\n Benztropine . Treats tremor restores ACh/Dopamine balance .\\n Ophthalmology \\n Tropicamide/Cyclopentolate . Induce Mydriasis Dilation & Cycloplegia Paralysis of accommodation for exams.\\n\\n 3. Toxicity Anticholinergic Toxidrome Slide 26-28 \\n Red as a Beet Cutaneous flushing vasodilation to dissipate heat .\\n Dry as a Bone No sweating Anhidrosis dry mouth.\\n Hot as Hades Hyperthermia cant sweat .\\n Blind as a Bat Mydriasis dilated pupils + Cycloplegia blurry near vision .\\n Mad as a Hatter Delirium Hallucinations Central toxicity .\\n Full as a Flask Urinary Retention.\\n Treatment Physostigmine crosses BBB - Use with caution! \\n\\n 4. Contraindications Slide 25 \\n Narrow-Angle Glaucoma Dilation blocks outflow → Acute crisis.\\n BPH Prostatic Hyperplasia Worsens urinary retention.\\n Elderly Risk of falls confusion delirium BEERS criteria .\\n GI Obstruction/Ileus Worsens constipation.\\n\\n 5. Clinical Correlates Slide 31 \\n\\n correlate\\n Drug Interactions \\n Oxybutynin + Macrolides Erythromycin Macrolides inhibit CYP3A4 . Oxybutynin is metabolized by CYP3A4. Result → Anticholinergic Toxicity .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Use in Special Populations \\n Children High risk of Hyperthermia \\ Hot as Hades\\ .\\n Elderly High risk of Delirium \\ Mad as a Hatter\\ and Falls. Avoid if possible.\\n Lecture 21 Muscarinic Antagonists Anticholinergics Neuroscience questions A 75-year-old male with benign prostatic hyperplasia BPH takes an over-the-counter cold medication and develops acute urinary retention. Which ingredient likely caused this? Guaifenesin Acetaminophen Diphenhydramine Anticholinergic Ibuprofen Pseudoephedrine 2 Diphenhydramine has strong anticholinergic properties which can precipitate urinary retention in patients with BPH. Why is Atropine contraindicated in patients with Narrow-Angle Glaucoma? It causes miosis It increases aqueous humor production It causes mydriasis which blocks the outflow angle It causes cataract formation It lowers intraocular pressure too much 2 Atropine causes Mydriasis dilation . In narrow-angle glaucoma the bunched-up iris blocks the trabecular meshwork dangerously increasing pressure. To prevent motion sickness a patient applies a Transderm Scop patch. What is the active drug and its mechanism? Scopolamine; Muscarinic Antagonist Scopolamine; Nicotinic Antagonist Meclizine; H1 Antagonist Ondansetron; 5-HT3 Antagonist Metoclopramide; D2 Antagonist 0 Scopolamine is a Muscarinic Antagonist used transdermally for motion sickness prevention. Which drug is used to treat tremors in Parkinson s disease by restoring the balance between Acetylcholine and Dopamine? Atropine Ipratropium Benztropine Oxybutynin Succinylcholine 2 Benztropine is a centrally acting antimuscarinic used to treat Parkinsonian tremors Parkinson s = Low Dopamine + High ACh . A child presents with flushed skin dry mouth dilated pupils and tachycardia after eating berries from a garden plant. The most likely plant is Foxglove Digitalis Jimson Weed Datura/Atropine Oleander Poison Ivy Mushrooms Muscarine 1 Jimson Weed Datura stramonium contains belladonna alkaloids Atropine/Scopolamine causing the classic anticholinergic toxidrome. Anticholinergic Toxidrome Mad as a Hatter Delirium / Hallucinations Toxicology Anticholinergic Toxidrome Blind as a Bat Mydriasis + Cycloplegia Blurred vision Toxicology Drug for Overactive Bladder Oxybutynin Pharmacology Inhaled Muscarinic Antagonist for COPD Tiotropium Long or Ipratropium Short Pharmacology Why Atropine causes Tachycardia Blocks M2 receptors on SA node Removes vagal brake Physiology Mechanism of Paradoxical Bradycardia Blockade of Presynaptic Autoreceptors M2 → Increased ACh release Physiology Contraindication Narrow Angle Glaucoma Muscarinic Antagonists Atropine Pharmacology Contraindication BPH Muscarinic Antagonists Causes Retention Pharmacology Drug for Motion Sickness Patch Scopolamine Pharmacology Treats Parkinson s Tremor Benztropine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Muscarinic Antagonists FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > Muscarinic Antagonists Anticholinergic Toxidrome Red Flush Dry No sweat Hot Hyperthermia Blind Mydriasis Mad Delirium Full Retention . Paradoxical Bradycardia Low dose Atropine blocks Presynaptic M2 autoreceptors → Increases ACh releases → Slows Heart. Reversal Agents Atropine reverses AChE Inhibitors.\\\\n Physostigmine reverses Atropine. Contraindications Glaucoma Angle Closure BPH Retention Elderly Delirium . Ipratropium/Tiotropium Quaternary amines No CNS . Inhaled for COPD/Asthma . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l22", "title": "Lecture 22: Ganglionic and Neuromuscular Blockers", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l22 Lecture 22 Ganglionic and Neuromuscular Blockers\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 22\\n\\n 1. Ganglionic Blockers Slide 7-9 \\n Target Neuronal Nicotinic Receptors Nn at autonomic ganglia.\\n Mecamylamine \\n Mechanism Blocks output of BOTH Sympathetic and Parasympathetic systems.\\n Effect Predictable based on Dominant Tone \\n Arterioles Symp dominant Vasodilation → Hypotension Orthostatic .\\n Heart Para dominant Tachycardia.\\n Gut/Bladder Para dominant Constipation Urinary Retention.\\n Eye Para dominant Mydriasis Cycloplegia.\\n\\n 2. Neuromuscular Blockers NMBAs Slide 19-23 \\nTarget Nicotinic Nm receptors at the Neuromuscular Junction NMJ .\\n\\n Depolarizing NMBA\\n Succinylcholine SCh \\n Structure Two ACh molecules.\\n Mechanism Agonist. Causes sustained depolarization → Fasciculations → Flaccid paralysis Phase I . Desensitization Phase II .\\n Metabolism Plasma Cholinesterase Pseudocholinesterase .\\n Duration Very short <8 min . Prolonged in patients with genetic deficiency. \\n Adverse Effects \\n Malignant Hyperthermia MH Triggered by SCh + Inhaled Anesthetics e.g. Halothane . Muscle rigidity hyperthermia. Treat with Dantrolene .\\n Hyperkalemia Life-threatening in Burn/Trauma patients due to receptor upregulation/rhabdomyolysis .\\n\\n Non-Depolarizing NMBAs Competitive Antagonists \\n Isoquinolines \\n Atracurium Degraded by Hofmann Elimination spontaneous no organ weakness . Side Effect Histamine release Hypotension + Laudanosine accumulation Seizures .\\n Cisatracurium Hofmann elimination. No histamine. Safe for organ failure.\\n Aminosteroids \\n Rocuronium Fast onset can replace SCh for intubation . Biliary excretion.\\n Vecuronium Renal/Biliary excretion.\\n\\n 3. Reversal Slide 27 \\n Cholinesterase Inhibitors Neostigmine Increase ACh to compete with NMBAs. Always give with Atropine to prevent muscarinic bradycardia.\\n Sugammadex Specialized chelator. Encapsulates Rocuronium/Vecuronium for rapid reversal. No muscarinic side effects.\\n\\n 4. Clinical Correlates\\n\\n correlate\\n Malignant Hyperthermia \\n Trigger Succinylcholine or Volatile Anesthetics.\\n Genetic Mutation in Ryanodine Receptor RyR1 .\\n Pathology Uncontrolled Ca2+ release from SR → Rigidity → Heat/Acidosis.\\n Cure Dantrolene Blocks RyR1 .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Hofmann Elimination \\n Drug Atracurium / Cisatracurium.\\n Concept Breaks down spontaneously in plasma based on pH and temperature.\\n Pearl Ideal for patients with multisystem organ failure Kidney/Liver don t matter .\\n Lecture 22 Ganglionic and Neuromuscular Blockers Neuroscience questions A patient undergoes surgery and develops muscle rigidity tachycardia and a temperature of 105\\u00b0F shortly after induction. Which drug is the most likely trigger? Rocuronium Succinylcholine Cisatracurium Propofol Fentanyl 1 Succinylcholine is a classic trigger for Malignant Hyperthermia along with volatile anesthetics . Which neuromuscular blocker undergoes spontaneous Hofmann elimination and is therefore safe in patients with renal and hepatic failure? Pancuronium Rocuronium Vecuronium Cisatracurium Succinylcholine 3 Cisatracurium and Atracurium degrades spontaneously in plasma Hofmann elimination independent of organ function. What is the mechanism of action of Sugammadex? Inhibits Acetylcholinesterase Blocks Ryanodine Receptors Chelates/Encapsulates Aminosteroid NMBAs Stimulates Nicotinic Receptors Blocks Muscarinic Receptors 2 Sugammadex forms a complex with aminosteroids Rocuronium/Vecuronium to reverse blockade rapidly. A patient with severe burns requires intubation. Which neuromuscular blocker is CONTRAINDICATED due to the risk of hyperkalemia? Rocuronium Cisatracurium Vecuronium Atracurium Succinylcholine 4 Succinylcholine causes depolarization and K+ efflux. In burn patients upregulated receptors this can cause massive hyperkalemia and cardiac arrest. The use of Neostigmine to reverse neuromuscular blockade can cause bradycardia. Which drug is co-administered to prevent this? Pralidoxime Physostigmine Atropine Epinephrine Dantrolene 2 Neostigmine increases ACh everywhere Muscarinic + Nicotinic . Atropine is given to block the Muscarinic effects Bradycardia while allowing Nicotinic effects Muscle strength . Contraindication for Succinylcholine Burns Trauma Hyperkalemia Hx of Malignant Hyperthermia Pharmacology Treatment for Malignant Hyperthermia Dantrolene Pharmacology NMBA metabolized by Plasma Cholinesterase Succinylcholine Pharmacology NMBA eliminated by Hofmann Elimination Cisatracurium / Atracurium Pharmacology Depolarizing Neuromuscular Blocker Succinylcholine Pharmacology Dominant Autonomic Tone Heart Parasympathetic Vagal Physiology Dominant Autonomic Tone Blood Vessels Sympathetic Physiology Sugammadex reverses which drugs? Rocuronium and Vecuronium Pharmacology Why give Atropine with Neostigmine reversal? To prevent Muscarinic side effects Bradycardia Pharmacology Ganglion Blocker Drug Name Mecamylamine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Neuromuscular Blockers FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > NMBAs Succinylcholine & Potassium Causes K+ efflux. Contraindicated in Burns Trauma Crush injuries and Denervation risk of Cardiac Arrest . Dantrolene Mechanism Blocks Ryanodine Receptors RyR1 on Sarcoplasmic Reticulum. Treats Malignant Hyperthermia. Hofmann Elimination Atracurium/Cisatracurium . Spontaneous degradation. Safe in renal/hepatic failure. Dominant Tone Concept Ganglionic blockers remove the dominant tone. Vessels = Symp Block → Hypotension . Heart = Para Block → Tachycardia . Sugammadex Encapsulates Rocuronium/Vecuronium . Reversal without cholinergic side effects. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l23", "title": "Lecture 23: Pathology of Auditory and Vestibular System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l23 Lecture 23 Pathology of Auditory and Vestibular System\\n\\n Lecturer A. Reno D.O.\\n Session Lecture 23\\n\\n 1. Approach to Dizziness Slide 6-27 \\n Vertigo Illusion of movement \\ Spinning\\ .\\n Peripheral Inner Ear \\n Nystagmus Unidirectional usually horizontal/rotary .\\n Fixation Extinguishes nystagmus.\\n Walking Patient can usually walk.\\n Hearing Often affected Tinnitus/Loss .\\n Central Brainstem/Cerebellum - Stroke/MS \\n Nystagmus Bidirectional or Vertical .\\n Fixation Does NOT extinguish .\\n Walking Patient often cannot stand/walk Ataxia .\\n Hearing Usually normal.\\n \\ Red Flags\\ Comparison to Stroke HINTS Exam .\\n\\n 2. Key Vestibular Pathologies\\n Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV \\n Duration Seconds < 1 minute .\\n Trigger Head movement Rolling over in bed .\\n Mechanism Dislodged Otoconia Calcium Carbonate crystals in posterior semicircular canal.\\n Diagnosis Dix-Hallpike maneuver Rotary nystagmus .\\n Treatment Epley Maneuver .\\n\\n Meniere s Disease Endolymphatic Hydrops \\n Duration Hours 20 min - 24 hrs .\\n Classic Triad \\n 1. Episodic Vertigo .\\n 2. Tinnitus \\ Roaring\\ .\\n 3. Sensorineural Hearing Loss Fluctuating low frequency .\\n Mechanism Excess Endolymph fluid.\\n Treatment Low salt diet Diuretics HCTZ/Triamterene .\\n\\n Vestibulo-Cochlear Infections\\n Vestibular Neuritis Vertigo only No hearing loss . Duration Days . Usually viral. Rx Corticosteroids.\\n Labyrinthitis Vertigo + Hearing Loss . Viral or Bacterial.\\n\\n 3. Hearing Loss Evaluation Slide 40-47 \\n Conductive CHL \\n Anatomy Outer/Middle ear Wax Otitis Media Otosclerosis .\\n Weber Lateralizes to BAD ear.\\n Rinne BC > AC Bone Conduction > Air Conduction .\\n\\n Sensorineural SNHL \\n Anatomy Cochlea/Nerve Presbycusis Noise Meniere s Acoustic Neuroma .\\n Weber Lateralizes to GOOD ear.\\n Rinne AC > BC Normal pattern but reduced .\\n\\n 4. Clinical Correlates\\n correlate\\n The HINTS Exam \\nUsed to distinguish Central Stroke from Peripheral vertigo in acute vestibular syndrome.\\n1. H ead I mpulse Normal impulse = Central bad . Peripheral has corrective saccade .\\n2. N ystagmus Direction changing = Central .\\n3. T est of S kew Vertical Skew = Central .\\n Pearl \\ INFARCT\\ Impulse Normal Fast-phase Alternating Refixation on Cover Test .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Acoustic Neuroma Vestibular Schwannoma \\n Unilateral Sensorineural Hearing Loss + Tinnitus.\\n Slow growing.\\n Diagnose with MRI .\\n Lecture 23 Pathology of Auditory and Vestibular System Neuroscience questions A 45-year-old woman complains of room-spinning dizziness that lasts for 30 seconds when she rolls over in bed. It is associated with nausea. What is the most likely diagnosis? Meniere s Disease Vestibular Neuritis Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV Acoustic Neuroma Multiple Sclerosis 2 BPPV is characterized by brief <1 min episodes of vertigo triggered by head position changes. A patient presents with vertigo tinnitus and hearing loss in the right ear. The episodes last for about 4 hours. Which treatment is most appropriate for long-term management? Epley Maneuver Antibiotics Low salt diet and Diuretics Carbamazepine Chemotherapy 2 The patient has Meniere s Disease Triad + Duration . Treatment involves reducing endolymph fluid with salt restriction and diuretics . Weber test lateralizes to the LEFT ear. Rinne test shows Bone Conduction > Air Conduction on the LEFT. What is the diagnosis? Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss Right Conductive Hearing Loss Left Sensorineural Hearing Loss Left Conductive Hearing Loss Normal Hearing 3 Weber to the Left + Rinne BC > AC on Left = Left Conductive Hearing Loss . In CHL bone conduction is better than air and it lateralizes to the affected ear . Which finding on physical exam strongly suggests a CENTRAL cause of vertigo e.g. Stroke ? Nystagmus that suppresses with fixation Unidirectional horizontal nystagmus Positive Head Impulse Test Corrective Saccade Vertical Nystagmus Hearing Loss 3 Vertical nystagmus or direction-changing nystagmus is a red flag for Central pathology. Peripheral nystagmus is usually horizontal/rotary and suppresses with fixation. A patient has sudden onset of severe vertigo lasting 3 days with nausea and vomiting but NO hearing loss. Findings include horizontal nystagmus. What is the most likely diagnosis? Labyrinthitis Vestibular Neuritis Meniere s Disease BPPV Otosclerosis 1 Vestibular Neuritis presents with continuous vertigo for days often post-viral with preserved hearing . Labyrinthitis would include hearing loss . Diagnosis Vertigo < 1 minute triggered by movement BPPV Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo ENT Diagnosis Vertigo Hours + Tinnitus + Hearing Loss Meniere s Disease ENT Diagnosis Vertigo Days + No Hearing Loss Vestibular Neuritis ENT Diagnosis Vertigo Days + Hearing Loss Labyrinthitis ENT Weber Test Lateralization Conductive Loss Lateralizes to the BAD affected ear Diagnosis Weber Test Lateralization Sensorineural Loss Lateralizes to the GOOD unaffected ear Diagnosis Maneuver to Diagnose BPPV Dix-Hallpike Diagnosis Maneuver to Treat BPPV Epley Maneuver Treatment Mechanism of Meniere s Disease Endolymphatic Hydrops Excess fluid in inner ear Pathology Central vs Peripheral Nystagmus Fixation Peripheral suppresses with fixation. Central does NOT. Diagnosis AMBOSS Neurology > Vertigo and Dizziness FirstAid Neurology > Otology BandB Neurology > The Ear Vertigo Duration Clues < 1 min BPPV.\\\\n Hours Meniere s Disease.\\\\n Days Vestibular Neuritis / Labyrinthitis. Peripheral vs. Central Nystagmus Peripheral Extinguishes with fixation. Horizontal/Rotary.\\\\n Central Does NOT extinguish. Vertical or Direction-Changing. Meniere s Triad 1. Episodic Vertigo\\\\n2. Tinnitus Roaring \\\\n3. Sensorineural Hearing Loss. Weber/Rinne Rules Conductive Bad ear hears Weber louder. BC > AC.\\\\n Sensorineural Good ear hears Weber louder. AC > BC. Treatment of BPPV Epley Maneuver Repositioning of otoconia . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l24", "title": "Lecture 24: Olfaction and Taste", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l24 Lecture 24 Olfaction and Taste\\n\\n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 24\\n\\n 1. Olfaction Smell \\n Receptors Bipolar neurons in olfactory epithelium.\\n Turnover Every 7 days stem cells .\\n Receptors GPCRs.\\n Pathway \\n 1. Olfactory Nerves CN I pass through Cribriform Plate of Ethmoid bone.\\n 2. Synapse in Glomeruli of Olfactory Bulb.\\n 3. Mitral/Tufted Cells Second order form Olfactory Tract.\\n 4. Project directly to Primary Olfactory Cortex Piriform Cortex Amygdala Entorhinal Cortex.\\n Note Olfaction is the ONLY sense that bypasses the Thalamus before reaching the cortex.\\n\\n 2. Taste Gustation \\n Modalities \\n Salty Na+ influx Amiloride-sensitive channels .\\n Sour H+ ions Block K+ channels .\\n Sweet/Bitter/Umami GPCRs G-protein coupled .\\n Innervation \\n Anterior 2/3 CN VII Facial - Chorda Tympani .\\n Posterior 1/3 CN IX Glossopharyngeal .\\n Epiglottis/Pharynx CN X Vagus .\\n Pathway \\n 1. Nerves project to Nucleus Tractus Solitarius NTS in Medulla.\\n 2. Project to VPM of Thalamus.\\n 3. Project to Gustatory Cortex Insula/Frontal Operculum .\\n\\n 3. Clinical Correlates\\n correlate\\n Kallmann Syndrome \\n Triad Anosmia + Delayed Puberty Hypogonadism .\\n Mechanism Failure of GnRH neurons to migrate from olfactory placode to hypothalamus.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Foster-Kennedy Syndrome \\n Cause Meningioma of the Olfactory Groove.\\n Triad \\n 1. Ipsilateral Anosmia Tumor compresses olfactory tract .\\n 2. Ipsilateral Optic Atrophy Tumor compresses Optic Nerve .\\n 3. Contralateral Papilledema Increased intracranial pressure .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Uncinate Fits Temporal Lobe Epilepsy \\n Seizures originating in the Uncus Medial Temporal Lobe .\\n Aura Hallucination of foul smells Cacosmia .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Neurodegenerative Diseases \\n Parkinson s / Alzheimer s Hyposmia decreased smell is often the earliest clinical sign appearing years before motor or cognitive symptoms.\\n Lecture 24 Olfaction and Taste Neuroscience questions A 16-year-old male presents with delayed puberty. Physical exam reveals small testes and a complete inability to smell since birth. What is the most likely diagnosis? Klinefelter Syndrome Kallmann Syndrome Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Turner Syndrome Uncinate Epilepsy 1 Kallmann Syndrome is characterized by hypogonadotropic hypogonadism delayed puberty and anosmia due to migration failure of GnRH neurons. A patient presents with seizures preceded by a hallucination of a foul smell burning rubber . Where is the seizure focus likely located? Postcentral Gyrus Occipital Lobe Uncus Temporal Lobe Frontal Lobe Cerebellum 2 Uncinate fits are temporal lobe seizures involving the Uncus primary olfactory cortex area resulting in olfactory hallucinations Cacosmia . Which cranial nerve is responsible for taste sensation on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue? Trigeminal V Facial VII Glossopharyngeal IX Vagus X Hypoglossal XII 1 The Facial Nerve CN VII carries taste from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue via the Chorda Tympani . A 55-year-old woman presents with loss of smell in her right ear and vision loss in her right eye. Fundoscopy reveals right optic atrophy and left papilledema. What is the most likely diagnosis? Pituitary Adenoma Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Multiple Sclerosis Occipital Stroke Glioblastoma Multiforme 1 Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Olfactory Groove Meningioma causes ipsilateral anosmia and optic atrophy direct compression and contralateral papilledema ICP . Loss of olfaction is often an early prodromal sign of which neurodegenerative disorder? Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis ALS Parkinson s Disease Huntington s Disease Multiple Sclerosis Myasthenia Gravis 1 Hyposmia loss of smell frequently precedes motor symptoms in Parkinson s Disease and Alzheimer s by several years. Diagnosis Anosmia + No Puberty Kallmann Syndrome Neurology Diagnosis Ipsilateral Anosmia/Optic Atrophy + Contralateral Papilledema Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Neurology CN for Taste Anterior 2/3 Tongue CN VII Facial Anatomy CN for Taste Posterior 1/3 Tongue CN IX Glossopharyngeal Anatomy Taste Modalities using Ion Channels Salty Na+ and Sour H+ Physiology Taste Modalities using GPCRs Sweet Bitter Umami Physiology Seizure aura with foul smell Cacosmia Uncinate Fit Temporal Lobe/Uncus Neurology Unique feature of Olfactory Pathway Does not relay in Thalamus before Cortex Anatomy Primary Olfactory Cortex location Piriform Cortex Temporal Lobe Anatomy Thalamic Nucleus for Taste VPM Ventral Posteromedial Anatomy AMBOSS Neurology > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Otology/Ophthalmology BandB Neurology > The Chemical Senses Olfactory Pathway Uniqueness Olfaction is the only sensory modality that projects directly to the cortex without relaying through the Thalamus first. Kallmann Syndrome Teenager with No Smell + No Puberty . GnRH neuron migration failure . Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Olfactory Groove Meningioma Ipsilateral Anosmia/Optic Atrophy + Contralateral Papilledema . Taste Innervation Anterior 2/3 = CN VII .\\\\nPosterior 1/3 = CN IX .\\\\nThroat = CN X . Taste Transduction Salty/Sour = Ion Channels .\\\\nSweet/Bitter/Umami = GPCRs . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l25", "title": "Lecture 25: Limbic System - Neuroanatomy, Regional Function and Limbic Pathology", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l25 Lecture 25 Limbic System - Neuroanatomy Regional Function and Limbic Pathology lecturer Jennifer Xie Ph.D. session Lecture 25 Neuroscience prev l24 next l26 pdf pdfs/Lecture 25. J. Xie Ph.D. PPT -1.pdf transcript transcripts/Lecture 25 Limbic System - Neuroanatomy Regional Function and Limbic Pathology Faculty J. Xie Ph.D..srt Lecture 25 Limbic System - Neuroanatomy Regional Function and Limbic Pathology Lecturer Jennifer Xie Ph.D. Session Lecture 25 1. Introduction to the Limbic System Slide 5-7 The Limbic System Latin limbus = border is a complex network of cortical and diencephalic structures located on the medial border of the cerebral hemisphere. It serves as a bridge linking the conscious intellectual functions of the cerebral cortex with the unconscious autonomic regulation of the brainstem and hypothalamus . Primary Function Regulates primitive emotional drives memory storage/retrieval and homeostasis. The 5 F s Mnemonic mnemonic Functions of the Limbic System The 5 F s F eeding Satiety/Hunger F leeing Fear response F ighting Aggression F eeling Emotion F ornicating Sexual behavior Key Structures While the exact composition is debated the Big 3 universally agreed-upon components are 1. Cingulate Gyrus Emotional processing of sensory input. 2. Hippocampal Formation Memory consolidation and spatial navigation. 3. Amygdala Fear anger and emotional association. Other components include the Mammillary Bodies Hypothalamus Anterior Thalamic Nuclei Septal Area Reward and Orbitofrontal Cortex Social behavior . 2. Cingulate Gyrus Slide 9-10 The Cingulate Gyrus wraps around the corpus callosum and acts as a gateway between the cortex and limbic system. Function It adds emotional coloring to sensory perceptions e.g. how pain feels emotionally . Clinical Relevance Cingulotomy A surgical lesioning of the anterior cingulate cortex. Historically used to treat intractable pain or severe depression/anxiety. Patients report they still feel the pain but it doesn t bother them anymore loss of the affective/suffering component . 3. Hippocampal Formation & Memory Slide 13-17 The Hippocampus Latin for Seahorse is critical for learning memory consolidation and spatial navigation. Structure Composed of the Dentate Gyrus Hippocampus Proper Cornu Ammonis CA1-CA4 and Subiculum . Vulnerability The CA1 pyramidal cells are exceptionally sensitive to hypoxia/ischemia . They will die within minutes of oxygen deprivation Sommer s Sector . Lateralization Right Hippocampus Spatial Orientation and navigation e.g. London Taxi Drivers have enlarged right posterior hippocampi . Left Hippocampus Verbal memory. The Fornix Slide 16-17 The major output tract of the hippocampus. Pathway Hippocampus → Fimbria → Crus → Body → Columns of Fornix → Mammillary Bodies Hypothalamus . correlate Temporal Lobe Epilepsy Pathophysiology The medial temporal lobe Hippocampus/Amygdala has the lowest seizure threshold in the brain. Presentation Complex Partial Psychomotor Seizures . Preceded by an Aura e.g. burning rubber smell/uncinate fits hallucinations deja vu . Automatisms Lip smacking chewing swallowing or fumbling of hands. Altered consciousness dreamy state . correlate Herpes Simplex Encephalitis Pathophysiology HSV-1 has a specific tropism for the limbic cortex Medial Temporal Lobes/Orbitofrontal cortex . Presentation Acute onset of fever headache seizures and bizarre behavior. Sequelae Bilateral necrosis of the medial temporal lobes leads to profound Anterograde Amnesia inability to form new memories and Kluver-Bucy-like symptoms. Imaging Hyperintensity in temporal lobes on MRI Slide 12 . 4. The Papez Circuit Slide 30-31 Discovered by James Papez 1937 this is the fundamental circuit for Episodic Memory Consolidation and emotional expression. highyield The Papez Circuit Pathway 1. Hippocampus Subiculum 2. $\\\\xrightarrow \\\\text Fornix $ Mammillary Bodies 3. $\\\\xrightarrow \\\\text Mammillothalamic Tract $ Anterior Thalamic Nucleus 4. $\\\\xrightarrow \\\\text Internal Capsule Ant. Limb $ Cingulate Gyrus 5. $\\\\xrightarrow \\\\text Cingulum bundle $ Entorhinal Cortex / Hippocampus The circuit closes 5. Amygdala & Emotion Slide 19-23 The Amygdala Almond is located deep in the uncus of the temporal lobe anterior to the hippocampus. Function Mediates Fear Anger Aggression and the emotional significance of memories e.g. fear conditioning . Connections Hypothalamus Triggers autonomic responses to emotion tachycardia sweating . Olfactory System Why smells trigger strong emotional memories Proustian memory . correlate Kluver-Bucy Syndrome Pathophysiology Bilateral lesion of the Amygdala . associated with HSV encephalitis trauma or lobectomies. Presentation 1. Placidity Loss of fear and aggression docile . 2. Hyperorality Putting inappropriate objects in mouth. 3. Hypersexuality Indiscriminate sexual behavior. 4. Hypermetamorphosis Compulsive visual exploration of objects. 5. Visual Agnosia Psychic blindness inability to recognize emotional significance of objects . 6. Memory Disorders Slide 36-39 Classification of Memory 1. Explicit Declarative Facts Semantic and Events Episodic . Requires Medial Temporal Lobe Hippocampus & Diencephalon. 2. Implicit Procedural Skills habits conditioning. Skills e.g. riding a bike Basal Ganglia Cerebellum Motor Cortex. Emotional Conditioning Amygdala. Skeletal Conditioning Cerebellum. Clinical Cases correlate Patient H.M. History Bilateral medial temporal lobectomy for intractable epilepsy. Deficit Severe Anterograde Amnesia cannot form new explicit memories . Retrograde memory pre-surgery was largely intact. Key Finding His Procedural Memory was intact he could learn to solve the Tower of Hanoi puzzle but couldn t remember practicing it . Implication Proved that hippocampus is essential for consolidation of new declarative memories but not for procedural skills. correlate Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome Pathophysiology Thiamine Vitamin B1 Deficiency intimately associated with chronic Alcoholism . Lesion Sites Mammillary Bodies hemorrhagic necrosis Dorsomedial Thalamus Periaqueductal gray. Wernicke s Encephalopathy Acute/Reversible Triad C onfusion A taxia N ystagmus/Ophthalmoplegia \\ CAN of beer\\ . Korsakoff s Psychosis Chronic/Irreversible Severe Anterograde Amnesia . Confabulation Making up stories to fill memory gaps lack of insight . Treatment IV Thiamine BEFORE Glucose Glucose first precipitates the encephalopathy . correlate Alzheimer s Disease Pathophysiology Widespread cortical atrophy specifically Hippocampal Atrophy Early memory loss. Nucleus Basalis of Meynert Degeneration of Cholinergic neurons. Neurochemistry Deficiency of Acetylcholine ACh . Treatment AChE Inhibitors Aricept to increase synaptic ACh Slide 38 . 7. Septal Area & Reward Slide 25 Located near the anterior commissure. Contains the Nucleus Accumbens . Function The Pleasure Center . Mediates reward and reinforcement. Neurotransmitter Dopamine Mesolimbic pathway . Clinical Implicated in Addiction . The 5 F s of the Limbic System Feeding Fleeing Fighting Feeling and Sex. Sommer s Sector Vulnerability CA1 Pyramidal cells in the hippocampus are the most sensitive neurons to hypoxia . First to die in ischemic stroke or cardiorespiratory arrest. Papez Circuit Sequence Hippocampus → Fornix → Mammillary Bodies → Ant. Thalamic Nucleus → Cingulate Gyrus → Hippocampus. Kluver-Bucy Syndrome Bilateral Amygdala lesion. Hyper sexuality Hyper orality Placidity No fear . Wernicke s Triad Confusion Ataxia Nystagmus/Ophthalmoplegia CAN of Beer . Caused by B1 Thiamine deficiency harming Mammillary Bodies . Procedural vs. Declarative Memory Hippocampal damage Patient H.M. destroys Declarative New facts/events but spares Procedural Skills/Habits - Basal Ganglia/Cerebellum . HSV Encephalitis Target HSV-1 loves the Medial Temporal Lobe . Causes hemorrhagic necrosis fever seizures and bizarre behavior. questions A chronic alcoholic is brought to the ER with confusion ataxia and ophthalmoplegia. MRI reveals degeneration of the mammillary bodies. Which nutrient deficiency is responsible? Vitamin B12 Cobalamin Vitamin B1 Thiamine Vitamin B3 Niacin Vitamin B6 Pyridoxine Vitamin C Ascorbic Acid 1 Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome is caused by Thiamine B1 deficiency common in alcoholics. It damages the Mammillary Bodies and medial thalamus. The triad is Confusion Ataxia Nystagmus. A patient undergoes bilateral temporal lobectomy for intractable epilepsy. Post-operatively he interacts with objects by putting them in his mouth displays inappropriate sexual behavior toward staff and appears completely fearless. Which structure s absence explains these symptoms? Hippocampus Cingulate Gyrus Amygdala Mammillary Bodies Septal Nuclei 2 This is Kluver-Bucy Syndrome Hyperorality Hypersexuality Placidity . It is caused by bilateral lesions to the Amygdala . A 65-year-old male with a history of cardiac arrest 2 weeks ago now presents with severe short-term memory loss. He cannot remember what he ate for breakfast or the names of his new doctors. Which specific neuronal population was likely damaged by hypoxia? Purkinje cells of the Cerebellum Anterior Horn cells of Spinal Cord CA1 Pyramidal cells of the Hippocampus Dopaminergic neurons of Substantia Nigra Cholinergic neurons of Nucleus Basalis 2 The CA1 Pyramidal neurons of the Hippocampus Sommer s sector are the most sensitive cells in the brain to hypoxic-ischemic injury . Damage here causes anterograde amnesia. In the Papez circuit fibers travel from the mammillary bodies to the anterior nucleus of the thalamus via which tract? Fornix Cingulum Stria Terminalis Mammillothalamic Tract Medial Forebrain Bundle 3 The Mammillothalamic Tract connects the Mammillary Bodies to the Anterior Nucleus of the Thalamus . Patient H.M. had his medial temporal lobes removed. He could no longer form new memories of events Episodic but he could learn to solve puzzles Procedural . Which structure is primarily responsible for the preserved procedural memory? Hippocampus Basal Ganglia Amygdala Septal Nuclei Mammillary Bodies 1 Procedural Memory skills/habits is processed by the Basal Ganglia Cerebellum and Motor Cortex which were intact in H.M. The Hippocampus removed drives Declarative/Episodic memory. Which limbic structure is responsible for spatial navigation? Right Hippocampus Physiology What is the classic triad of Wernicke s Encephalopathy? Confusion Ataxia Nystagmus/Ophthalmoplegia CAN of Beer Pathology Bilateral lesions of the Amygdala cause what syndrome? Kluver-Bucy Syndrome Placidity Hyperorality Hypersexuality Pathology What is the major output tract of the Hippocampus? The Fornix Anatomy Which neurons are most sensitive to cerebral hypoxia? CA1 Pyramidal neurons of the Hippocampus Pathology What is the primary neurotransmitter deficiency in Alzheimer s Disease? Acetylcholine ACh due to loss of Nucleus Basalis of Meynert Pathology HSV-1 Encephalitis typically affects which brain region? Medial Temporal Lobes Limbic Cortex Pathology Damage to the Mammillary Bodies is seen in whom? Alcoholics Thiamine/B1 deficiency → Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome Pathology What connects the Mammillary Bodies to the Anterior Thalamus? Mammillothalamic Tract Anatomy What defines Anterograde Amnesia? Inability to form NEW memories Old memories intact Pathology BandB Neurology > Limbic System FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Limbic System Pathoma CNS > Degenerative Diseases mindmap Limbic System Components The Big 3 Cingulate Gyrus Emotional processing Pain affect Hippocampus Memory Spatial nav Right Amygdala Fear Anger Kluver-Bucy Papez Circuit Hippocampus → Fornix Mammillary Bodies → Mammillothalamic Tract Ant. Thalamus → Internal Capsule Cingulate → Cingulum Pathology Kluver-Bucy Bilateral Amygdala. Hypersex/oral. Wernicke-Korsakoff B1 Def. Mammillary Bodies. Confabulation. HSV Encephalitis Medial Temporal Lobe. Bizarre behavior. Alzheimer s Hippocampal atrophy. Low ACh. Patient H.M. Medial Temporal removal. Anterograde Amnesia. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l26", "title": "Lecture 26: Neurologic Exam Part 1 - Cranial Nerves & Mental Status", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l26 Lecture 26 Neurologic Exam Part 1 - Cranial Nerves & Mental Status\\n\\n Lecturer L. Mark D.O.\\n Session Lecture 26\\n\\n 1. Mental Status & History\\n Mental Status Exam Appearance Behavior Speech Mood Cognition.\\n Glasgow Coma Scale GCS \\n Score 3 Coma to 15 Normal .\\n Components E ye opening V erbal response M otor response. \\ EVM-456\\ .\\n\\n 2. Cranial Nerve Examination\\n CN I Olfactory \\n Test Smell Coffee Cloves one nostril at a time.\\n Deficit Anosmia. DDx COVID-19 Kallmann Syndrome Trauma .\\n\\n CN II Optic \\n Visual Acuity Snellen Chart 20/20 .\\n Visual Fields Confrontation test Static finger wiggle . detects Hemianopsia.\\n Fundoscopy \\n Red Reflex Orange glow.\\n Optic Disc Check for Papilledema blurred margins or Cupping Glaucoma .\\n\\n CN III IV VI Oculomotor Trochlear Abducens \\n Pupils CN III \\n Direct Constriction in illuminated eye.\\n Consensual Constriction in opposite eye.\\n Near Triad Convergence Constriction Accommodation.\\n EOM H-Pattern.\\n CN VI Deficit Eye cannot look out Lateral deviation failure .\\n CN III Deficit Eye is \\ Down and Out\\ + Ptosis + Dilated Pupil.\\n\\n CN V Trigeminal \\n Sensory V1 Opthalmic V2 Maxillary V3 Mandibular - Pinprick/Light touch.\\n Motor Muscles of Mastication Temporalis/Masseter clench .\\n Corneal Reflex Afferent V1 Senses touch → Efferent VII Blink .\\n\\n CN VII Facial \\n Motor Facial expression Smile Frown Puff cheeks Close eyes tight .\\n Central Lesion Stroke Spares forehead.\\n Peripheral Lesion Bell s Palsy Affects entire half of face Forehead + Lower .\\n Sensory Taste Anterior 2/3.\\n\\n CN VIII Vestibulocochlear \\n Gross Hearing Whisper test / Finger rub.\\n Weber Test Tuning fork on vertex.\\n Lateralizes to BAD ear Conductive Loss.\\n Lateralizes to GOOD ear Sensorineural Loss.\\n Rinne Test Mastoid BC vs. Air AC .\\n AC > BC Normal or Sensorineural.\\n BC > AC Conductive Loss.\\n\\n CN IX X Glossopharyngeal Vagus \\n Palate Say \\ Ah\\ . Uvula deviates AWAY from lesion.\\n Gag Reflex Sensory IX Motor X.\\n\\n CN XI Accessory \\n Motor Shrug shoulders Trapezius Turn head SCM .\\n\\n CN XII Hypoglossal \\n Motor Protrude tongue. Tongue deviates TOWARDS lesion \\ Licks the lesion\\ . Lecture 26 Neurologic Exam Part 1 - Cranial Nerves & Mental Status Neuroscience questions A patient presents with right-sided hearing loss. Weber test lateralizes to the RIGHT ear. Rinne test shows Bone Conduction > Air Conduction on the right. What is the diagnosis? Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss Right Conductive Hearing Loss Left Sensorineural Hearing Loss Left Conductive Hearing Loss Normal Hearing 1 Weber lateralizing to the affected bad ear + Rinne BC > AC indicates a Conductive Hearing Loss in that ear. On physical exam a patient s tongue deviates to the LEFT when protruded. Which cranial nerve is lesioned? Right CN XII Left CN XII Right CN X Left CN X Left CN IX 1 The tongue deviates towards the side of the CN XII lesion weak side . Left deviation = Left CN XII lesion. When asking a patient to say Ah the uvula deviates to the RIGHT. This suggests a lesion of which cranial nerve? Right CN X Left CN X Right CN IX Left CN IX Right CN XII 1 The uvula deviates away from the CN X lesion pulled by the intact strong side . Right deviation = Left CN X lesion. Which component of the corneal reflex is carried by the Facial Nerve CN VII ? Sensory Afferent Pain Sensory Afferent Touch Motor Efferent Blink Lacrimation only None 2 CN V1 provides the sensory Afferent limb. CN VII provides the Motor Efferent limb to close the eyelid Orbicularis Oculi . A patient has a dilated right pupil that is unresponsive to light and the right eye is positioned down and out . Which cranial nerve is affected? CN II CN III CN IV CN VI CN VII 1 These are classic signs of a CN III Oculomotor Palsy Ptosis Mydriasis dilation and Down-and-Out deviation. Tongue Deviation Direction Towards the Lesion CN XII Neurology Uvula Deviation Direction Away from the Lesion CN X Neurology Corneal Reflex Afferent Limb CN V1 Ophthalmic Neurology Corneal Reflex Efferent Limb CN VII Facial Neurology Rinne Test Conductive Loss Result BC > AC Bone Conduction > Air Conduction Ent CN III Palsy Signs Down and Out eye Ptosis Dilated Pupil Neurology Glasgow Coma Scale Components Eyes 4 Verbal 5 Motor 6 Emergency Test for CN XI Shoulder Shrug Trapezius & Head Turn SCM Neurology CN VII Test Facial Expressions Smile Frown Puff Cheeks Neurology Weber Test Lateralization Sensorineural To the GOOD ear Ent AMBOSS Neurology > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cranial Nerves BandB Neurology > The Brainstem > Cranial Nerves Tongue Deviation CN XII Lesion Tongue deviates TOWARDS the side of the lesion Licks the lesion . Uvula Deviation CN X Lesion Uvula deviates AWAY from the side of the lesion pulled by the strong side . Corneal Reflex Afferent CN V1 Sensation .\\\\n Efferent CN VII Blink . Rinne Interpretation BC > AC = Conductive Hearing Loss Abnormal .\\\\n AC > BC = Normal or Sensorineural . GCS Scoring EVM-456 .\\\\nEyes 4 Verbal 5 Motor 6 .\\\\nMax 15 Min 3. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l27", "title": "Lecture 27: OMM Lab 17 Prep - Pelvic Counterstrain & HVLA", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l27 Lecture 27 OMM Lab 17 Prep - Pelvic Counterstrain & HVLA\\n\\n Lecturer T. Li D.O.\\n Session Lecture 27\\n\\n 1. Counterstrain CS Overview\\n Definition Indirect Passive technique. \\ Fold and Hold\\ .\\n Mechanism Shortens muscle to silence proprioceptive feedback Muscle Spindles/Gamma Gain .\\n Safety Very gentle. Good for acute pain.\\n Reaction 30-50% experience soreness for 1-2 days Warn patient! .\\n\\n 2. Steps of Counterstrain\\n1. Find Point Locate tenderpoint.\\n2. Scale Call it \\ 10/10\\ .\\n3. Position Move to position of ease reduce to 0/10 or $\\\\\\\\le$ 3/10 .\\n4. Hold 90 Seconds .\\n5. Return Passive return to neutral Slowly! .\\n6. Reassess Check tenderness again.\\n\\n 3. Pelvic Counterstrain Points\\n Iliacus Deep in iliac fossa.\\n Tx Flexion + External Rotation Frog Leg .\\n Psoas Medial to ASIS.\\n Tx F-S T -R A Flex Sidebend Toward Rotate Away .\\n Inguinal Lateral to pubic tubercle.\\n Tx F-ADD-IR Flex Adduct Internal Rotate .\\n Low Ilium Superior pubic ramus.\\n Tx Flexion.\\n\\n 4. Innominate HVLA\\n Diagnosis Reminder \\n Anterior Rotation ASIS Low PSIS High.\\n Posterior Rotation ASIS High PSIS Low.\\n Superior Shear All landmarks high.\\n HVLA Setup \\n Post. Rotation Extend the hip to rotate anteriorly .\\n Ant. Rotation Flex the hip to rotate posteriorly .\\n Superior Shear Traction leg Abduct + IR + Pull .\\n\\n 5. Clinical Correlates\\n correlate\\n Counterstrain Contraindications \\n Absolute No Somatic Dysfunction No Consent Lack of Cooperation.\\n Relative Severe Osteoporosis Acute Illness Instability.\\n Note Recent trauma or fracture would depend on location but Osteoporosis is the classic relative contraindication.\\n Lecture 27 OMM Lab 17 Prep - Pelvic Counterstrain & HVLA Neuroscience questions A patient feels soreness the day after a counterstrain treatment. How should this be managed? Immediate ER referral Repeat the treatment immediately Reassurance and hydration Normal reaction Prescribe Opioids Apply Heat only 2 Post-treatment soreness occurs in 30-50% of patients and lasts 1-2 days. It is a normal reaction due to metabolic release. Reassurance and water are key. What is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to Counterstrain? Osteoporosis Acute Pain Pregnancy Refusal of treatment No Consent Pediatric patient 3 Lack of consent and absence of somatic dysfunction are the main Absolute contraindications. Osteoporosis is a Relative contraindication. For a Psoas tenderpoint medial to ASIS what is the correct treatment position? Extension Sidebend Away Rotate Toward Flexion Sidebend Away Rotate Toward Flexion Sidebend Toward Rotate Away Extension only Flexion only 2 Psoas = F-ST-RA . Flexion Sidebend Toward the point Rotate Away from the point. Which finding suggests a Posterior Innominate Rotation? ASIS Low PSIS High ASIS High PSIS Low ASIS Low PSIS Low ASIS High PSIS High Pubic Shear only 1 Posterior rotation pulls the ASIS up High and pushes the PSIS down Low . What is the goal pain score reduction during Counterstrain positioning? Reduce by 10% Reduce to 5/10 Reduce to 7/10 Reduce to 0/10 or at least <= 3/10 Increase pain to provoke release 3 The goal is 100% reduction 0/10 but any reduction to 3/10 or less is considered acceptable to proceed with the hold. Psoas Counterstrain Setup F-ST-RA Flex SB Toward Rot Away OMM Iliacus Counterstrain Setup Frog Leg Flex + External Rotation OMM Inguinal Counterstrain Setup F-ADD-IR Flex Adduct Int Rotate OMM Counterstrain Hold Time 90 Seconds OMM Counterstrain Mechanism Silent Muscle Spindles Gamma Gain OMM Anterior Innominate Diagnosis ASIS Low PSIS High OMM Posterior Innominate Diagnosis ASIS High PSIS Low OMM Absolute Contraindication to CS No Consent / No Dysfunction OMM Relative Contraindication to CS Osteoporosis / Severe Illness OMM Post-CS Soreness Duration 1-2 Days Normal OMM BandB Musculoskeletal > Spine FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Anatomy Psoas Counterstrain Position F-S T -R A \\\\n F lexed S idebent T oward R otated A way. Counterstrain Hold Time 90 Seconds is the standard hold time for Counterstrain. Inguinal Counterstrain F-ADD-IR \\\\n F lexed ADD ucted I nternally R otated. Common crossing legs position . Anterior Innominate Findings ASIS Lower PSIS Higher on the affected side. Posterior Innominate Findings ASIS Higher PSIS Lower on the affected side. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l28", "title": "Lecture 28: Anatomy & Physiology of the Neocortex", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l28 Lecture 28 Anatomy & Physiology of the Neocortex\\n\\n Lecturer R. Ramos Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 28\\n\\n 1. Frontal Lobe & Executive Function\\n Functions Planning Inhibition Personality \\ Filter\\ Social norms .\\n Pathology \\n Frontotemporal Dementia Pick s Disease \\n Gene MAPT mutation Tau protein .\\n Symptoms Disinhibition Apathy Personality changes e.g. gambling rude comments early onset 45-65y .\\n Classic Case Phineas Gage Iron rod through frontal lobe .\\n Frontal Eye Fields Area 8 Controls biological/voluntary saccades rapid eye movements to contralateral side.\\n\\n 2. Parietal Lobe & Attention\\n Right Parietal Lobe Non-Dominant \\n Function Spatial attention to BOTH sides of the world.\\n Lesion Right MCA Stroke Hemispatial Neglect . Patient interacts only with the Right side eats half plate draws half clock .\\n Left Parietal Lobe Dominant - Angular Gyrus \\n Lesion Gerstmann Syndrome .\\n Tetrad \\n 1. Agraphia Can t write .\\n 2. Acalculia Can t do math .\\n 3. Finger Agnosia Can t ID fingers .\\n 4. Left-Right Disorientation .\\n\\n 3. Temporal Lobe & Recognition\\n Fusiform Face Area FFA Inferior temporal gyrus.\\n Function Facial recognition.\\n Lesion Prosopagnosia Face Blindness .\\n Medial Temporal Lobe MTL Hippocampus/Memory.\\n Alzheimer s Tau tangles Amyloid plaques. Atrophy of MTL.\\n\\n 4. Insula & Cingulate\\n Insula Buried deep in lateral sulcus.\\n Functions Gustatory Taste Interoception feeling inside body Disgust Addiction/Craving Urge to smoke .\\n Stimulation Elicits taste sensations or disgust .\\n Cingulate Gyrus Part of Limbic System.\\n Cingulotomy Surgical lesioning used for intractable Chronic Pain or severe OCD .\\n\\n 5. Histology Highlights\\n Betz Cells Giant pyramidal cells in Layer 5 of Primary Motor Cortex Output to Corticospinal tract .\\n Cytoarchitecture Neocortex has 6 layers. Layer 4 = Input Sensory Layer 5 = Output Motor . Lecture 28 Anatomy & Physiology of the Neocortex Neuroscience questions A 55-year-old man presents with significant personality changes. He has become sexually inappropriate with strangers and has lost his life savings gambling. He has no memory loss. Genetic testing reveals a MAPT mutation. What is the diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Frontotemporal Dementia Behavioral Variant Huntington s Disease Vascular Dementia Parkinson s Disease 1 The combination of Disinhibition gambling inappropriate behavior + Personality change + MAPT mutation + Early onset 55y points to Frontotemporal Dementia . A patient cannot distinguish their left hand from their right hand and has lost the ability to perform simple addition. Where is the lesion most likely located? Right Parietal Lobe Left Inferior Parietal Lobe Angular Gyrus Right Temporal Lobe Left Frontal Lobe Occipital Lobe 1 This is Gerstmann Syndrome Acalculia L-R confusion . The lesion is in the dominant usually Left Angular Gyrus Inferior Parietal Lobule . Electrical stimulation of which brain region is most likely to elicit a sensation of bad taste or disgust? Primary Motor Cortex Fusiform Gyrus Insular Cortex Broca s Area Wernicke s Area 2 The Insula processes Gustatory taste and visceral information including feelings of disgust . A patient recovering from a stroke combs only the right side of their hair and eats only food on the right side of their tray. Which artery was likely occluded? Left MCA Right MCA Left ACA Right ACA Basilar Artery 1 This is Hemispatial Neglect ignoring the Left side . The lesion is in the Right Parietal Lobe supplied by the Right MCA . Damage to Frontal Area 8 would most likely result in a deficit of Language comprehension Face recognition Voluntary saccadic eye movements Hearing Fine motor control of the hand 2 Frontal Area 8 corresponds to the Frontal Eye Fields which control voluntary rapid eye movements saccades . Role of Insula Taste Gustatory & Disgust / Interoception Cortex Lesion causing Hemispatial Neglect Right Posterior Parietal Lobe Right MCA Neurology Lesion causing Gerstmann Syndrome Left Angular Gyrus Dominant Parietal Neurology Symptoms of Gerstmann Syndrome Agraphia Acalculia Finger Agnosia L-R Disorientation Neurology Gene associated with Frontotemporal Dementia MAPT Tau protein Pathology Symptoms of Frontotemporal Dementia Disinhibition Apathy Personality Change Pathology Function of Fusiform Face Area Facial Recognition Lesion = Prosopagnosia Cortex Function of Frontal Eye Fields Area 8 Voluntary Saccades Physiology Layer of Neocortex with Betz Cells Layer 5 Output to Spinal Cord Histology Cingulotomy is a treatment for... Chronic Pain or OCD Psychiatry AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cerebral Cortex FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Lobes BandB Neurology > Cerebrum Gerstmann Syndrome Tetrad Lesion of Left Angular Gyrus .\\\\n1. Agraphia\\\\n2. Acalculia\\\\n3. Finger Agnosia\\\\n4. L-R Disorientation Hemispatial Neglect Lesion of Right Parietal Lobe Non-dominant .\\\\nPatient neglects the Left side of the world. Frontotemporal Dementia FTD Behavioral variant. Disinhibition Apathy Personality change.\\\\n MAPT gene Tau . Insula Functions Taste Disgust Interoception Addiction Cravings Smoking . Prosopagnosia Face blindness. Lesion of Fusiform Face Area Temporal Lobe . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l29", "title": "Lecture 29: Neocortical Development", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l29 Lecture 29 Neocortical Development\\n\\n Lecturer R. Ramos Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 29\\n\\n 1. The 3 P s of Brain Development\\n1. Production Proliferation Making neurons.\\n2. Placement Migration Moving neurons to correct layers.\\n3. Performance Synaptic function.\\n\\n 2. Production Proliferation \\n Location Ventricular Zone VZ .\\n Mechanism Radial Glial cells divide to produce neurons.\\n Pathology \\n Primary Microcephaly \\ Small brain\\ . Reduced proliferation.\\n Genes ASPM Abnormal Spindle-like Microcephaly Citron Kinase .\\n Hemi-megalencephaly One hemisphere is too large. excess proliferation. Risk of Epilepsy .\\n\\n 3. Placement Migration \\n Pattern Inside-Out . Oldest neurons are deep Layer 6 Youngest are superficial Layer 2 .\\n Guidance Radial Glia fibers guide neurons. Reelin secreted by Cajal-Retzius cells in Layer 1 tells them when to stop.\\n Pathology neuronal migration disorders \\n Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia PVH Neurons fail to leave VZ. Nodules lining ventricles.\\n Gene Filamin A .\\n Subcortical Band Heterotopia Double Cortex Neurons stuck in white matter Intermediate Zone .\\n Genes DCX LIS1 .\\n Lissencephaly \\ Smooth Brain\\ No gyri Agyria .\\n Gene Reelin mutations.\\n Molecular Layer Heterotopia Neurons migrate too far past Layer 2 into Layer 1 . Associated with Dyslexia and Epilepsy .\\n\\n 4. Gyrification\\n Timeline Large fissures Sylvian form first 14w . Frontal gyri form last. Lecture 29 Neocortical Development Neuroscience questions A newborn MRI shows a smooth brain surface with no gyri or sulci. What is the most likely diagnosis? Hemi-megalencephaly Lissencephaly Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Double Cortex Syndrome Porencephaly 1 Lissencephaly literally means Smooth Brain . It is a severe migration disorder where gyri fail to form Agyria . Which cell type serves as the scaffold for neuronal migration in the developing neocortex? Astrocytes Oligodendrocytes Radial Glia Microglia Schwann Cells 2 Radial Glia extend processes from the ventricle to the pial surface acting as train tracks for migrating neurons. An MRI reveals nodules of gray matter lining the lateral ventricles. Which gene mutation is most strongly associated with this finding? Reelin Filamin A DCX ASPM MAPT 1 This describes Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia PVH . The classic association is Filamin A an actin-binding protein needed for migration initiation . In the Inside-Out pattern of cortical development which layer is formed LAST? Layer 6 Layer 5 Layer 4 Layer 2 Subplate 3 Deep layers 6 form first. Superficial layers 2 form last. Layer 1 is the marginal zone and exists early but the cortical plate neurons fill in 6→2 . Molecular Layer Heterotopia neurons migrating too far is often found in the brains of patients with Parkinson s Disease Dyslexia ALS Huntington s Disease Multiple Sclerosis 1 Small clusters of neurons in Layer 1 Molecular Layer Heterotopia are seen in Dyslexia and Epilepsy . Inside-Out Migration meaning Deep layers 6 formed first Superficial 2 formed last Embryology Radial Glia function Progenitor cells + Scaffold for migration Embryology Gene for Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Filamin A Pathology Gene for Double Cortex Syndrome DCX / LIS1 Pathology Gene for Microcephaly ASPM / Citron Kinase Pathology Cells that secrete Reelin Cajal-Retzius Cells Layer 1 Embryology Lissencephaly means... Smooth Brain Agyria Pathology Molecular Layer Heterotopia association Dyslexia / Epilepsy Pathology Ventricular Zone function Site of neuronal proliferation Embryology 3 P s of Brain Dev Production Placement Performance Embryology AMBOSS Pediatrics > Neurology > Congenital anomalies FirstAid Neurology > Embryology Inside-Out Migration Neocortex forms Inside-Out . Layer 6 is born first. Layer 2 is born last. Double Cortex Syndrome Also called Subcortical Band Heterotopia . A band of gray matter is stuck in the white matter between the cortex and ventricle. Reelin Function Secreted by Cajal-Retzius cells in the Marginal Zone Layer 1 . Acts as a Stop signal for migrating neurons. Primary Microcephaly Genes ASPM and Citron Kinase . Defects in mitosis/cytokinesis. Filamin A Mutation Causes Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Neurons stuck at the starting line . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l30", "title": "Lecture 30: Supraspinal Motor Systems (Cortex & Brainstem)", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l30 Lecture 30 Supraspinal Motor Systems Cortex & Brainstem \\n\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 30\\n\\n 1. Pyramidal System Upper Motor Neurons \\n Definition UMNs originate in the Cortex M1 Premotor SMA and project to LMNs in the Brainstem or Spinal Cord.\\n Corticospinal Tract CST \\n Lateral CST 85% Decussates at Medullary Pyramids. Controls Distal Limbs Fine motor .\\n Ventral CST 15% Descends ipsilaterally decussates in spinal cord. Controls Axial/Proximal muscles Posture .\\n Corticobulbar Tract \\n Controls Cranial Nerves Head/Neck .\\n Innervation Most CN nuclei receive Bilateral input.\\n Exceptions \\n CN VII Face Lower Face receives only Contralateral input. Upper Face is Bilateral .\\n CN XII Tongue Tongue deviates AWAY from the UMN lesion towards the weak side .\\n\\n 2. UMN vs. LMN Lesions\\n\\n| Feature | UMN Lesion Cortex/Stem | LMN Lesion Nerve/Root |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Tone | Spastic Increased | Flaccid Decreased |\\n| Reflexes | Hyperreflexia | Hyporeflexia |\\n| Babinski | Present + Toe up | Absent Toe down |\\n| Atrophy | Mild Disuse | Severe Denervation |\\n| Fasciculations | No | Yes |\\n\\n 3. Brainstem Tracts & Posturing\\n Rubrospinal Tract Originates in Red Nucleus Midbrain . Flexor tones of upper limbs.\\n Vestibulospinal Tract Extensor tone Antigravity .\\n Decorticate Posture \\ Mummy\\ \\n Lesion ABOVE Red Nucleus e.g. Cerebral Hemispheres .\\n Presentation Arms Flexed Red nucleus intact Legs Extended.\\n Decerebrate Posture \\n Lesion BELOW Red Nucleus e.g. Pons/Midbrain .\\n Presentation Arms Extended Legs Extended Rubrospinal lost Vestibulospinal unopposed .\\n\\n 4. Specific Clinical Syndromes\\n Locked-In Syndrome \\n Lesion Pons Basilar Artery .\\n Effect Bilateral CST/Corticobulbar damage. Quadriplegia + Anarthria can t speak .\\n Spared Vertical eye movements/Blinking Midbrain/CN III spared . Consciousness intact.\\n Bell s Palsy LMN CN VII Paralysis of Entire Hemi-face Forehead + Chin .\\n Stroke UMN CN VII Paralysis of Lower Face Only Forehead spared . Lecture 30 Supraspinal Motor Systems Cortex & Brainstem Neurology questions A patient presents with right-sided facial weakness involving the forehead and chin. What is the most likely location of the lesion? Left Motor Cortex Face area Left Internal Capsule Right Facial Nerve CN VII Left Facial Nucleus Right Trigeminal Nerve 2 Weakness of the entire hemiface forehead + chin indicates an LMN lesion Bell s Palsy or CN VII injury on the same side . A comatose patient exhibits extension of both the upper and lower extremities when stimulated. This posturing suggests a lesion located Above the Red Nucleus Below the Red Nucleus e.g. Pons In the Spinal Cord In the Thalamus In the Cerebellum 1 Extension of all limbs is Decerebrate posturing which occurs with lesions below the Red Nucleus rubrospinal tract lost . Which of the following is a classic sign of an Upper Motor Neuron UMN lesion? Fasciculations Severe Atrophy Hyporeflexia Babinski Sign Flaccidity 3 Babinski sign extensor plantar response is a hallmark of UMN dysfunction. The others are LMN signs. A patient suffers a stroke affecting the Left Primary Motor Cortex. The tongue deviates to which side upon protrusion? Left Right Does not deviate Upward Downward 1 With a UMN lesion Left Cortex the tongue deviates AWAY from the lesion towards the weak side . So it points to the Right .\\\\n Wait let me double check my pearl... LMN = Toward. UMN = Away. Correct. So Left Cortex lesion → Right Genioglossus weak → Tongue points Right. Correct Answer is Right. Options Left/Right . Which tract is primarily responsible for voluntary fine motor control of the distal upper extremities? Ventral Corticospinal Tract Lateral Corticospinal Tract Vestibulospinal Tract Reticulospinal Tract Tectospinal Tract 1 The Lateral Corticospinal Tract controls distal muscles hands/fingers . The Ventral tract controls axial muscles. UMN Lesion Signs Spasticity Hyperreflexia Babinski + Neurology LMN Lesion Signs Flaccidity Atrophy Fasciculations Neurology Decorticate Posture Arms Flexed Mummy . Lesion Above Red Nucleus. Neurology Decerebrate Posture Arms Extended. Lesion Below Red Nucleus. Neurology Bell s Palsy vs Stroke Face Bell s LMN = Whole Face. Stoke UMN = Lower Face. Neurology Lateral Corticospinal Tract Decussation Medullary Pyramids Neuroanatomy Rubrospinal Tract Origin Red Nucleus Midbrain Neuroanatomy Locked-In Syndrome Lesion Pons Basilar Artery Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Pyramidal & Extrapyramidal FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Spinal Tracts UMN vs LMN Face UMN Stroke Spares forehead Look for wrinkles! .\\\\n LMN Bell s Affects whole face Forehead smooth . Decorticate vs Decerebrate De-COR-ticate Arms like C s Flexed to Cor e . Lesion Above Red Nucleus.\\\\n De-cere-brate Arms like E s Extended . Lesion Below Red Nucleus Worse prognosis . Tongue Deviation Lick your lesions. \\\\n LMN Tongue deviates TOWARD lesion.\\\\n UMN Tongue deviates AWAY from lesion. Locked-In Syndrome Pontine Stroke. Quadriplegia. Only eyes move Vertical . Consciousness preserved. Pyramidal Decussation Occurs at the Cervicomedullary Junction Lower Medulla . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l31", "title": "Lecture 31: Basal Ganglia", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l31 Lecture 31 Basal Ganglia\\n\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 31\\n\\n 1. Basal Ganglia Anatomy\\n Striatum Input Caudate + Putamen. Releases GABA .\\n Pallidum Output GPi Internal & GPe External . Releases GABA .\\n Subthalamic Nucleus STN Releases Glutamate Excitatory .\\n Substantia Nigra pars Compacta SNc Releases Dopamine .\\n\\n 2. The Pathways Albin-DeLong Model \\n1. Direct Pathway GO \\n Promotes movement.\\n Striatum inhibits GPi → Disinhibition of Thalamus.\\n D1 Receptors Dopamine excites this pathway.\\n2. Indirect Pathway NO-GO \\n Inhibits movement.\\n Striatum inhibits GPe → STN active → GPi active → Thalamus inhibited.\\n D2 Receptors Dopamine inhibits this pathway Releases the brake .\\n3. Hyperdirect Pathway Fast suppression Freeze! . Cortex → STN → GPi.\\n\\n 3. Parkinson s Disease Hypokinetic \\n Pathology Loss of Dopaminergic neurons in SNc . Lewy Bodies $\\\\alpha$-synuclein .\\n Symptoms TRAP \\n T remor Resting Pill-rolling .\\n R igidity Cogwheel .\\n A kinesia / Bradykinesia Slow movement Masked face .\\n P ostural Instability.\\n History MPTP toxin caused acute Parkinsonism in drug users \\ Frozen Addicts\\ .\\n\\n 4. Huntington s Disease Hyperkinetic \\n Pathology Atrophy of Caudate Nucleus Boxy ventricles on MRI . loss of GABA/ACh neurons.\\n Genetics CAG trinucleotide repeat >40 . Autosomal Dominant . Anticipation worse in next gen .\\n Symptoms Chorea Dance-like Dementia Depression. Signs appear age 30-50.\\n\\n 5. Other Disorders\\n Hemiballismus \\n Lesion Subthalamic Nucleus STN lacunar stroke .\\n Symptoms Wild ballistic flailing of contralateral arm/leg.\\n Tardive Dyskinesia \\n Cause Chronic use of Antipsychotics D2 blockers .\\n Symptoms Repetitive lip smacking tongue rolling. Late onset. Lecture 31 Basal Ganglia Neurology questions A patient presents with wild uncontrollable flailing movements of the right arm and leg. An MRI would most likely show a lacunar stroke in which structure? Left Substantia Nigra Left Subthalamic Nucleus Right Subthalamic Nucleus Left Caudate Right Putamen 1 Hemiballismus contralateral flailing is caused by a lesion in the Subthalamic Nucleus STN . Right movements = Left STN. Which neurotransmitter is primarily released by the neurons of the Subthalamic Nucleus? GABA Dopamine Glutamate Acetylcholine Serotonin 2 The STN is the only intrinsic BG nucleus that is excitatory releasing Glutamate . All others Striatum GPe GPi SNr release GABA. In Huntington s Disease which brain structure undergoes the most significant atrophy leading to ventriculomegaly? Substantia Nigra Caudate Nucleus Thalamus Hippocampus Cerebellum 1 Huntington s causes atrophy of the Striatum specifically the Caudate Nucleus leading to boxy dilated ventricles. A 65-year-old male presents with a resting tremor masked facies and a shuffling gait. Histology would likely reveal Neurofibrillary Tangles Lewy Bodies Amyloid Plaques Negri Bodies Pick Bodies 1 This is Parkinson s Disease. The hallmark histology is Lewy Bodies alpha-synuclein in the Substantia Nigra. Dopamine binding to D2 receptors in the striatum results in Excitation of the Indirect Pathway Inhibition of the Indirect Pathway Excitation of the Direct Pathway Inhibition of the Direct Pathway Inhibition of the Thalamus 1 D2 receptors are inhibitory . They are on the Indirect No-Go pathway. So Dopamine inhibits the Indirect pathway essentially turning off the stop signal which promotes movement. Hemiballismus Lesion Contralateral Subthalamic Nucleus STN Neurology Parkinson s Pathology Loss of Dopamine in SNc + Lewy Bodies Pathology Huntington s Genetic Defect CAG Repeat >40 on Ch 4 Genetics Direct Pathway Function Promotes Movement Disinhibits Thalamus Physiology Neurotransmitter of Striatum GABA Neuroscience Neurotransmitter of STN Glutamate Neuroscience Tardive Dyskinesia Cause Chronic D2 Blockers Antipsychotics Pharmacology MPTP causes... Parkinsonism Destroys SNc Pathology AMBOSS Neurology > Pathology > Degenerative diseases FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Movement Disorders Parkinson s TRAP T remor Resting \\\\n R igidity\\\\n A kinesia\\\\n P ostural Instability Huntington s CAG Mnemonic C audate lost\\\\n A Ch lost\\\\n G ABA lost\\\\n Also CAG Repeats Hemiballismus Lesion Damage to the Subthalamic Nucleus STN causes contralateral flailing. Dopamine Receptors D1 = Direct Go .\\\\n D2 = Indirect No-Go .\\\\nDopamine stimulates D1 and inhibits D2 both resulting in Movement . MPTP Toxin Destroys SNc neurons causing rapid permanent Parkinsonism. Synthetic heroin case . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l32", "title": "Lecture 32: Cerebellum", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l32 Lecture 32 Cerebellum\\n\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 32\\n\\n 1. Neuroanatomy\\n Organization Cortex 3 layers covering Deep Nuclei.\\n Deep Nuclei Lateral to Medial \\ D on t E at G reasy F ood\\ .\\n D entate.\\n E mboliform.\\n G lobose.\\n F astigial.\\n Circuits \\n Inputs \\n Mossy Fibers From Spinal cord/Brainstem. Excitatory Glutamate .\\n Climbing Fibers From Inferior Olive . Excitatory Glutamate .\\n Processing \\n Purkinje Cells The sole output of the cerebellar cortex. Releases GABA Inhibitory onto Deep Nuclei.\\n Output \\n Deep Nuclei send final signal to Thalamus/Brainstem.\\n\\n 2. Functional Divisions & Pathology\\n\\n| Division | Anatomy | Function | Lesion Symptoms |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Vestibulo-cerebellum | Flocculonodular Lobe | Balance Eye movements | Vertigo Nystagmus Wide-based gait Truncal Ataxia . |\\n| Spino-cerebellum | Vermis & Paravermis | Posture Trunk/Limb Gait | Ataxic Gait \\ Drunk walk\\ . Alcoholism damages Anterior Lobe Vermis . |\\n| Cerebro-cerebellum | Lateral Hemispheres | Fine motor planning | Intention Tremor Dysmetria Dysdiadochokinesia . |\\n\\n 3. Clinical Syndromes\\n Essential Tremor \\n Most common movement disorder. Familial Autosomal Dominant .\\n Postural/Action Tremor worse when holding a cup .\\n Note Improves with Alcohol . Often self-medicated .\\n Intention Tremor \\n Cerebellar lesion. Tremor worsens as you approach target .\\n Resting Tremor \\n Basal Ganglia Parkinson s .\\n\\n 4. DANISH Mnemonic Cerebellar Signs \\n D ysdiadochokinesia Can t do rapid alternating movements .\\n A taxia.\\n N ystagmus.\\n I ntention Tremor.\\n S peech Slurred/Scanning .\\n H ypotonia. Lecture 32 Cerebellum Neurology questions A chronic alcoholic presents with a wide-based staggering gait but normal arm coordination. Which region of the cerebellum is most likely degenerated? Flocculonodular Lobe Anterior Lobe Vermis Lateral Hemisphere Dentate Nucleus Inferior Olive 1 Chronic alcoholism typically causes atrophy of the Anterior Lobe Vermis leading to Truncal Ataxia and gait issues while sparing the limbs Lateral Hemispheres . Which of the following describes the output of Purkinje cells? Excitatory Glutamate to Deep Nuclei Inhibitory GABA to Deep Nuclei Excitatory Glutamate to Thalamus Inhibitory GABA to Thalamus Excitatory Acetylcholine to Spinal Cord 1 Purkinje Cells provide Inhibitory GABA input to the Deep Cerebellar Nuclei modulating the final output. A patient cannot rapidly supinate and pronate their hand Dysdiadochokinesia on the left side. Where is the lesion? Right Cerebellar Hemisphere Left Cerebellar Hemisphere Vermis Right Motor Cortex Right Basal Ganglia 1 Cerebellar symptoms are Ipsilateral . Dysdiadochokinesia limb coordination maps to the Lateral Hemisphere . So Left hand issue = Left Cerebellar Hemisphere . A child presents with frequent falls nystagmus and vomiting. An MRI reveals a tumor in the roof of the 4th ventricle compressing the flocculonodular lobe. What is the most likely diagnosis? Glioblastoma Multiforme Meningioma Medulloblastoma Schwannoma Pituitary Adenoma 2 Medulloblastoma is a common pediatric tumor that often arises in the Vermis/Flocculonodular lobe causing truncal ataxia and hydrocephalus vomiting . Climbing fibers which provide powerful excitatory input to Purkinje cells originate from which structure? Spinal Cord Pontine Nuclei Inferior Olive Red Nucleus Vestibular Nuclei 2 Climbing Fibers originate exclusively from the Inferior Olive Medulla . Deep Cerebellar Nuclei Lat to Med Dentate Emboliform Globose Fastigial Neuroanatomy Purkinje Cell Neurotransmitter GABA Inhibitory Neuroscience Climbing Fibers Origin Inferior Olive Neuroanatomy Lesion causing Dysdiadochokinesia Ipsilateral Lateral Cerebellar Hemisphere Neurology Classic sign of Cerebrocerebellar lesion Intention Tremor Neurology Sign of Vermis Lesion Truncal Ataxia Drunk Walk Neurology Essential Tremor characteristics Action/Postural. Improves w/ Alcohol. Neurology DANISH meaning Dysdiadochokinesia Ataxia Nystagmus Intention Tremor Slurred Speech Hypotonia Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cerebellum FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cerebellum Deep Nuclei Mnemonic D on t E at G reasy F ood Lateral to Medial \\\\n D entate E mboliform G lobose F astigial. Cerebellar Hemisphere vs Vermis Lateral lesions affect Limbs fall Toward side of lesion .\\\\n Medial Vermis lesions affect Trunk Truncal Ataxia fall anywhere . Alcoholic Cerebellar Degeneration Chronic alcohol use specifically damages the Anterior Lobe Vermis causing a wide-based ataxic gait. Essential Tremor Action tremor eating/writing . Improves with Alcohol. Tx Beta-blockers. Output of Cerebellar Cortex Purkinje Cells are the ONLY output of the cortex. They are Inhibitory GABA . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l33", "title": "Lecture 33: Sympathomimetics I - Direct Agonists", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l33 Lecture 33 Sympathomimetics I - Direct Agonists\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 33\\n\\n 1. Receptors & Physiology\\n $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ Gq Vasoconstriction Skin/Splanchnic Mydriasis Pupil Dilation Urinary Sphincter Contraction.\\n $\\\\\\\\alpha 2$ Gi Presynaptic Inhibition Decreases NE release .\\n $\\\\\\\\beta 1$ Gs Heart $\\\\\\\\uparrow$ HR $\\\\\\\\uparrow$ Contractility Kidney $\\\\\\\\uparrow$ Renin .\\n $\\\\\\\\beta 2$ Gs Bronchodilation Vasodilation Skeletal Muscle Uterine Relaxation Tocolysis .\\n $\\\\\\\\beta 3$ Gs Bladder Detrusor Relaxation.\\n\\n 2. Direct-Acting Sympathomimetics\\n\\n| Drug | Receptors | Action | Clinical Use |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Epinephrine | $\\\\\\\\alpha \\\\\\\\beta$ | Low Dose $\\\\\\\\beta 2$ Hypotension . High Dose $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ Hyperstension . | Anaphylaxis DOC Cardiac Arrest. |\\n| Norepinephrine | $\\\\\\\\alpha 1 = \\\\\\\\alpha 2 > \\\\\\\\beta 1$ | Potent Vasoconstriction. Reflex Bradycardia. | Septic Shock DOC . |\\n| Dopamine | D1 > $\\\\\\\\beta 1$ > $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ | Low Renal Vasodilation. Med Inotropy. High Vasoconstriction. | Shock Renal sparing theoretically Heart Failure. |\\n| Dobutamine | $\\\\\\\\beta 1 > \\\\\\\\beta 2$ | $\\\\\\\\uparrow$ Inotropy > Chronotropy. | Heart Failure Decompensated Stress Testing. |\\n| Phenylephrine | $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ | Vasoconstriction. | Nasal Decongestant Hypotension Mydriasis. |\\n| Isoproterenol | $\\\\\\\\beta 1 = \\\\\\\\beta 2$ | $\\\\\\\\uparrow$ HR Vasodilation $\\\\\\\\downarrow$ DBP . | Bradycardia Heart block Rarely used . |\\n\\n 3. Hemodynamics Key Concepts \\n Mean Arterial Pressure MAP $\\\\\\\\text CO \\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\text TPR $.\\n Pulse Pressure $\\\\\\\\text Systolic - \\\\\\\\text Diastolic $.\\n Epinephrine Reversal Giving an $\\\\\\\\alpha$-blocker before Epinephrine converts its pressor effect $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ to a depressor effect $\\\\\\\\beta 2$ unmasked → Hypotension.\\n Reflex Bradycardia Pure $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ agonists Phenylephrine/Norepinephrine cause massive vasoconstriction → $\\\\\\\\uparrow$ BP → Baroreceptors fire → Reflex $\\\\\\\\downarrow$ HR.\\n\\n 4. Clinical Pearls\\n Anaphylaxis Epinephrine is the ONLY first-line drug. Prevents shock $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ opens airway $\\\\\\\\beta 2$ stops mast cells.\\n Septic Shock Norepinephrine is first-line to restore MAP.\\n Tachyphylaxis Rapidly diminishing response to successive doses seen with Indirect agonists like Tyramine/Ephedrine less so with direct . Lecture 33 Sympathomimetics I - Direct Agonists Neurology questions A patient in septic shock requires a vasopressor to maintain Mean Arterial Pressure MAP . Which agent is the first-line choice due to its potent alpha-1 and beta-1 activity with minimal beta-2 effects? Epinephrine Dopamine Norepinephrine Phenylephrine Isoproterenol 2 Norepinephrine is the Drug of Choice DOC for Septic Shock. It provides strong vasoconstriction alpha-1 and some inotropy beta-1 without the hypotension associated with beta-2 stimulation. A patient is given a drug that increases Systolic BP decreases Diastolic BP and increases Heart Rate. Which drug fits this profile? Phenylephrine Norepinephrine Isoproterenol Clonidine Propranolol 2 Isoproterenol is a non-selective Beta agonist Beta-1 and Beta-2 . Beta-1 increases SBP and HR. Beta-2 causes vasodilation dropping DBP. Phenylephrine is administered intravenously. What represents the expected cardiovascular response? Increased BP Increased HR Increased BP Decreased HR Decreased BP Increased HR Decreased BP Decreased HR No change in BP Increased HR 1 Phenylephrine is a pure Alpha-1 agonist. It increases TPR and BP. The body responds with Reflex Bradycardia Decreased HR . Which receptor subtype is primarily responsible for the bronchodilatory effects of Epinephrine used in anaphylaxis? Alpha-1 Alpha-2 Beta-1 Beta-2 Dopamine-1 3 Beta-2 receptors mediate bronchodilation and skeletal muscle vasodilation . Alpha-1 mediates the vasoconstriction that supports BP. Dobutamine is primarily used in the setting of Anaphylactic Shock Septic Shock Decompensated Heart Failure Hypertension Asthma 2 Dobutamine Beta-1 > Beta-2 is an inotrope used to increase cardiac output in Decompensated Heart Failure or cardiogenic shock. Epinephrine Receptor Profile Alpha-1 Alpha-2 Beta-1 Beta-2 Pharmacology Norepinephrine Receptor Profile Alpha-1 > Alpha-2 > Beta-1 NO Beta-2 Pharmacology Phenylephrine Receptor Profile Pure Alpha-1 Agonist Pharmacology Isoproterenol Receptor Profile Beta-1 = Beta-2 Non-selective Beta Pharmacology Dobutamine Use Heart Failure Inotropy Pharmacology Drug of Choice for Anaphylaxis Epinephrine Pharmacology Drug of Choice for Septic Shock Norepinephrine Pharmacology Reflex response to Phenylephrine Bradycardia Decreased HR Pharmacology AMBOSS Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs > Sympathomimetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs Epinephrine Dosage Effects Low Dose = Beta-2 Dominant Vasodilation/Hypotension .\\\\n High Dose = Alpha-1 Dominant Vasoconstriction/Hypertension . Norepinephrine vs Epinephrine Norepinephrine has NO Beta-2 activity. It causes pure vasoconstriction and reflex bradycardia. Epinephrine has Beta-2 activity Bronchodilation . Dopamine Dosing Low Renal D1.\\\\n Medium Cardiac Beta-1.\\\\n High Pressor Alpha-1. Phenylephrine Reflex A pure Alpha-1 agonist Phenylephrine increases BP but causes Reflex Bradycardia lowers HR . Beta-1 Selectivity Dobutamine is a Beta-1 agonist used for Heart Failure to increase contractility without massively increasing Heart Rate Chronotropy . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l34", "title": "Lecture 34: Sympathomimetics II - Indirect & Mixed Acting", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l34 Lecture 34 Sympathomimetics II - Indirect & Mixed Acting\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 34\\n\\n 1. Beta-2 Agonists Lungs & Uterus \\n SABA Short-Acting Albuterol Levalbuterol.\\n Use Acute Asthma/COPD exacerbation \\ Rescue\\ .\\n LABA Long-Acting Salmeterol Formoterol.\\n Use Chronic maintenance. Always combine with Steroids ICS in asthma to prevent mortality.\\n Tocolytics Terbutaline .\\n Use Relaxes uterine smooth muscle to delay preterm labor allows time for corticosteroids to mature fetal lungs .\\n\\n 2. Beta-3 Agonists Bladder \\n Mirabegron \\n Action Relaxes Detrusor muscle.\\n Use Overactive Bladder OAB . Alternative to Anticholinergics .\\n\\n 3. Alpha-2 Agonists Central Sympatholytics \\nReduces sympathetic outflow from the brainstem.\\n Clonidine \\n Uses Hypertension resistant Opioid Withdrawal ADHD.\\n Adverse Rebound Hypertension if stopped abruptly. dry mouth sedation.\\n Methyldopa \\n Use Hypertension in Pregnancy Safe for fetus .\\n Adverse Coombs+ Hemolytic Anemia Autoimmune Drug-induced Lupus.\\n\\n 4. Indirect & Mixed Acting\\n Amphetamines Adderall \\n Mechanism Displaces NE/DA from vesicles into synapse Reverse transport .\\n Use ADHD Narcolepsy.\\n Cocaine \\n Mechanism Blocks Reuptake NET/DAT .\\n Effect Vasoconstriction Local Anesthetic + Pressor .\\n Ephedrine \\n Mechanism Mixed Direct Agonist + Indirect Release .\\n Use Hypotension during Anesthesia.\\n Pseudoephedrine \\n Use Nasal Decongestant. Precursor for Meth hence restricted .\\n\\n 5. MAO Inhibitors The Cheese Effect \\n Mechanism Inhibits Monoamine Oxidase enzyme that breaks down NE/DA/Serotonin .\\n Tyramine Interaction Tyramine found in Aged Cheese Wine Cured Meats releases stored NE. Commonly degraded by GI MAO.\\n Crisis If on MAO-I Tyramine is NOT degraded → Massive NE release → Hypertensive Crisis . Lecture 34 Sympathomimetics II - Indirect & Mixed Acting Neurology questions A pregnant woman presents with chronic hypertension. Which centrally acting alpha-2 agonist is considered safe and appropriate for management? Clonidine Methyldopa Lisinopril Losartan Propranolol 1 Methyldopa is the classic safe antihypertensive for pregnancy. ACE inhibitors Lisinopril and ARBs are teratogenic. A patient on a Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitor MAOI for depression consumes a charcuterie board with aged cheese and wine. They present to the ER with a severe headache and BP of 220/120. mechanism is Direct Alpha-1 agonism by Tyramine Displacement of stored Norepinephrine by Tyramine Blockade of Norepinephrine reuptake Allergic reaction to wine Serotonin Syndrome 1 Tyramine acts as an indirect sympathomimetic by displacing stored NE from vesicles. Usually broken down by MAO but persists in MAO-I patients. Which drug works by relaxing the detrusor muscle via Beta-3 agonism to treat overactive bladder? Oxybutynin Mirabegron Bethanecol Tamsulosin Finasteride 1 Mirabegron is a Beta-3 agonist. Oxybutynin is an Anticholinergic which also works but via M3 blockade . Amphetamines exert their stimulant effect primarily by Directly activating Alpha-1 receptors Blocking the degradation of Acetylcholine Displacing Norepinephrine and Dopamine from vesicles into the synapse Blocking the reuptake of Serotonin only Directly activating Beta-2 receptors 2 Amphetamines enter the nerve terminal via uptake and then displace NE/DA from vesicles reversing the transporter to pump NE/DA OUT into the synapse. Salmeterol is classified as a Short-Acting Beta Agonist SABA Long-Acting Beta Agonist LABA Alpha-1 Antagonist Muscarinic Antagonist Corticosteroid 1 Salmeterol is a LABA used for asthma maintenance. Albuterol is a SABA. Methyldopa Indication Hypertension in Pregnancy Pharmacology Methyldopa Adverse Effect Coombs + Hemolytic Anemia Pharmacology Clonidine Mechanism Central Alpha-2 Agonist Decreases Sympathetic Outflow Pharmacology Mirabegron Target Beta-3 Receptor Bladder Relaxation Pharmacology Terbutaline Use Tocolysis Delay Labor Pharmacology Tyramine Mechanism Displaces Stored NE Indirect Agonist Pharmacology Cocaine Mechanism Blocks Reuptake NET/DAT Pharmacology Albuterol vs Salmeterol Albuterol = SABA Acute . Salmeterol = LABA Maintenance . Pharmacology AMBOSS Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs > Sympathomimetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs Methyldopa in Pregnancy Methyldopa is the classic choice for Hypertension in Pregnancy Preeclampsia prophylaxis . Watch for Hemolytic Anemia . Clonidine Rebound Never stop Clonidine abruptly. Causes massive Rebound Hypertension due to upregulated receptors. Cocaine vs Amphetamine Cocaine BLOCKS reuptake NET blocker .\\\\n Amphetamine REVERSES transporter Displaces NE . Both increase synaptic NE . Cheese Reaction MAO Inhibitors + Tyramine Wine/Cheese = Hypertensive Crisis. Tyramine displaces NE . Terbutaline Tocolysis Beta-2 agonist used to Stop Labor Relax Uterus . Turbulence delays the flight birth . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l35", "title": "Lecture 35: Cranial Nerves I, II, III, IV, VI (Eye Movements)", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l35 Lecture 35 Cranial Nerves I II III IV VI Eye Movements \\n\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 35\\n\\n 1. CN I Olfactory & CN II Optic \\n CN I Visceral Sensory. Bypasses Thalamus.\\n Pathology Anosmia Early sign of Parkinson s - Lewy bodies in Olfactory Bulb .\\n CN II Special Sensory.\\n Reflex Pupillary Light Reflex Afferent II → Pretectal Area → Bilateral Edinger-Westphal → Efferent III .\\n\\n 2. CN III Oculomotor \\n Motor Medial Superior Inferior Recti; Inferior Oblique; Levator Palpebrae.\\n Parasympathetic Edinger-Westphal Nucleus → Pupil Constriction Miosis & Lens Accommodation.\\n Lesion CN III Palsy \\n \\ Down and Out\\ eye Unopposed SO4/LR6 .\\n Ptosis Droopy eyelid .\\n Mydriasis Blown pupil - loss of parasympathetics .\\n Note The Superior Rectus and Levator are innervated by the Contralateral nucleus but the nerve fibers cross inside the complex so a NERVE lesion affects the ipsilateral eye.\\n\\n 3. CN IV Trochlear \\n Motor Superior Oblique Depresses/Intorts eye .\\n Unique Features \\n 1. Only CN to exit Dorsally .\\n 2. Only CN to Decussate Completely Right Nucleus → Left Superior Oblique .\\n Lesion \\n Vertical Diplopia Worse when looking down e.g. stairs/reading .\\n Head Tilt Patient tilts head AWAY from the affected side to compensate/align eyes.\\n\\n 4. CN VI Abducens & Gaze Control\\n Motor Lateral Rectus Abducts eye .\\n Horizontal Gaze Circuit \\n To look RIGHT Right PPRF → Right Abducens Nucleus .\\n Right Abducens Nucleus does TWO things \\n 1. Contracts Ring Lateral Rectus Abducts Right Eye .\\n 2. Sends signal via MLF Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus to Left Oculomotor Nucleus → Contracts Left Medial Rectus Adducts Left Eye .\\n Pathology \\n CN VI Palsy Esotropia Eye turns in .\\n INO Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia \\n Lesion of MLF .\\n Ipsilateral eye cannot Adduct Medial Rectus palsy .\\n Contralateral eye has Nystagmus.\\n Convergence is SPARED because CN III is intact .\\n Classic Cause Multiple Sclerosis Bilateral INO .\\n\\n 5. Summary Table\\n\\n| Nerve | Muscles | Lesion Sign |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| CN III | All others + Levator + Pupil | Down & Out Ptosis Blown Pupil |\\n| CN IV | Superior Oblique | Head tilt AWAY Vertical Diplopia stairs |\\n| CN VI | Lateral Rectus | Esotropia Eye turns In | Lecture 35 Cranial Nerves I II III IV VI Eye Movements Neurology questions A patient presents with vertical diplopia that worsens when walking down stairs. They are observed tilting their head to the left. Which cranial nerve is likely injured? Left CN IV Right CN IV Left CN III Right CN VI Left CN VI 2 Vertical diplopia stairs problem suggests CN IV Trochlear palsy. The head tilts AWAY from the lesion. Left tilt = Right CN IV lesion. A patient cannot look to the right with the right eye. When attempting to look right the left eye also fails to adduct. Convergence is intact. Where is the lesion? Right Abducens Nerve Right Abducens Nucleus Right MLF Left MLF Right PPRF 2 This is a Right Abducens Nucleus lesion. It knocks out the Right LR Can t look right AND the signal to the Left MR via MLF causing a gaze palsy to that side. A simple nerve lesion would only affect the Right LR . In Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia INO the lesion is located in the Abducens Nerve Oculomotor Nerve Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus MLF Superior Colliculus Lateral Geniculate Nucleus 2 INO is caused by damage to the MLF disconnecting the Abducens nucleus from the contralateral Oculomotor nucleus. Which cranial nerve contains the parasympathetic fibers responsible for pupillary constriction? Optic II Oculomotor III Trochlear IV Trigeminal V Abducens VI 1 CN III Oculomotor carries parasympathetics from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus to the ciliary ganglion for miosis and accommodation. Anosmia is an early and often overlooked clinical sign of which neurodegenerative disorder? Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Huntington s Disease ALS Multiple Sclerosis 1 Parkinson s Disease is strongly associated with early Anosmia due to Lewy Body deposition in the olfactory bulb. Eye Muscles Innervation Mnemonic SO4 LR6 All Others 3 Neuroanatomy CN IV Exit Point Dorsal Brainstem Midbrain Neuroanatomy CN III Palsy Signs Down & Out Ptosis Mydriasis Neurology INO Lesion Location Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus MLF Neurology Head Tilt in CN IV Palsy Away from Lesion Neurology Vertical Diplopia Cause CN IV Palsy Neurology Parasympathetic Nucleus for Eye Edinger-Westphal Nucleus Neuroanatomy Abducens Nucleus Function Abducts Ipsilateral Eye + Adducts Contralateral Eye via MLF Neurophysiology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cranial Nerves SO4 LR6 All The Rest 3 S uperior O blique = CN 4 .\\\\n L ateral R ectus = CN 6 .\\\\nAll other eye muscles = CN 3 . CN IV Trochlear Trivia The only cranial nerve to exit from the BACK Dorsal of the brainstem. Also the only one to cross completely! INO Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia Lesion of MLF . Eye lags when looking towards nose Adduction deficit . The other eye shakes Nystagmus . Seen in MS . Parkinson s Early Sign Anosmia Loss of smell is often the FIRST sign of Parkinson s appearing years before motor symptoms. Head Tilt in CN IV Palsy Head tilts AWAY from the lesion. Right CN IV palsy = Left Head Tilt . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l36", "title": "Lecture 36: Cranial Nerves V, VII-XII (Brainstem Nuclei)", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l36 Lecture 36 Cranial Nerves V VII-XII Brainstem Nuclei \\n\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 36\\n\\n 1. CN V Trigeminal \\n Sensory Face \\n Mesencephalic Nucleus Proprioception Jaw jerk .\\n Principal Pontine Nucleus Discriminative Touch Face .\\n Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Pain & Temperature Face . Extends down to C-spine .\\n Motor V3 Muscles of Mastication Temporalis Masseter Pterygoids .\\n Clinical \\n Trigeminal Neuralgia \\ Suicide disease\\ . Sharp shooting pain.\\n Corneal Reflex Afferent V1 Ophthalmic → Efferent VII Facial .\\n\\n 2. CN VII Facial \\n Motor Muscles of Facial Expression Stapedius dampens sound Stylohyoid Digastric Post .\\n Parasympathetic Superior Salivatory Nucleus → Lacrimal Tears Submandibular/Sublingual Saliva .\\n Sensory Taste Anterior 2/3 of Tongue.\\n Clinical \\n Bell s Palsy LMN Ipsilateral paralysis of ENTIRE face Forehead involved . Hyperacusis Loss of taste.\\n UMN Lesion Stroke Contralateral paralysis of LOWER face only Forehead Spared due to bilateral innervation .\\n\\n 3. CN IX Glossopharyngeal & CN X Vagus \\n CN IX \\n Sensory Post 1/3 Tongue Taste/Feel Carotid Body/Sinus.\\n Motor Stylopharyngeus.\\n Reflex Gag Reflex Afferent .\\n CN X \\n Motor Nucleus Ambiguus → Pharynx/Larynx/Palate.\\n Visceral Dorsal Motor Nucleus → GI/Heart Parasymp .\\n Reflex Gag Reflex Efferent .\\n Lesion Uvula deviates AWAY from lesion. Hoarseness.\\n\\n 4. CN XI Accessory & CN XII Hypoglossal \\n CN XI Trapezius Shrug SCM Turn head . Lesion Shoulder droop cannot turn head away from lesion.\\n CN XII Tongue muscles. Lesion Tongue deviates TOWARD lesion \\ Lick your wounds\\ .\\n\\n 5. Brainstem Syndromes\\n Lateral Medullary Wallenberg Syndrome \\n Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery .\\n Nuclei \\n Ambiguus Dysphagia Hoarseness.\\n Vestibular Vertigo Nystagmus.\\n Spinal V Ipsilateral Facial Pain/Temp loss.\\n Spinothalamic Contralateral Body Pain/Temp loss.\\n Sympathetic Ipsilateral Horner s Syndrome.\\n Medial Medullary Syndrome \\n Artery ASA Anterior Spinal Artery .\\n Nuclei \\n Hypoglossal XII Tongue deviates to lesion.\\n Corticospinal Tract Contralateral Hemiparesis.\\n Medial Lemniscus Contralateral Proprioception/Vibration loss. Lecture 36 Cranial Nerves V VII-XII Brainstem Nuclei Neurology questions A patient presents with sudden onset of right-sided facial drooping involving the forehead and the lower face. They also complain that sounds seem abnormally loud in the right ear. The most likely location of the lesion is Left Motor Cortex Right Internal Capsule Right Facial Nerve Left Facial Nerve Right Trigeminal Nerve 2 Right Facial Nerve LMN lesion causes Bell s Palsy Whole hemiface paralysis and Hyperacusis loss of Stapedius . A patient sticks out their tongue and it deviates to the LEFT. There is atrophy of the left side of the tongue. Which nerve is injured? Right CN XII Left CN XII Right CN X Left CN X Left CN VII 1 The tongue deviates TOWARD the side of the lesion Lick your wounds . Left deviation + Atrophy = Left CN XII LMN lesion. In Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg which nucleus is responsible for the characteristic dysphagia and hoarseness? Solitary Nucleus Nucleus Ambiguus Dorsal Motor Nucleus of Vagus Hypoglossal Nucleus Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus 1 Nucleus Ambiguus provides motor innervation to the pharynx and larynx via IX and X . Damage causes dysphagia and hoarseness. Loss of the afferent limb of the corneal reflex would result from damage to which cranial nerve? CN II CN III CN V1 CN VII CN VIII 2 The afferent sensing limb of the corneal reflex is CN V1 Ophthalmic division of Trigeminal . The efferent blinking is CN VII. A patient has loss of pain and temperature sensation on the RIGHT side of the face and the LEFT side of the body. This crossed sensory deficit suggests a lesion in the Right Lateral Medulla Left Lateral Medulla Right Medial Medulla Left Medial Medulla Right Thalamus 0 Crossed findings Ipsi Face Contra Body are a hallmark of brainstem lesions. Right Lateral Medulla hits Right Spinal V Face and Right Spinothalamic carrying Left Body info . Corneal Reflex Limbs In V1. Out VII. Neurology Gag Reflex Limbs In IX. Out X. Neurology Tongue Deviation Rule Deviates TOWARD the lesion Lick your wounds Neurology Uvula Deviation Rule Deviates AWAY from the lesion Neurology Nucleus Ambiguus Function Motor to Pharynx/Larynx Swallowing/Speech - CN IX X XI Neuroanatomy Wallenberg Syndrome Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Neurology Bell s Palsy vs Stroke Face Bell s = Whole Face. Stroke = Lower Face only Forehead spared . Neurology Hyperacusis Nerve CN VII Stapedius muscle paralysis Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cranial Nerves Bell s Palsy vs Stroke Bell s LMN Can NOT wrinkle forehead Whole face paralyzed .\\\\n Stroke UMN CAN wrinkle forehead Forehead spared . Uvula vs Tongue Uvula CN X Deviates AWAY from lesion.\\\\n Tongue CN XII Deviates TOWARD lesion. Corneal Reflex In CN V1 Feels the cotton .\\\\n Out CN VII Blinks the eye . Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg PICA stroke. Specific sign Dysphagia/Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus + Crossed Pain/Temp Face same side Body opposite side . Gag Reflex In CN IX Glossopharyngeal .\\\\n Out CN X Vagus . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l37", "title": "Lecture 37: Neurologic Exam II - Motor & Reflexes", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l37 Lecture 37 Neurologic Exam II - Motor & Reflexes\\n\\n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O.\\n Session Lecture 37\\n\\n 1. Motor System\\n Grading 0-5 Scale \\n 0 No contraction.\\n 1 Trace / Flicker.\\n 2 Active movement with gravity eliminated horizontal .\\n 3 Active movement against gravity only .\\n 4 Active movement against resistance but weak .\\n 5 Normal strength.\\n Key Roots \\n C5/6 Biceps Deltoid Brachioradialis.\\n C7 Triceps Wrist Extension.\\n C8/T1 Hand intrinsics Grip .\\n L2/3/4 Iliopsoas Hip Flex Quads Knee Ext .\\n L5 Tibialis Ant Dorsiflex/Heel walk .\\n S1 Gastroc Plantarflex/Toe walk .\\n\\n 2. Reflexes DTRs \\n Grading 0-4 Scale \\n 0 Absent.\\n 2 Normal.\\n 4 Hyperactive with Clonus UMN .\\n Roots \\n Biceps C5 C6.\\n Brachioradialis C6.\\n Triceps C7.\\n Patellar L4.\\n Achilles S1.\\n Reinforcement Jendrassik Maneuver hook fingers and pull distracts patient to elicit hard-to-find reflexes.\\n\\n 3. UMN vs LMN Findings\\n\\n| Feature | UMN Upper Motor Neuron | LMN Lower Motor Neuron |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Tone | Spastic Increased | Flaccid Decreased |\\n| Reflexes | Hyperreflexia +Clonus | Hyporeflexia / Absent |\\n| Babinski | Positive Upgoing toe | Negative Downgoing |\\n| Atrophy | Mild Disuse | Severe Denervation |\\n| Fasciculations | Absent | Present |\\n\\n 4. Coordination & Gait Cerebellum \\n Dysdiadochokinesis Inability to perform Rapid Alternating Movements RAM .\\n Dysmetria Overshooting/Undershooting Finger-to-Nose .\\n Ataxia Unsteady gait. Tandem Walking Heel-to-toe brings out subtle ataxia.\\n Romberg Test \\n Patient stands feet together eyes CLOSED .\\n Positive Patient sways/falls.\\n Meaning Loss of Proprioception Dorsal Columns . Note Cerebellar ataxia occurs with eyes OPEN too. \\n\\n 5. Other Signs\\n Pronator Drift Eyes closed arms out palms up. If one arm pronates and drops → Contralateral UMN Corticospinal weakness.\\n Primitive Reflexes Grasp Rooting Snout. Seen in Frontal Lobe lesions or babies . Lecture 37 Neurologic Exam II - Motor & Reflexes Neurology questions A patient presents with weakness in the right arm. On examination they have increased tone spasticity and hyperactive reflexes 3+ in the right biceps and triceps. There is no atrophy. This pattern suggests a lesion in the Lower Motor Neuron e.g. Brachial Plexus Upper Motor Neuron e.g. Corticospinal Tract Neuromuscular Junction Cerebellum Muscle fiber directly 1 UMN signs include Spasticity Hyperreflexia and preserved muscle bulk no atrophy . LMN signs would be flaccidity and atrophy. Which of the following describes muscle strength Grade 3/5? Active movement against gravity and resistance Active movement against gravity only Active movement with gravity eliminated Flicker of contraction only Normal strength 1 Grade 3 is movement against gravity but NOT resistance. Grade 2 is gravity eliminated. Grade 4 is against resistance. A positive Romberg test swaying significantly only when eyes are closed primarily indicates dysfunction of The Cerebellum The Motor Cortex Proprioception Dorsal Columns The Basal Ganglia The Thalamus 2 Romberg tests Proprioception . If vision eyes open compensates but balance fails when eyes close it s a sensory ataxia Dorsal Columns . The Achilles tendon reflex is mediated primarily by which spinal nerve root? L3 L4 L5 S1 S2 3 Achilles reflex is S1 . Patellar is L4. Dysdiadochokinesis inability to perform rapid alternating movements is a hallmark sign of damage to the Cerebellum Frontal Lobe Spinal Cord Basal Ganglia Cranial Nerves 0 Incoordination of rapid movements RAM is a classic Cerebellar sign. S1 Reflex Achilles Ankle Jerk Neurology L4 Reflex Patellar Knee Jerk Neurology C7 Reflex Triceps Neurology C5/C6 Reflex Biceps / Brachioradialis Neurology Grade 3 Strength Against Gravity Only Neurology Grade 2 Strength Gravity Eliminated Neurology Jendrassik Maneuver Use Reinforce/Distract for Reflexes Neurology Positive Romberg Meaning Sensory Ataxia Dorsal Column Proprioception Loss Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Clinical Neurology > Neurological Examination FirstAid Neurology Reflex Roots Mnemonic S1 2 Buckle my shoe - Ankle .\\\\n L3 4 Kick the door - Knee .\\\\n C5 6 Pick up sticks - Biceps .\\\\n C7 8 Lay them straight - Triceps . Romberg is Sensory A positive Romberg falling only when eyes CLOSED indicates a Sensory Ataxia Dorsal Columns/Proprioception NOT Cerebellum. Pronator Drift Highly specific sign for subtle UMN Weakness . The supinator muscles are weaker than pronators in UMN lesions. Babinski Sign Stroke sole of foot. Normal = Toes curl DOWN. UMN Lesion = Big toe goes UP Dorsiflex + fanning . Jendrassik Maneuver Can t get a knee jerk? Have them hook hands and PULL. Distraction unmasks the reflex. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l38", "title": "Lecture 38: OMM Lab 18 - Sacral Diagnosis", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l38 Lecture 38 OMM Lab 18 - Sacral Diagnosis\\n\\n Lecturer P. Noto D.O.\\n Session Lecture 38\\n\\n 1. Axes of Motion\\n Oblique Axes Named for the superior pole.\\n Right Oblique Axis Runs from Right Superior to Left Inferior pole.\\n Left Oblique Axis Runs from Left Superior to Right Inferior pole.\\n Usage Walking cycle Torsions .\\n Transverse Axes \\n Superior S2 Respiratory / Craniosacral motion.\\n Middle S2 Postural / Flexion-Extension.\\n Inferior S3 Innominate rotation Ilial shear .\\n\\n 2. Diagnosing Torsions vs Shears\\n L5 Rules \\n 1. L5 rotates OPPOSITE to the sacrum.\\n 2. L5 sidebends to the SAME side as the Oblique Axis.\\n Example If L5 is Rotated Right Sacrum is Rotated Left.\\n Seated Flexion Test SFT \\n Positive on the side of Dysfunction Blocked motion .\\n The axis is on the Opposite side of the positive SFT.\\n Physiologic Forward vs Non-Physiologic Backward \\n Physiologic Neutral mechanics Type I . \\ Forward\\ torsions L on L R on R .\\n Spring Test Negative It springs / Good motion .\\n Sphinx Test Negative Symmetry improves with extension .\\n Non-Physiologic Non-neutral Type II . \\ Backward\\ torsions L on R R on L .\\n Spring Test Positive Hard end feel / No spring .\\n Sphinx Test Positive Asymmetry gets worse / Pain .\\n\\n 3. The 4-Step Diagnosis\\n1. Seated Flexion Test Determines the side of dysfunction and thus the axis is on the opposite side .\\n2. Spring/Sphinx Determines if it is Forward Negative or Backward Positive .\\n3. L5 Confirms rotation.\\n4. Deep Sulcus / Post ILA \\n Forward Torsion Deep Sulcus and Posterior/Inf ILA are on Opposite sides.\\n Backward Torsion Deep Sulcus and Posterior/Inf ILA are on Opposite sides.\\n Wait simplify \\n Deep Sulcus = Side of Forward Rotation Sacral Base Anterior .\\n Posterior ILA = Side of Backward Rotation Sacral Base Posterior .\\n\\n 4. Specific Diagnoses\\n Left on Left Forward Torsion \\n Facing Left.\\n Axis Left.\\n L5 Rotated Right.\\n Spring Negative Good .\\n Right on Left Backward Torsion \\n Facing Right.\\n Axis Left.\\n L5 Rotated Left.\\n Spring Positive Bad .\\n Unilateral Shears \\n Not an axis dysfunction. Whole side is stuck forward or backward.\\n SFT + on same side as deep sulcus/low ILA if flexion or shallow sulcus/high ILA if extension . Lecture 38 OMM Lab 18 - Sacral Diagnosis Neurology questions A patient has a positive Seated Flexion Test on the RIGHT. The Spring Test is NEGATIVE. L5 is Rotated Right. What is the diagnosis? Right on Right Sacral Torsion Left on Left Sacral Torsion Right on Left Sacral Torsion Left on Right Sacral Torsion Right Unilateral Sacral Flexion 1 Step 1 SFT Right + → Axis is on the Left . So it s ? on Left .\\\\n Step 2 Spring Negative → Forward Torsion L on L .\\\\n Check Forward torsions have SAME letters. L on L fits. Which of the following findings is consistent with a Non-Physiologic Backward Sacral Torsion? Negative Sphinx Test Negative Spring Test Positive Spring Test L5 Rotated opposite to Sacrum Symmetrical Sacral Sulci 2 Positive Spring Test Hard/Stiff indicates the sacral base is stuck backward Posterior which is a non-physiologic backward torsion. In a Right on Right sacral torsion L5 will be Rotated Left Sidebent Left Rotated Left Sidebent Right Rotated Right Sidebent Right Rotated Right Sidebent Left Neutral 1 R on R Axis is Right → L5 Sidebends Right . Rotation is Right → L5 Rotates Left Opposite . Pattern Rot Left SB Right Type I neutral mechanics . The Superior Transverse Axis S2 is associated with which motion? Postural Flexion/Extension Innominate Rotation Respiratory / Craniosacral Motion Walking Cycle Disc Herniation 2 Superior Axis Respiratory/Craniosacral.\\\\n Middle Axis Postural.\\\\n Inferior Axis Innominate Ilial . If the Seated Flexion Test is positive on the LEFT which oblique axis is engaged? Left Oblique Axis Right Oblique Axis Middle Transverse Axis Inferior Transverse Axis Vertical Axis 1 SFT is positive on the side of dysfunction. The functioning axis is on the OPPOSITE side. Positive Left → Axis is Right . SFT Positive Side Meaning Side of Dysfunction Axis is Opposite OMM Spring Test Positive Meaning Stuck Backward Non-physiologic / Backward Torsion OMM L5 Rotation Rule Rotates OPPOSITE to Sacrum OMM L5 Sidebending Rule Sidebends SAME side as Axis OMM Same Letters e.g. L on L Forward Torsion Physiologic OMM Different Letters e.g. R on L Backward Torsion Non-Physiologic OMM AMBOSS OMM > Sacrum FirstAid OMM > Sacrum L5 Rule Mnemonic L5 acts like a Bully . If L5 rotates Right the Sacrum turns away Left . Sidebending side = Axis side. Spring Test Rules If it Springs it s a Functional Physiologic/Forward torsion.\\\\nIf it s Stiff Positive it s a Stuck Non-physiologic/Backward torsion. Naming Torsions Rotation on Axis .\\\\n R on R Forward Physiologic .\\\\n L on L Forward Physiologic .\\\\n R on L Backward Non-Physiologic .\\\\n L on R Backward Non-Physiologic .\\\\n Hint Same letters = Forward. Different letters = Backward. Seated Flexion Test Finds the side of ILIOSACRAL dysfunction. The axis is on the OPPOSITE side of the positive test. Sphinx Test Logic Extension Sphinx makes a forward sacrum happy more symmetrical . It makes a backward sacrum sad more asymmetrical . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l39", "title": "Lecture 39: Pharmacology - Adrenergic Antagonists", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l39 Lecture 39 Pharmacology - Adrenergic Antagonists\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 39\\n\\n 1. Alpha-Adrenergic Antagonists Alpha Blockers \\n Non-Selective Alpha-1 & Alpha-2 \\n Phenoxybenzamine Irreversible Covalent bond . Long duration. Used pre-op for Pheochromocytoma prevents hypertensive crisis .\\n Phentolamine Competitive Reversible . Used for Epinephrine Reversal local anesthetic overdose and Pheochromocytoma diagnosis/surgery.\\n Side Effects Severe Orthostatic Hypotension Reflex Tachycardia due to Alpha-2 block increasing NE release .\\n Selective Alpha-1 Blockers -zosins \\n Prazosin Terazosin Doxazosin Used for HTN add-on and BPH.\\n Tamsulosin Flomax Alpha-1A selective Prostate specific . Used for BPH less hypotension .\\n Adverse Effects First-Dose Effect Syncope/Severe Orthostatis → Take at bedtime! Floppy Iris Syndrome Cataract surgery risk .\\n Selective Alpha-2 Antagonist \\n Yohimbine Increases NE release blocks feedback . \\ Aphrodisiac\\ . Causes anxiety/tremor.\\n\\n 2. Beta-Adrenergic Antagonists Beta Blockers \\n Non-Selective Beta-1 & Beta-2 \\n Propranolol Lipophilic CNS effects nightmares/sedation . Used for Migraine prophylaxis Performance Anxiety Stage fright Thyroid Storm.\\n Contraindication Asthma/COPD Bronchoconstriction via B2 .\\n Beta-1 Selective Cardioselective \\ A-M\\ - Atenolol Metoprolol Esmolol .\\n Safer in lung disease.\\n Combined Alpha/Beta Blockers \\n Labetalol Carvedilol Block Alpha-1 Vasodilation + Beta-1/2.\\n Use CHF Carvedilol decreases mortality Hypertensive Emergencies Labetalol .\\n\\n 3. Key Pharmacology Concepts\\n Epinephrine Reversal \\n Normal Epi Increases BP Alpha-1 vasoconstriction > Beta-2 dilation .\\n Epi + Alpha Blocker Decreases BP Alpha blocked unmasks Beta-2 vasodilation .\\n Diabetes & Beta Blockers \\n Beta blockers MASK symptoms of hypoglycemia tachycardia tremors .\\n Non-selectives delay recovery from hypoglycemia block liver gluconeogenesis .\\n Withdrawal \\n NEVER stop abruptly. Chronic use UPREGULATES receptors.\\n Sudden stop → Rebound HTN Angina MI Sudden Death.\\n\\n 4. Therapeutic Uses Summary\\n\\n| Indication | Preferred Agents | Mechanism |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Hypertension | Metoprolol Atenolol | Decrease CO Renin inhibition. |\\n| Angina/MI | Metoprolol Carvedilol | Decrease O2 demand HR/Contractility . |\\n| CHF | Carvedilol Metoprolol Succinate Bisoprolol | Block sympathetic remodeling. Increase survival. |\\n| BPH | Tamsulosin Doxazosin | Relax prostate smooth muscle Alpha-1 . |\\n| Pheochromocytoma | Phenoxybenzamine | Irreversible Alpha block before surgery. |\\n| Glaucoma | Timolol | Decrease aqueous production. | Lecture 39 Pharmacology - Adrenergic Antagonists Neurology questions A patient with Pheochromocytoma is scheduled for surgery. Which drug is most appropriate for preoperative management to prevent hypertensive crisis? Propranolol Phenoxybenzamine Atenolol Prazosin Yohimbine 1 Phenoxybenzamine is an Irreversible alpha blocker. It is the gold standard for pheochromocytoma pre-op to prevent catecholamine surges. A 65-year-old male is started on Terazosin for BPH. What important counseling point must be provided? Take on an empty stomach because food reduces absorption. Take with grapefruit juice to enhance effect. Take the first dose at bedtime to avoid syncope. Discontinue immediately if nasal congestion occurs. Expect heart rate to decrease significantly. 2 First-Dose Effect Alpha-1 blockers cause significant orthostatic hypotension/syncope initially. Taking it at bedtime mitigates the risk of falling. Which of the following beta blockers also possesses Alpha-1 blocking activity providing additional vasodilation? Metoprolol Atenolol Carvedilol Timolol Esmolol 2 Carvedilol and Labetalol trigger Combined blockade Beta-1 Beta-2 and Alpha-1 . This provides vasodilation + heart rate control. A diabetic patient taking insulin is prescribed a beta blocker. They should be warned that the drug might mask which symptom of hypoglycemia? Sweating Hunger Tachycardia Confusion Fatigue 2 Tachycardia and tremors are mediated by the sympathetic response to hypoglycemia. Beta blockers mask these. Sweating cholinergic is usually preserved. The phenomenon known as Epinephrine Reversal describes a drop in blood pressure when Epinephrine is administered in the presence of A Beta Blocker An Alpha Blocker A Muscarinic Agonist A Ganglionic Blocker A Calcium Channel Blocker 1 An Alpha Blocker like Phentolamine blocks the vasoconstriction Alpha-1 of Epi leaving the vasodilation Beta-2 unopposed → BP drop. Phenoxybenzamine Mechanism Irreversible Alpha Blocker Pharmacology First Dose Effect Drug Class Alpha-1 Blockers -zosins Pharmacology A-M Beta Blockers Atenolol Metoprolol Beta-1 Selective Cardioselective Pharmacology Carvedilol / Labetalol Mechanism Mixed Alpha-1 and Beta Blockers Pharmacology Epinephrine Reversal Cause Epi + Alpha Blocker Unmasks Beta-2 Vasodilation Pharmacology Hypoglycemia Signs Masked by Beta Blockers Tachycardia Tremors Anxiety Pharmacology Hypoglycemia Sign SPARED by Beta Blockers Sweating Cholinergic Pharmacology Pheochromocytoma Pre-op Drug Phenoxybenzamine Pharmacology primarySource SketchyPharm chapter Autonomic Drugs > Adrenergic Antagonists alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs resource AMBOSS chapter Pharmacology > Autonomic nervous system Epinephrine Reversal Giving an Alpha Blocker Phentolamine before Epinephrine turns Epi s Pressor effect into a Depressor effect BP drops due to unopposed Beta-2 vasodilation . First Dose Effect Alpha-1 blockers Prazosin/Terazosin cause severe orthostatic hypotension on the first dose. Counseling Take the first dose at Bedtime . Beta Blocker Withdrawal Chronic beta blockade causes receptor Upregulation . Abrupt withdrawal leads to supersensitivity = Rebound Hypertension / MI . Always TAPER. Diabetes & Beta Blockers Beta blockers mask the warning signs of hypoglycemia palpitations tremors anxiety . Sweating Cholinergic is the only sign that remains. Pheochromocytoma Rule Always give Alpha blockers BEFORE Beta blockers. A before B . Giving Beta first leaves Alpha unopposed → Hypertensive Crisis. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l40", "title": "Lecture 40: Spinal Cord Syndromes & Mononeuropathies", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l40 Lecture 40 Spinal Cord Syndromes & Mononeuropathies\\n\\n Lecturer A. Leder D.O.\\n Session Lecture 40\\n\\n 1. Spinal Cord Anatomy Review\\n Dorsal Columns Vibration Proprioception Fine Touch. Cross in Brainstem .\\n Spinothalamic Tract Pain Temperature Crude Touch. Cross immediately/1-2 levels up .\\n Corticospinal Tract Motor. Cross in Medulla .\\n\\n 2. Spinal Cord Syndromes\\n Central Cord Syndrome Syringomyelia \\n Lesion Center of cord crossing spinothalamic fibers .\\n Presentation \\ Cape-like\\ loss of pain/temperature across shoulders/arms. Upper extremities > Lower.\\n Causes Syringomyelia Chiari I Trauma Hyperextension Tumor.\\n Posterior Column Syndrome Tabes Dorsalis \\n Lesion Dorsal Columns.\\n Presentation Loss of Proprioception/Vibration → Sensory Ataxia Stomping Gait +Romberg . Lhermitte Sign Shock down back .\\n Causes Neurosyphilis Tabes Dorsalis - Triad Lightning pains Ataxia Argyll Robertson Pupil B12 Deficiency.\\n Posterolateral Column Syndrome Subacute Combined Degeneration \\n Lesion Dorsal Columns + Corticospinal Tract.\\n Presentation Ataxia/Numbness + Spastic Paresis.\\n Causes Vitamin B12 Deficiency Pernicious anemia Gastric bypass Vegans Nitrous Oxide abuse \\ Whippets\\ .\\n Anterior Cord Syndrome \\n Lesion Anterior 2/3 of cord Spares Dorsal Columns .\\n Presentation Complete Motor Paralysis + Loss of Pain/Temp. Proprioception/Vibration SPARED .\\n Causes Anterior Spinal Artery ASA infarct Aortic aneurysm repair/surgery .\\n Brown-S\\u00e9quard Syndrome Hemicord \\n Lesion Half of the cord Hemisection .\\n Presentation \\n Ipsilateral Loss of Motor CST + Proprioception/Vibration DC .\\n Contralateral Loss of Pain/Temp Spinothalamic starting 1-2 levels below .\\n Causes Stab wound MS Tumor.\\n Cauda Equina Syndrome \\n Lesion Nerve roots L2-S5 Horse s tail .\\n Presentation Saddle Anesthesia Bowel/Bladder dysfunction Retention/Incontinence Radicular pain.\\n Management Surgical Emergency Decompression within 48hrs .\\n\\n 3. Common Mononeuropathies\\n Median Nerve Carpal Tunnel . Numbness 1st 3.5 digits. Thenar atrophy.\\n Ulnar Nerve Cubital Tunnel . Numbness 4th/5th digits. \\ Claw Hand\\ weak interossei .\\n Radial Nerve Saturday Night Palsy . Spiral groove compression. Wrist Drop .\\n Common Peroneal Fibular Nerve Unilateral Foot Drop Steppage gait . Compression at Fibular Head.\\n Lateral Femoral Cutaneous Nerve Meralgia Paresthetica . Numbness of lateral thigh. Caused by tight belts obesity pregnancy. Lecture 40 Spinal Cord Syndromes & Mononeuropathies Neurology questions A patient presents with loss of pain and temperature sensation in a cape-like distribution over the shoulders and arms. Vibration and proprioception are intact. This pattern is consistent with Anterior Cord Syndrome Central Cord Syndrome Brown-S\\u00e9quard Syndrome Posterior Column Syndrome Cauda Equina Syndrome 1 Central Cord Syndrome Syringomyelia expands from the central canal disrupting the crossing Spinothalamic fibers first causing the cape-like dissociation. Following a stab wound to the right side of the spinal cord Hemisection at T10 which finding would be expected? Loss of pain and temperature on the Right leg Loss of vibration and proprioception on the Left leg Paralysis of the Left leg Loss of pain and temperature on the Left leg Bilateral loss of all sensation 3 Brown-S\\u00e9quard Contralateral loss of Pain/Temp. Right lesion → Left leg Pain/Temp loss. Right leg would lose Motor/Vibe . A patient recovering from abdominal aortic aneurysm repair develops bilateral leg paralysis and loss of pain sensation but preserves vibration sense. The most likely diagnosis is Posterior Spinal Artery Infarct Anterior Spinal Artery Infarct Vitamin B12 Deficiency Tabes Dorsalis Guillain-Barr\\u00e9 Syndrome 1 Anterior Spinal Artery supplies the anterior 2/3 Motor + Spinothalamic . Infarct causes Anterior Cord Syndrome Paralysis + Pain loss with Dorsal Column sparing Vibe intact . Which of the following creates a Saturday Night Palsy causing wrist drop? Median Nerve compression at carpal tunnel Ulnar Nerve compression at cubital tunnel Radial Nerve compression at spiral groove Common Peroneal compression at fibular head Sciatic Nerve compression 2 Radial Nerve compression at the spiral groove humerus often from falling asleep with arm over a chair Saturday Night causes Wrist Drop . Subacute Combined Degeneration of the spinal cord involves demyelination of the Posterior Columns and Corticospinal Tracts due to deficiency of Vitamin B1 Thiamine Vitamin B3 Niacin Vitamin B12 Cobalamin Vitamin E Copper 2 Vitamin B12 deficiency or Nitrous Oxide toxicity causes Subacute Combined Degeneration. Central Cord Syndrome Pattern Cape-like Pain/Temp Loss Neurology Tabes Dorsalis Cause Neurosyphilis Tertiary Neurology Brown-Sequard Pain/Temp loss side Contralateral Opposite side Neurology Brown-Sequard Motor/Vibe loss side Ipsilateral Same side Neurology Wrist Drop Nerve Radial Nerve Neurology Foot Drop Nerve Common Peroneal Fibular Nerve Neurology Saddle Anesthesia + Incontinence Cauda Equina Syndrome Emergency Neurology Subacute Combined Degeneration Cause B12 Deficiency / Nitrous Oxide Neurology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Spinal Cord Disorders alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Spinal Cord Lesions resource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Spinal Cord resource SketchyPath chapter Neurology > Spinal Cord Brown-S\\u00e9quard Rules You lose Motor & Vibe on the SAME side Ipsi .\\\\nYou lose Pain & Temp on the OPPOSITE side Contra . Anterior Cord Spare Anterior Spinal Artery stroke kills everything EXCEPT the Dorsal Columns . So they can still feel Vibration/Proprioception but can t move or feel pain. Syringomyelia Sign Cape-like distribution of numbness Pain/Temp loss across the shoulders and back. Often associated with Chiari Malformation . Whippets & B12 Nitrous Oxide irreversibly oxidizes B12 leading to Subacute Combined Degeneration Posterolateral cord syndrome . Look for ataxia + numbness in a party-goer. Cauda Equina Flags New onset Incontinence or retention + Saddle Anesthesia . This is an MRI + Surgery EMERGENCY. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l41", "title": "Lecture 41: From Myelopathy to Myopathy", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l41 Lecture 41 From Myelopathy to Myopathy\\n\\n Lecturer A. Leder D.O.\\n Session Lecture 41\\n\\n 1. Localization Approach\\n Myelopathy Spinal Cord UMN signs below Sensory level Bowel/Bladder.\\n Anterior Horn Cell LMN signs Weakness Atrophy Fasciculations . Sensory SPARED .\\n Radiculopathy Root Radiating pain Dermatomal sensory loss.\\n Plexopathy Multiple nerves/roots involved. Painful e.g. Parsonage-Turner .\\n Peripheral Neuropathy Stocking-glove sensory loss Distal weakness.\\n NMJ Fatigable weakness Diplopia/Ptosis. Sensory SPARED .\\n Myopathy Proximal muscle weakness. Sensory SPARED .\\n\\n 2. Anterior Horn Cell Disorders\\n ALS Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis \\n UMN + LMN Signs in same segment.\\n Asymmetric limb weakness Bulbar signs Dysarthria/Dysphagia .\\n Sensory Spared .\\n Polio & West Nile Virus \\n Acute Asymmetric flaccid paralysis LMN .\\n Fever/Meningeal signs.\\n Spinal Muscular Atrophy SMA \\n Genetic SMN1 . Symmetric LMN weakness.\\n\\n 3. Peripheral Nerve Disorders\\n Guillain-Barr\\u00e9 Syndrome AIDP \\n Acute Ascending Paralysis. Post-infectious Campylobacter Zika .\\n LP Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein Normal Cells .\\n Tx IVIG or Plasma Exchange. NO Steroids .\\n CIDP Chronic Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy \\n Chronic >8 weeks . \\ Chronic GBS\\ .\\n Tx Steroids IVIG.\\n Leprosy Hansen s Disease. Mutilating neuropathy Armadillos .\\n\\n 4. Neuromuscular Junction NMJ \\n Myasthenia Gravis MG \\n Post-synaptic ACh Receptor Antibodies.\\n Clinical Ptosis Diplopia Bulbar weakness. Fatigue Worse at end of day .\\n Association Thymoma .\\n Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome LEMS \\n Pre-synaptic Voltage-Gated Calcium Channel Antibodies.\\n Clinical Proximal leg weakness. Facilitation Strength IMPROVES with use . Dry mouth.\\n Association Small Cell Lung Cancer .\\n Botulism \\n Toxin blocks ACh release SNARE proteins .\\n Descending Paralysis. Pupils dilated/fixed. Honey infants .\\n\\n 5. Myopathies\\n Polymyositis/Dermatomyositis \\n Proximal symmetric weakness difficulty rising from chair .\\n Pain is uncommon .\\n Elevated CK. Lecture 41 From Myelopathy to Myopathy Neurology questions A 35-year-old male presents with ascending weakness starting in his legs and moving up. Reflexes are absent. Two weeks ago he had a diarrheal illness. CSF shows protein of 150 High and WBC of 2 Normal . What is the diagnosis? Multiple Sclerosis Guillain-Barr\\u00e9 Syndrome Myasthenia Gravis ALS Polio 1 GBS presents with acute ascending paralysis and areflexia after an infection Campylobacter . CSF shows Albuminocytologic Dissociation . Which of the following findings is classic for Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis ALS ? Pure sensory loss in a stocking-glove distribution Symmetric proximal muscle weakness with pain Combination of UMN and LMN signs with sensory sparing Diplopia and ptosis worsening at night Ascending paralysis with bladder dysfunction 2 ALS affects Anterior Horn Cells LMN and Corticospinal Tracts UMN . The hallmark is mixed UMN/LMN signs. Sensation is typically preserved. A patient with suspected Myasthenia Gravis is tested. Which antibody is most specific for this condition? Anti-Voltage Gated Calcium Channel Anti-Acetylcholine Receptor AChR Anti-Jo-1 Anti-GM1 Anti-Hu 1 AChR antibodies are pathogenic in Myasthenia Gravis Post-synaptic . Calcium channel antibodies are for Lambert-Eaton. Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome is most commonly associated with which underlying malignancy? Thymoma Small Cell Lung Cancer Renal Cell Carcinoma Lymphoma Melanoma 1 Small Cell Lung Cancer is found in ~50% of patients with LEMS. Thymoma is associated with Myasthenia Gravis . Which feature distinguishes Polio from ALS? Polio causes LMN signs only; ALS causes UMN & LMN signs. Polio is progressive; ALS resolves spontaneously. Polio affects sensation; ALS spares sensation. Polio causes spasticity; ALS causes flaccidity. Polio is autoimmune; ALS is viral. 0 Polio destroys Anterior Horn Cells LMN only → Flaccid paralysis. ALS destroys AHCs LMN + CSTs UMN → Mixed Spastic/Flaccid. ALS Finding Mixed UMN + LMN Signs Neurology GBS CSF Finding Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein Normal Cells Neurology Myasthenia Gravis Mechanism Antibodies to Post-synaptic ACh Receptors Neurology Lambert-Eaton Mechanism Antibodies to Pre-synaptic Voltage-Gated Calcium Channels Neurology Lambert-Eaton Association Small Cell Lung Cancer Neurology Myasthenia Gravis Association Thymoma Neurology Botulism Presentation Descending Paralysis + Dilated Pupils Neurology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Neuromuscular Junction Disorders alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Neuromuscular resource Pathoma chapter Musculoskeletal > Neuromuscular Junction resource SketchyPath chapter Neurology > Neuromuscular & Peripheral Nerve UMN vs LMN Signs UMN Spasticity Hyperreflexia Babinski + No atrophy.\\\\n LMN Flaccidity Hyporeflexia Fasciculations Atrophy. ALS Hallmark Presence of UMN and LMN signs in the same limb/segment. e.g. Atrophied muscle has a brisk reflex . Sensory is Intact . GBS vs CIDP Treatment Both get IVIG/PLEX.\\\\n GBS Steroids do NOT work.\\\\n CIDP Steroids DO work. MG vs LEMS MG Eyes Post-synaptic. Worse with use. Thymoma.\\\\n LEMS Legs Pre-synaptic Ca channels . BETTER with use. Small Cell Ca. Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein but Normal WBCs in CSF. Classic finding in Guillain-Barr\\u00e9 Syndrome . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l42", "title": "Lecture 42: Diseases of Myelin", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l42 Lecture 42 Diseases of Myelin\\n\\n Lecturer M. Plummer M.D.\\n Session Lecture 42\\n\\n 1. Demyelinating Diseases Myelin Destruction \\n Multiple Sclerosis MS \\n Autoimmune destruction of CNS myelin Oligodendrocytes .\\n Pathology Periventricular Plaques \\ Dawson s Fingers\\ . Reactive Gliosis. Preservation of Axons early .\\n CSF Oligoclonal IgG Bands Intrathecal synthesis .\\n Clinical Relapsing-Remitting course. Optic Neuritis Painful vision loss INO MLF lesion Lhermitte Sign Electric shock with neck flexion Uhthoff Phenomenon Heat worsens symptoms .\\n Epidemiology Women > Men. Far from equator Vitamin D .\\n Guillain-Barr\\u00e9 Syndrome GBS \\n Autoimmune destruction of PNS myelin Schwann Cells .\\n Pathology Segmental demyelination + Lymphocytic infiltration.\\n Clinical Acute Ascending Paralysis + Areflexia.\\n Trigger Campylobacter jejuni CMV EBV Molecular Mimicry .\\n CSF Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein Normal Cells .\\n Central Pontine Myelinolysis Osmotic Demyelination \\n Cause Rapid correction of Hyponatremia . \\ From Low to High your Pons will Die\\ .\\n Clinical Locked-In Syndrome Quadriplegia + Preserved Consciousness/Eye movements .\\n\\n 2. Dysmyelinating Diseases Leukodystrophies \\n Definition Genetic defects in myelin formation or turnover .\\n Metachromatic Leukodystrophy MLD \\n Deficiency Arylsulfatase A .\\n Accumulation Sulfatides.\\n Path Metachromasia change in dye color .\\n Krabbe Disease Globoid Cell Leukodystrophy \\n Deficiency Galactocerebrosidase .\\n Accumulation Galactosylsphingosine Psychosine - Toxic.\\n Path Globoid Cells Engorged Macrophages .\\n Adrenoleukodystrophy ALD \\n Genetics X-Linked Recessive ABCD1 gene .\\n Defect Peroxisomal transporter.\\n Accumulation Very Long Chain Fatty Acids VLCFA .\\n Clinical Adrenal Insufficiency + CNS deterioration in young boys.\\n\\n 3. Infectious Demyelination\\n Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML \\n Pathogen JC Virus Reactivation .\\n Host Immunosuppressed AIDS CD4 < 200 .\\n Path Lytic infection of Oligodendrocytes. Enlarged nuclei with \\ Ground Glass\\ viral inclusions.\\n Clinical Rapidly progressive multifocal deficits. Death. Lecture 42 Diseases of Myelin Neurology questions A 28-year-old female presents with sudden loss of vision in her right eye associated with pain on eye movement. MRI shows periventricular white matter plaques. Analysis of her CSF would most likely reveal Albuminocytologic Dissociation Oligoclonal IgG Bands Neutrophilic Pleocytosis Decreased Glucose Positive India Ink Stain 1 Oligoclonal Bands found in the CSF but not serum indicate intrathecal IgG production a hallmark of Multiple Sclerosis . A 45-year-old alcoholic is brought to the ER with severe hyponatremia Na = 102 . The residents rapidly correct his sodium to 135 over 6 hours. Two days later he is quadriplegic but can blink and move his eyes vertically. What happened? Stroke in the MCA territory Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Central Pontine Myelinolysis Wernicke s Encephalopathy Herniation 2 Central Pontine Myelinolysis Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome is caused by rapid correction of hyponatremia . It destroys the Pons causing Locked-In Syndrome . Which pattern of weakness is characteristic of Guillain-Barr\\u00e9 Syndrome? Descending paralysis with pupillary dilation Hemiparesis sparing the face Ascending flaccid paralysis with areflexia Proximal muscle weakness with preserved sensation Waxing and waning weakness improving with rest 2 GBS causes an acute Ascending paralysis toes to nose with loss of reflexes Areflexia . Adrenoleukodystrophy involves the accumulation of which substance due to a peroxisomal defect? Sphingomyelin Sulfatides Very Long Chain Fatty Acids VLCFA Galactocerebroside Copper 2 ALD is a defect in the ABCD1 transporter preventing the breakdown of Very Long Chain Fatty Acids in peroxisomes. Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML is caused by the reactivation of which virus in immunocompromised patients? Herpes Simplex Virus 1 HSV-1 Cytomegalovirus CMV JC Virus Epstein-Barr Virus EBV Varicella Zoster Virus VZV 2 JC Virus causes PML. Mnemonic J ohn C unningham virus destroys oligodendoctyes in AIDS . Multiple Sclerosis CSF Finding Oligoclonal IgG Bands Neurology Lhermitte Sign Electric shock down back with neck flexion MS/Spinal Cord Neurology Uhthoff Phenomenon Heat worsens MS symptoms Neurology Central Pontine Myelinolysis Cause Rapid correction of Hyponatremia Neurology Metachromatic Leukodystrophy Enzyme Arylsulfatase A Neurology Krabbe Disease Hallmark Globoid Cells Neurology Adrenoleukodystrophy Accumulation Very Long Chain Fatty Acids VLCFA Neurology PML Virus JC Virus Neurology primarySource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Demyelinating Disorders alternatives resource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Demyelinating Disorders resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Demyelinating resource SketchyPath chapter Neurology > Demyelinating MS Triad Charcot 1. Scanning Speech Dysarthria \\\\n2. Intention Tremor Cerebellar \\\\n3. Nystagmus INO/MLF Uhthoff s Phenomenon MS symptoms get WORSE with HEAT Hot bath exercise fever . Heat slows conduction in demyelinated axons. CPM Rule From Low to High your Pons will Die Hyponatremia → Rapid Correction → CPM/Locked-in .\\\\n From High to Low your Brain will Blow Hypernatremia → Rapid Correction → Cerebral Edema . Krabbe Findings Look for Globoid Cells Multinucleated Giant Cells and optic atrophy. Adrenoleukodystrophy Think Adrenal glands + Leuko White matter + Dystrophy Bad growth . Young boy with bronzing skin Addison s and behavioral changes. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l43", "title": "Lecture 43: Pharmacology of Multiple Sclerosis", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l43 Lecture 43 Pharmacology of Multiple Sclerosis\\n\\n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 43\\n\\n 1. Acute Exacerbations Flare-ups \\n Glucocorticoids High Dose IV Methylprednisolone \\n Mechanism Induces Lipocortin → Inhibits Phospholipase A2 → Decreased Prostaglandins/Leukotrienes.\\n Use Shortens duration of acute attacks e.g. Optic Neuritis . Does NOT change long-term prognosis.\\n Adverse Effects HPA axis suppression Taper needed Osteoporosis Hyperglycemia Insomnia.\\n\\n 2. Disease Modifying Therapies DMTs \\n Interferon Beta 1a & 1b \\n Mechanism Cytokine JAK/STAT ; Inhibits T-cell activation/migration across BBB.\\n Adverse Effects Flu-like symptoms Fever chills aches Depression Liver toxicity.\\n Glatiramer Acetate \\n Mechanism Synthetic polypeptide Myelin Basic Protein decoy . Shifts immune response from Th1 Pro-inflammatory to Th2 Anti-inflammatory .\\n Adverse Effects Injection site reactions Post-injection systemic reaction Flushing Chest chest tightness - benign but scary .\\n Note Safest in Pregnancy .\\n Natalizumab \\n Mechanism Monoclonal antibody against $\\\\\\\\alpha$4-integrin . Blocks leukocytes from binding VCAM-1 and entering the CNS.\\n Adverse Effects PML Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy due to JC Virus reactivation.\\n Fingolimod \\n Mechanism S1P Receptor Modulator . Sequesters lymphocytes in lymph nodes can t exit .\\n Adverse Effects Bradycardia Monitor EKG at first dose Macular Edema.\\n Dimethyl Fumarate \\n Mechanism Activates Nrf2 Antioxidant pathway .\\n Adverse Effects Flushing GI upset.\\n Teriflunomide \\n Mechanism Inhibits Pyrimidine synthesis Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase .\\n Adverse Effects Hepatotoxicity Teratogenicity Contraindicated in pregnancy .\\n Mitoxantrone \\n Mechanism Anthracycline analog. Intercalates DNA / Inhibits Topoisomerase II.\\n Use Secondary Progressive MS.\\n Adverse Effects Cardiotoxicity Dilated Cardiomyopathy - monitor EF Acute Myeloid Leukemia AML . \\ Blue\\ discoloration of urine/sclera.\\n Anti-CD20 Ocrelizumab Rituximab \\n Mechanism B-cell depletion.\\n Adverse Effects Infusion reactions Hepatitis B reactivation.\\n\\n 3. Symptomatic Treatment\\n Spasticity \\n Baclofen GABA-B agonist .\\n Tizanidine $\\\\\\\\alpha$2-adrenergic agonist .\\n Walking Speed \\n Dalfampridine K+ Channel Blocker - Enhances conduction.\\n Urinary Incontinence \\n Oxybutynin Muscarinic Antagonist .\\n Botulinum Toxin Cleaves SNARE proteins . Lecture 43 Pharmacology of Multiple Sclerosis Neurology questions A 32-year-old female with Multiple Sclerosis is started on a new medication. Since starting she complains of flushed skin and profound gastrointestinal distress. Which medication mechanism is most likely responsible? Inhibition of Dihydroorotate Dehydrogenase Activation of Nrf2 pathway Sequestration of lymphocytes in lymph nodes Blockade of alpha-4 integrin Depletion of CD20 B cells 1 Dimethyl Fumarate activates the Nrf2 antioxidant pathway. Its most common side effects are Flushing and GI upset. A patient being treated for MS develops Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML . Which drug mechanism is associated with this complication? Interferon beta-1a Glatiramer Acetate Natalizumab Baclofen Methylprednisolone 2 Natalizumab blocks alpha-4 integrin preventing immune surveillance in the CNS allowing JC Virus reactivation PML . Which medication helps improve walking speed in MS patients by blocking potassium channels? Tizanidine Dalfampridine Oxybutynin Modafinil Amantadine 1 Dalfampridine blocks voltage-gated Potassium channels increasing action potential duration and improving conduction. Before starting a patient on Fingolimod which baseline test is most critical? Liver Function Tests Echocardiogram Electrocardiogram EKG Pulmonary Function Tests Thyroid Stimulating Hormone 2 Fingolimod can cause significant Bradycardia and heart block after the first dose. Observation and EKG monitoring are required. Which agent treats spasticity in MS by acting as an agonist at GABA-B receptors? Diazepam Dantrolene Baclofen Tizanidine Cyclobenzaprine 2 Baclofen is a GABA-B agonist used centrally for spasticity. Tizanidine is an alpha-2 agonist; Dantrolene works on muscle Ryanodine receptors . Natalizumab Mechanism Blocks Alpha-4 Integrin prevents CNS entry Pharmacology Natalizumab Serious AE PML JC Virus Pharmacology Fingolimod Mechanism S1P Receptor Modulator Sequesters lymphocytes Pharmacology Fingolimod AE Bradycardia First dose Pharmacology Teriflunomide Contraindication Pregnancy Teratogenic Pharmacology Glatiramer Acetate Mechanism Myelin Basic Protein Decoy Th1 → Th2 Pharmacology Interferon Beta AE Flu-like symptoms Depression Pharmacology Dalfampridine Mechanism Potassium Channel Blocker Improves walking Pharmacology primarySource SketchyPharm chapter Smooth Muscle & CNS Drugs > Multiple Sclerosis Drugs alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Pharmacology > Neurologic resource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Multiple Sclerosis > Management PML Risk Natalizumab is highly effective but carries a risk of PML Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy if the patient is JC Virus positive. Natalizumab says NO to leukocytes entering the brain . Fingolimod & The Heart Fingolimod causes Bradycardia . You must monitor the patient s heart rate/EKG for 6 hours after the First Dose . Mitoxantrone Toxicity Like Doxorubicin Mitoxantrone is Cardiotoxic Dilated Cardiomyopathy . Must monitor Ejection Fraction. Pregnancy & MS Teriflunomide is Teratogenic . Glatiramer Acetate is generally considered the safest during pregnancy. Walking Drug Dalfampridine is the Walking Drug . It blocks Potassium Channels to prolong the action potential and improve conduction in demyelinated nerves. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l44", "title": "Lecture 44: Introduction to Ophthalmology", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l44 Lecture 44 Introduction to Ophthalmology\\n\\n Lecturer A. Nattis D.O.\\n Session Lecture 44\\n\\n 1. The Red Eye\\n Conjunctivitis \\n Viral Pink Eye Watery discharge. Adenovirus. Highly contagious.\\n Bacterial Purulent pus discharge. \\ Glued shut\\ in AM.\\n Allergic Itching Pruritus . Bilateral.\\n Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma \\n Presentation Severe eye pain Headache Nausea/Vomiting Haloes around lights .\\n Exam Mid-dilated fixed pupil . Cloudy cornea. High IOP. Emergency .\\n Uveitis Iritis \\n Presentation Deep pain Photophobia Light sensitivity Redness.\\n Exam Cells and Flare in anterior chamber. Irregular pupil Synechiae .\\n Association Autoimmune HLA-B27 Sarcoidosis .\\n Scleritis \\n Presentation Deep \\ boring\\ pain. Wakes patient at night.\\n Association Rheumatoid Arthritis.\\n\\n 2. Neuro-Ophthalmology Pupils \\n Horner s Syndrome \\n Triad Ptosis Droopy lid Miosis Small pupil Anhidrosis No sweat .\\n Cause Sympathetic pathway lesion Pancoast tumor Carotid dissection .\\n CN III Palsy \\n Presentation Eye is \\ Down and Out\\ . Ptosis.\\n Pupil If Dilated \\ Blown\\ it s a PCom Aneurysm Surgical Emergency . If pupil spared it s Ischemic Diabetes .\\n Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect RAPD / Marcus Gunn Pupil \\n Test Swinging Flashlight Test.\\n Result Pupil appears to Dilate when light is swung to the bad eye because it senses less light than the good eye .\\n Cause Optic Nerve lesion Optic Neuritis .\\n Argyll Robertson Pupil \\n Sign Accommodates but does not react to light \\ Prostitute s Pupil\\ .\\n Cause Neurosyphilis.\\n\\n 3. Retina & Optic Nerve\\n Retinal Detachment \\n Symptoms Flashes and Floaters . \\ Curtain coming down\\ over vision.\\n Tx Surgical Emergency.\\n Central Retinal Artery Occlusion CRAO \\n Sign Cherry Red Spot on macula. Pale retina. Sudden painless vision loss.\\n Cause Embolus Stroke of the eye .\\n Central Retinal Vein Occlusion CRVO \\n Sign \\ Blood and Thunder\\ Diffuse hemorrhages .\\n Optic Neuritis \\n Sign Painful vision loss. RAPD + . Associated with MS .\\n Giant Cell Arteritis Temporal Arteritis \\n Sign Ischemic Optic Neuropathy AION . Jaw claudication. Scalp tenderness. high ESR.\\n Tx High Dose Steroids immediately to save the other eye. Lecture 44 Introduction to Ophthalmology Neurology questions A 65-year-old male presents with sudden painless loss of vision in his right eye. Fundoscopy reveals a pale retina with a cherry-red spot at the fovea. What is the most likely diagnosis? Central Retinal Vein Occlusion Retinal Detachment Central Retinal Artery Occlusion Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma Optic Neuritis 2 Central Retinal Artery Occlusion CRAO presents with a pale retina edema and a Cherry Red Spot thin fovea showing choroid . It is an embolic event. A patient presents with a painful red eye nausea and halos around lights. The pupil is mid-dilated and non-reactive. What is the immediate concern? Viral Conjunctivitis Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma Uveitis Scleritis Corneal Abrasion 1 Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma is an ocular emergency characterized by high pressure pain halos and a fixed mid-dilated pupil. Which finding is part of the classic triad of Horner s Syndrome? Mydriasis Dilated Pupil Exophthalmos Bulging Eye Anhidrosis Lack of sweating Diplopia Double vision Hyperhidrosis Excess sweating 2 Horner s Triad Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis on the ipsilateral face . Does the pupil in a CN III palsy due to a PCom aneurysm typically present as dilated or constricted? Constricted Miotic Dilated Mydriatic Normal Irregular Pear-shaped 1 Parasympathetic fibers run on the outside of CN III. Compressive lesions like an PCom Aneurysm compress these fibers first causing a Dilated pupil. A patient with MS presents with pain on eye movement and decreased color vision. Swinging flashlight test shows a Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect RAPD . Diagnosis? Papilledema Optic Neuritis Glaucoma Cataract Macular Degeneration 1 Optic Neuritis is inflammation of the optic nerve common in MS. It causes painful vision loss and an RAPD . Horner s Syndrome Triad Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis Ophthalmology Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma Symptoms Pain Haloes Nausea Fixed Mid-dilated Pupil Ophthalmology Cherry Red Spot Diagnosis Central Retinal Artery Occlusion CRAO Ophthalmology Blood and Thunder Fundus Central Retinal Vein Occlusion CRVO Ophthalmology Retinal Detachment Symptoms Flashes Floaters Curtain falling Ophthalmology CN3 Palsy Appearance Down and Out Ophthalmology Painful Vision Loss + MS Optic Neuritis Ophthalmology Painless Vision Loss + Jaw Claudication Giant Cell Arteritis AION Ophthalmology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Ophthalmology > Red Eye / Retina alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Ophthalmology resource Pathoma chapter Eye Pathology resource BandB chapter Neurology > The Eye > Ophthalmology Basics Horner s vs CN3 Palsy Horner s Pupil is Small Miosis . Ptosis is mild.\\\\n CN3 Palsy Pupil is Large Dilated . Ptosis is severe Eye shut . The Painful Red Eye Sudden pain + Haloes + Hard eye = Acute Glaucoma .\\\\nPhotophobia + Autoimmune Hx = Uveitis .\\\\nContact Lens + Sleeping in them = Corneal Ulcer Pseudomonas . Retinal Detachment Warning New onset Flashes and Floaters is a Retinal Detachment until proven otherwise. Papilledema Bilateral Optic Disc Swelling due to increased Intracranial Pressure. In an obese young female think Pseudotumor Cerebri IIH . Hutchinson s Sign Shingles vesicle on the tip of the nose predicts ocular involvement Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l45", "title": "Lecture 45: Structure-Function Integration to Identify Lesions", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l45 Lecture 45 Structure-Function Integration to Identify Lesions\\n\\n Lecturers J. Xie Ph.D. & K. Poole Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 45\\n\\n 1. Brainstem Vascular Syndromes\\n Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg \\n Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery or Vertebral Artery.\\n Key Structures & Symptoms \\n Nucleus Ambiguus CN 9 10 Dysphagia Hoarseness Decreased Gang Reflex. Specific to Lateral .\\n Vestibular Nuclei Vertigo Nystagmus Vomiting.\\n Sympathetic Tract Horner s Syndrome Ipsilateral .\\n Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Loss of Pain/Temp on Ipsilateral Face .\\n Spinothalamic Tract Loss of Pain/Temp on Contralateral Body .\\n Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle Ataxia Fall toward lesion .\\n Mnemonic \\ Don t PICA horse hoarseness that can t eat dysphagia .\\ \\n Medial Medullary Syndrome \\n Artery Anterior Spinal Artery ASA .\\n Key Structures & Symptoms \\n Corticospinal Tract Contralateral Hemiparesis Upper & Lower limbs .\\n Medial Lemniscus Contralateral loss of Proprioception/Vibration.\\n CN XII Hypoglossal Tongue deviates Toward the lesion \\ Lick your wounds\\ .\\n Medial Pontine Syndrome \\n Artery Paramedian branches of Basilar Artery.\\n Key Contralateral Hemiparesis + CN VI Abducens palsy Eye can t look out .\\n\\n 2. Cranial Nerve & Vessel Associations\\n CN III Oculomotor \\n Runs between Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA and Superior Cerebellar Artery SCA .\\n Susceptible to PCom Aneurysms → Dilated Pupil + Down & Out Eye.\\n\\n 3. Cerebellar Lesions\\n Lateral Cerebellum Hemispheres \\n Affects Limbs .\\n Signs Dysdiadochokinesia Intention Tremor Dysmetria.\\n Side Ipsilateral fall/ataxia.\\n Medial Cerebellum Vermis/Flocculonodular \\n Affects Trunk .\\n Signs Truncal Ataxia Wide-based gait Nystagmus.\\n Alcohol Specific damage to Vermis .\\n\\n 4. Spinal Cord Tracts Review\\n Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML \\n Function Touch Vibration Proprioception.\\n Crossing Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers .\\n Spinothalamic Tract STT \\n Function Pain Temperature.\\n Crossing Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure 1-2 levels up .\\n Corticospinal Tract CST \\n Function Voluntary Motor.\\n Crossing Medulla Pyramids . Lecture 45 Structure-Function Integration to Identify Lesions Neurology questions A 65-year-old male presents with sudden onset vertigo vomiting and difficulty swallowing dysphagia . Exam shows a droopy left eyelid small left pupil loss of pain sensation on the left face and loss of pain sensation on the right body. Where is the lesion? Right Lateral Medulla Left Lateral Medulla Left Medial Medulla Right Medial Pons Left Lateral Pons 1 Left Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg Ipsilateral Horner s Ipsilateral Face sensory loss Contralateral Body sensory loss Dysphagia Nucleus Ambiguus . Lesion is on the Left . A patient presents with right-sided hemiparesis arm and leg and the tongue deviates to the left upon protrusion. Sensation of pain and temperature is intact. Which artery is likely occluded? Left PICA Right PICA Left Anterior Spinal Artery Right Anterior Spinal Artery Basilar Artery 2 Medial Medullary Syndrome Tongue deviates Toward lesion Left CN XII . Hemiparesis is Contralateral Right side . Artery is the Anterior Spinal Artery . Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration primarily affects which structure resulting in a wide-based ataxic gait? Flocculus Lateral Hemispheres Vermis Dentate Nucleus Inferior Olive 2 Chronic alcohol use causes atrophy of the Anterior Vermis leading to truncal ataxia and gait instability legs/trunk often sparing the arms. A cavernous sinus thrombosis would most likely affect which cranial nerve traveling through the sinus? Optic Nerve CN II Abducens Nerve CN VI Facial Nerve CN VII Vestibulocochlear Nerve CN VIII Hypoglossal Nerve CN XII 1 CN VI Abducens travels inside the cavernous sinus along with the Carotid Artery . CN III IV V1 V2 are in the lateral wall. CN VI is often the first affected. In Brown-S\\u00e9quard syndrome spinal cord hemisection pain and temperature sensation are lost Ipsilateral to the lesion at the level of the lesion Contralateral to the lesion 1-2 segments below Ipsilateral to the lesion 1-2 segments below Bilaterally at the level of the lesion only Completely preserved 1 Spinothalamic tract crosses almost immediately. A hemisection causes Contralateral loss of Pain/Temp starting 1-2 segments below the lesion. Wallenberg Syndrome Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Neurology Wallenberg Syndrome Key Symptom Dysphagia / Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus Neurology Medial Medullary Artery Anterior Spinal Artery ASA Neurology Medial Medullary Key Sign Tongue deviation to lesion side + Contralateral Hemiparesis Neurology Cerebellar Vermis Lesion Truncal Ataxia Drunk walk Neurology Cerebellar Hemisphere Lesion Limb Ataxia Finger-to-Nose Neurology CN 3 Palsy + Dilated Pupil PCom Aneurysm Neurology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Brainstem Syndromes alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Brainstem resource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Brainstem resource SketchyPath chapter Neurology > Brainstem Rule of 4s Medial vs Lateral Medial structures Motor pathway CST Medial Lemniscus MLF Motor CNs 3 4 6 12 .\\\\n Lateral structures Spinothalamic Spinocerebellar Spinal Trigeminal Sympathetic Sensory CNs 8 . Wallenberg Hallmark The ONLY brainstem stroke with Dysphagia/Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus is Lateral Medullary PICA . Tongue Deviation CN XII lesion Tongue deviates TOWARD the side of the lesion Lick your wounds . This is the hallmark of Medial Medullary Syndrome . PCom Aneurysm A Blown Pupil Dilated with a CN III palsy is a PCom Aneurysm until proven otherwise. Ischemic CN III palsy Diabetes spares the pupil. Cerebellar Hemisphere vs Vermis Lateral = L imbs Arm/Leg ataxia .\\\\n Medial Ver M is = Midline Trunk/Gait ataxia . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l46", "title": "Lecture 46: Sensory System & Special Techniques", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l46 Lecture 46 Sensory System & Special Techniques\\n\\n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O.\\n Session Lecture 46\\n\\n 1. Sensory Pathways Review\\n Spinothalamic Tract \\n Modality Pain Temperature Crude Touch.\\n Fibers Small Unmyelinated/Thinly myelinated.\\n Posterior Columns DCML \\n Modality Vibration Proprioception Fine Touch.\\n Fibers Large Heavily myelinated.\\n Testing Always test Distal first Toes/Fingers . If normal distally proximal is likely normal.\\n\\n 2. Cortical Sensation Discriminative \\n Requires Intact primary sensation DCML/STT + Intact Parietal Lobe Sensory Cortex .\\n Stereognosis Identify common object key coin in hand with eyes closed.\\n Graphesthesia Identify number drawn on palm.\\n Two-Point Discrimination Distinguish two points vs one.\\n Extinction Touch both sides simultaneously. If patient only feels one side suspects contralateral parietal lesion Neglect .\\n\\n 3. Meningeal Signs Meningitis/SAH \\n Brudzinski Sign \\n Maneuver Flex patient s neck chin to chest .\\n Positive Involuntary flexion of Hips and Knees .\\n Kernig Sign \\n Maneuver Flex hip/knee to 90 degrees then slowly Extend the knee.\\n Positive Pain/Resistance in hamstring.\\n\\n 4. Special Musculoskeletal Tests\\n Cervical Spine Radiculopathy \\n Spurling Test Extend and Rotate head to affected side + Axial Compression. Positive = Radiation of pain down arm.\\n Distraction Test Lift head Traction . Positive = Relief of pain.\\n Lumbar Spine Sciatica/Disc Herniation \\n Straight Leg Raise SLR Passive leg raise. Positive = Radicular pain between 30-70 degrees.\\n Bragard s Test SLR + Dorsiflexion of foot. Increases nerve stretch.\\n Crossed Well SLR Raise the Unaffected leg. Positive = Pain in the Affected leg. Highly specific for Disc Herniation .\\n Malingering / Functional \\n Hoover Test Cup both heels. Ask patient to lift the \\ paralyzed\\ leg.\\n Positive for Malingering You do NOT feel downward pressure from the \\ normal\\ heel. Normal physiology requires pushing down with the opposite leg to lift one leg . Lecture 46 Sensory System & Special Techniques Neurology questions You are performing a neurological exam on a patient claiming to have a paralyzed right leg. You cup your hands under both heels and ask him to lift his right leg. He strains but his leg doesn t move. You feel no pressure in your left hand under his left heel. What is the interpretation? True Right Leg Paralysis Left Leg Paralysis Positive Hoover Sign Lack of Effort Sciatic Nerve Impingement Cauda Equina Syndrome 2 Positive Hoover Sign Lack of downward pressure in the contralateral normal heel indicates the patient is not making a genuine effort to lift the affected leg. Which physical exam finding is most specific for a lumbar disc herniation? Positive Straight Leg Raise Ipsilateral Positive Crossed Well Straight Leg Raise Paraspinal muscle tenderness Decreased range of motion Negative Brudzinski sign 1 While the Ipsilateral SLR is sensitive the Crossed Well Straight Leg Raise pain in the affected leg when lifting the healthy leg is highly Specific for disc herniation. A patient presents with fever and severe headache. When you flex the patient s neck their hips and knees spontaneously flex. This is a positive Kernig Sign Brudzinski Sign Babinski Sign Lhermitte Sign Hoffmann Sign 1 Brudzinski Sign Neck flexion causes involuntary hip/knee flexion. It suggests meningeal irritation Meningitis . A patient complains of shooting pain down their right arm. You extend and rotate their head to the right and apply downward pressure. This maneuver reproduces the pain. What is the test? Distraction Test Spurling Test Tinel Sign Phalen Test Lhermitte Sign 1 Spurling Test Cervical compression with extension/rotation narrows the neural foramen. Reproduction of radicular pain indicates Cervical Radiculopathy. Which sensory modality is carried by the Dorsal Columns Posterior Columns ? Pain Temperature Crude Touch Vibration and Proprioception Itch 3 Dorsal Columns Vibration Proprioception Fine Touch. Spinothalamic = Pain Temp Crude Touch . Brudzinski Sign Neck flexion → Hip/Knee flexion Meningitis Neurology Kernig Sign Knee extension → Pain Meningitis Neurology Spurling Test Cervical Compression → Radicular Pain Radiculopathy Neurology Hoover Test Test for Malingering Lack of contralateral heel pressure Neurology Crossed Straight Leg Raise Lift Good Leg → Pain in Bad Leg Specific for Disc Herniation Neurology Stereognosis Identify object in hand Parietal Cortex Neurology Graphesthesia Identify number drawn on palm Parietal Cortex Neurology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Neurological Examination alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Exam resource BandB chapter Neurology > Clinical Neurology > Sensory Exam Hoover Test Logic In a True effort to lift one leg the other leg MUST push down. If you don t feel downward pressure from the good leg when they try to lift the bad leg they aren t really trying. BrudZinski vs Kernig B rudzinski = B rain Neck flexion causes knees to bend .\\\\n K ernig = K nee Knee extension causes pain . Spurling Test think Spurling crushes the Spine . Compression reproduces radicular pain. Distraction relieves it. Crossed SLR The Crossed Straight Leg Raise lifting the good leg causes pain in the bad leg is very Specific for a Herniated Disc. Stocking-Glove The classic pattern of Diabetic Polyneuropathy. Starts in toes/feet longest nerves and ascends. Sensory loss is symmetrical. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l48", "title": "Lecture 48: Pharmacology: Sedative Hypnotics", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l48 Lecture 48 Pharmacology Sedative Hypnotics Neurology Lecture 48 Pharmacology Sedative Hypnotics\\n\\n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 48\\n\\n 1. Mechanisms of CNS Depression\\n Story Mode \\nTo induce sedation or sleep we typically need to \\ calm down\\ the brain s excitatory pathways. The most effective way to do this is by enhancing the activity of GABA Gamma-Aminobutyric Acid the primary inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS. When GABA binds to its receptor it opens a chloride channel allowing negatively charged chloride ions $Cl^-$ to flow into the neuron. This causes hyperpolarization making the neuron less likelytor complex but interact with it in distinct ways that determine their safety profile.\\n\\n 1.1 GABA-A Receptor Interactions\\n GABA Dependent vs. Independent \\n Benzodiazepines & Z-Drugs \\n Bind to a specific site between $\\\\alpha$ and $\\\\gamma$ subunits .\\n Require GABA to be present to open the channel.\\n Effect Increase the frequency of channel opening.\\n Safety Exhibit a \\ Ceiling Effect\\ — once receptors are saturated they can t open more channels without GABA. Less risk of fatal respiratory depression alone.\\n Barbiturates & Ethanol \\n Bind to a distinct site Barbiturates between $\\\\alpha$ and $\\\\beta$ .\\n Do NOT require GABA at high doses can open the channel independently .\\n Effect Increase the duration of channel opening.\\n Safety No Ceiling Effect . Can cause profound CNS depression coma and fatal respiratory depression as dose increases.\\n\\n 1.2 Dose-Response Relationship\\n Linear Slope Drug A - Barbiturates/Alcohol \\n Steep linear increase in CNS depression with dose.\\n Sedation $\\\\rightarrow$ Hypnosis $\\\\rightarrow$ Anesthesia $\\\\rightarrow$ Coma $\\\\rightarrow$ Death.\\n Plateau Effect Drug B - Benzodiazepines \\n Curve flattens out.\\n Significant sedation but reduced risk of lethal respiratory depression unless combined with other depressants .\\n\\n correlate\\n Drug-Drug Interactions & Respiratory Depression \\n The Lethal Combo \\nWhile Benzodiazepines rarely cause fatal respiratory depression on their own due to the ceiling effect combining them with other CNS depressants removes this safety net.\\n Mechanism Synergistic effects on GABA/respiratory centers.\\n Combinations to Avoid \\n Benzodiazepines + Alcohol \\n Benzodiazepines + Opioids Fentanyl Heroin Oxycodone \\n Benzodiazepines + Barbiturates \\n Clinical Consequence A patient taking Xanax Alprazolam who drinks alcohol at a party runs a significantly higher risk of stopping breathing.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Barbiturates\\n Story Mode \\nOnce the mainstay of sedation Barbiturates \\ -barbital\\ have largely been replaced by Benzodiazepines due to their narrow therapeutic index and high abuse potential. However they retain niche uses in neurology epilepsy and anesthesia. Their ability to induce liver enzymes also makes them practically troublesome for drug interactions.\\n\\n 2.1 Key Agents & Uses\\n Phenobarbital \\n Use Seizure disorders Epilepsy . First-line for neonatal seizures .\\n Thiopental \\n Use Induction of anesthesia rapid onset .\\n Mechanism Highly lipid-soluble $\\\\rightarrow$ crosses Blood-Brain Barrier BBB instantly.\\n Primidone Metabolized to Phenobarbital.\\n\\n 2.2 Pharmacokinetics\\n Metabolism \\n Hepatic metabolism.\\n CYP Inducers especially CYP3A4 Increases metabolism of other drugs e.g. Oral Contraceptives $\\\\rightarrow$ unwanted pregnancy; Warfarin $\\\\rightarrow$ clotting risk .\\n Redistribution Thiopental \\n Rapid onset due to high blood flow to the brain.\\n Short duration not because of metabolism but because the drug redistributes to less vascular tissues muscle fat .\\n\\n 2.3 Adverse Effects\\n Respiratory Depression Fatal in overdose supported ventilation required; alkalize urine to trap and excrete .\\n Sedation/Hangover Especially in elderly.\\n Tolerance & Dependence \\n Metabolic Tolerance Enzyme induction leads to faster breakdown.\\n Pharmacodynamic Tolerance Receptor downregulation.\\n Contraindication Porphyria induces ALA synthase $\\\\rightarrow$ heme synthesis $\\\\rightarrow$ toxic porphyrin accumulation .\\n\\n correlate\\n Porphyria Exacerbation \\n The Mechanism \\nBarbiturates induce Cytochrome P450 enzymes. The synthesis of these enzymes requires heme . The body responds by ramping up heme production via ALA synthase.\\n In Porphyria Patients They have a genetic block in the heme pathway. Ramping up synthesis upstream leads to a toxic buildup of porphyrin intermediates.\\n Symptoms Abdominal pain psychiatric symptoms \\ madness\\ red/brown urine.\\n Rule Never give Barbiturates to a patient with acute intermittent porphyria.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Benzodiazepines\\n Story Mode \\nBenzodiazepines \\ -zolam\\ \\ -zepam\\ are the modern workhorses for anxiety acute seizure control and alcohol withdrawal. By requiring GABA to work they offer a safer profile. Choice of agent often depends on pharmacokinetics half-life and metabolic pathway especially in patients with \\ fragile\\ livers like the elderly.\\n\\n 3.1 Agents & Classification\\n Long-Acting \\n Diazepam Valium Chlordiazepoxide \\n Use Alcohol withdrawal smoother taper seizures muscle spasm.\\n Metabolism Converted to active metabolites e.g. desmethyldiazepam with very long half-lives 40-100 hours .\\n Intermediate/Short-Acting \\n Lorazepam Ativan Status epilepticus anxiety.\\n Alprazolam Xanax Panic disorder rapid onset high abuse potential.\\n Midazolam Versed Procedural sedation/anesthesia colonoscopy . Causes Anterograde Amnesia .\\n\\n 3.2 Metabolism & The Elderly\\n The Problem Elderly patients have reduced Phase I oxidative metabolism and renal function. Long-acting benzos Diazepam accumulate $\\\\rightarrow$ falls confusion pseudodementia .\\n The Solution \\ OTL\\ Use agents that undergo only Phase II Conjugation/Gluronidation .\\n O xazepam\\n T emazepam\\n L orazepam\\n\\n 3.3 Antidote Flumazenil\\n Mechanism Competitive antagonist at the Benzodiazepine binding site.\\n Use Reversal of Benzodiazepine overdose e.g. post-surgery sedation .\\n Warning Can precipitate acute withdrawal seizures in chronic benzodiazepine users. Administer with extreme caution.\\n\\n correlate\\n \\ Floppy Baby\\ Syndrome \\n Benzos in Pregnancy \\nBenzodiazepines cross the placenta.\\n Effect Neonatal CNS depression.\\n Presentation Hypotonia \\ rag doll\\ appearance lethargy difficulty sucking/feeding respiratory depression.\\n Contraindication Avoid benzos in pregnancy and breastfeeding if possible.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Z-Drugs & Insomnia\\n Story Mode \\nFor patients who just need sleep without the anxiolytic muscle relaxant or anticonvulsant effects of benzos we use \\ Z-Drugs\\ . They are structurally different but bind to a specific subtype of the benzodiazepine receptor BZ1/omega-1 that mediates sedation.\\n\\n 4.1 Agents\\n Zolpidem Ambien \\n Zaleplon Sonata \\n Eszopiclone Lunesta \\n\\n 4.2 Key Features\\n Mechanism Agonist at BZ1 receptor subtype.\\n Effect Induces sleep with minimal effect on sleep architecture REM/Slow Wave Sleep preserved better than benzos .\\n Pharmacokinetics Short half-life $\\\\rightarrow$ less daytime \\ hangover\\ .\\n Adverse Effects Sleep-walking/driving/eating behaviors.\\n Reversal Can be reversed by Flumazenil .\\n\\n 4.3 Melatonin Agonists\\n Ramelteon Tasimelteon \\n Mechanism Agonists at MT1/MT2 receptors in Suprachiasmatic Nucleus.\\n Use Sleep-onset insomnia Non-24 blind patients .\\n Benefit No abuse potential non-controlled no withdrawal.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 5. Ethanol Alcohol \\n Story Mode \\nEthanol is a \\ dirty drug\\ that hits multiple targets. It potentiates GABA sedation and inhibits NMDA/Glutamate loss of memory/excitation . Chronic use leads to a compensatory upregulation of NMDA receptors which explains the dangerous hyperexcitability seen in withdrawal.\\n\\n 5.1 Metabolism\\n1. Ethanol $\\\\xrightarrow Alcohol\\\\ Dehydrogenase $ Acetaldehyde Toxic flushing nausea \\n2. Acetaldehyde $\\\\xrightarrow Aldehyde\\\\ Dehydrogenase $ Acetate Excreted \\n Zero-Order Kinetics Enzymatic pathways saturate easily. Rate of elimination is constant regardless of concentration.\\n\\n 5.2 Alcohol Withdrawal\\n Mechanism Chronic suppression of NMDA + upregulation of receptors. Abrupt stop $\\\\rightarrow$ unopposed glutamate excitation.\\n Symptoms \\n 6-12h Tremors anxiety insomnia.\\n 12-48h Seizures hallucinosis.\\n 48-96h Delirium Tremens DTs - Autonomic instability BP/HR spikes delirium fatal if untreated.\\n Treatment \\n Benzodiazepines Long-acting Chlordiazepoxide/Diazepam to taper Lorazepam if liver failure .\\n\\n 5.3 Alcohol Use Disorder Treatments\\n Disulfiram Inhibits Aldehyde Dehydrogenase $\\\\rightarrow$ Acetaldehyde buildup upon drinking $\\\\rightarrow$ sickness negative reinforcement .\\n Naltrexone Opioid antagonist $\\\\rightarrow$ reduces \\ reward\\ /craving properties of alcohol dopamine link .\\n Acamprosate NMDA antagonist/GABA modulator $\\\\rightarrow$ reduces craving/withdrawal dysphoria.\\n\\n correlate\\n Fetal Alcohol Syndrome FAS \\n Mechanism Maternal alcohol assumption creates teratogenic accumulation of acetaldehyde and ROS.\\n Presentation \\n Growth Retardation IUGR .\\n Facial Dysmorphia Smooth philtrum thin upper lip small eye openings microphthalmia .\\n CNS Microcephaly intellectual disability behavioral issues.\\n Safe Limit There is no known safe amount of alcohol in pregnancy.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 6. Narcolepsy & Stimulants\\n Story Mode \\nIf the problem is too much sleep Narcolepsy or inability to focus ADHD we need to ramp up Norepinephrine and Dopamine. Note that one unique treatment Sodium Oxybate is actually a profound depressant used to force deep sleep at night to improve daytime wakefulness.\\n\\n 6.1 Stimulants\\n Amphetamines Adderall / Methylphenidate Ritalin \\n Mechanism Block reuptake AND increase release of NE/DA.\\n Use ADHD Narcolepsy.\\n SE Tachycardia hypertension anxiety appetite suppression weight loss growth stunting in kids.\\n Modafinil \\n Mechanism Unclear likely increases DA release; effects on Orexin/Histamine.\\n Use Narcolepsy Shift-Work Sleep Disorder.\\n Benefit \\ Wakefulness promoting\\ with less sympathomimetic jittery side effects than amphetamines.\\n\\n 6.2 Sodium Oxybate GHB \\n Mechanism GABA-B agonist.\\n Use Narcolepsy with Cataplexy sudden loss of muscle tone .\\n Effect Consolidates deep sleep at night $\\\\rightarrow$ improved daytime alertness.\\n Safety \\ Date Drug\\ potential. highly restricted REMS program . CNS depression risk.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 7. Transcript-Slide Gap Analysis\\n| Topic | Transcript Emphasis Included | Slide Only Excluded |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Elderly & Metabolism | Strong emphasis on why Lorazepam is preferred conjugation only and Diazepam is avoided active metabolites . | Simply listing metabolic pathways without the clinical \\ why\\ . |\\n| Redistribution | Detailed explanation of Thiopental kinetics Brain $\\\\rightarrow$ Muscle/Fat is why you wake up NOT metabolism. | Charts of distribution kinetics. |\\n| Situational Anxiety | Specific mention of Propranolol for stage fright violinist example to block peripheral symptoms without sedation. | General list of beta-blocker uses. |\\n| Fomepizole | Mentioned as antidote for Methanol/Ethylene Glycol antifreeze poisoning primarily distinct from Ethanol treatment. | Chemical structures of alcohols. |\\n| Date Drugs | Context on Flunitrazepam Rohypnol and GHB causing anterograde amnesia as a mechanism for assault. | Generic list of illicit substances. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n 8. AnKing & Resources\\n Primary \\n Zanki Pharmacology CNS Pharmacology Sedative-Hypnotics \\n Alternatives \\n Boards and Beyond Pharmacology > CNS > Anxiolytics & Hypnotics\\n Sketchy Pharm CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics The \\ Relaxing Barbershop\\ for Barbs \\ Ben s Diner\\ for Benzos .\\n Sketchy Pharm CNS Pharmacology > Alcohol Drugs \\ Al-Kohol s Bar\\ .\\n First Aid CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics & Alcohol.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 9. High Yield Slides\\n Mechanism of Action Diagrams Showing the GABA-A receptor and binding sites for Benzodiazepines vs. Barbiturates.\\n Dose-Response Curves The linear line Barbs/Coma vs. the plateau line Benzos/Safety .\\n Metabolic Pathways Diagram showing Phase I vs Phase II highlighting the \\ OTL\\ benzos skipping Phase I.\\n Alcohol Withdrawal Timeline Timeline showing Tremors $\\\\rightarrow$ Seizures $\\\\rightarrow$ DTs.\\n Narcolepsy Treatment Algorithm Flowchart involving Modafinil/Stimulants/Sodium Oxybate. questions A 75-year-old female requires a benzodiazepine for anxiety. Which agent is most appropriate to minimize the risk of accumulation falls? Diazepam Chlordiazepoxide Lorazepam Clorazepate Flurazepam 2 Lorazepam is an OTL benzo that only undergoes Phase II metabolism conjugation which is preserved in aging. Others have long-acting metabolites. Which sedative-hypnotic drug overdose can be reversed with Flumazenil? Phenobarbital Ethanol Zolpidem Thiopental Sodium Oxybate 2 Zolpidem a Z-drug acts at the Benzodiazepine binding site and CAN be antagonized by Flumazenil. Barbiturates Alcohol and GHB work at different sites/mechanisms. Which Benzodiazepines are safe in liver failure/elderly? OTL Oxazepam Temazepam Lorazepam Pharmacology Mechanism of Barbiturates Potentiate GABA-A Increase Duration of opening Pharmacology Mechanism of Benzodiazepines Potentiate GABA-A Increase Frequency of opening Pharmacology Zanki Pharmacology CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics BandB Pharmacology > CNS > Anxiolytics & Hypnotics Sketchy CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics OTL for Elderly The OTL benzos O xazepam T emazepam L orazepam bypass Phase I oxidation and only undergo Phase II conjugation. This makes them safer for the elderly. Barbs vs Benzos BarbiDURATes increase DURATion .\\\\n Frenzodiazepines increase FREQquency . Redistribution You wake up from Thiopental NOT because it s metabolized but because it leaves the brain to go into fat/muscle Redistribution . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l49", "title": "Lecture 49: Sleep and Sleep Disturbances", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l49 Lecture 49 Sleep and Sleep Disturbances Neurology Lecture 49 Sleep and Sleep Disturbances\\n\\n 1. Sleep Physiology\\n Story Mode \\nSleep isn t just a passive \\ off\\ switch; it s a highly active metabolic state essential for clearing neurotoxins like amyloid-beta and tau and consolidating memory. It follows a circadian rhythm 24-hour cycle driven by internal biological clocks. Interestingly without external cues sunlight our natural rhythm drifts to about 25 hours. This cycle orchestrates a shift from the catabolic/feeding state of wakefulness to the anabolic/fasting state of sleep prioritizing growth hormone secretion and oxidative repair.\\n\\n 1.1 Sleep Stages & EEG Architecture Slide 97 \\n Wakefulness \\n EEG Alpha waves 8-13 Hz when awake at rest; Beta waves >13 Hz when active.\\n Tone High muscle tone.\\n N1 Light Sleep \\n EEG Theta waves 4-7 Hz . Slowing of brain activity.\\n Signs Eyelids droop pupils constrict slow roving eye movements.\\n N2 Intermediate Sleep \\n EEG Sleep Spindles bursts of fast activity and K-complexes on a background of Theta.\\n Significance Most of total sleep time.\\n N3 Deep/Slow Wave Sleep \\n EEG Delta waves <4 Hz slow and high amplitude .\\n Function Physical restoration clearance of neurotoxins. Hardest to wake from.\\n REM Rapid Eye Movement \\n EEG Sawtooth/Erratic waves looks like wakefulness - \\ Paradoxical Sleep\\ .\\n Physiology Slide 154 \\n Brain Vivid illogical dreaming.\\n Body Atonia near-complete paralysis to prevent acting out dreams.\\n Autonomic Instability irregular HR/BP loss of thermoregulation.\\n Extraocular Muscles The only muscles that move rapid bursts .\\n\\n 1.2 Sleep Architecture & Latency\\n Cycles We do NOT cycle 1->2->3->REM->3->2->1 sequentially. Cycles vary; REM predominates later in the night early morning .\\n Sleep Latency Time from \\ lights out\\ to N1.\\n REM Latency Time from Sleep Onset to first REM period.\\n Clinical Relevance Short REM latency is a hallmark of Narcolepsy and Depression .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Dyssomnias Disorders of Timing/Quality \\n Story Mode \\nDyssomnias are problems with getting to sleep staying asleep or waking up. They can be intrinsic primary or extrinsic secondary to environment/illness . The most common offender is Insomnia which is often a symptom of poor habits sleep hygiene rather than a primary disease.\\n\\n 2.1 Insomnia Slide 192 \\n Definition Difficulty initiating or maintaining sleep resulting in daytime fatigue.\\n Acute Common 95% of adults often stress-related.\\n Chronic >3 months affects 10% of adults.\\n Secondary Causes 90% \\n Psychiatric Depression Anxiety PTSD.\\n Medical COPD Heart Failure PND GERD Pain.\\n Substances Caffeine Alcohol disrupts deep sleep Steroids Diuretics nocturia .\\n Treatment Hierarchy \\n 1. Treat Underlying Cause e.g. control pain stop caffeine .\\n 2. Sleep Hygiene Gold Standard \\n Same wake/sleep time daily.\\n Bedroom = Sleep & Sex ONLY no screens/stress .\\n Avoid late meals/alcohol.\\n 3. Pharmacology \\n First Line \\ Z-Drugs\\ Zolpidem Zaleplon Eszopiclone or Melatonin agonists Ramelteon .\\n Second Line Benzodiazepines Avoid in elderly due to falls; decreases N3 sleep .\\n Last Resort Antipsychotics/Antidepressants High side effects .\\n\\n 2.2 Narcolepsy Slide 315 \\n Story Mode \\nImagine REM sleep—with its paralysis and vivid dreams—suddenly intruding into your waking life. That is Narcolepsy. You might collapse while laughing Cataplexy because the REM paralysis switch got flipped while you were awake.\\n Pathophysiology Loss of Orexin Hypocretin producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus.\\n Genetic Link Strong association with HLA-DQB1 0602 .\\n Tetrad of Symptoms \\n 1. Excessive Daytime Sleepiness \\ Sleep Attacks\\ .\\n 2. Cataplexy Sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by strong emotion laughter surprise .\\n 3. Sleep Paralysis Inability to move upon waking/falling asleep.\\n 4. Hallucinations \\n Hypnagogic Go-ing to sleep.\\n Hypnopompic Pop-ping out of bed waking .\\n Diagnosis Polysomnogram showing Short REM Latency Sleep Onset REM periods .\\n Treatment Stimulants Modafinil Methylphenidate + Venlafaxine/SSRI for Cataplexy .\\n\\n 2.3 Obstructive Sleep Apnea OSA Slide 349 \\n Mechanism Soft tissue tongue/palate collapses during sleep $\\\\rightarrow$ Airway obstruction $\\\\rightarrow$ Hypoxia $\\\\rightarrow$ Arousal. Prevents deep sleep.\\n Risk Factors Obesity Large Neck Circumference Male Gender Retrognathia small jaw .\\n Presentation Snoring witness apnea partner usually reports it morning headaches daytime somnolence.\\n Diagnosis Polysomnogram Sleep Study .\\n Treatment \\n CPAP Continuous Positive Airway Pressure Pneumatic splint to keep airway open.\\n Weight loss.\\n Surgery rarely .\\n\\n 2.4 Restless Leg Syndrome RLS Slide 268 \\n Symptom Uncomfortable sensation creeping/crawling in legs at rest relieved by movement .\\n Timing Worse at night/evening.\\n Secondary Causes Iron Deficiency Pregnancy Renal Failure.\\n Treatment \\n Check Iron Ferritin levels first!\\n Dopamine Agonists Pramipexole Ropinirole Gabapentin/Pregabalin.\\n\\n correlate\\n Circadian Rhythm Disorders \\n Jet Lag \\n Mismatch between internal clock and external time.\\n Treatment Melatonin; adapt to destination light/dark cycle immediately.\\n\\n Delayed Sleep Phase \\n \\ Night Owls\\ . Sleep 2am - 10am. Common in adolescents.\\n Treatment Light therapy in morning.\\n\\n Advanced Sleep Phase \\n \\ Early Birds\\ . Sleep 8pm - 4am. Common in elderly.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Parasomnias Disorders of Behavior \\n Story Mode \\nParasomnias are unwanted behaviors occurring during sleep. The key distinction is WHEN they occur Non-REM N3 vs. REM. N3 parasomnias are \\ disorders of arousal\\ where the brain is half-awake but memory is off. REM parasomnias are \\ disorders of disinhibition\\ where the paralysis that usually protects us fails.\\n\\n 3.1 Non-REM Parasomnias Stage N3 \\n Sleep Terrors Pavor Nocturnus \\n Who Children 1% .\\n Event Child screams looks terrified autonomic markers high HR sweating . Cannot be consoled .\\n Outcome Goes back to sleep. Amnesia for the event in the morning.\\n Tx Reassurance grow out of it .\\n Sleepwalking Somnambulism \\n Who Children up to 30% rarely adults.\\n Event Motor activity walking eating driving while asleep.\\n Risk Injury/Safety.\\n Tx Safety measures locks/alarms . Benzodiazepines if severe/refractory.\\n\\n 3.2 REM Parasomnias\\n REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD \\n Mechanism Loss of normal REM atonia paralysis .\\n Event Patient acts out vivid/violent dreams punching kicking yelling .\\n Risk Injury to self or bed partner.\\n Clinical Pearl Highly predictive of future Synucleinopathies Parkinson s Disease Lewy Body Dementia .\\n Tx Clonazepam Melatonin.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Sleep Deprivation & Safety\\n Story Mode \\nA tired doctor is a dangerous doctor. Sleep deprivation specifically impairs the Prefrontal Cortex the center for judgment and executive function. This leads to rigid thinking inability to adapt to new info and increased risk-taking—a recipe for medical errors.\\n\\n 4.1 Consequences\\n1. Cognitive 4-5 hours sleep/night causes cumulative impairment equal to intoxication. Caffeine does NOT fix judgment errors.\\n2. Immune <7 hours sleep = 3x risk of Common Cold. Reduced vaccine response.\\n3. Fatal Familial Insomnia Prion disease. Progressive insomnia $\\\\rightarrow$ Death. Proves sleep is vital for life .\\n\\n highyield\\n The Intern Studies \\n House Staff & Driving \\nResidents working frequent 24+ hour shifts are a high-risk population.\\n 49% reported falling asleep at the wheel.\\n 90% of these events occurred post-call .\\n Result Increased MVAs and Near-Misses.\\n Takeaway You cannot \\ train\\ yourself to not need sleep. It is a biological imperative for safety.\\n questions A 68-year-old male presents with a complaint from his wife that he \\ fights\\ in his sleep. She reports he punches and yells and recently fell out of bed. When awakened he vividly recalls a dream where he was fighting off an attacker. What is the most likely diagnosis? Sleep Terrors Somnambulism REM Sleep Behavior Disorder Obstructive Sleep Apnea Nocturnal Seizures 2 The patient acts out dreams due to a loss of REM atonia. Key features are the violent nature occurring in an older male and recall of the dream . This is distinct from Sleep Terrors children no recall and is a risk factor for Parkinson s. A 22-year-old medical student reports difficulty staying awake in class. She reports falling asleep instantly when she lies down. She also describes episodes of sudden weakness in her knees when she laughs at a joke. Which of the following is likely deficient in her CNS? Dopamine Serotonin Acetylcholine Orexin Hypocretin GABA 3 This is Narcolepsy . The presentation of Excessive Daytime Sleepiness + Cataplexy muscle weakness with emotion is classic. It is caused by the autoimmune destruction of Orexin-producing neurons in the hypothalamus. Which of the following sleep stages is correctly matched with its characteristic EEG waveform? Stage N1 - Delta Waves Stage N2 - Sleep Spindles and K-Complexes Stage N3 - Alpha Waves REM Sleep - Delta Waves Wakefulness Active - Theta Waves 1 Stage N2 is characterized by Sleep Spindles and K-Complexes on a Theta background. N1 has Theta. N3 has Delta Slow waves . REM has \\ Sawtooth\\ or Beta-like waves. Wakefulness has Alpha rest or Beta active . A 45-year-old female complains of an uncomfortable \\ creeping\\ sensation in her legs every evening that makes it hard to relax. Walking around the room relieves the feeling. What is the most appropriate initial diagnostic step? Polysomnogram Nerve Conduction Study Serum Ferritin level MRI of Lumbar Spine Trial of Benzodiazepines 2 This is Restless Leg Syndrome RLS . It is strongly associated with Iron Deficiency . Checking iron stores Ferritin is mandatory before starting symptomatic treatment with dopamine agonists or gabapentin. What is the effect of chronic sleep deprivation on the immune system? Increased antibody response to vaccines Decreased risk of upper respiratory infections Increased susceptibility to common cold Rhinovirus Enhanced T-cell proliferation No significant effect 2 Study data shows that <7 hours of sleep significantly increases the risk of contracting the common cold Rhinovirus and lowers antibody responses to vaccinations. What sleep stage do Sleep Terrors and Sleepwalking occur in? Stage N3 Deep/Slow Wave Sleep Neurology What sleep stage do Nightmares occur in? REM Sleep Patient remembers them Neurology Mechanism of Narcolepsy Loss of Orexin Hypocretin neurons in lateral hypothalamus Neurology What genetic allele is associated with Narcolepsy? HLA-DQB1 0602 Neurology Clinical Tetrad of Narcolepsy EDS Cataplexy Sleep Paralysis Hypnagogic/pompic Hallucinations Neurology REM Sleep Behavior Disorder is a precursor to which diseases? Synucleinopathies Parkinson s Lewy Body Dementia Neurology First-line pharmacotherapy for Insomnia after hygiene Z-Drugs Zolpidem etc. or Melatonin agonists Pharmacology Secondary causes of Restless Leg Syndrome Iron Deficiency Check Ferritin! Pregnancy Renal Failure Neurology Effect of Benzodiazepines on Sleep Architecture Decreases N3 Deep Sleep Pharmacology Diagnosis of frequent snoring + daytime sleepiness in obese male Obstructive Sleep Apnea Diagnose with Polysomnogram Neurology Zanki Pharmacology CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics BandB Neurology > Sleep > Sleep Disorders Sketchy Path Neurology > Sleep First Aid Psychiatry > Sleep Disorders REM vs N3 Parasomnias N3 Deep Sleep Terrors Walking. NO Memory. Children.\\\\n REM Behavior Disorder Acting out dreams . Vivid Memory. Elderly Parkinson s risk . Cataplexy Trigger Cataplexy sudden loss of tone in Narcolepsy is specifically triggered by strong emotion most commonly laughter . Ferritin & RLS Always check Iron/Ferritin in clear-cut Restless Leg Syndrome. Iron deficiency is a reversible cause. Sleep Latency Definitions Sleep Latency Time to fall asleep High in Insomnia Low in Sleep Deprivation .\\\\n REM Latency Time to first dream Low in Narcolepsy & Depression . Z-Drugs Zolpidem Zaleplon Eszopiclone. They bind the BZ1 receptor subtype to induce sleep without the muscle relaxant/anticonvulsant effects of Benzos. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l50", "title": "Lecture 50: Seizures and Epilepsy", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l50 Lecture 50 Seizures and Epilepsy Neurology Lecture 50 Seizures and Epilepsy\\n\\n 1. Definitions & Pathophysiology\\n Story Mode \\nClassically we think of a seizure as a \\ storm\\ in the brain. Physiologically this is an imbalance between Excitation Glutamate and Inhibition GABA . Too much gas or not enough brakes leads to hypersynchronous aberrant neuronal firing.\\n Seizure Transient occurrence of signs/symptoms due to abnormal excessive or synchronous neuronal activity.\\n Epilepsy A disease of the brain defined by \\n 1. At least two unprovoked seizures >24 hours apart.\\n 2. One unprovoked seizure with a high probability >60% of recurrence e.g. structural lesion on MRI .\\n 3. Diagnosis of an Epilepsy Syndrome.\\n\\n 1.1 The \\ VITAMINS\\ of Etiology\\nCommon causes of seizures to remember \\n V ascular Stroke especially in elderly \\n I nfection Meningitis Encephalitis \\n T rauma TBI \\n A utoimmune Anti-NMDA \\n M etabolic Hypoglycemia Hyponatremia \\n I ngestion/Withdrawal Alcohol withdrawal Benzos \\n N eoplasm Tumors \\n S ychogenic Pseudo-seizures / PNES \\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Seizure Semiology & Classification\\n Story Mode \\nThe old terms \\ Grand Mal\\ and \\ Petit Mal\\ are out. We now classify based on Onset Focal vs. Generalized and Awareness .\\n\\n 2.1 Focal Seizures Start in one hemisphere \\n Focal Aware Old Simple Partial \\n Neuron storm is limited to one spot. Patient is Awake/Aware .\\n Jacksonian March Activity \\ marches\\ along the motor strip Thumb $\\\\rightarrow$ Hand $\\\\rightarrow$ Arm .\\n Aura A focal aware seizure manifesting as a subjective sensation e.g. chemical smell rising epigastric sensation fear before spreading.\\n Focal Impaired Awareness Old Complex Partial \\n Seizure affects consciousness/mentation.\\n Automatisms Repetitive non-purposeful movements Lip smacking picking at clothes .\\n Localization Most common in Temporal Lobe Hippocampus/Amygdala .\\n\\n 2.2 Generalized Seizures Both hemispheres involved \\n Generalized Tonic-Clonic GTC \\n Tonic Phase Stiffening \\ Epileptic Cry\\ air forced through closed cords Cyanosis.\\n Clonic Phase Rhythmic jerking.\\n Postictal Limp unresponsive loud snoring airway risk! .\\n Absence Seizures Old Petit Mal \\n Clinically Sudden behavioral arrest \\ blank stare\\ <15 seconds. No postictal confusion.\\n Triggers Hyperventilation .\\n Mechanism Hyperpolarization of Thalamocortical Relay neurons leads to de-inactivation of T-Type Calcium Channels $\\\\rightarrow$ Burst firing.\\n Gene CACNA1H .\\n EEG 3 Hz Spike and Wave Pathognomonic .\\n Treatment Ethosuximide .\\n\\n correlate\\n Status Epilepticus \\n Definition \\n Seizure lasting > 5 minutes convulsive or > 10 minutes non-convulsive .\\n OR Two seizures without return to baseline in between.\\n Management \\n Medical Emergency! \\ Time is Brain\\ Excitotoxicity .\\n 1st Line Benzodiazepines IV Lorazepam /Ativan .\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Epilepsy Syndromes Genetics & Structural \\n Story Mode \\nWe are moving away from \\ Idiopathic\\ epilepsy effectively. Advanced genetics now link specific channelopathies to specific syndromes.\\n\\n 3.1 Ion Channelopathies\\n Dravet Syndrome \\n Gene SCN1A Nav1.1 Sodium Channel loss of function in inhibitory interneurons .\\n Presentation Febrile seizures in infancy $\\\\rightarrow$ Refractory epilepsy developmental regression. High mortality SUDEP .\\n EAST Syndrome \\n Acronym E pilepsy A taxia S ensorineural deafness T ubulopathy Kidney .\\n Gene KCNJ10 Kir4.1 Potassium channel in Astrocytes .\\n Mechanism Astrocytes can t buffer extracellular K+ $\\\\rightarrow$ Neurons depolarize.\\n\\n 3.2 Structural & Neurocutaneous Syndromes\\n Tuberous Sclerosis Complex TSC \\n Genes TSC1 Hamartin or TSC2 Tuberin .\\n Pathway Loss of inhibition of mTOR pathway $\\\\rightarrow$ Uncontrolled cell growth Hamartomas .\\n Clinical \\n Brain Cortical Tubers cause epilepsy Subependymal Nodules.\\n Skin Ash Leaf Spots Hypopigmented Shagreen Patches Facial Angiofibromas.\\n Heart Rhabdomyoma.\\n Kidney Angiomyolipoma.\\n Sturge-Weber Syndrome \\n Gene GNAQ Somatic Mosaicism NOT hereditary .\\n Clinical Port-Wine Stain V1/V2 distribution Leptomeningeal Angioma Glaucoma Seizures.\\n Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome LGS \\n Presentation Childhood <8yo . Intellectual disability.\\n Seizures Multiple types Atonic/Drop Attacks Tonic .\\n EEG Slow Spike & Wave < 2.5 Hz .\\n Gene GABRB3 GABA receptor subunit .\\n\\n 3.3 Autoimmune & Encephalitis\\n Anti-NMDA Receptor Encephalitis \\n Antibody IgG against NR1 subunit of NMDA receptor.\\n Demographics Young women. Associated with Ovarian Teratoma .\\n Course Viral prodrome $\\\\rightarrow$ Psychosis/Hallucinations $\\\\rightarrow$ Catatonia/Mute $\\\\rightarrow$ Hyperkinetic Orofacial Dyskinesia .\\n Rasmussen Encephalitis \\n Pathology T-Cell mediated chronic encephalitis affecting one hemisphere .\\n Outcome Progressive atrophy and hemiparesis.\\n Treatment Hemispherectomy Functional hemispherectomy usually in children due to neuroplasticity .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Workup & Treatment Modalities\\n 4.1 Diagnostics\\n EEG Gold standard. Can use Hyperventilation or Photic Stimulation to provoke.\\n Imaged \\n CT Emergency Rule out bleed/mass . Quick.\\n MRI Elective/Detailed. Looks for Mesial Temporal Sclerosis Cortical Dysplasia Lissencephaly Heterotopia .\\n\\n 4.2 Treatments\\n Pharmacology Goal is seizure freedom with minimal side effects.\\n Ketogenic Diet High Fat Low Carb. Mechanism specific unclear but effective in peds.\\n Surgical \\n Resection Lesionectomy or Lobectomy e.g. for Temporal Lobe Epilepsy .\\n Corpus Callosotomy Palliative. Prevents spread generalization of seizures protecting against drop attacks. Spares the Splenium to preserve some function.\\n Neuromodulation \\n Vagal Nerve Stimulator VNS Pacemaker for the vagus nerve.\\n RNS Responsive Neurostimulation Closed-loop system. Detects seizure pattern $\\\\rightarrow$ Zaps it to abort.\\n DBS Deep Brain Stimulation Anterior Nucleus of Thalamus.\\n\\n highyield\\n The \\ Do Not Miss\\ Associations \\n| Syndrome | Gene/Antibody | Key Feature |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Dravet | SCN1A | Febrile Szs $\\\\rightarrow$ Regression |\\n| Absence | CACNA1H | 3Hz Spike/Wave Ethosuximide |\\n| Lennox-Gastaut | GABRB3 | Atonic Drop Szs Slow Spike/Wave |\\n| Tuberous Sclerosis | TSC1/TSC2 | Cortical Tubers Ash Leaf Spots mTOR |\\n| Anti-NMDA | Anti-NR1 | Ovarian Teratoma Psychiatric onset |\\n questions A 6-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician by his teacher who reports he \\ zones out\\ multiple times a day for 10 seconds. He does not respond to his name during these episodes but immediately resumes activity afterward. Hyperventilation in the office reproduces the event. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of this disorder? Loss of GABA-A receptor function SCN1A sodium channel mutation T-type calcium channel hypersynchrony Glutamate excitotoxicity via NMDA receptors Potassium channel accumulation in astrocytes 2 This is Absence Epilepsy . The hallmark is a brief <15s stare triggered by hyperventilation with no postictal state. The pathophysiology involves hyperpolarization of thalamocortical relay neurons leading to rebound burst firing via T-type Calcium Channels . Treatment is Ethosuximide. A 24-year-old female presents with new-onset seizures and acute psychosis. She has been acting strangely with fluctuating catatonia and agitation. Physical exam reveals rhythmic tongue-smacking movements. An MRI of the pelvis is ordered. What is the physician looking for? Renal Angiomyolipoma Ovarian Teratoma Uterine Fibroids Polycystic Ovaries Bladder transitional cell carcinoma 1 This clinical picture Psychosis + Seizures + Orofacial Dyskinesia in a young woman is classic for Anti-NMDA Receptor Encephalitis . It is a paraneoplastic syndrome strongly associated with Ovarian Teratomas . A 4-month-old infant presents with a prolonged febrile seizure. Over the next year he develops multiple seizure types including myoclonic and atonic seizures and shows signs of developmental regression. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in SCN1A. What is the most likely diagnosis? West Syndrome Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome Dravet Syndrome Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy Benign Rolandic Epilepsy 2 Dravet Syndrome is a severe myoclonic epilepsy of infancy caused by an SCN1A mutation. It classically begins with prolonged febrile seizures in the first year of life and progresses to intractable epilepsy and intellectual disability. A patient with intractable epilepsy undergoes a corpus callosotomy. Which of the following structures is typically spared during this procedure to preserve inter-hemispheric transfer of visual information? Rostrum Genu Body Splenium Anterior Commissure 3 The Splenium posterior portion of the corpus callosum is often spared anterior 2/3rds callosotomy to preserve the transfer of visual information between the occipital lobes maintaining some functional connectivity while preventing the generalization of seizures. Which of the following EEG findings is paired with the correct Epilepsy Syndrome? 3 Hz Spike and Wave - Lennox Gastaut Syndrome Hypsarrhythmia - Childhood Absence Epilepsy Slow <2.5 Hz Spike and Wave - Lennox Gastaut Syndrome Centrotemporal Spikes - Dravet Syndrome Generalized Polyspike - Focal Temporal Lobe Epilepsy 2 Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome is characterized by a Slow Spike and Wave pattern <2.5 Hz on EEG. Note 3 Hz Spike/Wave is Absence. Hypsarrhythmia is West Syndrome. Centrotemporal spikes are Rolandic Epilepsy . What is the pathognomonic EEG finding in Absence Seizures? 3 Hz Spike and Wave complexes Neurology What gene mutation is associated with Dravet Syndrome? SCN1A Sodium Channel Nav1.1 Neurology A young woman presents with psychosis oro-facial dyskinesia and seizures. What tumor should be screened for? Ovarian Teratoma Anti-NMDA Receptor Encephalitis Neurology What cutaneous finding is pathognomonic for Tuberous Sclerosis? Ash Leaf Spots Hypopigmented macules Neurology What is the mechanism of action of Ethosuximide? Blocks T-Type Calcium Channels in the Thalamus Pharmacology First-line treatment for Status Epilepticus? IV Benzodiazepines Lorazepam Pharmacology What epilepsy syndrome is associated with GABRB3 mutation and slow <2.5Hz spike-wave EEG? Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome Neurology Gene mutation associated with Sturge-Weber Syndrome? GNAQ Somatic mosaicism Neurology Which antiepileptic drug is contraindicated in pregnancy highly teratogenic ? Valproate Neural Tube Defects Pharmacology What is the Jacksonian March ? Progressive spread of focal seizure activity along the motor homunculus Neurology Zanki Step 2 Neurology > Epilepsy BandB Neurology > Seizures > Epilepsy Syndromes Sketchy Path Neurology > Seizures First Aid Neurology > Pathology > Seizures Absence vs Focal Impaired Absence <15s immediate recovery provoked by hyperventilation.\\\\n Focal Impaired Awareness >30s postictal confusion automatisms common. Sturge-Weber Triad 1. Port-Wine Stain V1/V2 \\\\n2. Leptomeningeal Angioma Seizures \\\\n3. Glaucoma Increased IOP Tuberous Sclerosis Mnemonic ASH LEAF A sh leaf spots S hagreen patches H eart rhabdomyoma L ung hamartomas E pilepsy Cortical Tubers A ngiomyolipoma Renal F acial angiofibroma. Status Epilepticus Tx 1st Line Benzodiazepines Lorazepam . 2nd Line Fosphenytoin/Valproate/Levetiracetam. 3rd Line Pentobarbital/Propofol. Jacksonian March Focal motor seizure activity that spreads along the motor homunculus e.g. finger to hand to arm to face reflecting electrical spread in the precentral gyrus. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l51", "title": "Lecture 51: Differential Diagnosis of Memory Disorders", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l51 Lecture 51 Differential Diagnosis of Memory Disorders Neurology Lecture 51 Differential Diagnosis of Memory Disorders\\n\\n 1. Evaluation of Memory Loss\\n Story Mode \\nNot all memory loss is dementia. The first step is to distinguish between Delirium acute fluctuating inattention and Dementia chronic progressive . We must also rule out MCI Mild Cognitive Impairment where function is preserved despite memory complaints.\\n\\n 1.1 Delirium vs. Dementia\\n Delirium \\n Time Acute onset hours to days .\\n Course Fluctuating.\\n Key Feature Inattention and altered level of consciousness.\\n Cause Often underlying medical illness infection metabolic on top of a vulnerable brain dementia .\\n Dementia \\n Time Chronic months to years .\\n Course Progressive decline.\\n Key Feature Memory/Cognitive loss without altered consciousness until late stages .\\n\\n 1.2 Reversible Causes\\nBefore condemning a patient to a neurodegenerative diagnosis rule out the fixable stuff \\n Metabolic Hypothyroidism B12 deficiency.\\n Structural Subdural hematoma Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH .\\n Infectious Syphilis HIV.\\n Toxic Alcohol Wernicke s Medications Benzos Anticholinergics .\\n Psychiatric Pseudodementia Depression .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Major Neurodegenerative Dementias\\n Story Mode \\nDifferent dementias attack different parts of the brain leading to distinct \\ signatures.\\ Alzheimer’s hits memory Hippocampus . FTD hits personality Frontal lobes . LBD hits the visual cortex and motor system.\\n\\n 2.1 Alzheimer s Disease AD \\n Epidemiology Most common cause. Age is the 1 risk factor.\\n Genetics \\n Late Onset APOE e4 allele Risk factor not causative .\\n Early Onset APP Presenilin-1 Presenilin-2 Autosomal Dominant .\\n Presentation \\n Short-term memory loss Hippocampus .\\n Language deficits Word finding .\\n Visuospatial issues Getting lost .\\n Pathology/Imaging \\n MRI Hippocampal Atrophy Mesial Temporal Lobes .\\n PET Temporoparietal Hypometabolism .\\n Treatment \\n AChE Inhibitors Donepezil Rivastigmine GI side effects .\\n NMDA Antagonist Memantine Moderate-Severe .\\n Anti-Amyloid mAbs Lecanemab/Donanemab Risk of ARIA Amyloid Related Imaging Abnormalities - bleed/edema .\\n\\n 2.2 Vascular Dementia\\n Mechanism \\ Strokes\\ accumulating over time.\\n Subcortical Slow processing speed lethargy small vessel disease .\\n Multi-Infarct Step-wise decline.\\n Cerebral Amyloid Angiopathy CAA \\n Buildup of amyloid in vessel walls.\\n Presentation \\ Amyloid Spells\\ Transient focal deficits mimicking TIA and lobar hemorrhages .\\n\\n 2.3 Lewy Body Dementia LBD \\n Core Features \\n Parkinsonism Rigidity bradykinesia.\\n Visual Hallucinations detailed often people/animals e.g. Robin Williams case .\\n Fluctuations Good days/bad days.\\n REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Acting out dreams loss of atonia .\\n Treatment Caution EXTREME sensitivity to Antipsychotics can cause severe rigidity/death . Avoid Benzos.\\n\\n 2.4 Frontotemporal Dementia FTD \\n Presentation \\n Behavioral Variant Onset in 50s-60s. Disinhibition apathy loss of empathy hyperorality craving sweets compulsive behaviors Bruce Willis case . Memory is spared early on.\\n Primary Progressive Aphasia Language loss first.\\n Genetics MAPT GRN C9ORF72.\\n Management SSRIs for behavior. AChE inhibitors do NOT help.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Other Specific Etiologies\\n\\n 3.1 Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH \\n Triad \\ Wet Wobbly Wacky\\ \\n Urinary Incontinence Wet .\\n Gait Apraxia / Magnetic Gait Wobbly .\\n Dementia Wacky .\\n Pathophysiology Enlarged ventricles comprising white matter tracts legs/bladder fibers run medially .\\n Diagnosis/Rx High Volume Lumbar Puncture drain 30cc . If gait improves $\\\\rightarrow$ VP Shunt .\\n\\n 3.2 Prion Diseases\\n Mechanism Conversion of normal PrPc helical to abnormal PrPsc Beta-pleated sheet $\\\\rightarrow$ rapid spongiform degeneration.\\n Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease CJD \\n Presentation Rapidly progressive dementia + Startle Myoclonus + Ataxia. Death < 1 year.\\n Diagnosis MRI Cortical ribboning CSF RT-QuIC is gold standard; 14-3-3 is old/nonspecific .\\n Fatal Familial Insomnia FFI - The Mystery Case \\n Alfredo s Case 53yo male with profound insomnia dysautonomia sweating pinpoint pupils tachycardia and dream-enactment.\\n Course Thalamic degeneration prevents sleep $\\\\rightarrow$ Death.\\n\\n 3.3 Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy CTE \\n Cause Repetitive mild TBI Football Boxing .\\n Timeline Symptoms start years/decades after sports career.\\n Presentation \\n Mood/Behavior Explosivity aggression suicide Aaron Hernandez .\\n Cognitive Dementia later.\\n Motor Parkinsonism/ALS-like features.\\n Pathology Tauopathy.\\n\\n 3.4 Wernicke s Encephalopathy\\n Cause Thiamine B1 deficiency Alcoholics .\\n Triad Confusion Ataxia Ophthalmoplegia Nystagmus/Lateral Rectus palsy .\\n Rx IV Thiamine BEFORE Glucose to prevent precipitating Korsakoff s .\\n Korsakoff s Psychosis Chronic irreversible amnesia + Confabulation damage to mammillary bodies/thalamus .\\n\\n highyield\\n Dementia Buzzwords \\n| Diagnosis | Buzzword / Key Feature |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Alzheimer s | Hippocampal Atrophy Amyloid Plaques/Tau Tangles |\\n| LBD | Visual Hallucinations Parkingonism Neuroleptic Sensitivity |\\n| Frontotemporal | Disinhibition Hyperorality Young onset 50s |\\n| NPH | Wet Wobbly Wacky Magnetic Gait |\\n| CJD | Startle Myoclonus Rapid progression |\\n| Wernicke s | Ataxia Ophthalmoplegia Thiamine |\\n questions A 72-year-old male is brought in by his family for evaluated of \\ acting strange.\\ They report he has been talking to people who aren t there and sometimes thinks his wife is an imposter. On exam he has mild rigidity in the upper extremities. Which medication should be AVOIDED in this patient? Donepezil Carbidopa-Levodopa Haloperidol Memantine Melatonin 2 The patient has features of Lewy Body Dementia Visual Hallucinations Capgras delusion/imposter Parkinsonism . LBD patients have severe neuroleptic sensitivity . Typical antipsychotics like Haloperidol can precipitate severe rigidity catatonia or even death. A 55-year-old woman is brought by her husband for personality changes. She has been making inappropriate sexual comments to strangers eating only candy bars and spending thousands of dollars on ceramic figurines. Her memory for recent events is relatively preserved. What is the most likely diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Frontotemporal Dementia Behavioral Variant Bipolar Disorder Vascular Dementia Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease 1 This is the classic presentation of Behavioral Variant FTD Early onset <60 Disinhibition Hyperorality dietary changes compulsive behaviors and spared memory early in the course. A 68-year-old male presents with a \\ magnetic gait \\ where his feet appear stuck to the floor. He has also had several episodes of urinary incontinence and mild memory decline. A high-volume lumbar puncture is performed and his walking improves significantly. What is the next best step in management? Start Carbidopa-Levodopa Start Donepezil Refer for Ventriculoperitoneal Shunt Repeat Lumbar Puncture in 1 month Start Finasteride 2 This patient has Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus Wet Wobbly Wacky . The positive response to the large volume LP diagnostic tap predicts a good response to permanent CSF diversion via a VP Shunt . A 45-year-old female presents with rapid cognitive decline over the last 3 months. She has developed severe ataxia and startle myoclonus. An EEG shows generalized periodic sharp wave complexes. What is the gold standard diagnostic test for this condition? CSF 14-3-3 protein CSF RT-QuIC assay Brain Biopsy Serum Ceruloplasmin MRI with contrast 1 This is Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease CJD Rapid dementia myoclonus ataxia . While 14-3-3 was used historically RT-QuIC Real-Time Quaking-Induced Conversion is now the specific gold standard test on CSF to detect the abnormal prion protein. Which of the following findings is most strongly associated with Cerebral Amyloid Angiopathy? Subcortical lacunar infarcts Lobar intracerebral hemorrhages Tremor at rest Early gait instability Choroid Plexus Cyst 1 Cerebral Amyloid Angiopathy CAA involves amyloid deposition in the vessel walls of the cortex making them friable. This leads to recurrent Lobar Hemorrhages in the hemispheres sparing deep structures and \\ Amyloid Spells.\\ What is the classic triad of Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus? Urinary Incontinence Gait disturbance Dementia Wet Wobbly Wacky Neurology Which dementia is characterized by visual hallucinations and parkinsonism? Lewy Body Dementia Neurology What is the gold standard CSF test for CJD? RT-QuIC Real-time Quaking-Induced Conversion Neurology What MRI finding is associated with Alzheimer s Disease? Hippocampal Atrophy Neurology A patient with dementia develops acute rigidity and obtundation after receiving Haloperidol. What is the diagnosis? Severe Neuroleptic Sensitivity in Lewy Body Dementia Pharmacology What vitamin must be replenished before glucose in an alcoholic patient? Thiamine B1 - to prevent Wernicke s precipitation Neurology What specific sleep disorder is a prodrome for synucleinopathies LBD/Parkinson s ? REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Neurology What protein accumulates in Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy CTE ? Tau It is a Tauopathy Neurology Side effect concern for new Anti-Amyloid monoclonal antibodies Lecanemab ? ARIA Amyloid Related Imaging Abnormalities - Edema/Hemorrhage Pharmacology Genetic risk factor allele for Late-Onset Alzheimer s? APOE e4 Neurology Zanki Step 2 Neurology > Dementia BandB Neurology > Dementia Sketchy Path Neurology > Neurodegenerative Delirium vs Dementia Delirium is acute fluctuating and affects ATTENTION. Dementia is chronic progressive and affects MEMORY attention preserved until late . NPH Triad Wet Incontinence Wobbly Magnetic Gait Wacky Dementia . Treat with Shunt. LBD Neuroleptic Sensitivity Avoid typical antipsychotics Haldol in Lewy Body Dementia. They can cause severe parkinsonism and death. Wernicke s Tx Give Thiamine BEFORE Glucose. Glucose first depletes remaining thiamine and precipitates Wernicke s. FTD Features Disinhibition Apathy Hyperorality sweets Compulsive behaviors. Memory often spared early. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l52", "title": "Lecture 52: Pharmacology: Antiseizure Drugs 1", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "l52 Lecture 52 Pharmacology Antiseizure Drugs 1 Pharmacology Lecture 52 Pharmacology Antiseizure Drugs 1\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 52\\n\\n 1. Introduction to Seizures & Epilepsy\\n Story Mode \\nA seizure is essentially an electrical storm in the brain defined as a paroxysmal event due to abnormal excessive or synchronous neuronal activity. When a patient has recurrent seizures due to a chronic underlying process we diagnose Epilepsy . The goal of pharmacotherapy is to stabilize these neurons either by putting the brakes on excitation blocking Sodium/Calcium channels inhibiting Glutamate or stepping on the clutch enhancing GABA inhibition .\\n\\n 1.1 Classification Slide 6 8 \\n Focal Onset Seizures \\n Arise from a specific discrete local population of neurons in one hemisphere.\\n Mechanism Hyperexcitability due to rapidly firing Voltage-Gated Sodium Channels .\\n Evolution Can evolve into bilateral tonic-clonic seizures.\\n Primary Generalized Onset Seizures \\n Arise in both hemispheres simultaneously.\\n Involve deep structures Thalamus/Brainstem .\\n Status Epilepticus \\n Definition >5 minutes of continuous seizure activity OR >2 seizures without recovery of consciousness in between.\\n\\n 1.2 Pharmacokinetics Overview Slide 15-22 \\n Absorption/Distribution All ASDs Anti-Seizure Drugs must cross the BBB. Most are highly protein bound potential for displacement interactions .\\n Metabolism Most undergo hepatic metabolism.\\n Inducers Carbamazepine Phenytoin Phenobarbital.\\n Inhibitor Valproate.\\n Withdrawal Gradual taper is mandatory. Abrupt cessation can precipitate seizures or status epilepticus.\\n\\n correlate\\nSuicidality in ASDs\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Mechanism unclear; controversial FDA warning.\\n- Presentation New onset suicidal ideation or behavior.\\n- Lecturer s Point All ASDs carry a warning for increased risk of suicidality. Patients especially young ones should be monitored for mood changes.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Voltage-Gated Sodium Channel Blockers\\n Story Mode \\nThe \\ fast\\ sodium current is responsible for the upstroke of the action potential. In a seizure neurons fire repetitively at high frequencies. Sodium channel blockers work by binding to the channel in its Inactivated State preventing it from resetting to the active state. This prolongs the refractory period. Importantly these drugs exhibit Use-Dependence they bind more strongly to neurons that are firing rapidly seizing while sparing normal neurons firing at normal rates.\\n\\n 2.1 Mechanism of Action Slide 30-35 \\n The Cycle Resting Closed $\\\\rightarrow$ Depolarization $\\\\rightarrow$ Open Na+ in $\\\\rightarrow$ Inactivated Gate closed $\\\\rightarrow$ Repolarization $\\\\rightarrow$ Resting.\\n Fast Inactivation Enhancers \\n Drugs Phenytoin Carbamazepine Lamotrigine Oxcarbazepine .\\n Bind to the channel pore during the Open/Inactivated state.\\n Stabilize the channel in the inactivated state refractory .\\n Slow Inactivation Enhancer \\n Drug Lacosamide .\\n Stabilizes the \\ Slow Inactivation\\ state a conformational change occurring over seconds during prolonged depolarization .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Phenytoin PHT & Fosphenytoin\\n Story Mode \\nPhenytoin is the \\ classic\\ dirty drug of neurology. It’s effective but it follows Zero-Order Kinetics saturation kinetics meaning small dose increases can lead to exponential spikes in blood levels. It also has a laundry list of cosmetic and teratogenic side effects \\ P-H-E-N-Y-T-O-I-N\\ . Fosphenytoin was developed as a water-soluble prodrug to solve the infusion issues of Phenytoin Phlebitis/Purple Glove .\\n\\n 3.1 Pharmacokinetics Slide 40-55 \\n Absorption \\n Phenytoin Base Chewable/Suspension Erratic absorption.\\n Phenytoin Sodium Capsules Better absorption.\\n Distribution Highly protein bound Albumin .\\n Hypoalbuminemia Liver failure Nephrotic syndrome Pregnancy Leads to INCREASED Free Phenytoin levels $\\\\rightarrow$ Toxicity.\\n Displacement Uremia kidney failure displaces PHT from albumin.\\n Metabolism CRITICAL \\n Zero-Order Kinetics Enzymes CYP2C9/2C19 become saturated at therapeutic levels.\\n Elimination is constant amount per time not constant fraction .\\n Consequence Small dose increase $\\\\rightarrow$ Disproportionately LARGE increase in serum concentration.\\n Induction Potent INDUCER of CYP3A4 UGT etc. Eats up other drugs .\\n\\n 3.2 Formulations Phenytoin vs. Fosphenytoin Slide 43-45 \\n| Feature | Phenytoin IV | Fosphenytoin IV/IM |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Solubility | Poor Lipophilic | High Water soluble Prodrug |\\n| Solvent | Propylene Glycol + Ethanol pH 12 | Water based |\\n| Adverse Effects | Hypotension/Arrhythmias due to Propylene Glycol Phlebitis | Less Hypotension Minimal irritation |\\n| Infusion Rate | Must be SLOW risk of chemical burn | Can be faster |\\n| IM Use | CONTRAINDICATED Tissue necrosis | Safe for IM |\\n\\n 3.3 Adverse Effects Slide 61-68 \\n mnemonic\\n PHENYTOIN Side Effects \\n PHENYTOIN \\n\\n- P P -450 Inducer / P eripheral Neuropathy / P urple Glove\\n- H H irsutism excess hair \\n- E E nlarged Gums Gingival Hyperplasia / E rythema Multiforme SJS \\n- N N ystagmus / Neurotoxicity Ataxia Slurred speech \\n- Y Y ellowing Hepatotoxicity - rare \\n- T T eratogenicity Fetal Hydantoin Syndrome \\n- O O steomalacia Vit D deficiency from enzyme induction \\n- I I nterference with Folate Megaloblastic Anemia \\n- N N europathy\\n \\n\\n correlate\\nPurple Glove Syndrome\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Extravasation of IV Phenytoin highly alkaline + propylene glycol .\\n- Presentation Distal limb edema discoloration purple/blue pain.\\n- Complications Skin necrosis compartment syndrome amputation.\\n- Prevention Use Fosphenytoin instead.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\nFetal Hydantoin Syndrome\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Teratogenic effect of Phenytoin and Carbamazepine/Valproate .\\n- Presentation Cleft lip/palate Microcephaly Hypoplastic fingernails IUGR.\\n- Risk 2-3x risk of major malformations.\\n- Management Folic acid supplementation though may not fully prevent .\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Carbamazepine CBZ & Pharmacogenetics\\n Story Mode \\nCarbamazepine is chemically related to Tricyclic Antidepressants. It is a potent sodium channel blocker used for focal seizures. Its claim to fame is Auto-Induction it induces its own metabolism so half-life decreases over the first few weeks and severe genetically determined skin reactions.\\n\\n 4.1 Pharmacogenetics Slide 23-28 \\nScreening is MANDATORY in at-risk populations before starting Carbamazepine and chemically related Oxcarbazepine/Phneytoin/Lamotrigine .\\n\\n HLA-B 1502 \\n Population Asian ancestry Han Chinese South Asian .\\n Risk SJS / TEN Stevens-Johnson Syndrome / Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis .\\n Action SCREEN all Asian patients. If positive $\\\\rightarrow$ AVOID Carbamazepine.\\n HLA-A 3101 \\n Population European Japanese Korean.\\n Risk DRESS Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms .\\n\\n highyield\\n Auto-Induction \\nCarbamazepine induces CYP3A4 the very enzyme that metabolizes it.\\n Effect Serum levels DROP over the first few weeks of therapy despite constant dosing.\\n Management Dose may need to be increased after 3-4 weeks once auto-induction is maximal.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 5. Lamotrigine LTG \\n Story Mode \\nLamotrigine is the \\ Clean\\ broad-spectrum agent. It blocks Sodium channels AND inhibits Glutamate release. It is effective for almost everything Focal Generalized Absence Bipolar . Its major Achilles heel is the Life-Threatening Rash SJS which is why we must titrate it painfully slowly \\ Start low go slow\\ .\\n\\n 5.1 Pharmacokinetics & Dosing Slide 69-75 \\n Interaction with Valproate \\n Valproate inhibits UGT glucuronidation .\\n Effect DOUBLES Lamotrigine half-life 60 hours vs 24 hours .\\n Risk Massive increase in SJS/Rash risk .\\n Management reduce dose by 50% also titrate even SLOWER every other day dosing initially .\\n Interaction with Inducers Phenytoin/Carbamazepine \\n Decreases Lamotrigine half-life.\\n Management Higher loading/maintenance doses needed.\\n\\n 5.2 Clinical Uses\\n Broad Spectrum Focal Generalized Tonic-Clonic Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome Absence 2nd line .\\n Bipolar Disorder Maintenance treatment prevents Depressive episodes ; NOT for acute Mania.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 6. Lacosamide LCM \\n Story Mode \\nLacosamide is the newer cleaner cousin. Instead of fast inactivation it enhances Slow Inactivation . It has excellent bioavailability 100% minimal protein binding and renal excretion fewer drug interactions . The one specific catch is the heart.\\n\\n 6.1 Mechanism & Side Effects Slide 80-87 \\n Mechanism Enhances Slow Inactivation of Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels.\\n Key Toxicity PR Interval Prolongation .\\n Risk Bradycardia AV Block.\\n Action Get a baseline ECG in patients with known cardiac conduction issues.\\n Pharmacokinetics \\n Renally excreted 40% unchanged .\\n No major CYP interactions unlike Phenytoin/Carbamazepine .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 7. Summary Tables\\n\\n 7.1 Sodium Channel Blockers Comparison\\n| Drug | Mechanism | Key PK Feature | Key Toxicity | Clinical Pearl |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Phenytoin | Fast Inactivation | Zero-Order Kinetics ; Potent Inducer | Gingival Hyperplasia Hirsutism Purple Glove Osteomalacia | Fosphenytoin is safer for IV. |\\n| Carbamazepine | Fast Inactivation | Auto-Inducer | SJS HLA-B 1502 DRESS Hyponatremia SIADH | Screen Asians for HLA-B 1502. |\\n| Lamotrigine | Fast Inactivation + Glutamate Inhibit | Valproate Doubles Half-life | SJS Rash | \\ Start Low Go Slow\\ titration. |\\n| Lacosamide | Slow Inactivation | Renal Elimination | PR Prolongation | Get ECG aimed at cardiac patients. |\\n\\n highyield\\n Teratogenicity Hierarchy \\n1. Valproate HIGHEST Risk Neural Tube Defects/Spina Bifida - AVOID.\\n2. Phenytoin/Carbamazepine High Risk Cleft palate cardiac defects .\\n3. Lamotrigine/Levetiracetam Safest options for pregnancy.\\n questions A 35-year-old female with a history of epilepsy is well-controlled on Valproate. She recently started a new medication as add-on therapy for breakthrough seizures. Four weeks later she presents to the ER with a severe peeling rash affecting her face and trunk. Which medication was most likely added? Levetiracetam Gabapentin Lamotrigine Lacosamide Topiramate 2 This case describes Stevens-Johnson Syndrome/Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis. Lamotrigine carries a Black Box Warning for serious skin rashes. Valproate inhibits the glucuronidation of Lamotrigine increasing its half-life and serum levels which drastically increases the risk of this rash if the dose isn t significantly reduced and titrated slowly. A patient is started on an anti-seizure medication that follows zero-order kinetics at therapeutic concentrations. Which of the following best describes the pharmacokinetic consequence of this property? Half-life is constant regardless of plasma concentration A small increase in dose can lead to a disproportionately large increase in serum concentration The drug is eliminated by the kidneys unchanged The drug induces its own metabolism A constant fraction of the drug is eliminated per unit time 1 Phenytoin follows Zero-Order Saturation Kinetics at therapeutic levels. This means metabolic enzymes are saturated and a constant amount not fraction of drug is eliminated per unit time. Consequently once saturation is reached even small dose increases can cause massive spikes in toxicity. Which of the following patients should be screened for the HLA-B 1502 allele prior to starting Carbamazepine? A 50-year-old Caucasian male with kidney failure A 25-year-old African American female with hypertension A 30-year-old Han Chinese male with no other medical history A 70-year-old Hispanic female with liver disease A 40-year-old of Northern European descent 2 The HLA-B 1502 allele is strongly associated with Stevens-Johnson Syndrome SJS and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis TEN induced by Carbamazepine and Phenytoin/Lamotrigine in patients of Asian ancestry particularly Han Chinese Thai Malaysian and South Asian . Screening is mandatory in these populations. A 6-year-old child presents with gingival hyperplasia overgrown gums and hirsutism. Which anti-seizure medication is the child most likely taking? Ethosuximide Phenytoin Valproic Acid Gabapentin Levetiracetam 1 Gingival Hyperplasia and Hirsutism are classic cosmetic side effects of Phenytoin . They are not typically seen with the other agents listed. A patient requiring IV phenytoin for status epilepticus is mistakenly given the drug via a rapid IV push into a small hand vein. The hand subsequently becomes swollen painful and turns purple. What is the mechanism of this reaction? Allergic reaction to the sulfa moiety Precipitation of phenytoin crystals in the tissue due to high pH and propylene glycol Vasoconstriction caused by sodium channel blockade Clotting factor inhibition Autoimmune vasculitis 1 This is Purple Glove Syndrome . Phenytoin is poorly soluble and requires Propylene Glycol and a high pH 12 solvent. Rapid infusion or extravasation causes severe focal toxicity crystal precipitation and vasoconstriction/thrombosis leading to necrosis. Fosphenytoin avoids this risk. What is the specific HLA allele associated with SJS/TEN in Asian patients taking Carbamazepine? HLA-B 1502 Pharmacogenetics Which anti-seizure drug follows Zero-Order kinetics at therapeutic levels? Phenytoin Pharmacology What effect does Valproate have on Lamotrigine metabolism? Inhibits glucuronidation -> Doubles Lamotrigine half-life Risk of Rash Pharmacokinetics Name two cosmetic side effects specific to Phenytoin. Gingival Hyperplasia Hirsutism Adverse Effects What is the mechanism of action of Lacosamide? Enhances Slow Inactivation of Na+ channels Mechanism Why is Fosphenytoin preferred over Phenytoin for IV loads? Water soluble less risk of hypotension/bradycardia/Purple Glove Syndrome Clinical What is the classic adverse effect of Carbamazepine that requires blood monitoring besides agranulocytosis ? Hyponatremia SIADH Adverse Effects Which ASD is an Auto-Inducer? Carbamazepine Pharmacokinetics What cardiac adverse effect is associated with Lacosamide? PR Interval Prolongation Bradycardia/Heart Block Adverse Effects Which vitamin deficiency is caused by Phenytoin induction? Folate Megaloblastic anemia and Vitamin D Osteomalacia Nutrition Physeo Pharmacology > Neurology > Antiepileptics BandB Neurology > Pharmacology > Antiepileptic drugs Sketchy Pharm Neuro/Psych > Antiepileptics HLA-B 1502 Warning Always screen Asian patients for HLA-B 1502 before starting Carbamazepine. Positive = High Risk for SJS/TEN. Zero-Order Kinetics Phenytoin metabolism saturates. Small dose increase = Huge blood level spike. Lamotrigine + Valproate Valproate DOUBLES Lamotrigine levels. You must cut the Lamotrigine dose in half and titrate very slowly to avoid THE RASH. Purple Glove Syndrome Caused by Phenytoin extravasation propylene glycol/pH 12 . Use Fosphenytoin to avoid it. Gingival Hyperplasia Classic side effect of Phenytoin along with Hirsutism . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l53", "title": "Lecture 53: Pharmacology: Antiseizure Drugs, Part 2", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "l53 Lecture 53 Pharmacology Antiseizure Drugs Part 2 Pharmacology Lecture 53 Pharmacology Antiseizure Drugs Part 2\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 53\\n\\n 1. Broad Spectrum Agents\\n Story Mode \\nBroad-spectrum anti-seizure drugs ASDs are the \\ utility players\\ of epilepsy treatment. Because they target multiple mechanisms\\u2014often hitting sodium channels to stop firing and enhancing GABA to increase inhibition\\u2014they are effective against a wide variety of seizure types including focal generalized tonic-clonic and absence seizures. This makes them an excellent choice when the exact seizure type is unclear.\\n\\n 1.1 Valproate Divalproex \\n Mechanisms The definition of \\ Broad Spectrum.\\ \\n Blocks voltage-dependent Sodium $Na^+$ channels .\\n Blocks T-type Calcium $Ca^ 2+ $ channels Treats Absence .\\n Enhances GABA activity inhibitory .\\n Therapeutic Uses \\n Seizure First-line for many types Focal Generalized Absence Myoclonic. Used for Status Epilepticus off-label .\\n Non-Seizure Bipolar Disorder Acute Mania and Migraine Prophylaxis .\\n Pharmacokinetics \\n Mitochondrial metabolism Beta-oxidation .\\n Inhibitor of CYP enzymes increases levels of other drugs like Lamotrigine .\\n Adverse Effects \\n GI Nausea/Vomiting common limit by taking with food or ER formulations .\\n Cosmetic Weight Gain Hair Loss Alopecia Tremor.\\n Serious Hepatotoxicity Monitor LFTs Pancreatitis Thrombocytopenia .\\n Teratogenicity Neural Tube Defects Spina Bifida . Avoid in pregnancy if possible.\\n\\n correlate\\n Hyperammonemic Encephalopathy \\n The Mechanism \\nValproate is metabolized in the mitochondria and can interfere with the Urea Cycle.\\n Result Accumulation of Ammonia $NH 3$ in the blood.\\n Presentation Acute change in mental status confusion lethargy in a patient taking Valproate even with normal liver enzymes.\\n Risk Factor Concurrent use with Topiramate synergistic risk .\\n \\n\\n 1.2 Topiramate Topamax \\n Mechanisms \\n Blocks $Na^+$ channels.\\n Enhances GABA-A activity.\\n Antagonizes AMPA/Kainate Glutamate receptors.\\n Weak Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitor .\\n Therapeutic Uses \\n Seizure Focal Generalized Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome.\\n Non-Seizure Migraine Prophylaxis Weight Loss in combination with Phentermine .\\n Adverse Effects \\n CNS \\ Dope-a-max\\ \\u2013 Cognitive slowing word-finding difficulties sedation paresthesias tingling .\\n Weight Loss contrast with Valproate .\\n Renal Kidney Stones Calcium Phosphate stones due to alkaline urine from carbonic anhydrase inhibition .\\n Ocular Acute Myopia/Angle Closure Glaucoma.\\n\\n correlate\\n Hypohidrosis & Hyperthermia \\n The Mechanism \\nTopiramate s carbonic anhydrase inhibition can impair sweat gland function.\\n Target Children are most vulnerable.\\n Risk Reduced sweating $\\\\downarrow$ heat dissipation leads to dangerous overheating Hyperthermia during play or hot weather.\\n Management Counsel parents to monitor for lack of sweating during exertion.\\n \\n\\n 1.3 Levetiracetam Keppra \\n Mechanism Unique. Binds to SV2A Synaptic Vesicle Protein 2A .\\n Prevents vesicle docking and neurotransmitter release.\\n Pharmacokinetics \\n Excellent Profile 100% Bioavailability Renal excretion unchanged No drug-drug interactions .\\n Adverse Effects \\n Generally well-tolerated sedation dizziness .\\n Neuropsychiatric \\ Keppra Rage\\ \\u2013 Irritability agitation aggression or psychosis rare .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Narrow Spectrum Agents\\n Story Mode \\nUnlike the broad-spectrum agents these drugs have a specific lane. Ethosuximide is a sniper it hits one target T-type channels to fix one problem Absence seizures . Gabapentinoids are \\ structural mimics\\ of GABA that don t actually touch GABA receptors but block calcium channels to stop neuropathic pain and focal seizures.\\n\\n 2.1 Ethosuximide\\n Mechanism Blocks T-Type Calcium Channels in the Thalamus.\\n disrupts the 3Hz spike-and-wave rhythm of absence seizures.\\n Therapeutic Use Absence Seizures ONLY Childhood Absence Epilepsy .\\n Note Ineffective for tonic-clonic seizures.\\n Adverse Effects \\n GI Nausea/Vomiting/Pain very common .\\n CNS Fatigue Dizziness.\\n Rare SJS/TEN Aplastic Anemia.\\n\\n 2.2 Gabapentinoids Gabapentin & Pregabalin \\n Mechanism Bind to the $\\\\alpha 2\\\\delta$ subunit of voltage-gated Calcium channels presynaptic .\\n Reduces calcium influx $\\\\rightarrow$ reduces glutamate release.\\n Note Do NOT bind GABA receptors despite the name.\\n Therapeutic Uses \\n Neuropathic Pain Diabetic neuropathy Post-herpetic neuralgia Fibromyalgia.\\n Seizure Focal Seizures Adjunct . Exacerbates Generalized seizures. \\n Adverse Effects Sedation Peripheral Edema Weight gain.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. GABA Modulators Barbiturates & Benzos \\n Story Mode \\nThese drugs work directly on the GABA-A receptor the brain s primary \\ off switch.\\ By opening the chloride channel they hyperpolarize neurons. The distinction is in how they open it Benzos increase Frequency Benzos = Frenz while Barbiturates increase Duration Barbi-durates .\\n\\n 3.1 Phenobarbital\\n Mechanism Barbiturate. Binds allosteric site on GABA-A.\\n Increases Duration of $Cl^-$ channel opening.\\n Danger At high doses can open the channel without GABA direct agonist $\\\\rightarrow$ Coma/Death.\\n Pharmacokinetics \\n Potent Inducer of CYP450 enzymes eats up other drugs .\\n Long half-life days .\\n Therapeutic Uses \\n Neonatal Seizures First line .\\n Status Epilepticus Refractory .\\n Adverse Effects Profound sedation Respiratory Depression Hyperactivity in children paradoxical .\\n\\n 3.2 Benzodiazepines Diazepam Lorazepam Midazolam \\n Mechanism Binds allosteric site on GABA-A.\\n Increases Frequency of $Cl^-$ channel opening.\\n Safety Require GABA to work Ceiling effect .\\n Therapeutic Uses \\n Acute Status Epilepticus First Line - \\ Abortive therapy\\ .\\n Chronic Rarely used due to Tolerance .\\n Specific Agents \\n Lorazepam IV Preferred for Status stays in brain longer .\\n Diazepam IV/Rectal Highly lipid soluble $\\\\rightarrow$ rapid entry $\\\\rightarrow$ rapid Redistribution to fat/muscle short duration of action .\\n Midazolam IM/Buccal/Nasal Used when IV access is not available e.g. EMS .\\n\\n correlate\\n \\ Floppy Baby\\ Syndrome \\n Benzos in Pregnancy \\nChronic use of benzodiazepines near delivery can cause neonatal withdrawal and hypotonia.\\n Presentation Neonate with severe hypotonia \\ rag doll\\ difficulty suckling apnea and hypothermia.\\n Management Supportive care; avoid benzos in pregnancy if possible.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Other Agents\\n\\n 4.1 Cannabidiol Epidiolex \\n Mechanism Unknown does not act on CB1/CB2 receptors .\\n Uses Severe childhood epilepsy syndromes \\n Dravet Syndrome \\n Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome \\n Tuberous Sclerosis Complex \\n Adverse Effects Somnolence elevated liver enzymes LFTs .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 5. Summary Tables\\n\\n highyield\\n Drug Selection by Seizure Type \\n| Seizure Type | First Line / Effective | Contraindicated / Ineffective |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Focal | Carbamazepine Phenytoin Lamotrigine Levetiracetam | -- |\\n| Generalized Tonic-Clonic | Valproate Levetiracetam Lamotrigine Topiramate | Carbamazepine Phenytoin can worsen some generalized types |\\n| Absence | Ethosuximide Valproate | Phenytoin Carbamazepine Gabapentin |\\n| Status Epilepticus | Benzodiazepines Lorazepam $\\\\rightarrow$ Fosphenytoin/Valproate | -- |\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n Key Side Effects & Buzzwords \\n| Drug | Buzzword / Key Side Effect |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Valproate | Neural Tube Defects Hepatotoxicity Pancreatitis Hyperammonemia |\\n| Topiramate | Weight Loss Kidney Stones Mental dulling \\ Dopeamax\\ |\\n| Levetiracetam | Agitation/Aggression \\ Keppra Rage\\ |\\n| Ethosuximide | GI Distress Pain/Nausea |\\n| Phenytoin | Gingival Hyperplasia Hirsutism Zero-order kinetics |\\n| Lamotrigine | Stevens-Johnson Syndrome Rash |\\n questions A 7-year-old boy is brought to the clinic for frequent staring spells where he is unresponsive for 5-10 seconds. An EEG shows a 3-Hz spike-and-wave pattern. You prescribe the first-line finding for this condition. Which of the following is the mechanism of action of this drug? Binds to SV2A vesicle protein Blocks voltage-gated Sodium channels Blocks T-Type Calcium Channels Enhances GABA-A receptor frequency Blocks Alpha-2-Delta Calcium subunit 2 The clinical presentation staring spells and EEG 3Hz spike-and-wave are classic for Absence Seizures . The drug of choice is Ethosuximide which works by blocking T-Type Calcium Channels in the Thalamus. A 25-year-old male with a history of obesity and migraines presents for management of his focal seizures. He is interested in a medication that might also help him lose weight. Which agent would be most appropriate? Valproate Gabapentin Topiramate Phenytoin Carbamazepine 2 Topiramate is associated with weight loss and is also used for migraine prophylaxis making it an excellent choice for this patient. In contrast Valproate and Gabapentin are associated with weight gain. A neonate is born with severe hypotonia difficulty feeding and respiratory depression. The mother reports taking an anti-seizure medication for anxiety during her pregnancy. Which mechanism best explains the infant s presentation? Sodium channel blockade T-type calcium channel blockade Increased frequency of GABA-A channel opening Folate antagonism SV2A binding 2 This is \\ Floppy Baby\\ Syndrome caused by benzodiazepine withdrawal/effect in the neonate. Benzodiazepines work by increasing the frequency of GABA-A chloride channel opening. Phenytoin Folate antagonist causes Fetal Hydantoin Syndrome cleft lip/palate not acute hypotonia. A patient with refractory seizures is started on Valproate. Two weeks later he presents with confusion and lethargy. LFTs are normal but ammonia levels are elevated. Prior to this he was taking another ASD. Which co-medication most likely increased the risk of this complication? Levetiracetam Topiramate Lamotrigine Gabapentin Ethosuximide 1 Topiramate and Valproate both can cause hyperammonemia and their concurrent use significantly increases the risk of Hyperammonemic Encephalopathy . Which of the following anti-seizure medications functions by binding to the SV2A synaptic vesicle protein? Phenobarbital Lamotrigine Levetiracetam Tiagabine Vigabatrin 2 Levetiracetam has a unique mechanism of action it binds to SV2A Synaptic Vesicle Glycoprotein 2A to prevent neurotransmitter release. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l54", "title": "Lecture 54: Aphasia and Common Strokes", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l54 Lecture 54 Aphasia and Common Strokes Neurology Lecture 54 Aphasia and Common Strokes\\n\\n Lecturer A. Leder D.O.\\n Session Lecture 54\\n\\n 1. Aphasia Syndromes\\n Story Mode \\nAphasia is a disorder of language not speech interruption of the \\ mind-to-mouth\\ connection . To diagnose the type you simply need to check three boxes Fluency Is speech smooth or broken? Comprehension Do they understand you? and Repetition Can they say \\ No ifs ands or buts\\ ? .\\n\\n 1.1 The \\ Perisylvian\\ Aphasias Repetition is Impaired \\nThese occur around the Sylvian fissure where the main language arc resides.\\n Broca s Aphasia Expressive \\n Lesion Left Inferior Frontal Gyrus . usually superior division of Left MCA .\\n Features \\n Non-Fluent Speech is broken effortful telegraphic \\ Car... go... store\\ .\\n Comprehension Intact They know what they want to say and get frustrated .\\n Repetition Impaired.\\n Associated Contralateral Right face/arm weakness.\\n Wernicke s Aphasia Receptive \\n Lesion Left Superior Temporal Gyrus . Inferior division of Left MCA .\\n Features \\n Fluent Speech is voluminous but meaningless \\ Word Salad\\ .\\n Comprehension Impaired They don t realize they aren t making sense .\\n Repetition Impaired.\\n Associated Right Superior Quadrantanopia \\ Pie in the sky\\ .\\n Global Aphasia \\n Lesion Large Left MCA infarct knocking out both Broca s and Wernicke s areas.\\n Features All Imapired Non-fluent No comprehension No repetition .\\n Conduction Aphasia \\n Lesion Arcuate Fasciculus The bridge between Broca and Wernicke .\\n Features \\n Fluent speech Intact comprehension.\\n Repetition IMPAIRED The connection is broken . They act like Wernicke s but know they are making mistakes and try to correct them.\\n\\n 1.2 The \\ Transcortical\\ Aphasias Repetition is SPARED \\nThese lesions are \\ around\\ the language centers watershed zones but spare the core Arcuate Fasciculus loop.\\n Transcortical Motor Like Broca s Non-fluent Good Comp but Repetition is Intact . ACA/MCA Watershed .\\n Transcortical Sensory Like Wernicke s Fluent Poor Comp but Repetition is Intact . MCA/PCA Watershed . They may exhibit Echolalia parroting back words .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Ischemic Stroke Syndromes by Territory\\n\\n 2.1 Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA \\n Territory Medial surface of the brain Leg homunculus area .\\n Presentation \\n Contralateral Lower Extremity Weakness Leg > Arm .\\n Abulia / Akinetic Mutism Lack of will to move or speak Frontal lobe damage .\\n Urinary Incontinence. \\n\\n 2.2 Middle Cerebral Artery MCA \\n Territory Lateral surface of the brain Face/Arm homunculus area .\\n Presentation \\n Contralateral Face and Arm Weakness Face/Arm > Leg .\\n Gaze Preference Eyes look Toward the lesion away from the weakness .\\n Dominant Left Hemi Aphasia.\\n Non-Dominant Right Hemi Hemineflect ignoring the left side of the world .\\n\\n 2.3 Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA \\n Territory Occipital lobe and Inferior Temporal lobe.\\n Presentation \\n Contralateral Homonymous Hemianopsia Visual field cut with Macular Sparing .\\n Alexia without Agraphia Left PCA + Splenium of Corpus Callosum . Patient can write but cannot read even what they just wrote .\\n Gerstmann Syndrome Dominant Angular Gyrus .\\n 1. Agraphia Can t write .\\n 2. Acalculia Can t do math .\\n 3. Finger Agnosia Can t identify fingers .\\n 4. Left-Right Disorientation.\\n\\n 2.4 Internal Carotid Artery ICA \\n Mechanism Occlusion of the source pipe for both ACA and MCA.\\n Presentation Combination of ACA + MCA symptoms Dense hemiplegia face/arm/leg .\\n Clinical Pearl Amaurosis Fugax \\ Curtain coming down\\ . Transient monocular blindness due to embolus to the Ophthalmic artery first branch of ICA .\\n\\n 2.5 Watershed Infarcts\\n Mechanism Systemic hypoperfusion e.g. Cardiac Arrest or severe hypotension . The areas between vascular territories ACA-MCA or MCA-PCA borders die first.\\n Presentation \\ Man-in-a-Barrel\\ Syndrome .\\n Bilateral proximal arm and weakness Shoulders/Thighs .\\n spares the hands and feet and face.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Lacunar Strokes\\n Story Mode \\nThese are small vessel strokes lenticulostriate arteries caused by Hypertension lipohyalinosis . They affect deep structures Internal Capsule Thalamus Pons and have very specific \\ Pure\\ presentations.\\n Pure Motor Posterior Limb of Internal Capsule. Contralateral weakness only .\\n Pure Sensory VPL of Thalamus. Contralateral numbness only .\\n Dysarthria-Clumsy Hand Genu of Internal Capsule or Pons.\\n Ataxic Hemiparesis Pons or Internal Capsule.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Brainstem Syndromes\\n Story Mode \\nThe brainstem is high-value real estate. A small stroke here causes massive problems. The rule of thumb Crossed Signs Ipsilateral Cranial Nerve deficit + Contralateral Body deficit .\\n\\n 4.1 Lateral Medullary Wallenberg Syndrome\\n Vessel PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Remember \\ Don t Pica a horse hoarseness \\ .\\n Key Features \\n Dysphagia / Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus CN IX X . Loss of Gag reflex.\\n Vertigo / Nystagmus Vestibular Nuclei.\\n Ipsilateral Horner s Syndrome Sympathetic fibers.\\n Ipsilateral Facial Numbness / Contralateral Body Numbness Pain/Temp .\\n Ipsilateral Ataxia. \\n\\n 4.2 Locked-In Syndrome\\n Vessel Basilar Artery thrombosis.\\n Lesion Bilateral Ventral Pons.\\n Presentation \\n Quadriplegia all motor tracts cut .\\n Anarthria cannot speak .\\n Consciousness is Preserved Reticular formation is spared .\\n Communication Vertical Eye Movements and Blinking spared midbrain centers .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 5. Hemorrhagic Strokes\\n\\n| Type | Etiology | CT Appearance | Clinical Pearl |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Epidural Hematoma | Trauma Temporal bone fx $\\\\rightarrow$ Middle Meningeal Artery rupture. | Lens-shaped Biconvex . Does NOT cross suture lines. | \\ Talk and Die\\ Lucid interval followed by rapid deterioration. |\\n| Subdural Hematoma | Trauma Falls/Elderly/Alcoholics $\\\\rightarrow$ Bridging Veins rupture. | Crescent-shaped . Crosses suture lines. | Slow onset. associated with cerebral atrophy. |\\n| Subarachnoid Hemorrhage | Aneurysm Rupture Berry Aneurysm or Trauma. | Blood in cisterns/sulci \\ Starfish\\ pattern . | \\ Worst Headache of my Life\\ Thunderclap . Risk of vasospasm days later. |\\n\\n correlate\\n Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis CVST \\n The Story \\nA stroke that doesn t follow arterial rules. Think of this in a Hypercoagulable patient Pregnancy OCP use Cancer Factor V Leiden .\\n Mechanism Clot in the Dural Venous Sinuses e.g. Sagittal Sinus prevents blood drainage $\\\\rightarrow$ Congestion $\\\\rightarrow$ Hemorrhage.\\n Presentation Headache High ICP Seizures Papilledema.\\n Diagnosis MR Venogram MRV or CT Venogram.\\n Treatment Anticoagulation even if there is some hemorrhage you must clear the clot .\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n Arterial Dissection \\n Patient Young patient with neck trauma chiropractic manipulation or spontaneous.\\n Presentation \\n Neck Pain + Headache.\\n Horner s Syndrome Ipsilateral The sympathetic fibers run along the carotid artery. Enlargement of the artery compresses them.\\n TIA/Stroke symptoms from emboli.\\n \\n questions A 72-year-old male presents with right-sided face and arm weakness but with relative sparing of the leg. He is also found to have gaze deviation to the left. Which vascular territory is most likely affected? Left Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA Left Middle Cerebral Artery MCA Right Middle Cerebral Artery MCA Left Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Basilar Artery 1 Left MCA Stroke . The MCA supplies the lateral surface of the cortex Face/Arm area . Weakness is contralateral Right . Gaze deviation is toward the lesion Left away from the hemiparesis. A patient presents with word salad speech. They speak fluently and with normal rhythm but the content is meaningless. They cannot repeat phrases or follow commands. Where is the most likely lesion? Inferior Frontal Gyrus Superior Temporal Gyrus Arcuate Fasciculus Angular Gyrus Occipital Lobe 1 This is Wernicke s Aphasia Fluent Impaired Comprehension Impaired Repetition . The lesion is in the Superior Temporal Gyrus Wernicke s Area of the dominant hemisphere. A 25-year-old female presents with severe headache and specialized imaging reveals a thrombosis of the superior sagittal sinus. Which of the following is a key risk factor for this condition? Hypertension Atrial Fibrillation Tobacco Use Oral Contraceptive Use Diabetes Mellitus 3 Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis CVST is associated with hypercoagulable states. Oral Contraceptive Use is a major risk factor especially in young women. HTN/DM/Smoking are risks for arterial strokes. A patient is able to speak fluently and understand commands perfectly but cannot repeat the phrase No ifs ands or buts . They are aware of their errors and try to correct them. What is the diagnosis? Broca s Aphasia Wernicke s Aphasia Conduction Aphasia Global Aphasia Transcortical Motor Aphasia 2 Conduction Aphasia is characterized by Fluent speech + Intact Comprehension + Impaired Repetition . It is caused by a lesion to the Arcuate Fasciculus . A patient presents with sudden onset of the worst headache of my life . A non-contrast CT shows a starfish pattern of hyperdensity in the basal cisterns. What represents the most likely etiology? Rupture of Middle Meningeal Artery Rupture of Bridging Veins Rupture of Berry Aneurysm Hypertensive lipohyalinosis Carotid Dissection 2 This is a Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Thunderclap headache + Starfish pattern . The most common non-traumatic cause is the rupture of a Saccular Berry Aneurysm . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l55", "title": "Lecture 55: Cerebrovascular Diseases", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l55 Lecture 55 Cerebrovascular Diseases Neurology Lecture 55 Cerebrovascular Diseases\\n\\n Lecturer W. Zaloga D.O.\\n Session Lecture 55\\n\\n 1. Ischemic Stroke Pathology\\n Story Mode \\nIschemia is a lack of blood flow which means lack of Oxygen Hypoxia and lack of Glucose Hypoglycemia . The brain is incredibly hungry and has zero reserves.\\n\\n 1.1 The Ischemic Cascade Excitotoxicity \\nWhen neurons run out of energy ATP their ion pumps fail.\\n1. Depolarization Sodium/Calcium flow IN Potassium flows OUT.\\n2. Glutamate Dump The dying neuron releases a massive amount of Glutamate excitatory neurotransmitter .\\n3. Calcium Influx Glutamate opens NMDA channels on neighbors $\\\\rightarrow$ Massive Calcium influx.\\n4. Enzyme Activation Calcium activates \\ executioner\\ enzymes proteases lipases that chew up the cell from the inside.\\n5. Result Liquefactive Necrosis .\\n\\n 1.2 Timeline of Ischemic Changes\\n| Time | Microscopic Change | Macroscopic Change |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0-12 Hours | \\ Red Neurons\\ Eosinophilic cytoplasm pyknotic nuclei . First visible sign. | Normal or slight blurring of gray-white junction. |\\n| 12-24 Hours | Neutrophils infiltrate Acute inflammation . | Tissue becomes pale and soft. |\\n| 1-3 Days | Macrophages Microglia arrive to clean up the mess. | Swelling/Edema Risk of Herniation . |\\n| 1-2 Weeks | Reactive Gliosis Astrocytes proliferate + Vascular proliferation. | Liquefactive Necrosis Mushy cavity . |\\n| > 2 Weeks | Glial Scar formation Astrocytic border . | Cystic cavity hole in the brain . |\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Stroke Classifications\\n 2.1 Thrombotic Stroke\\n Mechanism Clot forms locally at the site of infarction.\\n Cause Ruptured Atherosclerotic plaque.\\n Key Locations Carotid bifurcation MCA origin Basilar artery.\\n Presentation \\ Stuttering\\ onset symptoms wax and wane .\\n\\n 2.2 Embolic Stroke\\n Mechanism Clot travels from elsewhere .\\n Causes \\n Cardiogenic Atrial Fibrillation Left Atrial Appendage Post-MI thrombus Vegetation Endocarditis .\\n Fat Emboli Long bone fractures femur . Triad Hypoxemia + Petechial Rash + Confusion .\\n Key Features Sudden maximal deficit at onset. Affects multiple vascular territories Embolic Shower .\\n\\n 2.3 Global Ischemia Watershed Infarcts \\n Mechanism Systemic Hypotension Shock Cardiac Arrest .\\n Location Border zones between ACA/MCA or MCA/PCA.\\n Histology Laminar Necrosis Pyramidal neurons of Hippocampus and Cortex layers 3 5 & 6 are most vulnerable .\\n\\n 2.4 Lacunar Strokes\\n Mechanism Lipohyalinosis Hyaline Arteriolosclerosis of small penetrating vessels due to Chronic Hypertension .\\n Vessels Lenticulostriate arteries.\\n Location Basal Ganglia Thalamus Internal Capsule Pons.\\n Clinical Pure Motor or Pure Sensory deficits. No cortical signs No aphasia no neglect .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Hemorrhagic Strokes & Vascular Malformations\\n\\n| Condition | Etiology | Key Features |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Intracerebral Hemorrhage ICH | Hypertension Deep bleeds Basal Ganglia/Thalamus via Charcot-Bouchard Microaneurysms . <br> Amyloid Angiopathy Lobar/Cortical bleeds in elderly . | Rapid onset focal deficit + Headache + Nausea/Vomiting High ICP . |\\n| Subarachnoid Hemorrhage SAH | Ruptured Berry Aneurysm ACA-Comm is 1 site . Associated with PCKD Ehlers-Danlos. | \\ Thunderclap Headache\\ . Nuchal rigidity. Lumbar Puncture Xanthochromia yellow CSF . |\\n| A-V Malformation AVM | Congenital tangle of arteries-to-veins no capillaries . High flow shunting. | present in Young Adults 10-30yo with Seizures or Hemorrhage. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Traumatic Brain Injury TBI \\n\\n 4.1 Skull Fractures\\n Linear Simple crack.\\n Depressed Bone fragment pushed inward requires surgery .\\n Diastatic Separation of cranial sutures.\\n Basilar Fracture of the skull base.\\n Signs Raccoon Eyes Periorbital ecchymosis Battle Sign Mastoid ecchymosis CSF Rhinorrhea/Otorrhea Halo sign .\\n\\n 4.2 Parenchymal Injuries\\n Concussion Reversible altered mental status amnesia/confusion with NO structural damage on CT/MRI.\\n Contusion Bruising of brain tissue Hemorrhage + Edema .\\n Coup Injury at site of impact Head hits wall .\\n Contrecoup Injury opposite to impact Brain sloshes back . Common in frontal/temporal poles.\\n Diffuse Axonal Injury DAI \\n Mechanism Shearing/Rotational forces e.g. High-speed car crash .\\n Pathology Stretching/tearing of axons in deep white matter Corpus Callosum/Brainstem .\\n Imaging Multiple punctate hemorrhages on MRI SWI sequence . CT often normal.\\n Clinical Instant coma. Poor prognosis.\\n Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy CTE Accumulation of Tau protein neurofibrillary tangles after repetitive mild TBI Boxers Football . Dementia aggression parkinsonism.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 5. Herniation Syndromes\\n The Story \\nThe skull is a closed box. If you add volume Blood Tumor Edema the brain has nowhere to go but... squished against rigid membranes or out the holes.\\n\\n 5.1 Subfalcine Cingulate Herniation\\n Movement Cingulate gyrus pushed under the Falx Cerebri.\\n Compression Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA .\\n Clinical Contralateral Leg Weakness .\\n\\n 5.2 Transtentorial Uncal Herniation\\n Movement Uncus Medial Temporal Lobe pushed under the Tentorium Cerebelli.\\n Compression \\n 1. CN III Ipsilateral \\ Blown Pupil\\ Dilated/Fixed + \\ Down and Out\\ eye. First sign .\\n 2. Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Contralateral Homonymous Hemianopsia Cortical Blindness .\\n 3. Cerebral Peduncle Contralateral Hemiparesis or Ipsilateral if Kernohan s Notch phenomenon .\\n 4. Duret Hemorrhages Tearing of paramedian basilar arteries $\\\\rightarrow$ Brainstem bleed $\\\\rightarrow$ Death.\\n\\n 5.3 Tonsillar Herniation\\n Movement Cerebellar Tonsils pushed through the Foramen Magnum.\\n Compression Brainstem Medulla .\\n Clinical Cardiorespiratory Arrest \\\\\\ Coning\\\\\\ .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 6. High Yield Renders\\n\\n highyield\\n Stroke Syndromes Cheat Sheet \\n| Vessel | Territory | Key Deficit | Buzzword |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| ACA | Medial Surface | Leg > Arm Weakness | \\ Abulia\\ Lack of will |\\n| MCA | Lateral Surface | Face/Arm > Leg Weakness | Aphasia Left Neglect Right |\\n| PCA | Occipital Lobe | Homonymous Hemianopsia | \\ Macular Sparing\\ |\\n| Lacunar | Deep Structures | Pure Motor or Pure Sensory | Hypertension No Cortical Signs |\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n Herniation Syndromes \\n Subfalcine Cingulate gyrus under Falx $\\\\rightarrow$ ACA compression $\\\\rightarrow$ Leg weakness.\\n Uncal Medial temporal lobe under Tentorium $\\\\rightarrow$ CN III compression $\\\\rightarrow$ \\ Down & Out\\ Pupil.\\n Tonsillar Cerebellar tonsils into Foramen Magnum $\\\\rightarrow$ Brainstem compression $\\\\rightarrow$ Death.\\n \\n questions A patient suffers a severe ischemic stroke and dies 6 hours later. An autopsy is performed. What is the most likely microscopic finding in the affected brain tissue? Neutrophilic infiltration Macrophage infiltration Red Neurons Reactive Gliosis Liquefactive Necrosis 2 Red Neurons eosinophilic cytoplasm pyknotic nuclei are the earliest visible microscopic change appearing within 12-24 hours sometimes as early as 6 hours . Neutrophils arrive at 24 hours. A 65-year-old male with a history of poorly controlled hypertension presents with sudden onset pure motor hemiparesis. MRI reveals a small fluid-filled cavity in the internal capsule. What is the underlying pathophysiology? Ruptured Berry Aneurysm Amyloid Angiopathy Lipohyalinosis Cardiogenic Embolism Global Ischemia 2 This is a Lacunar Stroke . The mechanism is Lipohyalinosis thickening/destabilization of the small penetrating lenticulostriate arteries due to chronic Hypertension . A patient involved in a high-speed motor vehicle accident is comatose on arrival. CT scan is unremarkable but MRI shows multiple punctate hemorrhages in the corpus callosum and brainstem. What is the diagnosis? Subdural Hematoma Epidural Hematoma Diffuse Axonal Injury Coup-Contrecoup Injury Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy 2 Diffuse Axonal Injury DAI is caused by shearing forces in high-speed trauma. It affects deep white matter tracts. It is often invisible on CT but shows as microhemorrhages on MRI SWI . During a brain herniation event a patient develops a dilated fixed pupil on the right side. Which structure is being compressed? Optic Nerve Oculomotor Nerve CN III Trochlear Nerve CN IV Abducens Nerve CN VI Sympathetic chain 1 This is Uncal Herniation . The uncus of the temporal lobe herniates under the tentorium compressing the ipsilateral Oculomotor Nerve CN III causing a blown pupil . What is the primary neurotransmitter responsible for excitotoxicity and subsequent neuronal death in ischemic stroke? GABA Dopamine Serotonin Glutamate Acetylcholine 3 Glutamate is the major excitatory neurotransmitter. In ischemia it is dumped into the synapse overstimulating NMDA receptors and causing massive Calcium influx which kills the cell. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l56", "title": "Lecture 56: Neurodegenerative Disease and Selective Neuropathy", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l56 Lecture 56 Neurodegenerative Disease and Selective Neuropathy Neurology Lecture 56 Neurodegenerative Disease and Selective Neuropathy\\n\\n Lecturer M. Plummer M.D.\\n Session Lecture 56\\n\\n 1. General Principles\\n Common Theme Accumulation of protein aggregates inclusions resistant to degradation.\\n Toxicity Aggregates elicit stress responses damaging neurons.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Information on Specific Diseases\\n\\n 2.1 Alzheimer s Disease AD \\n Epidemiology Most common cause of dementia in elderly.\\n Clinical Slow onset memory loss $\\\\rightarrow$ disorientation $\\\\rightarrow$ severe variability/immobility.\\n Pathology \\n Gross Global Atrophy Widened sulci Narrowed gyri . Hydrocephalus ex vacuo ventricles dilate to fill space .\\n Microscopic \\n 1. Senile Neuritic Plaques Extracellular. Core of Beta-Amyloid A$\\\\beta$ .\\n Derived from Amyloid Precursor Protein APP cleavage by $\\\\beta$-secretase and $\\\\gamma$-secretase.\\n A$\\\\beta$42 is the toxic aggregation-prone form.\\n 2. Neurofibrillary Tangles NFTs Intracellular. Hyperphosphorylated Tau protein microtubule-associated .\\n Correlation Number of Tangles correlates better with degree of dementia than plaques.\\n Genetics \\n APP Chr 21 Down Syndrome patients get AD by age 40 Trisomy 21 = extra APP gene .\\n Presenilin 1 Chr 14 & 2 Chr 1 Early-onset familial AD increase A$\\\\beta$42 .\\n ApoE4 Chr 19 Increased risk for sporadic late-onset AD.\\n\\n 2.2 Frontotemporal Lobar Degeneration Pick Disease \\n Clinical Signification behavioral/personality changes disinhibition precede memory loss. Aphasia.\\n Gross \\ Knife-Edge\\ Atrophy of Frontal and Temporal lobes. Spares Parietal/Occipital.\\n Microscopic Tau inclusions Pick Bodies in some variants FTLD-Tau . Spheroidal inclusions.\\n Note FTLD-TDP43 is another variant Ubiquitin/TDP-43 inclusions associated with ALS.\\n\\n 2.3 Parkinson s Disease PD \\n Pathophysiology Loss of Dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra Pars Compacta .\\n Clinical TRAP \\n T remor Resting \\ Pill-rolling\\ .\\n R igidity Cogwheel .\\n A kinesia / Bradykinesia Slow movement shuffling gait masked facies .\\n P ostural Instability.\\n Microscopic Lewy Bodies Intracellular eosinophilic inclusions with halo .\\n Composed of $\\\\alpha$-Synuclein .\\n MPTP Contaminant in illicit opioids $\\\\rightarrow$ Acute Parkinsonism destroys Substantia Nigra .\\n\\n 2.4 Huntington s Disease HD \\n Genetics Autosomal Dominant. CAG Trinucleotide Repeat expansion in HTT gene Chr 4 .\\n Anticipation Repeats strictly increase during spermatogenesis Paternal transmission $\\\\rightarrow$ earlier onset in next gen .\\n Pathology Atrophy of the Caudate Nucleus and Putamen . Loss of GABAergic neurons.\\n Clinical Chorea jerky purposeless movements Dementia Depression/Suicide. Presents in 30s-40s.\\n\\n 2.5 Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis ALS \\n Definition degeneration of Upper Motor Neurons UMN in cortex and Lower Motor Neurons LMN in spinal cord/brainstem.\\n Clinical \\n LMN signs Weakness Atrophy Fasciculations.\\n UMN signs Hyperreflexia Spasticity Babinski sign.\\n Sensation is intact. \\n Genetics Familial cases often linked to SOD1 mutation Superoxide Dismutase 1 .\\n\\n 2.6 Prion Disease Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease - CJD \\n Mechanism Conversion of normal Prion protein PrP$^ c $ $\\\\alpha$-helix to pathogenic form PrP$^ sc $ $\\\\beta$-sheet .\\n PrP$^ sc $ induces conformational change in normal PrP$^ c $ chain reaction .\\n Pathology Spongiform Encephalopathy vacuoles in gray matter . No inflammation.\\n Clinical Rapidly Progressive Dementia Startle Myoclonus. Death < 1 year.\\n Types Sporadic most common Variant Mad Cow/BSE Familial Iatrogenic.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Peripheral Neuropathies\\n\\n 3.1 Charcot-Marie-Tooth CMT Disease\\n Etiology Hereditary Motor and Sensory Neuropathy HMSN . Auto Dominant PMP22 duplication is common .\\n Clinical Distal muscle atrophy \\ Stork leg\\ deformity / Inverted/Champagne bottle legs Pes Cavus High arch Hammer toes.\\n Microscopic Repetitive Demyelination/Remyelination $\\\\rightarrow$ \\ Onion Bulb\\ formation. Palpable nerves.\\n\\n 3.2 Diabetic Neuropathy\\n Patterns \\n 1. Distal Symmetric Sensorimotor \\ Stocking and Glove\\ sensory loss/paresthesia. Ulcers.\\n 2. Autonomic Orthostatic hypotension erectile dysfunction urinary retention neurogenic bladder .\\n Pathogenesis High glucose $\\\\rightarrow$ AGEs Advanced Glycation End-products $\\\\rightarrow$ Microvascular damage vasa nervorum + Sorbitol accumulation osmotic damage .\\n\\n 3.3 Traumatic/Compression Neuropathies\\n Traumatic Neuroma Tangled mass of axons/Schwann cells after nerve transection failed regeneration . Painful.\\n Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Compression of Median Nerve at wrist. Numbness in thumb/index/middle fingers. Thenar atrophy.\\n Morton s Neuroma Compression of interdigital nerve usually 3rd/4th toe interspace . Related to high heels.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. High Yield Renders\\n\\n highyield\\n Neurodegenerative Diseases The \\ Protein & Location\\ Match \\n| Disease | Protein Aggregate | Location of Atrophy | Key Clinical Feature |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Alzheimer s | Beta-Amyloid Plaques Tau Tangles | Temporoparietal / Global | Memory Loss |\\n| Pick s FTD | Tau Pick Bodies | Frontal / Temporal \\ Knife-edge\\ | Personality Change > Memory |\\n| Parkinson s | Alpha-Synuclein Lewy Bodies | Substantia Nigra | TRAP Tremor Rigidity Akinesia |\\n| Huntington s | Huntingtin CAG Repeats | Caudate Nucleus | Chorea + Dementia |\\n| ALS | TDP-43 / SOD1 | UMN Cortex + LMN spinal cord | Weakness + Spasticity No sensory loss |\\n| CJD | Prion $PrP^ sc $ | Gray Matter Spongiform | Rapid Dementia + Myoclonus |\\n \\n questions A 55-year-old male presents with changes in personality and inappropriate social behavior. His family reports he has become disinhibited and apathetic. Memory is relatively preserved. MRI shows significant atrophy of the frontal and temporal lobes. What is the most likely diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Pick Disease Frontotemporal Dementia Huntington s Disease Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease 2 This is Pick Disease Frontotemporal Dementia . The key features are early behavioral/personality changes disinhibition sparing memory initially and Knife-edge atrophy of frontal/temporal lobes. Histology often shows Tau inclusions Pick bodies . A 40-year-old male presents with choreiform movements and progressive dementia. His father had similar symptoms starting at age 50. Genetic testing reveals an expanded CAG repeat sequence on Chromosome 4. Which brain structure is most likely atophied? Substantia Nigra Hippocampus Caudate Nucleus Cerebellum Motor Cortex 2 This is Huntington s Disease CAG repeats Autosomal Dominant Anticipation . The characteristic pathology is atrophy of the Caudate Nucleus and putamen leading to chorea. A 70-year-old male presents with a resting tremor in his right hand rigidity and a shuffling gait. Histological examination of his brain post-mortem would most likely reveal which finding in the Substantia Nigra? Neurofibrillary Tangles Senile Plaques Negri Bodies Lewy Bodies Spongiform change 3 This is Parkinson s Disease TRAP symptoms . The hallmark histology is Lewy Bodies alpha-synuclein inclusions in the Substantia Nigra . A 60-year-old female presents with rapidly progressive dementia and myoclonus. She dies 4 months later. Autopsy reveals spongiform changes in the gray matter with no inflammation. What is the underlying pathogenic mechanism? Accumulation of Beta-Amyloid Loss of Dopaminergic neurons Conversion of PrPc to PrPsc CAG repeat expansion SOD1 mutation 2 This is Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease CJD a Prion disease. The mechanism is the conversion of normal alpha-helical PrPc to the pathological beta-sheet rich PrPsc which aggregates and causes spongiform encephalopathy. A patient with long-standing diabetes mellitus develops numbness and tingling in both feet that has progressed up to the ankles. This stocking-and-glove distribution is characteristic of which type of neuropathy? Mononeuritis Multiplex Distal Symmetric Polyneuropathy Autonomic Neuropathy Radiculopathy Traumatic Neuroma 1 Distal Symmetric Polyneuropathy is the most common form of diabetic neuropathy. It presents as a sensory loss/paresthesia starting in the toes and moving proximally Stocking distribution and later affecting hands Glove . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l57", "title": "Lecture 57: Parkinson", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l57 Lecture 57 Parkinson s Disease and its Variants Neurology Lecture 57 Parkinson s Disease and its Variants\\n\\n Lecturer A. Leder D.O.\\n Session Lecture 57\\n\\n 1. Parkinson s Disease PD \\n Story Mode \\nParkinson s is a disease of \\ Too Little Dopamine\\ movement is stuck . It s a synucleinopathy meaning the garbage protein dumping into the cells is Alpha-Synuclein. The classic patient is elderly slow stiff and has a tremor.\\n\\n 1.1 Clinical Features TRAP \\n T remor Resting tremor 4-6 Hz . \\ Pill-rolling\\ . Usually Asymmetric at onset.\\n R igidity \\ Cogwheel\\ rigidity ratchet-like resistance to passive movement .\\n A kinesia / Bradykinesia Slowness of movement.\\n Masked Facies Hypomimia .\\n Micrographia Small handwriting .\\n Shuffling Gait Festinating gait with reduced arm swing.\\n Freezing Feet stuck to floor especially in doorways .\\n P ostural Instability Loss of balance frequent falls usually a Late finding .\\n\\n 1.2 Non-Motor Symptoms\\nOften precede motor symptoms by years.\\n Anosmia Loss of smell.\\n Constipation. \\n REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Acting out dreams punching/kicking due to loss of REM atonia.\\n Depression/Anxiety. \\n\\n 1.3 Pathology & Genetics\\n Pathology \\n Loss of Dopaminergic Neurons in the Substantia Nigra Pars Compacta .\\n Lewy Bodies Intracellular eosinophilic inclusions composed of Alpha-Synuclein .\\n Genetics \\n GBA Glucocerebrosidase Risk factor for PD and Lewy Body Dementia. Linked to Gaucher s Disease Ashkenazi Jews .\\n LRRK2 Common cause of familial PD Ashkenazi Jews North African Berbers . Slower progression.\\n Parkin Early-onset PD <40 yo . Autosomal Recessive.\\n Environmental \\n Risks Pesticides Paraquat Rotenone Well water Head trauma.\\n Protective Smoking Caffeine.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Atypical Parkinsonism Parkinson-Plus Syndromes \\n Story Mode \\nThese look like Parkinson s but \\ Something is Wrong.\\ They don t respond well to Levodopa progress faster and have unique \\ Red Flags.\\ \\n\\n 2.1 Multiple System Atrophy MSA \\n Pathology Alpha-Synucleinopathy Glial Cytoplasmic Inclusions .\\n Key Features Parkinsonism +\\n 1. Autonomic Dysfunction Dysautonomia Severe Orthostatic Hypotension fainting when standing Urinary Incontinence/Retention Erectile Dysfunction.\\n 2. Cerebellar Signs Ataxia.\\n Clinical Pearl Inspiratory Stridor.\\n\\n 2.2 Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP \\n Pathology Tauopathy Tau protein accumulation .\\n Key Features \\n Vertical Gaze Palsy Cannot look Down trouble reading going down stairs .\\n Early Falls Often falling Backwards .\\n Pseudobulbar Palsy Dysphagia dysarthria \\ growling speech\\ .\\n\\n 2.3 Corticobasal Degeneration CBD \\n Pathology Tauopathy.\\n Key Features \\n Markedly Asymmetric symptoms.\\n Alien Limb Phenomenon One hand acts on its own e.g. unbuttoning shirt while other buttons it .\\n Apraxia Inability to perform complex motor tasks despite motor strength.\\n\\n 2.4 Dementia with Lewy Bodies DLB \\n Pathology Alpha-Synucleinopathy Cortical Lewy Bodies .\\n Key Features \\n Dementia within 1 year of motor symptoms or before .\\n Visual Hallucinations Early vivid often children or animals.\\n Fluctuating Cognition \\ Good days and bad days.\\ \\n Severe Neuroleptic Sensitivity Antipsychotics cause severe worsening of parkinsonism/death.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Pharmacology of Parkinson s\\n Strategy Increase Dopamine Levodopa Agonists MAO/COMT inhibitors or Decrease Acetylcholine Anticholinergics .\\n\\n 3.1 Levodopa / Carbidopa\\n Mechanism \\n Levodopa Precursor to Dopamine. Crosses Blood-Brain Barrier Dopamine cannot .\\n Carbidopa DOPA Decarboxylase Inhibitor . Does NOT cross BBB. Prevents peripheral conversion of L-DOPA $\\\\rightarrow$ Dopamine reduces systemic side effects like nausea/arrhythmias .\\n Use Gold Standard. Best for motor symptoms bradykinesia/rigidity .\\n Issues \\n \\ Wearing Off\\ Effects last shorter over time.\\n Dyskinesias Chorea-like movements at peak dose.\\n On-Off Phenomenon Unpredictable fluctuations.\\n\\n 3.2 Dopamine Agonists Pramipexole Ropinirole Rotigotine \\n Mechanism Directly stimulate Dopamine receptors D2/D3 .\\n Side Effects \\n Impulse Control Disorders Pathological Gambling Hypersexuality Excessive Spending.\\n Sleep Attacks Sudden onset of sleep danger while driving .\\n Hallucinations.\\n\\n 3.3 Enzyme Inhibitors\\n MAO-B Inhibitors Selegiline Rasagiline \\n Inhibit breakdown of Dopamine in the brain.\\n Selegiline Metabolized to Amphetamine $\\\\rightarrow$ Insomnia don t take at night .\\n COMT Inhibitors Entacapone Opicapone \\n Inhibit peripheral breakdown of Levodopa methyl-transferase .\\n Must be given with Levodopa .\\n Side Effect Orange/Dark Urine .\\n\\n 3.4 Amantadine\\n Mechanism Antiviral. NMDA Antagonist + Increases Dopamine release.\\n Use Treating Levodopa-induced Dyskinesias .\\n Side Effect Livedo Reticularis Lacy purplish discoloration of skin on legs .\\n\\n 3.5 Anticholinergics Trihexyphenidyl Benztropine \\n Mechanism Restores Dopamine/ACh balance.\\n Use Predominantly for Tremor in younger patients \\ Shake & Bake\\ .\\n Side Effects \\ Anti-SLUDGE\\ Dry mouth urinary retention constipation confusion . Avoid in Elderly .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. High Yield Renders\\n\\n highyield\\n Parkinsonism Differential Diagnosis \\n| Condition | Key Clinical Feature The \\ Red Flag\\ | Pathology |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Parkinson s Disease | Asymmetric Resting Tremor Good L-DOPA response | Synuclein Lewy Body |\\n| MSA | Autonomic Failure Orthostasis ED + Cerebellar signs | Synuclein Glial Inclusion |\\n| PSP | Vertical Gaze Palsy Can t look down + Early Falls | Tau |\\n| Lewy Body Dementia | Visual Hallucinations + Dementia early <1 yr | Synuclein Cortical Lewy Body |\\n| Corticobasal CBD | Alien Limb + Apraxia + Severe Asymmetry | Tau |\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n Parkinson s Pharmacology Buzzwords \\n| Drug Class | Examples | Buzzword Side Effect |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Dopamine Agonists | Pramipexole Ropinirole | Gambling / Hypersexuality Sleep Attacks |\\n| Amantadine | -- | Livedo Reticularis Lacy skin rash |\\n| COMT Inhibitors | Entacapone | Orange Urine |\\n| MAO-B Inhibitors | Selegiline | Metabolized to Amphetamine Insomnia |\\n| Levodopa | -- | Dyskinesia Wriggling movements |\\n \\n questions A 68-year-old male presents with a resting tremor in his right hand that improves with movement. He also has a shuffling gait and decreased facial expression. Which of the following pathological findings is most likely present in his brain? Neurofibrillary tangles in the Hippocampus Caudate Nucleus atrophy Loss of pigmented neurons in the Substantia Nigra Spongiform change in the Cortex Demyelination of the Periventricular White Matter 2 This characteristic presentation of Parkinson s Disease TRAP Tremor Rigidity Akinesia Postural Instability is caused by the loss of Dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra Pars Compacta . A 72-year-old male with Parkinson s disease is started on a new medication. Two months later his wife reports that he has gambled away their life savings and has become hypersexual. Which class of medication is most likely responsible for these behaviors? MAO-B Inhibitor Selegiline Dopamine Agonist Pramipexole COMT Inhibitor Entacapone Anticholinergic Benztropine Amantadine 1 Dopamine Agonists like Pramipexole and Ropinirole are known to cause Impulse Control Disorders such as pathological gambling hypersexuality and excessive shopping due to their stimulation of the mesolimbic reward pathway. A 65-year-old female presents with frequent falls often falling backwards. On exam she has difficulty looking down vertical gaze palsy and her speech is slurred. She has not responded well to Levodopa. What is the most likely diagnosis? Idiopathic Parkinson s Disease Multiple System Atrophy MSA Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP Corticobasal Degeneration CBD Dementia with Lewy Bodies DLB 2 This is Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP . The classic triad is Vertical Gaze Palsy trouble looking down early Falls especially backwards and Pseudobulbar palsy. It is a Tauopathy. Levodopa is almost always administered with Carbidopa. What is the primary function of Carbidopa? It increases the breakdown of Levodopa in the brain It facilitates transport of Levodopa across the Blood-Brain Barrier It inhibits DOPA decarboxylase in the periphery It directly stimulates dopamine receptors It prevents the reuptake of dopamine 2 Carbidopa is a peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor . It cannot cross the BBB so it inhibits the conversion of Levodopa to Dopamine in the periphery increasing the amount of Levodopa that reaches the brain and reducing systemic side effects. A 45-year-old Ashkenazi Jewish male presents with symptoms of Parkinson s disease. He mentions that his cousin had Gaucher s disease. Which genetic mutation is most likely involved? SNCA Alpha-Synuclein LRRK2 GBA Glucocerebrosidase Parkin PINK1 2 Mutations in the GBA gene which encodes Glucocerebrosidase are the most common genetic risk factor for Parkinson s disease especially in the Ashkenazi Jewish population and are linked to Gaucher s Disease . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l58", "title": "Lecture 58: Pharmacology: Parkinson and Alzheimer", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l58 Lecture 58 Pharmacology Parkinson and Alzheimer Neurology Lecture 58 Pharmacology Parkinson and Alzheimer\\n\\n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 58\\n\\n 1. Parkinson s Disease PD Pharmacology\\n Goal Restore Dopamine DA levels or reduce Acetylcholine ACh activity to fix the DA/ACh imbalance.\\n\\n 1.1 Levodopa / Carbidopa Sinemet \\n Mechanism \\n Levodopa L-Dopa Precursor to Dopamine. Crosses the Blood-Brain Barrier BBB via neutral amino acid transporter. Converted to DA by Dopa Decarboxylase DDC .\\n Carbidopa Inhibits Peripheral DDC . Does NOT cross BBB.\\n Why Combine? Without Carbidopa only 1-3% of L-Dopa enters the brain most converted peripherally $\\\\rightarrow$ side effects . With Carbidopa 10% enters brain.\\n Adverse Effects \\n Peripheral Nausea/Vomiting CTZ stimulation Orthostatic Hypotension Cardiac Arrhythmias.\\n Central Dyskinesias writhing movements after long-term use Psychosis Hallucinations/Delusions .\\n Drug Interactions \\n High Protein Meals Amino acids compete for transporter $\\\\rightarrow$ Decrease efficacy.\\n Vitamin B6 Pyridoxine Increases peripheral breakdown of L-Dopa Avoid high doses .\\n\\n 1.2 COMT Inhibitors \\ -capone\\ \\n Mechanism Inhibit Catechol-O-Methyltransferase COMT blocking conversion of L-Dopa to 3-O-Methyldopa 3-OMD . Prolongs L-Dopa half-life \\ On\\ time .\\n Agents \\n Entacapone Peripheral inhibition only.\\n Tolcapone Central & Peripheral. Black Box Warning Hepatotoxicity Monitor LFTs .\\n Opicapone Long-acting Once daily .\\n Side Effect Orange/Brown discoloration of urine .\\n\\n 1.3 MAO-B Inhibitors \\ -giline\\ \\n Mechanism Inhibit Monoamine Oxidase B MAO-B preventing central breakdown of Dopamine.\\n Agents \\n Selegiline Metabolized to Amphetamine/Methamphetamine $\\\\rightarrow$ Insomnia Do not take late at night .\\n Rasagiline Irreversible inhibitor.\\n Safinamide Reversible MAO-B inhibitor + Glutamate modulation.\\n Neuroprotection May prevent MPTP conversion to toxic MPP+ preventing mitochondrial damage .\\n Interactions Risk of Serotonin Syndrome if combined with SSRIs/TCAs though risk is lower with selective MAO-B vs MAO-A .\\n\\n 1.4 Dopamine Agonists\\n Mechanism Directly stimulate Post-synaptic Dopamine Receptors D2/D3 . No conversion needed.\\n Agents \\n Pramipexole Mirapex D3 > D2. Antioxidant properties. Also for Restless Leg Syndrome RLS .\\n Ropinirole Requip D2/D3. Also for RLS.\\n Rotigotine Transdermal Patch stable levels .\\n Adverse Effects \\n Impulse Control Disorders Pathological Gambling Hypersexuality Shopping.\\n Sleep Attacks Sudden onset of sleep without warning.\\n\\n 1.5 Rescue Therapy Apomorphine\\n Use Acute subcutaneous injection for severe \\ Off\\ episodes Freezing .\\n Adverse Effect Severe Nausea/Vomiting.\\n Management Pre-treat with Trimethobenzamide .\\n Contraindication Do NOT use 5-HT3 Antagonists Ondansetron/Zofran $\\\\rightarrow$ Causes profound Hypotension and Syncope.\\n\\n 1.6 Amantadine\\n Mechanism Antiviral Flu A - M2 channel blocker . In PD NMDA Receptor Antagonist + increases DA release.\\n Use Treating Levodopa-induced Dyskinesias .\\n Side Effect Livedo Reticularis Purplish net-like skin rash .\\n\\n 1.7 Adenosine Antagonist Istradefylline\\n Mechanism Adenosine A2A receptor antagonist in Basal Ganglia. Reduces \\ Off\\ time.\\n Side Effects Insomnia Structurally related to Caffeine .\\n\\n 1.8 Anticholinergics Benztropine Trihexyphenidyl \\n Use Tremor predominant PD.\\n Adverse Effect Antimuscarinic Dry mouth urinary retention confusion . Avoid in Geriatrics Beers List .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Alzheimer s Disease AD Pharmacology\\n Goal Increase Acetylcholine ACh or reduce Glutamate Excitotoxicity.\\n\\n 2.1 Acetylcholinesterase Inhibitors AChEIs \\n Mechanism Inhibit breakdown of ACh in the synaptic cleft.\\n Agents \\n Donepezil Aricept . \\n Rivastigmine Inhibits AChE and Butyrylcholinesterase. Patch available.\\n Galantamine Also acts as a nicotinic receptor agonist.\\n Adverse Effects Cholinergic Excess SLUDGE .\\n Nausea Diarrhea Vomiting.\\n Bradycardia Risk of syncope Caution with Beta-blockers .\\n Bronchoconstriction Caution in asthma/COPD .\\n\\n 2.2 NMDA Receptor Antagonist\\n Agent Memantine .\\n Mechanism Uncompetitive NMDA Antagonist . Blocks tonic/excessive glutamate stimulation excitotoxicity while allowing physiologic signals.\\n Use Moderate to Severe AD. Can be combined with AChEIs.\\n\\n 2.3 Anti-Amyloid Monoclonal Antibodies\\n Mechanism Bind and clear Amyloid Beta plaques.\\n Agents \\n Lecanemab Targets Protofibrils.\\n Donanemab. \\n Adverse Effects ARIA Amyloid-Related Imaging Abnormalities .\\n ARIA-E Edema/Effusion.\\n ARIA-H Micro-Hemorrhage/Hemosiderin.\\n Monitoring Requires serial MRIs .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. High Yield Renders\\n\\n highyield\\n Parkinson s Drug Toxicities & Pearls \\n| Drug | Key Mechanism/Fact | The \\ Board Exam\\ Side Effect |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Carbidopa | Peripheral DDC Inhibitor | Reduces Peripheral side effects Nausea/Ortho |\\n| Tolcapone | COMT Inhibitor Central/Periph | Hepatotoxicity Monitor LFTs |\\n| Entacapone | COMT Inhibitor Peripheral | Orange Urine |\\n| Selegiline | MAO-B Inhibitor | Metabolized to Amphetamine Insomnia |\\n| Dopamine Agonists | Pramipexole Ropinirole | Impulse Control Gambling Sleep Attacks |\\n| Apomorphine | \\ Rescue\\ Injection | Severe Nausea NO Zofran! $\\\\rightarrow$ Hypotension |\\n| Amantadine | NMDA Antagonist | Livedo Reticularis Lacy rash |\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n Alzheimer s Drug Toxicities \\n| Class | Agents | Key Side Effects |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| AChE Inhibitors | Donepezil Rivastigmine Galantamine | Bradycardia Falls/Syncope Diarrhea Nausea |\\n| NMDA Antagonist | Memantine | Dizziness Confusion Use in Mod-Severe AD |\\n| Monoclonals | Lecanemab Donanemab | ARIA Edema/Hemorrhage on MRI |\\n \\n questions A 75-year-old male with Parkinson s disease is started on Selegiline as an adjunct to his regimen. He complains of difficulty falling asleep. This side effect is most likely due to the metabolism of the drug into which substance? Dopamine Norepinephrine Amphetamine Serotonin Histamine 2 Selegiline an MAO-B inhibitor is metabolized into l-methamphetamine and l-amphetamine which have stimulant properties and can cause insomnia . It should be taken earlier in the day. A patient with Parkinson s disease experiences severe \\ off\\ episodes where he freezes and cannot move. His physician prescribes a subcutaneous rescue medication. Which antiemetic is CONTRAINDICATED in this patient due to the risk of profound hypotension? Trimethobenzamide Ondansetron Promethazine Prochlorperazine Metoclopramide 1 The rescue medication is likely Apomorphine . It causes severe nausea but it is CONTRAINDICATED to use 5-HT3 antagonists like Ondansetron Zofran because the combination causes severe hypotension and loss of consciousness. Trimethobenzamide is the preferred antiemetic. A 68-year-old female is prescribed Entacapone to help manage her motor fluctuations. She calls the clinic in a panic because her urine has changed color. What color is her urine likely to be? Blue Red Orange/Brown Green Black 2 Entacapone a COMT inhibitor is known to cause a harmless orange or brownish discoloration of the urine . A patient with Alzheimer s disease is started on Donepezil. During a follow-up visit his heart rate is noted to be 48 bpm. This bradycardia is a result of Blockade of Nicotinic receptors Increased Acetylcholine activity at the SA node Inhibition of Calcium channels Stimulation of Beta-1 receptors Blockade of Muscarinic receptors 1 Donepezil is an Acetylcholinesterase Inhibitor which increases levels of Acetylcholine. Excess ACh acts on M2 muscarinic receptors in the heart parasympathetic activation leading to Bradycardia which can increase the risk of syncope and falls. A patient with early Alzheimer s disease is started on Lecanemab. Two months later a surveillance MRI is performed. It shows areas of edema ARIA-E . What is the mechanism of action of this drug? Uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist Acetylcholinesterase inhibitor Monoclonal antibody against Amyloid Beta Muscarinic receptor agonist Inhibitor of Tau phosphorylation 2 Lecanemab is a monoclonal antibody that targets Amyloid Beta protofibrils. A known adverse effect of this class is Amyloid Related Imaging Abnormalities ARIA which can present as edema ARIA-E or hemorrhage ARIA-H . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l61", "title": "Lecture #61: Gait and Movement", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l61 Lecture 61 Gait and Movement lecturer Adena Leder D.O. Neurology session Lecture 61 prev l60 next l62 pdf pdfs/Lecture 61. A. Leder D.O. PPT - updated 1.29.26 .pdf highYieldPdf content/L61 HighYield Render.pdf transcript Lecture 61 Gait and Movement\\n \\n Lecturer Adena Leder D.O.\\n Session Lecture 61\\n\\n Introduction to Movement Disorders Slide 2 \\n\\nMovement disorders represent a complex interplay between the basal ganglia cerebellum and motor cortex. The approach to a patient with a suspected movement disorder such as Parkinson s Disease PD requires a systematic evaluation of their history observation \\ Look\\ passive tone \\ Feel\\ and active strength/gait \\ Move\\ . Understanding the distinction between hyperkinetic and hypokinetic states is fundamental to narrowing the differential diagnosis.\\n\\n Core Assessement Objectives\\n- History Establish onset progression and risk factors.\\n- Observation \\ Look\\ for tremors posture and involuntary movements.\\n- Tone \\ Feel\\ for rigidity lead-pipe vs. cogwheel .\\n- Function \\ Assess\\ strength and observed gait mechanics.\\n\\n History Taking The Foundations Slide 3-8 \\n\\nA detailed history is the cornerstone of diagnosis as many movement disorders are clinical diagnoses without definitive biomarkers.\\n\\n Demographics and Onset Slide 3 \\n- Age Early vs. Late onset changes the differential.\\n- Handedness Asymmetry is a hallmark of idiopathic PD symptoms often start on the dominant side .\\n- Timeline Establishing the \\ Year 0\\ of symptom onset.\\n- Prodromal Phase Non-motor symptoms often precede motor symptoms by years e.g. loss of smell constipation .\\n\\n Medical and Medication History Slide 4-5 \\n- Secondary Parkinsonism Rule out drug-induced causes.\\n - Anti-emetics Metoclopramide Reglan used long-term.\\n - Anti-psychotics Dopamine blockers Haloperidol Risperidone .\\n - Other Offenders Lithium and Valproic Acid Depakote can cause tremor.\\n- Comorbidities Cardiovascular disease Diabetes Cancer history.\\n\\n correlate\\nDrug-Induced Parkinsonism\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Blockade of dopamine D2 receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway.\\n- Presentation Symmetric parkinsonism unlike asymmetric idiopathic PD often without resting tremor.\\n- Diagnosis Temporal relationship with offending agent; lack of response to Levodopa.\\n- Treatment Stop the offending agent.\\n- Risk Factors Elderly high potency antipsychotics anti-emetics.\\n \\n\\n Environmental Exposures Slide 8 \\nCertain toxins are selectively toxic to dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra.\\n\\n| Exposure | Context | Mechanism/Notes |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Pesticides | Farm/Vineyard work | Rotenone Paraquat Glyphosate Roundup |\\n| Well Water | Rural living | Heavy metals/Pesticide runoff |\\n| Agent Orange | Vietnam Veterans | Increased risk of PD |\\n| MPTP | Synthetic Heroin | Acute permanent destruction of Substantia Nigra |\\n| Head Trauma | Boxers/Football | Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy CTE overlap |\\n\\n Genetics Slide 6-7 \\nWhile most PD is sporadic idiopathic genetics play a role especially in young-onset cases.\\n- Prevalence 15% of patients have a family history.\\n- Risk Group Ashkenazi Jewish descent Higher carrier rate .\\n- Major Genes \\n - LRRK2 Common autosomal dominant.\\n - GBA1 Associated with Gaucher s carrier state.\\n - PRKN Parkin Early onset recessive.\\n\\n Review of Systems The \\ Hidden\\ Signs Slide 11-12 \\n\\nParkinson s Disease is a whole-body synucleinopathy not just a brain disease. Autonomic and sensory dysfunction often constitute the \\ Prodrome.\\ \\n\\n highyield\\n The Parkinson s Prodrome \\nSymptoms that can precede motor signs by 10-20 years \\n1. Anosmia Loss of smell.\\n2. REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Acting out vivid dreams injuring bed partner due to loss of REM atonia.\\n3. Constipation Slow colonic transit.\\n4. Autonomic Dysfunction Orthostatic hypotension urinary frequency/urgency.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\nSeborrheic Dermatitis\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Dysregulation of autonomic control of sebaceous glands sebum production .\\n- Presentation \\ Greasy \\ scaly erythematous rash typically on the face brow nasolabial folds and scalp.\\n- Lecturer s Point A common dermatologic finding in PD patients.\\n \\n\\n The Physical Examination Slide 14-17 \\n\\n Cranial Nerves and face\\n- Hypomimia \\ Masked Facies\\ \\u2013 reduction in facial expressivity and blinking.\\n- Vertical Gaze Limitation suggests Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP an atypical parkinsonism.\\n- Glabellar Tap Persistent blinking when tapping the forehead Myerson s sign .\\n\\n Motor Tone Assessment\\nExamination of tone differentiates spasticity corticospinal from rigidity extrapyramidal .\\n- Lead-Pipe Rigidity Uniform constant resistance throughout range of motion.\\n- Cogwheel Rigidity Ratchet-like resistance Rigidity + Tremor .\\n- Asymmetry Idiopathic PD typically starts unilaterally .\\n\\n Tremor Analysis\\n- Resting Tremor \\ Pill-rolling\\ 4-6 Hz . Suppressed with movement. Classic for PD.\\n- Postural/Action Tremor Occurs when holding a pose against gravity. Classic for Essential Tremor .\\n\\n correlate\\nEssential Tremor\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Cerebellar-thalamic circuit oscillation.\\n- Presentation Bilateral symmetric action tremor hands voice head . improves with alcohol.\\n- Diagnosis Clinical. Spiral drawing test shows shaky axis.\\n- Treatment Propranolol Beta-blocker or Primidone.\\n- Differentiation PD is Resting; ET is Action.\\n \\n\\n Gait Assessment Slide 18 \\nThe \\ Get Up and Go\\ or observable gait provides high-yield diagnostic clues.\\n- Shuffling Short steps marche \\u00e0 petits pas .\\n- Fenestration Accelerating steps to keep balance.\\n- Freezing Feet feel \\ stuck to the floor \\ especially in doorways/turning.\\n- En Bloc Turning Turning like a statue/log rather than pivoting segments.\\n- Reduced Arm Swing Often the first motor sign early asymmetric .\\n\\n Diagnosis and Management Slide 19 \\n\\nDiagnosis remains primarily clinical requiring Bradykinesia plus either Rigidity or Resting Tremor.\\n\\n Diagnostic Tests\\n- DaTscan Dopamine transporter SPECT scan. Shows loss of uptake in striatum comma shape becomes a period .\\n- Levodopa Challenge Significant improvement in symptoms confirms Dopaminergic deficiency responsive in PD non-responsive in Parkinson-Plus syndromes .\\n- Skin Biopsy For Alpha-synuclein deposits.\\n\\n mnemonic\\n The \\ S\\ Signs of Parkinson s Disease \\n S-WORDS Slide 20 \\n\\n- S hake Resting Tremor \\n- S tiffness Rigidity \\n- S low Bradykinesia \\n- S tate Masked Facies \\n- S huffle Gait \\n- S mall writing Micrographia \\n- S mell loss Anosmia \\n- S leep REM behavior disorder \\n- S oft speech Hypophonia \\n- S eborrhea Dermatitis \\n- S adness Depression \\n \\n questions 1. A 62-year-old male presents with a resting tremor in his right hand and general slowness of movement. His wife notes that he has been acting out violent dreams and shouting in his sleep for the past 5 years. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Essential Tremor Parkinson s Disease Drug-Induced Parkinsonism Huntington s Disease Vascular Dementia 1 Parkinson s Disease is characterized by the triad of resting tremor bradykinesia and rigidity. REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD is a strong prodromal sign that can precede motor symptoms by years. 2. Which of the following clinical findings is REQUIRED for a diagnosis of Parkinson s Disease? Resting Tremor Bradykinesia Postural Instability Cogwheel Rigidity Anosmia 1 Bradykinesia slowness of movement is the central and required feature for the diagnosis of parkinsonism. While tremor is common some patients present with the akinetic-rigid variant without tremor. 3. A 25-year-old male presents with new-onset resting tremor and rigidity. He has a history of Schizophrenia and recently started a new medication. Which mechanism explains his motor symptoms? Loss of dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra Blockade of Dopamine D2 receptors Depletion of Acetylcholine in the Striatum Cerebellar atrophy Autoimmune attack on the neuromuscular junction 1 Blockade of D2 receptors by antipsychotic medications e.g. Haloperidol causes Drug-Induced Parkinsonism . This is a functional blockade not the structural cell death seen in idiopathic PD. 4. During a gait examination a patient takes multiple small shuffling steps and turns his body as a single rigid unit. This turning pattern is best described as Ataxic turning En bloc turning Magnetic gait Scissoring gait Steppage gait 1 En bloc turning is the classic description of a PD patient turning like a statue or log requiring multiple small steps to reorient due to axial rigidity and bradykinesia. 5. Which environmental exposure is historically linked to the rapid destruction of the Substantia Nigra and acute parkinsonism? Lead paint MPTP Asbestos Silica dust Radon 1 MPTP is a neurotoxin found in synthetic heroin contaminants that selectively destroys dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra serving as a model for MPTP-induced parkinsonism. What is the cardinal motor feature required for a diagnosis of Parkinson s Disease? Bradykinesia plus Rigidity OR Tremor Diagnosis What type of tremor is characteristic of Parkinson s Disease? Resting Tremor 4-6 Hz Pill-rolling Clinical What dermatologic condition is commonly associated with Parkinson s Disease? Seborrheic Dermatitis Clinical What sleep disorder is a strong prodromal sign of synucleinopathies like PD? REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Clinical What is the mechanism of Drug-Induced Parkinsonism? Dopamine D2 receptor blockade e.g. Antipsychotics Metoclopramide Pathology What genetic mutation is most common in Ashkenazi Jewish patients with PD? LRRK2 or GBA1 Genetics What physical exam finding suggests Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP rather than PD? Vertical Gaze Palsy Inability to look up/down Diagnosis What is En bloc turning? Turning the body as a single rigid unit/log requiring multiple steps Clinical What effect does Cigarette Smoking have on Parkinson s risk? It is associated with a DECREASED risk protective Epidemiology What differentiates Essential Tremor from Parkinson s Tremor? Essential Tremor is Action/Postural and symmetric; PD is Resting and asymmetric Diagnosis BandB Neurology > Movement Disorders Pathoma CNS > Degenerative Disorders > Parkinson Disease SketchyPath Neuro > Degenerative > Parkinson FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Parkinson Disease The Parkinson s Prodrome Non-motor symptoms like Anosmia Constipation and REM Sleep Behavior Disorder can appear 10-20 years before the first tremor. Asymmetry is Key Idiopathic PD almost always starts asymmetrically one hand/side . Symmetric onset should raise suspicion for Drug-Induced Parkinsonism or Atypical syndromes. Tremor vs. Bradykinesia Bradykinesia is the disability-causing symptom. Tremor is the cosmetic symptom. Some patients are Akinetic-Rigid and never develop a tremor. Arm Swing Reduced arm swing on one side is often the earliest detectable motor sign of PD during gait analysis. mindmap Gait and Movement Disorders\\n \\n Parkinson s Disease TRAP \\n- T remor Resting Pill-rolling Asymmetric\\n- R igidity Cogwheel vs Lead-pipe\\n- A kinesia/Bradykinesia Slow movement Masked facies Decreased blink\\n- P ostural Instability Late finding Pull test\\n\\n Etiology\\n- Idiopathic Most common Age > 60\\n- Genetic LRRK2 GBA1 Ashkenazi Jewish \\n- Environmental Pesticides MPTP Agent Orange\\n- Drug-Induced Antipsychotics Metoclopramide D2 Blockers \\n\\n Prodromal Signs\\n- Sensory Anosmia Smell loss \\n- Sleep REM Sleep Behavior Disorder\\n- Autonomic Constipation Orthostasis\\n\\n Differential Diagnosis\\n- Essential Tremor Action tremor Improves w/ Alcohol Bilateral\\n- PSP Vertical Gaze Palsy Early Falls\\n- MSA Severe Autonomic Dysfunction anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l62", "title": "Lecture #62: Brachial Plexus Lesions and Neuropathies", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l62 Lecture 62 Brachial Plexus Lesions and Neuropathies lecturer Matthew Mihlbachler Ph.D. Neurology session Lecture 62 prev l61 next l63 pdf pdfs/Lecture 62. M. Mihlbachler Ph.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L62 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L62 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 62 Brachial Plexus Lesions and Neuropathies\\n\\n Lecturer Matthew Mihlbachler Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 62\\n\\n Introduction to Peripheral Nerve Injuries Slide 2-3 \\n\\nUnderstanding peripheral nerve injuries requires a solid grasp of the brachial plexus architecture and the relationship between spinal nerves dermatomes and myotomes. A \\ nerve\\ is a bundle of axons and injury can be partial or complete. The clinical presentation\\u2014whether it s a motor deficit weakness/paralysis or sensory deficit numbness/tingling \\u2014depends entirely on the location of the lesion. We differentiate between injuries at the \\ Root/Trunk\\ level affecting multiple nerves and \\ Peripheral Nerve\\ level affecting specific muscles/skin areas .\\n\\n Key Principles\\n- Roots vs. Peripheral Nerves Lesions at the root level affect myotomes groups of muscles and dermatomes skin strip . Peripheral nerve lesions affect the specific muscles and skin supplied by that nerve.\\n- Variation Anatomical variations exist; not every patient presents with textbook symptoms.\\n- Scope This lecture focuses on classic well-documented neuropathies.\\n\\n Brachial Plexus Trunk Lesions\\n\\nInjuries to the trunks of the brachial plexus cause widespread deficits because they affect fibers destined for multiple peripheral nerves. These are often associated with traumatic traction injuries during birth or severe trauma in adults.\\n\\n Superior Trunk Lesion C5-C6 Erb-Duchenne Palsy Slide 35-40 \\n\\nExcessive separation of the neck and shoulder e.g. shoulder dystocia during birth motorcycle accident tears the C5 and C6 roots of the Superior Trunk. This devastates the muscles of the shoulder and arm supplied by these roots.\\n\\n- Nerves Affected \\n - Musculocutaneous n. Loss of Biceps Brachii Flexion Supination .\\n - Axillary n. Loss of Deltoid Abduction and Teres Minor Lateral Rotation .\\n - Suprascapular n. Loss of Supraspinatus Abduction initiation and Infraspinatus Lateral Rotation .\\n- Resulting Posture The \\ Waiter s Tip\\ position. The arm is adducted loss of deltoid/supraspinatus medially rotated loss of lateral rotators and extended/pronated loss of biceps .\\n\\n correlate\\nErb-Duchenne Palsy Erb s Palsy \\n\\n- Pathophysiology Traction injury rupturing Superior Trunk C5-C6 .\\n- Presentation \\ Waiter s Tip\\ position \\n - Adducted shoulder \\n - Medially Rotated shoulder \\n - Extended elbow \\n - Pronated forearm \\n- Sensory Loss Lateral aspect of arm and forearm C5-C6 dermatomes .\\n- Risk Factors Shoulder dystocia macrosomia motorcycle accidents.\\n \\n\\n mnemonic\\n Erb-Duchenne Palsy \\n \\ Waiter s Tip\\ \\n\\n- Imagine a waiter subtly accepting a tip behind their back Arm hangs by side rotated internally palm facing back.\\n \\n\\n Inferior Trunk Lesion C8-T1 Klumpke s Palsy Slide 43-49 \\n\\nHyper-abduction of the arm e.g. catching a tree branch while falling pulling an infant s arm during birth tears the C8 and T1 roots. This primarily affects the intrinsic muscles of the hand.\\n\\n- Nerves Affected \\n - Ulnar n. C8-T1 All intrinsic hand muscles it supplies.\\n - Median n. C8-T1 component Intrinsic hand muscles Thumbnail lateral lumbricals .\\n- Key Deficit Loss of ALL intrinsic hand muscles Lumbricals and Interossei .\\n - Function of Lumbricals Flex MCP Extend PIP/DIP.\\n - Loss Extension of MCP by unopposed extensor digitorum Flexion of PIP/DIP by unopposed flexors .\\n- Result Total Claw Hand .\\n\\n correlate\\nKlumpke s Palsy\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Traction injury rupturing Inferior Trunk C8-T1 .\\n- Presentation \\ Total Claw Hand\\ Slide 49 .\\n - Clawing Hyperextension of MCP + Flexion of PIP/DIP in ALL digits 2-5 .\\n - Atrophy Wasting of intrinsic hand muscles interossei guttering .\\n- Sensory Loss Medial aspect of arm forearm and hand C8-T1 dermatomes .\\n- Lecturer s Point Distinguish from isolated Ulnar Claw which spares digits 2-3 . Klumpke affects ALL digits because C8-T1 feeds both Ulnar and Median intrinsic muscles.\\n \\n\\n Peripheral Nerve Injuries The Scapula and Shoulder\\n\\nMoving distally isolated nerve injuries present with more specific functional losses.\\n\\n Long Thoracic Nerve C5-C7 Slide 52 \\nLesion causes paralysis of the Serratus Anterior which anchors the scapula to the thoracic wall.\\n\\n correlate\\nWinged Scapula\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Damage to Long Thoracic Nerve Saltatory/Stab wound Radical Mastectomy .\\n- Presentation Medial border of scapula protrudes posteriorly \\ wings\\ when patient pushes against a wall.\\n- Functional Deficit Inability to abduct arm above horizontal loss of scapular rotation .\\n \\n\\n Axillary Nerve C5-C6 Slide 54-55 \\nCommonly injured in Surgical Neck Fractures of the humerus or Anterior-Inferior Dislocation of the shoulder.\\n\\n highyield\\n Axillary Nerve Injury \\n- Motor Loss of Abduction >15 degrees due to Deltoid paralysis Slide 54 .\\n- Sensory Loss of sensation over the \\ Regimental Badge\\ area Lateral shoulder .\\n \\n\\n Radial Nerve Injuries Posterior Cord \\n\\nThe Radial nerve supplies the Extensors of the arm forearm and hand. Presentation depends heavily on the level of injury.\\n\\n Axilla Level \\ Saturday Night Palsy\\ Slide 57-58 \\nCompression in the axilla e.g. drunk arm over chair crutches .\\n- Deficits \\n - Triceps Loss of elbow extension often spared if gravity assists .\\n - Wrist/Fingers Loss of extension -> Wrist Drop .\\n - Sensory Posterior arm forearm and dorsum of hand.\\n\\n correlate\\nSaturday Night Palsy Radial Neuropathy at Axilla \\n\\n- Pathophysiology Compression of Radial Nerve in the axilla.\\n- Presentation Wrist Drop inability to extend wrist/fingers .\\n- Sensory Numbness on posterior arm/forearm/hand.\\n- Differentiation Triceps reflex may be diminished unlike distal lesions .\\n \\n\\n Mid-Shaft Humerus Fracture Radial Groove Slide 60-61 \\nThe radial nerve spirals around the humerus in the radial groove.\\n- Deficits \\n - Triceps SPARED Branches to triceps arise proximal to the groove. Elbow extension is intact.\\n - Wrist/Fingers Wrist Drop persists extensors of forearm are distal .\\n - Sensory Posterior Arm sensory SPARED . Numbness is limited to the dorsum of the hand.\\n\\n highyield\\n Radial Nerve Axilla vs. Spiral Groove \\n- Axilla Saturday Night Triceps weak/paralyzed + Wrist Drop + Posterior Arm Numbness.\\n- Spiral Groove Humerus Fracture Triceps SPARED + Wrist Drop + NO Posterior Arm Numbness.\\n \\n\\n Median Nerve Injuries Lateral/Medial Cords \\n\\nThe Median nerve supplies the Flexors of the forearm except FCU and ulnar half of FDP and the Thenar muscles OAF Oppose Abduct Flex thumb and Lateral 2 Lumbricals .\\n\\n Lesion at Elbow Supracondylar Fracture Slide 65-68 \\n- Motor Loss \\n - Forearm Flexors Loss of Pronation Wrist Flexion weak Digit Flexion Digits 1-3 .\\n - Hand Loss of Thenar muscles No opposition .\\n- Presentation \\n - Hand of Benediction occurs when asked to MAKE A FIST . Digits 1 2 3 cannot flex. Digits 4 5 flex Ulnar nerve intact .\\n - Ape Hand Atrophy of Thenar eminence thumb lies flat .\\n\\n correlate\\nHand of Benediction Benediction Sign \\n\\n- Pathophysiology High Median Nerve Injury at Elbow/Proximal .\\n- Presentation When asked to MAKE A FIST index and middle fingers remain extended; thumb remains extended. Ring and little fingers flex.\\n- Mechanism Loss of FDS all and FDP lateral half . FDP medial half supplied by Ulnar n. remains intact.\\n- Lecturer s Point Do not confuse with Ulnar Claw! Benediction is an ACTIVE sign asking to make a fist .\\n \\n\\n Lesion at Wrist Carpal Tunnel Syndrome / Slash Slide 69-70 \\n- Motor Loss LOAF muscles Lateral 2 Lumbricals Opponens Abductor pollicis brevis Flexor pollicis brevis .\\n - Forearm Flexors SPARED Patient CAN flex fingers can make a fist . NO Hand of Benediction.\\n- Sensory Loss Lateral 3.5 digits.\\n - Carpal Tunnel Palmar cutaneous branch SPARED branches proximal to tunnel . Palm sensation intact.\\n - Suicide Slash Palmar cutaneous branch cut. Palm numb.\\n\\n correlate\\nCarpal Tunnel Syndrome\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Compression of Median Nerve in carpal tunnel.\\n- Presentation Paresthesia in lateral 3.5 digits. Thenar atrophy late .\\n- Spare Palmar Cutaneous Branch sensation over thenar eminence is INTACT .\\n- Risk Factors Repetitive use pregnancy hypothyroidism.\\n \\n\\n Ulnar Nerve Injuries Medial Cord \\n\\nThe Ulnar nerve supplies FCU Medial half of FDP and most Intrinsic Hand Muscles Hypothenar Interossei Medial 2 Lumbricals Adductor Pollicis .\\n\\n Lesion at Elbow Cubital Tunnel Syndrome Slide 72 \\n- Motor Loss \\n - FCU & Medial FDP Weak wrist flexion Loss of DIP flexion in digits 4-5.\\n - Intrinsics Loss of abduction/adduction Interossei Loss of lumbricals 3-4.\\n- Presentation Ulnar Claw when opening hand .\\n- Ulnar Paradox Clawing is LESS severe at the elbow than at the wrist. Why? Because the FDP flexor to digits 4-5 is ALSO paralyzed so the fingers aren t pulled into as much flexion.\\n\\n correlate\\nCubital Tunnel Syndrome\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Compression of Ulnar Nerve at medial epicondyle funny bone .\\n- Presentation Numbness in medial 1.5 digits. Weak grasp. Mild Clawing.\\n- Ulnar Paradox High lesion = Less Clawing. Low lesion = More Clawing.\\n \\n\\n Lesion at Wrist Guyon s Canal Syndrome Slide 76-78 \\n- Motor Loss Intrinsic muscles ONLY. Forearm flexors FDP are SPARED.\\n- Presentation Severe Ulnar Claw .\\n - Since FDP is working it pulls the DIP joints of digits 4-5 into flexion against the paralyzed lumbricals/interossei.\\n\\n correlate\\nGuyon s Canal Syndrome Ulnar Tunnel \\n\\n- Pathophysiology Compression at wrist e.g. cyclist handlebars .\\n- Presentation Ulnar Claw Severe .\\n - Claw Hyperextension of MCP + Flexion of PIP/DIP Digits 4-5 .\\n- Sensory Medial 1.5 digits. Dorsal cutaneous branch SPARED branches proximal to wrist -> Dorsal hand sensation intact.\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n The \\ Hand\\ Signs Summary Slide 80 \\n\\n| Sign | Nerve | Level | Action/Context | Feature |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Wrist Drop | Radial | Axilla/Humerus | Resting/Extension | Cannot extend wrist |\\n| Hand of Benediction | Median | Elbow High | Making a Fist | Digits 1-3 won t flex |\\n| Ape Hand | Median | Any | Resting | Thenar atrophy |\\n| Ulnar Claw | Ulnar | Wrist Low | Extending Fingers | Digits 4-5 clawed |\\n| Total Claw | C8-T1 Klumpke | Trunk | Resting | All digits clawed |\\n \\n questions 1. A 21-year-old wrestler complains of weakness in his hand and tingling in his 5th digit. Examination reveals weakness in finger adduction and abduction. He has tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Which diagnosis is most likely? Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Cubital Tunnel Syndrome Guyon s Canal Syndrome Erb-Duchenne Palsy Pronator Teres Syndrome 1 Cubital Tunnel Syndrome is compression of the ulnar nerve at the medial epicondyle. It causes paresthesia in the ulnar distribution and weakness of intrinsic hand muscles interossei leading to difficulty holding a piece of paper adduction . 2. A newborn presents with the right arm adducted medially rotated elbow extended and forearm pronated Waiter s Tip position . Where is the lesion? C8-T1 Roots Inferior Trunk C5-C6 Roots Superior Trunk Posterior Cord Axillary Nerve Long Thoracic Nerve 1 Erb-Duchenne Palsy involves the Superior Trunk C5-C6 . Loss of abductors C5 lateral rotators C5 and flexors/supinators C5-C6 leads to the classic posture. 3. A patient with a mid-shaft humeral fracture presents with wrist drop. Detailed sensation testing reveals numbness on the dorsum of the hand but sensation on the posterior arm is INT. Which nerve structure is spared? Deep branch of Radial Nerve Posterior Brachial Cutaneous Nerve Superficial branch of Radial Nerve Axillary Nerve Musculocutaneous Nerve 1 In a Spiral Groove lesion mid-shaft fracture the Posterior Brachial Cutaneous Nerve supplying posterior arm skin is spared because it branches off the Radial nerve proximal to the groove. 4. When asked to make a fist a patient s index and middle fingers remain extended while the ring and little fingers flex normally. The thumb also fails to flex. This presentation is best described as Ulnar Claw Total Claw Hand Hand of Benediction Wrist Drop Dupuytren s Contracture 2 Hand of Benediction is a sign of High Median Nerve Injury at the elbow . It is an active sign seen when attempting to make a fist due to paralysis of FDS and lateral FDP. 5. A patient presents with winging of the right scapula when pushing against a wall. The injured nerve originates from which spinal levels? C3 C4 C5 C5 C6 C7 C8 T1 C5 C6 C6 C7 C8 1 The Long Thoracic Nerve which innervates the Serratus Anterior originates from roots C5 C6 C7 C5-6-7 wings your heaven . What is the classic posture of Erb-Duchenne Palsy? Waiter s Tip Adducted Medially Rotated Extended Pronated Clinical Which nerve roots are damaged in Klumpke s Palsy? C8-T1 Inferior Trunk Anatomy What physical exam finding is characteristic of Long Thoracic Nerve injury? Winged Scapula medial border protrudes Diagnosis What area of sensation is lost in Axillary Nerve injury? Lateral shoulder Regimental Badge area Clinical Distinguish Radial Nerve injury at Axilla vs. Mid-Humerus regarding Triceps function. Axilla Triceps weak/paralyzed. Mid-Humerus Triceps SPARED. Diagnosis What nerve injury causes Hand of Benediction ? High Median Nerve injury at elbow Clinical Why is sensation over the thenar eminence spared in Carpal Tunnel Syndrome? The Palmar Cutaneous branch of the Median nerves branches PROXIMAL to the carpal tunnel. Anatomy What is the Ulnar Paradox ? High ulnar lesions cause LESS clawing than low lesions because FDP to digits 4-5 is also paralyzed . Concept Which deformity results from combined Combined Median and Ulnar intrinsic paralysis e.g. Klumpke s ? Total Claw Hand Clawing of all digits Clinical What is the common cause of Saturday Night Palsy ? Compression of the Radial Nerve in the axilla e.g. arm over chair . Etiology BandB Neurology > Peripheral Nervous System > Brachial Plexus FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Brachial Plexus Lesions Pathoma N/A Benediction vs. Claw Benediction is an ACTIVE sign High Median injury asked to make fist . Ulnar Claw is a PASSIVE resting deformity Low Ulnar injury . C5-6-7 Wings Back to Heaven Mnemonic for Long Thoracic Nerve C5-C6-C7 -> Serratus Anterior -> Winged Scapula. The Palmar Cutaneous Exception Both Median and Ulnar nerves have palmar cutaneous branches that arise PROXIMAL to the wrist tunnels. If the PALM is numb the lesion is NOT in the tunnel Carpal/Guyon . mindmap Brachial Plexus Injuries\\n\\n Trunk Lesions\\n- Superior Trunk C5-C6 Erb-Duchenne Waiter s Tip \\n- Inferior Trunk C8-T1 Klumpke s Total Claw \\n\\n Peripheral Nerves\\n- Long Thoracic Winged Scapula C5-7 \\n- Axillary Deltoid paralysis Surgical neck \\n- Radial Wrist Drop Saturday Night Palsy \\n- Median \\n - High Hand of Benediction\\n - Low Ape Hand / Carpal Tunnel\\n- Ulnar \\n - High Cubital Tunnel\\n - Low Ulnar Claw Guyon s Canal anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l63", "title": "Lecture #63: Headache", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l63 Lecture 63 Headache lecturer Adam Reno D.O. Neurology session Lecture 63 prev l62 next l64 pdf pdfs/Lecture 63. A. Reno DO MPH PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L63 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L63 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 63 Headache\\n\\n Lecturer Adam Reno D.O.\\n Session Lecture 63\\n\\n Introduction and Assessment Slide 2-16 \\n\\nHeadaches are a massive burden on patients leading to lost work social isolation and suffering. The primary goal of assessment is to exclude urgent/emergent causes Secondary Headaches and accurately diagnose benign recurrent ones Primary Headaches . Diagnosis relies heavily on History \\u2014imaging is often unnecessary unless \\ Red Flags\\ are present.\\n\\n Primary vs. Secondary\\n- Primary Benign recurrent the headache is the disease Migraine Tension Cluster .\\n- Secondary Symptom of underlying pathology Tumor Bleed Infection Giant Cell Arteritis .\\n\\n Red Flags \\ SNOOP\\ Equivalents Slide 55-59 \\nThe lecturer emphasizes several \\ Red Flags\\ that demand immediate imaging/workup \\n- Sudden Onset \\ Thunderclap\\ or \\ Worst headache of my life\\ Subarachnoid Hemorrhage .\\n- New Onset > 50 Giant Cell Arteritis or Tumor.\\n- Systemic Signs Fever Weight Loss HIV Cancer history.\\n- Neurological Deficits Altered mental status focal signs.\\n- Positional Worse when standing Low pressure or laying/bending High pressure .\\n\\n Primary Headaches\\n\\n Migraine Headache Slide 23-35 \\nMigraine is a neuronal dysfunction not just vascular . It involves Cortical Spreading Depression and activation of the Trigeminal-Vascular system.\\n\\n Phases\\n1. Prodrome Irritability food cravings frequent urination hours/days before .\\n2. Aura 20-30% Transient neurological deficits Visual > Sensory > Motor . Lasts 5-60 mins.\\n3. Headache Unilateral throbbing nausea photophobia.\\n4. Postdrome \\ Migraine Hangover\\ tired washed out .\\n\\n correlate\\nMigraine Headache\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Cortical Spreading Depression + Trigeminal activation + CGRP release.\\n- Presentation \\n - Unilateral 60-70% \\n - Throbbing/Pulsating \\n - Aggravated by activity \\n - Nausea/Vomiting \\n - Photophobia/Phonophobia \\n- Diagnosis Clinical 5 attacks lasting 4-72h .\\n- Triggers Stress Estrogen Menses/OCPs Chocolate Cheese Sleep changes.\\n- Acute Treatment NSAIDs Triptans 5-HT agonists Antiemetics Metoclopramide .\\n- Prophylaxis Beta-blockers Propranolol Topiramate Amitriptyline CGRP antagonists.\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n Migraine Diagnostic Criteria Visual Mnemonic \\n \\ POUND\\ \\n- P ulsating\\n- O ne-day duration 4-72 hours \\n- U nilateral\\n- N ausea/Vomiting\\n- D isabling intensity\\n \\n\\n Tension-Type Headache Slide 37-41 \\nThe most common headache type. Often associated with stress anxiety and depression.\\n\\n correlate\\nTension-Type Headache\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Pericranial muscle tenderness/sensitization.\\n- Presentation \\n - Bilateral \\ Band-like\\ squeezing .\\n - Non-pulsating Pressing/Tightening .\\n - Mild-Moderate intensity.\\n - NO Nausea/Vomiting.\\n - NO aggravation by activity.\\n- Treatment \\n - Episodic NSAIDs Acetaminophen OMT.\\n - Chronic >15 days/mo Amitriptyline TCA Stress management.\\n \\n\\n Cluster Headache Slide 42 \\nA Trigeminal Autonomic Cephalalgia. Severe suicide-inducing pain.\\n\\n correlate\\nCluster Headache\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Hypothalamic activation.\\n- Presentation \\n - Male > Female 20-50s .\\n - Strictly Unilateral Peri-orbital \\ Ice pick in eye\\ .\\n - Autonomic Signs Ipsilateral Tearing Rhinorrhea Ptosis Miosis Conjunctival injection.\\n - Behavior Agitated pacing unlike Migraineurs who lie still .\\n - Timing Attacks occur in \\ clusters\\ daily for weeks often at night.\\n- Acute Treatment 100% Oxygen Non-rebreather Sumatriptan Subcutaneous .\\n- Prophylaxis Verapamil.\\n \\n\\n Secondary Headaches Slide 55-59 \\n\\nThese encompass life-threatening conditions requiring urgent intervention.\\n\\n Subarachnoid Hemorrhage SAH \\n- Cause Rupture of Saccular Berry Aneurysm Circle of Willis .\\n- Presentation \\ Thunderclap Headache\\ Maximum intensity in seconds \\ Worst headache of my life\\ .\\n- Workup Non-contrast CT Head first line . If negative but suspicion high -> Lumbar Puncture Xanthochromia .\\n\\n Meningitis / Encephalitis\\n- Triad Headache + Fever + Nuchal Rigidity Stiff Neck .\\n- Encephalitis Add Altered Mental Status and Seizures.\\n\\n Giant Cell Temporal Arteritis\\n- Pathophysiology Vasculitis of medium/large arteries branches of External Carotid .\\n- Demographics age > 50 Female > Male.\\n- Presentation Unilateral temporal headache Jaw Claudication Scalp tenderness.\\n- Complication Blindness Ophthalmic artery occlusion . This is a medical emergency.\\n- Workup/Tx Elevated ESR/CRP . Start High-Dose Steroids immediately before biopsy confirms .\\n\\n correlate\\nGiant Cell Arteritis Temporal Arteritis \\n\\n- Presentation Elderly patient >50 with new unilateral headache jaw pain when chewing and tender temporal artery.\\n- Risk Irreversible Blindness .\\n- Management Start Steroids Prednisone IMMEDIATELY . Do not wait for temporal artery biopsy.\\n \\n\\n Intracranial Mass Tumor \\n- Red Flag Headache that is worse in the morning due to hypoventilation/increased CO2/vasodilation during sleep or worse with Valsalva coughing bending .\\n\\n Post-Dural Puncture Headache Lumbar Puncture Headache \\n- Cause CSF leak after LP or Epidural e.g. C-section .\\n- Presentation Positional Headache Worse when standing/sitting RELIEVED by lying flat .\\n- Treatment Blood Patch Caffeine.\\n\\n highyield\\n Headache Comparison Table \\n\\n| Type | Location | Quality | Duration | Associated Sx |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Migraine | Unilateral | Throbbing | 4-72 hrs | Nausea Photo/Phonophobia Aura |\\n| Tension | Bilateral | Tight Band | 30m - 7d | Muscle tenderness No Nausea |\\n| Cluster | Unilateral Eye | Boring/Stabbing | 15-180 min | Tearing Rhinorrhea Ptosis Autonomic |\\n \\n\\n questions 1. A 30-year-old male presents with severe lancinating pain behind his right eye. He is pacing around the room and appears agitated. Exam reveals right-sided ptosis miosis and rhinorrhea. Treatment with 100% Oxygen provides relief. What is the diagnosis? Migraine with Aura Tension-Type Headache Cluster Headache Trigeminal Neuralgia Giant Cell Arteritis 2 Cluster Headache presents in males with severe unilateral periorbital pain and autonomic symptoms tearing rhinorrhea Horner s syndrome . Patients are agitated pacing . 100% Oxygen is the first-line abortive therapy. 2. A 72-year-old female presents with a new onset right-sided headache and pain in her jaw when chewing steak. She reports some blurring of vision in her right eye. What is the most appropriate immediate next step? Temporal Artery Biopsy Start High-Dose Corticosteroids Non-contrast CT Head Prescribe Sumatriptan Lumbar Puncture 1 This patient has symptoms of Giant Cell Temporal Arteritis Age >50 Headache Jaw Claudication Visual changes . The risk of permanent blindness is high. You must Start Corticosteroids immediately to prevent vision loss; do not delay treatment for the biopsy. 3. A 25-year-old female complains of a tight band squeezing her head bilaterally. The pain is mild-moderate and does not worsen with routine physical activity. She denies nausea or vomiting. What is the most likely diagnosis? Migraine without Aura Tension-Type Headache Cluster Headache Pseudotumor Cerebri Sinusitis 1 Tension-Type Headache is bilateral non-pulsating band-like and notably lacks the nausea/vomiting and aggravation by activity seen in migraines. 4. A 45-year-old male presents to the ER screaming This is the worst headache of my life! It started instantly like a thunderclap while he was lifting weights. He has no fever but has some nuchal rigidity. The most urgent diagnostic test is MRI Brain Non-contrast CT Head Lumbar Puncture EEG Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate ESR 1 Subarachnoid Hemorrhage presents as a Thunderclap headache. The initial test of choice is a Non-contrast CT Head to look for blood in the subarachnoid space. 5. A patient who recently underwent a C-section with epidural anesthesia complains of a severe headache that is relieved when lying flat but returns immediately upon standing. What is the mechanism? Corebral Venous Thrombosis CSF Leak Low Pressure Meningitis Preeclampsia Postpartum Migraine 1 Post-Dural Puncture Headache is caused by a persistent CSF leak lowering intracranial pressure. The hallmark is the postural nature worse standing better lying down . What are the POUND criteria for Migraine? Pulsating One-day 4-72h Unilateral Nausea/Vomiting Disabling. Diagnosis What are the autonomic features of Cluster Headache? Ptosis Miosis Lacrimation Rhinorrhea Conjunctival Injection all ipsilateral . Clinical What is the first-line ABORTIVE treatment for Cluster Headache? 100% Oxygen and Sumatriptan . Pharmacology How do you distinguish Tension Headache from Migraine? Tension is Bilateral Non-pulsating and has NO Nausea/Vomiting or Aura. Diagnosis What is the most feared complication of Giant Cell Arteritis? Permanent Blindness need immediate steroids . Clinical Describe the headache of a Brain Tumor. Worse in the morning awakening or with Valsalva coughing/bending . Clinical What is a Thunderclap headache suggestive of? Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Ruptured Aneurysm . Clinical What characteristic defines a Post-Dural Puncture Headache? Positional Worse standing Improved lying flat. Diagnosis Which population is most affected by Cluster Headaches? Males 20-50s often smokers. Epidemiology What is the classic presentation of Temporal Arteritis? Elderly >50 Unilateral Temporal Headache Jaw Claudication Elevated ESR. Clinical BandB Neurology > Headache FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Headache Pathoma N/A Migraine vs. Cluster Behavior A Migraine patient wants to lie still in a dark quiet room. A Cluster Headache patient is agitated restless and paces around the room. Treat First Biopsy Later If you suspect Giant Cell Arteritis start Steroids immediately to save vision. Do not wait for the temporal artery biopsy results. SNOOP for Red Flags Systemic Ssx Fever/Weight Loss Neurologic Ssx Onset Sudden/Thunderclap Older age >50 new onset Pattern change Positional/Valsalva . mindmap Headaches\\n\\n Primary Benign Recurrent \\n- Migraine Unilateral Pulsating Nausea Aura. Tx Triptans \\n- Tension Bilateral Band-like No Nausea. Tx NSAIDs \\n- Cluster Unilateral Eye Autonomic Ssx Restless. Tx 100% O2 \\n\\n Secondary Red Flags \\n- Vascular \\n - SAH Thunderclap \\n - Temporal Arteritis Jaw Claudication Blindness risk \\n- Infection Meningitis Fever Stiff Neck \\n- Structural \\n - Tumor Morning headache \\n - Low Pressure Post-LP Positional anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l64", "title": "Lecture #64: Introduction to Neuropharmacology", "module": "Neuropharmacology", "content": "l64 Lecture 64 Introduction to Neuropharmacology lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 64 prev l63 next l65 pdf pdfs/Lecture 64. L. Goldstein Pharm.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L64 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L64 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 64 Introduction to Neuropharmacology General Anesthetics \\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 64\\n\\n Inhaled Anesthetics Pharmacokinetics\\n\\nThe goal of inhaled anesthesia is to achieve equilibrium between the partial pressure of the gas in the alveoli FA and the brain FCNS . The speed of induction depends on the rate at which FA approaches the inspired concentration FI .\\n\\n Key Factors Affecting Uptake Slide 10 28-36 \\n\\n1. Solubility Blood Gas Partition Coefficient \\n - Low Solubility e.g. Nitrous Oxide Desflurane The gas does not want to dissolve in blood. It quickly saturates the blood and raises partial pressure -> Rapid Induction and Rapid Recovery .\\n - High Solubility e.g. Halothane Isoflurane The gas dissolves readily in blood like sugar in tea . It takes a lot of gas to saturate the blood before partial pressure rises -> Slow Induction and Slow Recovery .\\n\\n2. Cardiac Output \\n - Increased CO Removes gas from alveoli faster -> Slows the rise of FA -> Slower Induction . Counter-intuitive but high yield .\\n - Decreased CO Speeds induction especially for soluble agents .\\n\\n3. Alveolar Ventilation Hyperventilation speeds induction.\\n\\n highyield\\n Solubility & Induction Speed \\n- Low Blood Gas Coefficient N2O Desflurane = FAST Onset.\\n- High Blood Gas Coefficient Halothane = SLOW Onset.\\n \\n\\n MAC Minimum Alveolar Concentration Slide 52 \\n- Definition The alveolar concentration that prevents movement in 50% of patients in response to surgical incision Standardized Stimulus .\\n- Potency MAC is inversely related to potency. Low MAC = High Potency .\\n - Halothane MAC 0.75% Very Potent.\\n - Nitrous Oxide MAC >100% Weak anesthetic cannot achieve surgical anesthesia alone .\\n- Additivity MAC values are additive 0.5 MAC N2O + 0.5 MAC Isoflurane = 1 MAC .\\n\\n Inhaled Anesthetics Agents & Toxicity\\n\\n Nitrous Oxide N2O \\n- Properties Gas at room temp. Low solubility Fast onset . Weak potency. Good Analgesia.\\n- Side Effects \\n - Diffusion Hypoxia On discontinuation N2O rushes into alveoli displacing O2. Prevention Administer 100% O2 for minutes after stopping N2O.\\n - Expansion of Air-Filled Spaces Contraindicated in Pneumothorax Bowel Obstruction Middle Ear surgery.\\n - Vitamin B12 Inhibition Inhibits Methionine Synthase -> Megaloblastic Anemia Neuropathy.\\n\\n Halogenated Agents Isoflurane Desflurane Sevoflurane Halothane \\n- Mechanism GABA-A agonism NMDA antagonism.\\n- Organ Effects \\n - CV Vasodilation -> Hypotension. Desflurane causes transient Tachycardia Sympathetic stim .\\n - Resp Respiratory Depression Bronchodilation Sevoflurane is best for asthmatics . Desflurane is Pungent airway irritation .\\n - CNS Increased Cerebral Blood Flow -> Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP .\\n- Toxicities \\n - Malignant Hyperthermia Genetic disorder Ryanodine receptor . Triggered by Halogenated agents + Succinylcholine. Tx Dantrolene .\\n - Halothane Hepatitis Immune-mediated liver necrosis Rare .\\n - Nephrotoxicity Sevoflurane -> \\ Compound A\\ but rare in humans .\\n\\n correlate\\nMalignant Hyperthermia\\n\\n- Trigger Volatile Anesthetics Isoflurane Sevoflurane etc. + Succinylcholine.\\n- Pathophysiology Uncontrolled Ca2+ release from Sarcoplasmic Reticulum.\\n- Signs Muscle Rigidity Masseter spasm Hyperthermia Hypercapnia High CO2 Tachycardia Acidosis.\\n- Treatment Stop agent Give Dantrolene .\\n \\n\\n Intravenous Anesthetics Slide 62-70 \\n\\nUsed for Induction Rapid onset due to high lipophilicity . Action is terminated by Redistribution Blood -> Brain -> Muscle/Fat NOT metabolism.\\n\\n Propofol\\n- Mechanism GABA-A agonist.\\n- Clinical Use Induction Maintenance TIVA ICU Sedation. \\ Milk of Amnesia\\ .\\n- Pros Anti-emetic prevents nausea Rapid recovery.\\n- Cons Hypotension Vasodilation Respiratory Depression Pain on Injection described as burning.\\n- Notes Formulated in egg/soybean emulsion bacterial growth risk allergy .\\n\\n Etomidate\\n- Mechanism GABA-A agonist.\\n- Clinical Use Induction in patients with Cardiovascular Instability .\\n- Pros Hemodynamic Stability Minimal change in BP/HR .\\n- Cons Adrenocortical Suppression inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase Nausea/Vomiting.\\n\\n Ketamine\\n- Mechanism NMDA Receptor Antagonist .\\n- Clinical Use Dissociative Anesthesia eyes open but unresponsive Trauma/Shock.\\n- Pros Sympathomimetic Increases BP HR Cardiac Output Bronchodilation Good for Asthmatics .\\n- Cons Emergence Delirium Hallucinations Nightmares .\\n\\n Dexmedetomidine\\n- Mechanism Alpha-2 Agonist central .\\n- Clinical Use ICU Sedation Adjunct.\\n- Effects Sedation usually without respiratory depression. Causes Bradycardia/Hypotension.\\n\\n highyield\\n IV Anesthetic Choice High Yields \\n- Patient with Hypotension/Trauma Use Ketamine Boosts BP or Etomidate Stable BP . Avoid Propofol.\\n- Patient with Asthma Use Ketamine or Sevoflurane Bronchodilators . Avoid Desflurane Pungent .\\n- Patient with Head Injury High ICP Avoid Ketamine increases ICP and Volatile agents increase ICP . Propofol/Barbiturates are preferred decrease metabolic rate .\\n- Post-Op Nausea Use Propofol Anti-emetic . Avoid N2O and Opioids.\\n \\n questions 1. An anesthesiologist wishes to speed up the induction of anesthesia with a highly soluble volatile agent. Which maneuver would be most effective? Decreasing Alveolar Ventilation Increasing Cardiac Output Increasing Alveolar Ventilation Using an agent with a higher Blood Gas partition coefficient Administering a beta-blocker 2 Increasing Alveolar Ventilation delivers more gas to the alveoli speeding the rise of partial pressure FA and thus speeding induction. Conversely increasing Cardiac Output slows induction by removing gas from the alveoli too quickly. 2. A trauma patient with severe hypotension requires emergency intubation. Which induction agent is preferred due to its ability to increase blood pressure and heart rate? Propofol Etomidate Ketamine Thiopental Sevoflurane 2 Ketamine is a sympathomimetic that increases BP HR and cardiac output making it ideal for hemodynamically unstable patients shock . Propofol causes hypotension. 3. Following a surgery using Nitrous Oxide the patient is extubated and allowed to breathe room air. Minutes later she becomes hypoxic. What is the cause? Malignant Hyperthermia Diffusion Hypoxia Laryngospasm Pneumothorax expansion Opioid overdose 1 Diffusion Hypoxia occurs when N2O is stopped abruptly; the gas rushes from the blood into the alveoli displacing oxygen. It is prevented by administering 100% O2 for several minutes post-anesthesia. 4. Which anesthetic agent is associated with adrenocortical suppression via inhibition of 11-beta-hydroxylase? Ketamine Propofol Midazolam Etomidate Dexmedetomidine 3 Etomidate inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase preventing cortisol synthesis. It is generally safe for single-dose induction but typically avoided for long-term infusions in septic patients. 5. Comparing two inhaled anesthetics Drug A has a Blood Gas partition coefficient of 0.42. Drug B has a coefficient of 2.30. Which statement is true? Drug A has a slower onset of action than Drug B Drug B has a faster recovery time than Drug A Drug A is more soluble in blood than Drug B Drug A has a faster onset of action than Drug B Drug B is less potent than Drug A 3 Typically Low Solubility Low Blood Gas coefficient correlates with Fast Onset . Drug A 0.42 is less soluble than Drug B 2.30 so Drug A will have a faster onset . What does a LOW Blood Gas partition coefficient indicate about induction speed? Fast Induction and Fast Recovery . Pharmacokinetics What is the measure of Potency for inhaled anesthetics? MAC Minimum Alveolar Concentration . Low MAC = High Potency. Pharmacokinetics Which induction agent causes Adrenocortical Suppression? Etomidate. Pharmacology Which induction agent is Dissociative and increases Cardiac Output? Ketamine. Pharmacology What terminates the action of a single bolus of Propofol? Redistribution not metabolism . Pharmacokinetics What is the trigger for Malignant Hyperthermia? Halogenated Anesthetics + Succinylcholine. Pathology What is the antidote for Malignant Hyperthermia? Dantrolene. Pharmacology Why is Nitrous Oxide contraindicated in Pneumothorax? It expands air-filled spaces. Safety Which volatile anesthetic is associated with Nephrotoxicity Compound A ? Sevoflurane. Pharmacology Which IV anesthetic is known as an Anti-emetic? Propofol. Pharmacology Physeo Pharmacology > Neuro > General Anesthetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Anesthetics Solubility and Speed Think of Solubility like a sink . If the blood sink is huge high solubility it takes forever to fill up rise in partial pressure before it spills over into the brain. Low solubility = tiny sink = fills fast = fast anesthesia. Ketamine & Asthma Ketamine is a bronchodilator. It s an excellent choice for induction in an asthmatic patient who is actively wheezing. Propofol Burn Propofol hurts on injection vascular irritation . Lidocaine is often mixed with it to blunt the pain. mindmap General Anesthetics\\n\\n Inhaled\\n- Pharmacokinetics \\n - Low Solubility -> Fast Onset Desflurane N2O \\n - High Solubility -> Slow Onset Halothane Isoflurane \\n - Increased CO -> Slower Onset\\n- Agents \\n - N2O Weak Fast Diffusion Hypoxia.\\n - Halogenated Potent MH risk Hypotension.\\n - Sevoflurane Bronchodilation.\\n - Desflurane Pungent Tachycardia.\\n\\n Intravenous\\n- Propofol Anti-emetic Hypotension Standard induction.\\n- Etomidate Cardiac Stability Adrenal suppression.\\n- Ketamine Sympathomimetic High BP Dissociative Bronchodilator.\\n- Dexmedetomidine Alpha-2 agonist Sedation w/o Resp Depression .\\n- Thiopental Barbiturate High ICP control . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l65", "title": "Lecture #65: Pharmacology of General Anesthetics Part 2", "module": "Neuropharmacology", "content": "l65 Lecture 65 Pharmacology of General Anesthetics Part 2 lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 65 prev l64 next l66 pdf pdfs/Lecture 65. L. Goldstein Pharm.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L65 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L65 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 65 Pharmacology of General Anesthetics Part 2\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 65\\n\\n---\\n\\n Effect of Inhaled Anesthetics on Organ Systems \\n\\n Cardiovascular Effects \\nMost inhaled anesthetics cause a dose-dependent decrease in blood pressure but the mechanisms differ.\\n- Isoflurane Desflurane Sevoflurane Primarily decrease blood pressure by decreasing systemic vascular resistance vasodilation while preserving cardiac output.\\n - Note This preservation of cardiac output makes them preferable for patients with impaired myocardial function compared to halothane.\\n- Halothane & Enflurane Decrease blood pressure primarily by depressing myocardial contractility leading to decreased cardiac output.\\n- Nitrous Oxide N2O Has negligible cardiovascular effects overall because its mild sympathetic stimulation offsets any direct myocardial depression.\\n- Baroreflex Generally depressed by volatile anesthetics.\\n - Desflurane Exception Causes transient sympathetic stimulation tachycardia hypertension when the concentration is increased rapidly.\\n - correlate\\n Coronary Artery Disease CAD \\n Limit the use of Desflurane or increase concentration slowly in patients with CAD as the resulting tachycardia and hypertension can increase myocardial oxygen demand and induce ischemia or infarction .\\n \\n\\n Respiratory Effects \\nVolatile anesthetics are respiratory depressants.\\n- Ventilation Decrease tidal volume and increase respiratory rate rapid shallow breathing . The overall effect is a decrease in minute ventilation.\\n- CO2 Response They blunt the ventilatory response to hypercapnia increased PaCO2 .\\n- Airway Irritation Desflurane and Isoflurane are pungent and can cause coughing breath-holding or laryngospasm poor for mask induction .\\n- Bronchodilation All volatiles cause bronchodilation. Sevoflurane is non-pungent and a potent bronchodilator making it the drug of choice for inhalational induction especially in patients with reactive airways e.g. asthma .\\n\\n Cerebral Effects \\n- Uncoupling Volatile anesthetics increase cerebral blood flow CBF vasodilation and intracranial pressure ICP while simultaneously decreasing cerebral metabolic rate of oxygen CMRO2 .\\n - Note Nitrous oxide increases both CBF and CMRO2.\\n - Note Sevoflurane increases ICP less than others.\\n- correlate\\n Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP \\n Inhaled anesthetics should be used with caution in patients with space-occupying lesions tumors or head trauma as the increase in CBF can dangerously elevate intracranial pressure . Hyperventilation to lower PaCO2 can help counteract this vasodilation.\\n \\n\\n Neuromuscular & Renal/Hepatic \\n- Muscle Volatiles relax skeletal muscle and potentiate the effects of neuromuscular blockers. N2O does not .\\n- Kidney/Liver Decrease renal and hepatic blood flow dose-dependent .\\n- Uterus Volatiles relax uterine smooth muscle can lead to uterine atony/hemorrhage . N2O does not.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Toxicities of Inhaled Anesthetics \\n\\n Malignant Hyperthermia MH \\nA life-threatening hypermetabolic state triggered by all halogenated volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine in genetically susceptible individuals Ryanodine receptor mutations .\\n- Pathophysiology Uncontrolled calcium release causing sustained muscle contraction.\\n- Signs Hypercapnia early sign tachycardia muscle rigidity hyperthermia late sign metabolic acidosis hyperkalemia.\\n- Treatment Stop agent hyperventilate give Dantrolene .\\n- Important Nitrous Oxide does NOT trigger MH. \\n\\n Hepatotoxicity \\n- Halothane Hepatitis A specific immune-mediated necrosis caused by the formation of trifluoroacetyl chloride from CYP2E1 metabolism which haptenates liver proteins.\\n - Risk factors Female obesity prior exposure.\\n - Note Desflurane Isoflurane and Sevoflurane have minimal metabolism and very low risk.\\n\\n Nephrotoxicity \\n- Compound A Sevoflurane interacts with soda lime CO2 absorbers to form Compound A which is nephrotoxic in rats. No proven renal injury in humans but valid theoretical concern use adequate fresh gas flow .\\n\\n Carbon Monoxide CO Production \\n Desflurane most > Isoflurane > Sevoflurane can produce CO when passed through desiccated dry CO2 absorbers .\\n- Prevent by replacing absorbents regularly.\\n\\n Nitrous Oxide N2O Specific Toxicities \\n1. Expansion of Air-Filled Spaces N2O is 35x more soluble than N2. It diffuses into air cavities faster than N2 leaves increasing pressure/volume.\\n - correlate\\n N2O Contraindications \\n Avoid N2O in patients with pneumothorax bowel obstruction air embolism or during middle ear surgery as expansion of these spaces can cause catastrophic rupture or pressure damage.\\n \\n2. Diffusion Hypoxia Upon discontinuation N2O rushes from blood into alveoli diluting O2. Reversed by giving 100% O2 for several minutes after stopping N2O.\\n3. Vitamin B12 Inhibition N2O oxidizes the cobalt in B12 inhibiting methionine synthase .\\n - correlate\\n Megaloblastic Anemia & Neuropathy \\n Prolonged exposure or use in patients with preexisting deficiency can lead to megaloblastic anemia and subacute combined degeneration of the cord peripheral neuropathy . Avoid in patients with B12 deficiency/malnutrition.\\n \\n4. Second Gas Effect High uptake of N2O concentrates the remaining gases in the alveoli speeding up induction of co-administered agents.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Intravenous Anesthetics \\n\\n Propofol \\nThe most common induction agent.\\n- Mechanism Potentiates chloride current through the GABA-A receptor.\\n- Effects Rapid induction and recovery \\ clear headed\\ . Antiemetic Anti-pruritic Bronchodilation . Decreases CBF ICP and CMRO2.\\n- Adverse Effects \\n - Pain on injection mitigate with lidocaine .\\n - Hypotension Causes profound vasodilation and myocardial depression.\\n - Respiratory Depression Significant apnea.\\n- correlate\\n Propofol Infusion Syndrome PRIS \\n Rare but fatal reaction to high-dose/long-term infusion. Signs include metabolic acidosis rhabdomyolysis hyperkalemia hepatomegaly and cardiac failure.\\n \\n- Formulation Egg lecithin/soybean oil emulsion risk of bacterial growth careful in egg/soy allergy—though most reactions are to egg white protein not lecithin .\\n\\n Etomidate \\n- Mechanism GABA-A agonist.\\n- Key Feature Hemodynamic Stability . Minimal change in HR BP or CO.\\n- Indication Induction agent of choice for patients with cardiovascular instability or shock .\\n- Adverse Effects \\n - Adrenal Suppression Inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase preventing cortisol synthesis. Avoid in septic shock patients who need their adrenal reserve.\\n - Myoclonus Involuntary muscle movements.\\n - PONV High incidence of postoperative nausea and vomiting.\\n\\n Ketamine \\n- Mechanism NMDA receptor antagonist blocks glutamate .\\n- Effects Dissociative anesthesia catatonia amnesia analgesia .\\n - Cardiovascular Causes Sympathetic Stimulation increase HR BP CO via catecholamine release. Good for hypovolemic shock.\\n - Respiratory Maintains airway reflexes and respiratory drive. Potent bronchodilator good for severe asthma induction .\\n - Cerebral Increases CMRO2 CBF and ICP.\\n- Adverse Effects \\n - Emergence Delirium Hallucinations vivid dreams disorientation. Prevent with benzodiazepines.\\n - correlate\\n Contraindications for Ketamine \\n Avoid in patients with severe hypertension angina or active ischemia due to increased myocardial oxygen demand. Also historically avoided in head trauma due to ICP though this is debated.\\n \\n\\n Dexmedetomidine Precedex \\n- Mechanism Highly selective Alpha-2 adrenergic agonist central action .\\n- Effects Sedation mimics natural sleep easily rousable Anxiolysis Analgesia . NO respiratory depression.\\n- Adverse Effects Bradycardia and Hypotension .\\n\\n Benzodiazepines Midazolam \\n- Mechanism GABA-A agonist.\\n- Use Preoperative anxiolysis amnesia anterograde .\\n- Reversal Flumazenil .\\n\\n---\\n\\n Adjuncts \\n- Opioids Fentanyl etc. Provide analgesia. Reversal Naloxone .\\n- Lidocaine IV bolus blunts airway reflexes coughing during intubation.\\n\\n highyield\\n Quick Drug Selection Guide \\n Asthma/Bronchospasm Sevoflurane inhaled Ketamine IV Propofol IV .\\n Hemodynamic Instability/Shock Etomidate IV Ketamine IV .\\n Head Injury High ICP Propofol Barbiturates Etomidate . Avoid Ketamine Volatiles unless hyperventilated .\\n Reactive Airway/Smoking Sevoflurane. Avoid Desflurane.\\n History of PONV Propofol TIVA . Avoid Etomidate N2O Opioids.\\n Bowel Obstruction/Pneumothorax Avoid Nitrous Oxide.\\n CAD/Ischemia Avoid Ketamine Desflurane tachycardia .\\n \\n questions 1. A 55-year-old male with a history of severe coronary artery disease presents for coronary artery bypass grafting. During induction the anesthesiologist chooses an agent known for maintaining hemodynamic stability but causing adrenal suppression. Which drug was likely used? Propofol Ketamine Etomidate Midazolam Sevoflurane 2 Etomidate is known for its excellent hemodynamic stability minimal effect on HR/BP but has the unique side effect of inhibiting 11-beta-hydroxylase leading to adrenal suppression. 2. A 24-year-old male undergoes surgery for a bowel obstruction. Upon induction with a gas anesthetic the bowel rapidly distends complicating the surgery. Which agent is the most likely cause? Sevoflurane Isoflurane Desflurane Nitrous Oxide Propofol 3 Nitrous Oxide N2O is much more soluble than nitrogen. It diffuses into air-filled spaces like an obstructed bowel faster than nitrogen can leave causing rapid expansion of the space. It is contraindicated in bowel obstruction pneumothorax and middle ear surgery. 3. A child undergoing a brief procedure with sevoflurane anesthesia develops rapid breathing tachycardia muscle rigidity and a rapidly rising body temperature. What is the mechanism of the antidote for this condition? Blockade of NMDA receptors Inhibition of calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum Activation of GABA-A receptors Competitive antagonism of acetylcholine receptors Agonism of alpha-2 adrenergic receptors 1 The presentation is Malignant Hyperthermia . The antidote is Dantrolene which works by inhibiting calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum via ryanodine receptors stopping the uncontrolled contraction. 4. A trauma patient with significant blood loss and hypotension requires emergency intubation. Which induction agent would best support this patient s blood pressure via sympathetic stimulation? Propofol Etomidate Ketamine Thiopental Midazolam 2 Ketamine stimulates the sympathetic nervous system increasing HR and BP making it an excellent induction agent for patients in hypovolemic shock who need hemodynamic support. Propofol causes hypotension. 5. A patient with a known deficiency of Vitamin B12 undergoes a lengthy procedure using a specific inhaled anesthetic. Postoperatively they develop signs of megaloblastic anemia and neuropathy. This effect is due to inhibition of which enzyme? 11-beta-hydroxylase Methionine synthase Ala-synthase Monoamine oxidase Pseudocholinesterase 1 Nitrous Oxide irreversibly oxidizes the cobalt atom of Vitamin B12 which inhibits Methionine Synthase . This can lead to megaloblastic anemia and neurologic sequelae subacute combined degeneration especially in B12-deficient patients. Which IV anesthetic is known for its hemodynamic stability and minimal respiratory depression? Etomidate Clinical What is the primary mechanism of action of Ketamine? NMDA receptor antagonist Pharmacology Which inhaled anesthetic should be avoided in patients with pneumothorax or bowel obstruction? Nitrous Oxide N2O Contraindications What drug is the specific treatment for Malignant Hyperthermia? Dantrolene Antidote Which IV anesthetic causes adrenal suppression by inhibiting 11-beta-hydroxylase? Etomidate Adverse Effects What is a unique adverse effect of Ketamine during recovery? Emergence delirium hallucinations vivid dreams Adverse Effects Which inhaled anesthetic causes the most airway irritation and coughing? Desflurane Isoflurane is also pungent Adverse Effects Which IV anesthetic has antiemetic properties? Propofol Clinical What potentially nephrotoxic substance is formed when Sevoflurane degrades in soda lime? Compound A Toxicology Which induction agent increases intracranial pressure and cerebral blood flow? Ketamine Physiology BandB Neurology > Pharmacology > Anesthetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Anesthetics Desflurane & CAD Desflurane can cause transient sympathetic stimulation tachycardia/HTN making it risky for patients with CAD. Sevoflurane Induction Sevoflurane is the best agent for inhalational induction due to its lack of pungency and potent bronchodilation. N2O in Pneumothorax Nitrous Oxide is contraindicated in pneumothorax and bowel obstruction because it diffuses into air-filled spaces 35x faster than nitrogen leaves causing expansion. Etomidate & Shock Etomidate is the drug of choice for induction in hemodynamically unstable patients but causes adrenal suppression. Ketamine & ICP Ketamine is a dissociative anesthetic that increases sympathetic tone good for shock but increases ICP bad for head trauma . Propofol & PONV Propofol has antiemetic properties making it ideal for TIVA to reduce PONV. MH Trigger Malignant Hyperthermia is triggered by halogenated volatiles and succinylcholine; treat with Dantrolene. mindmap \\n- Inhaled Anesthetics \\n - Mechanism Unknown globally but likely GABA potentiators / NMDA inhibitors.\\n - PK MAC potency Solubility speed .\\n - Organ Effects \\n - CV Vasodilation Iso/Des/Sevo Myocardial Depression Halothane .\\n - Resp Depression Bronchodilation Sevo ++ .\\n - Brain Increased CBF/ICP Decreased CMRO2.\\n - Toxicities \\n - Malignant Hyperthermia All volatiles .\\n - Halothane Hepatitis Immune .\\n - Nephrotoxicity Sevo -> Cmpd A .\\n - N2O Specifics Expansion of air spaces B12 inhibition Diffusion Hypoxia.\\n- Intravenous Anesthetics \\n - Propofol GABA Standard induction. Hypotension/Apnea. Antiemetic.\\n - Etomidate GABA CV Stability. Adrenal suppression. Myoclonus.\\n - Ketamine NMDA Dissociative. Sympathetic boost Shock . Hallucinations.\\n - Dexmedetomidine Alpha-2 Sedation without resp depression.\\n anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l66", "title": "Lecture #66: Pharmacology: Local Anesthetics", "module": "Neuropharmacology", "content": "l66 Lecture 66 Pharmacology Local Anesthetics lecturer L. Goldstein Neuropharmacology session Lecture 66 prev l65 next l67 pdf pdfs/Lecture 66. L. Goldstein.pdf highYieldPdf content/L66 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L66 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 66 Pharmacology Local Anesthetics\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein\\n Session Lecture 66\\n\\n---\\n\\n Introduction & Mechanism of Action\\n\\nLocal anesthetics LAs are diverse agents used to block sensation in a specific area of the body without loss of consciousness. Their primary utility lies in preventing pain transmission during surgical or dental procedures. To understand how they work we must first appreciate the physiology of the voltage-gated sodium channel. Nerves transmit signals via action potentials which are propagated by the rapid influx of sodium ions through these channels.\\n\\nLocal anesthetics function by diffusing through the nerve membrane into the axoplasm where they bind to the intracellular portion of voltage-gated sodium channels. Crucially they bind preferentially to channels in the open and inactivated states—a phenomenon known as state-dependent block . By stabilizing the inactivated state they prevent the channel from resetting to the resting state thereby halting the propagation of action potentials. This blockade is also use-dependent meaning nerves that are firing rapidly such as pain fibers are blocked more effectively than resting nerves.\\n\\n highyield\\n Mechanism of Action \\n- Target Intracellular portion of Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels .\\n- Binding State High affinity for Open and Inactivated states State-Dependent .\\n- Effect Stabilizes the inactivated state $\\\\\\\\rightarrow$ prevents Na+ influx $\\\\\\\\rightarrow$ blocks action potential propagation.\\n- Use-Dependence Higher firing frequency = Stronger block Pain fibers > Motor fibers .\\n \\n\\n Susceptibility to Blockade\\nNot all nerve fibers are blocked equally. The sensitivity of a nerve fiber to LA blockade is determined by its diameter myelination and firing frequency. In general smaller diameter and higher firing frequency increase sensitivity.\\n\\n- Order of Blockade \\n 1. Small myelinated Sympathetic fibers - Blocked First \\n 2. Small unmyelinated Pain/Temp - C fibers & Small myelinated Pain/Temp - A$\\\\\\\\delta$ fibers \\n 3. Large myelinated Touch Pressure Motor - Blocked Last \\n\\n mnemonic\\n Fiber Blockade Order \\n \\ Silly Puns Make Me Laugh\\ \\n- S iopathetic Sympathetic \\n- P ain Pain/Temp \\n- T ouch Touch/Pressure \\n- M otor Motor \\n \\n\\n Structure & Pharmacokinetics\\n\\nLocal anesthetics consist of a lipophilic aromatic ring connected to a hydrophilic amine by an intermediate chain. This intermediate chain defines the two major classes Esters and Amides . This distinction is critical for determining metabolism and allergic potential.\\n\\n Esters vs. Amides\\n- Esters Metabolized by plasma pseudocholinesterase to PABA para-aminobenzoic acid . PABA is a common allergen making hypersensitivity reactions more common in this class.\\n- Amides Metabolized in the liver by CYP450 enzymes. Hypersensitivity is extremely rare.\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Amides have Two I s \\n- Esters One i Procaine Cocaine Tetracaine Benzocaine\\n- Amides Two i s L i doca i ne Mep i vaca i ne Bup i vaca i ne Rop i vaca i ne Pr i loca i ne\\n \\n\\n pH and pKa The Onset of Action\\nLocal anesthetics are weak bases pKa 7.5–9.0 . They exist in equilibrium between a non-ionized lipid-soluble form and an ionized water-soluble charged form.\\n- Non-Ionized Form Crosses the nerve membrane Lipid soluble .\\n- Ionized Form Binds to the receptor inside the channel Active form .\\n\\n The Infection Concept \\nInfected tissue is acidic low pH . In an acidic environment the equilibrium shifts towards the ionized form $LA + H^+ \\\\\\\\rightarrow LAH^+$ . Since the ionized form cannot cross the membrane the drug cannot reach its target site. Therefore local anesthetics are less effective in infected/acidic tissue.\\n\\n Potency Duration and Absorption\\n- Potency Defined by Lipid Solubility . Highly lipophilic drugs e.g. Bupivacaine penetrate membranes easily $\\\\\\\\rightarrow$ High Potency.\\n- Duration Defined by Protein Binding and Vascularity .\\n - High Protein Binding = Long Duration Drug stays at receptor .\\n - High Vascularity = Short Duration Drug washed away .\\n- Vasoconstrictors Epinephrine is often added to LAs to cause vasoconstriction. This decreases systemic absorption reduces toxicity and prolongs the duration of action by keeping the drug at the site.\\n\\n correlate\\n Vasoconstrictor Contraindication \\n Epinephrine should NOT be used in tissues with end-arterial supply Fingers Toes Penis Nose due to risk of ischemia and necrosis.\\n Obstetrics Epinephrine is avoided in labor epidurals as vasoconstriction can compromise uteroplacental blood flow and cause maternal hypertension.\\n \\n\\n Clinical Pharmacology of Agents\\n\\n Ester Local Anesthetics\\n1. Procaine Prototype short duration. Metabolized to PABA. Rarely used now.\\n2. Chloroprocaine Rapid onset short duration. Used in epidurals for C-sections.\\n3. Tetracaine Long duration high potency. Used for spinal anesthesia and ophthalmology. Note CSF has little pseudocholinesterase prolonged action Slide 36 .\\n4. Benzocaine Topical only low pKa very lipophilic . Used for mucous membranes e.g. teething endoscopy .\\n5. Cocaine Only LA with intrinsic vasoconstrictive properties blocks NE reuptake . Used Topically for ENT procedures nose/throat to shrink mucosa and reduce bleeding.\\n\\n correlate\\n Methemoglobinemia Benzocaine \\n Benzocaine spray/gel can cause oxidation of iron in hemoglobin $Fe^ 2+ \\\\\\\\rightarrow Fe^ 3+ $ leading to Methemoglobinemia .\\n- Presentation \\ Chocolate brown\\ blood cyanosis shortness of breath.\\n- Treatment Methylene Blue.\\n \\n\\n Amide Local Anesthetics\\n1. Lidocaine The \\ Standard\\ . Rapid onset medium duration. Broad utility local spinal epidural IV antiarrhythmic .\\n2. Mepivacaine Similar to Lidocaine but less vasodilation. Used in dentistry.\\n3. Bupivacaine High potency long duration. Preferred for epidurals post-op pain. High Cardiotoxicity. \\n4. Ropivacaine Similar to Bupivacaine but less cardiotoxic and less motor block good for labor—\\ walking epidural\\ .\\n5. Prilocaine Medium duration. Risk of methemoglobinemia.\\n\\n Toxicity Systemic & Neural \\n\\nLocal Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity LAST occurs when drug levels rise excessively in the blood usually due to accidental IV injection or excessive dose. The toxicity targets the CNS and Cardiovascular systems.\\n\\n Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity LAST \\n- Phase 1 CNS Excitation Perioral numbness metallic taste tinnitus agitation seizures.\\n- Phase 2 CNS Depression Coma respiratory arrest.\\n- Phase 3 CV Collapse Hypotension arrhythmias cardiac arrest.\\n\\n highyield\\n LAST progression \\nNumbness/Tinnitus $\\\\\\\\rightarrow$ Seizures $\\\\\\\\rightarrow$ Coma $\\\\\\\\rightarrow$ Cardiac Arrest.\\n Key Concept CNS signs usually precede CV signs Warning sign! EXCEPT with Bupivacaine where they can happen simultaneously.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Bupivacaine Cardiotoxicity \\n Bupivacaine has high affinity for cardiac Na+ channels and dissociates slowly \\ Fast In Slow Out\\ .\\n- Risk Severe refractory arrhythmias and cardiovascular collapse.\\n- Avoid Do not use for IV regional anesthesia Bier Block .\\n- Rescue Intralipid Emulsion Lipid Rescue acts as a lipid sink to soak up the lipophilic drug.\\n \\n\\n Specific Neural Toxicities\\n1. Transient Neurologic Symptoms TNS \\n - Associated with Lidocaine Spinal .\\n - Presentation Pain/dysesthesia in buttocks/legs resolving in days.\\n - Prognosis Full recovery no permanent damage.\\n2. Cauda Equina Syndrome \\n - Associated with Lidocaine Spinal Continuous Microcatheters - historical .\\n - Presentation Bowel/bladder dysfunction saddle anesthesia paralysis.\\n - Prognosis Potential for permanent damage.\\n\\n Hematologic Toxicity\\n- Methemoglobinemia Caused by Prilocaine metabolite o-toluidine and Benzocaine .\\n\\n Allergies\\n- Esters High risk due to PABA . Cross-reactivity exists among esters.\\n- Amides Very rare. No cross-reactivity with esters. If allergic to one amide may try another preservative-free .\\n questions 1. A 25-year-old woman in labor requests an epidural. The anesthesiologist selects an agent that provides adequate sensory analgesia but minimizes motor blockade to allow her to participate in labor. Which agent was most likely chosen? Tetracaine Ropivacaine Procaine Cocaine Benzocaine 1 Ropivacaine is a long-acting amide local anesthetic similar to bupivacaine but with less motor blockade at analgesic doses. 2. A patient undergoing a dental procedure receives a potentially toxic dose of a local anesthetic. Which early sign would most likely alert the dentist to impending systemic toxicity? Cardiovascular collapse Respiratory depression Perioral numbness and metallic taste Profound hypotension Bradycardia 2 CNS toxicity typically precedes CV toxicity. Early symptoms include circumoral numbness tinnitus and metallic taste. 3. Which local anesthetic is an ester that is metabolized to a product known to cause hypersensitivity reactions? Lidocaine Bupivacaine Mepivacaine Tetracaine Ropivacaine 3 Tetracaine is an ester one i . Esters are metabolized to PABA which is immunogenic. 4. A child is brought to the ER with cyanosis and shortness of breath after his parents applied a generous amount of a teething gel. Blood appears chocolate-brown. Which agent is implicated? Lidocaine Benzocaine Bupivacaine Ropivacaine Procaine 1 Benzocaine can cause Methemoglobinemia oxidizes Fe2+ to Fe3+ presenting with cyanosis and chocolate-brown blood. 5. Why is epinephrine often co-administered with lidocaine for infiltration anesthesia? To increase the pH of the solution To preventing oxidation of lidocaine To reduce the pain of injection To prolong the duration of action via vasoconstriction To block potassium channels 3 Epinephrine is a vasoconstrictor; it keeps the drug at the site longer prolonging duration and reducing systemic absorption. Which class of local anesthetics has a higher risk of allergic reactions? Esters due to PABA metabolite Concept What is the mechanism of action of local anesthetics? Block intracellular voltage-gated Na+ channels Open/Inactivated states Concept Which nerve fibers are blocked first? Small myelinated fibers Sympathetic Concept What is the treatment for Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity LAST ? IV Lipid Emulsion Intralipid Clinical Which local anesthetic causes methemoglobinemia? Prilocaine and Benzocaine Clinical Which local anesthetic is the most cardiotoxic? Bupivacaine Clinical Why is epinephrine added to local anesthetics? To cause vasoconstriction prolong duration and decrease systemic absorption Concept What is the primary metabolism of Amide anesthetics? Hepatic CYP450 Concept What is the primary metabolism of Ester anesthetics? Plasma pseudocholinesterase Concept Why are local anesthetics less effective in infected tissue? Acidic pH causes ionization; ionized drug cannot cross membrane Concept What is a specific side effect of spinal Lidocaine? Transient Neurologic Symptoms TNS Clinical Which ion channel state do LAs bind to most strongly? Inactivated state and Open Concept BandB Neurology > Pharmacology > Local Anesthetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Anesthetics > Local Anesthetics SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Local Anesthetics Pathoma Systemic > Pharmacology Esters vs Amides Rule If the drug name has two i s it s an Amide Lidocaine Bupivacaine . If it has one i it s an Ester Procaine Cocaine . Infection and pH Local anesthetics are ineffective in infected tissue because the acidic environment ionizes the drug preventing it from crossing the nerve membrane. Bupivacaine Toxicity Bupivacaine is severely cardiotoxic. Resuscitation is difficult; use Lipid Rescue Intralipid . Order of Block Small myelinated > Small unmyelinated > Large myelinated > Large unmyelinated. Pain is lost before Touch which is lost before Motor. Cocaine s Unique Feature Cocaine is the only LA that causes vasoconstriction blocks NE reuptake . All others are vasodilators often require Epinephrine . mindmap \\n- Mechanism \\n - Block Na+ channels Intracellular .\\n - Prefer Open/Inactivated states.\\n - Use-Dependent High frequency = more block .\\n- Structure \\n - Esters 1 i PABA metabolite Allergy . Pseudocholinesterase.\\n - Amides 2 i s Hepatic metabolism.\\n- Pharmacokinetics \\n - pH Acidic tissue infection = Ionized drug = No effect.\\n - Potency = Lipid Solubility.\\n - Duration = Protein Binding + Vasoconstrictors Epi .\\n- Toxicity LAST \\n - CNS Numbness -> Seizures.\\n - CV Hypotension -> Arrest.\\n - Bupivacaine Cardiotoxic Lipid Rescue .\\n - Benzocaine/Prilocaine Methemoglobinemia.\\n anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l67", "title": "Lecture #67: Pharmacology: Opioid Analgesics", "module": "Neuropharmacology", "content": "l67 Lecture 67 Pharmacology Opioid Analgesics lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 67 prev l66 next l68 pdf pdfs/Lecture 67. M. Pino Ph.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L67 HighYield.pdf transcript transcripts/Lecture 67 Pharmacology Opioid Analgesics Faculty M. Pino Ph.D..srt Lecture 67 Pharmacology Opioid Analgesics\\n\\n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 67\\n\\n Download High Yield PDF ./L67 HighYield.pdf \\n\\n Introduction & Mechanism Slide 2-3 \\n\\nOpioids are the cornerstone of management for moderate to severe pain. Derived initially from the opium poppy Papaver somniferum these agents work by mimicking endogenous peptides endorphins enkephalins dynorphins at specific receptors in the central and peripheral nervous systems. To understand their pharmacology think of the \\ Mu\\ $\\\\mu$ receptor as the primary switch for analgesia—but also for the major side effects like respiratory depression and dependence.\\n\\nWhen an opioid binds to a G-protein coupled receptor GPCR on a neuron two things happen to stop the pain signal \\n1. Presynaptically It closes voltage-gated $Ca^ 2+ $ channels preventing the release of excitatory neurotransmitters glutamate substance P .\\n2. Postsynaptically It opens $K^+$ channels causing potassium efflux and hyperpolarization making the neuron harder to excite.\\n\\nThis dual mechanism effectively \\ silences\\ the pain pathway. However because these receptors are ubiquitous the \\ silence\\ extends to the gut constipation the brainstem respiratory centers apnea and the inhibitory pathways in the midbrain euphoria/reward .\\n\\n highyield\\n Mechanism of Action \\n- Receptors Mu $\\\\mu$ Delta $\\\\delta$ Kappa $\\\\kappa$ . All are Gi/Go-coupled GPCRs .\\n- Presynaptic Blocks $Ca^ 2+ $ channels $\\\\rightarrow$ $\\\\downarrow$ Neurotransmitter release.\\n- Postsynaptic Opens $K^+$ channels $\\\\rightarrow$ Hyperpolarization $\\\\rightarrow$ $\\\\downarrow$ Neuronal firing.\\n- Result Inhibits pain transmission in the Dorsal Horn and stimulates descending inhibitory pathways.\\n \\n\\n Opioid Receptors Slide 5-6 \\n\\n| Receptor | Key Effects | Endogenous Ligand |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Mu $\\\\mu$ | Analgesia supraspinal/spinal Respiratory Depression Euphoria Sedation Constipation Miosis Dependence | Endorphins |\\n| Kappa $\\\\kappa$ | Analgesia spinal Sedation Dysphoria Hallucinations Psychotomimetic | Dynorphins |\\n| Delta $\\\\delta$ | Analgesia antidepressants effects | Enkephalins |\\n\\n Classification of Opioids Slide 7-12 \\n\\nOpioids are classified by their origin Natural vs. Synthetic and their intrinsic activity at the receptor Full Agonist vs. Partial Agonist vs. Antagonist .\\n\\n 1. Strong Agonists Full Mu Agonists \\nThese drugs have high affinity and high intrinsic activity at the $\\\\mu$ receptor. They are used for severe pain.\\n\\n- Morphine The prototype. Used for severe pain MI cancer trauma . Metabolized to Morphine-6-Glucuronide M6G active potent and Morphine-3-Glucuronide M3G inactive/neuroexcitatory .\\n- Methadone Full agonist with Unique Properties \\n - Long half-life variable 15-40+ hours allowing for once-daily dosing.\\n - NMDA antagonist activity helps with neuropathic pain/tolerance .\\n - Use Opioid detoxification prevents withdrawal and maintenance; chronic pain.\\n - Toxicity Can cause QT prolongation Torsades de Pointes .\\n- Fentanyl Highly lipophilic 100x more potent than morphine. Rapid onset short duration redistribution . Used in anesthesia chronic pain patches and breakthrough pain lollipop/lozenge . No histamine release cardiac stable .\\n- Meperidine Antimuscarinic effects tachycardia dry mouth . Metabolite Normeperidine accumulates in renal failure $\\\\rightarrow$ Seizures . Contraindicated with MAOIs Serotonin Syndrome . Lecturer Note Rarely used now due to toxicity. \\n- Hydromorphone / Oxymorphone Potent semi-synthetics.\\n\\n 2. Moderate/Weak Agonists\\nUsed for mild-to-moderate pain or cough. often combined with NSAIDs/Acetaminophen.\\n\\n- Codeine Prodrug. Metabolized to Morphine by CYP2D6 .\\n - Pharmacogenetics Ultra-rapid metabolizers high CYP2D6 can have fatal overdose respiratory depression . Poor metabolizers get no analgesia.\\n - Use Antitussive cough suppression and mild pain.\\n- Oxycodone / Hydrocodone Oral agents. Oxycodone is metabolized by CYP2D6/3A4. often formulated with acetaminophen Percocet/Vicodin .\\n- Tramadol Dual Mechanism Weak $\\\\mu$ agonist + Inhibits Serotonin/NE reuptake.\\n - Risk Seizures lowers threshold and Serotonin Syndrome .\\n\\n 3. Mixed Agonist-Antagonists\\nDesigned to provide analgesia with a \\ ceiling effect\\ on respiratory depression and lower abuse potential. However they can precipitate withdrawal in patients dependent on full agonists.\\n\\n- Buprenorphine Partial $\\\\mu$ agonist $\\\\kappa$ antagonist.\\n - High Affinity Slow Dissociation Hard to reverse with Naloxone.\\n - Use Opioid Use Disorder Suboxone = Buprenorphine + Naloxone and pain.\\n - Ceiling Effect Safer therapeutic index for respiratory depression.\\n- Nalbuphine / Pentazocine $\\\\kappa$ agonist $\\\\mu$ antagonist.\\n - Pentazocine Causes Dysphoria/Psychosis via $\\\\kappa$ activation.\\n\\n correlate\\n Precipitated Withdrawal \\nAdministering a partial agonist Buprenorphine or mixed agonist-antagonist Pentazocine to a patient physically dependent on a full agonist Morphine/Heroin will displace the full agonist from the receptor.\\n- Result Immediate severe withdrawal symptoms pain agitation diarrhea .\\n \\n\\n 4. Antagonists\\n- Naloxone Short-acting IV/Intranasal. Used for Acute Overdose .\\n - Clinical Pearl Duration 1-2 hrs is shorter than most opioids; monitor for re-narcotization .\\n- Naltrexone Long-acting Oral/IM. Used for Maintenance relapse prevention in Opioid and Alcohol Use Disorder.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Pharmacokinetics & Adverse Effects Slide 15-20 \\n\\n Metabolism\\n- Hepatic glucuronidation is the main pathway.\\n- Renal Excretion Metabolites M6G M3G Normeperidine accumulate in kidney failure.\\n - Caution Avoid Morphine and Meperidine in renal failure. Use Fentanyl clean metabolism or Hydromorphone.\\n\\n Acute Adverse Effects \\ MORPHINES\\ \\n- M iosis Pinpoint pupils - Tolerance does NOT develop. \\n- O rthostatic Hypotension Histamine release .\\n- R espiratory Depression Decreased response to $CO 2$ - Cause of death in overdose. \\n- P hysical Dependence/Pain control.\\n- H istamine Release Itching/Pruritus hypotension - Morphine/Codeine/Meperidine. \\n- I ncreased ICP Head injury contraindication - secondary to increased $pCO 2$ causing vasodilation .\\n- N ausea/Vomiting Stimulates CTZ .\\n- E uphoria.\\n- S edation.\\n\\n Chronic Adverse Effects\\n- Constipation Receptors in GI tract decrease motility. Tolerance does NOT develop. Regimen needed Senna/Stool Softener .\\n- Tolerance Needing higher doses for same effect. Develops to Analgesia Euphoria Sedation Respiratory Depression.\\n - NO Tolerance Miosis Constipation.\\n\\n Opioid Toxidrome The \\ Triad\\ \\n1. Coma CNS Depression \\n2. Miosis Pinpoint Pupils \\n3. Respiratory Depression Cyanosis Apnea \\n\\n correlate\\n Serotonin Syndrome \\nCombining Meperidine Tramadol or Dextromethorphan with MAOIs/SSRIs/Linezolid can trigger Serotonin Syndrome.\\n- Presentation Hyperthermia Autonomic Instability Hyperreflexia/Clonus Agitation.\\n- Libby Zion Case Caused by Meperidine + MAOI.\\n \\n\\n Clinical Uses beyond Pain\\n\\n1. Cough Suppression Antitussive \\n - Dextromethorphan NMDA antagonist isomer of codeine analog. No analgesic/addictive properties at varying doses.\\n - Codeine Effective at sub-analgesic doses.\\n2. Diarrhea \\n - Loperamide Imodium $\\\\mu$ agonist that does not cross BBB. Acts locally on gut.\\n - Diphenoxylate Combined with Atropine to discourage abuse.\\n questions 1. A 65-year-old male with end-stage renal disease requires opioid analgesia for a fracture. Which agent is the safest choice to avoid metabolite accumulation and toxicity? Morphine Meperidine Fentanyl Codeine Hydrocodone 2 Fentanyl is metabolized by the liver to inactive metabolites that are nontoxic making it safer for patients with renal failure. Morphine and Meperidine have toxic metabolites that accumulate. 2. A patient chronically taking high-dose morphine for cancer pain arrives with respiratory depression and pinpoint pupils. Naloxone is administered stabilizing breathing. Which of the following effects of morphine will persist despite chronic use i.e. minimal tolerance develops ? Euphoria Sedation Constipation Nausea Analgesia 2 Tolerance develops to most opioid effects analgesia sedation respiratory depression euphoria . Little to no tolerance develops to Constipation and Miosis . 3. A heroin addict is admitted for surgery. To control pain the resident administers an analgesic that unexpectedly precipitates severe agitation abdominal cramps and dilated pupils. Which drug was likely given? Methadone Fentanyl Buprenorphine Hydromorphone Oxycodone 2 Buprenorphine is a partial mu agonist . In a patient tolerant to a full agonist heroin a partial agonist acts as a net antagonist precipitating acute withdrawal. 4. Which opioid is associated with serotonin syndrome when combined with SSRIs and can lower the seizure threshold? Morphine Fentanyl Tramadol Codeine Methadone 2 Tramadol has a dual mechanism weak mu agonism + inhibition of Serotonin/NE reuptake. This affects seizure threshold and serotonin syndrome risk. 5. Which agent works as an anti-diarrheal by stimulating mu receptors in the GI tract without crossing the blood-brain barrier? Dextromethorphan Loperamide Diphenoxylate Naloxone Methylnaltrexone 1 Loperamide is a peripheral mu agonist that does not cross the BBB. It slows gut motility to treat diarrhea without causing CNS effects. What is the primary mechanism of action of Opioids? Presynaptic Close Ca2+ channels decrease neurotransmitter release ; Postsynaptic Open K+ channels hyperpolarization Concept Which opioid adverse effects do NOT develop tolerance? Constipation and Miosis Pinpoint pupils Clinical What is the specific toxicity of Meperidine? Seizures due to toxic metabolite Normeperidine Clinical Which opioid can prolong the QT interval? Methadone Clinical Why is Buprenorphine used in opioid maintenance? Partial agonist with long duration and ceiling effect on respiratory depression Concept What drug is used to reverse acute opioid overdose? Naloxone Mu antagonist Clinical Which opioid is safe to use in renal failure? Fentanyl hepatic metabolism no active metabolites Clinical What is the classical triad of Opioid Overdose? Coma Miosis Pinpoint pupils Respiratory Depression Clinical Which opioid has dual action as a Mu agonist and Serotonin/NE reuptake inhibitor? Tramadol Concept What happens if you give a partial agonist Buprenorphine to a heroin addict? Precipitated Withdrawal Clinical Which opioid is used primarily as an antitussive cough suppressant ? Dextromethorphan or Codeine Clinical What is the mechanism of Loperamide? Peripheral Mu agonist does not cross BBB ; slows gut motility Concept SketchyPharm Smooth Muscle & CNS > Opioids > Opioid Agonists BandB Neurology > Pharmacology > Opioids FirstAid Pharmacology > Toxicities and Side Effects > Opioid Withdrawal Pathoma N/A Tolerance Exceptions Tolerance develops to analgesia euphoria and respiratory depression but NOT to Constipation or Miosis. Renal Failure Safety Avoid Morphine M6G/M3G and Meperidine Normeperidine in renal failure. Use Fentanyl. Meperidine Risks Causes Seizures Normeperidine and Serotonin Syndrome with MAOIs . Mydriasis antimuscarinic instead of miosis. Tramadol s Dual Danger Tramadol lowers seizure threshold and can cause Serotonin Syndrome. Precipitated Withdrawal Buprenorphine is a partial agonist with high affinity. It kicks full agonists off the receptor causing immediate withdrawal in dependent patients. mindmap \\n- Mechanism \\n - Block Na+ channels Intracellular .\\n - Prefer Open/Inactivated states.\\n - Use-Dependent High frequency = more block .\\n- Structure \\n - Esters 1 i PABA metabolite Allergy . Pseudocholinesterase.\\n - Amides 2 i s Hepatic metabolism.\\n- Pharmacokinetics \\n - pH Acidic tissue infection = Ionized drug = No effect.\\n - Potency = Lipid Solubility.\\n - Duration = Protein Binding + Vasoconstrictors Epi .\\n- Toxicity LAST \\n - CNS Numbness -> Seizures.\\n - CV Hypotension -> Arrest.\\n - Bupivacaine Cardiotoxic Lipid Rescue .\\n - Benzocaine/Prilocaine Methemoglobinemia.\\n anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l68", "title": "Lecture #68: Physiology: Drug Addiction", "module": "Neuropharmacology", "content": "l68 Lecture 68 Physiology Drug Addiction lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 68 prev l67 next l69 pdf pdfs/Lecture 68. G. Otazu Ph.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L68 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L68 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 68 Physiology Drug Addiction\\n\\n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 68\\n\\n---\\n\\n Introduction to Drug Addiction Slide 2-3 \\n\\nDrug addiction remains a critical public health crisis with over 64 000 overdose deaths reported in 2015 alone. It is a leading cause of accidental death often initiating in young adulthood around age 20 with gateway substances like marijuana. Beyond the staggering human toll the economic burden—including healthcare lost productivity and crime—is estimated at $740 billion annually.\\n\\nTo understand addiction we must move beyond the stigma and view it through a physiological lens. Addiction is defined as a chronic relapsing disease characterized by compulsive drug seeking and use despite harmful consequences. It is not merely a failure of willpower but a fundamental hijacking of the brain s reward circuitry. The process begins with recreational use which in susceptible individuals progresses to compulsive use. The cycle of addiction involves three stages binge/intoxication driven by the reward pathway withdrawal/negative affect driven by the stress pathway and preoccupation/anticipation driven by executive dysfunction and craving .\\n\\n The Mesolimbic Reward Pathway Slide 6-8 \\n\\n The core of addiction pathology lies in the Mesolimbic Dopamine Pathway . This system evolved to reinforce behaviors essential for survival such as eating drinking and reproduction. When we engage in these activities the brain releases dopamine the \\ learning signal\\ that says \\ This was good do it again.\\ \\n\\nAnatomy is destiny here. The pathway originates in the Ventral Tegmental Area VTA of the midbrain. Dopaminergic neurons from the VTA project to the Nucleus Accumbens NAc in the limbic system. This VTA-NAc connection is the primary highway of reward. However the system is more complex involving \\n- Amygdala Processes emotions and stress conditioned learning .\\n- Hippocampus Encodes context and memory e.g. remembering where drugs were used .\\n- Prefrontal Cortex Governing executive function planning and decision-making.\\n\\n highyield\\n The Mesolimbic Pathway \\n- Origin Ventral Tegmental Area VTA in the midbrain.\\n- Target Nucleus Accumbens NAc in the limbic system.\\n- Neurotransmitter Dopamine DA .\\n- Function Reinforcement Learning Reward Prediction Error .\\n \\n\\n Reward Prediction Error Slide 10-12 \\nWait isn t dopamine just \\ pleasure\\ ? Not exactly. Seminal experiments by Wolfram Schultz revealed that dopamine neurons signal Reward Prediction Error .\\n1. Unexpected Reward Dopamine fires strongly Positive error . The brain learns \\ Surprise reward! Learn this context.\\ \\n2. Predicted Reward Dopamine firing shifts to the cue stimulus not the reward itself. The brain expects the reward.\\n3. Omitted Reward Dopamine firing drops below baseline Negative error . The brain learns \\ Prediction failed. Update behavior.\\ \\n\\nDrugs of abuse bypass this natural calibration. They cause a massive pharmacological release of dopamine that never habituates . The brain receives a constant \\ Better than expected!\\ signal cementing the drug-taking behavior as a survival priority outweighing food or safety.\\n\\n Mechanisms of Drug Classes Slide 14 \\n\\nDrugs of abuse can be categorized by their specific molecular targets in the reward pathway. While they all ultimately increase dopamine in the Nucleus Accumbens they get there by different routes.\\n\\n Class 1 Psychostimulants Direct Sympathomimetics \\nThese agents directly increase monoamine concentration in the synaptic cleft.\\n\\n Amphetamines Slide 16-17 \\nAmphetamines are molecular trojan horses. They resemble dopamine and enter the presynaptic neuron via the Dopamine Transporter DAT . Once inside they invade synaptic vesicles via VMAT2 displacing endogenous dopamine into the cytoplasm.\\n- Mechanism \\n 1. Enters via DAT.\\n 2. Displaces DA from vesicles.\\n 3. Reverses DAT The high intracellular DA concentration causes DAT to pump DA out into the synapse reverse transport .\\n 4. Result Massive non-impulse-dependent DA release.\\n- Intoxication Euphoria psychomotor agitation insomnia tachycardia mydriasis dilated pupils .\\n- Withdrawal \\ The Crash\\ —depression lethargy increased appetite sleep disturbance.\\n\\n Cocaine Slide 18-20 \\nCocaine is a blockade artist. unlike amphetamine it does not enter the neuron to work.\\n- Mechanism Binds to and blocks DAT and NET/SERT . This prevents reuptake trapping dopamine in the synapse.\\n- Unique Feature Cocaine is a locally anesthetic Na+ channel blocker and a Vasoconstrictor blocks NE reuptake .\\n- Clinical Consequence Cocaine chest pain is due to coronary artery vasospasm .\\n- Complication Ischemia myocardial infarction intracranial hemorrhage.\\n- Micro-aneurysms Chronic use can cause \\ crack lung\\ or cerebral micro-aneurysms.\\n\\n correlate\\n Cocaine-Induced Chest Pain \\n- Pathophysiology Inhibition of biochemical reuptake of Norepinephrine NE $\\\\rightarrow$ Excess NE stimulation of Alpha-1 receptors $\\\\rightarrow$ Vasoconstriction.\\n- Presentation Chest pain hypertension agitation.\\n- Treatment Benzodiazepines first-line Nitroglycerin Phentolamine.\\n- CONTRAINDICATION Beta-Blockers are contraindicated!\\n- Why? Blocking Beta-2 vasodilatory receptors leaves Alpha-1 vasoconstrictive receptors unopposed $\\\\rightarrow$ Worsening hypertension and ischemia \\ Unopposed Alpha Stimulation\\ .\\n \\n\\n Nicotine Slide 22 \\n- Mechanism Agonist at Nicotinic Acetylcholine Receptors nAChRs on VTA dopamine neurons.\\n- Effect Direct depolarization of VTA neurons $\\\\rightarrow$ increased firing $\\\\rightarrow$ DA release in NAc.\\n- Intoxication Restlessness anxiety.\\n- Withdrawal Irritability anxiety craving increased appetite.\\n\\n Class 2 Depressants GABA-ergic Agents \\nThese drugs act like the brakes of the brain primarily targeting the GABA-A receptor.\\n\\n Alcohol Ethanol Slide 24-27 \\n- Mechanism Enhances GABA-A receptor activity inhibitory and inhibits NMDA glutamate receptors excitatory .\\n- Intoxication Disinhibition ataxia slurred speech blackouts.\\n- Chronic Sequelae Cirrhosis Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome peripheral neuropathy.\\n- Withdrawal Tremors anxiety seizures \\ Rum fits\\ and the life-threatening Delirium Tremens .\\n\\n correlate\\n Delirium Tremens DTs \\n- Pathophysiology Chronic alcohol use downregulates GABA receptors and upregulates NMDA receptors to maintain homeostasis . Abrupt cessation leads to massive overexcitation.\\n- Timing 2-4 days 48-96 hours after last drink.\\n- Presentation Altered mental status autonomic instability fever tachycardia hypertension hallucinations.\\n- Treatment Benzodiazepines Chlordiazepoxide Diazepam Lorazepam .\\n- Lecturer s Point This is a medical emergency with high mortality if untreated.\\n \\n\\n Benzodiazepines & Barbiturates Slide 29-32 \\nBoth bind allosterically to the GABA-A receptor chloride channel but at different sites.\\n- Benzodiazepines Increase the Frequency of channel opening. \\ Frenzodiazepines\\ \\n- Barbiturates Increase the Duration of channel opening. \\ Barbi-durates\\ \\n- Addiction Mechanism How do inhibitors cause dopamine release? via Disinhibition .\\n - VTA dopamine neurons are normally kept in check by local GABAergic interneurons.\\n - Benzos/Barbs bind to GABA-A receptors on these interneurons .\\n - Inhibiting the inhibitor Interneuron $\\\\rightarrow$ Releases the brake on the DA neuron $\\\\rightarrow$ DA firing increases.\\n- Receptor Nuance Slide 31 The GABA interneurons in the VTA have Alpha-1 containing GABA-A receptors. VTA dopamine neurons have Alpha-3. Benzos preferentially target Alpha-1 explaining why they silence the interneuron but not the DA neuron itself.\\n\\n Class 3 Opioids Slide 34-36 \\n- Agents Heroin Morphine Fentanyl Oxycodone.\\n- Mechanism Agonists at the Mu-Opioid Receptor Gi-coupled .\\n- Pathway Similar to repressants they work via Disinhibition . They bind Mu receptors on GABA interneurons in the VTA $\\\\rightarrow$ hyperpolarization decrease GABA release $\\\\rightarrow$ VTA DA neurons fire freely.\\n- Intoxication \\ Pinpoint pupils\\ Miosis respiratory depression CNS depression constipation euphoria.\\n\\n correlate\\n Opioid Withdrawal \\n- Pathophysiology Upregulation of cAMP/NE pathways to counteract chronic inhibition. Removal of drug leads to noradrenergic storm.\\n- Presentation Dilated pupils mydriasis piloerection \\ cold turkey\\ rhinorrhea lacrimation yawning nausea/vomiting diarrhea.\\n- Lecturer s Point While alcohol withdrawal can kill you seizures opioid withdrawal makes you want to die misery but is rarely fatal.\\n- Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome Newborns of dependent mothers presenting with high-pitched cry tremors and irritability.\\n \\n\\n Class 4 Hallucinogens & Others\\n\\n Phencyclidine PCP Slide 38 \\n- Mechanism Non-competitive antagonist at NMDA Glutamate receptors .\\n- Presentation Violent behavior nystagmus vertical/horizontal impulsivity analgesia psychosis.\\n- Complication Trauma due to analgesia/aggression rhabdomyolysis.\\n\\n MDMA Ecstasy Slide 43 \\n- Mechanism Functions like amphetamine reverses transporters but with high affinity for SERT Serotonin Transporter .\\n- Effect Massive release of Serotonin 5-HT > Dopamine/NE.\\n- Intoxication Euphoria emotional closeness empathogen hyperthermia hyponatremia due to excess water intake + SIADH-like effect .\\n- Complication Serotonin Syndrome especially if combined with SSRIs .\\n\\n Marijuana THC Slide 41 \\n- Mechanism Partial agonist at CB1 Cannabinoid receptors .\\n- Action CB1 receptors are presynaptic and Gi-coupled. They inhibit GABA release Disinhibition in the VTA.\\n- Intoxication Euphoria increased appetite \\ munchies\\ conjunctival injection red eyes dry mouth slowed reaction time.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Summary of Mechanisms Table\\n\\n| Drug Class | Primary Target | Mechanism on Reward Pathway | Key Intoxication Sign |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Amphetamine | DAT / VMAT2 | Reverses DAT; Displaces DA from vesicles | Agitation Pupil Dilation |\\n| Cocaine | DAT Transporter | Blocks Reuptake of DA/NE/5-HT | Chest Pain Pupil Dilation |\\n| Opioids | Mu-Receptor | Disinhibition inhibits GABA interneuron | Pinpoint Pupils Resp Depression |\\n| Benzos | GABA-A Alpha-1 | Disinhibition inhibits GABA interneuron | Sedation Resp Depression |\\n| Nicotine | nAChR | Direct depolarization of VTA neuron | Alertness Anxiety |\\n| PCP | NMDA Receptor | Antagonist Blocks Glutamate | Nystagmus Violence |\\n| MDMA | SERT > DAT | Reverses SERT Massive 5-HT release | Hyperthermia Hyponatremia |\\n| Marijuana | CB1 Receptor | Disinhibition Presynaptic inhibition of GABA | Red Eyes Munchies |\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Pupil Changes \\n- Miosis Constriction Opioids Cholinergics. \\ Pinpoint requires opioids\\ \\n- Mydriasis Dilation Amphetamines Cocaine LSD Withdrawal from Opioids. \\ Wide eyed excitement\\ \\n \\n questions 1. A 22-year-old male is brought to the ED by friends after a rave. He is agitated hypertensive and hyperthermic 104°F . Sodium is 120 mEq/L. He reports feeling \\ connected to everyone\\ earlier. Which neurotransmitter mechanism is primarily responsible for his condition? Direct agonism of GABA-A receptors Reversal of Serotonin SERT transporters Antagonism of NMDA receptors Inhibition of Voltage-gated Na+ channels Agonism of Mu-opioid receptors 1 MDMA Ecstasy causes massive release of Serotonin by reversing SERT. It presents with hyperthermia hyponatremia excess water intake and empathy. 2. A 35-year-old man presents with severe chest pain and agitation. ECG shows ST depression. Blood pressure is 180/110 mmHg. He admits to using cocaine. Which medication is CONTRAINDICATED? Lorazepam Nitroglycerin Phentolamine Metoprolol Verapamil 3 Beta-blockers Metoprolol are contraindicated in cocaine toxicity due to \\ Unopposed Alpha Stimulation \\ which can worsen vasoconstriction and hypertension. 3. Which drug of abuse works by acting as a non-competitive antagonist at the NMDA glutamate receptor? LSD Heroin PCP Phencyclidine Cocaine Methamphetamine 2 PCP acts as an antagonist at NMDA receptors . Key signs include nystagmus vertical/horizontal and violent behavior. 4. A patient in the ED presents with rhinorrhea lacrimation dilated pupils and severe abdominal cramping. He is yawning frequently. Withdrawal from which substance is most likely? Alcohol Cocaine Heroin Benzodiazepines Marijuana 2 Opioid Heroin withdrawal presents with flu-like symptoms rhinorrhea lacrimation yawning dilated pupils and GI distress. 5. Benzodiazepines increase dopamine release in the Nucleus Accumbens via which mechanism? Direct stimulation of D2 receptors Inhibition of GABAergic interneurons in the VTA Disinhibition Blocking the Dopamine Transporter DAT Stimulating Nicotinic receptors on VTA neurons Reversing VMAT2 transport 1 Benzodiazepines inhibit GABAergic interneurons via Alpha-1 GABA-A receptors in the VTA. This removes inhibition on the DA neurons Disinhibition allowing them to fire. What is the primary neurotransmitter of the reward pathway? Dopamine Concept Where do the dopaminergic neurons of the reward pathway originate? Ventral Tegmental Area VTA Concept What is the mechanism of action of Cocaine? Blocks reuptake of Dopamine NE and Serotonin Blocks DAT/NET/SERT Concept What is the mechanism of action of Amphetamines? Enters neuron via DAT and reverses it pumps DA out ; displaces DA from vesicles Concept Why is Methadone used for opioid withdrawal? It is a long-acting Mu-opioid agonist prevents withdrawal without high Clinical What are the classic trio of signs for Opioid overdose? Pinpoint pupils Miosis Respiratory Depression Coma CNS depression Clinical What drug causes vertical/horizontal nystagmus and violent behavior? PCP Phencyclidine Clinical Which receptors does Alcohol primarily affect? Enhances GABA-A inhibitory and inhibits NMDA excitatory Concept What is the specific danger of combining Tyramine-rich foods with MAOIs? Hypertensive Crisis Clinical How do Benzodiazepines affect the GABA-A receptor? Increase Frequency of channel opening Concept How do Barbiturates affect the GABA-A receptor? Increase Duration of channel opening Concept What is the treatment for Delirium Tremens? Benzodiazepines Clinical What is the mechanism of MDMA Ecstasy ? Reverses SERT flods synapse with Serotonin Concept Why are Beta-blockers contraindicated in Cocaine toxicity? Unopposed Alpha-1 vasoconstriction worsens hypertension Clinical B&B Psychiatry > Substance Abuse > Substance Abuse FirstAid Psychiatry > Pharmacology > Substance Use SketchyPharm Psych > Substance Abuse Schreber Addiction Module Disinhibition Concept Depressants Opioids Benzos Alcohol increase dopamine by inhibiting the GABAergic interneurons that normally braking the VTA dopamine neurons. Cocaine vs Amphetamine Cocaine BLOCKS the door DAT blocker . Amphetamine BREAKS IN and pushes dopamine out DAT reversal/Vesicle displacement . Beta-Blocker Trap Never give Beta-blockers for Cocaine chest pain. It leaves Alpha receptors unopposed causing severe vasoconstriction. Opioid Withdrawal vs Alcohol Withdrawal Alcohol withdrawal is fatal seizures/DTs . Opioid withdrawal makes you wish you were dead flu-like misery but is rarely fatal. Nystagmus Vertical/Horizontal nystagmus in an agitated patient is pathognomonic for PCP intoxication. mindmap \\n- Mesolimbic Pathway \\n - VTA -> Nucleus Accumbens DA release .\\n - Mechanism Reward Prediction Error.\\n- Stimulants \\n - Cocaine Blocks DAT. Chest pain Vasospasm . No Beta-blockers.\\n - Amphetamine Reverses DAT. Displaces vesicles.\\n - Nicotine Agonist at nAChR VTA .\\n- Depressants Disinhibition \\n - Alcohol GABA-A agonist / NMDA antagonist. DTs Fatal .\\n - Benzos GABA-A Frequency Alpha-1 subunit in VTA .\\n - Opioids Mu-agonist. Pinpoint pupils Resp Depression. Withdrawal = Flu-like.\\n- Others \\n - PCP NMDA Antagonist. Violence Nystagmus.\\n - MDMA SERT Reversal. Serotonin Syndrome Hyponatremia.\\n - Marijuana CB1 Agonist Presynaptic inhibition .\\n anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l69", "title": "Lecture #69: Substance Use Disorders", "module": "Neuropharmacology", "content": "l69 Lecture 69 Substance Use Disorders lecturer A. Coladner D.O. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 69 prev l68 next l70 pdf pdfs/Lecture 69. A. Coladner D.O. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L69 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L69 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 69 Substance Use Disorders Lecturer A. Coladner D.O. Session Lecture 69 Substance Use Disorders SUD Overview Substance Use Discovery involves a complex interaction between brain circuitry genetics and environmental factors where an individual continues to use a substance despite significant harm. It is a chronic relapsing brain disease characterized by compulsive drug seeking and use. The DSM-5 defines SUD based on a pathological pattern of behaviors related to the use of the substance. Diagnosis requires meeting specific criteria within a 12-month period and severity is graded based on the count of these criteria met Mild 2-3 Moderate 4-5 and Severe 6+ . The 11 Criteria Grouped Slide 4 Impaired Control Taking the substance in larger amounts or longer than intended. Persistent desire or unsuccessful efforts to cut down. Spending a great deal of time obtaining using or recovering from the substance. Craving A strong desire or urge to use. Social Impairment Recurrent use resulting in failure to fulfill major role obligations work school home . Continued use despite persistent social or interpersonal problems. Important social occupational or recreational activities are given up. Risky Use Recurrent use in situations where it is physically hazardous e.g. driving . Continued use despite knowledge of having a persistent physical or psychological problem caused by the substance. Pharmacological Criteria Tolerance Need for increased amounts to achieve intoxication or diminished effect with the same amount. Withdrawal The characteristic withdrawal syndrome for the substance or taking the substance to relieve withdrawal. Note Tolerance and withdrawal do NOT count as criteria if the medication is taken as prescribed under medical supervision e.g. opioids for chronic pain . highyield Medical Use Exception Tolerance and Withdrawal generally do NOT count toward the diagnosis of a Substance Use Disorder if the substance is being used solely under appropriate medical supervision e.g. a patient on chronic opioids for cancer pain . --- Alcohol Use Disorder Alcohol affects the central nervous system as a depressant. It has profound public health implications contributing significantly to emergency room visits overdoses and deaths annually. Screening and history taking are critical as patients may minimize their use. The disorder is characterized by the inability to stop or control alcohol use despite adverse social occupational or health consequences. Alcohol Intoxication Slide 8-9 Presentation Slurred speech Incoordination / Ataxia Unsteady gait Nystagmus Involuntary eye movement Impaired attention or memory Stupor or Coma Signs Flushed face Conjunctival Injection also in Cannabis decreased level of consciousness. Alcohol Poisoning Slide 10-14 Mechanism Alcohol level overwhelms the liver s metabolic capacity. Signs Respiratory Depression Breathing slows/stops Hypothermia Incontinence Seizures Coma. Loss of gag reflex risk of aspiration . Management Airway protection intubation if necessary . IV Fluids and Glucose Check fingerstick first! . Thiamine Vitamin B1 Administer BEFORE or with glucose to prevent Wernicke s Encephalopathy . Haloperidol Preferred for severe agitation. Alcohol Withdrawal Slide 16-17 Timeline Minor Withdrawal 6-36 hours Tremors The Shakes Anxiety Headache Diaphoresis Palpitations GI upset. Seizures 6-48 hours Generalized tonic-clonic. Alcoholic Hallucinosis 12-48 hours Visual/Tactile hallucinations with intact orientation. Delirium Tremens DTs 48-96 hours MEDICAL EMERGENCY . Delirium Tremens DTs Slide 17 Triad Delirium Confusion/Disorientation + Hallucinations often visual/tactile bugs + Autonomic Instability Fever Tachycardia Hypertension . Mortality High if untreated. correlate Delirium Tremens DTs - Pathophysiology Chronic alcohol use downregulates GABA receptors and upregulates NMDA Glutamate receptors. Abrupt cessation leads to uninhibited excitation Glutamate storm . - Presentation Delirium Confusion Agitation Fever Tachycardia >100 bpm Hypertension Diaphoresis. - Diagnosis Clinical diagnosis based on history of cessation + hyperadrenergic state. - Treatment Benzodiazepines Diazepam Chlordiazepoxide Lorazepam . IV Fluids. - Lecturer s Point Fifteen percent will develop DTs... Treatment is Diazepam. Treatment of Alcohol Use Disorder Detoxification Benzodiazepines Gold Standard . Maintenance Naltrexone Opioid antagonist; reduces cravings and the high from drinking. Acamprosate NMDA modulator. Disulfiram Aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibitor Causes sickness if drinking . --- Opioid Use Disorder Slide 26-36 Opioids bind to mu-opioid receptors to produce analgesia and euphoria. The epidemic of opioid misuse involves natural Morphine Codeine semi-synthetic Heroin Oxycodone and synthetic Fentanyl Methadone varieties. Fentanyl is particularly dangerous due to its extreme potency 50-100x morphine and its clandestine addition to other street drugs. Opioid Intoxication Classic Triad 1. Coma / CNS Depression 2. Respiratory Depression Lethal mechanism 3. Miosis Pinpoint Pupils Other Signs Constipation Slurred speech Impairment in attention. Opioid Withdrawal Mechanism Loss of inhibitory opioid tone leads to Noradrenergic NE storm. Presentation Flu-like illness. Not life-threatening unlike Alcohol/Benzo withdrawal but extremely uncomfortable. Symptoms Dysphoria & Craving. Rhinorrhea Runny nose & Lacrimation Tearing . Yawning . Piloerection Gooseflesh -> term Cold Turkey . Mydriasis Dilated pupils . Muscle aches Diarrhea Nausea/Vomiting. mnemonic Opioid Withdrawal SLUDGE stops flight begins Symptoms are the opposite of intoxication - Intoxication Constipation Miosis Dry. - Withdrawal Diarrhea Mydriasis Rhinorrhea Lacrimation Yawning. Treatment of Opioid Use Disorder Slide 37 Overdose Naloxone Mu-antagonist . Intranasal formulation is widely available. Maintenance Therapy MAT 1. Methadone Full Mu-Agonist . Long half-life. Reduces cravings and withdrawal. Requires daily clinic visits. 2. Buprenorphine Partial Mu-Agonist . High affinity low intrinsic activity. Ceiling effect creates safety profile. Can be prescribed in office Suboxone = Buprenorphine + Naloxone . 3. Naltrexone Mu-Antagonist . Blocks opioid effects. Patient must be fully detoxed first to avoid precipitated withdrawal. --- Stimulant Use Disorder Cocaine Amphetamines Slide 44-53 Stimulants increase synaptic levels of Dopamine Norepinephrine and Serotonin. Cocaine blocks reuptake; Amphetamines increase release. The clinical picture is one of sympathetic overdrive. Stimulant Intoxication Psychological Euphoria Agitation Hypervigilance Grandiosity Paranoia. Physical Mydriasis Dilated pupils . Tachycardia / Hypertension. Chest Pain Coronary vasospasm . Weight loss Chills Sweating. Complications Cardiac Arrest Seizures Stroke. Stimulant Withdrawal The Crash Symptoms Dysphoria / Severe Depression Suicide risk . Fatigue / Psychomotor retardation. Hypersomnia with Vivid unpleasant dreams . Increased Appetite . --- Cannabis Use Disorder Slide 22-24 Cannabis acts on CB1 and CB2 receptors. It is the most commonly used illicit drug. Modern strains have very high THC content leading to higher risks of acute psychosis and anxiety. Cannabis Intoxication Psychological Euphoria High Anxiety Sensation of slowed time Impaired judgment and motor coordination. Physical Signs Conjunctival Injection Red Eyes - Hallmark . Increased Appetite The Munchies . Dry Mouth Cotton mouth . Tachycardia. Cannabis Withdrawal Timeline Starts within ~1 week of cessation. Symptoms Irritability Anger Aggression. Anxiety / Restlessness. Insomnia / Disturbing dreams. Decreased appetite / Weight loss. Depressed mood. --- Nicotine Use Disorder Slide 19-21 Nicotine stimulates nicotinic acetylcholine receptors releasing Dopamine reward . It is highly addictive with relapse rates similar to heroin. Nicotine Withdrawal Symptoms Irritability Frustration Anger. Anxiety . Difficulty Concentrating . Increased Appetite / Weight Gain. Restlessness Insomnia Depressed mood. Treatment Smoking Cessation Varenicline Chantix Slide 20 Mechanism Partial Agonist at alpha-4-beta-2 nicotinic receptors. Effect Reduces cravings agonist activity AND blocks reward from smoking antagonist activity . Efficacy Most effective monotherapy. Bupropion Zyban Slide 20 Mechanism NE/DA Reuptake Inhibitor NDRI . Use Reduces cravings. Contraindicated in seizure disorders. Nicotine Replacement Therapy NRT Patch Gum Lozenge Agonists . --- Sedative-Hypnotic Use Disorder Benzodiazepines Barbiturates Slide 38-42 Mechanism involves potentiation of GABA-A receptors inhibitory . The clinical profile of intoxication and withdrawal mirrors alcohol almost exactly. Intoxication Slurred speech Incoordination Unsteady gait Nystagmus Stupor/Coma. Danger Respiratory depression especially when combined with alcohol Synergistic effect . Withdrawal Warning Life-Threatening Like alcohol . Symptoms Autonomic hyperactivity Sweating Pulse >100 Hand tremor Insomnia Nausea. Severe Grand Mal Seizures Delirium. --- Hallucinogen Use Disorder PCP LSD Slide 24 Phencyclidine PCP Intoxication Behavior Belligerence excessive impulsivity Violence unpredictability. Physical Signs Nystagmus Vertical or Horizontal - Key Differentiator . Analgesia Diminished response to pain -> Superhuman strength . Hypertension Tachycardia Seizures. LSD Lysergic Acid Diethylamide Signs Perceptual changes depersonalization derealization illusions Mydriasis Tachycardia. Not usually violent like PCP. --- Substance Intoxication & Withdrawal Comparison Table | Substance | Intoxication Signs | Withdrawal Signs | Key Life Risks | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Alcohol | Slurred speech Ataxia Nystagmus | Tremor DTs Autonomic instability Seizures | Withdrawal is Fatal | | Opioids | Miosis Resp Depression Coma | Mydriasis Rhinorrhea Flu-like Yawning | Overdose is Fatal | | Stimulants | Mydriasis Tachycardia Chest Pain | Depression Crash Hunger Sleep | Cardiac Arrest Intox | | Cannabis | Red Eyes Munchies Dry Mouth | Irritability Insomnia No Appetite | Impaired Driving | | Benzos | Same as Alcohol | Same as Alcohol Seizures | Withdrawal is Fatal | | PCP | Nystagmus Vert/Horiz Violence | N/A | Trauma/Violence | questions 1. A 24-year-old male is brought to the ED by police after being found acting aggressively. He is agitated shouting and requires 4-point restraints. Physical exam is remarkable for vertical nystagmus and profound insensitivity to pain. Which substance is most likely responsible? Cocaine Heroin Lysergic acid diethylamide LSD Phencyclidine PCP Methamphetamine 3 PCP intoxication is classically associated with violent functionality/belligerence vertical nystagmus and analgesia high pain tolerance . 2. A 45-year-old female with a history of alcohol use disorder is admitted for surgery. 72 hours post-admission she becomes confused agitated and claims to see insects crawling on the walls. Her vitals are HR 115 BP 160/95 Temp 101.5°F. What is the most appropriate pharmacologic treatment? Haloperidol Diazepam Thiamine only Propranolol Fomepizole 1 The patient is experiencing Delirium Tremens DTs Confusion + Hallucinations + Autonomic Instability . Benzodiazepines e.g. Diazepam Lorazepam are the treatment of choice to substitute for the withdrawal of alcohol s GABAergic effect. 3. A 30-year-old male presents requesting help with smoking cessation. He is concerned about weight gain and craving. Which medication acts as a partial agonist at the alpha-4-beta-2 nicotinic receptor? Nicotine Patch Bupropion Varenicline Nortriptyline Clonidine 2 Varenicline is a partial agonist at the alpha-4-beta-2 nicotinic receptor. It reduces cravings and withdrawal agonist effect while blocking the rewarding effects of nicotine antagonist effect . 4. A patient presents with severe rhinorrhea lacrimation yawning and dilated pupils. They report feeling like they have the \\ worst flu of their life\\ . Which substance withdrawal is this clinical picture most consistent with? Alcohol Cocaine Heroin Cannabis Tobacco 2 Opioid Heroin withdrawal typically presents as a severe flu-like illness with Rhinorrhea Lacrimation Yawning Piloerection and Mydriasis . 5. Which physical exam finding is most specific for Cannabis intoxication? Miosis Vertical Nystagmus Horizontal Nystagmus Conjunctival Injection Bradypnea 3 Conjunctival Injection Red eyes is the most consistent physical sign of acute cannabis intoxication often accompanied by tachycardia and dry mouth. What is the classic triad of Delirium Tremens? Delta Mental Status Delirium Hallucinations Visual/Tactile Autonomic Instability Fever HR^ . Alcohol What is the classic triad of Opioid Overdose? Coma Respiratory Depression Miosis Pinpoint pupils . Opioids Which withdrawal syndromes can be fatal? Alcohol and Sedative-Hypnotics Benzodiazepines/Barbiturates . Safety What is the mechanism of Varenicline? Partial Agonist at alpha-4-beta-2 nicotinic receptors. Pharmacology What is the key physical exam differentiator for PCP intoxication? Nystagmus Vertical or Horizontal . Diagnosis What physical sign distinguishes Opioid Withdrawal from Intoxication? Pupil Size Intoxication = Miosis Pinpoint ; Withdrawal = Mydriasis Dilated . Diagnosis What is the treatment for Alcohol Withdrawal? Benzodiazepines e.g. Diazepam Chlordiazepoxide Lorazepam . Treatment What signs characterize Stimulant Withdrawal? Dysphoria \\ The Crash\\ Hypersomnia Increased Appetite. Stimulants Which sign is hallmark for Cannabis intoxication? Conjunctival Injection Red Eyes . Cannabis Why is Thiamine given before glucose in alcoholics? To prevent precipitation of Wernicke s Encephalopathy. Safety BandB Behavioral Science > Substance Use Disorder > Alcohol Pathoma Fundamentals > Neoplasia Not a direct match check Psych FirstAid Psychiatry > Substance Use SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetic Physeo Psychiatry > Substance Use Disorder Pupils The Window to the Drug Pinpoint Miosis Opioids Intoxication .\\n Dilated Mydriasis Stimulants Cocaine/Amphetamines Opioids Withdrawal Hallucinogens LSD . Deadly Withdrawal Remember You can die from withdrawal of Booze Alcohol and Benzos . You will just wish you were dead from Opioid withdrawal. PCP Presentation Violent Super-strength Vertical Nystagmus. Treat with Benzodiazepines and antipsychotics. mindmap Substance Use Disorders\\n\\n Alcohol\\n- Intoxication Slurred speech Ataxia.\\n- Withdrawal Tremors -> Seizures -> DTs Fatal .\\n- Tx Benzos.\\n\\n Opioids\\n- Intoxication Miosis Resp Depression.\\n- Withdrawal Flu-like Mydriasis Yawning.\\n- Tx Naloxone Rescue Methadone/Suboxone Maint .\\n\\n Stimulants\\n- Intoxication Mydriasis Chest Pain Tachycardia.\\n- Withdrawal Sleepy Hungry Depressed.\\n\\n Hallucinogens\\n- PCP Nystagmus Violence.\\n- Cannabis Red Eyes Munchies. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l77", "title": "Lecture #77: Neurodevelopmental and Behavioral Problems in Children and School Dysfunction", "module": "Pediatrics", "content": "l77 Lecture 77 Neurodevelopmental and Behavioral Problems in Children and School Dysfunction lecturer Dr. Adam Hurst Pediatrics session Lecture 77 prev l76 next l78 pdf pdfs/Lecture 77. A. Hurst M.D. PPT -1.pdf Lecture 77 Neurodevelopmental and Behavioral Problems in Children and School Dysfunction Lecturer Dr. Adam Hurst Session Lecture 77 Developmental Delays and General Concepts Slide 2 Normal child development has a wide range of normal variation. Developmental milestones provide a framework to monitor a child s progress over time. A developmental delay occurs when a child falls outside this range of normal variation in any domain motor speech cognitive or social . A crucial distinction for a provider is recognizing when an area of concern is merely a delay versus a disorder the latter of which features a significant impairment in day-to-day functioning. - Key Principles of Delay - Can be isolated one domain or global two or more domains . - Delay does not inherently mean disorder; disorder implies functional limitation. - Loss of milestones is ALWAYS a red flag and warrants immediate workup often correlating with genetic conditions or underlying neurologic insults. - Early intervention speech therapy physical therapy occupational therapy shows great progress and is the cornerstone of management. Communication and Language Disorders Slide 13 Language development serves as an early marker for cognitive development. When a child fails to develop typical language abilities the primary objective is to differentiate a simple delay from a structural sensory or pervasive neurodevelopmental issue. A critical step in any child with a speech or language delay regardless of a normal newborn screening is to undergo formal audiology testing to rule out hearing loss. - Approach to Speech/Language Delay - History & Physical Assess prenatal/birth history family history and perform a full physical exam to look for dysmorphic features suggesting genetic etiologies. - Hearing Assessment MANDATORY All children presenting with speech/language concerns at any age must be referred for a formal hearing assessment by an audiologist. - Treatment Early referral to speech-language pathology Speech Therapy should not be delayed while awaiting further evaluations. correlate Language Disorder - Pathophysiology Deficit in comprehension receptive and/or production expressive of language. - Presentation Reduced vocabulary limited sentence structure and limited conversation skills. Often presents as a delay in the onset of words up to 3 years of age. Child may compensate using gestures but often becomes frustrated leading to tantrums. - Diagnosis Clinical diagnosis established via formal speech-language evaluation. Must rule out hearing impairment environmental deprivation or other medical conditions. - Treatment Speech Therapy. - Lecturer s Point Despite compensation strategies deficits lead to functional limitations. Early therapy is crucial. correlate Speech Sound Disorder - Pathophysiology Persistent difficulty with speech and sound articulation/production. - Presentation Inability to articulate sounds interfering with speech intelligibility. Expected intelligibility 50% by age 2 75% by age 3 and 100% by age 4. A 5-year-old with only 50% intelligibility is highly concerning for this disorder. - Diagnosis Clinical evaluation. - Treatment Speech Therapy. correlate Child-Onset Fluency Disorder Stuttering - Pathophysiology Impairment in speech fluency and timing. - Presentation Stuttering. Occurs in 1% of school-aged children 3x more common in boys. - Diagnosis Clinical. - Treatment Speech Therapy. correlate Social Pragmatic Communication Disorder - Pathophysiology Impairment in the use of language for social exchanges and in adapting communication styles to context. - Presentation Difficulty interpreting ambiguous language humor or sarcasm. Does not follow conventional conversation norms. These children are actively seeking social interaction but have trouble achieving it properly leading to being bullied or ostracized. - Diagnosis Clinical. Must be explicitly differentiated from Autism Spectrum Disorder ASD . - Treatment Speech Therapy and Social Skills Therapy. - Lecturer s Point Unlike children with ASD children with social pragmatic communication disorder possess a social drive and want to interact but lack the ability to adapt their communication style. Autism Spectrum Disorder ASD Slide 18 Autism Spectrum Disorder is a lifelong neurodevelopmental disorder characterized by two core dimensions deficits in social communication/interaction and the presence of restrictive or repetitive behaviors. The prevalence of ASD is increasing now estimated at 1 in 36 children 2020 data with males affected four times more frequently than females. The umbrella term ASD now encompasses previous diagnoses like pervasive developmental delay and Asperger s syndrome. Notably most individuals with ASD have normal cognitive intelligence IQ > 70 . - Risk Factors - Genetic Chromosomal disorders Fragile X is the most common Down syndrome Rett syndrome Tuberous sclerosis. High sibling recurrence rate up to 25% . - Biological Older parental age prenatal exposures alcohol heavy metals TORCH infections prematurity low birth weight. - Lecturer s Nuance Vaccines do NOT cause autism. - Diagnostic Criteria DSM-5 Based structure - Social Deficits Needs at least 1 - Lack of social-emotional reciprocity no back-and-forth conversation limited joint attention . - Deficits in nonverbal communication poor eye contact lack of facial expression absence of social smile or pointing . - Difficulty developing maintaining or understanding relationships poor imaginative play lack of interest in peers . - Restrictive/Repetitive Behaviors Needs at least 2 of 4 - Stereotyped or repetitive movements/speech echolalia idiosyncratic phrases lining up toys . - Insistence on sameness inflexible adherence to routines extreme distress at small changes. - Highly restricted fixated interests. - Hyper- or hypo-reactivity to sensory input indifference to pain visual fascination with lights excessive smelling/touching . highyield Autism Spectrum Disorder Diagnosis and Management - Screening The M-CHAT-R is commonly performed at 18 and 24 months of age. - Management Principle Early diagnosis and intervention are optimal. Do not delay management steps speech therapy occupational therapy Applied Behavioral Analysis ABA while awaiting a formal in-depth evaluation by a developmental pediatrician or multidisciplinary team. Intellectual Disability Slide 28 Intellectual disability formerly mental retardation involves a combination of impairments in both cognitive intellectual and adaptive development. The severity of the impairment normally dictates the age of diagnosis; severe cases are recognized early while mild intellectual disability may only be recognized when academic struggles arise during the school years. - Key Components - Cognitive Development Reduced IQ typically < 70 . - Adaptive Functioning Deficits in conceptual skills language reading writing social skills and practical skills activities of daily living personal care . - Etiology - Most common preventable cause Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorder. - Most common genetic causes Fragile X Trisomy 21 Down syndrome . - Common underlying finding Normal physical exam does not rule out genetic cause. Up to 14% of children with normal exams have an underlying genetic etiology. - Workup & Management - History & Physical Screen for dysmorphia growth parameters and refer for vision/hearing testing. - Assessment Psychoeducational testing e.g. WPPSI WISC WAIS for cognition; VABS ABAS for adaptive functioning usually performed by a psychologist. - Management Multidisciplinary team. Under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act IDEA states must provide a free and appropriate public education in the least restrictive environment. Children are given an Individualized Education Program IEP . Motor Disorders Slide 30 Motor disorders manifest as physical movement abnormalities that can interfere with daily life though they generally lack concurrent cognitive delays. These can range from coordination issues to the involuntary presence of tics. correlate Developmental Coordination Disorder - Presentation Deficits in executing coordinated motor skills. Child is extremely clumsy slow or inaccurate with tasks like using scissors or riding a bike. Avoids sports. - Diagnosis Clinical. correlate Stereotypical Movement Disorder - Presentation Repetitive purposeless motor behaviors e.g. body rocking hand shaking head banging . May or may not be self-injurious. correlate Tourette Syndrome vs. Chronic Tic Disorder - Tourette Syndrome Defined by the presence of multiple motor tics AND one or more vocal tics present for at least one year. - Persistent/Chronic Tic Disorder Defined by the presence of either motor OR vocal tics but not both present for at least one year. - Symptoms - Motor Tics Eye blinking facial grimacing head jerking. - Vocal Tics Grunting clearing throat repetition of words. - Key Feature Tics are involuntary but can sometimes be voluntarily suppressed temporarily. Learning Disorders Slide 35 Learning disorders are a specific category of school dysfunction where a child exhibits a discrepancy between their intellectual ability and their academic performance. Unlike Intellectual Disability children with varying forms of Learning Disorders have a normal overall IQ but experience isolated academic skill deficits that result in achievement scores substantially below their expected age or intelligence level. - Presentation - Poor academic performance. Often not detected until older grades as the child compensates by memorizing early on. Eventually leads to failing grades behavioral acting out due to frustration or spending excessive amounts of time on homework. - Symptoms must be present for at least 6 months despite interventions. - Risk Factors Prematurity family history and preschool developmental problems strong association with prior speech/language delayed children . - Categories - Reading Learning Disability Dyslexia Most common 80% of learning disorders . Issues with word recognition decoding or comprehension. - Writing Learning Disability. - Math Learning Disability. - Workup - Ensure difficulties are NOT due to hearing/vision issues intellectual disability motor disability or inadequate educational instruction. - Detailed school history grades teacher concerns IEP presence homework length. - Psychoeducational testing accomplished by schools or psychologists combines cognitive assessment and achievement testing. Behavior Problems ODD and Conduct Disorder Slide 40 Behavior disorders often present as significant school problems and frequently co-occur with underlying learning disorders or psychosocial stressors. Diagnosing these requires obtaining a comprehensive multi-source history parents teachers child and assessing if the behaviors are disproportionate for the child s developmental stage. correlate Oppositional Defiant Disorder ODD - Presentation A pattern of behavior characterized by trouble with authority breaking rules argumentativeness and non-compliance. Typically presents before age 8 and no later than early adolescence. - Duration Symptoms must be present for at least 6 months. - Lecturer s Point Primarily defiance and rule-breaking lacking the physical aggression and norm-violating destruction seen in Conduct Disorder. correlate Conduct Disorder CD - Presentation Severe behavior that violates the rights of others or major societal norms. Characterized by aggression towards others/animals destruction of property setting fires and breaking laws . - Duration/Onset Can be child-onset <10 years or adolescent-onset >10 but <16 . Symptoms usually present for at least 6 months with 3 or more incidents in the past year. - Risk Factors Male sex higher prevalence neglect psychological mistreatment witnessing domestic violence or experiencing physical discipline. - Lecturer s Point Conduct disorder is distinguished from ODD by the presence of true aggression and destruction of property. - Management of Behavior Problems - Parent Behavior Management Teaches techniques and strategies to reinforce good behaviors and use appropriate consequences. Consistency is key. - Cognitive Behavior Therapy Problem-solving skills. - Medications Used as an adjunct to behavioral therapies for severely aggressive behaviors or to treat comorbid conditions e.g. using stimulants if the underlying trigger is untreated ADHD . What defines a global developmental delay? A delay that involves at least two or more developmental domains motor speech cognitive social . Concept What historical finding in a child s development is ALWAYS considered a red flag requiring immediate evaluation? Loss of developmental milestones. Concept What is the mandatory first step in the workup of any child presenting with a speech or language delay? Formal audiology testing to assess for hearing loss. Clinical How much of a 3-year-old s speech should be intelligible to a stranger? 75%. 50% by age 2 and 100% by age 4 . Concept What distinguishes Social Pragmatic Communication Disorder from Autism Spectrum Disorder ASD ? Children with Social Pragmatic Communication Disorder have a social drive and desire interaction but lack the communication skills whereas ASD is characterized by a lack of social interest/drive. Clinical What is the most common known genetic cause of Autism Spectrum Disorder and Intellectual Disability? Fragile X syndrome. Glossary What are the two core dimensions of deficits required for a diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Disorder? 1 Deficits in social communication/interaction and 2 restrictive or repetitive behaviors/interests. Clinical What is the key difference between an Intellectual Disability and a Learning Disorder regarding IQ? Intellectual Disability features a reduced overall IQ <70 whereas Learning Disorders occur in children with a normal range of intelligence. Concept How is Tourette Syndrome definitively differentiated from Persistent/Chronic Tic Disorder? Tourette Syndrome requires BOTH multiple motor tics AND at least one vocal tic. Chronic Tic Disorder is either motor OR vocal but not both. Clinical What is the most common type of learning disability? Reading Learning Disability Dyslexia accounting for 80% of specific learning disorders. Concept What is the primary difference in presentation between Oppositional Defiant Disorder ODD and Conduct Disorder? ODD involves defiance non-compliance and argumentativeness. Conduct Disorder involves severe violations of societal norms including aggression towards people/animals setting fires and destruction of property. Clinical questions A 3-year-old boy is brought to the clinic by his parents because he cannot be understood when speaking to unfamiliar relatives at family gatherings. The parents state the child follows complex instructions at home and eagerly points to toys to share them with his parents. On exam the child interacts playfully with the physician but his speech is only 50% intelligible. Formal audiological testing is normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? A. Autism Spectrum Disorder B. Social Pragmatic Communication Disorder C. Intellectual Disability D. Speech Sound Disorder E. Language Disorder D. Speech Sound Disorder Speech Sound Disorder is characterized by persistent difficulty with speech and sound articulation/production that interferes with speech intelligibility. A 3-year-old should be 75% intelligible to strangers; being only 50% intelligible indicates an articulation disorder. His normal receptive language joint attention pointing/sharing and sociability rule out Autism Intellectual Disability and Language Disorder. A 24-month-old boy is evaluated for delayed speech. His mother notes he only says the word no . He also displays poor eye contact does not respond to a name and throws severe tantrums when his daily routine of lining up cars is interrupted. He has no dysmorphic features. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management? A. Await the results of a formal multidisciplinary autism evaluation before initiating therapy B. Reassure the mother that delayed speech is common in boys C. Refer for formal audiological testing and initiate speech/behavioral therapies immediately D. Obtain a chromosomal microarray to rule out Fragile X syndrome E. Prescribe a trial of an SSRI for his restrictive behaviors C. Refer for formal audiological testing and initiate speech/behavioral therapies immediately This child exhibits core features of Autism Spectrum Disorder ASD including social deficits and restrictive/repetitive behaviors along with a speech delay. Crucially any child with a speech delay must undergo formal audiological testing to rule out hearing loss. Furthermore management steps such as early intervention speech therapy or ABA should be initiated as soon as possible and never delayed while awaiting a formal diagnosis from a specialist. An 8-year-old girl is brought to the pediatrician due to difficulties in school over the past 8 months. Her teacher reports she is failing reading and often becomes irritable and disruptive during designated quiet reading time. She has no physical difficulties copying from the board or participating in gym class. Her overall IQ was recently tested at 105. Audiology and vision screens are normal. What is the most likely underlying mechanism for her school dysfunction? A. Deficit in general cognitive ability and adaptive functioning B. Isolated deficit in an academic skill despite normal intelligence C. Impairment in adapting communication to specific social contexts D. An underlying sensory deficit limiting educational input E. A genetic syndrome affecting overall neurodevelopment B. Isolated deficit in an academic skill despite normal intelligence This presentation is consistent with a Learning Disorder specifically reading learning disability or dyslexia . Learning disorders are isolated academic skill deficits that yield poor achievement despite a normal intelligence quotient IQ which she has 105 . They are not due to sensory deficits or intellectual disability which requires low IQ and adaptive deficits choice A . A 12-year-old boy is brought to the clinic by his mother because he has been repeatedly suspended from school. Over the past year he has been involved in multiple physical fights with peers was caught torturing neighborhood stray cats and was recently arrested for intentionally setting a trash can on fire at a local park. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis? A. Oppositional defiant disorder B. Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder C. Tourette syndrome D. Conduct disorder E. Bipolar disorder D. Conduct disorder Conduct disorder is characterized by a severe pattern of behavior that violates the basic rights of others or major societal norms specifically highlighted by aggression toward people or animals destruction of property setting fires and serious rule violations laws . Oppositional defiant disorder involves defiance and non-compliance but lacks physical aggression and extreme destruction. A 9-year-old boy is evaluated for recurrent sudden brief intermittent movements. He has a history of facial grimacing and shoulder shrugging that began at age 7 and has periodically changed over the years. Over the past 14 months he has also developed recurrent episodes of loud involuntary grunting in class. He is able to suppress these sounds for brief periods when prompted by the teacher but ultimately must release them. What is the most likely diagnosis? A. Chronic tic disorder B. Stereotypical movement disorder C. Tourette syndrome D. Developmental coordination disorder E. Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder C. Tourette syndrome Tourette Syndrome is defined by the presence of BOTH multiple motor tics facial grimacing shoulder shrugging AND at least one vocal tic grunting present for at least 1 year. The tics are semi-voluntary in that they feel like an urge that can be temporarily suppressed. Chronic tic disorder requires the presence of EITHER motor OR vocal tics but not both. BandB Psychiatry > Child Psychiatry > Autism Spectrum Disorder BandB Psychiatry > Child Psychiatry > ADHD & Conduct Disorder BandB Psychiatry > Child Psychiatry > Learning Disorders FirstAid Psychiatry > Childhood Disorders Red Flag Milestone Loss A loss of previously acquired developmental milestones is ALWAYS a red flag that requires immediate neurologic and genetic workup. ASD Core Concept Unlike most neurodevelopmental or communicative disorders Social Pragmatic Communication Disorder presents with a desire for social interaction whereas Autism Spectrum Disorder ASD fundamentally lacks an intrinsic social drive. Universal Hearing Assessment Any child presenting with a speech or language delay at any age MUST undergo formal audiological testing regardless of whether they passed their newborn hearing screen. Normal vs Decreased IQ Learning disorders feature isolated academic skill deficits with a NORMAL IQ and normal adaptive functioning. Intellectual Disability requires a DECREASED IQ <70 and deficits in adaptive functional skills. ODD vs CD Oppositional Defiant Disorder ODD is characterized by defiance and argumentativeness without severe aggression. Conduct Disorder CD is defined by severe aggression toward people/animals and overt destruction of property or laws. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l102", "title": "Lecture #102: Pharmacology: CNS Infections", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "l102 Lecture 102 Pharmacology CNS Infections lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. Pharmacology pdf content/L102 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 102 Pharmacology CNS Infections\\n\\n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 102\\n\\nThis lecture covers pharmacological management of CNS infections bacterial viral fungal meningitis . Empiric treatment requires broad-spectrum bactericidal drugs that cross the blood-brain barrier. Key drug classes mechanisms toxicities resistance patterns and adjunctive glucocorticoids are discussed.\\n\\n Antimicrobial Principles Slide 5 \\n- Bactericidal agents kill bacteria — essential for meningitis where host immunity alone is insufficient.\\n- Bacteriostatic agents inhibit growth relying on host defenses. Not preferred as monotherapy for meningitis.\\n- Broad spectrum gram-positive + gram-negative is vital for empiric therapy before pathogen identification.\\n\\n Cell Wall Inhibitors Beta-Lactams Slide 8 \\nAll share a beta-lactam ring; bactericidal via binding PBPs and blocking transpeptidation peptidoglycan cross-linking .\\n\\n Penicillins & Aminopenicillins \\n- Penicillin G/Dicloxacillin narrow-spectrum gram-positive. Dicloxacillin covers MRSA.\\n- Ampicillin broader spectrum; used for Listeria meningitis elderly pregnant immunosuppressed .\\n- Anti-pseudomonal Piperacillin Ticarcillin Carbenicillin.\\n- Adverse effects anaphylaxis pseudomembranous colitis interstitial nephritis hapten reaction with eosinophilia/rash .\\n- Probenecid blocks renal tubular secretion prolonging half-life.\\n\\n Cephalosporins \\n- Third-generation Ceftriaxone Ceftazidime = mainstay for empiric meningitis. Bactericidal broad-spectrum excellent CSF penetration. Fifth-generation Ceftaroline covers MRSA.\\n- 1-2% cross-reactivity with penicillin allergies older agents with similar side chains . Some with methylthiotetrazole ring cause bleeding vitamin K epoxide reductase inhibition and disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol.\\n\\n Carbapenems & Monobactams \\n- Imipenem always given with Cilastatin renal dehydropeptidase inhibitor to prevent toxic metabolite formation and seizure risk.\\n- Aztreonam Gram-negative aerobes only. Safe in true penicillin anaphylaxis no cross-reactive side chains .\\n\\n Beta-Lactam Resistance Slide 10 \\n- Beta-lactamases Cleave the ring. Countered by suicide inhibitors Clavulanic acid Sulbactam Tazobactam — e.g. Augmentin = Amoxicillin + Clavulanic acid.\\n- Mutated PBPs Primary MRSA mechanism.\\n- Porin down-regulation & efflux pumps Gram-negative resistance.\\n\\n Cell Wall Inhibitors Non-Beta-Lactams Slide 16 \\n\\n Vancomycin \\n- Binds D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of peptidoglycan precursors blocking cross-linking. Effective against gram-positives.\\n- Use MRSA meningitis with 3rd-gen cephalosporin ; oral for severe C. difficile .\\n- Toxicity ototoxicity nephrotoxicity caution with aminoglycosides/loop diuretics . Rapid IV infusion causes Red Man Syndrome direct mast cell degranulation releasing histamine — NOT IgE-mediated; treat by slowing infusion + diphenhydramine .\\n- Resistance VRE replaces D-alanine with D-lactate → use Linezolid Daptomycin or Ceftaroline.\\n\\n Cycloserine Blocks alanine racemase L-ala → D-ala . Second-line for MDR-TB. Significant CNS toxicity.\\n\\n Protein Synthesis Inhibitors Slide 23 \\n\\n Aminoglycosides Gentamicin Amikacin Tobramycin \\n- Irreversibly bind 30S subunit. Bactericidal. Post-antibiotic effect allows once-daily dosing.\\n- Neuromuscular blockade Decrease presynaptic ACh release — dangerous in myasthenia gravis or with paralytics.\\n- Ototoxicity Free radical damage to inner ear hair cells; irreversible deafness including fetal .\\n- Nephrotoxicity Proximal tubule accumulation → reversible non-oliguric ATN.\\n\\n Chloramphenicol \\n- Binds 50S subunit inhibits peptidyltransferase. Bacteriostatic broad-spectrum. Extremely lipophilic → excellent BBB penetration brain abscesses Rickettsial meningitis/RMSF .\\n- Bone marrow suppression Dose-dependent reversible anemia OR idiosyncratic irreversible aplastic anemia.\\n- Gray Baby Syndrome Neonates lack UDP-glucuronyl transferase → drug accumulates → cyanosis hypotension shock.\\n\\n Tetracyclines Doxycycline Minocycline Demeclocycline \\n- Bind 30S subunit block aminoacyl-tRNA access.\\n- Doxycycline = DOC for Lyme disease and Rickettsia. Demeclocycline inhibits ADH → treats SIADH.\\n- Chelates Ca2+/Fe2+/antacids → impairs own absorption and deposits in developing bone/teeth contraindicated in pregnancy and children <8 .\\n- Expired tetracyclines degrade into toxins causing Fanconi Syndrome proximal tubule damage . Minocycline causes vestibular toxicity.\\n\\n Fluoroquinolones Slide 32 \\nCiprofloxacin Levofloxacin Moxifloxacin \\ -floxacin\\ . Broad-spectrum bactericidal.\\n- Inhibit DNA gyrase topoisomerase II and topoisomerase IV.\\n- Older agents Cipro = gram-negatives; newer respiratory quinolones Levo Moxi = strong gram-positive coverage.\\n- QT prolongation cardiac K+ channel blockade . Tendon rupture upregulate matrix metalloproteinases; risk heightened in elderly concurrent steroids . Contraindicated in pregnancy and children <18.\\n- Chelation by dietary cations; photosensitivity.\\n\\n Rifampin Slide 18 \\n- Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase .\\n- TB multidrug therapy RIPE 6 months ; prophylaxis for meningococcal contacts .\\n- Strong CYP450 inducer reduces OCP efficacy . Harmless red-orange discoloration of all body fluids.\\n\\n Antiviral Agents Slide 39 \\nMost common viral meningitis Enterovirus supportive care only . HSV-2 requires targeted therapy.\\n\\n- Acyclovir/Valacyclovir Guanosine analogs requiring first phosphorylation by viral thymidine kinase then host kinases → active triphosphate inhibits DNA polymerase. IV acyclovir crystallizes in renal tubules → obstructive nephropathy requires aggressive hydration . Resistance mutated viral TK → use Foscarnet.\\n- Ganciclovir/Valganciclovir 100x more potent against CMV . Dose-limiting toxicity bone marrow suppression neutropenia .\\n- Foscarnet Pyrophosphate analog; directly inhibits viral DNA polymerase without kinase activation salvage for acyclovir-resistant strains . Chelates calcium → hypocalcemia ; renal Mg2+ wasting → arrhythmia risk.\\n- Neuraminidase inhibitors Oseltamivir Zanamivir Block viral neuraminidase preventing influenza virion release.\\n\\n Antifungal Agents Slide 45 \\nFungal meningitis targets immunocompromised patients e.g. Cryptococcus in HIV/AIDS .\\n\\n- Amphotericin B Binds ergosterol in fungal membrane creating pores → ion leak → cell death. Broad-spectrum fungicidal. Toxicity \\ shake and bake\\ infusion reaction premedicate with NSAIDs ; nephrotoxicity binds mammalian cholesterol vasospasms renal arterioles ; K+/Mg2+ wasting → cardiac arrhythmias . Liposomal formulations reduce renal toxicity.\\n- Azoles Fluconazole Itraconazole Voriconazole Block 14-alpha-sterol demethylase lanosterol → ergosterol . Fluconazole has excellent CSF penetration. All inhibit CYP450 → drug interactions. Ketoconazole suppresses androgen synthesis → gynecomastia.\\n- Flucytosine 5-FC Deaminated inside fungal cells to 5-FU → halts pyrimidine synthesis. Often combined with Amphotericin B for synergy. Toxicity bone marrow suppression.\\n\\n Adjunct Glucocorticoid Therapy\\nDexamethasone given with the first antibiotic dose to suppress the inflammatory surge as bacteria lyse. Blocks phospholipase A2 suppressing prostaglandins/leukotrienes. Extended steroid use risks hyperglycemia osteoporosis immunosuppression hypertension.\\n\\n Empirical Therapy Summary\\n\\n| Scenario | Regimen | Rationale |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Empiric | 3rd-gen cephalosporin + Vancomycin | Bactericidal broad-spectrum BBB penetration MRSA coverage |\\n| Immunocompromised/Elderly | Add Ampicillin | Covers Listeria |\\n| Pediatric/Pregnant | Beta-lactams | Avoids bone/teeth toxicity of tetracyclines/fluoroquinolones |\\n| Penicillin Allergy | Moxifloxacin or Aztreonam + Vancomycin | Bypasses beta-lactam cross-reactivity |\\n| Contact Prophylaxis | Rifampin | Meningococcal chemoprophylaxis |\\n\\n highyield\\nEmpiric meningitis treatment 3rd-gen cephalosporin Ceftriaxone + Vancomycin . In elderly pregnant or immunocompromised patients add Ampicillin for Listeria coverage. Adjust to targeted therapy upon culture results.\\n \\n\\n highyield\\nAminoglycosides post-antibiotic effect once-daily dosing but triple threat of ototoxicity irreversible nephrotoxicity ATN and neuromuscular blockade contraindicated with myasthenia gravis or paralytics .\\n \\n\\n mnemonic\\n Protein Synthesis Inhibitors \\n BUY AT 30 CCEL at 50 \\n\\n- AT 30 30S A minoglycosides bactericidal T etracyclines bacteriostatic \\n- CCEL at 50 50S C hloramphenicol C lindamycin E rythromycin Macrolides L inezolid\\n BandB Infectious Disease > Bacteria > Antibiotics SketchyPharm Antimicrobials > Antibiotics SketchyPharm Antimicrobials > Antivirals FirstAid Microbiology > Antimicrobials SketchyPharm Antimicrobials > Antifungals What is the key difference between a bactericidal and bacteriostatic antibiotic? Bactericidal antibiotics kill the bacteria outright while bacteriostatic antibiotics only inhibit their replication forcing reliance on the host immune system. Concept Why is Cilastatin co-administered with Imipenem? Cilastatin inhibits renal dehydropeptidase blocking the drug s degradation in the kidney and avoiding the accumulation of a highly neurotoxic/seizure-inducing metabolite. Pharmacology What unique feature of Aztreonam makes it clinically indispensable? Aztreonam is a monobactam completely devoid of structural cross-reactivity with penicillins; it is safe for patients with a true penicillin anaphylactic allergy. Clinical What is the mechanism of action of Vancomycin? Vancomycin tightly binds to the D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of peptidoglycan precursors heavily blocking cell wall synthesis in gram-positive bacteria. Pharmacology A patient experiences sudden flushing and pruritus after a rapid IV infusion of Vancomycin. What is the mechanism of this response? Red Man Syndrome. It is caused by direct Mast Cell degranulation releasing histamine; it is not an IgE-mediated allergy. Clinical What is the major danger of co-administering Aminoglycosides alongside neuromuscular blockers? Aminoglycosides heavily decrease acetylcholine release from the pre-synaptic terminal worsening or triggering dangerous neuromuscular blockade and profound weakness. Clinical What is the root pathophysiology causing Gray Baby Syndrome in neonates receiving Chloramphenicol? Neonates lack sufficient UDP-glucuronyl transferase in their premature livers making them unable to conjugate and excrete the drug. Pathophysiology Which antibiotic vividly deposits in newly calcifying bone and developing teeth? Tetracyclines. They strongly chelate divalent/trivalent cations like calcium causing permanent tooth discoloration. Pharmacology What is the classic severe connective tissue complication of treating an athlete with Fluoroquinolones? Tendon rupture e.g. Achilles tendon . Fluoroquinolones significantly upregulate matrix metalloproteinases which destroy cartilage. Clinical Patients placed on Rifampin must be warned of what specific harmless side effect? Red-orange discoloration of all bodily secretions prominently including sweat tears and urine. Clinical What is the required initial activation step for Acyclovir inside an infected cell? Acyclovir is a prodrug that strictly requires its first phosphorylation to be performed by the viral Thymidine Kinase. Concept What is the primary dose-limiting toxicity of Ganciclovir when treating CMV? Profound bone marrow suppression commonly manifesting as extreme neutropenia. Clinical What systemic electrolyte abnormalities does Foscarnet classically induce? It dangerously chelates calcium causing powerful hypocalcemia and aggressive renal wasting of magnesium. Clinical What is the mechanism of action of Amphotericin B? It binds specifically to ergosterol in the fungal membrane violently tearing physical pores that leak out essential cellular ions killing the cell. Pharmacology match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best Vancomycin Toxicity Mechanism Red Man Syndrome is caused by rapid infusion leading to direct mast cell degranulation of histamine not an IgE-mediated anaphylactic allergy. Premedicate with diphenhydramine and infuse slowly. Empiric Meningitis Regimen A third-generation cephalosporin Ceftriaxone plus Vancomycin is the gold standard for empiric treatment. If the patient is elderly pregnant or immunocompromised Ampicillin must be added to cover Listeria. Acyclovir Crystal Nephropathy Intravenous acyclovir has a propensity to precipitate and crystallize inside the renal tubules. Aggressive pre-and-post-hydration with normal saline is mandatory to physically flush the drug out and stop obstructive kidney injury. Amphotericin Cardiotoxicity While classically nephrotoxic Amphotericin creates membrane pores that leak large amounts of K+ and Mg2+. This massive electrolyte wasting directly triggers severe and deadly cardiac arrhythmias. questions 1 A 68-year-old male is hospitalized for severe pneumonia and is placed on empiric antibiotics. During his stay he develops generalized muscle weakness and difficulty breathing. Review of his medical history indicates he has well-controlled myasthenia gravis. His symptoms rapidly worsened shortly after the administration of an antibiotic designed to target an aerobic gram-negative pathogen. Which of the following is the mechanism of the antibiotic most likely responsible for his sudden deterioration? Inhibition of cell wall transpeptidation Bacteriostatic inhibition of the 50S ribosomal subunit Irreversible binding to the 30S ribosomal subunit Inhibition of bacterial DNA gyrase Inhibition of 14-alpha-sterol demethylase 3 The patient received an aminoglycoside e.g. Gentamicin or Tobramycin to treat a gram-negative infection. Aminoglycosides bind irreversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit to become bactericidal. However they carry a dangerous side effect of decreasing acetylcholine release from the pre-synaptic nerve terminal exacerbating neuromuscular blockade and precipitating acute crises in patients with myasthenia gravis. 2 A 23-year-old college student presents to the student health clinic reporting that his urine and tears have turned a persistent bright red-orange color. He strongly denies any dysuria flank pain or visual disturbances. Two days ago his roommate was rapidly hospitalized for Neisseria meningitidis. Which of the following most accurately describes the drug this patient was given for prophylaxis? It selectively inhibits viral thymidine kinase It aggressively inhibits the synthesis of ergosterol It inhibits RNA polymerase and strongly induces CYP450 It competitively inhibits dihydrofolate reductase It binds D-alanyl-D-alanine to disrupt cell wall synthesis 3 The patient is taking Rifampin as a prophylactic measure against meningococcal disease spreading in his dorm. Rifampin works by inhibiting bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase. It classically secretes a harmless red-orange coloration in bodily fluids. Furthermore it is a formidable inducer of hepatic CYP450 enzymes. 3 A 35-year-old pregnant woman in her 34th week of gestation presents with nuchal rigidity high fever and photophobia. Lumbar puncture confirms bacterial meningitis. Her history is significant for a recorded life-threatening anaphylactic reaction to amoxicillin. Seeking to prevent structural cartilage toxicity or tooth discoloration in the fetus the physician opts out of fluoroquinolones and tetracyclines. The pathogen is subsequently identified as a strictly aerobic gram-negative rod. Which of the following beta-lactam antibiotics is safest to administer? Cefazolin Piperacillin Ceftriaxone Aztreonam Imipenem-Cilastatin 4 Aztreonam is a monobactam structurally designed solely for gram-negative aerobes. It crucially lacks the standard immunogenic side-chains inherent in penicillins and cephalosporins. It is universally considered safe to give to patients bearing a true documented anaphylactic penicillin allergy. 4 A 55-year-old severely immunocompromised HIV patient is aggressively treated with intravenous medications for confirmed Cytomegalovirus CMV retinitis. Three weeks into continuous therapy routine laboratory testing reveals a critically depressed absolute neutrophil count ANC of 300 cells/mm\\u00b3 Normal 1500-8000 alongside anemia and thrombocytopenia. The treating physician pauses the current regimen replacing it with Foscarnet. What enzyme does the initially prescribed drug utilize to achieve its active form before inhibiting DNA polymerase? Host cellular tyrosine kinase Bacterial DNA gyrase Host hepatic UDP-glucuronyl transferase Viral neuraminidase Viral thymidine kinase / UL97 kinase 5 The patient was experiencing severe bone marrow suppression caused by Ganciclovir. Ganciclovir is highly potent against CMV but requires initial activation. Depending on the herpesvirus strain like CMV it is preferentially phosphorylated by viral-specific enzymes like UL97 kinase or thymidine kinase prior to final activation by host kinases. Since Foscarnet doesn t need kinase activation it was utilized as a substitute. 5 An 81-year-old woman with a history of recurrent cystitis is treated with repeated courses of expired oral antibiotics she kept in her medical cabinet. She presents to the clinic lethargic passing profound volumes of dilute urine and displaying glucosuria alongside hypokalemia despite having a normal serum glucose. Which of the following antimicrobial classes did she most likely consume leading to her current condition? Aminoglycosides Tetracyclines Cephalosporins Macrolides Fluoroquinolones 2 The patient is presenting with Fanconi Syndrome a severe toxic form of proximal renal tubular dysfunction resulting in the mass wasting of glucose amino acids and bicarbonate into the urine. This is a classic adverse complication resulting specifically from the ingestion of outdated or chemically degraded tetracyclines."}, {"id": "l103", "title": "Lecture #103: Pathology of Skeletal Muscle", "module": "Pathology", "content": "l103 Lecture 103 Pathology of Skeletal Muscle lecturer W. Zaloga D.O. Pathology pdf content/L103 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 103 Pathology of Skeletal Muscle\\n\\n Lecturer W. Zaloga D.O.\\n Session Lecture 103\\n\\nThis lecture covers skeletal muscle pathology normal muscle structure inherited disorders muscular dystrophies channelopathies congenital myopathies metabolic and mitochondrial myopathies inflammatory myopathies and autoimmune neuromuscular junction disorders. A key theme is distinguishing neurogenic from myopathic patterns on biopsy.\\n\\n Normal Muscle Structure and the Sarcomere Slide 3 \\nSkeletal muscle fibers are multinucleated cells with peripherally located nuclei beneath the sarcolemma. The sarcomere is the contractile unit A-band myosin dark I-band actin light H-band and Z-disc. Creatine kinase CK at the M-line catalyzes ADP + phosphocreatine to ATP + creatine providing a rapid ATP reserve. When muscle is damaged CK is released into blood serum CK = clinical marker of muscle injury .\\n\\n Myofiber Types Slide 5 \\nType 1 and Type 2 fibers normally interdigitate in a checkerboard pattern on NADH-TR stain.\\n\\n| Feature | Type 1 Slow Twitch | Type 2 Fast Twitch |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Metabolism | Oxidative phosphorylation | Anaerobic glycolysis |\\n| Mitochondria | High | Low |\\n| Fatigue Resistance | High endurance | Low rapid fatigue |\\n| Function | Posture sustained | Sprinting resistance |\\n| NADH-TR Stain | Dark | Lighter with speckling |\\n\\n highyield\\nThe normal checkerboard pattern is lost in neurogenic injury. When denervated fibers are reinnervated by sprouting from adjacent healthy motor neurons large groups of the same fiber type cluster together fiber-type grouping .\\n \\n\\n Myogenic vs. Neurogenic Injury Slide 10 \\n\\n- Myopathic Segmental degeneration/regeneration of individual fibers scattered not grouped . Elevated serum CK. Regenerating fibers show central nucleation and basophilic staining. Chronic fibrosis and fatty infiltration.\\n- Neurogenic Grouped atrophy of angular flattened fibers. Adjacent healthy motor neurons sprout to reinnervate producing fiber-type grouping. Surrounding fibers may hypertrophy.\\n\\n highyield\\nNeurogenic = groups of angular atrophic fibers denervation . Myopathic = random mixture of necrotic regenerating hypertrophied and atrophic fibers with increased central nucleation.\\n \\n\\n Spinal Muscular Atrophy Type 1 Werdnig-Hoffmann Slide 19 \\nAutosomal recessive disorder SMN gene causing progressive motor neuron loss and skeletal muscle denervation. SMA1 presents in infancy with generalized hypotonia floppy infant within 4 months; death by age 2-3 from respiratory failure. Biopsy shows grouped denervation atrophy neurogenic pattern with fiber-type grouping.\\n\\n Muscular Dystrophies Duchenne and Becker Slide 20 \\nBoth are X-linked recessive mutations in the dystrophin gene. Dystrophin connects the actin cytoskeleton to the ECM via a glycoprotein complex; without it contraction damages the sarcolemma.\\n\\n- Duchenne DMD Dystrophin completely absent frameshift/nonsense mutations . Onset ages 2-5. Proximal weakness pelvic girdle first . Pseudohypertrophy of calves replaced by fat/fibrous tissue . Gower s sign climbing up own legs to stand . Elevated CK. Cardiomyopathy mild intellectual impairment. Death from respiratory insufficiency in 20s-30s . Biopsy segmental degeneration fiber size variation progressive fatty/fibrous replacement.\\n- Becker BMD Dystrophin partially functional in-frame deletions . Later onset slower progression near-normal lifespan.\\n\\n Myotonic Dystrophy Slide 25 \\nAutosomal dominant trinucleotide repeat disorder affecting myotonin protein kinase causing chloride channel dysfunction. Chloride channels are essential for relaxation; dysfunction causes myotonia inability to relax after contraction . Hallmark grip myotonia cannot release a sustained grip . Onset 20-40 years. Progressive wasting cardiac arrhythmias elevated CPK.\\n\\n Ion Channel Myopathies Channelopathies Slide 26 \\n- Calcium channel mutations Most common cause of hypokalemic periodic paralysis.\\n- Chloride channel mutations Myotonic dystrophy.\\n- Ryanodine receptor mutations Central core disease malignant hyperthermia.\\n\\n Malignant Hyperthermia Slide 28 \\nAutosomal dominant ryanodine receptor RyR mutation. Triggered by halogenated inhaled anesthetics halothane sevoflurane or succinylcholine. Uncontrolled calcium release from SR causes severe muscle tetany and hyperthermia >106 F . Treatment dantrolene RyR antagonist blocking calcium release .\\n\\n Congenital Myopathies Slide 32 \\n- Central Core Disease RyR mutation; hypotonia at birth; biopsy shows central cores of reduced enzyme activity.\\n- Nemaline Myopathy AD or AR; thin filament protein mutations; floppy baby syndrome; biopsy shows nemaline rods on Gomori trichrome dark spindle-shaped particles beneath sarcolemma .\\n\\n Metabolic Myopathies\\n\\n| Disease | Enzyme Deficiency | Key Features | Trigger |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| CPT2 Deficiency | Carnitine palmitoyltransferase 2 | Toxic fatty acid buildup hypotonia dilated cardiomyopathy | Exercise/fasting |\\n| McArdle Disease Type 5 | Myophosphorylase | Painful cramps myoglobinuria flat lactate curve normal glucose | Exercise |\\n| Pompe Disease Type 2 | Acid alpha-glucosidase | Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy severe hypotonia in infants | Progressive |\\n\\n Mitochondrial Myopathies Slide 39 \\nMitochondrial DNA mutations with maternal inheritance . Impaired ATP generation affects high-demand tissues muscle heart neurons . Features muscle weakness elevated CK cardiac dysrhythmias seizures chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia CPEO ptosis diplopia .\\n\\n MERRF Myoclonic Epilepsy with Ragged Red Fibers Progressive myoclonus generalized seizures limb-girdle weakness. Biopsy hallmark ragged red fibers on Gomori trichrome sub-sarcolemmal aggregates of abnormal mitochondria .\\n\\n Leber s Hereditary Optic Neuropathy Primarily adult males; CPEO and visual loss.\\n\\n Inflammatory Myopathies\\n\\n Dermatomyositis Slide 46 \\nImmune-mediated attack on muscle capillaries complement + CD4+ T cells in perimysial distribution causing ischemic myocyte necrosis. Peak 50-60 female predominance.\\n\\n- Skin Heliotrope rash lilac eyelid discoloration + periorbital edema Gottron s papules knuckles/elbows/knees mechanic s hands.\\n- Symmetric proximal weakness dysphagia possible. 24% have associated malignancy. \\n- Biopsy perifascicular atrophy perimysial CD4+ infiltration complement in capillaries.\\n- Antibodies Anti-MI2 skin Anti-JO1 lung/arthritis . Treatment immunosuppressives.\\n\\n Polymyositis Slide 50 \\nSimilar to dermatomyositis but no skin involvement . CD8+ cytotoxic T-cells directly attack individual fibers in endomysial distribution. Peak 30-60 female predominance. Proximal weakness elevated CK.\\n\\n| Feature | Dermatomyositis | Polymyositis |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Skin | Heliotrope Gottron s | None |\\n| Inflammation | Perimysial CD4+ | Endomysial CD8+ |\\n| Vascular Injury | Yes complement | No |\\n| Biopsy | Perifascicular atrophy | Scattered CD8+ infiltration |\\n| Malignancy | 24% | Lower |\\n\\n Inclusion Body Myositis Slide 53 \\nMost common inflammatory myopathy in patients over 68 . Affects distal muscles first unlike DM/PM can be asymmetric. Biopsy inclusion bodies degraded organelles some CD8+ infiltration vacuolization. cN1A antibody positive in ~50%. Poorly responsive to immunosuppressives.\\n\\n Myasthenia Gravis Slide 56 \\nAutoantibodies against postsynaptic AChR reduce receptor density. Painless fatigable weakness worsening with exertion improving with rest. Diplopia/ptosis ocular muscles first . Thymic abnormalities thymoma 10% thymic hyperplasia 30% young patients . EMG decremental response. Treatment AChE inhibitors also diagnostic plasmapheresis immunosuppressives thymectomy.\\n\\n Lambert-Eaton Syndrome Slide 57 \\nAutoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels prevent ACh release. Weakness improves with exertion opposite of MG . Autonomic symptoms dry mouth constipation . 50% have associated malignancy small cell lung CA . EMG incremental response.\\n\\n| Feature | Myasthenia Gravis | Lambert-Eaton |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Target | Postsynaptic AChR | Presynaptic Ca2+ channel |\\n| Weakness | Worsens with use | Improves with use |\\n| EMG | Decremental | Incremental |\\n| Associations | Thymoma | 50% malignancy SCLC |\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Myasthenia vs. Lambert-Eaton \\n MY-asthenia = MY muscles get WEAKER with use \\n- MG Antibodies destroy AChR post-synaptic . More use = more fatigue = worse.\\n- Lambert-Eaton Antibodies block Ca2+ channels pre-synaptic . More use = more Ca2+ accumulates = improves.\\n BandB Musculoskeletal > Muscle Disorders Pathoma Musculoskeletal > Skeletal Muscle Pathology FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Muscular Dystrophies SketchyPath Musculoskeletal > Neuromuscular Junction Disorders Physeo Pathology > Musculoskeletal > Myopathies SketchyBiochem Metabolism > Carbohydrates > Glycogen Storage Diseases What is the function of creatine kinase CK at the M-line of the sarcomere? CK catalyzes the reversible conversion of ADP + phosphocreatine to ATP + creatine serving as a rapid ATP reserve for the muscle. Concept What is fiber-type grouping and what does it indicate? Fiber-type grouping is the loss of the normal checkerboard pattern of Type 1 and Type 2 fibers replaced by large clusters of the same type. It indicates neurogenic injury with denervation followed by reinnervation via axonal sprouting. Concept What is the inheritance pattern and protein defect in Duchenne muscular dystrophy? X-linked recessive with complete absence of dystrophin a subsarcolemmal protein connecting actin to the extracellular matrix. Clinical What is Gower s sign? A maneuver where a child with proximal muscle weakness climbs up their own legs with their hands to stand from the floor classically seen in Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Clinical What is pseudohypertrophy of the calf muscles? Calf muscles appear enlarged despite being weak because functional muscle tissue is progressively replaced by fat and fibrous tissue seen in DMD. Clinical What is the most common cause of death in Duchenne muscular dystrophy? Respiratory insufficiency typically in the 20s-30s as the diaphragm and respiratory muscles progressively fail. Clinical What ion channel is affected in myotonic dystrophy and what is the hallmark sign? Chloride channels are affected leading to myotonia grip myotonia inability to relax a sustained grip . Clinical What is the treatment for malignant hyperthermia? Dantrolene a ryanodine receptor antagonist that blocks uncontrolled calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Clinical What triggers malignant hyperthermia? Halogenated inhaled anesthetics halothane sevoflurane and succinylcholine in patients with inherited ryanodine receptor mutations. Clinical How does McArdle disease differ from Pompe disease? McArdle Type 5 involves myophosphorylase deficiency with episodic painful cramps myoglobinuria and normal glucose. Pompe Type 2 involves lysosomal acid maltase deficiency with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and severe hypotonia. Clinical What is the diagnostic hallmark of MERRF syndrome on muscle biopsy? Ragged red fibers on modified Gomori trichrome stain representing sub-sarcolemmal accumulations of abnormal mitochondria. Clinical How do you distinguish dermatomyositis from polymyositis on biopsy? Dermatomyositis shows perimysial inflammation with CD4+ cells complement in capillaries and perifascicular atrophy. Polymyositis shows endomysial inflammation with CD8+ cytotoxic T-cells scattered around individual fibers. Clinical How does weakness differ between myasthenia gravis and Lambert-Eaton syndrome? In myasthenia gravis weakness worsens with repeated exertion. In Lambert-Eaton weakness improves with exertion. Clinical What percentage of dermatomyositis patients have an associated malignancy? 24%. Clinical match Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy DMD resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Muscular Dystrophies; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Muscle Disorders; SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > Muscular Dystrophies match Becker Muscular Dystrophy resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Muscular Dystrophies > Becker; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Muscle Disorders match Myasthenia Gravis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Myasthenia Gravis; SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > NMJ Disorders; Physeo > Pathology > Musculoskeletal match Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Lambert Eaton; SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > NMJ Disorders match Dermatomyositis and Polymyositis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Inflammatory Myopathies; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > Inflammatory Myopathies; BandB > Musculoskeletal match Malignant Hyperthermia resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Malignant Hyperthermia; SketchyPharm > Anesthetics > Inhaled; BandB > Musculoskeletal match McArdle Disease Type 5 Glycogen Storage Disease resources AnKing > FirstAid > Biochemistry > Metabolism > Glycogen Storage Diseases > Type 5; SketchyBiochem > Metabolism > Carbohydrates > GSD; Pixorize > Biochem > GSD > McArdle match Pompe Disease Type 2 Glycogen Storage Disease resources AnKing > FirstAid > Biochemistry > Metabolism > Glycogen Storage Diseases > Type 2; SketchyBiochem > Metabolism > Carbohydrates > GSD; Physeo > Biochem > Glycogen match Myotonic Dystrophy resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Myotonic Dystrophy; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Muscle Disorders match Mitochondrial Myopathies MERRF resources AnKing > FirstAid > Biochemistry > Metabolism > Mitochondrial Diseases; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal; BandB > Genetics > Mitochondrial Inheritance match Spinal Muscular Atrophy Werdnig-Hoffmann resources AnKing > FirstAid > Neurology > Pathology > Motor Neuron Diseases; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Muscle Disorders Neurogenic vs. Myopathic Biopsy Pattern Neurogenic injury shows grouped angular atrophic fibers denervation with eventual fiber-type grouping. Myopathic injury shows a random mixture of necrotic regenerating hypertrophied and atrophic fibers with increased central nucleation. Duchenne vs. Becker Dystrophin Duchenne = dystrophin completely ABSENT severe early onset death by 20s-30s . Becker = dystrophin partially FUNCTIONAL milder later onset near-normal lifespan . Malignant Hyperthermia Treatment Dantrolene is the specific antidote for malignant hyperthermia. It is a ryanodine receptor antagonist that blocks the uncontrolled calcium efflux causing the hypermetabolic crisis. McArdle Disease Lactate Test In McArdle disease the venous lactate curve is flat during exercise does not rise while ammonia still rises. This is because glycogen cannot be broken down to feed into glycolysis. Dermatomyositis vs. Polymyositis Inflammation Dermatomyositis = perimysial inflammation CD4+ complement with perifascicular atrophy. Polymyositis = endomysial inflammation CD8+ scattered among individual fibers. Myasthenia Gravis vs. Lambert-Eaton MG post-synaptic AChR antibodies weakness worsens with use associated with thymoma. Lambert-Eaton pre-synaptic Ca2+ channel antibodies weakness improves with use 50% have malignancy small cell lung CA . Ragged Red Fibers Ragged red fibers on Gomori trichrome stain are the hallmark of mitochondrial myopathies especially MERRF . They represent sub-sarcolemmal accumulations of abnormal mitochondria. Grip Myotonia Grip myotonia inability to release a sustained grip is the hallmark sign of myotonic dystrophy caused by chloride channel dysfunction from a trinucleotide repeat expansion. questions 1 A 4-year-old boy presents with progressive difficulty walking and frequent falls. Physical examination reveals enlarged calf muscles that feel firm on palpation and proximal muscle weakness. He uses his hands to push off his thighs when rising from the floor. Serum creatine kinase is markedly elevated. Which of the following is the most likely eventual cause of death in this patient? Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy Respiratory insufficiency Rhabdomyolysis-induced renal failure Central nervous system degeneration Malignant hyperthermia 2 This patient presents with Duchenne muscular dystrophy DMD evidenced by early childhood onset of proximal weakness pseudohypertrophy of the calves Gower s sign and markedly elevated CK. The most common cause of death in DMD is respiratory insufficiency in the 20s-30s as the diaphragm and respiratory muscles progressively fail. While cardiomyopathy is also common respiratory failure is the leading cause of death. 2 A 25-year-old male undergoes an appendectomy under general anesthesia. Shortly after induction with a halogenated inhaled anesthetic and administration of succinylcholine for intubation he develops severe muscle rigidity tachycardia tachypnea and a rapidly rising body temperature of 107 degrees F. Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacological intervention? Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors Intravenous calcium gluconate Dantrolene Propranolol Dexamethasone 3 This patient is experiencing malignant hyperthermia a life-threatening condition caused by an inherited autosomal dominant mutation in the ryanodine receptor. Halogenated anesthetics and succinylcholine trigger uncontrolled calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum causing severe tetany and a hypermetabolic state. Dantrolene is the specific antidote; it is a ryanodine receptor antagonist that blocks calcium release. 3 A 55-year-old woman presents with symmetric proximal muscle weakness a violaceous discoloration of her eyelids with periorbital edema and scaling erythematous papules over her knuckles. Muscle biopsy shows perifascicular atrophy with perimysial inflammatory infiltrate. Which of the following cell types predominates in the inflammatory infiltrate? CD8+ cytotoxic T-cells CD4+ T-helper cells Natural killer cells B lymphocytes Neutrophils 2 This patient presents with dermatomyositis characterized by the heliotrope rash violaceous eyelid discoloration with edema Gottron s papules scaling eruptions over the knuckles and proximal muscle weakness. The perimysial inflammatory infiltrate in dermatomyositis is predominantly composed of CD4+ T-helper cells with complement deposition in capillaries in contrast to polymyositis which features endomysial CD8+ cytotoxic T-cells. 4 A 35-year-old female presents with proximal muscle weakness that has gradually worsened over six months. She reports difficulty washing her hair and climbing stairs. She has no skin findings. Muscle biopsy reveals endomysial lymphocytic inflammation surrounding individual muscle fibers with the inflammatory cells staining positive for CD8. Serum creatine kinase is elevated. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Dermatomyositis Polymyositis Inclusion body myositis Duchenne muscular dystrophy Myasthenia gravis 2 This patient presents with proximal muscle weakness without skin involvement and the biopsy shows endomysial inflammation with CD8+ T-cells which is the classic histologic pattern of polymyositis. Dermatomyositis would show perimysial inflammation with CD4+ cells and skin findings. Inclusion body myositis presents in patients over 68 with distal muscle weakness. DMD presents in childhood. MG is a neuromuscular junction disorder without biopsy inflammation. 5 A 28-year-old female presents with new onset diplopia and ptosis that worsens throughout the day. Electromyography demonstrates a decremental response with repetitive nerve stimulation. CT of the chest reveals an anterior mediastinal mass. Which of the following best describes the underlying pathophysiology? Autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels Autoantibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors Mutation in the ryanodine receptor causing uncontrolled calcium release Immune-mediated endomysial CD8+ T-cell infiltration Trinucleotide repeat expansion affecting chloride channels 2 This patient has myasthenia gravis an autoimmune disorder where autoantibodies destroy postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction. The weakness worsens with exertion diplopia/ptosis worse at end of day EMG shows decremental response and the anterior mediastinal mass is a thymoma which is associated with MG in ~10% of patients. Lambert-Eaton presynaptic Ca2+ channel antibodies would show improving weakness with use."}, {"id": "l104", "title": "Lecture #104: Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 1", "module": "Pathology", "content": "l104 Lecture 104 Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 1 lecturer E. Petcu M.D. Pathology pdf content/L104 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 104 Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 1\\n\\n Lecturer E. Petcu M.D.\\n Session Lecture 104\\n\\nThis lecture surveys the major pathologic conditions of bones and joints osteoarthritis OA vs. rheumatoid arthritis RA juvenile idiopathic arthritis JIA skeletal dysplasias achondroplasia thanatophoric dysplasia osteogenesis imperfecta osteoporosis osteopetrosis osteomalacia/rickets Paget disease and osteonecrosis.\\n\\n Osteoarthritis Slides 2-7 \\nOA is the most common joint disease a mechanical/degenerative process with secondary not primary inflammation. Progressive wear causes loss of chondrocytes degradation of hyaline cartilage ECM and compensatory chondrocyte clustering that cannot keep pace with destruction. Subchondral bone scleroses eburnation osteophytes form at joint margins subchondral cysts develop and loose cartilage/bone fragments joint mice enter the joint space Slide 4 .\\n\\n- Pain worsens with activity improves with rest.\\n- Asymmetric weight-bearing joints hips knees spine and hands.\\n- Heberden nodes DIP osteophytes and Bouchard nodes PIP osteophytes .\\n- Crepitus; no systemic symptoms; non-inflammatory aspirate; no autoantibodies.\\n- Radiographs joint space narrowing osteophytes subchondral sclerosis/cysts.\\n\\n Rheumatoid Arthritis Slides 8-16 \\nRA is the most common autoimmune arthritis driven by CD4+ T cells reacting to an unknown arthritogenic antigen. About 50% of risk is linked to HLA-DR4. Environmental factors smoking infections promote citrullination of self-proteins creating neoantigens Slide 9 .\\n\\n- Pathogenesis CD4+ T cells release cytokines. IFN-gamma Th1 activates macrophages; IL-17 Th17 recruits neutrophils/monocytes; RANKL stimulates osteoclast bone resorption; TNF/IL-1 drive synovial protease release destroying cartilage. Anti-TNF biologics target this pathway.\\n- Histology Synovium shows germinal centers plasma cells and pannus edematous synovium + inflammatory cells + granulation tissue growing over and eroding cartilage . Progresses to fibrous then bony ankylosis.\\n- Autoantibodies Anti-CCP most specific ~70% of patients and RF IgM against IgG Fc ~80% less specific .\\n- Clinical Symmetric small joints hands/feet DIP spared morning stiffness systemic symptoms fever fatigue weight loss . Hand findings ulnar deviation radial deviation of wrist swan neck/boutonniere deformities Slide 13 . Lab elevated ESR CRP positive RF/anti-CCP decreased complement in joint fluid.\\n- Extra-articular Rheumatoid nodules necrotizing granulomas with fibrinoid necrosis and palisading macrophages; pulmonary nodules = Caplan syndrome vasculitis uveitis/scleritis interstitial lung disease pericarditis anemia of chronic disease Felty syndrome RA + neutropenia + splenomegaly .\\n\\n highyield\\n Osteoarthritis vs. Rheumatoid Arthritis is one of the most tested comparisons in musculoskeletal pathology \\n- OA Mechanical/degenerative asymmetric weight-bearing joints DIP involvement Heberden nodes worse with activity no systemic symptoms non-inflammatory aspirate.\\n- RA Autoimmune CD4+ T cells symmetric small joints of hands/feet DIP spared worse in the morning systemic symptoms anti-CCP/RF positive pannus formation.\\n \\n\\n| Feature | Osteoarthritis | Rheumatoid Arthritis |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Mechanism | Mechanical/degenerative | Autoimmune CD4+ T cells |\\n| Inflammation | Secondary minimal | Primary severe |\\n| Symmetry | Asymmetric | Symmetric |\\n| DIP Joints | Involved Heberden nodes | Spared |\\n| Pain Pattern | Worse with activity | Worse in the morning |\\n| Systemic | None | Fever fatigue weight loss |\\n| Pathology | Osteophytes eburnation joint mice | Pannus erosions ankylosis |\\n| Lab | Non-inflammatory aspirate | Anti-CCP RF elevated ESR/CRP |\\n| Extra-articular | None | Nodules vasculitis Felty syndrome |\\n\\n Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis Slide 16 \\nJIA is a heterogeneous group of disorders developing before age 16 lasting at least 6 weeks with unknown etiology possible HLA/PTPN22 associations . Classic presentation daily spiking fevers salmon-pink macular rash polyarthritis 2+ joints anterior uveitis. No rheumatoid nodules. Labs show leukocytosis thrombocytosis anemia positive ANA elevated ESR/CRP. About 10% develop serious disability. Treatment NSAIDs steroids methotrexate TNF inhibitors.\\n\\n correlate\\nJuvenile Idiopathic Arthritis\\n\\n- Presentation Daily spiking fevers salmon-pink macular rash polyarthritis anterior uveitis; no rheumatoid nodules.\\n- Diagnosis Age <16 arthritis 6+ weeks ANA positive elevated ESR/CRP.\\n- Lecturer s Point Salmon-pink rash with spiking fevers = hallmark of systemic JIA Still disease .\\n \\n\\n Achondroplasia Slides 17-19 \\nThe most common skeletal dysplasia and cause of dwarfism. Autosomal dominant gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3 which normally inhibits endochondral bone growth. The activating mutation exaggerates this inhibition selectively impairing long bone growth while intramembranous ossification skull facial bones clavicle remains normal Slide 18 .\\n\\n- Rhizomelic shortening proximal limbs normal trunk length frontal bossing depressed nasal bridge trident hand narrow foramen magnum lordosis genu varum.\\n- Normal lifespan intelligence reproductive status. 85% sporadic; risk increases with advanced paternal age.\\n\\n Thanatophoric dysplasia is the most common lethal form of dwarfism 1 in 20 000 . Also FGFR3 gain-of-function different mutation . Underdeveloped thoracic cavity causes respiratory insufficiency and death at birth.\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Achondroplasia = FGFR3 Gain-of-Function \\nFGFR3 normally puts the brakes on bone growth. Activating mutation = too much braking = short bones.\\nEndochondral = affected. Intramembranous = normal.\\n \\n\\n Osteogenesis Imperfecta Slides 20-21 \\nOI brittle bone disease is the most common inherited disorder of connective tissue. Mutations in COL1A1/COL1A2 cause defective type 1 collagen synthesis. Bone forms but in insufficient quantity Slide 20 .\\n\\n- Multiple fractures from minimal trauma may mimic child abuse .\\n- Blue sclera decreased collagen makes sclera translucent revealing underlying choroidal veins.\\n- Hearing loss sensorineural + conductive from middle/inner ear bone abnormalities.\\n- Dentinogenesis imperfecta small misshapen blue-yellow teeth dentin deficiency .\\n- Type 1 normal lifespan fractures decrease after puberty. Type 2 fatal in utero/perinatally.\\n\\n correlate\\nOsteogenesis Imperfecta\\n\\n- Pathophysiology AD mutations in COL1A1/COL1A2 cause defective type 1 collagen. Bone forms but too little of it.\\n- Classic Triad Fractures from minimal trauma + blue sclera + dentinogenesis imperfecta.\\n- Lecturer s Point Blue sclera = translucent collagen-deficient sclera revealing choroidal veins NOT a pigmentation change.\\n \\n\\n Osteoporosis Slides 22-26 \\nDecreased bone mass severe enough to increase fracture risk. DEXA T-score <= -2.5 defines osteoporosis. Male-to-female ratio 1 4. Two main forms \\n\\n- Senile Reduced osteoblast proliferative/biosynthetic capacity + decreased physical activity.\\n- Postmenopausal Estrogen loss increases inflammatory cytokines IL-6 TNF IL-1 increases RANKL decreases OPG stimulating osteoclast activity. Resorption dominates. Up to 2% cortical and 9% cancellous bone lost per year. Vertebral bodies most affected.\\n\\nFracture sites vertebral body most common; compression fractures causing height loss/kyphosis femoral neck highest mortality distal radius. Plain X-ray detects only after 30-40% bone loss. DEXA recommended for all women over 65. Treatment calcium/vitamin D weight-bearing exercise bisphosphonates.\\n\\n highyield\\n DEXA Scan T-Score Thresholds \\n- T >= -1.0 Normal\\n- T between -1.0 and -2.5 Osteopenia\\n- T <= -2.5 Osteoporosis\\n\\nMost common fracture sites vertebral body most common femoral neck highest mortality distal radius .\\n \\n\\n Osteopetrosis Slides 27-29 \\nRare genetic diseases with reduced bone resorption due to defective osteoclast function causing diffuse symmetric skeletal sclerosis. Despite the name petra = stone bones are brittle because they consist of unremodeled woven bone not organized lamellar bone Slide 27 .\\n\\n- Classic mutation carbonic anhydrase 2 CA2 preventing acidification of the resorption pit.\\n- Bone fills marrow space causing pancytopenia and extramedullary hematopoiesis hepatosplenomegaly .\\n- Imaging bone-in-bone pattern Erlenmeyer flask deformity flared metaphyses narrow neural foramina cranial nerve palsies deafness .\\n- Severe infantile form AR ; milder adult form AD .\\n- Treatment bone marrow transplant provides functional osteoclast precursors .\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Osteopetrosis vs. Osteoporosis \\n- Osteo petrosis = too MUCH bone but brittle/woven . Osteoclasts DON T WORK. Marrow crowded out = pancytopenia.\\n- Osteo porosis = too LITTLE bone but normal quality . Osteoclasts work TOO WELL. Marrow preserved.\\n \\n\\n Osteomalacia and Rickets Slides 30-31 \\nDefective mineralization from vitamin D deficiency low sunlight dietary deficiency kidney disease . Osteomalacia = adults osteoid not mineralized ; rickets = children growth plates not mineralized . US incidence ~1 in 1 000 due to dairy fortification.\\n\\n- Labs Low vitamin D low calcium high PTH low phosphate high alkaline phosphatase hyperactive osteoblasts compensating .\\n- Osteomalacia Looser zones insufficiency fractures at scapula ribs ulna .\\n- Rickets Epiphyseal widening metaphyseal cupping/fraying genu varum bow legs rachitic rosary beading at costochondral junctions .\\n- Treatment Vitamin D >1 000 IU/day.\\n\\n Paget Disease of Bone Slides 34-40 \\nDisordered bone remodeling with increased but structurally abnormal bone mass. Affects ~1% of US adults over 40. SQSTM1 gene mutations 50% familial 10% sporadic increase NF-kB/osteoclast activity. Possible measles virus contribution.\\n\\n- Three phases 1 Osteolytic bone resorption 2 Mixed resorption + formation; osteoclasts with up to 100 nuclei 3 Osteosclerotic/burnout osteoblast-dominant . All three may coexist.\\n- Histologic hallmark Mosaic jigsaw puzzle pattern of lamellar bone with prominent cement lines.\\n- Often asymptomatic. Monostotic ~50% or polyostotic. Axial skeleton/proximal femur in ~80%.\\n- Skull thickening with hearing loss picture-frame vertebrae anterior bowing of femur/tibia chalk-stick fractures high-output heart failure AV shunts leontiasis ossea sarcomatous transformation <1% .\\n- Lab Elevated alkaline phosphatase; calcium phosphorus PTH all NORMAL.\\n- Treatment Calcitonin bisphosphonates.\\n\\n correlate\\nPaget Disease of Bone\\n\\n- Key Lab Elevated alk phos with NORMAL calcium/phosphorus/PTH distinguishes from hyperparathyroidism and osteomalacia .\\n- Histology Mosaic jigsaw puzzle pattern is pathognomonic.\\n- Complications High-output heart failure secondary OA sarcoma <1% .\\n \\n\\n Osteonecrosis Avascular Necrosis Slides 40-41 \\nIschemic death of bone and marrow. Most common causes fractures and corticosteroid use. Most common site femoral head medial circumflex femoral artery . Bilateral in 80% of cases. Other risk factors alcohol bisphosphonates connective tissue disease sickle cell crisis Gaucher disease decompression sickness.\\n\\n- Medullary infarcts geographic trabecular bone + marrow vs. subchondral infarcts wedge-shaped subchondral plate . Overlying cartilage survives via synovial fluid nutrition.\\n- Histology empty lacunae + necrotic adipocytes. Subchondral collapse leads to secondary OA. BandB Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders Pathoma Musculoskeletal > Bone Pathology FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Bone Disorders SketchyPath Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders Physeo Pathology > Musculoskeletal > Bone SketchyBiochem Metabolism > Vitamins > Vitamin D What is the key pathologic difference between osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis? OA is a mechanical/degenerative process with secondary minimal inflammation. RA is a primary autoimmune disease driven by CD4+ T cells with severe chronic inflammation pannus formation and cartilage/bone destruction. Concept What are Heberden nodes and Bouchard nodes and which arthritis are they associated with? Heberden nodes are osteophytes at DIP joints OA only . Bouchard nodes are osteophytes at PIP joints seen in both OA and RA . DIP involvement helps distinguish OA from RA which spares the DIP joints. Clinical What is pannus and in which disease is it found? Pannus is a mass of edematous synovium inflammatory cells granulation tissue and fibroblasts that grows over and erodes articular cartilage. It is the hallmark pathologic finding in rheumatoid arthritis. Concept Which autoantibody is the most specific and sensitive serologic marker for rheumatoid arthritis? Anti-citrullinated peptide antibody anti-CCP detected in up to 70% of RA patients. It is more specific than rheumatoid factor RF . Clinical What is the genetic basis of achondroplasia and which type of ossification is affected? Achondroplasia is caused by an autosomal dominant gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3. It affects endochondral ossification long bone growth while intramembranous ossification skull facial bones clavicle is unaffected. Concept What causes the blue sclera in osteogenesis imperfecta? Decreased type 1 collagen content makes the sclera translucent allowing partial visualization of the underlying choroidal veins giving a blue appearance. Clinical What are the DEXA scan T-score thresholds for osteoporosis osteopenia and normal bone? T-score <= -2.5 = osteoporosis. T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 = osteopenia. T-score >= -1.0 = normal bone density. Clinical What mechanism drives postmenopausal osteoporosis? Estrogen deficiency increases inflammatory cytokines IL-6 TNF IL-1 which increase RANKL and decrease OPG stimulating osteoclast recruitment and activity. Resorption dominates over formation. Concept What is the underlying defect in osteopetrosis and why are the bones brittle despite being dense? Defective osteoclast function often due to carbonic anhydrase 2 mutation prevents bone resorption. Bones are dense but brittle because they consist of unremodeled woven bone rather than organized lamellar bone. Concept What is the treatment for osteopetrosis? Bone marrow transplant which provides hematopoietic precursors for functional osteoclasts. Clinical What is the histologic hallmark of Paget disease of bone? A mosaic jigsaw puzzle-like pattern of lamellar bone produced by unusually prominent cement lines joining haphazardly oriented units of lamellar bone. Concept What laboratory finding distinguishes Paget disease from osteomalacia/rickets? In Paget disease alkaline phosphatase is elevated but calcium phosphorus and PTH are NORMAL. In osteomalacia/rickets alkaline phosphatase is elevated AND calcium is low PTH is high phosphate is low and vitamin D is low. Clinical What are the classic clinical features of rickets in children? Epiphyseal widening metaphyseal cupping and fraying genu varum bow legs and rachitic rosary palpable beading at costochondral junctions from unmineralized osteoid deposition . Clinical What are the two most common causes of osteonecrosis avascular necrosis ? Fractures disrupting blood supply and corticosteroid administration. The most common site is the femoral head. Clinical What is Caplan syndrome? The presence of rheumatoid nodules in the lungs seen in patients with rheumatoid arthritis especially coal miners with concurrent pneumoconiosis . Clinical match Osteoarthritis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Osteoarthritis; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > Joint Pathology match Rheumatoid Arthritis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Rheumatoid Arthritis; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > Joint Disorders match Achondroplasia resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Achondroplasia; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal match Osteogenesis Imperfecta resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Osteogenesis Imperfecta; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > Bone match Osteoporosis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Osteoporosis; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > Metabolic Bone Disease match Osteopetrosis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Osteopetrosis; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal match Rickets and Osteomalacia resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Rickets; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; SketchyBiochem > Vitamins > Vitamin D match Paget Disease of Bone resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Paget Disease; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > Paget Disease match Osteonecrosis Avascular Necrosis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Avascular Necrosis; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders match Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders OA vs. RA DIP Involvement DIP joints are involved in osteoarthritis Heberden nodes but SPARED in rheumatoid arthritis. The presence or absence of DIP involvement is a high-yield distinguishing feature. Anti-CCP vs. Rheumatoid Factor Anti-CCP is more SPECIFIC than RF for rheumatoid arthritis. RF can be positive in many other conditions SLE Sjogren hepatitis C . Anti-CCP is detected in ~70% and RF in ~80% of RA patients. Achondroplasia Endochondral vs. Intramembranous FGFR3 gain-of-function inhibits endochondral ossification long bones shortened but intramembranous ossification is unaffected skull and facial bones normal . This is why patients have a large head relative to shortened limbs. OI Blue Sclera Mechanism Blue sclera in osteogenesis imperfecta results from decreased type 1 collagen making the sclera translucent revealing underlying choroidal veins. This is NOT a pigmentation change. Osteoporosis DEXA Scores T-score <= -2.5 = osteoporosis. Between -1 and -2.5 = osteopenia. >= -1 = normal. DEXA recommended for all women over 65. Plain X-ray only detects osteoporosis after 30-40% of bone mass is lost. Osteopetrosis Dense but Brittle Despite the name implying stone-like hardness osteopetrotic bones are BRITTLE and fracture-prone because they consist of unremodeled woven bone not organized lamellar bone. Treatment is bone marrow transplant. Paget Disease Lab Values Elevated alkaline phosphatase with NORMAL calcium phosphorus and PTH distinguishes Paget disease from hyperparathyroidism and osteomalacia. The mosaic jigsaw puzzle pattern is the histologic hallmark. AVN Femoral Head and Bilateral The most common site for avascular necrosis is the femoral head medial circumflex femoral artery injury . 80% of cases are bilateral. Most common causes fractures and corticosteroid use. questions 1 A 65-year-old woman presents with pain in her right knee that worsens throughout the day and improves with rest. Physical examination reveals crepitus with range of motion and bony enlargement of the distal interphalangeal joints bilaterally. Radiographs show joint space narrowing subchondral sclerosis and osteophyte formation. Synovial fluid analysis reveals a non-inflammatory aspirate. Which of the following best describes the underlying pathologic process in this patient s condition? Autoimmune destruction of articular cartilage by pannus formation Mechanical wear and degradation of articular cartilage with chondrocyte clustering and subchondral changes Deposition of monosodium urate crystals in the joint space Immune complex deposition in the synovial membrane Infection of the joint space with purulent exudate 2 This patient presents with osteoarthritis OA evidenced by pain that worsens with activity Heberden nodes DIP osteophytes crepitus non-inflammatory aspirate and radiographic findings of joint space narrowing with osteophytes and subchondral sclerosis. OA is a mechanical/degenerative process with progressive cartilage destruction chondrocyte clustering eburnation and osteophyte formation not a primary autoimmune or inflammatory process. 2 A 42-year-old woman presents with symmetric swelling and pain in her metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints that is worst in the morning and improves throughout the day. She also reports fatigue and low-grade fevers. Laboratory studies show a positive anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide anti-CCP antibody and elevated ESR. A biopsy of the synovium would most likely show which of the following? Monosodium urate crystal deposition with neutrophilic infiltration Chondrocyte clusters with subchondral cyst formation Pannus composed of edematous synovium granulation tissue and inflammatory cells growing over articular cartilage Caseous granulomas with Langhans giant cells Fibrinoid necrosis of vessel walls with neutrophilic infiltration 3 This patient has rheumatoid arthritis RA characterized by symmetric small joint involvement morning stiffness systemic symptoms and positive anti-CCP antibody. The hallmark histologic finding in RA is pannus formation a mass of edematous synovium inflammatory cells granulation tissue and fibroblasts that grows over and erodes articular cartilage. Anti-CCP is the most specific serologic marker for RA. 3 A newborn is found to have disproportionately shortened proximal extremities with a normal-length trunk frontal bossing and a depressed nasal bridge. Genetic testing reveals a gain-of-function mutation in the FGFR3 gene. Which of the following types of bone formation is primarily affected in this condition? Intramembranous ossification Endochondral ossification Appositional bone growth Periosteal bone formation Both intramembranous and endochondral ossification equally 2 This infant has achondroplasia the most common skeletal dysplasia and cause of dwarfism. It is caused by an autosomal dominant gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3 that inhibits endochondral ossification long bone growth while intramembranous ossification skull facial bones clavicle is unaffected. This is why patients have rhizomelic limb shortening with a relatively large head and normal trunk. 4 A 70-year-old man is found to have an elevated serum alkaline phosphatase on routine bloodwork. Calcium phosphorus and PTH levels are all within normal limits. A skull radiograph shows thickened calvarium. A bone biopsy demonstrates lamellar bone arranged in a mosaic pattern with prominent cement lines. Which of the following is the most serious potential complication of this patient s condition? Osteomalacia Osteomyelitis High-output heart failure Hypercalcemia with renal stones Pathologic fracture from osteoporosis 3 This patient has Paget disease of bone characterized by elevated alkaline phosphatase with normal calcium phosphorus and PTH skull thickening and the mosaic jigsaw puzzle pattern of lamellar bone on biopsy. The most serious complication is high-output heart failure which results from arteriovenous shunts created in the hypervascular pagetic bone. Other complications include chalk-stick fractures hearing loss and sarcomatous transformation <1% . 5 A 3-year-old child is brought to the emergency department with a femoral shaft fracture after a minor fall. The parents report this is the child s fourth fracture in the past year. Physical examination reveals blue sclera and small misshapen teeth with a blue-yellow discoloration. Which of the following is the most likely underlying defect? Deficiency of osteoclast function due to carbonic anhydrase 2 mutation Gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3 affecting endochondral ossification Defective type 1 collagen synthesis due to COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutations Vitamin D deficiency causing impaired mineralization of osteoid SQSTM1 mutation causing disordered bone remodeling 3 This child has osteogenesis imperfecta brittle bone disease the most common inherited disorder of connective tissue. It is caused by mutations in COL1A1 or COL1A2 type 1 collagen genes leading to defective collagen synthesis. The classic triad includes multiple fractures from minimal trauma blue sclera translucent collagen-deficient sclera revealing choroidal veins and dentinogenesis imperfecta small misshapen blue-yellow teeth . Type 1 OI has a normal lifespan with decreasing fracture frequency after puberty."}, {"id": "l105", "title": "Lecture #105: Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 2", "module": "Pathology", "content": "l105 Lecture 105 Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 2 lecturer E. Petcu M.D. Pathology pdf content/L105 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 105 Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 2\\n\\n Lecturer E. Petcu M.D. | Session Lecture 105\\n\\nContinuation of L104 . This lecture covers fractures osteomyelitis seronegative spondyloarthropathies Lyme arthritis fibrous dysplasia bone cysts and both benign and malignant bone tumors. The key theme patient age lesion location and radiographic appearance are the strongest diagnostic clues.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Fractures and Fracture Repair\\n\\n Types Simple closed skin intact compound open communicates with skin comminuted multiple fragments displaced ends not aligned stress repetitive loading greenstick incomplete in infants and pathological through weakened bone e.g. tumor .\\n\\n Repair sequence Hematoma fibrin mesh + growth factors PDGF/TGF-beta/FGF activate osteoprogenitor cells --> Soft callus pro-callus provides anchorage but no rigidity --> Hard callus woven bone trabeculae weeks 2-3 --> Endochondral ossification mineralization weight-bearing --> Remodeling restoration of medullary cavity . Inadequate immobilization --> non-union --> cystic degeneration --> pseudoarthrosis false joint lined by synovial-like cells .\\n\\n highyield\\nFracture repair Hematoma --> Soft Callus --> Hard Callus --> Endochondral Ossification --> Remodeling. Non-union leads to pseudoarthrosis.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Osteomyelitis\\n\\nInflammation of bone and marrow. S. aureus causes 80-90% of pyogenic cases. E. coli/Pseudomonas/Klebsiella in GU infections; H. influenzae/Group B Strep in neonates; Salmonella in sickle cell disease . Organisms reach bone hematogenously by contiguous spread or direct implantation.\\n\\n Acute stage Neutrophil recruitment --> bone necrosis within 48 hours --> subperiosteal abscess --> periosteal lifting impairs blood supply. Key terms Dead bone = sequestrum ; reactive new bone shell = involucrum ; draining sinus = cloaca indicates chronicity . Chronic osteomyelitis in 5-25% of cases. X-ray lytic focus surrounded by sclerosis.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies\\n\\nDefined by RF-negative enthesopathy not synovitis sacroiliac involvement HLA-B27 association bony ankylosis.\\n\\n Ankylosing spondylitis Bony ankylosis of sacroiliac and vertebral joints. Lower back pain + spinal immobility in 2nd-3rd decades. 90% HLA-B27 positive.\\n\\n Reactive Arthritis Reiter Triad of arthritis + urethritis + conjunctivitis . Triggered by Salmonella/Shigella/Campylobacter GI or Chlamydia trachomatis STI . Acute asymmetric oligoarthritis of lower extremities. RF-negative HLA-B27 positive. 50% recur; active disease beyond 6 months = chronic.\\n\\n Lyme Arthritis Borrelia burgdorferi via Ixodes ticks. Three phases 1 Early localized erythema migrans bull s-eye rash round red expanding non-pruritic ; 2 Early disseminated cranial nerves heart meninges; 3 Late disseminated migratory arthritis in 80% untreated cases large joints 1-2 at a time . Arthritis occurs in less than 10% overall because most are treated early. Treatment doxycycline/amoxicillin/cefuroxime axetil 90%+ cure .\\n\\n---\\n\\n Fibrous Dysplasia\\n\\nBenign condition normal bone components present but fail to mature. Caused by somatic GNAS1 gain-of-function mutations constitutively active Gs protein . Histology curvilinear woven bone trabeculae \\ Chinese letters\\ without osteoblastic rimming.\\n\\n| Form | Features |\\n|---|---|\\n| Monostotic | Adolescence; femur/tibia/ribs/jaw; often asymptomatic |\\n| Polyostotic | Earlier age; craniofacial involvement; crippling deformities; rarely transforms to sarcoma |\\n| McCune-Albright | Polyostotic unilateral + cafe-au-lait spots ipsilateral + precocious puberty + endocrinopathies |\\n\\n---\\n\\n Bone Cysts\\n\\n- Simple unicameral Fluid-filled fibrous-lined first 2 decades near growth plate. Not a neoplasm. May fracture if large.\\n- Aneurysmal ABC Blood-filled multiloculated. Adolescence; femur/tibia/vertebra. X-ray expansive lytic lesion with sclerotic eggshell rim. 70% have USP6 rearrangements on 17p13. Benign but locally aggressive; 10-50% recurrence.\\n\\n Ganglion cysts Small near wrist joints no cell lining. Baker cyst Popliteal synovial cyst associated with RA.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Bone Tumors Overview\\n\\nPrimary bone tumors are rare and outnumbered by metastases. Most occur in the first 3 decades at the distal femur/proximal tibia. Age is the most important diagnostic clue. Painful lesions suggest malignancy.\\n\\n Benign Tumors\\n\\n| Feature | Osteoid Osteoma | Osteoblastoma |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Size | Less than 2 cm | Greater than 2 cm |\\n| Location | Long bones | Spine |\\n| Pain | Nocturnal PGE2 ; relieved by aspirin/NSAIDs | NOT nocturnal; NOT aspirin-responsive |\\n| Treatment | Radiofrequency ablation | Curettage/en bloc excision |\\n\\n highyield\\nOsteoid osteoma less than 2 cm nocturnal pain relieved by aspirin PGE2 . Osteoblastoma greater than 2 cm spine pain NOT relieved by aspirin.\\n \\n\\n Osteochondroma exostosis Most common benign bone tumor. Bony stalk + cartilage cap. Metaphysis near growth plate knee . 85% solitary. Men 3 1.\\n\\n Enchondroma Benign hyaline cartilage tumor in medullary cavity. Ages 20-50. IDH1/IDH2 mutations. Ollier disease unilateral enchondromas 25-30% chondrosarcoma risk and Maffucci syndrome enchondromas + spindle cell hemangiomas glioma risk .\\n\\n Giant cell tumor osteoclastoma Epiphysis near knee. X-ray soap-bubble appearance . Giant cells with 100+ nuclei. 4% metastasize to lungs; 40-60% local recurrence.\\n\\n Malignant Tumors\\n\\n Osteosarcoma Most common primary malignant bone tumor 20% of bone cancers . Bimodal 75% before age 20 growth spurt ; older adults with Paget/radiation. Metaphysis near knee. X-ray sunburst pattern or Codman triangle . Diagnosis requires malignant cells producing osteoid. Genetics RB 75% sporadic TP53 Li-Fraumeni CDKN2A. All patients assumed to have occult metastasis. 5-year survival less than 20% with mets.\\n\\n Ewing sarcoma Second most common pediatric bone sarcoma. 80% under 20. Caucasian predilection. Diaphysis of long bones/pelvis. Mimics infection fever elevated ESR leukocytosis . X-ray onion-skin periosteal reaction . Histology small round blue cells forming Homer Wright rosettes . t 11;22 EWSR1-FLI1 fusion in 90%+. Treatment neoadjuvant chemo + surgery + adjuvant chemo; 75% 5-year survival.\\n\\n Chondrosarcoma Malignant cartilage tumor. Age 40s+ men 2 1. Axial skeleton pelvis/shoulder/ribs . Grade 1 80-90% 5-year survival. Grade 3 43% survival 70% metastasize to lungs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Metastatic Bone Tumors\\n\\nFar more common than primary tumors. Adults prostate blastic breast kidney lung more than 75% . Kidney/lung/GI/melanoma = lytic . Breast = mixed . Children neuroblastoma Wilms osteosarcoma Ewing rhabdomyosarcoma.\\n\\nTumor cells secrete PTHrP/prostaglandins --> upregulate RANKL --> stimulate osteoclast-driven resorption --> released growth factors TGF-beta IGF-1 feed back to support tumor growth vicious cycle . Treatment bisphosphonates zoledronic acid analgesics radiotherapy.\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Primary Malignant Bone Tumors by Age OEC \\n O steosarcoma - under 20 metaphysis sunburst/Codman\\n E wing - children diaphysis onion-skin t 11;22 \\n C hondrosarcoma - adults 40+ axial skeleton\\n Pathoma Musculoskeletal Pathology > Bone Tumors BandB Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders SketchyPath Musculoskeletal & Derm > Primary Bone Tumors FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Primary Bone Tumors Bootcamp Musculoskeletal > Bone Tumors What is the sequence of fracture repair? Hematoma formation soft callus pro-callus hard callus bony callus with woven bone endochondral ossification and remodeling with restoration of the medullary cavity. Concept What is a pseudoarthrosis? A false joint that forms when persistent fracture non-union leads to cystic degeneration of the malformed callus with the luminal surface becoming lined by synovial-like cells. Concept What organism causes osteomyelitis in patients with sickle cell disease? Salmonella. Sickle cell disease predisposes patients to Salmonella osteomyelitis S. aureus remains most common overall at 80-90% . Clinical What are the sequestrum involucrum and cloaca in osteomyelitis? Sequestrum = dead bone; Involucrum = living shell of reactive new bone surrounding the infected dead bone; Cloaca = draining sinus tract through which dead bone fragments are released indicating chronicity. Concept What is the classic triad of Reactive Arthritis Reiter Syndrome ? Arthritis + non-gonococcal urethritis or cervicitis + conjunctivitis. It is seronegative RF-negative and often HLA-B27 positive. Clinical What is the pathognomonic rash of Lyme disease? Erythema migrans a bull s-eye rash that is round red at least 2 inches across slowly expanding warm to touch and non-pruritic/non-painful. Clinical What gene mutation causes fibrous dysplasia? Somatic gain-of-function mutations in GNAS1 which encodes the stimulatory alpha subunit of Gs protein leading to constitutively active Gs signaling. Concept How does osteoid osteoma pain differ from osteoblastoma pain? Osteoid osteoma causes nocturnal bone pain due to excess PGE2 from proliferating osteoblasts and this pain is relieved by aspirin/NSAIDs. Osteoblastoma pain is NOT nocturnal and NOT relieved by aspirin/NSAIDs. Clinical What is the most common primary malignant tumor of bone and what are its hallmark radiographic findings? Osteosarcoma 20% of all bone cancers . Hallmark X-ray findings include the sunburst pattern and Codman triangle both indicating aggressive periosteal reaction. Clinical What translocation defines Ewing sarcoma and what is its characteristic histologic and radiographic appearance? Balanced t 11;22 creating EWSR1-FLI1 fusion gene. Histology sheets of small round blue cells forming Homer Wright rosettes. X-ray destructive lytic lesion with onion-skin periosteal reaction. Clinical What is the X-ray appearance of a giant cell tumor osteoclastoma ? A soap-bubble appearance in the epiphysis most commonly near the knee distal femur or proximal tibia . Clinical Which cancers most commonly metastasize to bone in adults? Prostate blastic breast kidney and lung carcinomas account for more than 75% of skeletal metastases. Kidney lung GI and melanoma produce lytic lesions; prostate is predominantly blastic. Clinical What are Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome? Both are non-hereditary disorders characterized by multiple enchondromas. Ollier disease enchondromas predominantly on one side of the body 25-30% risk of chondrosarcoma by age 40 . Maffucci syndrome multiple enchondromas + spindle cell hemangiomas risk of brain gliomas and other malignancies . Clinical match Osteomyelitis resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > search osteomyelitis sequestrum involucrum match Fracture repair and healing resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB > Musculoskeletal > search fracture repair callus match Osteosarcoma Codman triangle sunburst pattern resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma / SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > search osteosarcoma codman sunburst match Ewing sarcoma onion-skin Homer Wright rosettes t 11;22 resources AnKing Step 1 > SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > search ewing sarcoma onion skin EWSR1 match Giant cell tumor osteoclastoma soap bubble resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > search giant cell tumor soap bubble epiphysis match Ankylosing spondylitis HLA-B27 resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma / BandB > MSK > search ankylosing spondylitis HLA-B27 match Reactive Arthritis / Reiter Syndrome resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search reactive arthritis reiter conjunctivitis urethritis match Lyme disease Borrelia erythema migrans resources AnKing Step 1 > SketchyMicro > Spirochetes > search borrelia lyme erythema migrans match Fibrous dysplasia GNAS1 McCune-Albright resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search fibrous dysplasia GNAS1 cafe au lait match Osteoid osteoma vs Osteoblastoma resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > search osteoid osteoma nocturnal aspirin osteoblastoma match Osteochondroma / Enchondroma / Chondrosarcoma resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma / SketchyPath > MSK > search osteochondroma enchondroma chondrosarcoma ollier maffucci match Bone metastases RANKL PTHrP lytic vs blastic resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search bone metastasis prostate blastic lytic RANKL match Sickle cell and Salmonella osteomyelitis resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK / Heme > search sickle cell salmonella osteomyelitis Osteoid Osteoma vs. Osteoblastoma Both are benign bone-forming tumors in young males. The key distinction osteoid osteoma is less than 2 cm causes nocturnal pain relieved by aspirin PGE2-mediated and arises in long bones. Osteoblastoma is greater than 2 cm pain is NOT nocturnal and NOT aspirin-responsive and arises in the spine. Sickle Cell + Salmonella Patients with sickle cell disease are uniquely predisposed to Salmonella osteomyelitis. S. aureus remains the most common overall cause of osteomyelitis 80-90% but Salmonella is the classic association with sickle cell. Osteosarcoma Radiology Two hallmark X-ray findings the sunburst pattern stretched-out bone fibers from rapid growth and the Codman triangle triangular periosteal shadow . Both indicate an aggressive bone-forming tumor. This is the most common primary malignant bone tumor peaking in adolescence. Ewing Sarcoma Mimics Infection Ewing sarcoma can present with fever elevated ESR anemia and leukocytosis mimicking osteomyelitis. The onion-skin periosteal reaction on X-ray and small round blue cells with Homer Wright rosettes on histology clinch the diagnosis. Bone Metastases Lytic vs. Blastic Prostate = blastic bone-forming . Kidney lung GI melanoma = lytic bone-destroying . Breast = mixed. Tumor cells do NOT directly resorb bone; they secrete PTHrP and prostaglandins to upregulate RANKL on osteoblasts indirectly driving osteoclast activity. McCune-Albright Syndrome Triad Polyostotic fibrous dysplasia unilateral + cafe-au-lait spots ipsilateral coast of Maine borders + precocious puberty and other endocrinopathies hyperthyroidism GH-secreting pituitary adenomas . Caused by GNAS1 gain-of-function mutation. questions 1 A 14-year-old boy presents with a 3-week history of progressive right knee pain and swelling. X-ray of the distal femur reveals a destructive mass with a mixed lytic-blastic pattern periosteal elevation forming a triangular shadow and a sunburst pattern of radiating bone spicules. Biopsy shows pleomorphic spindle cells producing pink amorphous osteoid. Which of the following genetic mutations is most commonly found in the sporadic form of this neoplasm? EWSR1-FLI1 fusion gene RB gene mutation GNAS1 gain-of-function mutation IDH1 heterozygous mutation TP53 somatic deletion 2 The clinical presentation adolescent distal femur destructive mass with sunburst pattern and Codman triangle malignant osteoid production is classic for osteosarcoma. RB mutations are found in 75% of sporadic osteosarcoma cases. EWSR1-FLI1 is Ewing sarcoma; GNAS1 is fibrous dysplasia; IDH1 is enchondroma/chondrosarcoma. 2 A 10-year-old African American girl with known sickle cell disease presents with fever left tibial tenderness and an elevated ESR. Blood cultures grow a gram-negative motile rod. X-ray of the tibia shows a focal lytic area of bone destruction surrounded by periosteal elevation. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism? Staphylococcus aureus Pseudomonas aeruginosa Group B Streptococcus Salmonella species Escherichia coli 4 While S. aureus is the most common cause of osteomyelitis overall 80-90% patients with sickle cell disease are specifically predisposed to Salmonella osteomyelitis due to functional asplenia and intestinal ischemia. The gram-negative motile rod description fits Salmonella. 3 A 22-year-old man presents with low back pain bilateral sacroiliac joint tenderness and limited spinal mobility. He is rheumatoid factor negative and HLA-B27 positive. His symptoms started gradually and worsened over the past year. X-ray shows bilateral sacroiliac joint fusion. Which of the following best describes the pathological changes underlying this condition? Pannus formation with destruction of articular cartilage in synovial joints Crystal deposition causing neutrophilic inflammation in joint spaces Destruction of articular cartilage and bony ankylosis at ligamentous attachment sites Immune complex deposition causing vasculitis of joint vasculature Granulomatous inflammation of the synovium with non-caseating granulomas 3 This is ankylosing spondylitis a seronegative spondyloarthropathy. It is characterized by pathological changes in ligamentous attachments enthesopathy not synovium with destruction of articular cartilage and bony ankylosis especially of sacroiliac and vertebral apophyseal joints. Pannus formation is rheumatoid arthritis; crystal deposition is gout. 4 A 16-year-old Caucasian boy presents with a 2-month history of a painful enlarging mass on his left femoral diaphysis. He has had intermittent fevers and an elevated ESR. X-ray reveals a permeative lytic lesion with concentric layers of reactive bone deposited in a layered pattern around the cortex. Biopsy shows sheets of uniform small round cells with scant cytoplasm arranged in rosette patterns. What is the most likely cytogenetic finding? Amplification of N-MYC oncogene Deletion of chromosome 13q14 RB gene Translocation t 11;22 producing EWSR1-FLI1 fusion Trisomy of chromosomes 3 7 and 17 Rearrangement of chromosome 17p13 involving USP6 3 The clinical picture Caucasian adolescent boy diaphyseal location fever mimicking infection onion-skin periosteal reaction small round blue cells forming Homer Wright rosettes is classic for Ewing sarcoma. The balanced t 11;22 translocation creating the EWSR1-FLI1 fusion gene is found in more than 90% of cases. 5 A 19-year-old male athlete presents with progressive right leg pain that is worse at night and is completely relieved by taking ibuprofen. X-ray of the tibial cortex reveals a small less than 2 cm radiolucent nidus surrounded by a thick rim of reactive sclerotic bone. Histology shows randomly interconnected trabeculae of woven bone rimmed by osteoblasts within a vascularized fibrous stroma. Which of the following best explains the pathogenesis of his nocturnal pain? Periosteal stretching from an enlarging soft tissue mass Pathological fracture through weakened cortical bone Excessive prostaglandin E2 production by proliferating osteoblasts Neoplastic invasion of periosteal sensory nerve fibers Compression of adjacent neurovascular structures by expanding cyst 3 This is osteoid osteoma a benign bone tumor less than 2 cm classically producing nocturnal bone pain that is relieved by aspirin/NSAIDs. The pain mechanism is excess prostaglandin E2 PGE2 secreted by proliferating osteoblasts within the nidus which sensitizes pain receptors. NSAIDs block PGE2 synthesis explaining pain relief."}, {"id": "l106", "title": "Lecture #106: Pathology of Soft Tissue", "module": "Pathology", "content": "l106 Lecture 106 Pathology of Soft Tissue lecturer W. Zaloga D.O. Pathology pdf content/L106 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 106 Pathology of Soft Tissue\\n\\n Lecturer W. Zaloga D.O. | Session Lecture 106\\n\\nSoft tissue tumors arise from mesenchymal tissue -- adipose fibrous skeletal muscle smooth muscle and tissues of uncertain origin. These are rare overall but commonly tested because each tumor has characteristic histologic and clinical features that serve as diagnostic anchors. This lecture organizes tumors by cell of origin and moves from benign to malignant for each lineage. For every tumor three diagnostic pillars matter most demographics age/sex location and histologic pattern . Sarcomas are mesenchymal malignancies; they are invasive unencapsulated hemorrhagic/necrotic from outstripping their blood supply and show high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic N C ratios with hyperchromatic nuclei.\\n\\n---\\n\\n General Principles of Soft Tissue Tumors Slides 4-6 \\n\\nSoft tissue tumors may be benign suffix -oma or malignant suffix -sarcoma . Sarcomas are rare constituting less than 1% of all cancers but are disproportionately important on boards. Risk factors discussed include chronic inflammation prior irradiation and pre-existing benign tumors that can undergo malignant transformation.\\n\\n- Benign features mobile painless well-encapsulated mature-appearing cells cured by simple excision\\n- Malignant features fixed painful invasive unencapsulated or pseudo-encapsulated from compression hemorrhagic/necrotic high N C ratio hyperchromatic nuclei abnormal mitoses\\n- Key cell types and their morphology \\n - Fibroblasts spindle-shaped cells with pointy nuclear ends\\n - Smooth muscle spindle-shaped with cigar-shaped nuclei with rounded ends; stain for smooth muscle actin\\n - Skeletal muscle multinucleated long cylindrical cells with peripheral nuclei and cross-striations from actin/myosin filaments\\n\\n highyield\\nBenign tumors are mobile painless encapsulated and cured by excision. Malignant sarcomas are fixed invasive hemorrhagic/necrotic and show high N C ratios.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Adipose Tissue Tumors\\n\\n Lipoma Slides 13-14 \\n\\nThe lipoma is the most common soft tissue tumor in adults. It is a benign accumulation of mature adipocytes located in the subcutaneous tissue of the proximal extremities or trunk typically in middle-aged individuals. Because the cells are mature and the tumor is encapsulated it is mobile painless and cured by simple excision.\\n\\n- Gross yellow soft lobulated well-encapsulated with a thin fibrous capsule\\n- Histology mature adipocytes surrounded by a fibrous capsule\\n- Pseudo-encapsulation vs. true capsule some tumors compress surrounding tissue mimicking a capsule. Lipomas have a true fibrous capsule that contains the tumor\\n\\n Liposarcoma Slides 16-25 \\n\\nThe liposarcoma is the malignant counterpart and the most common sarcoma in adults. It arises deep in the soft tissues of the extremities or retroperitoneum typically in the 50-65 age range. Patients present with pain swelling fatigue and decreased range of motion.\\n\\n| Subtype | Histology | Genetics | Behavior |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Well-differentiated | Mature adipocytes + scattered lipoblasts | 12q13-15 amplification MDM2 p53 inhibitor | Indolent rarely metastasizes |\\n| Myxoid | Abundant basophilic mucopolysaccharide stroma chicken-wire capillaries primitive cells | t 12;16 FUS-DDIT3 fusion gene arrests differentiation | Intermediate aggressiveness |\\n| Pleomorphic | Bizarre nuclei spindle cells high-grade anaplastic cells | Complex karyotype without reproducible abnormalities | Aggressive frequent metastases |\\n\\n- Lipoblast an immature adipocyte with a large dark nucleus that has begun producing lipid droplets in the cytoplasm creating characteristic nuclear scalloping\\n- IHC S100-positive adipocyte marker keratin-negative excludes epithelial desmin-negative excludes muscle \\n- Malignant features not encapsulated hemorrhagic/necrotic from rapid growth outstripping blood supply less fatty tissue than well-differentiated types\\n- Recurrence may recur locally if not adequately excised due to invasive nature\\n\\n highyield\\nLiposarcoma subtypes Well-differentiated indolent MDM2 amplification Myxoid chicken-wire capillaries FUS-DDIT3 fusion Pleomorphic high-grade complex karyotype frequent metastases .\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Fibroblastic Tumors\\n\\n Nodular Fasciitis Slides 26-29 \\n\\nNodular fasciitis is a benign self-limited fibroblastic and myofibroblastic proliferation that can mimic a sarcoma clinically and histologically. It occurs in young adults on the forearm chest or back usually following trauma. Despite rapid growth over weeks to months it is benign and often regresses spontaneously.\\n\\n- Key feature contains a clonal MYH9-USP6 translocation making it technically neoplastic yet it behaves benignly and rarely recurs after excision\\n- Gross not encapsulated but circumscribed remains small usually less than 3 cm \\n- Histology plump immature fibroblasts and myofibroblasts spindle-shaped nuclei with punctate nucleoli frequent normal-appearing mitoses tissue-culture appearance randomly oriented proliferating cells \\n- Zonation hypercellular zones with storiform/fascicular patterns and hypocellular zones with myxoid stroma\\n- Infiltrating lymphocytes are common which historically led to the mistaken belief it was a reactive process\\n\\n correlate\\nNodular Fasciitis\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Clonal neoplastic proliferation of fibroblasts/myofibroblasts with MYH9-USP6 translocation; self-limited despite neoplastic nature\\n- Presentation Rapidly growing firm rubbery tender subcutaneous nodule on forearm/chest/back in young adults often with history of trauma\\n- Diagnosis Histology shows plump spindle cells with tissue-culture appearance and zonation; IHC for smooth muscle actin positive in myofibroblasts\\n- Treatment Excision rarely recurs ; often regresses spontaneously\\n- Lecturer s Point Despite having a true translocation and being clonal it is NOT malignant and does not transform -- a key mimic of sarcoma\\n \\n\\n Superficial Fibromatosis Slides 30-33 \\n\\nSuperficial fibromatosis encompasses benign fibroblastic proliferations in superficial tissues. These tend to resolve spontaneously but may cause local deformity. Males are more commonly affected.\\n\\n| Type | Location | Key Features |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Dupuytren contracture | Palmar fascia | Incidence increases with age; may cause skin dimpling and progressive flexion contracture; one or both hands |\\n| Ledderhose disease | Plantar fascia | Usually in young boys; unilateral; no contractures; can associate with other fibromatoses |\\n| Peyronie disease | Penile shaft dorsolateral | Abnormal curvature and urethral constriction; some resolve spontaneously |\\n\\n- Histology ill-defined fascicles of plump spindle-cell fibroblasts with abundant dense collagen nodular and proliferative\\n- Genetics no well-defined mutations unlike deep fibromatosis though focal beta-catenin accumulation has been found with unclear significance\\n- Palmar and plantar fibromatosis progress in about 50% of cases\\n\\n Deep Fibromatosis Desmoid Tumors Slides 33-36 \\n\\nDeep fibromatosis desmoid tumors are histologically similar to superficial fibromatosis but are locally aggressive. They average 10 cm infiltrate surrounding muscle nerve and fat and frequently recur but never metastasize. They primarily affect women in their teens to 30s.\\n\\n- Location abdominal wall musculoaponeurotic structures shoulder pelvis mesentery\\n- Genetics APC and beta-catenin mutations leading to increased WNT signaling. Most have sporadic beta-catenin mutations\\n- FAP/Gardner syndrome patients with germline APC mutations are predisposed to desmoid tumors\\n- Histology bland fibroblasts in long parallel fascicles with dense collagen no cellular atypia no mitoses -- looks like a scar\\n- Treatment challenging due to infiltrative growth; oncologists often manage these patients despite the lesion being technically benign\\n\\n correlate\\nDeep Fibromatosis Desmoid Tumor \\n\\n- Pathophysiology Fibroblastic proliferation driven by APC/beta-catenin mutations with increased WNT signaling\\n- Presentation Large average 10 cm firm fixed painful mass in abdominal wall or extremities of young women\\n- Diagnosis Gray-white rubbery poorly demarcated mass infiltrating surrounding structures; microscopy shows bland fibroblasts without atypia\\n- Treatment Wide excision; high recurrence rate due to infiltration; may require oncologic management\\n- Risk Factors FAP/Gardner syndrome germline APC mutations \\n- Lecturer s Point Acts like cancer locally aggressive but technically benign -- never metastasizes\\n \\n\\n Fibrosarcoma Slides 37-40 \\n\\nFibrosarcoma is the rare malignant fibroblast tumor. It is a diagnosis of exclusion as many previously classified fibrosarcomas were reclassified when better diagnostic tools became available. Most patients are middle-aged and it occurs in the deep soft tissues of the lower extremity or trunk.\\n\\n- Two types Infantile rarely metastasizes and Adult frequently metastasizes to lung and bone \\n- Gross pseudo-encapsulated fleshy fish-flesh appearance hemorrhagic necrotic white-tan invasive\\n- Histology characteristic herringbone pattern highly cellular increased N C ratio hyperchromatic nuclei mitotic activity\\n- IHC vimentin-positive mesenchymal marker S100-negative excludes adipocyte cytokeratin-negative excludes epithelial \\n- Treatment radical excision + radiation for positive margins; chemotherapy for high-grade tumors\\n- 5-year survival approximately 41%; 10-year survival approximately 29%\\n- Predisposing factors sites of prior injury chronic inflammation irradiated tissue de-differentiation from a well-differentiated liposarcoma\\n\\n highyield\\nFibrosarcoma herringbone pattern on histology diagnosis of exclusion vimentin-positive S100/cytokeratin-negative. 5-year survival approximately 41%.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Skeletal Muscle Tumors\\n\\n Rhabdomyosarcoma Slides 37-40 \\n\\nRhabdomyosarcoma is the most common soft tissue sarcoma of childhood. It shows skeletal muscle differentiation and presents as a rapidly growing painful mass with unexplained weight loss. Treatment requires surgical resection chemotherapy and possibly radiation.\\n\\n| Subtype | Age | Location | Key Features | Prognosis |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Embryonal | Childhood under 20 | GU tract head/neck sinuses | Primitive round cells + rare rhabdomyoblasts | Intermediate |\\n| Sarcoma botryoides | Young children | Vagina bladder nasopharynx | Grape-like polypoid growth cambium layer beneath epithelium; variant of embryonal | Best prognosis |\\n| Alveolar | Older kids/young adults 10-35 | Arms legs trunk | Alveolar pattern resembles lung alveoli discohesive round cells on fibrous septa | Poor; PAX-FOXO1 fusion = worse |\\n| Pleomorphic | Adults 50+ | Extremities | Highly pleomorphic spindle cells high N C ratio | Often fatal |\\n\\n- IHC desmin and myogenin positive skeletal muscle markers S100-negative cytokeratin-negative\\n- Rhabdomyoblast primitive cell showing early skeletal muscle differentiation with developing cross-striations of actin and myosin\\n- Molecular PAX-FOXO1 fusion gene interferes with differentiation and predicts poor survival\\n- Sarcoma botryoides best prognosis; 65% remission with treatment but only 15% four-year survival with metastasis\\n\\n correlate\\nSarcoma Botryoides\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Variant of embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma arising in walls of hollow mucosal-lined structures\\n- Presentation Polypoid grape-like mass protruding from vagina bladder or nasopharynx in young children\\n- Diagnosis Cambium layer dense tumor cell layer beneath epithelium ; desmin/myogenin positive\\n- Treatment Surgery + chemotherapy; most favorable prognosis of all rhabdomyosarcoma subtypes\\n- Lecturer s Point Remember the grape-like cluster gross appearance and that this is the best prognosis among rhabdomyosarcomas\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Tumors of Uncertain Origin\\n\\n Synovial Sarcoma Slides 37-40 \\n\\nDespite its name synovial sarcoma does not originate from synovium. It was historically named for its occurrence near joints but molecular and immunohistochemical studies show it is inconsistent with synovial origin. It occurs in the deep soft tissues adjacent to large joints especially the knee in patients aged 20-40.\\n\\n- Presentation deep-seated pain numbness limited range of motion firm/fixed swelling near joints; often mistaken for arthritis or bursitis for years\\n- Genetics characteristic t X;18 translocation producing proteins that interfere with chromatin gene expression\\n- Histologic types \\n - Monophasic uniform spindle cells with high N C ratio in tightly packed fascicles\\n - Biphasic spindle cells PLUS gland-like structures with cuboidal to columnar epithelioid cells\\n- IHC epithelial components positive for CK7 and other cytokeratins; spindle component variably positive for cytokeratins\\n- Treatment limb-sparing surgery + chemotherapy; surgery + chemo + radiation\\n- 5-year survival 50-60%; 10-year survival 40-50%\\n- Metastasis commonly to lungs; unusually for sarcomas also to regional lymph nodes\\n\\n Undifferentiated Pleomorphic Sarcoma UPS Slides 37-40 \\n\\nUPS is the diagnosis given to high-grade mesenchymal tumors that cannot be classified into any recognized lineage by histomorphology immunohistochemistry or molecular studies. It arises in middle-aged to older adults 50-60+ in the deep soft tissues of the extremity especially the thigh.\\n\\n- Gross can grow up to 20 cm; gray-white fleshy hemorrhagic\\n- Histology sheets of large anaplastic spindle or polygonal cells hyperchromatic bizarre nuclei pleomorphic cells with variable sizes and shapes abnormal mitotic figures\\n- IHC by definition lacks markers of differentiation desmin/myogenin negative S100 negative cytokeratin negative -- no consistent genetic pattern\\n- Treatment surgery + adjuvant chemo and/or radiation\\n- Prognosis generally poor; metastasis occurs in up to 50% of cases\\n\\n---\\n\\n Other Soft Tissue Tumors Briefly Mentioned \\n\\n- Rhabdomyoma benign skeletal muscle tumor; commonly found in the heart; associated with tuberous sclerosis \\n- Leiomyoma benign smooth muscle tumor; most common in the uterus fibroids \\n- Leiomyosarcoma malignant smooth muscle tumor; also commonly found in the uterus; may metastasize\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Soft Tissue Tumor Cell Origins AFSU \\n A dipose Lipoma benign / Liposarcoma malignant \\n F ibroblast Nodular fasciitis / Fibromatosis / Fibrosarcoma\\n S keletal muscle Rhabdomyoma benign / Rhabdomyosarcoma malignant \\n U ncertain Synovial sarcoma t X;18 / UPS diagnosis of exclusion \\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Summary Table\\n\\n| Tumor | Cell of Origin | Benign/Malignant | Key Feature | Hallmark |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Lipoma | Adipose | Benign | Most common soft tissue tumor in adults | Encapsulated mobile painless |\\n| Liposarcoma | Adipose | Malignant | Most common adult sarcoma | Lipoblasts S100+ three subtypes |\\n| Nodular fasciitis | Fibroblast | Benign | Mimics sarcoma | MYH9-USP6 translocation self-limited |\\n| Dupuytren contracture | Fibroblast | Benign | Palmar fibromatosis | Flexion contracture skin dimpling |\\n| Desmoid tumor | Fibroblast | Locally aggressive | Never metastasizes | APC/beta-catenin WNT signaling |\\n| Fibrosarcoma | Fibroblast | Malignant | Diagnosis of exclusion | Herringbone pattern vimentin+ |\\n| Rhabdomyosarcoma | Skeletal muscle | Malignant | Most common childhood sarcoma | Desmin/myogenin+ multiple subtypes |\\n| Synovial sarcoma | Uncertain | Malignant | Misnomer -- NOT from synovium | t X;18 biphasic pattern CK7+ |\\n| UPS | Uncertain | Malignant | Unclassifiable high-grade | By exclusion no lineage markers | Pathoma Musculoskeletal Pathology > Soft Tissue Tumors BandB Musculoskeletal > Soft Tissue Tumors SketchyPath Musculoskeletal & Derm > Soft Tissue Tumors FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Soft Tissue Tumors What is the most common soft tissue tumor in adults? Lipoma. It is a benign well-encapsulated tumor of mature adipocytes found in the subcutaneous tissue of the proximal extremities or trunk in middle-aged adults. It is mobile painless and cured by simple excision. Concept What are the three subtypes of liposarcoma? Well-differentiated mature adipocytes + lipoblasts MDM2/12q13-15 amplification indolent Myxoid mucopolysaccharide stroma with chicken-wire capillaries FUS-DDIT3 fusion intermediate and Pleomorphic bizarre nuclei complex karyotype aggressive with frequent metastases . Concept What is a lipoblast? An immature adipocyte with a large dark nucleus that is beginning to produce lipid droplets in the cytoplasm. The lipid droplets create characteristic nuclear scalloping. Lipoblasts are a hallmark of liposarcoma. Concept What is nodular fasciitis and why can it mimic sarcoma? A benign self-limited fibroblastic/myofibroblastic proliferation in young adults. It mimics sarcoma because it grows rapidly weeks to months is highly cellular with frequent mitoses and contains a clonal MYH9-USP6 translocation. Despite this it never becomes malignant and often regresses spontaneously. Clinical What is Dupuytren contracture? A superficial palmar fibromatosis causing progressive flexion contracture of the fingers. It involves irregular thickening of the palmar fascia with nodular fibroblastic tissue and abundant collagen. Incidence increases with age. Clinical What mutations drive deep fibromatosis desmoid tumors ? APC and beta-catenin mutations both leading to increased WNT signaling. Most cases have sporadic beta-catenin mutations. Patients with FAP familial adenomatous polyposis or Gardner syndrome germline APC mutations are predisposed. Concept What is the characteristic histologic pattern of fibrosarcoma? The herringbone pattern -- alternating bundles of spindle cells creating a V-shaped arrangement. Other features include high N C ratio hyperchromatic nuclei and mitotic activity. It is vimentin-positive S100-negative and cytokeratin-negative. Clinical What is the most common soft tissue sarcoma of childhood? Rhabdomyosarcoma. It shows skeletal muscle differentiation and stains positive for desmin and myogenin. The embryonal subtype is most common while sarcoma botryoides has the best prognosis. Concept What is sarcoma botryoides and what is its classic gross appearance? A variant of embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma that develops in the walls of hollow mucosal-lined structures vagina bladder nasopharynx in young children. It grows in a grape-like cluster polypoid fashion and has a cambium layer beneath the epithelium. It has the best prognosis of all rhabdomyosarcoma subtypes. Clinical What translocation defines synovial sarcoma? t X;18 . It produces fusion proteins that interfere with chromatin/gene expression. Synovial sarcoma occurs near large joints in patients aged 20-40 and has monophasic spindle cells only or biphasic spindle cells + glandular epithelial structures patterns. Clinical How is undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma UPS diagnosed? By exclusion. UPS is a high-grade mesenchymal tumor that cannot be classified along any recognized cell lineage. It lacks differentiation markers on IHC desmin/myogenin negative S100 negative cytokeratin negative and has no consistent/reproducible genetic abnormalities. Concept What benign tumor is associated with tuberous sclerosis? Rhabdomyoma -- a benign skeletal muscle tumor most commonly found in the heart. It is classically associated with tuberous sclerosis. Clinical match Lipoma most common soft tissue tumor resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > search lipoma soft tissue benign encapsulated match Liposarcoma subtypes well-differentiated myxoid pleomorphic resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma / BandB > Musculoskeletal > search liposarcoma lipoblast MDM2 myxoid match Nodular fasciitis sarcoma mimic resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search nodular fasciitis self-limited fibroblast match Dupuytren contracture palmar fibromatosis resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma / BandB > Musculoskeletal > search dupuytren contracture palmar fibromatosis match Deep fibromatosis / Desmoid tumors APC beta-catenin WNT resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search desmoid fibromatosis APC beta-catenin WNT Gardner match Fibrosarcoma herringbone pattern resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search fibrosarcoma herringbone vimentin match Rhabdomyosarcoma subtypes embryonal alveolar botryoides pleomorphic resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma / SketchyPath > MSK > search rhabdomyosarcoma botryoides desmin myogenin match Sarcoma botryoides grape-like cambium layer resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK / Repro > search sarcoma botryoides grape cambium vagina match Rhabdomyoma tuberous sclerosis cardiac resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Cardiac / MSK > search rhabdomyoma tuberous sclerosis cardiac benign match Synovial sarcoma t X;18 biphasic resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search synovial sarcoma translocation X 18 biphasic match Leiomyoma and leiomyosarcoma uterine smooth muscle tumors resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Repro / MSK > search leiomyoma fibroid leiomyosarcoma uterus match Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma UPS formerly MFH resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma UPS MFH match In-house lecture primary resource resources In-house lecture is best. No specific AnKing deck match found for this topic -- supplement with Pathoma Ch. 22 MSK First Aid MSK section and Boards & Beyond MSK Pathology. Lipoma vs. Liposarcoma Lipoma is the most common soft tissue tumor benign encapsulated mature adipocytes subcutaneous . Liposarcoma is the most common adult sarcoma deep invasive contains lipoblasts . The presence of lipoblasts immature adipocytes with nuclear scalloping is a key indicator of liposarcoma. Nodular Fasciitis The Great Sarcoma Mimic Nodular fasciitis is benign despite having a clonal translocation MYH9-USP6 and rapid growth with frequent mitoses. It is self-limited and rarely recurs after excision. Misdiagnosis as sarcoma can lead to inappropriately aggressive treatment. Desmoid Tumors and FAP/Gardner Syndrome Desmoid tumors deep fibromatosis are driven by APC/beta-catenin mutations that increase WNT signaling. Patients with FAP or Gardner syndrome are predisposed. These tumors never metastasize but act locally aggressive infiltrating surrounding structures. They are managed by oncologists despite being technically benign. Fibrosarcoma Herringbone Pattern The herringbone pattern -- alternating V-shaped bundles of spindle cells -- is the histologic hallmark of fibrosarcoma. It is a diagnosis of exclusion requiring IHC and molecular studies to rule out other sarcomas. Many historical fibrosarcomas were reclassified as other tumor types. Rhabdomyosarcoma Subtypes by Age Embryonal/sarcoma botryoides children. Alveolar older children to young adults. Pleomorphic adults 50+. Sarcoma botryoides grape-like cluster in hollow organs has the best prognosis. PAX-FOXO1 fusion alveolar predicts poor survival. Synovial Sarcoma is NOT from Synovium Despite its name synovial sarcoma does not originate from synovial tissue. It has a characteristic t X;18 translocation and can show biphasic histology spindle cells + epithelial glands . It commonly metastasizes to lungs and unusually for sarcomas to regional lymph nodes. questions 1 A 55-year-old man presents with a large deep-seated mass in the retroperitoneum. Biopsy reveals malignant adipose tissue with bizarre hyperchromatic nuclei of various shapes and sizes scattered lipoblasts with nuclear scalloping and areas of necrosis. Which of the following best describes the expected genetic findings in this neoplasm? t 12;16 creating a FUS-DDIT3 fusion gene Amplification of 12q13-15 including MDM2 Complex karyotype without reproducible genetic abnormalities t X;18 creating an SS18-SSX fusion gene Germline APC mutation with loss of heterozygosity 3 The description retroperitoneum malignant adipose bizarre hyperchromatic nuclei of various shapes/sizes lipoblasts necrosis is classic for pleomorphic liposarcoma. Pleomorphic liposarcomas have complex karyotypes without reproducible abnormalities. t 12;16 FUS-DDIT3 = myxoid liposarcoma; 12q13-15 amplification = well-differentiated liposarcoma; t X;18 = synovial sarcoma; APC mutations = desmoid tumors. 2 A 28-year-old woman presents with a rapidly growing firm tender nodule on her forearm that appeared 3 weeks after trauma. Biopsy shows highly cellular tissue with plump spindle cells frequent mitoses all normal-appearing and a tissue-culture-like growth pattern. Despite these alarming features the lesion is most likely benign. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Fibrosarcoma Nodular fasciitis Deep fibromatosis desmoid tumor Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans Superficial fibromatosis 2 Nodular fasciitis is a benign self-limited fibroblastic proliferation that classically occurs in young adults on the forearm after trauma. Despite rapid growth high cellularity and frequent mitoses all mitotic figures are normal-appearing. The tissue-culture appearance randomly oriented proliferating cells is characteristic. It contains a MYH9-USP6 translocation but behaves benignly. 3 A 6-year-old girl presents with vaginal bleeding. Examination reveals a polypoid grape-like cluster of tissue protruding from the vagina. Biopsy shows a dense layer of tumor cells beneath the vaginal epithelium cambium layer that stains positive for desmin and myogenin. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma sarcoma botryoides Synovial sarcoma Leiomyosarcoma Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcoma 2 The clinical presentation young girl vaginal polypoid grape-like mass with cambium layer and positive desmin/myogenin staining are classic for sarcoma botryoides a variant of embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma. It develops in the walls of hollow mucosal-lined structures in children and has the best prognosis of all rhabdomyosarcoma subtypes. 4 A 35-year-old man presents with a deep-seated painful mass near his knee that has been slowly growing for 2 years. MRI shows a well-defined mass in the popliteal fossa. Biopsy reveals biphasic histology with uniform spindle cells in tightly packed fascicles interspersed with gland-like structures lined by cuboidal to columnar epithelioid cells. The epithelial component stains positive for CK7. Which of the following cytogenetic findings is most likely? t 11;22 producing EWSR1-FLI1 fusion gene t X;18 translocation t 12;16 producing FUS-DDIT3 fusion gene Amplification of 12q13-15 region Complex karyotype with ring chromosomes 2 The location deep soft tissue near the knee patient age 20-40 biphasic histology spindle cells + glandular epithelial structures and CK7 positivity are classic for synovial sarcoma. The t X;18 translocation is the defining cytogenetic finding producing proteins that interfere with chromatin gene expression. Despite its name synovial sarcoma does not originate from synovium. 5 A 25-year-old woman with a history of familial adenomatous polyposis FAP presents with a large firm fixed abdominal wall mass. Biopsy shows bland fibroblasts arranged in long parallel fascicles with dense collagen no cellular atypia and no mitotic activity. The mass infiltrates into the adjacent rectus abdominis muscle. Which of the following pathways is most likely aberrantly activated in this neoplasm? RAS-MAPK signaling pathway JAK-STAT signaling pathway WNT signaling pathway Hedgehog signaling pathway Notch signaling pathway 3 This is a desmoid tumor deep fibromatosis in a patient with FAP. FAP involves germline APC mutations and APC normally degrades beta-catenin. Loss of APC function leads to beta-catenin accumulation and constitutive activation of the WNT signaling pathway which drives fibroblast proliferation. The bland cytology with no atypia or mitoses but locally aggressive/infiltrative behavior is characteristic."}, {"id": "l107", "title": "Lecture #107: DPR - History and Physical Exam of the Hand and Wrist", "module": "DPR", "content": "l107 Lecture 107 DPR - History and Physical Exam of the Hand and Wrist lecturer R. Morgan D.O. DPR pdf content/L107 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 107 History and Physical Exam of the Hand and Wrist\\n\\n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O. | Session Lecture 107\\n\\nThis DPR lecture provides a structured systematic approach to evaluating hand and wrist complaints. The clinical pearl that anchors the entire session the diagnosis is in the history . A thorough history of present illness will guide your differential before you ever lay hands on the patient and the physical exam then confirms or excludes what you are already thinking. The lecture moves through history-taking the four cardinal signs of inflammation inspection palpation landmarks range of motion motor strength grading special tests Tinel s Phalen s Finkelstein Allen neurovascular assessment and osteopathic exam and treatment. A case study of carpal tunnel syndrome ties it all together.\\n\\n---\\n\\n History-Taking for the Hand and Wrist\\n\\nThe first step is always to determine the mechanism of injury . Ask What were you doing when the pain began? The answer may reveal an acute event such as a fall on an outstretched hand FOOSH or an insidious chronic onset from repetitive use such as typing.\\n\\n Key History Elements\\n\\n- Location Have the patient point with one finger. The hand and wrist contain 8 carpal bones multiple phalanges and the distal radius/ulna so precise localization matters enormously\\n- Laterality Is the pain unilateral or bilateral? Did both sides begin simultaneously or sequentially? Symmetric bilateral involvement raises suspicion for systemic arthritis\\n- Pain grading OPPQRSTA Rate severity 0-10 assess progression over time and determine if pain disproportionate to exam raises concern for compartment syndrome \\n- Radiation Does pain stay localized to a bone tendon or muscle or does it travel from the neck or shoulder?\\n- Swelling Is it constant or intermittent? Worse with specific activities such as typing?\\n- Paresthesias Which fingers are involved? Numbness in digits 4-5 suggests ulnar neuropathy possibly originating at the elbow. Distribution determines the nerve root or peripheral nerve involved\\n- Weakness Can they write turn a doorknob do a push-up or type? For elderly patients living alone wrist dysfunction can profoundly affect activities of daily living bathing feeding dressing \\n- Aggravating activities and occupation Typists secretaries drivers and athletes have different risk profiles. Repetitive hobbies sewing painting archery also matter\\n\\n highyield\\nAlways determine the mechanism of injury first. Ask what the patient was doing when pain began. Pain out of proportion to exam suggests compartment syndrome. The distribution of paresthesias identifies the specific nerve root or peripheral nerve.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n The Four Signs of Inflammation\\n\\nOn exam systematically assess each cardinal sign applied to the wrist and hand \\n\\n| Sign | Latin | What to Assess |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Swelling | Tumor | Synovial membrane boggy/doughy joint effusion or soft tissue structures bursa tendons tendon sheaths |\\n| Warmth | Calor | Use the backs of your fingers; compare involved joint with contralateral side. Present in arthritis tendonitis bursitis and osteomyelitis |\\n| Redness | Rubor | Least common sign of inflammation; when present over superficial joints fingers toes knees suspect septic arthritis crystalline arthritis or rheumatoid arthritis |\\n| Pain/Tenderness | Dolor | Localize the specific anatomic structure. Diffuse tenderness + warmth over thickened synovium = arthritis or infection. Focal tenderness = specific bone or muscle injury |\\n\\n highyield\\nRedness rubor over a joint is the least common inflammatory sign but the most specific for septic crystalline or rheumatoid arthritis. Always compare the involved side with the contralateral side.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Inspection\\n\\nBefore palpating visually evaluate both hands simultaneously for \\n\\n- Deformity asymmetry and obvious dislocations\\n- Lacerations bruising and erythema\\n- Muscle atrophy especially thenar atrophy hallmark of chronic carpal tunnel syndrome from median nerve damage \\n- Ganglion cysts may only be visible with the wrist flexed; in neutral/extension the cyst can be hidden between tendons \\n- Nodules such as Heberden s nodes DIP osteoarthritis or Bouchard s nodes PIP osteoarthritis \\n\\n---\\n\\n Palpation Landmarks\\n\\nAlways start as far from the painful area as possible and systematically work toward it. This prevents unnecessary patient discomfort and may reveal additional areas of involvement. Never use your thumb to palpate -- the pulse in your thumb can be confused with the patient s.\\n\\n Key Landmarks\\n\\n| Landmark | Clinical Significance |\\n|---|---|\\n| Anatomical snuffbox scaphoid | Tenderness here after FOOSH = suspect scaphoid fracture most common carpal fracture ; risk of avascular necrosis of the scaphoid due to retrograde blood supply |\\n| CMC joint base of thumb | Common site for osteoarthritis and tendon insertions |\\n| Hook of hamate | Lateral proximal wrist; fracture or dislocation causes tenderness here |\\n| MCP joints dorsal hand | Tenderness suggests synovitis OA or RA; check all MCP joints systematically |\\n\\n correlate\\nScaphoid Fracture\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Most common carpal fracture typically from FOOSH; the scaphoid has a retrograde blood supply enters distally so proximal fractures risk avascular necrosis\\n- Presentation Pain in anatomical snuffbox wrist pain after fall may have negative initial X-ray\\n- Diagnosis Snuffbox tenderness on palpation; if X-ray negative but clinical suspicion high immobilize and re-image in 10-14 days or obtain MRI/CT\\n- Treatment Thumb spica cast for non-displaced fractures; surgical fixation for displaced or proximal pole fractures\\n- Lecturer s Point Pain may begin before you even reach the snuffbox on palpation -- still consider this a positive finding\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Range of Motion\\n\\n Active ROM\\n\\n- Fist and spread Have the patient make a full fist and then spread all fingers fully. Compare bilaterally and simultaneously to detect subtle differences\\n- Radial and ulnar deviation Normal is approximately 20-30 degrees each direction slightly less radially \\n- Wrist flexion and extension Normal is approximately 70 degrees each direction\\n- Always compare both sides simultaneously when possible\\n\\n Passive ROM\\n\\n- Performed by the examiner to determine whether limitation is from pain swelling or structural restriction\\n- Perform on any joint felt to be abnormal during active ROM\\n- Important to identify what is limiting the range -- pain versus mechanical block\\n\\n---\\n\\n Motor Strength Testing\\n\\n Grading Scale 0-5 \\n\\n| Grade | Finding |\\n|---|---|\\n| 5/5 | Full strength against resistance |\\n| 4/5 | Slightly diminished strength but still against gravity and resistance |\\n| 3/5 | Movement against gravity only |\\n| 2/5 | Movement only with gravity eliminated |\\n| 1/5 | Slight involuntary muscle contraction fasciculation/trace |\\n| 0/5 | No movement at all |\\n\\n Grip Strength\\n\\n- Test intrinsic hand muscles by having the patient grasp your two fingers bilaterally and simultaneously\\n- Decreased grip strength = weakness of finger flexors and/or intrinsic hand muscles\\n- Grip weakness + wrist pain = consider De Quervain s tenosynovitis\\n- Test individual finger strength when a specific digit is injured\\n\\n highyield\\nGrip weakness combined with wrist pain is suggestive of De Quervain s tenosynovitis. 3/5 motor strength means movement against gravity only. Always test bilaterally and simultaneously for subtle comparison.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Special Tests\\n\\n Tinel s Sign Carpal Tunnel \\n\\nTap over the median nerve at the volar wrist. A positive test reproduces pain and/or paresthesias in the median nerve distribution thumb index middle and radial half of ring finger . Even pain alone without paresthesias can be considered positive but both together strengthen the finding.\\n\\n Phalen s Test Carpal Tunnel \\n\\nHyperflex both wrists by pressing the dorsa of the hands together. Hold for 30-60 seconds. A positive test reproduces pain and/or paresthesias in the median nerve distribution. This manually compresses the median nerve to reproduce carpal tunnel symptoms.\\n\\n Finkelstein s Test De Quervain s Tenosynovitis \\n\\nHave the patient tuck the thumb into the palm and make a fist then ulnar-deviate the wrist. This stretches the EPB extensor pollicis brevis and APL abductor pollicis longus tendons. Pain along the radial wrist at the first dorsal compartment is a positive result indicating inflammation of the tendon sheaths tenosynovitis .\\n\\n Thumb Abduction Test Carpal Tunnel \\n\\nHave the patient abduct the thumb against resistance. Weakness indicates thenar muscle compromise from median nerve compression. This is especially significant in chronic carpal tunnel syndrome where thenar atrophy has developed.\\n\\n Allen Test Vascular Patency \\n\\nOcclude both radial and ulnar arteries at the wrist. Have the patient open and close the fist several times until the hand blanches. Release one artery at a time and observe for color return within 2-3 seconds. If the hand remains pale there is vascular compromise of that artery. Repeat for the other artery. Also performed before placing an arterial line.\\n\\n correlate\\nCarpal Tunnel Syndrome\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Compression of the median nerve within the carpal tunnel bounded by carpal bones and transverse carpal ligament/flexor retinaculum \\n- Presentation Tingling and numbness in digits 1-3 and radial half of digit 4; pain worse with typing driving or repetitive wrist use; may wake patient at night\\n- Diagnosis Positive Tinel s sign and Phalen s test; thenar atrophy on inspection chronic ; decreased grip strength; decreased sensation in median nerve distribution; EMG/nerve conduction study for confirmation\\n- Treatment Wrist splinting especially at night NSAIDs corticosteroid injection osteopathic manipulation myofascial release of flexor retinaculum and surgical carpal tunnel release for refractory cases\\n- Lecturer s Point Thenar atrophy indicates chronic longstanding median nerve damage and is often irreversible. Pain out of proportion to exam = compartment syndrome not carpal tunnel\\n \\n\\n correlate\\nDe Quervain s Tenosynovitis\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Inflammation of the synovial sheaths surrounding the EPB and APL tendons in the first dorsal compartment of the wrist\\n- Presentation Pain along the radial side of the wrist worsened by thumb use gripping and wrist deviation; grip weakness often present\\n- Diagnosis Positive Finkelstein s test pain with ulnar deviation while thumb is tucked into fist \\n- Treatment Thumb spica splint NSAIDs corticosteroid injection and surgical release for refractory cases\\n- Lecturer s Point This test is very sensitive -- if there is inflammation in those tendon sheaths the patient will have a significantly positive response\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Neurovascular Exam\\n\\n- Pulses Palpate radial artery graded on 4-point scale; 2+/4 = normal bounding . The ulnar artery is often too deep to palpate directly so use the Allen test instead\\n- Capillary refill Compress the nail bed for 2-3 seconds then release. Normal refill is less than 3 seconds. Greater than 3-5 seconds is a positive finding indicating vascular compromise\\n- Sensation Test light touch cotton swab and sharp sensation broken tongue depressor in dermatomal distributions C6 C7 C8 across all five digits bilaterally\\n- After any significant wrist trauma especially FOOSH always assess neurovascular integrity due to risk of vascular compromise or avascular necrosis\\n\\n---\\n\\n Osteopathic Exam and Treatment\\n\\nThe osteopathic component assesses somatic dysfunction of the carpal bones decreased motion of lunate capitate etc. and provides in-office treatment. Key techniques discussed \\n\\n- Myofascial wrist retinaculum release The physician uses specific hand placement over the palmar surface and flexor retinaculum to apply transverse distraction until soft tissue release is achieved\\n- Carpal bone HVLA The physician engages a dorsiflexion or palmar flexion barrier and applies a mobilization with impulse thrust to restore carpal bone motion\\n\\n highyield\\nOMT techniques for the wrist provide immediate pain relief in office. Decreased motion of the lunate and capitate bones is a common finding in carpal tunnel syndrome. The myofascial retinaculum release targets the flexor retinaculum directly.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Sample Case Carpal Tunnel Syndrome\\n\\nA 46-year-old female presents with right wrist pain that started 10 days ago and has progressively worsened. She describes a tingling numbness in the middle of her palm 4/10 currently 7/10 at worst 3/10 at best . Typing at work aggravates the pain. She has tried stretches ice heat and ibuprofen 200 mg daily without improvement. She denies radiation.\\n\\n Exam findings No gross abnormalities on inspection; tenderness localized to midline right wrist; full passive and active ROM in unaffected digits; symmetric pulses bilaterally; decreased grip strength right hand; positive Tinel s sign and Phalen s test on the right; osteopathic exam reveals decreased motion of lunate and capitate of the right hand.\\n\\n Diagnosis Carpal tunnel syndrome.\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Wrist Special Tests TPF-A \\n T inel s = tapping the median nerve carpal tunnel \\n P halen s = pressing backs of flexed hands together for 30-60 sec carpal tunnel \\n F inkelstein s = thumb tucked + ulnar deviation De Quervain s \\n A llen = occlude both arteries then release one at a time vascular patency \\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Summary Table Special Tests\\n\\n| Test | What It Assesses | Positive Finding | Associated Condition |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Tinel s sign | Median nerve irritability | Pain/paresthesias in median nerve distribution with tapping | Carpal tunnel syndrome |\\n| Phalen s test | Median nerve compression | Pain/paresthesias after 30-60 sec of wrist hyperflexion | Carpal tunnel syndrome |\\n| Finkelstein s test | EPB/APL tendon inflammation | Pain along radial wrist with ulnar deviation and thumb tucked | De Quervain s tenosynovitis |\\n| Thumb abduction | Thenar muscle strength | Weakness against resistance | Carpal tunnel chronic |\\n| Allen test | Radial/ulnar artery patency | Hand remains blanched after releasing one artery | Vascular compromise |\\n| Grip strength | Intrinsic hand muscles | Decreased grip bilaterally or unilaterally | Neuropathy arthritis De Quervain s | BandB Musculoskeletal > Upper Extremity Exam SketchyPath Musculoskeletal & Derm > Carpal Tunnel Syndrome FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Upper Extremity > Carpal Tunnel & De Quervain s Pathoma Musculoskeletal > Peripheral Nerve Injuries What is the first and most important question to ask a patient presenting with hand or wrist pain? What were you doing when the pain began? This determines the mechanism of injury acute vs. chronic/insidious onset and guides the remainder of the history and physical exam. Concept What are the four cardinal signs of inflammation and what are their Latin names? Swelling tumor warmth calor redness rubor and pain/tenderness dolor . Rubor is the least common but when present over a superficial joint suggests septic crystalline or rheumatoid arthritis. Concept What does thenar atrophy on inspection suggest? Chronic carpal tunnel syndrome. Prolonged median nerve compression leads to denervation and wasting of the thenar muscles opponens pollicis abductor pollicis brevis flexor pollicis brevis . This is often irreversible. Clinical What is the most common carpal fracture and how is it assessed on exam? The scaphoid fracture assessed by palpating the anatomical snuffbox. Tenderness here after a fall on outstretched hand FOOSH is highly suspicious. Risk of avascular necrosis exists due to the scaphoid s retrograde blood supply. Clinical How is Tinel s sign performed and what does a positive result indicate? Tap over the median nerve at the volar wrist. A positive test reproduces pain and/or paresthesias in the median nerve distribution digits 1-3 and radial half of digit 4 indicating carpal tunnel syndrome. Clinical How is Phalen s test performed and what does a positive result indicate? Hyperflex both wrists by pressing the dorsa of the hands together for 30-60 seconds. If this reproduces pain and/or paresthesias in the median nerve distribution it is positive for carpal tunnel syndrome. Clinical How is Finkelstein s test performed and what does it diagnose? Patient tucks the thumb into the palm makes a fist and the examiner ulnar-deviates the wrist. Pain along the radial wrist first dorsal compartment is positive for De Quervain s tenosynovitis inflammation of EPB and APL tendon sheaths . Clinical What does the Allen test assess and how is it performed? Vascular patency of the radial and ulnar arteries. Occlude both arteries have the patient pump the fist until the hand blanches then release one artery at a time. Normal color returns within 2-3 seconds. Abnormal hand stays blanched vascular compromise of the released artery. Clinical What motor strength grade indicates movement against gravity only? 3/5. The full scale 5/5 = full strength 4/5 = slight weakness 3/5 = against gravity only 2/5 = gravity eliminated 1/5 = trace contraction 0/5 = no movement. Concept What is the normal capillary refill time and what does a prolonged refill suggest? Normal is less than 3 seconds. A refill time of 3-5 seconds or greater is a positive finding suggesting vascular compromise. Assessed by compressing the nail bed for 2-3 seconds and releasing. Clinical Why should you never use your thumb when palpating for a pulse? The thumb has its own arterial pulse princeps pollicis artery which can be mistaken for the patient s pulse leading to a false-positive finding. Concept What combination of grip weakness and wrist pain should make you think of De Quervain s tenosynovitis? Grip weakness accompanied by radial-sided wrist pain especially worsened by thumb use gripping or lifting. Confirm with Finkelstein s test. The EPB and APL tendons in the first dorsal compartment are inflamed. Clinical match Carpal tunnel syndrome median nerve compression Tinel s Phalen s resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB / Pathoma > MSK / Neuro > search carpal tunnel median nerve Phalen Tinel thenar match Scaphoid fracture FOOSH anatomical snuffbox avascular necrosis resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB / FirstAid > MSK > search scaphoid fracture snuffbox avascular necrosis FOOSH match De Quervain s tenosynovitis Finkelstein test EPB APL resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB / FirstAid > MSK > search de quervain tenosynovitis finkelstein EPB APL radial match Allen test radial/ulnar artery patency arterial line resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB > MSK / Cardiovascular > search Allen test radial ulnar artery patency blanch match Motor strength grading scale 0-5 resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB > Neuro > search motor strength grading scale 0 5 gravity match Cardinal signs of inflammation tumor calor rubor dolor resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Inflammation > search cardinal signs inflammation tumor calor rubor dolor match Median nerve distribution thumb index middle radial ring finger resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB / FirstAid > Neuro / MSK > search median nerve hand distribution thenar match Ulnar nerve digits 4-5 ulnar neuropathy at elbow resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB / FirstAid > Neuro > search ulnar nerve neuropathy digits 4 5 elbow cubital match Ganglion cyst wrist dorsal flexion to visualize resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB > MSK > search ganglion cyst wrist dorsal transillumination match Compartment syndrome pain out of proportion to exam resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB / FirstAid > MSK > search compartment syndrome pain proportion 5 Ps match In-house lecture primary resource resources In-house lecture is best. No specific AnKing deck match found for this DPR topic -- supplement with Boards & Beyond MSK Upper Extremity Exam First Aid MSK section and clinical skills resources for wrist/hand exam techniques. The Diagnosis Is in the History A thorough HPI will give you the likely diagnosis before you ever touch the patient. Every question you ask should have a purpose to include or exclude a diagnosis already on your differential. The physical exam confirms what you already suspect. Scaphoid Fracture The Silent Emergency Scaphoid tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox after FOOSH demands imaging even if initial X-rays are negative. The scaphoid has retrograde blood supply enters distally so proximal fractures risk avascular necrosis if not immobilized promptly. Pain may begin before you even reach the snuffbox -- still a positive finding. Thenar Atrophy = Chronic Carpal Tunnel Visible wasting of the thenar eminence is a late sign of carpal tunnel syndrome indicating longstanding median nerve damage that is often irreversible. This is why early diagnosis with Tinel s and Phalen s testing matters. Finkelstein s Test Is Very Sensitive If there is inflammation in the EPB and APL tendon sheaths the patient will have a strongly positive Finkelstein s test. The combination of grip weakness + radial wrist pain should always prompt this test. Pain Out of Proportion = Compartment Syndrome Carpal tunnel causes low-to-medium grade pain. If pain is disproportionate to exam findings consider compartment syndrome -- a surgical emergency with potential for tissue necrosis. Palpation Technique Matters Never start palpation at the point of maximum tenderness. Always begin as far from the painful area as possible and work toward it. Never use your thumb to palpate its pulse can be mistaken for the patient s . Be systematic -- proximal to distal or distal to proximal but be consistent. questions 1 A 52-year-old female secretary presents with a 3-week history of numbness and tingling in her right thumb index finger and middle finger. The symptoms are worse at night and with typing. On exam she has thenar atrophy of the right hand. Which of the following tests if positive would best support the suspected diagnosis? Finkelstein s test Allen test Phalen s test Adson s test Speed s test 3 The presentation numbness/tingling in median nerve distribution worse at night and with typing thenar atrophy is classic for carpal tunnel syndrome. Phalen s test wrist hyperflexion for 30-60 seconds manually compresses the median nerve and reproduces symptoms. Finkelstein s tests for De Quervain s Allen s tests vascular patency Adson s tests for thoracic outlet syndrome and Speed s tests for biceps tendinopathy. 2 A 25-year-old male falls on an outstretched hand during a basketball game. He presents with wrist pain and tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox. X-ray is negative. What is the most appropriate next step? Reassure the patient and discharge without treatment Immobilize in a thumb spica cast and repeat imaging in 10-14 days Obtain an immediate CT angiogram Begin physical therapy and follow up in 6 weeks Perform an Allen test to assess vascular compromise 2 Snuffbox tenderness after FOOSH highly suggests scaphoid fracture even with a negative initial X-ray fractures may not be visible for 10-14 days . The correct approach is to immobilize in a thumb spica cast treat presumptively and repeat imaging. The scaphoid has retrograde blood supply and missed fractures risk avascular necrosis. MRI can also be obtained for earlier diagnosis. 3 A 30-year-old new mother presents with radial-sided wrist pain that worsens when she lifts her infant. On exam the patient has pain when she tucks her thumb into her palm and the examiner ulnar-deviates her wrist. Which structures are most likely inflamed? Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus Median nerve and flexor retinaculum Extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor carpi radialis brevis Ulnar nerve and Guyon s canal 2 The positive Finkelstein s test pain with ulnar deviation while thumb is tucked diagnoses De Quervain s tenosynovitis -- inflammation of the EPB extensor pollicis brevis and APL abductor pollicis longus tendon sheaths in the first dorsal compartment. This is common in new mothers from repetitive lifting of infants. 4 During a pre-procedural assessment before placing a radial arterial line a physician occludes both the radial and ulnar arteries has the patient pump the fist and then releases the ulnar artery. The hand remains pale for more than 5 seconds. What does this finding indicate? Normal finding; the ulnar artery is dominant The radial artery is the sole supply to the hand; radial arterial line is safe Inadequate perfusion from the ulnar artery alone; the radial line may not be safe Venous insufficiency of the hand Compartment syndrome is developing 3 The Allen test assesses collateral blood supply to the hand. When the ulnar artery is released and the hand remains pale it means the ulnar artery alone cannot adequately perfuse the hand. This means the hand relies significantly on the radial artery and cannulating it could compromise blood supply. Normal result color returns within 2-3 seconds of releasing either artery. 5 A patient with wrist pain has the following motor strength findings she can move her fingers against gravity but cannot overcome any applied resistance. How would this be graded on the standard motor strength scale? 1/5 2/5 3/5 4/5 5/5 3 3/5 indicates movement against gravity only with no ability to resist applied force. 5/5 = full strength; 4/5 = slight weakness but overcomes resistance; 2/5 = movement with gravity eliminated; 1/5 = trace contraction; 0/5 = no movement."}, {"id": "l108", "title": "Lecture #108: DPR: History and Physical Exam of the Shoulder and Elbow", "module": "DPR", "content": "l108 Lecture 108 DPR History and Physical Exam of the Shoulder and Elbow lecturer A. Coladner D.O. DPR pdf content/L108 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 108 DPR History and Physical Exam of the Shoulder and Elbow Lecturer A. Coladner D.O. | Session Lecture 108 The evaluation of the shoulder and elbow requires a systematic approach moving from a thorough musculoskeletal history to a standardized physical examination sequence Inspection Palpation Range of Motion Active and Passive Strength Testing and Special Testing. Understanding the anatomy—the shoulder as a ball-and-socket joint and the elbow as a hinge joint—is critical for localizing pathology to specific structures such as tendons ligaments or the joint capsule itself. --- History and General Approach A musculoskeletal history should utilize the OPQRST mnemonic while emphasizing specific factors like prior injuries repetitive movements occupational or recreational and the exact localization of pain. - Deep vs. Superficial Deep pain often suggests tears or bone bruises while superficial pain may relate to skin lesions or fatty tumors. - Pain Localization Asking the patient to point with one finger helps distinguish intra-articular from extra-articular pathology. - Radiation Pain persisting for more than three months can radiate significantly e.g. carpal tunnel pain radiating to the shoulder . Practitioners must identify where the pain started to avoid being misled by radiation. - Constitutional Symptoms Fever chills or night sweats suggest an infectious or systemic inflammatory process rather than purely mechanical injury. highyield Radiation of musculoskeletal pain is common; always ask where the pain started . Pointing with one finger to the joint line often indicates intra-articular pathology. --- The Shoulder Physical Exam Inspection and Palpation Inspection must be performed from both the front and back to check for asymmetry muscular atrophy or scars. Bony Landmarks to Palpate - Clavicle and Acromioclavicular AC joint. - Acromion and Spine of the scapula. - Greater Tuberosity/Head of the humerus. - Bicipital Groove Contains the long head of the biceps tendon a frequent site of tenosynovitis. Range of Motion ROM and Strength Shoulder ROM is tested in multiple planes Flexion Extension Abduction Adduction Internal and External rotation. | Movement | Primary Muscles | Clinical Note | |---|---|---| | Flexion | Anterior Deltoid Pectoralis Major | | | Abduction | Supraspinatus first 15° Deltoid after 15° | Essential for Painful Arc testing | | Internal Rotation | Subscapularis Pectoralis Major | Measured by level on T-spine reached by thumb | | External Rotation | Infraspinatus Teres Minor | Tested with elbow at 90° | Manual Muscle Testing MMT Scale - 5/5 Normal strength against full resistance. - 4/5 Movement against gravity with some resistance. - 3/5 Movement against gravity only. - 2/5 Movement with gravity eliminated. - 1/5 Trace flicker of muscle contraction. - 0/5 No contraction paralysis . correlate Scapulohumeral Rhythm Abduction of the shoulder requires both glenohumeral and scapulothoracic movement. The supraspinatus initiates the first 15° of abduction after which the deltoid becomes the primary abductor. Weakness or pain during initiation often points to supraspinatus pathology. --- Shoulder Special Testing Special tests are designed to isolate specific structures or pathologies through provocation. | Test | Target / Finding | Technique | |---|---|---| | Neer Impingement | Shoulder Impingement | Forward pressure on scapula while raising arm; pain = positive | | Hawkins Impingement | Shoulder Impingement | Internal rotation of arm flexed at 90° at elbow/shoulder | | Empty Can Test | Supraspinatus Tendon | Push up against resistance with arms at 30° thumbs down | | Drop Arm Codman | Rotator Cuff Tear | Patient lowers abducted arm slowly; sudden drop = positive | | Apprehension Test | Anterior Instability | Patient feels shoulder will dislocate with external rotation/anterior pressure | | Yergason Test | Biceps Tendon Long Head | Resistance to supination with elbow flexed; pain in bicipital groove | | Crossover Test | AC Joint Pathology | Passive adduction of arm across the chest | highyield Impingement Signs Neer and Hawkins tests are the clinical standards for assessing subacromial impingement. Rotator Cuff Integrity The Empty Can test specifically isolates the supraspinatus while the Drop Arm test highly suggests a full-thickness tear. --- The Elbow Physical Exam The elbow is a stable hinge joint capable of flexion extension pronation and supination. Inspection and Palpation Palpation focuses on the Medial and Lateral Epicondyles and the Olecranon . The Ulnar Nerve is palpated within the ulnar groove medial to the olecranon ; it is superficial and easily irritated. Elbow Special Testing - Tinel s Sign Elbow Tapping over the ulnar nerve at the medial epicondyle. Paresthesia in the ulnar distribution suggests cubital tunnel syndrome. - Cozen’s Test Lateral Epicondylitis Resisted wrist extension with the elbow flexed. Pain at the lateral epicondyle indicates Tennis Elbow. - Medial Epicondylitis Test Resisted wrist flexion. Pain at the medial epicondyle indicates Golfer’s Elbow. correlate Epicondylitis vs. Nerve Entrapment - Lateral Epicondylitis Overuse of wrist extensors attachment site . - Medial Epicondylitis Overuse of wrist flexors attachment site . - Cubital Tunnel Nerve compression ulnar nerve rather than tendonitis; produces neurological symptoms numbness/tingling in 4th/5th digits . --- Summary of Common Diagnoses - Shoulder Rotator cuff pathology tendonitis/tears Glenohumeral instability Biceps tendinopathy Adhesive capsulitis Frozen shoulder . - Elbow Lateral/Medial epicondylitis Cubital tunnel syndrome Olecranon bursitis posterior . mnemonic Elbow Flexor Attachment M edial = M ake a fist Flexors L ateral = L ift the hand Extensors In-house lecture is best Shoulder and Elbow Physical Exam OnlineMedEd OMM > Shoulder What muscle initiates the first 15° of shoulder abduction? The supraspinatus . After 15° the deltoid takes over as the primary abductor. Anatomy A positive Drop Arm Codman test is highly suggestive of what pathology? A rotator cuff tear typically full-thickness . Clinical Which nerve is evaluated by tapping in the ulnar groove at the elbow Tinel s sign ? The ulnar nerve . Clinical What is the clinical name for pain at the lateral epicondyle during resisted wrist extension? Lateral epicondylitis Tennis Elbow . Clinical Describe a grade 3/5 on the Manual Muscle Testing MMT scale. The patient can perform the full range of motion against gravity only but cannot tolerate any added resistance from the examiner. Concept The Neer and Hawkins tests are designed to evaluate for what clinical syndrome? Impingement syndrome of the shoulder. Clinical match Shoulder Physical Exam Neer Hawkins Empty Can resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 01 Anatomy & Physiology 03 Upper Extremity Nerves | AK Step1 v11 Physeo 02 Anatomy 08 MSK 04 Shoulder match Rotator Cuff Muscles SITS resources AnKing Step 1 > search rotator cuff supraspinatus infraspinatus teres minor subscapularis match Lateral and Medial Epicondylitis resources AnKing Step 1 > search lateral epicondylitis medial tennis golfer match Ulnar Nerve Entrapment Cubital Tunnel resources AnKing Step 1 > search ulnar nerve cubital tunnel elbow match In-house lecture primary resource resources In-house lecture is best. Supplement with OnlineMedEd Shoulder and MSK Physical Exam modules. C5 Radiculopathy Mimic Pain in the shoulder area can occasionally be a referred symptom of a C5 radiculopathy; always screen the cervical spine if the shoulder exam is inconclusive. Dislocation Direction Anterior dislocations often occur when the arm is in external rotation while posterior dislocations rarer often via seizures/electrocution usually involve internal rotation . Bicipital Groove Palpation To best palpate the bicipital groove the arm should be slightly externally rotated to bring the groove anteriorly. questions 1 A 45-year-old painter presents with worsening right shoulder pain when working overhead. On examination there is a painful arc of motion between 60° and 120° of abduction. Strength is 5/5 in all planes. Both Neer and Hawkins tests result in sharp pain in the subacromial region. What is the most likely diagnosis? Adhesive Capsulitis Subacromial Impingement Syndrome Full-thickness Supraspinatus Tear Biceps Tendon Rupture Glenohumeral Osteoarthritis 1 Overhead activity painful arc and positive impingement signs Neer/Hawkins with preserved strength 5/5 are classic for impingement syndrome. A full-thickness tear would typically show weakness e.g. positive Empty Can or Drop Arm . 2 During a physical exam a physician asks a patient to hold their arms out at 30° of horizontal adduction and 90° of abduction with their thumbs pointing toward the floor. The patient is asked to resist downward pressure. Weakness and pain are elicited on the left side. Which muscle is primarily being tested? Deltoid Infraspinatus Teres Major Supraspinatus Subscapularis 3 This describes the Empty Can Jobe test which is specifically used to evaluate the integrity and strength of the supraspinatus muscle and tendon. 3 An amateur golfer presents with pain on the inner aspect of his elbow that worsens when he flips his wrist downward while holding a club. On examination there is point tenderness over a bony prominence on the medial side of the humerus. Pain is reproduced by resisting which of the following movements? Wrist Extension Wrist Flexion Elbow Extension Forearm Supination Shoulder External Rotation 1 Medial epicondylitis Golfer s elbow involves the common flexor tendon origin. It is characterized by tenderness at the medial epicondyle and pain reproduced by resisted wrist flexion and pronation. 4 A patient is able to actively flex their elbow against gravity through the full range of motion. However as soon as the examiner applies even minimal resistance the patient s arm gives way. What grade should be assigned to this patient s elbow flexion strength on the MMT scale? Grade 2 Grade 3 Grade 4 Grade 5 Grade 1 1 Grade 3 indicates the ability to move against gravity but not against any added resistance. Grade 4 would require some resistance and Grade 2 is for movement only with gravity eliminated. 5 A high school quarterback is tackled and feels his shoulder pop out. He presents to the ER with his arm held in a slightly abducted and externally rotated position. He is extremely resistant to any internal rotation. Which of the following special tests would most likely be positive in this patient once the acute injury is managed? Empty Can Test Drop Arm Test Apprehension Test Cozen s Test Yergason Test 2 Anterior dislocations are most common and often occur in external rotation. The apprehension test evaluates for glenohumeral instability and susceptibility to recurrent anterior dislocation."}, {"id": "l110", "title": "Lecture #110: Introduction to Orthopedics of the Upper Extremity", "module": "Orthopedics", "content": "l110 Lecture 110 Introduction to Orthopedics of the Upper Extremity lecturer M. Heller D.O. Presented by N. Chernik Academic Medicine Scholar Orthopedics pdf content/L110 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 110 Introduction to Orthopedics of the Upper Extremity Lecturer M. Heller D.O. Presented by N. Chernik Academic Medicine Scholar | Session Lecture 110 This lecture provides a systematic survey of orthopedic pathology of the upper extremity moving proximal-to-distal from the shoulder through the hand. Before diving into specific conditions the lecture establishes a framework for evaluating any orthopedic complaint obtain a thorough history focused on the mechanism of injury acute trauma vs. chronic overuse perform a complete musculoskeletal exam with relevant special tests utilize imaging X-ray CT MRI ultrasound for diagnosis and begin with conservative management RICE Rest Ice Compression Elevation before escalating to injections or surgery. A recurring clinical theme is that every fracture or dislocation carries the risk of neurovascular compromise so you must always consider the surrounding anatomy when evaluating these injuries. --- General Orthopedic Concepts Slides 2-4 The approach to any orthopedic concern begins with understanding the mechanism of injury. Is this an acute high-impact trauma that occurred minutes ago or a chronic insidious process that has been worsening over weeks to months? This distinction drives the differential and urgency. A full musculoskeletal exam should follow paired with appropriate imaging. Treatment generally starts conservative with RICE pain control and potentially joint injections escalating to surgical fixation nails pins plates fracture reductions only when necessary. - Complications to Consider with Any Fracture/Dislocation - Avascular necrosis death of bone from loss of blood supply - Nerve damage or entrapment - Compartment syndrome - Osteomyelitis infection - Deep vein thrombosis DVT --- Bone Anatomy and Fracture Types Slides 5-8 Understanding the anatomy of a long bone is essential for describing fracture location and predicting complications. The epiphysis is the end of the bone the metaphysis is the flared region between the epiphysis and the shaft the growth plate physis sits between them and the diaphysis is the long shaft. - Fracture Type Classification | Type | Description | Key Feature | |---|---|---| | Transverse | Fracture line perpendicular to the long axis | Clean horizontal break | | Oblique | Fracture at an angle to the long axis | Diagonal line | | Spiral | Fracture line encircles the shaft | Twisting mechanism | | Comminuted | More than two bone fragments | High-energy injury | | Avulsion | Fragment torn away from main bone mass | Ligament/tendon pull | | Impacted | Fragments driven into each other | Bones wedge together | | Displaced | Fragments separated from each other | Malalignment on imaging | | Compound Open | Bone pierces the skin | Infection risk; surgical emergency | - Pediatric Fractures - Greenstick Fracture Incomplete fracture extending partially through the bone due to bending stress. Fails on the tension side but not the compression side. Slide 7 - Torus Buckle Fracture Cortex buckles on the compression side. Distinguished from greenstick by buckling appearance rather than a fracture line. Slide 7 highyield Greenstick fractures fail on the tension side and are intact on the compression side. Torus fractures buckle on the compression side. Both are seen in pediatric patients due to the plasticity of immature bone. --- Salter-Harris Classification Slide 8 Pediatric growth plate injuries are classified by the Salter-Harris system which predicts the risk of growth arrest. The higher the type the greater the risk of permanent growth disturbance. - Type 1 S = Straight across Fracture runs along the growth plate only. Often occult on X-ray. - Type 2 A = Above Fracture through the growth plate and metaphysis. Most common type. - Type 3 L = Lower/below Fracture through the growth plate and epiphysis. - Type 4 T = Through/Two Fracture through the metaphysis growth plate and epiphysis. Increased risk of growth arrest. - Type 5 R = Rammed/Crush Compression injury to the growth plate. 100% growth arrest rate. Often not visible on initial imaging. mnemonic Salter-Harris Classification SALTR - S Straight across Type 1 - along the growth plate - A Above Type 2 - through metaphysis - L Lower/Below Type 3 - through epiphysis - T Through/Two Type 4 - through all three - R Rammed/Crush Type 5 - compression 100% growth arrest correlate Salter-Harris Type 5 Growth Plate Injury - Pathophysiology Axial compression force crushes the growth plate physis leading to premature closure. - Presentation May present with minimal findings on initial X-ray; diagnosed clinically or retrospectively when growth arrest becomes apparent. - Diagnosis Often a clinical diagnosis; initial X-rays may be normal. MRI can help confirm. - Complications 100% rate of growth arrest leading to limb length discrepancy or angular deformity. - Lecturer s Point Type 5 is the most feared because the growth plate is essentially obliterated and the injury is often invisible on initial imaging. --- Compartment Syndrome Slides 9-10 Skeletal muscles are organized within rigid osseofascial compartments. Compartment syndrome develops when pressure within these non-compliant compartments rises to the point of compromising perfusion. It is a surgical emergency. - Acute Compartment Syndrome - A true medical emergency that can lead to permanent functional loss - Internal causes Hemorrhage or edema e.g. from fractures - External causes Constrictive bandages casts traction - Most common cause Tibial diaphyseal fracture - Other causes Burns animal bites/stings trauma - Chronic Exertional Compartment Syndrome Exercise-induced much less severe self-limiting - Clinical Features 5 Ps - Pain Especially with passive movement most sensitive early finding - Pressure Increased tension of the muscle compartments - Paresthesia Due to nerve ischemia; may become irreversible - Paralysis From nerve and muscle ischemia - Pallor/Pulselessness Late findings indicating vascular compromise - Treatment Fasciotomy surgical decompression correlate Acute Compartment Syndrome - Pathophysiology Increased intracompartmental pressure from hemorrhage or edema compromises perfusion to the muscles and nerves within a rigid osseofascial compartment. - Presentation Pain out of proportion to injury pain with passive stretch most sensitive tense compartment paresthesia progressing to paralysis pallor and pulselessness. - Diagnosis Clinical diagnosis supported by intracompartmental pressure measurement greater than 30 mmHg or within 30 mmHg of diastolic pressure . - Treatment Emergent fasciotomy. - Risk Factors Fractures especially tibial shaft crush injuries burns constrictive casts/bandages. - Complications Volkmann s contracture ischemic contracture of forearm flexors permanent nerve damage limb loss. - Lecturer s Point Pain with passive movement is the earliest and most sensitive clinical finding. Pulselessness is a late sign. highyield Compartment Syndrome 5 Ps Pain with passive stretch - most sensitive Pressure Paresthesia Paralysis Pallor/Pulselessness late . Treatment is emergent fasciotomy . --- Bursitis Slide 11 A bursa is a lubricated fluid-filled sac located between soft tissues that reduces friction near joints. When bursae become irritated or inflamed the result is bursitis. A key distinguishing feature from tendinopathy is that bursitis pain occurs at rest whereas tendinopathy pain is elicited with movement . Lecturer Emphasis - Acute Bursitis Pain and swelling developing over a few days. If onset is rapid hours consider septic bursitis. - Chronic Bursitis Symptoms for weeks to months; may cause functional limitation from joint contractures. - Causes Chronic overuse infection autoimmune disorders - Common Locations Olecranon shoulder subacromial knee prepatellar Achilles --- Shoulder Anatomy and Dislocations Slides 12-17 The shoulder girdle consists of the clavicle and scapula. The sternoclavicular SC joint is the only true articulation connecting the upper extremity to the axial skeleton. The glenohumeral joint is a ball-and-socket joint with the greatest mobility of all joints in the body but this mobility comes at the cost of stability. The labrum dense fibrous tissue lining the glenoid fossa and the rotator cuff muscles serve as stabilizers. - Rotator Cuff Muscles SITS - Supraspinatus Initiates abduction 0-15 degrees ; innervated by suprascapular nerve C5-C6 - Infraspinatus External rotation; innervated by suprascapular nerve C5-C6 - Teres Minor External rotation with adduction; innervated by axillary nerve C5-C6 - Subscapularis Internal rotation; innervated by subscapular nerves C5-C6 - Shoulder Abduction Range of Motion - 0-15 degrees Supraspinatus - 15-90 degrees Deltoid - Greater than 90 degrees Trapezius and serratus anterior Anterior Shoulder Dislocation Slide 14 The most common type of shoulder dislocation. The mechanism involves a force applied when the arm is in abduction external rotation and extension e.g. a blow to the medial elbow in this position . The humeral head dislocates anteriorly traumatizing the joint capsule and labrum. - Associated Lesions - Bankart Lesion Anterior-inferior capsular injury with tear of the glenoid labrum - Hill-Sachs Lesion Compression fracture of the posterolateral humeral head - Both lesions increase the likelihood of recurrent dislocations - Presentation Arm held in slight abduction forearm supported by opposite hand unable to internally rotate dimple under the acromion - Treatment Reduce as quickly as possible immobilize 2-6 weeks then rehab focused on rotator cuff strengthening correlate Anterior Shoulder Dislocation with Bankart and Hill-Sachs Lesions - Pathophysiology Force applied during abduction external rotation and extension drives the humeral head anteriorly tearing the anterior-inferior labrum Bankart and creating a compression fracture on the posterolateral humeral head Hill-Sachs as it impacts the glenoid rim. - Presentation Patient holds arm in slight abduction unable to internally rotate. Dimple visible under the acromion. - Diagnosis AP and lateral X-ray; MRI for labral tear confirmation. - Treatment Emergent reduction immobilization for 2-6 weeks physical therapy focusing on rotator cuff strengthening. Surgery if recurrent. - Complications Recurrent instability especially in young patients axillary nerve injury rotator cuff tears. - Lecturer s Point Both Bankart and Hill-Sachs lesions increase the likelihood of recurrent dislocations. Posterior Shoulder Dislocation Slide 15 Rare and commonly missed on imaging. Associated with seizures electrocution or long-term shoulder instability. - Light Bulb Sign On AP X-ray the internally rotated humerus appears like a light bulb due to widening of the glenohumeral joint space. - Presentation Arm held with hand on the belly; arm cannot be externally rotated. --- Labral Tears Bankart and SLAP Slides 16-17 Bankart Tear A tear of the anterior-inferior labrum typically occurring with anterior dislocations. Symptoms include popping catching grinding and locking of the shoulder. SLAP Tear Superior Labrum Anterior to Posterior A tear of the superior labrum running from front to back. The superior glenoid serves as the attachment point for the long head of the biceps. - Mechanism Acute injury or chronic degradation from repetitive overhead motion throwers swimmers - Presentation Popping catching grinding locking; limited bicep contraction due to long head of biceps attachment on superior labrum - Treatment Unclear ideal treatment; start conservatively --- AC Joint Dislocations and Clavicular Fractures Slides 18-19 AC Joint Dislocation The AC joint is the only articulation between the clavicle and scapula. Injury typically occurs from direct trauma to the shoulder with the arm adducted e.g. a fall onto the point of the shoulder . - Presentation Pain loss of scapular function tenderness over the AC joint palpable step-off deformity - Management Conservative with ice immobilization sling for a few days to six weeks depending on severity Clavicular Fracture Slide 19 The most common fracture in children and a common birth trauma injury. Typically caused by a fall on an outstretched hand FOOSH or direct shoulder trauma. - Most common site Middle third midshaft - Presentation Shoulder drop - the SCM muscle elevates the medial fragment but the lateral fragment drops due to the weight of the arm - Treatment Sling and non-surgical management --- Rotator Cuff Injuries and Tears Slides 20-22 The supraspinatus is the most commonly injured rotator cuff muscle. All four rotator cuff muscles are innervated by C5-C6. The supraspinatus infraspinatus and teres minor attach to the greater tubercle of the humerus while the subscapularis attaches to the lesser tubercle. - Mechanism Acute trauma younger patients or chronic overuse/degradation older patients - Presentation - Anterolateral shoulder pain - Nighttime pain cannot sleep on affected side Lecturer Emphasis - Atrophy of supraspinatus/infraspinatus muscles - Weakness in specific ROM depending on which muscle is torn - Special Tests - Empty Can Jobe Test Arms elevated to shoulder level thumbs pointing down emptying a can . Weakness/pain with downward pressure = supraspinatus tear. - Drop Arm Test Patient fully abducts then slowly lowers the arm. Sudden drop = supraspinatus tear suggests full-thickness tear . - Treatment Conservative management for partial tears; arthroscopic surgery for severe tears in younger patients; conservative approach for elderly correlate Rotator Cuff Tear - Pathophysiology Disruption of the rotator cuff tendon s most commonly the supraspinatus from acute trauma or chronic degenerative overuse. - Presentation Anterolateral shoulder pain worse with overhead activity and at night unable to sleep on affected side . Weakness in abduction initiation supraspinatus external rotation infraspinatus or internal rotation subscapularis . - Diagnosis MRI is the gold standard. Empty Can test and Drop Arm test on physical exam. - Treatment Conservative PT NSAIDs corticosteroid injections for partial tears. Arthroscopic repair for full-thickness tears especially in younger active patients. - Risk Factors Age greater than 40 repetitive overhead activity prior shoulder injury. - Lecturer s Point The supraspinatus is the most commonly injured rotator cuff muscle. Nighttime pain and inability to sleep on the affected side is a classic presentation. --- Shoulder Impingement Slides 23-25 One of the most common causes of shoulder pain. It encompasses pain caused by rotator cuff tendinitis subacromial bursitis and biceps tendinitis. The mechanism involves anatomic narrowing of the subacromial space irritating the tendons within. - Clinical Progression Edema then fibrosis then tendinitis - History Repetitive overhead activity - Special Tests - Hawkins Test Flex the humerus and elbow to 90 degrees then maximally internally rotate the shoulder with overpressure. Pain in the superior shoulder = positive. - Neer Test Stabilize the scapula with downward force then fully flex the humerus overhead with overpressure. Pain in the superior shoulder = positive. - Treatment Mild disease RICE. Chronic pain Cortisone injections. Severe Surgical acromioplasty removal of part of the acromion/bursa . --- Adhesive Capsulitis Frozen Shoulder Slide 26 Fibrosis inflammation and capsular contracture of the shoulder capsule causing pathologic loss of both active and passive range of motion. - Types Primary idiopathic and secondary due to underlying conditions - Demographics Middle-aged patients non-dominant arm - Associations Diabetes Lecturer Emphasis - Presentation Pain on palpation of the anterior and posterior capsule; loss of both active and passive ROM - Treatment Conservative - physical therapy and oral medications correlate Adhesive Capsulitis Frozen Shoulder - Pathophysiology Fibrosis and contracture of the glenohumeral joint capsule leading to progressive loss of both active and passive range of motion. - Presentation Insidious onset of shoulder pain and stiffness. Loss of both active and passive ROM distinguishes it from rotator cuff pathology where passive ROM is preserved . - Diagnosis Clinical diagnosis. Loss of external rotation is typically the earliest and most affected movement. - Treatment Physical therapy NSAIDs corticosteroid injections. Most cases self-resolve over 1-3 years. - Risk Factors Diabetes mellitus thyroid disease prolonged immobilization. - Lecturer s Point Affects middle-aged patients in the non-dominant arm and is commonly associated with diabetes. --- Humeral Fractures and Nerve Injuries Slides 27-31 A critical concept in this lecture is that each level of humeral fracture has a predictable nerve and artery at risk. The mnemonic ARM maps the first letter of each nerve Axillary Radial Median to the fracture location from proximal to distal. | Fracture Site | Nerve at Risk | Artery at Risk | Key Presentation | |---|---|---|---| | Surgical Neck | Axillary nerve | Posterior circumflex humeral artery | Flattened deltoid loss of abduction greater than 15 degrees loss of sensation over lateral deltoid | | Mid-shaft | Radial nerve | Deep brachial artery | Wrist drop loss of elbow/wrist/finger extension loss of sensation over posterior arm/forearm/dorsal hand | | Supracondylar | Median nerve | Brachial artery | Loss of wrist flexion loss of lateral 2 lumbricals opponens pollicis and abductor pollicis brevis function | | Medial Epicondyle | Ulnar nerve | -- | Paresthesia in digits 4-5 decreased grip from loss of 4th/5th digit flexion | mnemonic Humeral Fracture Nerve Injuries ARM - A Axillary nerve Surgical neck fracture - R Radial nerve Mid-shaft fracture - M Median nerve Supracondylar fracture highyield Mid-shaft humeral fracture damages the radial nerve causing wrist drop loss of wrist/finger extension and lost sensation over the posterior arm forearm and dorsal hand. This presentation is similar to Saturday Night Palsy compression of the radial nerve from draping the arm over a hard surface . --- Volkmann s Contracture Slide 32 A devastating complication of supracondylar humeral fracture. Swelling from the fracture can cause compartment syndrome in the forearm compressing the brachial artery and median nerve. This leads to irreversible ischemia of the forearm flexor muscles which shorten and scar over time. - Presentation Fingers and wrists held in permanent flexion; passive extension is nearly impossible. correlate Volkmann s Contracture - Pathophysiology Irreversible ischemic contracture of the forearm flexor compartment due to compartment syndrome most commonly following a supracondylar humeral fracture. The brachial artery is compressed causing ischemia. The median nerve may also be damaged. - Presentation Fingers and wrist held in flexion with passive extension nearly impossible. The forearm is firm and tender. - Treatment Prevention via early fasciotomy for compartment syndrome. Established contracture may require tendon lengthening or release surgery. - Lecturer s Point This is the link between a supracondylar fracture and compartment syndrome - the swelling from the fracture compresses the brachial artery leading to ischemia of the forearm flexors. --- Elbow Pathology Slides 33-39 Elbow Fractures and the Fat Pad Sign Slide 33 The posterior fat pad is normally pressed into the olecranon fossa by the triceps tendon making it invisible on lateral X-ray. When an elbow fracture occurs the resulting joint effusion pushes the fat pad out of the fossa making it visible the sail sign . Lecturer Emphasis - This may be the only visible marker of a fracture especially in pediatric patients Elbow Dislocations Slide 34 - Anterior dislocation Anterior-to-posterior force - Posterior dislocation Fall on an outstretched hand FOOSH - more common - Simple no fracture vs. Complex with associated fractures - Can have associated ligament tears fractures and ulnar nerve injury Olecranon Bursitis Slide 35 Can look similar to an elbow dislocation on inspection but mechanism is key. It is a chronic slowly developing condition from repeated excessive pressure on the elbow. Common in students athletes mechanics and plumbers. - Treatment RICE avoid aggravating activity aspiration if needed corticosteroid injections - If septic bursitis is suspected draw cultures when draining Nursemaid s Elbow Radial Head Subluxation Slide 36 Occurs when a child is pulled rapidly by the hand or wrist displacing the radial head from the immature annular ligament. - Presentation Child holds arm in flexion pronation and adduction - Treatment Reduction with marked supination and flexion audible pop immediate improvement in ROM UCL Tear Tommy John Injury Slide 37 The ulnar collateral ligament attaches the humerus to the ulna. Injury occurs from valgus force at 50-90 degrees of flexion typically during the acceleration phase of throwing pitchers quarterbacks . --- Forearm and Wrist Slides 38-44 Epicondylitis Slides 38-39 | Condition | Location | Mechanism | Nerve | Physical Exam | |---|---|---|---|---| | Lateral Epicondylitis Tennis Elbow | Lateral epicondyle | Overuse of wrist/finger extensors | Radial nerve | Pain with palpation of lateral epicondyle; pain with resisted active wrist extension | | Medial Epicondylitis Golfer s Elbow | Medial epicondyle | Overuse of wrist flexors/forearm pronators | Median nerve | Pain with palpation of medial epicondyle; pain with resisted active wrist flexion | mnemonic Epicondylitis Mnemonic MeGaLaTe - Me = Me dial epicondylitis = Golfer s elbow flexors median nerve - LaTe = La teral epicondylitis = Te nnis elbow extensors radial nerve Distal Radius Fracture Slide 40 One of the most common injuries in orthopedics. Caused by a fall on an outstretched hand FOOSH . Higher risk with osteoporosis in the elderly. Cubital Tunnel Syndrome Slide 41 Entrapment of the ulnar nerve at the elbow within the cubital tunnel 2-3 inch tunnel near the medial epicondyle . The ulnar nerve is compressed by rigid connective tissue. - Presentation Electrical shooting sensation down the ulnar distribution digits 4-5 with tapping over cubital tunnel Tinel s sign at the elbow ; paresthesia in digits 4-5; weakness of intrinsic hand muscles; inability to flex digits 4-5 weak flexor digitorum profundus Scaphoid Fracture Slide 42 The most commonly fractured carpal bone. Caused by a FOOSH. The scaphoid has a retrograde blood supply from a branch of the radial artery blood enters distally and flows proximally . This means a more proximal fracture has a higher risk of avascular necrosis and nonunion. - Presentation Pain and limited wrist ROM; tenderness in the anatomic snuffbox very sensitive test - Key Point X-rays may have low sensitivity for scaphoid fractures. If snuffbox tenderness is present with a negative X-ray further imaging CT/MRI is warranted. Lecturer Emphasis - Treatment Distal fractures low AVN risk Spica cast for up to 20 weeks. Proximal fractures high AVN risk or athletes Consider surgical fixation. correlate Scaphoid Fracture with Avascular Necrosis Risk - Pathophysiology The scaphoid receives its blood supply in a retrograde fashion distal-to-proximal from a branch of the radial artery. Proximal fractures disrupt this supply leading to avascular necrosis. - Presentation Wrist pain after FOOSH with tenderness in the anatomic snuffbox. X-ray may be initially negative. - Diagnosis Clinical suspicion based on snuffbox tenderness. If X-ray is negative obtain MRI or repeat imaging in 10-14 days. - Treatment Thumb spica cast for up to 20 weeks for distal fractures. Surgical fixation for proximal fractures or displaced fractures. - Complications Avascular necrosis nonunion more common in proximal pole fractures . - Lecturer s Point Scaphoid fractures are often missed on your initial X-ray - a negative X-ray with snuffbox tenderness should prompt further imaging. --- Hand Pathology Slides 45-52 Metacarpal Fracture Boxer s Fracture Slide 45 The most common hand injury. Caused by a direct blow to the hand punching . Typically involves the 4th and 5th metacarpals. Deformity at the metacarpal base shortening of the fractured metacarpal but typically no motor deficits. Fight Bite Slide 46 A clenched fist striking a tooth can rupture the MCP joint capsule inoculating it with oral flora. Must be presumed to be a joint-penetrating wound until proven otherwise. - Common pathogens Streptococcus viridans Group A Strep Staphylococcus aureus Eikenella corrodens - Treatment Prophylactic antibiotics; wound exploration Ganglion Cyst Bible Cyst Slide 47 A fluid-filled swelling overlying a joint or tendon sheath most commonly on the dorsal wrist. The key differentiating feature is that it transilluminates light shines through it distinguishing it from solid tumors. - Usually painless; spontaneous resolution is common - Treatment Observation. Aspiration or surgery if symptomatic. Trigger Finger Stenosing Tenosynovitis Slide 48 The finger catches and locks in flexion due to mechanical impingement of the flexor tendon at the A1 pulley of the hand. The tendon gets trapped in flexion and may remain locked. - Risk Factor Diabetes Lecturer Emphasis Mallet Finger Slide 49 Prolonged flexion of the DIP joint due to disruption of the terminal extensor tendon distal to the DIP. Caused by a blow to the distal tip of the digit e.g. catching a ball or an avulsion fracture at the DIP. - Treatment Splinting the DIP in extension Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Slides 50-51 Entrapment of the median nerve in the carpal tunnel between the transverse carpal ligament and the carpal bones . Can also result from a lunate dislocation. - Presentation - Paresthesia pain numbness in the median nerve distribution digits 1-3 and lateral half of digit 4 - Thenar eminence atrophy with spared sensation - the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve enters the hand external to the carpal tunnel preserving thenar sensation Lecturer Emphasis - Worse at night - Special Tests - Tinel s Sign Percussion over the carpal tunnel causes tingling - Phalen s Maneuver Bilateral wrist flexion causes tingling - Associations Pregnancy rheumatoid arthritis hypothyroidism diabetes acromegaly amyloidosis repetitive wrist use correlate Carpal Tunnel Syndrome - Pathophysiology Compression of the median nerve within the carpal tunnel due to increased pressure edema tenosynovitis anatomic variants . The palmar cutaneous branch travels superficial to the transverse carpal ligament sparing thenar sensation. - Presentation Numbness and tingling in digits 1-3 and lateral 4. Thenar atrophy but spared thenar sensation. Worse at night. Positive Tinel s and Phalen s tests. - Diagnosis Clinical diagnosis confirmed by nerve conduction studies/electromyography. - Treatment Wrist splinting especially at night NSAIDs corticosteroid injection. Surgical carpal tunnel release for refractory cases. - Risk Factors Pregnancy hypothyroidism diabetes rheumatoid arthritis acromegaly amyloidosis repetitive wrist flexion. - Lecturer s Point Thenar atrophy with spared sensation is the hallmark - the palmar cutaneous branch bypasses the carpal tunnel. Guyon s Canal Syndrome Slide 52 Compression of the ulnar nerve at the wrist in Guyon s canal. Can be caused by a hook of hamate fracture. Classically seen in cyclists due to pressure at the wrist. - Presentation Weakness of intrinsic hand muscles decreased grip clawing of digits 4-5 hypothenar atrophy - Key Distinction from Cubital Tunnel In Guyon s canal syndrome extrinsic forearm muscles flexor carpi ulnaris flexor digitorum profundus are normal because the compression is distal to their innervation. In cubital tunnel syndrome these muscles are weak because the compression is proximal. De Quervain s Tenosynovitis Slide 53 Thickening of the abductor pollicis longus APL and extensor pollicis brevis EPB tendons due to excessive friction against the extensor retinaculum. - Commonly seen in New parents holding a child with thumb extended racket sports golfers - Presentation Pain at the radial styloid swelling - Special Test Finkelstein s Test - make a fist around the thumb then ulnar deviate the wrist. Pain = positive. - Treatment Splinting Septic Flexor Tenosynovitis Slide 54 Infection of the synovial sheath surrounding the flexor tendon typically from penetrating trauma. Diagnosed by the Kanavel Signs 4 cardinal signs 1. Flexed posturing of the digit 2. Tenderness to palpation over the tendon sheath 3. Marked pain with passive extension of the digit 4. Fusiform sausage-like swelling of the digit - Treatment IV antibiotics and surgical irrigation/debridement correlate Septic Flexor Tenosynovitis - Pathophysiology Bacterial infection of the flexor tendon sheath usually from a penetrating wound. The closed-space infection can spread rapidly along the tendon sheath. - Presentation Kanavel signs flexed posturing pain with passive extension fusiform swelling and tenderness along the tendon sheath. - Diagnosis Clinical Kanavel signs . Aspiration of the tendon sheath for culture if uncertain. - Treatment IV antibiotics and emergent surgical irrigation and debridement to prevent tendon necrosis. - Complications Tendon necrosis loss of digit function spread of infection to adjacent compartments. - Lecturer s Point The four Kanavel signs are the key to recognizing this surgical emergency. BandB Musculoskeletal > Orthopedics > Upper Extremity Pathoma Musculoskeletal Pathology > Bone Tumors and Fractures FirstAid Musculoskeletal Skin and Connective Tissue > Upper Extremity Injuries SketchyPath Musculoskeletal > Bone and Joint Disorders Physeo Anatomy > MSK > Shoulder and Upper Extremity What fracture classification system is used for pediatric growth plate injuries? The Salter-Harris classification Types 1-5 . Remember SALTR Straight across Above Lower Through/Two Rammed/crush. Type 5 has a 100% growth arrest rate. Concept What are the 5 Ps of compartment syndrome? Pain especially with passive stretch - most sensitive Pressure Paresthesia Paralysis Pallor/Pulselessness late findings . Treatment is emergent fasciotomy. Clinical A mid-shaft humeral fracture damages which nerve and what is the classic presentation? The radial nerve . Classic presentation is wrist drop inability to extend the wrist fingers and elbow with loss of sensation over the posterior arm forearm and dorsal hand. Clinical What two lesions are associated with anterior shoulder dislocation? 1 Bankart lesion - tear of the anterior-inferior glenoid labrum. 2 Hill-Sachs lesion - compression fracture of the posterolateral humeral head. Both increase risk of recurrent dislocation. Clinical What is the light bulb sign on X-ray? It indicates a posterior shoulder dislocation . The humerus internally rotates causing the glenohumeral joint to widen giving the humeral head a light bulb appearance on AP X-ray. Clinical What muscle initiates the first 0-15 degrees of shoulder abduction? The supraspinatus . The deltoid handles 15-90 degrees and the trapezius and serratus anterior handle greater than 90 degrees. Concept What mnemonic maps humeral fracture locations to their at-risk nerves? ARM A xillary nerve surgical neck R adial nerve mid-shaft M edian nerve supracondylar . The medial epicondyle fracture injures the ulnar nerve. Concept What is Volkmann s contracture and what causes it? Irreversible ischemic contracture of the forearm flexor muscles. Caused by compartment syndrome usually from a supracondylar humeral fracture compressing the brachial artery. Fingers and wrist are held in permanent flexion. Clinical Why does a proximal scaphoid fracture have a higher risk of avascular necrosis? The scaphoid has a retrograde blood supply from a branch of the radial artery entering distally and flowing proximally . A proximal fracture disrupts blood flow to the proximal pole causing AVN. Concept What is the key physical exam finding to differentiate a ganglion cyst from a solid tumor? A ganglion cyst transilluminates light passes through the fluid-filled cyst while solid tumors do not. Clinical In carpal tunnel syndrome why is thenar sensation spared despite thenar muscle atrophy? The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve travels superficial to the transverse carpal ligament external to the carpal tunnel so sensation to the thenar region is preserved even though the motor fibers within the tunnel are compressed. Clinical What are the 4 Kanavel signs of septic flexor tenosynovitis? 1 Flexed posturing of the digit 2 Tenderness along the tendon sheath 3 Pain with passive extension 4 Fusiform sausage-like swelling. Treatment is IV antibiotics and surgical irrigation. Clinical What is nursemaid s elbow and how is it treated? Radial head subluxation in a child caused by sudden traction on the hand/wrist. The immature annular ligament slips over the radial head. Treatment reduction with supination and flexion audible pop immediate improvement . Clinical How do you differentiate cubital tunnel syndrome from Guyon s canal syndrome? Both involve the ulnar nerve. In Guyon s canal wrist compression extrinsic forearm muscles FCU FDP are normal . In cubital tunnel elbow compression these forearm muscles are weak because the lesion is proximal to their innervation. Clinical What is the Finkelstein test and what does it diagnose? Make a fist around the thumb then ulnar deviate the wrist. Pain at the radial styloid = positive for de Quervain s tenosynovitis thickening of the APL and EPB tendons . Clinical match Salter-Harris Fracture Classification resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 17 Common fractures | search salter harris growth plate match Compartment Syndrome 5 Ps Fasciotomy resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 17 Common fractures | search compartment syndrome fasciotomy match Rotator Cuff Muscles SITS and Injuries resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 01 Anatomy & Physiology 03 Upper Extremity Nerves | search rotator cuff supraspinatus infraspinatus teres minor subscapularis match Upper Extremity Nerve Injuries ARM Mnemonic resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 01 Anatomy & Physiology 03 Upper Extremity Nerves | search axillary nerve radial nerve wrist drop median nerve match Shoulder Dislocation Bankart Hill-Sachs resources AnKing Step 1 > search anterior shoulder dislocation bankart hill sachs match Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Median Nerve Compression resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 01 Anatomy & Physiology 03 Upper Extremity Nerves | search carpal tunnel median nerve tinel phalen match Scaphoid Fracture and Avascular Necrosis resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 17 Common fractures | search scaphoid avascular necrosis snuffbox match Volkmann s Contracture Supracondylar Fracture Complication resources AnKing Step 1 > search volkmann contracture supracondylar compartment syndrome match Epicondylitis Tennis Elbow Golfer s Elbow resources AnKing Step 1 > search lateral epicondylitis tennis elbow medial golfer match Nursemaid s Elbow Radial Head Subluxation resources AnKing Step 1 > search nursemaid elbow radial head subluxation annular ligament match De Quervain s Tenosynovitis Finkelstein Test resources AnKing Step 1 > search de quervain tenosynovitis finkelstein test match Septic Flexor Tenosynovitis Kanavel Signs resources AnKing Step 1 > search kanavel signs flexor tenosynovitis septic ARM Mnemonic for Humeral Fracture Nerves Surgical neck = Axillary Mid-shaft = Radial Supracondylar = Median. The first letter of each nerve spells ARM. Medial epicondyle = Ulnar nerve. Scaphoid X-ray Pitfall Scaphoid fractures are frequently missed on initial X-ray. If the patient has anatomic snuffbox tenderness after a FOOSH treat as a fracture thumb spica cast and obtain follow-up imaging CT or MRI even if the initial X-ray is negative. Thenar Sensation in Carpal Tunnel Carpal tunnel syndrome causes thenar muscle atrophy but spared thenar sensation because the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve travels superficial to the transverse carpal ligament. Bursitis vs. Tendinopathy Bursitis causes pain at rest while tendinopathy causes pain with movement . This distinction helps differentiate two conditions that otherwise present very similarly. Posterior Dislocation Clue A posterior shoulder dislocation is rare and commonly missed. Look for the light bulb sign on AP X-ray and an arm held in internal rotation hand on belly cannot externally rotate . Associate with seizures and electrocution. Guyon s vs. Cubital Tunnel Both involve the ulnar nerve but location matters . Guyon s canal wrist spares the forearm muscles FCU FDP remain normal . Cubital tunnel elbow affects them because the lesion is proximal. Greenstick vs. Torus Fracture Greenstick fractures show a fracture line on the tension side. Torus fractures show buckling on the compression side. Both occur in pediatric patients due to bone plasticity. Adhesive Capsulitis and Diabetes Frozen shoulder adhesive capsulitis is strongly associated with diabetes mellitus . It presents with loss of both active AND passive ROM distinguishing it from rotator cuff pathology where passive ROM is typically preserved. questions 1 A 7-year-old boy falls off a jungle gym onto his outstretched left arm. X-ray reveals a fracture line that extends through the growth plate and into the metaphysis but the epiphysis is intact. According to the Salter-Harris classification what type of fracture does this patient most likely have? Type 1 Type 2 Type 3 Type 4 Type 5 1 A Salter-Harris Type 2 fracture runs through the growth plate AND the metaphysis \\ Above\\ . Type 1 is along the growth plate only Type 3 involves the epiphysis Type 4 involves all three and Type 5 is a compression injury. 2 A 30-year-old male is brought to the ED after a motorcycle accident. He has severe pain in his left leg that worsens with passive toe extension. The anterior compartment is tense and firm. He has paresthesia over the dorsum of his foot. What is the most appropriate next step in management? Apply ice and elevate the limb Administer IV morphine for pain control Obtain an emergent MRI of the leg Perform an emergent fasciotomy Apply a compression bandage 3 This presentation pain with passive stretch tense compartment paresthesia is classic for acute compartment syndrome which is a surgical emergency. The definitive treatment is emergent fasciotomy. Compression or elevation may worsen the condition. 3 A 40-year-old man falls from a ladder onto his outstretched arm. X-ray shows a fracture of the humeral mid-shaft. On exam he is unable to extend his wrist and has decreased sensation over the posterior forearm and dorsal hand. Which nerve is most likely injured? Axillary nerve Musculocutaneous nerve Radial nerve Median nerve Ulnar nerve 2 Mid-shaft humeral fractures classically damage the radial nerve which runs in the spiral radial groove. This causes wrist drop inability to extend the wrist and loss of sensation over the posterior arm/forearm and dorsal hand. The ARM mnemonic Axillary surgical neck Radial mid-shaft Median supracondylar . 4 A 48-year-old accountant presents with numbness and tingling in her first three fingers that is worst at night. On exam she has thenar atrophy but intact sensation over the thenar eminence. Which anatomic structure explains the preserved thenar sensation? The ulnar nerve s dorsal cutaneous branch The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve The superficial branch of the radial nerve The anterior interosseous nerve The recurrent branch of the median nerve 1 In carpal tunnel syndrome the median nerve is compressed within the carpal tunnel causing thenar atrophy. However the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve travels superficial to the transverse carpal ligament outside the tunnel so thenar sensation is spared. 5 A 22-year-old college student presents with wrist pain after falling onto her outstretched hand during a volleyball game. X-ray of the wrist is unremarkable. However she has significant tenderness in the anatomic snuffbox. What is the most appropriate next step? Discharge with NSAIDs and reassurance Immobilize in a thumb spica cast and obtain follow-up imaging Perform an emergent MRI of the cervical spine Inject corticosteroids into the wrist joint Refer for immediate surgical fixation 1 Anatomic snuffbox tenderness after a FOOSH is highly suspicious for a scaphoid fracture which is frequently missed on initial X-ray. The standard approach is to immobilize in a thumb spica cast and obtain follow-up imaging CT or repeat X-ray in 10-14 days . The retrograde blood supply of the scaphoid makes AVN a significant concern if the fracture is not properly managed."}, {"id": "l111", "title": "Lecture #111: Orthopedics: Lower Extremity", "module": "Orthopedics", "content": "l111 Lecture 111 Orthopedics Lower Extremity lecturer M. Heller D.O. Orthopedics pdf content/L111 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 111 Orthopedics Lower Extremity Lecturer M. Heller D.O. | Session Lecture 111 1. Introduction Shattering the Four-Minute Mile Barrier Dr. Heller a sports medicine specialist and NYITCOM alumnus begins this deep dive into lower extremity orthopedics with a historical parallel Roger Bannister . In 1954 Bannister—a medical student—ran the first sub-four-minute mile a milestone once deemed physiologically impossible. Once he shattered that psychological barrier the impossible became routine for top athletes worldwide. For medical students lower extremity orthopedics often represents a similar barrier—a dense complex region of anatomy and trauma. The lesson Persistence and diligence transform the daunting into the attainable. The lower extremity is built for weight-bearing and locomotion requiring a masterclass in stability and mechanical alignment. This session moves from the proximal hip down to the distal ankle covering trauma degeneration and the silent emergencies of the vascular system. --- 2. The Hip Anatomy Stability and Blood Supply The Ball-and-Socket Architecture The hip is a true ball-and-socket joint. Unlike the shallow glenohumeral joint of the shoulder the hip is inherently stable with the femoral head the ball nesting deep within the acetabulum the socket . - Acetabulum & Labrum The acetabulum is part of the pelvic bone. Its surface is lined with articular cartilage for low-friction movement. The labrum is a specialized fibrocartilage rim that deepens the socket providing a secondary layer of stability through intra-articular pressure effectively suctioning the femoral head into place . - Surgical Clock Orthopedists often describe the hip anatomy using a clock system where 12 o clock is superior and 3 o clock is always anterior . correlate The Medial Circumflex Femoral Artery - Anatomy The blood supply to the femoral head is precarious. It is primarily provided by the Medial Circumflex Femoral Artery which gives rise to the retinacular arteries . - The Ring of Fire These retinacular arteries ring the bell of the femoral neck. In intracapsular fractures or dislocations this ring is frequently severed. - Critical Risk Disruption of this supply leads to Avascular Necrosis AVN —the death of bone cells. Because the femoral head is a weight-bearing surface AVN results in collapse severe pain and eventual joint destruction. --- 3. Sports Medicine of the Hip Femoroacetabular Impingement FAI FAI is a mechanical mismatch causing groin pain in young athletes. It occurs when the ball and socket don t fit perfectly leading to friction and labral tears. - Cam Type A bony bump on the femoral head-neck junction. During flexion this bump grinds into the acetabular rim. - Pincer Type The acetabulum has an overhanging edge that pinches the femoral neck and labrum. - Combined Most patients have elements of both. correlate FAI and the FADIR Test - Physical Exam The hallmark for FAI is a positive FADIR Test Flexion ADduction Internal Rotation . This maneuver mechanical stresses the impingement site reproducing the patient s groin pain. - Long-term Outcome Untreated FAI is a leading cause of early-onset osteoarthritis in the hip. Hamstring and Hip Flexor Injuries - Hamstring Avulsion Often occurs at the Ischial Tuberosity the origin . It is a musculotendinous junction injury where the tendon may pull a small fragment of bone with it avulsion . Runners and athletes experiencing sudden forceful deceleration are at risk. Watch for numbness in the sciatic nerve distribution if the hematoma is large. - Hip Pointer An exquisitely painful contusion of the Iliac Crest . Because the bone is superficial direct contact e.g. a football tackle causes significant bruising and swelling of the attached muscles TFL sartorius . --- 4. High-Energy Trauma Hip Dislocations Posterior Dislocation 90% of cases This is the classic Dashboard Injury. In a motor vehicle accident MVA the knee strikes the dashboard driving the femoral head backward out of the acetabulum and into the iliac fossa. highyield Posterior Hip Dislocation Physical Exam The leg is classically 1. Shortened 2. Internally Rotated 3. Adducted and Flexed Contrast with a Hip Fracture which classically presents with External Rotation . - Case Studies - Bo Jackson A legendary multi-sport athlete whose career was cut short not by a full dislocation but by a subluxation . The hip popped out and immediately back in but this minor movement severs the blood supply leading to the AVN that ended his football career. - Pro-Wrestling Example Dr. Heller recounts a wrestler whose back was slammed into another s hip causing a true dislocation. In these cases the hip is locked and cannot be walked off. - Tua Tagovailoa The Dolphins QB suffered a high-energy hip dislocation early in his collegiate career requiring urgent reduction to save the joint. - Nerve Risk The Sciatic Nerve runs directly behind the hip and is the most commonly injured nerve in posterior dislocations. Anterior Dislocation Rare Occurs via a forced external rotation mechanism such as a skier whose skis suddenly splay apart while moving downhill. - Physical Exam The leg is Externally Rotated flexed and abducted. - Neurovascular Risk The Femoral Nerve and Artery are at direct risk in anterior injuries. --- 5. Femur Fractures and Management Classification by Location - Femoral Neck Intracapsular Extremely high risk for AVN due to the ring of fire retinacular artery disruption. - Intertrochanteric Extracapsular Occurs outside the capsule. Lower AVN risk but higher risk of surgical bleeding. - Femoral Shaft Occurs in the mid-thigh. correlate Femoral Shaft Fracture Complications - Blood Loss The thigh can hold a massive amount of blood. A closed femur fracture can lead to 1-2 liters of occult blood loss potentially causing hypovolemic shock. - Management The gold standard is Intramedullary IM Nailing where a titanium rod is placed through the center of the bone. This allows for early weight-bearing and preserves the surrounding soft tissue. --- 6. Osteoarthritis OA The Wear-and-Tear Grind OA is the progressive loss of articular cartilage height. Without this cushion bone grinds on bone. - Clinical Course OA pain is mechanical —it worsens at the end of the day and after weight-bearing activity. - Radiographic Findings Look for joint space narrowing osteophytes bone spurs subchondral sclerosis whitening of the bone and cysts. - Treatment Escalation - Conservative PT weight loss NSAIDs. - Injections Corticosteroids fast relief but can damage cartilage vs. Viscosupplementation hyaluronic acid gel shots that act as synthetic lubricant . - Definitive Total Joint Arthroplasty. --- 7. The Knee Cruciates Collaterals and Menisci Ligamentous Directionality Dr. Heller uses a fingers on the knee technique to remember ligament orientation. If you place your right hand on your right knee crossing your middle finger over your index finger - The Middle Finger represents the ACL Traveling Lateral to Medial . - The Index Finger represents the PCL Traveling Medial to Lateral . ACL The Pop and the Pivot - Mechanism Often a non-contact deceleration or pivoting injury. Odell Beckham Jr. OBJ famously tore his ACL on turf—a classic case of the foot planting while the body continues to rotate. - The Female Risk Female athletes have a 2-8x higher risk due to a wider pelvis increased Q-angle and hormonal effects on ligament laxity. - Diagnosis A sudden pop followed by immediate swelling. Lachman s Test is the gold standard most sensitive . mnemonic O Donoghue’s Unhappy Triad MAM The classic catastrophic knee injury caused by a lateral valgus blow 1. M edial Collateral Ligament MCL 2. A CL 3. M edial Meniscus Note In acute ACL tears the Lateral meniscus is actually more commonly injured first but the Medial is the textbook triad component . PCL and the Sag Sign The PCL is twice as strong as the ACL and usually requires a direct anterior blow e.g. knee hitting a dashboard . - Physical Exam The Posterior Sag Sign —the tibia drops backward when the knee is flexed to 90 degrees. Meniscal Tears Occur during twisting on a weight-bearing knee. - Symptoms Mechanical symptoms are key—locking catching or clicking. - Healing Zones The outer 1/3 Red Zone has a blood supply and can be repaired. The inner 2/3 White Zone is avascular and typically requires debridement meniscectomy because it cannot heal on its own. --- 8. Patellar and Tibial Trauma Patellar Dislocation The patella naturally wants to dislocate laterally . - Mechanism Usually a twist on an extended knee. - The Mahomes Case Patrick Mahomes famously dislocated his patella on national TV. The reduction is often simple straighten the leg and the patella glides back into place. - Medial Patellofemoral Ligament MPFL This is the primary stabilizer that prevents the patella from sliding lateral. It is almost always torn in a dislocation. Open Tibia Fracture The Kevin Ware Injury Dr. Heller cites the horrific injury of Kevin Ware whose tibia bone was seen protruding through the skin on live TV. - High Alert Open fractures are surgical emergencies . The priority is preventing osteomyelitis bone infection through immediate irrigation debridement and stabilization. --- 9. Leg and Ankle Complications and Sprains Deep Vein Thrombosis DVT A silent but high-yield orthopedic complication. mnemonic Virchow’s Triad SHE - S tasis Bed rest long-haul flights >8-9 hours post-op immobilization. - H ypercoagulability Malignancy OCPs pregnancy or genetic factors. - E ndothelial injury Surgery or the initial trauma itself. - Diagnosis Venous Doppler Ultrasound. The key finding is non-compressibility of the vein. If the vein doesn t collapse under probe pressure a clot is present. - Risk DVT can embolize to the lungs Pulmonary Embolism which is potentially fatal. Ankle Sprains The ATFL is King - Inversion Injury Low Sprain Most common. The Anterior Talofibular Ligament ATFL is always the first to go Mnemonic Always Torn First Ligament . - Eversion Injury Result in a Deltoid Ligament tear. Recovery takes twice as long. - High Ankle Sprain Injury to the Syndesmosis AITFL . - Physical Exam The Squeeze Test compressing the tibia and fibula together and the External Rotation Test reproduce syndesmosis pain. highyield Ottawa Ankle Rules Guidelines to avoid unnecessary X-rays - Get an Ankle X-ray ONLY IF Bone tenderness at the posterior edge/tip of the malleoli OR inability to walk 4 steps. - Get a Foot X-ray ONLY IF Bone tenderness at the Base of the 5th Metatarsal or the Navicular bone. --- 10. Compartment Syndrome The Surgical Emergency To conclude Dr. Heller presents a case of a patient post-reperfusion who develops intense burning pain. - Pathophysiology Massive swelling within the rigid fascia increases pressure cutting off capillary flow. - Treatment Immediate emergency fasciotomy . mnemonic The 6 P s of Compartment Syndrome 1. P ain out of proportion to injury or pain with passive stretch— the earliest sign 2. P aresthesia numbness 3. P allor pale skin 4. P aralysis 5. P ulselessness a very late and ominous sign 6. P oikilothermia Coldness What leg position is classic for a posterior hip dislocation? Shortened flexed internally rotated and adducted. Which specific ligament is most commonly injured in a low inversion ankle sprain? Anterior Talofibular Ligament ATFL . What is Virchow s Triad associated with DVT risk? Stasis Endothelial damage and Hypercoagulability. What is the most sensitive physical exam test for an ACL rupture? Lachman s Test. Which nerves are at risk in lateral compartment syndrome of the leg? Superficial Fibular Nerve. What artery is most crucial for femoral head survival in a neck fracture? Medial Circumflex Femoral Artery. What does a positive Thompson Test indicate? Achilles Tendon Rupture lack of plantarflexion on calf squeeze . Which landmark is palpated for the Ottawa Foot Rules? Base of the 5th Metatarsal and the Navicular bone. Boards & Beyond Musculoskeletal > Anatomy > Lower Extremity match Lower extremity fractures hip joint anatomy knee ligamentous injuries source First Aid 2024 page 452-458 source Pathoma 18.3 - Bone Fractures & Osteoarthritis source Sketchy Pathology Lower Extremity Orthopedics Overview source AnKing V12 tags AK Step1 v12 MSK LowerExtremity Fractures questions 1 A 45-year-old male feels a sudden pop in his ankle while playing tennis and feels like he was kicked. He is unable to stand on his toes. Which clinical test would confirm the diagnosis? Lachman s Test Thompson Test McMurray Test FADIR Test Squeeze Test 1 This describes an Achilles Tendon Rupture. The Thompson test squeezing the calf will fail to produce plantarflexion when the tendon is ruptured. 2 A 19-year-old female basketball player lands from a jump and immediately feels a pop followed by rapid swelling. Why is she at higher risk than her male counterparts? Increased pelvic tilt Increased Q-angle Decreased femoral anteversion Increased bone density Decreased ligament laxity 1 Female athletes have a wider pelvis and increased Q-angle creating more valgus stress on the ACL during landing and pivoting. 3 The Dashboard Injury mechanism is most likely to produce which combination of orthopedic injuries? Anterior Hip Dislocation and ACL Tear Femoral Neck Fracture and Meniscal Tear Posterior Hip Dislocation and PCL Tear Patellar Dislocation and MCL Tear Intertrochanteric Fracture and LCL Tear 2 The force striking the knee in a flexed position like hitting a dashboard drives the femur backward relative to the acetabulum Posterior Dislocation and the tibia backward relative to the femur PCL tear . Ottawa Ankle Rules Only X-ray if malleolar tenderness OR inability to take 4 steps. Maisonneuve Fracture A high ankle sprain with a proximal fibula fracture. Always palpate the knee in ankle injuries! Avascular Necrosis AVN A risk in any intracapsular femoral neck fracture due to disruption of the Medial Circumflex artery Ring of Fire . mindmap root Lower Extremity Orthopedics children name Hip children name Anatomy children name Medial Circumflex Art. name Labrum Suction name Dislocation children name Posterior Shortened IR Adducted name Anterior ER Skier name Fractures children name Neck AVN name Shaft Blood loss IM Nail name Knee children name Ligaments children name ACL Pop OBJ Pivot-shift name PCL Sag Sign name Patella children name Mahomes Dislocation Lateral name MPFL Tear name Meniscus children name Catching/Clicking name Red vs White Zone name Ankle / Leg children name DVT children name Virchow s Triad name Doppler Compression name Sprain children name ATFL Always Torn First name Syndesmosis Squeeze Test name Rules children name Ottawa Ankle vs Foot"}, {"id": "l112", "title": "Lecture #112: Orthopedic Concerns of the Spine", "module": "Orthopedics", "content": "l112 Lecture 112 Orthopedic Concerns of the Spine lecturer M. Heller D.O. Orthopedics pdf content/L112 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 112 Orthopedic Concerns of the Spine Lecturer M. Heller D.O. | Session Lecture 112 Orthopedic concerns of the spine represent one of the most ubiquitous challenges in clinical medicine. Regardless of your future specialty—whether it be pediatrics geriatrics or obstetrics—you will inevitably encounter patients presenting with neck or back pain. This lecture provides a comprehensive framework for the evaluation and management of spinal pathology beginning with a systematic approach to history and physical examination and culminating in the identification of surgical emergencies. The central theme of the lecture is the differentiation between mechanical degenerative and high-risk inflammatory or traumatic conditions. --- The Clinical Framework History and Physical Examination Evaluating spinal pain begins with a granular history. Orthopedic pain is rarely just pain ; its character timing and associated symptoms provide the roadmap to diagnosis. - Pain Characterization OPQRST - Localized Pain Often suggests a focal process such as a tumor or localized infection. - Mechanical Pain Typically worsens with activity and improves with rest; common in fractures or discogenic disease. - Radicular Pain Describes pain that travels along a dermatome often sharp or electric suggesting nerve root impingement e.g. herniated disc or stenosis . - Night Pain A significant red flag. Pain that wakes a patient from sleep or prevents them from lying comfortably can indicate metastatic malignancy. - Red Flags and Systemic Review Always screen for fever and weight loss infection/malignancy neurologic deficits weakness/numbness and trauma history. Understanding the patient s occupation FED TACOS and recreational activities is vital as repetitive stress often drives spinal pathology e.g. gymnasts and spondylolysis . - Physical Exam Techniques - Inspection and Gait Observe the patient walking. Look for compensatory movements or shopping cart signs. - Palpation Check for step-offs spondylolisthesis or midline tenderness compression fractures . - Range of Motion Assess for limitations in flexion extension and rotation. - Specialized Referral If a patient presents with a cervical complaint you MUST examine the shoulders. Conversely if the complaint is lumbar you MUST examine the hips and lower extremities to rule out referred pain. highyield Night pain is a major red flag for tumor/metastatic disease . Mechanical pain that improves with rest suggests vertebral or discogenic issues. If a patient has neck pain always check the shoulders ; back pain requires a hip/lower extremity exam. --- Diagnostic Imaging and Laboratory Evaluation - Plain Radiographs X-ray Usually the first-line imaging study. It is excellent for identifying acute fractures and assessing alignment. - CT Scan Provides superior detail for bony anatomy. Often used for pre-operative planning or post-operative hardware evaluation. - MRI The Gold Standard MRI is the imaging of choice for cervical thoracic and lumbar disease. It provides unparalleled soft-tissue resolution for identifying herniated discs spinal cord lesions like syringomyelia tumors and infections. - Bone Scan Skeletal Scintigraphy A nuclear medicine test using a radioactive tracer to find areas of high metabolic activity. It is invaluable for detecting occult stress fractures metastatic disease or early infection. highyield MRI is the imaging of choice for spinal soft tissue including discs cord lesions and impingement. Bone scans are used to detect high metabolic activity like metastases or infections . --- Cervical Spinal Disorders The cervical spine presents unique anatomical challenges. One key distinction emphasized by Dr. Heller is that cervical nerve roots exit horizontally whereas lumbar roots drop and exit at a lower level. Cervical Sprain and Strain Whiplash Often resulting from acceleration-deceleration injuries whiplash involves soft tissue damage where muscles respond by contracting. - Findings Non-focal non-radiating neck pain trapezius spasm decreased range of motion and frequent association with occipital headaches. - X-ray May show loss of normal cervical lordosis straightening of the neck due to muscle spasm. Cervical Spondylosis This is a chronic age-related wear-and-tear process involving disc degeneration and facet arthropathy. As the disc loses water content it becomes stiffer leading to discogenic pain. - Progression It starts as simple neck pain can progress to Radiculopathy unilateral arm pain/numbness exacerbated by Spurling’s maneuver and eventually to Myelopathy . - Myelopathy Red Flags Bilateral weakness Upper > Lower loss of fine motor skills cannot button a shirt and gait disturbances. This signifies spinal cord compression and requires urgent evaluation. Rheumatoid Cervical Spondylitis This is a critical can t miss condition for board exams. In patients with chronic Rheumatoid Arthritis RA the transverse ligament of the Atlas C1 can become lax or rupture. - Clinical Pearl This leads to C1-C2 subluxation . Dr. Heller warns that aggressive skull manipulation or intubation in these patients can cause fatal spinal cord compression quadriplegia . correlate Rheumatoid Arthritis and C1-C2 Instability - Pathophysiology Chronic inflammation leads to erosion of the transverse ligament of the atlas causing atlantoaxial instability. - Presentation Chronic RA patient now with neck pain or progressive neurologic signs. - Danger High risk of quadriplegia during maneuvers like intubation due to cord compression. - Management Obtain flexion/extension X-rays before any surgical procedure involving neck manipulation. --- Thoracic and Lumbar Disorders The lumbar spine bears the brunt of the body s weight making it a frequent site of mechanical failure. Vertebral Compression Fractures Commonly associated with osteoporosis in elderly patients. They typically present with midline spinal process SP point tenderness usually in the thoracic spine. - Management Treat the underlying osteoporosis with bisphosphonates and manage pain. Lumbar Herniated Nucleus Pulposus HNP Herniation typically occurs posterolaterally because the Posterior Longitudinal Ligament is relatively weak. Dr. Heller identifies L4-L5 and L5-S1 as the most common sites. - Nerve Root Mapping - L4 Root Weakness in knee extension quads decreased patellar reflex. - L5 Root Weakness in great toe extension/dorsiflexion difficulty with heel walking. - S1 Root Weakness in plantarflexion toe walking decreased Achilles reflex. mnemonic AchilleS-1 The Achilles reflex tests the S1 nerve root. Spondylolysis and Spondylolisthesis - Spondylolysis A stress fracture of the pars interarticularis . Classic in young athletes gymnasts football linemen due to repetitive hyperextension. On oblique X-ray look for the Scotty Dog sign a collar on the dog s neck . - Spondylolisthesis The actual slipping of one vertebral body over another usually L4 on L5 . This creates a palpable step-off on physical exam. Ankylosing Spondylitis AS An inflammatory arthritis typically affecting young men in their 20s-30s. It belongs to the seronegative spondyloarthropathies which are characterized by the absence of rheumatoid factor and a strong association with the HLA-B27 gene. - Clinical Profile Persistent morning stiffness that lasts for several hours and crucially improves with activity and exercise. Patients often describe the pain as deep in the gluteal region or lower back. - Extra-articular Findings AS is a systemic disease. It frequently presents with acute anterior uveitis eye pain/redness aortic regurgitation due to aortitis and restricted chest expansion. The latter occurs due to fusion of the costovertebral joints leading to a restrictive lung pattern. - Imaging The hallmark is sacroiliitis often the first sign on X-ray. Progression leads to the development of syndesmophytes—bony bridges between vertebrae—resulting in the classic Bamboo spine appearance. This fusion makes the spine brittle and prone to chalk-stick fractures even with minor trauma. Spinal Stenosis Narrowing of the spinal canal central stenosis or the neural foramina lateral stenosis most commonly due to degenerative changes like facet hypertrophy ligamentum flavum thickening and disc bulging. - The Shopping Cart Sign This is the pathognomonic finding for neurogenic claudication. Patients experience radiating leg pain heaviness or cramping while standing or walking downhill. These symptoms are relieved by leaning forward flexion as this posture increases the cross-sectional area of the spinal canal and reduces compression on the neural elements. - Differential Diagnosis A critical board-style distinction must be made between Neurogenic Claudication Stenosis and Vascular Claudication Peripheral Vascular Disease . - Neurogenic Relieved by sitting or leaning forward; pain can occur with standing alone; pulses are typically normal. - Vascular Relieved simply by stopping activity rest regardless of posture; pain is strictly effort-related; pulses are usually diminished or absent and skin changes may be present. --- Cauda Equina Syndrome The Surgical Emergency The spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris L1 . Below this the canal is filled with the cauda equina—a bundle of peripheral nerve roots L2-S4 . Dr. Heller emphasizes that compression here is a surgical emergency . - Etiology Massive central disc herniation trauma epidural abscess or tumor. - Clinical Presentation - Saddle Anesthesia Numbness in the perineal region. - Bowel/Bladder Dysfunction Urinary retention with overflow incontinence; loss of anal sphincter tone. - Motor Deficits Flaccid paralysis of the lower extremities. - Management Emergent MRI followed by surgical decompression within 48 hours to prevent permanent damage. highyield Cauda Equina Syndrome causes flaccid paralysis peripheral nerve injury while Myelopathy cord injury causes spastic paralysis . Saddle anesthesia and urinary retention make this a surgical emergency. --- Summary and Key Takeaways Spinal pathology requires a keen eye for red flags and a solid grasp of dermatomal anatomy. Start with conservative management NSAIDs PT activity modification for mechanical issues but maintain a high index of suspicion for inflammatory AS RA and emergent Cauda Equina conditions. - Primary Diagnostic Tool MRI. - Emergent Referral Cauda Equina severe motor deficits or suspicion of cord compression. - Board Favorite C1-C2 subluxation in RA patients and HLA-B27/Bamboo spine in AS. BandB Musculoskeletal > Orthopedics > Spine Pathoma Musculoskeletal Pathology > Bone Tumors and Fractures FirstAid Musculoskeletal Skin and Connective Tissue > Spinal Pathology SketchyPath Musculoskeletal > Bone and Joint Disorders Physeo Anatomy > MSK > Spine and Back What is the imaging modality of choice for evaluating herniated discs and spinal cord lesions? MRI is the gold standard for soft tissue resolution in the spine. Diagnostic Which red flag in a back pain history strongly suggests metastatic malignancy? Night pain that wakes the patient from sleep or prevents them from lying comfortably. Clinical In a patient with chronic Rheumatoid Arthritis what potentially fatal spinal complication should you be aware of? C1-C2 subluxation atlantoaxial instability due to erosion of the transverse ligament of the atlas. High Yield What is the difference between Spondylolysis and Spondylolisthesis? Spondylolysis is a stress fracture of the pars interarticularis; Spondylolisthesis is the slipping of one vertebral body over another. Anatomy What is the classic physical exam finding for Spondylolisthesis? A palpable step-off deformity during midline spinal palpation. Physical Exam Which nerve root is likely affected if a patient has a decreased patellar reflex and weak knee extension? The L4 nerve root. Neurology Which nerve root is likely affected if a patient has weakness in great toe extension dorsiflexion ? The L5 nerve root. Neurology What mnemonic correlates the S1 nerve root with its reflex? AchilleS-1 S1 root handles the Achilles reflex. Mnemonic A young man with morning back stiffness that improves with activity likely has what condition? Ankylosing Spondylitis often HLA-B27 positive . Clinical What are the common extra-articular findings in Ankylosing Spondylitis? Uveitis aortic regurgitation and restricted chest expansion. Clinical The Shopping Cart Sign is indicative of which condition? Spinal Stenosis pain is relieved by leaning forward/flexion . Clinical How do you differentiate Neurogenic Claudication stenosis from Peripheral Vascular Disease PVD ? Stenosis is relieved by leaning forward ; PVD is relieved by stopping activity regardless of posture. Clinical Where does the spinal cord end in adults? At the conus medullaris typically level L1 . Anatomy What are the hallmarks of Cauda Equina Syndrome? Saddle anesthesia bowel/bladder incontinence urinary retention and flaccid paralysis . Emergency Is Cauda Equina Syndrome considered a peripheral or central nerve injury? It is a peripheral nerve injury nerve roots below the cord . Concept Dr. Heller s recommendation for Cauda Equina management? Emergent MRI and surgical decompression within 48 hours . Clinical What sign on an oblique X-ray indicates Spondylolysis? The Scotty Dog sign fracture through the neck of the dog . Imaging What is Spurling’s maneuver used to diagnose? Cervical Radiculopathy reproduces arm pain/paresthesia . Physical Exam Define Myelopathy vs. Radiculopathy. Myelopathy is spinal cord compression bilateral signs upper motor neuron ; Radiculopathy is nerve root compression unilateral lower motor neuron . Concept Which population most commonly experiences vertebral compression fractures? Elderly patients with osteoporosis . Clinical What is the imaging modality used to screen for C1-C2 instability in RA patients? Flexion/Extension Radiographs of the cervical spine. Diagnostic Why is intubation risky in a patient with severe Rheumatoid Arthritis? The neck extension required for intubation can cause atlantoaxial subluxation and fatal cord compression. High Yield What blood markers are commonly elevated in spinal infections or tumors? ESR CRP and potentially abnormal SPEP/UPEP for myeloma . Lab Describe the typical presentation of a patient with a lumbar disc herniation at L5-S1. Lateral leg/foot pain weak plantarflexion and diminished Achilles reflex. Clinical What is Whiplash formally known as? Cervical Sprain or Cervical Strain . Clinical match Spinal Stenosis Shopping Cart Sign resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 24 Spinal stenosis | search spinal stenosis shopping cart match Cauda Equina Syndrome Saddle Anesthesia resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 23 Cauda equina syndrome | search cauda equina saddle anesthesia match Ankylosing Spondylitis HLA-B27 Bamboo Spine resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 13 Seronegative Spondyloarthritis Ankylosing Spondylitis | search ankylosing spondylitis bamboo spine match Spondylolysis and Spondylolisthesis resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 18 Spondylolysis & Spondylolisthesis | search scotty dog spondylolysis match Lumbar Herniated Disc Dermatome Mapping resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 01 Anatomy & Physiology 06 Lower Extremity Nerves | search L4 L5 S1 herniation reflexes match Rheumatoid Arthritis Cervical Involvement resources AnKing Step 1 > search rheumatoid arthritis C1 C2 subluxation questions 1 A 28-year-old male gymnast presents with persistent low back pain that worsens with hyperextension. Oblique X-rays of the lumbar spine reveal a collar on the Scotty dog appearance of the pars interarticularis. What is the most likely diagnosis? Spondylolisthesis Spondylolysis Spinal Stenosis Ankylosing Spondylitis Herniated Nucleus Pulposus 1 Spondylolysis is a stress fracture of the pars interarticularis common in athletes like gymnasts or football linemen who perform repetitive hyperextension. The Scotty dog sign on oblique X-ray where the collar represents the fracture is the classic radiographic finding. 2 A 72-year-old woman with a history of severe Rheumatoid Arthritis RA is being prepared for elective hip surgery. Why is it standard practice to obtain cervical spine X-rays prior to general anesthesia in such patients? To assess for osteoporotic compression fractures To screen for atlantoaxial C1-C2 instability To evaluate for ossification of the posterior longitudinal ligament To rule out cervical spondylotic myelopathy To check for disk herniation that might complicate positioning 1 Chronic RA can erode the transverse ligament of the atlas and the odontoid process leading to atlantoaxial C1-C2 instability. Neck manipulation during intubation can cause catastrophic subluxation and fatal spinal cord compression. 3 A 65-year-old male complains of bilateral leg pain and heaviness that occurs when walking but is relieved by leaning forward on his grocery cart. Physical exam shows normal peripheral pulses and no skin changes. What is the most likely underlying pathophysiology? Atherosclerotic occlusion of the femoral arteries Compression of the spinal cord due to a midline disc herniation Narrowing of the spinal canal leading to nerve root compression Venous insufficiency with valvular incompetence Autoimmune inflammation of the sacroiliac joints 2 This describes neurogenic claudication the shopping cart sign characteristic of Spinal Stenosis. Leaning forward spinal flexion increases the cross-sectional area of the spinal canal relieving the pressure on compressed nerve roots. 4 A patient presents with sudden onset saddle anesthesia urinary retention and bilateral lower extremity weakness after lifting a heavy box. What is the most appropriate immediate next step in management? Strict bed rest and high-dose NSAIDs Urgent physical therapy referral Epidural steroid injection Emergent MRI and neurosurgical consultation Oral muscle relaxants and follow-up in one week 3 These are red flag symptoms for Cauda Equina Syndrome CES . CES is a surgical emergency because delayed decompression can lead to permanent bowel/bladder dysfunction and paralysis. Emergent MRI is required for diagnosis. 5 A 30-year-old man presents with chronic morning back stiffness that significantly improves with swimming. X-ray of the pelvis reveals bilateral sacroiliitis. This condition is most strongly associated with which of the following? HLA-DR4 HLA-B27 HLA-B51 HLA-DR3 HLA-DQ8 1 The presentation young male inflammatory back pain improving with exercise sacroiliitis is diagnostic for Ankylosing Spondylitis. This seronegative spondyloarthropathy is classically associated with HLA-B27 seen in ~90% of cases ."}, {"id": "l113", "title": "Lecture #113: Imaging of the MSK System", "module": "Radiology", "content": "l113 Lecture 113 Imaging of the MSK System lecturer J. Mazzie D.O. Radiology pdf content/L113 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 113 Imaging of the MSK System Lecturer J. Mazzie D.O. | Session Lecture 113 Radiological evaluation of the musculoskeletal system is not merely about identifying fractures; it is about applying a rigorous systematic approach to visualize anatomy that is often hidden. A single view in radiology is often a no view as the three-dimensional complexity of bones and joints can mask significant pathology on a two-dimensional film. By systematically evaluating alignment joint spaces bone density and soft tissues clinicians can differentiate between benign variants and aggressive malignancies. This lecture establishes the fundamental principles for MSK imaging emphasizing the need for multiple orthogonal views and a high index of suspicion for invisible injuries like stress fractures and occult joint effusions. --- The Systematic Approach to MSK Imaging Slides 4-7 Before attempting to diagnose a specific pathology the radiologist must ensure the study is adequate. This starts with identifying the correct body part and ensuring a minimum of two orthogonal views 90 degrees to each other . The age of the patient is a critical contextual clue; for instance a bone lesion in a child is statistically more likely to be a primary bone tumor whereas in an older adult metastatic disease is the primary concern. - The Four-Step Checklist 1. Check Alignment Trace the relationship between adjacent bones. Slide 8 2. Evaluate Joint Spaces Look for narrowing cartilage loss/arthritis or widening dislocation/dissociation . Slide 10 3. Examine Bone Cortex Trace every millimeter of the white cortical line for breaches. Slide 11 4. Evaluate Soft Tissues Look for effusions or abnormal swelling. Slide 13 --- Joint Alignment and Dislocations Slides 8-9 Alignment refers to the normal anatomical relationship between bones in a joint. Loss of alignment typically indicates either a fracture or a dislocation. In some cases misalignment is chronic such as the hallux valgus bunion deformity seen in the foot which may also feature prominent sesamoid bones—small bones that form within tendons like the patella . - Anterior Glenohumeral Dislocation The humeral head is displaced anteriorly relative to the glenoid Slide 9 . - Lunate Dislocation Often more visible on a lateral view of the wrist; the lunate dislocates volarly towards the palm . correlate Anterior Glenohumeral Dislocation - Pathophysiology Disruption of the glenohumeral joint where the humeral head sits anterior to the glenoid fossa. - Presentation Often seen in trauma; the shoulder appears squared off. - Diagnosis AP and lateral Y-view X-rays are diagnostic. - Lecturer s Point You must have a Y-view or lateral view to appreciate the relationship between the humeral head and the glenoid. --- Bone Cortex and Normal Variants Slide 11-12 Tracing the cortex is a high-yield habit. Each white cortical line must be traced entirely. A breach in this line is often a fracture but the clinician must be careful not to misidentify normal anatomical features as pathology. - Nutrient Foramina These are normal channels in the mid-diaphysis of long bones like the femur and humerus that allow blood vessels to enter. They can look like thin lucent lines but are a normal variant not fractures. Lecturer Emphasis --- Soft Tissues and Joint Effusions Slides 13-14 Soft tissue evaluation often provides the clue to an underlying bone injury. Swelling or an effusion within a joint can indicate pathology even if the fracture itself is subtle or initially invisible. - Lipohemarthrosis This is a pathognomonic finding on a lateral knee X-ray. It presents as a fat-fluid level within the suprapatellar bursa. Because bone marrow contains fat a fracture that extends into the joint allows marrow fat to leak into the synovial fluid. Slide 14 highyield Lipohemarthrosis Fat-Fluid Level A fat-fluid level on a lateral view of a joint commonly the knee indicates an intra-articular fracture like a proximal tibial fracture . Fat floats on top of blood/fluid. --- Principles of Viewing Orthogonal Views Slides 15-22 The lecturer emphasizes the Satisfaction of Search pitfall the tendency to stop looking once one abnormality is found. To avoid this clinicians must follow a strict routine and always obtain at least two views ideally 90 degrees apart. - Orthogonal Power A fracture line may be perfectly parallel to the X-ray beam in one view making it invisible but glaringly obvious in another view Slide 15 . - Comparison Views In pediatric cases or complex anatomy comparing the normal side to the abnormal side is a powerful tool to identify subtle differences Slide 17 . - Don t Get Distracted A large incidental finding like a calcified uterine fibroid should not distract from a subtle femoral neck fracture Slide 19 . --- Pancoast Tumors and Referred Symptoms Slide 20 Radiology requires looking at the whole film. A patient presenting with arm pain or radiculopathy might not have a primary MSK issue in the arm. Instead they may have a large tumor at the apex of the lung. correlate Pancoast Tumor Superior Sulcus Tumor - Pathophysiology A primary lung malignancy arising at the extreme apex that invades the brachial plexus and/or cervical sympathetic chain. - Presentation Can present with radicular arm pain or Horner s Syndrome miosis ptosis anhidrosis . - Lecturer s Point We are responsible for everything on the film; a patient with arm pain may actually have a lung tumor visible on a shoulder or chest film. --- Fracture Descriptions and Pathology Slides 23-28 Fractures are described by their orientation transverse oblique spiral longitudinal and their relationship to the skin open vs. closed . - Open Fractures These are surgical emergencies due to the high risk of infection osteomyelitis . - Pathologic vs. Stress Fractures - Pathologic Fracture Normal stress applied to abnormal bone e.g. bone with a tumor or osteoporosis . - Stress Fracture Abnormal repetitive stress applied to normal bone . correlate Stress Fracture Marsh Fracture - Pathophysiology Repetitive mechanical stress causes micro-fractures in normal bone. - Presentation Insidious onset of localized pain. - Diagnosis Initially X-ray negative. Periosteal reaction new bone formation appears 7-14 days later. MRI is more sensitive early on showing marrow edema. - Lecturer s Point These were first described in soldiers Marching and are common in runners. --- Pediatric Fractures The Greenstick Slide 26 Children s bones are more pliable than adults. They often suffer from incomplete fractures where the bone bends or buckles rather than snapping completely. correlate Greenstick Fracture - Pathophysiology An incomplete fracture occurring in children where the bone is broken on one side and bent on the other. - Presentation Localized pain and deformity in a child. - Lecturer s Point This is an important pediatric fracture pattern to recognize for exams. --- Bone Tumors and Radiographic Aggression Slides 29-37 The appearance of a bone lesion s margins and its effect on the periosteum the outer layer of bone tell a story of how fast the lesion is growing. | Appearance | Description | Growth Rate | Likelihood of Malignancy | |---|---|---|---| | Geographic | Well-defined can trace with a pencil | Slow | Benign e.g. 95% benign | | Sclerotic Rim | Thick white border around lesion | Very Slow | Benign | | Moth-eaten | Multiple small holes ill-defined | Fast | Aggressive e.g. Malignancy or Osteomyelitis | | Permeative | Tiny pin-sized holes widespread | Very Fast | Highly Aggressive | Periosteal Reactions The periosteum reacts to insults by laying down new bone. - Solid Slow growing benign. - Laminated Onion Skin More aggressive. Slide 34 - Speculated Sunburst Very aggressive; indicates a rapidly growing malignancy. - Codman’s Triangle The periosteum is lifted at an angle by a tumor. This is a very aggressive malignant sign. Slide 37 highyield Codman’s Triangle This is the most aggressive form of periosteal reaction. It occurs when a tumor grows so fast it lifts the periosteum off the bone forming a triangular shadow. It is highly indicative of a malignant process. --- Summary of MSK Imaging Pearls - Symmetry is your friend Compare sides. - Air to Metal Densities range from darkest Air to lightest Metal . - Rule of Two At least two orthogonal views are mandatory. - Search Everything Don t stop at the first finding. BandB Musculoskeletal > Radiology > MSK Imaging Basics FirstAid Musculoskeletal Skin and Connective Tissue > Pathology > Bone Tumors Pathoma Musculoskeletal Pathology > Bone Tumors SketchyPath Musculoskeletal > Bone and Joint Disorders What is the Rule of Two in MSK imaging? Always obtain at least two views ideally orthogonal 90 degrees to each other. Concept What does a fat-fluid level on a lateral knee X-ray indicate? Lipohemarthrosis which is pathognomonic for an intra-articular fracture fat leaking from marrow into the joint . Clinical What is the difference between a pathologic and a stress fracture? Pathologic Normal stress on abnormal bone. Stress Abnormal stress on normal bone. Concept What is a Greenstick fracture? An incomplete fracture in a child where one side of the bone breaks and the other side bends. Clinical A Codman s Triangle on X-ray indicates what? An aggressive likely malignant bone tumor causing rapid periosteal lifting. Clinical What is the radiographic term for a well-defined lesion you can trace with a pencil ? Geographic appearance; typically benign. Concept How long does it typically take for a periosteal reaction to appear on X-ray after a stress fracture? 7 to 14 days. Concept What normal variant at the mid-diaphysis of long bones can be mistaken for a fracture? Nutrient foramen. Glossary Which lung tumor can present with radicular arm symptoms due to brachial plexus invasion? Pancoast tumor Superior Sulcus tumor . Clinical What density is the darkest on a standard X-ray? Air gas . Concept match Periosteal reactions Sunburst Codman s Triangle resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 09 Bone Tumors Osteosarcoma match Greenstick and Torus Fractures resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 17 Common fractures Pediatric match Scaphoid Fracture Snuffbox Tenderness resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 17 Common fractures Upper Extremity Satisfaction of Search The tendency for a radiologist to stop looking once they find one abnormality. Always finish your systematic checklist Alignment Joint Spaces Cortex Soft Tissues . Orthogonal Views One view is no view. Fractures parallel to the beam are invisible so you MUST have two views at 90 degrees. Clinical Hint Stress Fractures Early X-rays for stress fractures are usually negative. If pain persists get an MRI sensitive early or repeat X-ray in 2 weeks wait for periosteal reaction . Lipohemarthrosis If you see a fat-fluid level on a knee lateral there s a fracture into the joint even if you can t see the fracture line yet. questions 1 A 10-year-old child presents after a fall onto their arm. X-ray shows the radius is broken on one side but only bent on the other side. What is this fracture called? A. Transverse fracture B. Greenstick fracture C. Torus fracture D. Pathologic fracture E. Spiral fracture 1 A Greenstick fracture is an incomplete pediatric fracture where one side of the cortex breaks and the other side remains intact but bowed. 2 A 24-year-old marathon runner presents with persistent foot pain. Initial X-rays are normal. Two weeks later repeat X-rays show a thin line of new bone formation periosteal reaction along the second metatarsal shaft. What is the most likely diagnosis? A. Osteosarcoma B. Osteomyelitis C. Stress fracture D. Geographic lesion E. Pathologic fracture 2 Stress fractures Marsh fractures are often X-ray negative initially but develop a periosteal reaction after 7-14 days. 3 A lateral knee X-ray on a trauma patient reveals a horizontal line in the suprapatellar bursa with separate densities representing fat and fluid. This finding is highly suggestive of which of the following? A. Septic arthritis B. Patellar tendon rupture C. Intra-articular fracture D. Prepatellar bursitis E. Pancoast tumor 2 Lipohemarthrosis fat-fluid level occurs when marrow fat leaks into the joint space signifying an intra-articular fracture. 4 An aggressive bone lesion exhibits a sunburst appearance with lifting of the periosteum at a triangular angle. This triangular lifting is known as A. Geographic margin B. Onion skinning C. Codman s Triangle D. Nutrient foramen E. March fracture 2 Codman s Triangle is an aggressive periosteal reaction where a rapidly growing tumor lifts the periosteum off the bone. 5 A 65-year-old male with a history of smoking presents with numbness and pain in his right arm. A shoulder X-ray appears mostly normal but a large ill-defined mass is noted at the lung apex. What is the most likely cause of his arm pain? A. Rotator cuff tear B. Glenohumeral dislocation C. Pancoast tumor D. Lunate dislocation E. Greenstick fracture 2 Pancoast tumors at the lung apex can invade the brachial plexus causing referred radicular pain in the upper extremity. mindMap MSK Imaging Fundamentals Systematic Approach - Alignment Relationships between bones - Joint Spaces Narrowing arthritis vs. Widening dislocation - Cortex Tracing the entire cortical line - Soft Tissue Effusions swelling fat-fluid levels Principles of Viewing - Orthogonal Views 2 views at 90 degrees - Satisfaction of Search Fatal error of stopping after one finding - Age Context Pediatrics bone tumors vs. Adults metastasis Fracture Patterns - Greenstick Incomplete pediatric break/bend - Stress Marsh Repetitive stress initially X-ray negative - Pathologic Normal stress on diseased bone - Open Surgical emergency; infection risk Radiographic Aggression - Benign Geographic sclerotic rim slow growth - Aggressive Moth-eaten permeative - Periosteal Reaction - Solid Benign - Onion Skin Aggressive - Sunburst Highly Aggressive - Codman’s Triangle Malignant indicator"}, {"id": "l114", "title": "Introduction to Musculoskeletal Ultrasound", "module": "Radiology", "content": "l114 Introduction to Musculoskeletal Ultrasound lecturer J. Mazzie D.O. session Lecture 114 Radiology readingTime 12-15 min highYieldPdf content/L114 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 114 Introduction to Musculoskeletal Ultrasound Lecturer J. Mazzie D.O. Session Lecture 114 The Evolution and Strategic Utility of MSK Sonography Musculoskeletal MSK sonography has rapidly evolved from a specialized imaging technique into a ubiquitous front-line diagnostic tool. In clinical practice it serves as a bridge between the physical exam and high-end cross-sectional imaging like MRI. The lecturer emphasizes that understanding the fundamental strengths of ultrasound is as important as the physics itself. MSK ultrasound is not merely a cheaper version of MRI; it offers unique diagnostic capabilities—specifically real-time dynamic imaging—that static imaging simply cannot replicate. The Magic of MSK Ultrasound lies in its ability to provide high-detail patient-interactive imaging. It is a modality that allows for immediate clinical correlation. If a patient pointedly says It hurts right here the sonographer can place the probe exactly on that spot and see the anatomy under the patient s area of maximal tenderness. This targeted approach is often more revealing than a broad-field MRI that might miss a focal superficial abnormality. correlate Patient-Interactive Imaging Unlike traditional Radiology where the radiologist often never sees the patient MSK Ultrasound is a contact sport. The examiner is talking to the patient moving their limb and correlating symptoms with images in real-time. This significantly reduces the rate of false positives from incidental findings that aren t actually causing the patient s pain. --- Technical Foundations Transducers and Sound Waves To master MSK ultrasound one must first understand the tools. The transducer is the brain of the operation utilizing the Piezoelectric Effect to convert electrical energy into sound waves and vice versa. Choosing the Right Probe The Golden Rule of ultrasound physics is the inverse relationship between Frequency and Penetration . - High-Frequency Linear Probes These provide superior axial resolution but have poor depth penetration. In MSK these are used for almost everything—shoulders wrists fingers and ankles—where the structures are superficial. - Low-Frequency Curvilinear Probes These trade resolution for depth. They are typically reserved for deep structures like the hip joint or for scanning obese patients where significant soft tissue attenuation occurs. The Grayscale Language To effectively interpret an MSK ultrasound a clinician must speak in grayscale. The brightness of an image or its echogenicity is determined by how much of the sound wave is reflected back to the transducer versus how much is absorbed or transmitted through the tissue. Standard Sonographic Terminology Hyperechoic Bright/White Structures reflecting a high percentage of sound. Examples Bone cortex tendons epineurium. Isoechoic Gray Structures that match the brightness of the surrounding tissue background. Hypoechoic Dark/Gray Structures that absorb/transmit more than they reflect. Example Normal skeletal muscle. Anechoic Black/Void Completely black; indicates simple pure fluid e.g. joint fluid simple cysts . Posterior Acoustic Shadowing Occurs behind very dense structures bone calcifications that block all sound waves leaving a dark void deep to the structure. highyield Lecturer Emphasis The Power of Doppler When a structure is hypoechoic dark it could be a tear or just inflammation. Power Doppler allows us to see neovascularization. If a tendon is dark AND lights up on Doppler it confirms the tissue is acutely inflamed The tendon is mad . --- Normal Anatomic Appearances Mastering the Baseline The lecturer emphasizes that a student must look at thousands of normals to truly recognize the abnormal. Each tissue type in the MSK system has a unique architectural pattern. 1. The Tendon The Braid of Hair Tendons are comprised of highly organized collagen bundles arranged in parallel. This creates a signature fibrillar pattern . - Long-Axis View A healthy tendon looks like a series of thousands of tiny parallel lines often described as a braid of hair or raw silk. - Significance Disruption of these parallel lines indicates tendinosis wear or a tear. - Example The Achilles tendon is the gold standard for visualizing this due to its size and superficial location. 2. Skeletal Muscle The Starry Sky Muscles consist of dark hypoechoic contractile tissue separated by thin bright hyperechoic connective tissue perimysium . - Long-Axis Muscle looks marbled with fine bright streaks. - Short-Axis Transverse Where the Starry Sky Appearance is seen. The hyperechoic fibro-fatty septa look like bright stars against the dark midnight sky of the muscle fibers. 3. Nerves The Honeycomb In the transverse view a nerve looks like a Honeycomb. The holes of the honeycomb are the hypoechoic nerve fascicles and the walls are the bright white epineurium. - Dynamic Check How to tell a nerve from a tendon? Move the joint. Tendons glide like a piston; nerves just jiggle or move slightly side-to-side. 4. Bone and Cartilage - Bone Cortex Always a smooth continuous bright white hyperechoic line. Any step-off or break in this line is a fracture until proven otherwise. - Hyaline Cartilage e.g. covering the femoral head Appears as a uniform hypoechoic dark layer. - Fibrocartilage e.g. Meniscus Labrum Denser than hyaline appearing bright hyperechoic and often triangular. --- The Crucial Technical Challenge Anisotropy important Anisotropy is the most common pitfall for beginner sonographers. It is an artifact that makes a normal tendon look dark hypoechoic mimicking a tear. The Physics of the Fallacy Sound waves reflect most efficiently when they hit a surface at a 90-degree angle . Because tendons are made of highly reflective parallel fibers if the beam hits them at even a slight angle the sound reflects away from the probe. - The Solution The Heel-Toe technique . Angle the probe until it is exactly perpendicular to the tendon. If the dark spot vanishes and becomes bright white it was anisotropy. If it stays dark it s pathology. --- Pathological Findings and Case Correlations Tendinosis vs. Tears - Tendinosis Chronic degeneration. The tendon becomes swollen thickened hypoechoic dark and loses its fibrillar pattern. - Tears Appear as anechoic foci completely black where the fibers have snapped and the gap is filled with fluid or hematoma. Case Study 1 The Pacemaker Dilemma An 18-year-old female injured her shoulder at CrossFit. MRI was contraindicated due to her pacemaker . Ultrasound provided high-resolution imaging that confirmed a supraspinatus tendon tear allowing for surgical planning that MRI could not facilitate. Case Study 2 The Tickle Fracture A physician felt a snap while tickling his child. Ultrasound showed a cortical break in the 5th metacarpal. This was a pathologic fracture occurring through an enchondroma a benign tumor that thinned the bone cortex to eggshell-thinness . Case Study 3 The Popliteal Fullness A 45-year-old with fullness behind the knee. Ultrasound revealed an anechoic collection between the semimembranosus tendon and the medial head of the gastrocnemius . - Diagnosis Baker s Cyst. - The Tail Sign The key to diagnosis is finding the neck connecting the cyst to the joint space. mnemonic The S-M-G of Baker s Cysts S emimembranosus and M edial G astrocnemius are the walls of the Baker s Shop. --- Summary of Clinical Utility MSK Ultrasound is an adjunct tool . It should always be interpreted in the context of the patient s history and prior radiographs. The ultimate superpower of ultrasound is its dynamic testing —the ability to see pathology that only appears when the patient moves. questions 1 A 22-year-old athlete presents with shoulder pain. On ultrasound the supraspinatus tendon appears dark when the probe is angled at 45 degrees but appears bright and fibrillar when the probe is oriented perpendicular to the fibers. What is the most likely explanation for this finding? Acute tendinitis Anisotropy Complete tendon tear Calcific tendinosis Neovascularization 1 Anisotropy is a common ultrasound artifact where tendons appear hypoechoic dark if the sound beam is not perpendicular 90 degrees to the parallel collagen fibers. Correcting the angle to 90 degrees restores the normal hyperechoic fibrillar appearance. A true tear or tendinitis would remain hypoechoic regardless of probe angle. 2 Which of the following sonographic descriptions best fits a normal skeletal muscle in the transverse short axis view? Completely anechoic Fibrillar braid of hair pattern Honeycomb appearance Starry sky appearance Refractive shadowing 3 In the transverse short axis view muscle exhibits a starry sky appearance. The stars are the bright hyperechoic fibro-fatty septa perimysium and the sky is the dark hypoechoic contractile muscle tissue. The braid of hair refers to tendons in long-axis and honeycomb refers to nerves in transverse view. 3 A 55-year-old patient with an implanted metallic heart valve requires imaging of a suspected rotator cuff tear. After negative plain radiographs which imaging modality is the most appropriate next step for assessing the soft tissue architecture? MRI with contrast CT without contrast MSK Ultrasound PET scan Bone scan 2 Ultrasound is the ideal modality for soft tissue evaluation in patients where MRI is contraindicated e.g. pacemakers certain metallic implants . It provides excellent resolution for tendons and ligaments without ionizing radiation or electromagnetic interference issues. 4 A patient presents with fullness in the popliteal fossa. Sonography reveals a Baker s cyst. Between which two anatomic structures is this fluid collection classically situated? Biceps femoris and lateral gastrocnemius Semimembranosus and medial gastrocnemius Quadriceps tendon and patella Popliteus and soleus ACL and PCL 1 A Baker s cyst popliteal cyst typically develops in the bursal space between the semimembranosus tendon and the medial head of the gastrocnemius muscle. Finding the neck of the fluid collection between these two structures is the hallmark diagnostic feature. 5 A physician feels a sudden snap while playing with his child leading to hand pain. Ultrasound shows a focal step-off in the brightest white line of the 5th metacarpal. What is the fundamental sonographic sign of a fracture identified here? Anechoic collection in the joint space Increased echogenicity of the subcutaneous fat Interruption of the hyperechoic bone cortex Honeycomb appearance of the adjacent nerve Starry sky appearance of the tendon 2 The bone cortex is highly reflective and appears as a continuous smooth bright white hyperechoic line on ultrasound. An interruption gap or step-off in this continuous line is the definitive sonographic sign of a fracture. Echogenicity The degree to which a tissue reflects ultrasound waves brightness on the monitor . Glossary Hyperechoic Brighter than surrounding tissue e.g. bone tendons . Glossary Hypoechoic Darker than surrounding tissue e.g. muscle inflamed nerves . Glossary Anechoic Black/void of echoes; represents simple fluid e.g. cysts effusions . Glossary Fibrillar Pattern The sonographic braid of hair appearance characteristic of normal tendons long-axis . Concept Starry Sky Appearance The transverse sonographic appearance of muscle bright septa in dark tissue . Concept Honeycomb Appearance The transverse sonographic appearance of nerves dark fascicles in bright epineurium . Concept Anisotropy Artifact where a tendon appears falsely hypoechoic due to a non-perpendicular probe angle. Clinical Tendinosis on US Swollen hypoechoic tendon with morphology change rounded and neovascularity Power Doppler . Clinical Baker s Cyst Location Fluid collection between the semimembranosus and the medial head of the gastrocnemius. Clinical Pathologic Fracture A fracture occurring through abnormal bone e.g. an enchondroma with minimal trauma. Clinical Dynamic Maneuver Moving a joint during ultrasound to observe tendon subluxation or real-time impingement. Concept Bone Shadowing The dark void deep to the bright white bone cortex due to complete sound reflection. Concept Heel-Toe Technique Angling the probe to ensure it is perpendicular to a tendon to resolve anisotropy artifact. Clinical Power Doppler Ultrasound mode used to detect low-flow neovascularization in inflamed tissues. Glossary BandB Orthopedics > Diagnostic Imaging FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Radiology SketchyPath MSK > Bone & Soft Tissue Physeo Anatomy > Upper Extremity > Shoulder Pathoma MSK > Bone Tumors Bootcamp MSK > Imaging match Musculoskeletal ultrasound for rotator cuff tear resources AnKing Step 2 > MedicalArk > Musculoskeletal ultrasound match Baker s Cyst anatomy resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > Baker s Cyst match Anisotropy artifact resources AnKing Step 1 > Radiology > Artifacts > Anisotropy Dynamic Distinction To distinguish nerves from tendons move the joint; tendons glide like pistons while nerves remain relatively stationary jiggle . Anisotropy Check Always heel-toe the probe to ensure perpendicularity before diagnosing tendinosis; anisotropy resolves with probe adjustment. The Baker s Tail To confirm a Baker s cyst you must find the neck or tail tracking between the semimembranosus and medial gastrocnemius. Reflective Cortex The bone cortex should be a single smooth bright white line; any gap is a fracture until proven otherwise. Pacemaker Safety Ultrasound is the preferred imaging modality for soft tissue in patients with MRI-incompatible devices like pacemakers. mindmap Musculoskeletal Ultrasound Imaging Principles - Advantages Cost-effective Readily Available Real-time/Dynamic No Radiation MRI-Incompatible Safe - Terminology Hyperechoic Bright Bone Tendon Hypoechoic Dark Muscle Inflamed Nerve Anechoic Black Simple Fluid Cysts - Artifacts Anisotropy False hypoechogenicity due to angle. Correct with Heel-Toe probe adjustment. Tissue Signatures - Tendon Fibrillar pattern braid of hair . - Muscle Starry sky view transverse ; hypoechoic with hyperechoic septa. - Nerve Honeycomb appearance; dark fascicles surrounded by bright epineurium. - Bone Highly reflective white line with deep acoustic shadowing. - Cartilage Hyaline Hypoechoic Uniform layer . Fibrocartilage Hyperechoic/Bright Triangular e.g. Meniscus . Key Pathologies - Tendinosis Swollen hypoechoic neovascularity Doppler . - Tendon Tears Anechoic fluid-filled gaps. - Neuropathy Swollen dark nerve; loss of honeycomb pattern. - Baker s Cyst Fluid between semimembranosus and medial gastrocnemius. - Fracture Interruption of the cortical white line."}, {"id": "l115", "title": "Lecture #115: Sports Medicine: Overuse Injuries", "module": "Orthopedics", "content": "l115 Lecture 115 Sports Medicine Overuse Injuries lecturer H. Zwibel D.O. Orthopedics pdf content/L115 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 115 Sports Medicine Overuse Injuries Lecturer H. Zwibel D.O. | Session Lecture 115 1. Introduction The Pathophysiology of Overuse Dr. Zwibel introduces a critical framework for understanding sports medicine the distinction between acute trauma and overuse injuries . While acute injuries are sudden overuse injuries result from repetitive microtrauma to bone muscle and tendon tissues. To effectively treat these patients clinicians must move beyond identifying the injury itself and investigate the precipitating risk factors —both extrinsic and intrinsic. Without addressing why the injury occurred the cycle of dysfunction will inevitably recur. --- 2. Risk Factors Why Injuries Happen Extrinsic Factors Environment & Training Extrinsic risk factors are external to the individual and are often the most modifiable. - Training Errors Rapid increases in intensity volume or duration the too much too soon phenomenon . Lack of adequate recovery time is a leading cause of tissue failure. - Technique Improper form during repetitive motions e.g. bowling or pitching places asymmetric stress on tissues. - Environment & Equipment Hard training surfaces e.g. concrete vs. track and worn-out or inappropriate footwear directly impact force transmission. Intrinsic Factors The Individual Intrinsic factors are inherent to the patient’s anatomy and physiology. - Malalignment Anatomical irregularities such as leg length discrepancies flat feet pes planus or high arches pes cavus . - Muscle Imbalances Differences in length and strength between agonist and antagonist muscle groups e.g. tight hamstrings vs. weak quadriceps . - Systemic Issues Genetic endocrine e.g. thyroid dysfunction and metabolic factors that impact tissue healing and bone density. --- 3. Bone Stress Injuries The Continuum of Failure Bone stress injuries represent a spectrum ranging from bone strain to stress reactions and finally stress fractures . Pathophysiology The Modeling Response When bone is subjected to repetitive stress it undergoes a vascular congestion and thrombosis response. This leads to a mismatch where osteoclastic activity bone breakdown outpaces osteoblastic activity bone building . The weakened trabecular bone develops microfractures that can progress to a complete cortical break if the stress continues. highyield The Female Athlete Triad Women are 2 to 10 times more likely to suffer stress injuries than men. This is often driven by the Female Athlete Triad 1. Low Energy Availability Disordered Eating 2. Menstrual Dysfunction Amenorrhea 3. Low Bone Mineral Density Osteoporosis The resulting hypoestrogenic state severely impairs bone health. Clinical Presentation & Diagnosis - Crescendo Pain Pain typically starts as a dull ache and progresses to sharp localized pinpoint pain . In advanced stages pain occurs at rest and even at night. - Imaging Modalities - X-ray Initial test of choice; however it has low sensitivity early on and may not show changes for 4-6 weeks. Look for periosteal reaction or a dreaded black line. - Bone Scan Highly sensitive for metabolic activity but poorly specific for differentiating reaction vs. fracture. - MRI The Gold Standard . Highly sensitive and specific for visualizing bone marrow edema and cortical defects. Treatment The mainstay of treatment is relative rest typically for 6 weeks . While the injured area is protected the patient should engage in cross-training to prevent systemic atrophy. NSAIDs should be avoided as they can hinder bone healing; acetaminophen is the preferred analgesic. --- 4. Muscle Overuse Injuries Adhesions and Pressure Focal Tissue Thickening and Fibrosis Overuse leads to microtrauma and the formation of fascial cross-linkages and adhesions between muscle fibers. On palpation these feel like knots or tight bands. These adhesions reduce the muscle s ability to contract and relax rapidly predisposing the athlete to further injury. - Treatment Stretching strengthening and Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment OMT are effective in breaking down fibrotic tissue. Chronic Exertional Compartment Syndrome CECS Unlike acute compartment syndrome a surgical emergency CECS is a chronic reversible condition. - Mechanism Exercise increases muscle volume; however the surrounding fascia is inelastic and refuses to expand. This raises intercompartmental pressure impairing blood flow and compressing nerves. - Symptoms Pain and pressure that begin with activity and improve immediately with rest . - Diagnosis Measurement of compartmental pressures anterior lateral deep posterior or superficial posterior at rest and post-exercise. - Treatment Conservative management first; fasciotomy is considered only if conservative measures fail. correlate Delayed Onset Muscle Soreness DOMS DOMS peaks 1-2 days after exercise particularly after eccentric movements muscle lengthening under tension . It is caused by muscle fiber damage and cell membrane swelling not lactic acid buildup. --- 5. Tendinopathy The Non-Inflammatory Cascade Modern sports medicine has transitioned from the term tendonitis implying inflammation to tendinopathy as these are primarily degenerative rather than inflammatory processes. Three Stages of Tendinopathy 1. Reactive Tendinopathy A non-inflammatory proliferative response in the cell matrix leading to short-term thickening. 2. Tendon Disrepair The matrix begins to break down. Tenocytes become disorganized and neovascularization ingrowth of small vessels and nerves occurs. 3. Degenerative Tendinopathy Progression to cell death apoptosis and large areas of matrix disorganization with minimal potential for recovery. caution Steroid Injections & Tendon Rupture Corticosteroid injections should not be placed directly into a tendon especially weight-bearing ones like the Achilles . High-dose steroids can weaken the collagen structure leading to catastrophic tendon rupture. --- 6. Clinical Applications Epicondylitis - Lateral Epicondylitis Tennis Elbow Involves the Extensor Carpi Radialis Brevis ECRB . Pain is reproduced with resisted wrist extension. - Medial Epicondylitis Golfer s/Pitcher s Elbow Involves the flexors and pronators Pronator Teres and Flexor Carpi Radialis . Often associated with ulnar nerve irritation. --- 7. Summary & Key Takeaways - Listen to the Body Pain is a feedback mechanism indicating tissue strain. - Diagnostic Flow Start with X-ray but don t rule out stress fractures if initial films are negative; use MRI for confirmation. - Comprehensive Care Correct the underlying biomechanical or systemic dysfunction to prevent recurrence. What are the three components of the Female Athlete Triad? 1. Low energy availability disordered eating 2. Menstrual dysfunction amenorrhea 3. Low bone mineral density osteoporosis . Which imaging modality is considered the gold standard for diagnosing stress fractures? MRI Magnetic Resonance Imaging . Why should NSAIDs generally be avoided in the treatment of stress fractures? NSAIDs have been shown to hinder the bone healing process; acetaminophen is preferred. What is the primary difference between the pain of tendinopathy and chronic compartment syndrome? Tendinopathy pain often improves after warming up but returns later; CECS pain starts with activity and dissipates immediately with rest. What muscle is most commonly involved in lateral epicondylitis? Extensor Carpi Radialis Brevis ECRB . True or False Tendinopathy is primarily an inflammatory condition. False. It is a degenerative and maladaptive process rather than an acute inflammatory one. Which type of muscle contraction is most associated with Delayed Onset Muscle Soreness DOMS ? Eccentric contractions lengthening under tension . In the diagnosis of stress fractures what is the Dreaded Black Line seen on X-ray? A transverse radiolucent line indicating a high-risk cortical stress fracture often seen on the anterior tibia. AnKing Step 1 V12 Musculoskeletal match Stress fractures Female Athlete Triad Epicondylitis Overuse Injuries source Boards & Beyond Musculoskeletal > Anatomy > Upper Extremity Epicondylitis source First Aid 2024 page 448-450 source Pathoma 18.3 - Bone Diseases source AnKing V11 tags AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 12 Musculoskeletal 05 Pathology Sports Medicine questions 1 A 20-year-old female distance runner presents with a 3-week history of worsening right shin pain. Initially the pain only occurred at the end of runs but now it is present during daily walking. Physical exam reveals point tenderness over the distal third of the medial tibia. Initial X-rays are negative. What is the most appropriate next step in management? Return to running with a supportive brace Immediate MRI to confirm diagnosis Recommend relative rest and cross-training Start high-dose Ibuprofen to reduce inflammation Technetium-99 bone scan 2 This case is highly suspicious for a tibial stress injury. Relative rest is the primary treatment even if imaging is initially negative. MRI is a valid diagnostic step but clinical management rest is the priority. NSAIDs should be avoided. 2 A 44-year-old recreational tennis player reports persistent pain in her lateral elbow. The pain is reproduced when she is asked to extend her wrist against resistance while her elbow is extended. What is the most likely histopathological finding in this patient’s affected tendon? Acute neutrophilic infiltration Neovascularization and tenocyte disorganization Amyloid deposition Eosinophilic micro-abscesses Granulomatous inflammation 1 Lateral epicondylitis tennis elbow is a tendinopathy. Histopathology shows a degenerative process characterized by neovascularization tenocyte proliferation and collagen matrix disorganization rather than acute inflammation. 3 An athlete complains of intense forearm tightness and numbness during high-intensity weightlifting. He notes that the symptoms are severe enough to stop his workout but vanish within 5 minutes of halting the exercise. Physical exam at rest is unremarkable. What is the definitive treatment if conservative measures fail? Intramedullary nailing Fasciotomy Corticosteroid injection Surgical tendon transfer Casting for 6 weeks 1 This describes Chronic Exertional Compartment Syndrome CECS . The symptoms are volume-dependent and resolve with rest. While OMT and PT are initial steps fasciotomy is the definitive surgical solution for cases failing conservative care. Pinpoint vs. Diffuse Pain Pinpoint tenderness on bone is highly suggestive of a stress fracture whereas diffuse tenderness along the medial tibial border is more common in Medial Tibial Stress Syndrome Shin Splints . Relative Rest Rest the injury not the athlete. Cross-training swimming cycling maintains cardiovascular fitness while allowing the target tissue to heal. The Ring of Fire In overuse scenarios affecting the hip always maintain a high index of suspicion for Femoral Neck Stress Fractures as displacement can lead to Avascular Necrosis. mindmap root Overuse Injuries children name Bone children name Stress Fracture children name Crescendo Pain name MRI = Gold Standard name Female Triad children name Energy Deficiency name Amenorrhea name Osteoporosis name Muscle children name CECS children name Exertional Pressure name Rest=Resolution name Fasciotomy name DOMS children name Eccentric Loading name 1-2 Day Peak name Tendon children name Tendinopathy children name Degenerative name Neovascularization name Epicondylitis children name Lateral ECRB name Medial Flexors"}, {"id": "l116", "title": "Pediatric Orthopedics", "module": "Orthopedics", "content": "l116 Pediatric Orthopedics lecturer M. Scotten M.D. session Lecture 116 Orthopedics readingTime 12-15 min pdf content/L116 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L116 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 116 Pediatric Orthopedics Lecturer M. Scotten M.D. Session Lecture 116 The Pediatric Skeleton Distinct from Adults Slide 3 The fundamental axiom of pediatric care is that children are not small adults and this is nowhere more evident than in the skeletal system. A neonate enters the world with approximately 300 bones many of which are largely cartilaginous to allow for the buoyancy and compression required for life in utero and the passage through the birth canal. Over time these structures fuse into the standard 206 bones seen in adulthood. Because of this high cartilage-to-bone ratio pediatric bones possess unique biomechanical properties—they are more pliable more porous and less mineralized than adult bone yet they possess a much thicker periosteum and a remarkable capacity for rapid healing. Unique Biomechanical Properties Slide 5 Pliability and Porosity Children s bones can bend significantly before breaking often leading to incomplete fractures rarely seen in adults. Thick Periosteum The outer covering of the bone is robust in children often keeping displaced fractures contained and providing a massive blood supply for rapid osteoblastic activity. The Physis Growth Plate This is the most critical anatomical distinction. The growth plate is the primary site of longitudinal growth through cellular proliferation and hypertrophy. Clinical Significance Damage to the physis can lead to permanent limb-length discrepancies or angular deformities if the growth is arrested or asymmetrical. --- Pediatric Fracture Patterns Bowing Buckle and Greenstick Slide 10 Because pediatric bones are less dense and more elastic they respond to trauma with patterns that reflect their springy nature. When a child falls on an outstretched hand FOOSH the bone may simply bow without a visible cortical break or it may buckle under the axial load. These fractures often require a high index of clinical suspicion because the radiologic findings can be subtle compared to the dramatic snap seen in adult cortical bone. However the robust blood supply means these injuries often heal in half the time of an equivalent adult injury. Common Patterns Bowing Plastic Fracture The bone bends beyond its limit of elastic recoil but does not break. Torus Buckle Fracture Slide 12 The cortex buckles out under compression. Presentation Common in 5-10 year olds. Management Often just requires splinting; heals in ~2 weeks. Greenstick Fracture Slide 13 Like a young tree branch the bone breaks on one side but remains intact on the other. Management Requires 4-8 weeks of casting due to higher force involved. --- The Salter-Harris Classification Slide 24 Standardized in 1962 the Salter-Harris system is the universal language for describing fractures that involve the growth plate. While these represent a minority of total pediatric fractures they carry the highest risk for long-term morbidity and are a frequent target for board examinations. The system is predicated on where the fracture line travels relative to the physis metaphysis and epiphysis. The higher the number the more likely the joint surface or the regenerative cells of the growth plate are compromised. mnemonic SALTER Classification - S Straight across Type I - Through the physis itself often seen as widening. - A Above Type II - Through the physis and up into the metaphysis. Most Common 75% . - L Lower Type III - Through the physis and down into the epiphysis/joint. Risk of arthritis. - T Through Type IV - Through metaphysis physis and epiphysis. Surgical emergency. - ER ERasure/Crush Type V - Compression of the growth plate. Rare but high-risk for growth arrest. --- Non-Accidental Trauma The Mandatory Reporter s Mandate Slide 31 Orthopedic injuries are frequently the first visible signs of non-accidental trauma NAT or child . As of 2021 over 600 000 children are reported as abused annually in the U.S. with one-third being under the age of two. For a pediatrician a broken bone in a non-ambulatory infant is a massive red flag because breaking infant bone requires significant force. The clinician must maintain a high index of suspicion looking not just at the fracture itself but at the mechanism provided by the caregiver and the presence of other hidden injuries. correlate Non-Accidental Trauma NAT - Pathophysiology Physical force applied intentionally often involving shaking pulling or blunt force. - Presentation Bruises on non-extensor surfaces back torso cigarette burns belt marks or an overly nervous/suspicious parent. - Diagnosis Skeletal survey looking for fractures of different ages CT/MRI for head trauma. - Lecturer s Point Posterior rib fractures are pathognomonic for shaking/squeezing. Coup-contrecoup injuries and retinal hemorrhages define Shaken Baby Syndrome. --- Developmental and Acquired Hip Disorders Slide 42 The pediatric hip is a complex site of development where mechanical instability or vascular compromise can lead to life-altering outcomes. Disorders here are often age-dependent a click in a newborn suggests DDH while a limp in an overweight adolescent points toward SCFE. Understanding the demographics—such as the female predominance in DDH versus the male predominance in LCPD—is essential for rapid narrowing of the differential diagnosis. correlate Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip DDH - Pathophysiology Mechanical instability where the femoral head does not sit tightly in the acetabulum. - Risk Factors Female Firstborn Breech delivery Oligohydramnios. - Diagnosis Barlow dislocating and Ortolani relocating maneuvers; Ultrasound <4-6 months . - Treatment Pavlik harness 6-12 weeks Spica casting. - Lecturer s Point Use the Galeazzi test to check for leg-length discrepancy. correlate Legg-Calve-Perthes Disease LCPD - Pathophysiology Idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head. - Presentation Insidious onset painless or painful limp in children avg age 4-8 . - Diagnosis X-ray showing flattening or fragmentation of the femoral head. - Treatment Weight-bearing limits physical therapy bracing/surgery. - Lecturer s Point 5x more common in males. Presentation includes Trendelenburg gait and loss of internal rotation. correlate Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis SCFE - Pathophysiology The femoral head slips off the neck often compared to ice cream falling off a cone. - Presentation Overweight adolescent in a growth spurt; complains of hip OR knee pain. - Diagnosis Frog-leg lateral X-ray. - Treatment Surgical stabilization with screws. - Lecturer s Point 25% are bilateral. If the patient is a girl think SCFE Skippy over LCPD. --- Foot and Knee Deformities Osgood-Schlatter and Clubfoot Slide 59 Malformations of the lower extremities are among the most common reasons for orthopedic referrals. Osgood-Schlatter disease is the classic growing pain of the active athlete involving the site where the patellar tendon pulls on the tibial tuberosity. In contrast Clubfoot is a congenital deformity that requires immediate intervention following birth to ensure the child can eventually walk with a plantigrade foot. correlate Osgood-Schlatter Disease - Pathophysiology Osteochondritis/apophysitis of the tibial tuberosity from repetitive traction. - Presentation Exquisite point tenderness and swelling on the anterior knee in athletic adolescents. - Management Rest ice NSAIDs. Stopped sport for few weeks . - Lecturer s Point Diagnosis is clinical push on the bump . X-rays are only to rule out tumors/infection if fever/night sweats are present. correlate Clubfoot Talipes Equinovarus - Pathophysiology Congenital deformity involving plantar flexion midfoot adduction and hindfoot inversion. - Management Serial casting Ponseti method started immediately after birth. - Complications Recurrence if parents fail to follow up with physical therapy/stretching. - Lecturer s Point 1 in 1000 births. Must differentiate from Metatarsus Adductus which only requires stretching . --- Bone and Joint Infections The Medical Emergencies Slide 73 Infections of the musculoskeletal system in children are high-stakes scenarios. Bacteria—most commonly Staph aureus —can seed the highly vascular metaphysis of long bones or the synovial space of joints. While Osteomyelitis often presents with localized point tenderness and a grumbling course Septic Arthritis is a true orthopedic emergency that can destroy a joint within hours. The toxic appearance of the child is the primary clinical discriminator used to separate these from the more benign Transient Synovitis. Pathogenesis and Microbiology Routes of Infection Hematogenous spread most common direct inoculation trauma or spread from adjacent tissue. Primary Pathogen Staphylococcus aureus >80% . Special Populations Sickle Cell patients are at unique risk for Salmonella and Pseudomonas . Emerging Pathogens Kingella kingae increasing in incidence . correlate Acute Osteomyelitis - Pathophysiology Hematogenous seeding of the bone metaphysis. - Presentation Limp fever and focal Point Tenderness over the bone. - Diagnosis 2 of Purulence on biopsy + Culture Erythema Positive Imaging . - Treatment 2-6 weeks of IV antibiotics Vanco Clinda or Linezolid to cover MRSA . correlate Septic Arthritis - Pathophysiology Bacterial infection of the joint space. - Presentation Toxic look high fever exquisite pain with any joint movement joint widening on X-ray. - Management Surgical drainage and IV antibiotics. A Medical Emergency . correlate Transient Toxic Synovitis - Pathophysiology Sterile immune-mediated joint inflammation typically following a viral URI or GI illness. - Presentation Ages 3-10; limp but not toxic; normal inflammatory markers WBC CRP ESR . - Management Rest and NSAIDs; usually resolves in 7-10 days. - Lecturer s Point If they don t get better in 24-48 hours reconsider Septic Arthritis. --- Metabolic Bone Disease Rickets Slide 90 Rickets represents a failure of bone mineralization due to a disruption in calcium and phosphate homeostasis. While historically associated with extreme malnutrition it remains relevant today particularly in exclusively breastfed infants without Vitamin D supplementation or those with limited sun exposure. The clinical hallmark is the bowing of weight-bearing bones as the soft unmineralized osteoid fails to support the child s body weight. correlate Rickets - Pathophysiology Vitamin D deficiency nutritional or sun-related leading to low calcium/phosphate and failure to mineralize bone. - Presentation Bowed legs Genu varum Rachitic Rosary costochondral swelling and delayed fontanelle closure. - Diagnosis Low Vitamin D High Alkaline Phosphatase X-ray showing fraying/cupping of metaphyses. - Treatment Vitamin D and Calcium supplementation. --- High Yield Concepts Summary Slide 93 highyield The Physis Priority The epiphyseal growth plate is the single most important structure in pediatric orthopedics. Any injury involving the physis Salter-Harris classification must be managed with extreme care to prevent longitudinal growth arrest. highyield The Toxic Limp When evaluating a child with a limp the first priority is determining if the child is Toxic Septic Arthritis or Non-Toxic Transient Synovitis/LCPD . Fever tachycardia and elevated inflammatory markers CRP/ESR mandate an emergency joint aspiration. questions 1 A 10-month-old infant who is not yet walking is brought to the ED after the mother noticed he was not moving his right arm. An X-ray reveals a transverse humerus fracture. The mother states he rolled off the couch. What is the most appropriate next step in management? Apply a long-arm cast and follow up in 2 weeks Perform a full skeletal survey and notify child protective services Reassure the mother that infant bones are more porous and break easily Test the child for Osteogenesis Imperfecta Check a Vitamin D level to rule out rickets 1 Breaking an infant s bone requires significant force because it is mostly cartilage. A fracture in a non-ambulatory infant especially with an inconsistent mechanism rolled off couch is a massive red flag for non-accidental trauma NAT . Mandatory reporting and a skeletal survey are required. 2 A 13-year-old overweight male presents with a two-week history of dull left knee pain and a limp. Physical exam reveals limited internal rotation of the left hip. What is the most likely diagnosis? Legg-Calve-Perthes Disease Osgood-Schlatter Disease Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis SCFE Septic Arthritis of the knee Transient Synovitis 2 SCFE characteristically presents in overweight adolescents during puberty. It often presents as referred pain to the knee and the classic physical exam finding is loss of internal rotation of the hip. LCPD occurs in younger children avg 4-8 . 3 A 6-year-old male presents with a fracture that extends through the physis and up into the metaphysis of the distal radius. According to the Salter-Harris classification what type of fracture is this? Type I Type II Type III Type IV Type V 1 Salter-Harris Type II involves the Physis and the Metaphysis Above the growth plate . This is the most common type 75% . 4 A 4-year-old girl is brought in for a limp. She had a cold two weeks ago but is otherwise healthy. She is running around the exam room but limps slightly. She is afebrile and her CRP and ESR are within normal limits. What is the best management? Emergency joint aspiration IV Vancomycin for 2 weeks Rest and NSAIDs Surgical stabilization with screws Ponseti serial casting 2 The presentation is classic for Transient Toxic Synovitis a non-toxic child with a limp following a viral illness and normal inflammatory markers. It is treated conservatively with rest and NSAIDs. 5 A newborn is found to have a clunk when the pediatrician abducts the hips and applies anterior pressure to the greater trochanter during the newborn exam. What is the name of this maneuver and the likely diagnosis? Barlow maneuver; Clubfoot Ortolani maneuver; Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip DDH Galeazzi maneuver; SCFE Trendelenburg test; LCPD Ponseti maneuver; Metatarsus adductus 1 The Ortolani maneuver involves relocating a dislocated hip abduction + anterior pressure which produces a clinical clunk. This is the diagnostic exam for DDH in a newborn. Physis The epiphyseal growth plate; the primary site of longitudinal bone growth. Glossary Torus Fracture A buckle fracture of the bone cortex; heals quickly 2 weeks with splinting. Clinical Greenstick Fracture An incomplete fracture where one side of the cortex breaks and the other remains intact. Clinical Salter-Harris Type II Fracture through the physis and metaphysis; the most common type 75% . Concept Pathognomonic NAT signs Posterior rib fractures retinal hemorrhages and coup-contrecoup head injuries. Clinical DDH Risk Factors Female Firstborn Breech delivery Oligohydramnios. Clinical Barlow vs Ortolani Barlow dislocates the hip adduction ; Ortolani relocates it abduction . Clinical SCFE Classic Patient Overweight adolescent with hip/knee pain and loss of internal hip rotation. Concept LCPD Pathophysiology Idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head. Concept Osgood-Schlatter Apophysitis of the tibial tuberosity typically seen in jumping/running athletes. Clinical Clubfoot Treatment Serial casting Ponseti method started immediately after birth. Clinical Septic Arthritis Signs Toxic appearance high fever refusal to move joint joint widening on X-ray. Clinical Rachitic Rosary Knob-like swelling of the costochondral junctions seen in rickets. Clinical Sickle Cell Osteomyelitis Higher risk for Salmonella and Pseudomonas species. Clinical Kingella kingae An emerging pathogen increasingly recognized as a cause of pediatric bone/joint infection. Concept BandB Pediatrics > Orthopedics Pathoma MSK > Bone and Joints FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Pediatric Orthopedics SketchyPath MSK > Bone & Soft Tissue FirstAid Pediatrics > Orthopedics match Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis resources AnKing Step 2 > Pediatrics > Orthopedics > SCFE match Salter-Harris Fracture Classification resources AnKing Step 1 > MSK > Fractures > Salter-Harris match Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip resources AnKing Step 2 > Pediatrics > Orthopedics > DDH match Osgood-Schlatter Disease resources AnKing Step 1 > MSK > Knee > Osgood-Schlatter match Acute Osteomyelitis Pediatrics resources AnKing Step 1 > Microbiology > Staph > Osteomyelitis The Physis Rule Pediatric bones are mostly cartilage; look for widening of the growth plate or step-offs in the periosteum to find subtle fractures. Bilateral SCFE SCFE is bilateral in 25% of cases; always image the contralateral hip even if symptoms are unilateral. Septic Hip Aspiration A high fever and refusal to weight-bear mandate emergency joint aspiration to rule out septic arthritis before joint destruction occurs. NAT Red Flag Any long-bone fracture in a non-walking infant is an automatic red flag for non-accidental trauma until proven otherwise. Clubfoot Urgency Clubfoot treatment serial casting should begin in the first week of life while the neonatal skeleton is most pliable. mindmap Pediatric Orthopedics Pediatric Bone Structure - Cartilage Higher ratio vs adults; buoyant/compressive birth canal . - Physis Growth Plate Site of longitudinal growth proliferation/hypertrophy . - Periosteum Thicker/Stronger; contains displaced fractures; massive blood supply. - Biomechanics Pliable porous less mineralized; rapid healing. Fracture Patterns - Plastic Bowing Bone bends without cortical break. - Torus Buckle Cortical buckling under compression Slide 12 . - Greenstick One side breaks other stays intact Slide 13 . - Salter-Harris Growth Plate Type I S Straight across physis. Type II A Above Physis + Metaphysis - MOST COMMON 75% . Type III L Lower Physis + Epiphysis . Type IV T Through M + P + E . Type V ER ERasure/Crush compression. Non-Accidental Trauma NAT - Epidemiology 600k/year; 1/3 < 2yo; 70% by trusted person. - Signs Bruises torso/back cigarette burns belt marks nervous parent. - X-ray Signs Posterior rib fractures shaking coup-contrecoup head trauma retinal hemorrhage spiral/bucket-handle fractures. Hip Disorders - DDH Females firstborn breech. Barlow Dislocate / Ortolani Relocate . Pavlik harness. - LCPD Avascular necrosis. Males 5x. Insidious limp. X-ray flattening. - SCFE Overweight adolescent. Referred knee pain. Frog-leg X-ray ice cream off cone . Foot & Knee - Osgood-Schlatter Apophysitis of tibial tuberosity. Atheltes. Point tenderness. - Clubfoot Plantar flexion adduction inversion. Serial casting Ponseti . - Metatarsus Adductus Midfoot only; stretching only. Infections - Osteomyelitis Hematogenous; Staph aureus 80% ; metaphysis. IV Abx 2-6 wks . - Septic Arthritis EMERGENCY. Toxic child; refusal to move. Joint widening. - Transient Synovitis Post-viral; non-toxic; normal markers. Rest/NSAIDs. Metabolic - Rickets Vit D deficiency. Bowing Genu varum Rachitic rosary Fraying metaphyses."}, {"id": "l117", "title": "Pharmacology of NSAIDs", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "l117 Pharmacology of NSAIDs lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. session Lecture 117 Pharmacology readingTime 12-15 min pdf content/L117 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L117 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 117 Pharmacology of NSAIDs Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Session Lecture 117 Introduction to the Eicosanoid Pathway Slide 7 Prostanoids are bioactive lipid mediators derived from arachidonic acid that play diverse roles in homeostasis and inflammation. The eicosanoid cascade begins when cell membrane phospholipids are cleaved by phospholipase A2 releasing arachidonic acid which is then processed by cyclooxygenase COX enzymes. Understanding this physiological foundation is essential because NSAIDs exert their therapeutic and toxic effects by blocking these enzymes thus inhibiting the production of prostaglandins thromboxanes and prostacyclins. Arachidonic Acid AA Cascade Source Phospholipids in the cell membrane are converted to AA by Phospholipase A2 . Metabolism AA is metabolized by Cyclooxygenase COX-1 and COX-2 into unstable endoperoxides PGG2 PGH2 . Final Products Specific Isomerases convert endoperoxides into tissue-specific prostanoids PGE2 Involved in fever pain and GI protection. PGI2 Prostacyclin Vasodilator; inhibits platelet aggregation. TXA2 Thromboxane A2 Vasoconstrictor; promotes platelet aggregation Slide 13 . PGF2α Involved in uterine contraction. PGD2 Involved in sleep and mast cell function. Physiological Characteristics Produced in minute quantities by virtually all tissues. Act locally autocrine/paracrine with a very short half-life ~90 seconds . Signal via specific G-protein coupled receptors GPCRs . COX-1 vs. COX-2 Isoenzymes Slide 16 Cyclooxygenase exists in two primary isoforms that differ in their expression patterns and physiological roles. COX-1 is the constitutive housekeeping enzyme responsible for maintaining basic homeostatic functions like gastric mucosal integrity and platelet aggregation. In contrast COX-2 is primarily an inducible enzyme up-regulated during injury or immune response by inflammatory cytokines TNFα IL-1 . However it is vital to recognize that COX-2 is also constitutively expressed in certain critical tissues including the kidney brain and ciliary body which explains why even selective inhibitors carry renal and central nervous system risks. COX-1 Constitutive Housekeeping functions. Present in GI tract cytoprotection Platelets aggregation via TXA2 and Kidney autoregulation . COX-2 Inducible & Constitutive Inducible Up-regulated in inflammatory sites Slide 16 . Constitutive Sites Kidney Medulla and Cortex CNS Ciliary Body of the eye and Female Reproductive tract . Selectivity Some NSAIDs e.g. Celecoxib are designed to target the larger side pocket of the COX-2 catalytic channel to avoid GI toxicity though this selectivity is dose-dependent. highyield The Side Pocket of COX-2 COX-2 selective inhibitors Coxibs are bulky molecules that bind into a distinct side pocket within the COX-2 enzyme that COX-1 lacks. This allows for anti-inflammatory action without disrupting the housekeeping prostaglandins of the GI tract produced by COX-1. However at high doses this selectivity may be lost. NSAID Class Pharmacokinetics Slide 20 NSAIDs are a chemically heterogeneous group of drugs yet they share several key pharmacokinetic properties due to their nature as weak organic acids. Rapid and complete absorption is the norm and their high degree of protein binding—primarily to albumin—is a critical factor in their distribution and potential drug interactions. Their tendency to accumulate at sites of inflammation and in synovial fluid makes them particularly effective for arthritic conditions while their ability to cross the blood-brain barrier facilitates their central analgesic and antipyretic effects. Absorption Rapid and complete; not significantly affected by food. Distribution Highly Protein Bound ~99% Primarily to albumin. Tissue Accumulation Accumulate in synovial fluid Arthritis efficacy and inflammatory sites. CNS Entry Cross the blood-brain barrier to modulate central pain pathways and the hypothalamus fever . Metabolism & Excretion Hepatic Minor substrates of CYP3A4 and CYP2C families; undergo glucuronidation. Enterohepatic Circulation Contributes to prolonged half-lives in some agents. Renal Excretion Metabolites and small amounts of unchanged drug are excreted via the kidneys. Half-Life Variations Short < 6 hrs Ibuprofen Diclofenac Aspirin. Long > 10 hrs Naproxen Meloxicam Celecoxib. Therapeutic Actions and Class Effects Slide 24 The primary therapeutic goals of NSAID therapy are the management of pain fever and inflammation. These effects are mediated through the inhibition of COX-2-derived prostaglandins which sensitize peripheral nociceptors and mediate the hypothalamic set-point for body temperature. While all NSAIDs are roughly equivalent in their analgesic and antipyretic efficacy at therapeutic doses the choice of agent is often dictated by the patient s individual risk profile the desired duration of action and the specific clinical context such as the need for cardiovascular prophylaxis or closure of a patent ductus arteriosus. Analgesia Peripheral Reduces prostaglandin sensitization of nociceptors to mediators like bradykinin. Central Modulates descending pain pathways in the CNS. Antipyretic Blocks PGE2 synthesis in the hypothalamus to reset the thermal set-point. Anti-inflammatory Inhibits recruitment of inflammatory cells and release of mediators. Special Uses Ophthalmic Topical drops for ocular inflammation Slide 24 . Niacin Flushing Aspirin can block PGD2-mediated flushing caused by high-dose Niacin Slide 367 Lecturer Emphasis . Ductus Arteriosus Used to close a Patent Ductus Arteriosus PDA in neonates Indomethacin is classic . correlate Patent Ductus Arteriosus PDA - Pathophysiology Prostaglandins PGE2 normally maintain the patency of the ductus arteriosus in utero to bypass pulmonary circulation. - Presentation If the ductus remains open after birth it can lead to heart failure and pulmonary hypertension. - Treatment NSAIDs e.g. Indomethacin Ibuprofen are used to inhibit PG synthesis and induce closure. - Lecturer s Point Maternal use of NSAIDs in the 3rd trimester can cause premature closure of the PDA in utero leading to fetal hydrops Slide 50 . Adverse Effects The Gastrointestinal System Slide 41 The most frequent site of NSAID-induced injury is the gastrointestinal tract ranging from minor dyspepsia to life-threatening perforation. This toxicity stems primarily from the inhibition of COX-1-derived PGE2 and PGI2 which are vital for maintaining the gastric mucosal barrier. These prostaglandins normally stimulate the secretion of protective mucus and bicarbonate maintain mucosal blood flow and suppress gastric acid production. By removing these defenses NSAIDs leave the mucosa vulnerable to acid-pepsin injury and impair the repair mechanisms required to heal existing erosions. Mechanism Loss of cytoprotective PGE2/PGI2 COX-1 mediated . Spectrum of Injury Dyspepsia gastric/duodenal ulcers GI bleeding and perforation. Increased Risk Factors History of peptic ulcer disease. H. pylori infection Lecturer Emphasis . Concurrent use of Glucocorticoids impair repair Slide 49 . Concurrent use of Anticoagulants increase bleeding risk . Alcohol consumption direct mucosal irritant . correlate Helicobacter pylori Infection - Pathophysiology Gram-negative bacterium that colonizes the gastric mucosa causing chronic inflammation and weakening defenses. - Risk Factors Significantly increases the risk of peptic ulcers when combined with NSAID use. - Lecturer Point Patients with suspected H. pylori or active ulcers should avoid NSAIDs to prevent catastrophic GI bleeding Slide 426 . Adverse Effects Cardiovascular and Hematologic Slide 33 42 The cardiovascular safety of NSAIDs is governed by a delicate balance between Thromboxane A2 TXA2 produced by platelets and Prostacyclin PGI2 produced by the vascular endothelium. While TXA2 promotes platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction PGI2 acts as a counter-regulatory vasodilator and inhibitor of aggregation. Non-selective NSAIDs and COX-2 selective inhibitors can disrupt this balance toward a pro-thrombotic state. Aspirin is the notable exception; at low doses its irreversible inhibition of platelet COX-1 provides unique cardioprotective effects because mature platelets cannot resynthesize the enzyme unlike vascular endothelial cells. Prothrombotic Risk MI and Stroke Mechanism Preferential inhibition of endothelial PGI2 COX-2 over platelet TXA2 COX-1 . nsNSAIDs COX-2 is more sensitive to inhibition than platelet COX-1 in systemic circulation. COX-2 Selective Only block PGI2 leaving TXA2 unopposed. Bleeding Risk Inhibition of platelet TXA2 synthesis reduces platelet plug formation. Contraindication Perioperative CABG surgery US Boxed Warning due to high thrombotic risk Slide 35 . highyield Aspirin The Irreversible Exception Aspirin is the only NSAID that irreversibly acetylates a serine residue in the COX catalytic channel. Because mature platelets lack a nucleus they cannot resynthesize COX-1 and the antiplatelet effect lasts the life of the platelet 8-10 days . In contrast other NSAIDs are reversible competitive inhibitors Slide 33 . Adverse Effects Renal and Electrolytes Slide 38 47 In the healthy individual prostaglandins play a minimal role in renal hemodynamics; however they become vital emergency regulators in states of reduced effective circulating volume. When the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system RAAS is activated PGE2 and PGI2 are produced to maintain afferent arteriolar vasodilation and preserve the glomerular filtration rate GFR . NSAIDs disrupt this autoregulatory mechanism leading to afferent vasoconstriction acute kidney injury AKI and significant sodium and water retention. Mechanism of AKI Normal Auto-regulation PGs dilate the afferent arteriole ; Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole to maintain GFR. NSAID Effect Blocks afferent vasodilation → reduced RBF and GFR Slide 38 . Electrolyte Disturbances Sodium/Water Retention Reduced PG-mediated excretion leads to peripheral edema and worsened Hypertension or Heart Failure. Hyperkalemia PGs normally facilitate potassium excretion; inhibition leads to K+ reabsorption risking cardiac arrhythmias . correlate The Triple Whammy - Mechanism Concurrent use of NSAIDs ACE Inhibitors/ARBs and Diuretics . - Pathophysiology Diuretics/ACEi reduce volume; ACEi/ARBs dilate the efferent arteriole; NSAIDs constricts the afferent arteriole. - Outcome The combined effect severely drops glomerular pressure frequently precipitating Acute Kidney Injury AKI . - Lecturer s Point Avoid this combination in elderly patients or those with heart failure/CKD Slide 47 735 . Special Populations Pregnancy and Lactation Slide 50 NSAID use during pregnancy requires extreme caution due to specific risks to fetal renal and cardiovascular development. While not strongly linked to congenital malformations in the first trimester regular use after 20 weeks gestation can impair fetal renal function leading to a critical reduction in amniotic fluid—a condition known as oligohydramnios. Furthermore use in the third trimester risks the premature closure of the patent ductus arteriosus which is maintained by prostaglandin activity potentially resulting in irreversible fetal pulmonary hypertension and hydrops fetalis. Oligohydramnios Beginning 20 weeks Amniotic fluid is primarily fetal urine. NSAID-induced fetal renal dysfunction → ↓ urine production → oligohydramnios Slide 910 . PDA Closure Beginning 30 weeks Premature closure in utero → pulmonary hypertension and right-sided heart failure Fetal Hydrops . Recommendation Generally avoid all NSAIDs from 20 weeks gestation onward. Low-dose aspirin may be used if the CV benefits outweigh the risks but only under close supervision. Drug-Drug Interactions Slide 46-49 The pharmacologic profile of NSAIDs predisposes them to numerous clinically significant drug interactions. Many of these are pharmacodynamic involving synergistic toxicities with other agents that affect bleeding renal function or mucosal integrity. For instance the use of SSRIs can potentiate NSAID-induced bleeding by depleting platelet serotonin stores which are necessary for aggregation. Additionally NSAIDs can interfere with the renal clearance of drugs with narrow therapeutic windows such as Lithium leading to potentially fatal toxicity levels. Anticoagulants/Antiplatelets Synergistic risk of major bleeding. SSRIs Block serotonin reuptake into platelets → ↓ serotonin release during activation → ↓ aggregation → Increased Bleeding Risk Slide 49 . ACE Inhibitors/ARBs Increased risk of hyperkalemia and AKI. Diuretics Blunted diuretic and antihypertensive efficacy. Lithium NSAIDs increase renal reabsorption of Lithium leading to toxicity Slide 49 . Glucocorticoids Synergistic GI mucosal damage and impaired healing. mnemonic NSAIDs Toxicity G-R-C-H-A - G GI Ulcers/Bleeding - R Renal AKI Na+ retention K+ retention - C Cardiovascular MI Stroke risk - except Aspirin - H Hematologic Bleeding risk - A Asthma Aspirin-induced questions 1 A 72-year-old male with a history of congestive heart failure and chronic kidney disease is prescribed a loop diuretic and an ACE inhibitor. He presents to the clinic with a 3-day history of severe knee pain and admits to taking high doses of over-the-counter ibuprofen. Laboratory tests reveal a significantly elevated serum creatinine. Which of the following mechanisms best explains his acute injury? Efferent arteriolar vasoconstriction Afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction Directly toxic effect on the renal tubular cells Competitive inhibition of the loop diuretic Unopposed action of Prostacyclin in the medulla 1 NSAIDs inhibit the synthesis of PGE2 and PGI2 which are the primary mediators of afferent arteriolar vasodilation in volume-depleted or RAS-activated states like CHF and CKD . This leads to unopposed afferent vasoconstriction reduced glomerular filtration pressure and AKI. ACE inhibitors simultaneously dilate the efferent arteriole further dropping filtration pressure The Triple Whammy . 2 A 32-year-old pregnant woman in her 32nd week of gestation is advised to avoid taking ibuprofen for her back pain. What is the primary fetal risk associated with NSAID use during this specific stage of pregnancy? Neural tube defects Premature labor Premature closure of the ductus arteriosus Spontaneous abortion Fetal macrosomia 2 Prostaglandins are required to keep the ductus arteriosus open in utero. Inhibition of PT synthesis by NSAIDs in the 3rd trimester especially after 30 weeks can cause its premature closure leading to fetal pulmonary hypertension and hydrops fetalis. 3 Why does low-dose aspirin 81 mg provide cardiovascular protection while other non-selective NSAIDs like naproxen may increase the risk of myocardial infarction? Aspirin is selective for COX-2 in the endothelium. Naproxen has a shorter half-life than aspirin. Aspirin irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1. Naproxen does not reach the portal circulation. Aspirin stimulates the production of Prostacyclin. 2 Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1 in platelets. Since platelets lack nuclei and cannot resynthesize the enzyme the anti-thrombotic effect decreased TXA2 lasts for the life of the platelet 8-10 days . Non-selective NSAIDs are reversible inhibitors and tend to inhibit endothelial PGI2 anti-thrombotic more effectively than platelet TXA2 in systemic circulation potentially shifting the balance toward thrombosis. 4 A patient taking fluoxetine an SSRI for depression is started on an NSAID for osteoarthritis. Which of the following adverse developments should the clinician monitor most closely? Serotonin syndrome Increased risk of gastric perforation Excessive platelet aggregation Petechiae and mucosal bleeding Hypertensive crisis 3 SSRIs inhibit the reuptake of serotonin into platelets. Serotonin is a key mediator required for platelet aggregation. When combined with NSAIDs which inhibit TXA2 there is a synergistic reduction in platelet function and a significantly increased risk of bleeding. 5 Which of the following patients is at the highest risk for developing an NSAID-induced peptic ulcer? A 25-year-old with a history of asthma. A 45-year-old with a high-protein diet. A 60-year-old patient who is also taking prednisone. A 30-year-old using a COX-2 selective inhibitor. A 50-year-old with primary hypertension controlled by an ARB. 2 Glucocorticoids like prednisone do not cause ulcers on their own but significantly increase the risk of GI injury when combined with NSAIDs by impairing the mucosal repair and turnover mechanisms. Advanced age is also an independent risk factor for NSAID-induced GI toxicity. What is the primary source of arachidonic acid in the cell? Cell membrane phospholipids released by Phospholipase A2. Concept Which prostanoid is primarily responsible for promoting platelet aggregation? Thromboxane A2 TXA2 . Concept What is the key mechanism of Aspirin s unique antiplatelet effect? Irreversible acetylation of a serine residue in the COX-1 enzyme. Mechanism Why are COX-2 selective inhibitors associated with an increased CV risk? They inhibit endothelial PGI2 anti-thrombotic without inhibiting platelet TXA2 pro-thrombotic . Mechanism What is the Triple Whammy regarding renal function? NSAIDs constrict afferent + ACEi/ARBs dilate efferent + Diuretics volume depletion . Clinical Define oligohydramnios in the context of maternal NSAID use. Too little amniotic fluid due to fetal renal dysfunction occurring after 20 weeks gestation. Clinical Which GI protection mechanism is lost with COX-1 inhibition? Secretion of mucus and bicarbonate mucosal blood flow and reduction of gastric acid. Concept What is the classic clinical use of Indomethacin in neonates? Closure of a Patent Ductus Arteriosus PDA . Clinical Why should NSAIDs be avoided in patients with hepatic cirrhosis? Increased risk of AKI due to dependence on PG-mediated renal blood flow in cirrhotic states. Clinical What is the effect of NSAID use on serum potassium? Hyperkalemia increased reabsorption which increases the risk of arrhythmias. Clinical BandB Immunology > Pharm > Glucocorticoids & NSAIDs SketchyPharm Blood & Inflammation > Anti-inflammatory > NSAIDs FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Pharm > Arachidonic Acid Pathway Pathoma Hemostasis > Primary Hemostasis > Aspirin match Aspirin mechanism resources Irreversibly inactivates COX via acetylation; impairs platelet aggregation TXA2 decrease . match NSAID renal side effects resources Decreased GFR via afferent arteriole constriction; Na+ and H2O retention. match COX-2 selective inhibitors resources Celecoxib; binds COX-2 side pocket; higher CV risk but lower GI risk. match Aspirin-induced asthma resources Shifting AA metabolism to leukotriene pathway; bronchoconstriction. The 20-Week Rule Avoid NSAIDs after 20 weeks gestation to prevent fetal oligohydramnios. The 30-Week Rule Avoid NSAIDs after 30 weeks gestation to prevent premature closure of the fetal ductus arteriosus. Aspirin & Platelets Aspirin s antiplatelet effect is permanent for the life of the platelet 8-10 days due to irreversible COX-1 inhibition. The AKI Prereq Prostaglandins are essentially emergency vasodilators; they are most vital for kidney function in volume-depleted or heart failure states. SSRI Synergism SSRIs increase NSAID bleeding risk by depleting platelet serotonin stores. mindmap Pharmacology of NSAIDs Prostanoid Pathway - Arachidonic Acid Source Phospholipid cell membrane via Phospholipase A2 - COX Enzymes COX-1 Constitutive/Housekeeping vs. COX-2 Inducible/Inflammatory - Key Products PGE2 GI cytoprotection Fever Pain sensitization TXA2 Platelet aggregation Vasoconstriction PGI2 Anti-aggregation Vasodilation NSAID Class PK - Absorption Rapid/Complete; Weak acids pKa 3-5 - Distribution 99% Albumin bound; Synovial fluid accumulation - Metabolism Hepatic CYPs ; Enterohepatic circulation Adverse Effects - GI Ulcers Perforation Dyspepsia COX-1 loss - CV MI and Stroke risk PGI2/TXA2 imbalance ; BBW for CABG - Renal AKI Afferent constriction ; Hyperkalemia; Hypertensive blunting - Pregnancy Oligohydramnios >20w ; PDA closure >30w Aspirin The Outlier - Mechanism Irreversible acetylation of Serine residue - Dose-Effect Low dose Anti-platelet ; High dose Anti-inflammatory Drug Interactions - The Triple Whammy NSAIDs + ACEi/ARBs + Diuretics - Bleeding Synergism SSRIs Warfarin Anticoagulants - Narrow Window Drugs Lithium reabsorption increases"}, {"id": "l118", "title": "Pharmacology of NSAIDs Part 2", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "l118 Pharmacology of NSAIDs Part 2 lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. session Lecture 118 Pharmacology readingTime 15-18 min pdf content/L118 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L118 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 118 Pharmacology of NSAIDs Part 2 Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Session Lecture 118 Aspirin ASA The Irreversible Inhibitor Slides 29-34 Aspirin acetylsalicylic acid is unique among NSAIDs due to its mechanism of irreversible acetylation of the cyclooxygenase COX enzyme. By covalently modifying a serine residue in the catalytic channel Aspirin permanently disables both COX-1 and COX-2. This effect is most profound in platelets which lack a nucleus and cannot resynthesize new enzymes. Consequently the antiplatelet effect of a single dose of Aspirin persists for the entire 8–10 day lifespan of the platelet. Dose-Dependent Effects Low Dose <325 mg/day Primary use is antiplatelet cardioprotection . It effectively inhibits platelet TXA2 without significantly suppressing endothelial PGI2 throughout the systemic circulation. Intermediate Dose 325–2400 mg/day Used for analgesia and antipyresis. High Dose 2400–4000 mg/day Required for potent anti-inflammatory effects though often limited by toxicity. Cardioprotective Balance Aspirin reduces the risk of myocardial infarction and stroke by shifting the balance towards anti-thrombosis blocking TXA2 while endothelial PGI2 recovers due to enzyme resynthesis in nucleated endothelial cells . Salicylate Toxicity and Management Slides 35-38 Toxicity from Aspirin salicylism presents with a classic progression of symptoms and acid-base disturbances. Early signs include tinnitus ringing in the ears dizziness and hearing loss. Acid-Base Disturbance 1. Stage 1 Respiratory Alkalosis Salicylates directly stimulate the medullary respiratory center causing hyperventilation and a drop in PCO2. 2. Stage 2 Metabolic Acidosis Salicylates uncouple oxidative phosphorylation leading to the accumulation of organic acids lactic acid ketoacids . This result is a High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis HAGMA . Clinical Pearl The most common finding in adults is a mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis. Management Ion Trapping Salicylic acid is a weak organic acid pKa 3.5 . By administering Sodium Bicarbonate to alkalinize the urine pH > 7.5 the drug becomes ionized Sal- and trapped in the renal tubules preventing reabsorption and facilitating excretion. highyield Reye Syndrome Reye syndrome is a rare but life-threatening condition characterized by fulminant hepatic failure microvesicular steatosis and encephalopathy . It is strongly associated with Aspirin use in children during a viral illness specifically Influenza or Varicella Chickenpox . For this reason Aspirin is strictly contraindicated in the pediatric population; Acetaminophen or Ibuprofen are the preferred alternatives for fever. Aspirin-Exacerbated Respiratory Disease AERD Slide 47 AERD also known as the Aspirin Triad consists of Asthma Nasal Polyps and Rhinosinusitis . In susceptible individuals blocking the COX pathway shunts arachidonic acid towards the lipoxygenase LOX pathway. This leads to an overproduction of leukotrienes LTC4 LTD4 LTE4 which mediate intense bronchoconstriction and nasal inflammation. Propionic Acid Derivatives Ibuprofen and Naproxen Slides 55-60 These are the most commonly prescribed non-selective NSAIDs nsNSAIDs . They are reversible competitive inhibitors of COX. Ibuprofen Short half-life ~2 hours . Available intravenously for the closure of a Patent Ductus Arteriosus PDA in neonates. Naproxen Notable for a significantly longer half-life ~12–17 hours allowing for convenient twice-daily dosing. Drug Interaction with Aspirin If a patient takes ibuprofen before aspirin the ibuprofen can competitively bind to the COX-1 active site preventing aspirin from irreversibly acetylating it. This eliminates the cardioprotective antiplatelet effect of aspirin. Guidance Patients should take Aspirin at least 30-60 minutes before taking other nsNSAIDs. Acetic Acid Derivatives Indomethacin Ketorolac and Diclofenac Slides 61-70 Indomethacin A very potent NSAID. Uses Acute gouty arthritis closure of PDA and as a tocolytic to stop preterm labor . Adverse Effects High incidence of CNS effects including severe frontal headaches dizziness and confusion. Ketorolac Toradol A potent systemic analgesic with efficacy similar to morphine for acute pain. Limitation Carries an extremely high risk of gastrointestinal bleeding and renal toxicity. Its use is strictly limited to a maximum of 5 days . Diclofenac Shows a slight relative preference for COX-2. Hepatotoxicity Uniquely associated with significant elevations in serum aminotransferases ; monitoring of LFTs is recommended for long-term use. Selective COX-2 Inhibitors Celecoxib Slides 80-85 Celecoxib is designed to target the larger side pocket of the COX-2 enzyme while sparing the narrower COX-1 channel. Benefit Significantly reduced incidence of gastrointestinal ulcers and bleeding compared to nsNSAIDs. Risk Increased risk of cardiovascular thrombotic events MI and stroke . This occurs because it inhibits endothelial PGI2 anti-thrombotic but does NOT inhibit platelet TXA2 pro-thrombotic shifting the physiological balance toward clotting. Contraindication Patients with Sulfonamide allergy as Celecoxib contains a sulfa moiety. Acetaminophen Tylenol Paracetamol Slides 90-100 Acetaminophen is an effective analgesic and antipyretic but lacks significant anti-inflammatory or anti-platelet activity . Metabolism and Toxicity Normally metabolized via glucuronidation and sulfation. A minor portion is converted by CYP2E1 into NAPQI a highly reactive and toxic metabolite. Glutathione normally detoxifies NAPQI. In overdose glutathione is depleted and NAPQI causes central lobular hepatic necrosis . Antidote N-acetylcysteine NAC . It acts by restoring glutathione stores and directly binding to and detoxifying NAPQI. correlate Kawasaki Disease Kawasaki disease is one of the few pediatric exceptions where high-dose Aspirin is used. It is a medium-vessel vasculitis in children that specifically risks the development of coronary artery aneurysms . Aspirin is used here for its both anti-inflammatory and anti-platelet properties to prevent thrombus formation within the aneurysms. questions 1 A 65-year-old male with a history of a recent myocardial infarction is prescribed low-dose aspirin and ibuprofen for osteoarthritis. He is advised to space the timing of these medications. Which of the following is the most appropriate dosing instruction to ensure aspirin maintains its antiplatelet effect? Take ibuprofen 30 minutes before taking aspirin. Take aspirin at least 30-60 minutes before taking ibuprofen. Take both medications simultaneously with food. Discontinue the aspirin as ibuprofen provides equivalent cardioprotection. Take aspirin only once every 8-10 days. 1 Ibuprofen is a reversible inhibitor that competitively blocks the COX-1 binding site. If taken first it prevents aspirin from irreversibly acetylating the enzyme. Aspirin must be taken first at least 30-60 minutes prior to allow it to permanently modify the enzyme before ibuprofen competes for the site. 2 A 24-year-old patient is brought to the emergency department after an intentional aspirin overdose. Arterial blood gas results show a pH of 7.42 a low PCO2 and a low bicarbonate level. The patient also reports persistent ringing in the ears . Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacological treatment to facilitate drug excretion? Ammonium chloride N-acetylcysteine Sodium bicarbonate Activated charcoal only Hemodialysis 2 Salicylic acid is a weak acid pKa 3.5 . Alkalinization of the urine with Sodium Bicarbonate increases the fraction of the drug that is in its ionized form Sal- within the renal tubules. The ionized drug cannot be reabsorbed ion trapping thereby significantly increasing the rate of renal excretion. 3 A 6-year-old boy presents with nausea vomiting and confusion after recovering from a recent flu-like illness. Physical examination reveals hepatomegaly without jaundice. Laboratory studies show elevated AST/ALT and hyperammonemia. A liver biopsy shows microvesicular steatosis. Which of the following medications likely contributed to this presentation? Acetaminophen Ibuprofen Aspirin Indomethacin Celecoxib 2 This case describes Reye syndrome encephalopathy and microvesicular fatty liver which is triggered by using aspirin in children with viral infections like Influenza or Varicella. Acetaminophen and Ibuprofen are the safe alternatives in this population. 4 A 45-year-old patient with a history of severe post-operative pain has been receiving parenteral ketorolac in the hospital for 4 days. Why must the clinician discontinue or switch this medication to another agent tomorrow? Risk of tachyphylaxis and loss of efficacy. Increased risk of serious GI bleeding and renal failure. Potential for serotonin syndrome. Risk of hepatotoxicity serum transaminase elevation . Development of tolerance similar to opioids. 1 Ketorolac is a highly potent analgesic but carries an exceptionally high risk of gastrointestinal bleeding ulceration and acute kidney injury compared to other NSAIDs. Clinical guidelines and the FDA strictly limit its systemic use to a maximum of 5 days. 5 A patient is admitted for an acetaminophen overdose. The mechanism of hepatotoxicity in this patient involves the depletion of which of the following substances? CYP2E1 enzyme Glutathione N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine NAPQI Vitamin K Glucuronic acid 1 In acetaminophen overdose the normal glucuronidation and sulfation pathways are saturated. Increased amounts are metabolized by CYP2E1 to the toxic metabolite NAPQI. NAPQI is normally detoxified by Glutathione; once Glutathione stores are depleted NAPQI causes direct central lobular hepatic necrosis. What is the unique mechanism of Aspirin compared to other NSAIDs? Irreversible acetylation of COX-1 and COX-2. Mechanism Why does the antiplatelet effect of Aspirin last 8-10 days? Platelets lack a nucleus and cannot resynthesize COX-1 after irreversible inhibition. Mechanism What is the primary treatment for salicylate toxicity Salicylism ? Alkalinization of urine with Sodium Bicarbonate Ion Trapping . Clinical What are the components of the Aspirin Triad AERD ? Asthma Nasal Polyps and Aspirin/NSAID sensitivity. Clinical Why is Aspirin contraindicated in children with viral illnesses? Risk of Reye Syndrome fatty liver and encephalopathy . Clinical What is the maximum duration for systemic Ketorolac use? 5 days due to high GI and renal risk . Clinical Which NSAID is specifically used for acute gout and PDA closure? Indomethacin. Clinical Why does Celecoxib increase CV risk but decrease GI risk? Inhibits PGI2 endothelium but not TXA2 platelets favoring thrombosis; spares GI protective COX-1. Mechanism Which medication is used as the antidote for Acetaminophen overdose? N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione . Clinical How should Aspirin be dosed relative to Ibuprofen? Aspirin should be taken at least 30-60 minutes BEFORE ibuprofen. Clinical SketchyPharm Blood & Inflammation > Anti-inflammatory > NSAIDs Physeo Pharm > MSK > Aspirin FirstAid MSK > Pharm > Aspirin match Aspirin ASA resources AK Step1 v11 Physeo 09 Pharm 09 Musculoskeletal 02 Aspirin AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 04 Pharm 03 Aspirin match Acetaminophen resources AK Step1 v11 Physeo 09 Pharm 09 Musculoskeletal 03 Acetaminophen match NSAIDs & Celecoxib resources AK Step1 v11 SketchyPharm 03 Blood & Inflammation 03 Anti-inflammatory 01 NSAIDs AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 04 Pharm 04 Celecoxib The Salicylism Triad Tinnitus Hyperventilation Respiratory Alkalosis and Metabolic Acidosis. Ion Trapping Use bicarbonate to treat Aspirin overdose; an alkaline urine keeps salicylic acid ionized and trapped for excretion. ASA/NSAID Interference Always dose Aspirin FIRST. Reversible NSAIDs can block the binding of irreversible Aspirin if taken beforehand. The 5-Day Rule Limit Ketorolac use to 5 days to prevent severe GI bleeding and renal failure. NAPQI & NAC Acetaminophen toxicity is caused by NAPQI. N-acetylcysteine NAC restores glutathione to detoxify it. mindmap NSAIDs Part 2 Aspirin - Mechanism Irreversible acetylation - Duration Life of platelet 8-10 days - Toxicity Tinnitus acid-base disturbances Salicylism - Contraindications Children Reye Syndrome - Liver/Brain Specific NSAIDs - Propionic Acids Ibuprofen PDA Naproxen Long half-life - Acetic Acids Indomethacin Gout PDA Ketorolac 5-day limit Diclofenac Hepatotoxicity - Enolic Acids Meloxicam once daily Selective COX-2 Celecoxib - Structure Sulfonamide Sulfa allergy - Mechanism Bind side pocket of COX-2 - Pros/Cons Lower GI risk Higher CV risk Acetaminophen - Role No anti-inflammatory/anti-platelet effect - Overdose NAPQI metabolite depletes glutathione - Antidote N-acetylcysteine NAC"}, {"id": "l119", "title": "Innate Immunity Part 1", "module": "Immunology", "content": "l119 Innate Immunity Part 1 lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. session Lecture 119 Immunology readingTime 15-20 min pdf content/L119 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L119 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 119 Innate Immunity Part 1 Lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. Session Lecture 119 Overview of the Immune System Slide 2-5 The human immune system is broadly divided into two interlinked branches the innate immune response and the adaptive immune response. The innate system serves as the body’s instantaneous non-specific first and second lines of defense. Contrastingly the adaptive system constitutes the highly specific third line of defense that develops over days to weeks generating specialized T-lymphocytes B-lymphocytes and long-lasting memory. The innate system does not have memory; it mounts the exact same response upon repeated exposure to a pathogen using germline-encoded receptors to rapidly hold the fort and prevent disseminated infection until the adaptive system can mount a targeted strike. Core Differences Time Course Innate is immediate minutes to hours ; Adaptive is delayed days to weeks . Specificity Innate recognizes conserved generic molecular patterns PAMPs ; Adaptive recognizes specific antigenic epitopes. Memory Innate possesses no immunological memory; Adaptive confers life-long immunity to re-exposure. Components Innate Physical barriers phagocytes neutrophils macrophages NK cells complement proteins and cytokines. Adaptive T-cells cell-mediated and B-cells/Antibodies humoral . The First Line of Defense Barriers Slide 6-9 Before a pathogen can even trigger a cellular immune response it must bypass the body s mechanical and chemical barricades. These barriers make up the first line of defense constantly preventing the colonization and systemic entry of ubiquitous environmental microbes. The sheer effectiveness of these basic barriers is evident when they fail. Mechanical and Physical Barriers Intact Epithelium The tightly packed squamous cells of the epidermis form a rugged impenetrable armor. Simply breaching this barrier is fatal; burn victims are incredibly susceptible to systemic sepsis specifically because they lose this primary mechanical shield. Mucociliary Escalator In the respiratory tract goblet cells produce sticky mucus that traps inhaled pathogens. Ciliated epithelial cells then consistently sweep this contaminated mucus upward toward the pharynx to be swallowed and destroyed by stomach acid or coughed out. Flushing Mechanisms Tears saliva and urine constantly wash away microbes preventing adherence to the mucosal surfaces. Chemical and Biological Barriers Acidic Microenvironments The low pH of the stomach pH 1-3 skin pH 5.5 and vagina pH 4.5 creates a hostile environment that severely stunts bacterial growth. Microcidal Molecules Lysozyme Found in tears and saliva this enzyme continuously cleaves the peptidoglycan backbone NAG-NAM bonds of bacterial cell walls leading to osmotic lysis. Defensins & Cathelicidins Short cationic antimicrobial peptides that insert themselves into and disrupt microbial membranes. The Microbiome Biological Barrier The trillions of commensal bacteria residing on our skin and within our GI tract outcompete pathogenic invaders for space and nutrients. Furthermore metabolic byproducts from the microbiome modulate the host s immune tone. correlate Microbiome Dysbiosis and Systemic Disease - Pathophysiology Disruption of the normal flora dysbiosis —often through the overuse of broad-spectrum antibiotics or poor diet—can lead to unchecked proliferation of opportunistic pathogens e.g. Clostridioides difficile . - Systemic Implications The lecturer emphasized that the microbiome s influence extends deeply into systemic physiology. An imbalanced microbiome is now heavily implicated not just in local GI diseases but in widespread systemic and neurologic disorders notably playing a role in the pathogenesis of Parkinson s Disease . - Lecturer s Point Always weigh the necessity of antibiotics as collateral damage to the biological barrier can trigger devastating downstream effects. The Second Line of Defense Inflammation Slide 10-15 If a pathogen successfully breaches the first-line barriers e.g. via a laceration or puncture the second line of defense is triggered. The hallmark of the second line of defense is Inflammation a localized tissue response aimed at containing the destructive agent and preparing the tissue for repair. The Cardinal Signs of Inflammation The classic clinical signs of local inflammation driven entirely by vasodilation and increased vascular permeability are memorized by their Latin roots 1. Rubor Redness Caused by arteriolar vasodilation increasing local blood flow. 2. Calor Heat Also caused by increased blood flow and local metabolic activity. 3. Tumor Swelling Edema resulting from fluid and plasma proteins leaking into the interstitial space due to increased microvascular permeability. 4. Dolor Pain Edema placing physical pressure on nerve endings combined with chemical irritation from inflammatory mediators like bradykinin and prostaglandins. 5. Functio Laesa Loss of Function A conscious and reflexive protective mechanism due to pain and swelling. Macrophages as the Sentinels The initial inflammatory cascade is centrally coordinated by tissue-resident Macrophages . These cells are the vigilant generals sitting in the peripheral tissues. Recognition and Alarm Macrophages use specialized receptors to detect signs of damage or microbial invasion. Upon detection they rapidly secrete potent pro-inflammatory cytokines Tumor Necrosis Factor alpha TNF-α Interleukin-1 IL-1 and Interferon-gamma IFN-γ . The Master Switch NF-κB These cytokines act on local host cells by activating NF-κB . NF-κB is a critical transcription factor described as the master switch of the immune system. Once activated NF-κB translocates to the nucleus to rapidly transcribe a massive array of inflammatory genes amplifying the local response. Systemic Effects of Cytokines TNF-α and IL-1 don t just act locally; they enter the bloodstream to cause systemic whole-body effects. Fever IL-1 acts on the hypothalamus as an endogenous pyrogen raising the body s set-point temperature to inhibit bacterial replication. Leukocyte Recruitment TNF-α strongly upregulates adhesion molecules on the vascular endothelium promoting the capture and migration of neutrophils. Hemostasis TNF-α shifts the local endothelium to a pro-thrombotic state intentionally creating micro-clots to wall off the infection and prevent systemic dissemination. Soluble Defense The Complement System Slide 24-29 The innate immune system employs a lethal self-amplifying cascade of approximately 16 circulating plasma proteins known as the Complement System . These proteins continuously circulate in an inactive zymogen state. When triggered they rapidly undergo sequential cleavage and activation to clear pathogenic threats. The system can be initiated via three distinct pathways. The Pathways of Activation Regardless of how it starts all three pathways converge on the creation of the C3 Convertase the most pivotal enzyme in the cascade that cleaves the central C3 protein. 1. The Classical Pathway Traditionally considered part of the adaptive response since it requires antibodies this pathway is triggered when the C1 complex binds to Antibody-Antigen complexes . Specifically it requires either one IgM pentamer or two closely spaced IgG molecules. Therefore IgM is significantly more efficient at activating complement. This pathway uniquely requires Calcium Ca2+ for the C1q-r-s complex assembly. 2. The Lectin Pathway An entirely innate mechanism. It is activated when circulating Mannan-binding lectin MBL binds to repeating mannose or N-acetylglucosamine NAG residues on the surface of fungi bacteria and some viruses. 3. The Alternative Pathway Also entirely innate. This pathway operates in a constant state of low-level spontaneous activation often called tick-over . Normally host cells immediately degrade these active rogue proteins. However if the proteins land on foreign unprotected microbial surfaces e.g. bacterial LPS the pathway rapid amplifies. This pathway specifically requires Magnesium Mg2+ and Factor B . The Four Main Functions of Complement Once the C3 convertase has cleaved C3 into C3a and C3b the cascade executes four essential defensive maneuvers Opsonization C3b and C4b covalently bind to the bacterial surface acting as bright eat me tags for phagocytes. Opsonization is absolutely critical for the clearance of heavily encapsulated bacteria e.g. Streptococcus pneumoniae Neisseria meningitidis which otherwise repel phagocytes. Inflammation Anaphylatoxins The small cleavage fragments C3a and C5a are potent chemoattractants and anaphylatoxins. They recruit neutrophils to the site of infection and trigger mast cells to degranulate releasing histamine and intensely driving up vascular permeability. Lysis The MAC C5b recruits C6 C7 C8 and many molecules of C9 to form the Membrane Attack Complex MAC . This structure inserts a literal pore into the bacterial cell membrane destroying its osmotic integrity and lysing the cell especially critical for fighting Gram-negative bacteria like Neisseria . Immune Complex Clearance Complement binds to circulating antibody-antigen complexes allowing them to be recognized and safely cleared by erythrocytes transporting them to the spleen. correlate Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria PNH - Pathophysiology The complement alternative pathway is constantly ticking over meaning host cells must actively defend themselves from friendly fire. They do this by expressing GPI-anchored regulatory proteins like DAF Decay-Accelerating Factor / CD55 and MAC-inhibitory protein CD59 on their membranes. PNH is an acquired genetic mutation in the PIGA gene leading to the loss of these protective GPI anchors. - Presentation Without CD55/CD59 shielding the host s red blood cells they are destroyed by the alternative complement pathway MAC lysis . This leads to episodic hemolytic anemia thrombosis and the classic sign of dark hemoglobin-rich urine notably typically seen in the morning upon waking nocturnal hemoglobinuria . - Diagnosis & Treatment Diagnosed via flow cytometry absence of CD55/CD59 . Treated with Eculizumab a terminal complement inhibitor . questions 1 A 45-year-old male burn victim hospitalized for extensive third-degree burns over 40% of his body surface area becomes medically unstable with a high fever and hypotension. Blood cultures grow Pseudomonas aeruginosa. His rapid clinical deterioration is primarily attributed to the loss of which branch of his immunological defense? The cellular adaptive immune response The mechanical component of the first line of defense The soluble complement cascade The phagocytic second line of defense The regulatory action of Natural Killer cells 1 The intact epithelium of the skin serves as an immensely powerful mechanical barrier against environmental pathogens functioning as the crucial first line of defense. Burn victims lose this barrier making them highly susceptible to disseminated life-threatening infections from opportunistic organisms like Pseudomonas. 2 While investigating a patient with recurrent bacterial rhinosinusitis and pneumonia a physician orders functional assays of the patient s immune system. Results indicate a severe defect in the activation of the Master Switch transcription factor responsible for the massive upregulation of inflammatory and immune-related genes following macrophage signaling. Which molecule is defective in this patient? Bradykinin Interleukin-1 IL-1 Interferon-gamma IFN-γ C3 Convertase NF-κB 4 NF-κB Nuclear Factor kappa-light-chain-enhancer of activated B cells is widely recognized as the master switch of the immune system. Upon stimulation by cytokines like TNF-α and IL-1 it translocates to the nucleus to rapidly switch on the transcription of hundreds of genes crucial for the inflammatory response. 3 An immunology researcher is running in vitro experiments to optimize the activation of the Classical Complement Pathway to study its lytic effects on target cells. She prepares a specialized buffer solution for the assay. For the C1q-C1r-C1s complex to assemble and successfully initiate this specific pathway which of the following ions MUST be present in her buffer? Magnesium Mg2+ Calcium Ca2+ Potassium K+ Iron Fe2+ Zinc Zn2+ 1 The Classical Pathway strictly relies on Calcium Ca2+ for the initial structural assembly of the C1 complex holding the C1r and C1s subunits onto the C1q backbone . Conversely the Alternative Pathway requires Magnesium Mg2+ to function. 4 A 30-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with fatigue and episodes of passing very dark urine predominantly in the mornings. Flow cytometry of her red blood cells reveals an absence of CD55 and CD59 on the cellular surface. This patient s hematologic destruction is directly mediated by an inability to regulate which of the following processes? Macrophage phagocytosis Spontaneous alternative complement pathway activation Antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity ADCC T-cell mediated apoptosis Pathogen opsonization via the lectin pathway 1 The patient has Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria PNH . RBCs rely on DAF CD55 and MAC-inhibitory protein CD59 to protect themselves from the alternative complement pathway which runs in a constant state of low-level spontaneous activation tick-over . Without these regulators host cells are subjected to unregulated complement-mediated lysis. 5 A massive influx of neutrophils is noted in the purulent sputum of a patient infected with a heavily encapsulated strain of Streptococcus pneumoniae. To effectively engulf and destroy this pathogen the neutrophils rely on the host s complement system to physically tag the thick bacterial capsule. Which complement fragment is primarily responsible for performing this critical function? C5a C3b C5b-9 MAC C3a Factor B 1 C3b along with C4b acts as the primary opsonin in the complement cascade. It covalently binds to the outer surface of pathogens allowing the complement receptors natively present on phagocytes to grab onto the slick protective capsules and initiate phagocytosis. What is the primary function of Lysozyme and where is it found? Found in tears and saliva; it s a microcidal enzyme that cleaves the peptidoglycan cell walls of bacteria. First Line Defense What are the 5 cardinal signs of inflammation in Latin? Rubor Redness Calor Heat Tumor Swelling Dolor Pain and Functio Laesa Loss of Function . Inflammation Which transcription factor acts as the master switch for the inflammatory immune response? NF-κB. Concept Which pro-inflammatory cytokine is a potent endogenous pyrogen that causes fever? Interleukin-1 IL-1 . Cytokines Which immunoglobulin class is most efficient at activating the Classical Complement pathway? IgM due to its large pentameric structure providing multiple binding sites for the C1 complex . Complement What specific ion is required for the assembly of the C1 complex in the Classical pathway? Calcium Ca2+ . Complement Which complement pathway is constantly active in a state of low-level spontaneous activation tick-over ? The Alternative Pathway. Complement What is the primary function of the complement fragments C3a and C5a? Anaphylatoxins They trigger mast cell degranulation and serve as potent chemoattractants to recruit neutrophils. Complement What is the key molecular defect in Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria PNH ? Loss of GPI anchors resulting in a deficiency of CD55 DAF and CD59 on the surface of red blood cells. Clinical Which portion of the complement system directly punches holes pores into Gram-negative bacterial membranes? The Membrane Attack Complex MAC comprised of C5b-9. Complement B&B Immunology > Innate Immunity > Complement System FirstAid Immunology > Complement SketchyIM Innate Immunity > Complement Pathoma Inflammation and Healing > Inflammation match Complement System Pathways resources AK Step1 v11 B&B 14 Immunology 01 Innate Immunity 01 Complement System AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 12 Immunology 02 Innate Immunity 02 Complement match Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria PNH resources AK Step1 v11 Pathoma 05 RBC Disorders 03 Normocytic Anemia Extravascular Hemolysis PNH AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 08 Hematology Oncology 04 Pathology 13 Nonhemolytic Normocytic Anemias PNH match Acute Inflammation & Cardinal Signs resources AK Step1 v11 Pathoma 02 Inflammation and Healing 01 Introduction to Inflammation AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 03 Pathology 01 Inflammation 01 Acute Inflammation The Master Switch NF-κB immediately translocates to the nucleus to trigger the vast array of systemic inflammatory genes required for the second line of defense. Opsonization is Essential Un-opsonized encapsulated organisms are inherently invisible or repulsive to phagocytes. C3b tagging is absolute critical for fighting organisms like Strep. pneumo and N. meningitidis. The Constant Tick-Over The Alternative Pathway doesn t need to be consciously triggered ; it is always running in the background. If a rogue C3b lands on host cells DAF/CD55 rapidly deactivates it. If it lands on unprotected pathogen LPS it amplifies instantly. Microbiome as a Defense Mechanism Never underestimate the power of the microbiome. It occupies real estate on mucosal surfaces completely blocking pathogenic adherence through sheer competitive saturation at the first line of defense. mindmap Innate Immunity Part 1 First Line of Defense - Physical Barriers Skin epithelium mucosal membranes mucociliary escalator - Chemical Barriers - Gastric/Vaginal/Skin pH - Lysozyme cleaves peptidoglycan - Defensins & Cathelicidins - Biological Barriers The normal microbiome competes for space/nutrients; linked to systemic health/Parkinson s Second Line of Defense - Cardinal Signs Calor Dolor Rubor Tumor Loss of Function - Macrophages The primary tissue sentinels overseeing inflammation - Cytokines Secrete TNF-alpha IL-1 endogenous pyrogen IFN-gamma - NF-κB The Master Switch; activated by cytokines to upregulate immune genes The Complement System - Pathways of Activation - Classical Relies on Antibody-Antigen complexes IgM > IgG ; requires Ca2+ - Lectin Relies on Mannan-Binding Lectin MBL connecting to microbial sugars - Alternative Spontaneous tick-over activation on foreign surfaces LPS ; requires Mg2+ - Effector Functions - Opsonization C3b/C4b tag encapsulated bacteria - Inflammation C3a/C5a act as anaphylatoxins mast cells chemoattractants - Lysis MAC C5b-9 punches holes in Gram-negative bacteria - Regulation Error PNH - Defect Loss of GPI anchor leading to absence of CD55 and CD59 - Result RBCs destroyed by tick-over alternative pathway Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria"}, {"id": "l120", "title": "Innate Immunity Part 2", "module": "Immunology", "content": "l120 Innate Immunity Part 2 lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. session Lecture 120 Immunology readingTime 15-20 min pdf content/L120 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L120 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 120 Innate Immunity Part 2 Lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. Session Lecture 120 Pattern Recognition Receptors PRRs Slide 17-21 The innate immune system is able to rapidly identify hostile invaders because it is hard-wired with a set of germline-encoded sensors known as Pattern Recognition Receptors PRRs . Unlike the B and T cell receptors of the adaptive immune system—which undergo complex and time-consuming V D J recombination to recognize infinitely variable specific antigens—PRRs are identical across all individuals of a species. They are evolutionary relics designed to recognize generic highly conserved microbial structures essential for pathogen survival collectively termed Pathogen-Associated Molecular Patterns PAMPs . Examples of PAMPs include bacterial lipopolysaccharide LPS peptidoglycan and viral double-stranded RNA. Toll-Like Receptors TLRs The most extensively studied class of PRRs are the Toll-Like Receptors TLRs . Found abundantly on macrophages dendritic cells and mast cells TLRs serve as the primary alarm system for the innate immune response. Structurally they are transmembrane proteins. Notably they are not only found on the outer cell surface to detect extracellular bacteria but also lining endocytic vesicles to detect intracellular viruses that have been engulfed . Activation Profile Binding of a PAMP to its specific TLR physically activates the phagocyte turning it into a professional Antigen Presenting Cell APC . Crucially TLR activation aggressively drives the transcription of NF-κB the master switch that unleashes a storm of pro-inflammatory cytokines. TLR3 and HSV Different TLRs recognize different threats. TLR3 specifically identifies the double-stranded RNA typically produced during viral replication. A genetic deficiency in TLR3 renders an individual extraordinarily susceptible to aggressive infections by the Herpes Simplex Virus HSV frequently leading to devastating HSV encephalitis. TLR7 and HIV TLR7 recognizes single-stranded viral RNA. Clinical data shows that in patients struggling with HIV a functional limitation or polymorphism in TLR7 is heavily correlated with soaring viral loads an inability to mount an effective initial innate defense and significantly accelerated disease progression from HIV into frank AIDS. The Cytosolic Receptors NLRs and RLHs Slide 40-42 While TLRs patrol the cell surface and endosomal compartments the actual cytoplasm of the cell is guarded by specialized intracellular PRRs. These are essential for detecting viruses that have bypassed the endosomes or intracellular bacteria that have escaped into the cytosol. RLHs Retinoic acid-inducible gene I-like helicases These are the primary cytosolic sensors for viral double-stranded RNA dsRNA . Upon detection RLHs rapidly trigger the cell to produce massive amounts of Type I Interferons IFN-α and IFN-β which shut down local cellular protein synthesis to halt viral replication. NOD-Like Receptors NLRs and The Inflammasome NLRs are cytosolic receptors that primarily recognize bacterial peptidoglycan fragments. When an NLR binds its specific PAMP it physically oligomerizes with other intracellular proteins to form a massive multi-protein complex called the Inflammasome . The inflammasome essentially acts as a molecular executioner; it activates the enzyme Caspase-1 . Active Caspase-1 rapidly cleaves inactive pro-IL-1β into mature highly inflammatory IL-1β and simultaneously triggers a fiery highly inflammatory form of programmed cell death known as pyroptosis . By sacrificing itself through pyroptosis the infected cell deliberately triggers a massive local inflammatory response to warn neighboring cells and attract phagocytes to clean up the liberated intracellular bacteria. correlate NOD Polymorphisms and Chronic Inflammatory Disease - Pathophysiology Because the Inflammasome built by NLRs is so profoundly pro-inflammatory its activity must be tightly regulated. Genetic variants polymorphisms in the genes coding for NOD receptors lead to a trigger-happy innate immune system that constantly inappropriately senses PAMPs—even highly benign ones from the normal gut flora. - Presentation Individuals bearing these NOD polymorphisms suffer from a generalized poorly regulated hyper-inflammatory state. Clinically this manifests as significantly increased incidences of Atopic eczema severe Asthma chronically elevated IgE levels triggering rampant allergies and most notably an extensively documented astronomical increase in the risk of developing Crohn s disease . - Lecturer s Point Paradoxically because their innate sensors are constantly misfiring rather than effectively targeting true threats these individuals are simultaneously more prone to experiencing recurrent unresolving true bacterial infections. Cellular Sentinels Neutrophils vs. Macrophages Slide 43-48 Both Neutrophils and Macrophages are primary amoeboid phagocytes but they operate under vastly different tactical doctrines. The lecturer explicitly contrasted these two cells emphasizing that understanding their unique roles is critical to grasping both acute and chronic infectious physiology. Neutrophils The Shock Troops Neutrophils Polymorphonuclear leukocytes or PMNs are the most abundant white blood cell. They are specifically designated for the acute response . Lifespan Extremely short-lived hours to days . They rapidly enter the tissue engage the enemy and die in mass quantities forming the bulk of clinical pus . Microbicidal Arsenal Neutrophils are inherently highly cytotoxic. Nearly 50% of the contents of their dense intracellular granules consist of defensins —peptides that aggressively punch holes in bacterial membranes. The Oxidative Burst Upon engulfing a pathogen neutrophils furiously consume immense amounts of oxygen to rapidly generate highly toxic superoxide radicals via the NADPH oxidase enzyme complex. This immediate oxidative burst is their primary killing mechanism. Because they are the first responders they do not require complex prior activation commands to begin killing. Macrophages The Generals Macrophages derived from circulating monocytes are the long-term residents of the tissue. They are designated for chronic inflammation and cellular cleanup. Lifespan Long-lived months to years allowing them to persistently hold ground in the tissue. Recruitment Command While inherently less acutely cytotoxic than a fresh neutrophil their primary role is orchestration. Upon detecting a pathogen via their PRRs macrophages secrete powerful chemokines most notably Interleukin-8 IL-8 which serves as a powerful magnet to continuously pull fresh neutrophils out of the bloodstream and into the battlefield. Specific Activation Requirement To reach their full microbicidal angry potential macrophages require a specific two-key activation sequence. They must first detect a PAMP via their PRRs and secondly they must receive the cytokine Interferon-gamma IFN-γ secreted by Th1 cells or NK cells. Once fully activated they become ferocious eaters and highly competent Antigen Presenting Cells APCs . Primary Immunodeficiencies of the Innate System Slide 50-53 Defects in the phagocytic machinery or the complement cascade present with a stark inability to clear common ubiquitous environmental pathogens. Understanding the specific molecular failure dictates the clinical presentation. Phagocyte Disorders Chediak-Higashi Syndrome An autosomal recessive genetic defect leading to a systemic failure in microtubule polymerization. Without functional microtubules cellular trafficking grinds to a halt. Specifically in phagocytes the phagosome containing the engulfed bacteria physically cannot traffic to fuse with the destructive lysosome failure of phagosome-lysosome fusion . The cell continues to accumulate these disconnected organelles leading to the pathognomonic finding on a blood smear massive giant fused granules inside the cytoplasm. Patients suffer from recurrent pyogenic infections partial albinism due to failed melanin trafficking and progressive neurologic abnormalities. Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD An extremely high-yield disorder caused by a genetic deficiency in the NADPH oxidase enzyme complex located on the phagolysosomal membrane. Without this enzyme neutrophils absolutely cannot perform their signature oxidative burst to generate superoxide radicals. While they can engulf bacteria they cannot quickly destroy them. This defect specifically renders the patient highly susceptible to catalase-positive organisms classically Staphylococcus aureus Pseudomonas Serratia Nocardia and Aspergillus species . Because the neutrophils fail to clear the inciting pathogen the body s only remaining strategy is to constantly wall off the unresolved infections with macrophages forming extensive chronic granulomas everywhere in the body. Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency LAD-1 and LAD-2 Driven by an absence of the crucial beta-integrin adhesion molecules specifically CD18 on the surface of neutrophils. Without integrins the neutrophils cannot lock onto the vascular endothelium. They can roll along the vessel wall but they cannot firmly attach and undergo diapedesis to squeeze into the infected tissue. Blood tests paradoxically reveal a massive neutrophilia since the cells are all helplessly trapped in the circulation while infected wounds are completely devoid of neutrophils resulting in absent pus formation and severely delayed umbilical cord separation. IFN-γ Receptor Deficiency A severe defect where macrophages lack the receptor to hear the activating call from Th1 cells or NK cells. Because the macrophages can never achieve full activation the patient suffers from rampant severe and often fatal infections from otherwise low-virulence atypical mycobacterial species. highyield Complement System Deficiencies The presentation of a complement deficiency heavily depends on exactly which link in the chain is broken. They do not all present as simple bacterial susceptibilities. 1. Early and Alternative Pathway Defects Defects in MBL or the Alternative pathway the early innate triggers generally result in a broad non-specific increase in susceptibility to early childhood infections. 2. C3 Deficiency C3 is the central convergence point for all three compliment pathways. Losing C3 disables almost the entire system. Because C3b is the primary opsonin these patients present with severe devastating and recurrent pyogenic infections caused predominantly by encapsulated bacteria such as S. pneumoniae . 3. Classical Pathway Deficiencies C1 C2 & C4 Remember that the early classical pathway components uniquely function to clear out spent antibody-antigen immune complexes from the circulation. When these are deficient the immune complexes build up and indiscriminately crash out into the body s capillary beds especially in the kidneys and skin . This manifests as a classic Type III hypersensitivity disease heavily mimicking the symptoms of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus SLE e.g. photosensitive rash arthritis and glomerulonephritis . 4. C1 Inhibitor Deficiency C1-INH normally acts as the brakes on the complement system and the related kallikrein-kinin system. Without these brakes Kallikrein runs unchecked generating massive inappropriate amounts of Bradykinin . This causes a rare but extremely dangerous condition called Hereditary Angioedema . Patients suffer from unpredictable episodic massive swelling edema of the face limbs GI tract and crucially the larynx. Noticeably because this is driven by bradykinin and not histamine this severe swelling is completely completely painless and non-pruritic no hives . questions 1 A 25-year-old female presents to the emergency department with profound rapid-onset swelling of her lips tongue and eyelids. She denies any pain itching or recent exposure to known allergens and physical examination reveals a complete absence of urticaria hives . Her mother reportedly suffered from similar episodes one of which resulted in a near-fatal airway obstruction. Based on the most likely diagnosis which specific physiological regulator is deficient in this patient? C3 Convertase NADPH Oxidase C1 Inhibitor C1-INH Beta-integrins Decay-Accelerating Factor CD55 3 The patient s presentation of massive painless non-pruritic swelling angioedema without hives combined with a strong family history is the classic hallmark of Hereditary Angioedema. This is caused by an inherited deficiency in C1-INH. Without C1-INH the kallikrein pathway operates unregulated leading to a massive overproduction of the potent vasodilator bradykinin which drives the severe plasma leakage and ensuing edema. 2 A 3-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician for evaluation of recurrent severe cutaneous and deep abscesses. Culture of the latest abscess grows Aspergillus fumigatus. Utilizing a Nitroblue Tetrazolium NBT test the physician determines that the patient s neutrophils are completely incapable of mounting an oxidative burst. Which crucial component of the innate immune response is fundamentally defective in this child? Phagosome-lysosome fusion Firm adhesion to the vascular endothelium via integrins Superoxide radical production via NADPH oxidase Opsonization of encapsulated organisms via C3b Activation of the Inflammasome via NOD-like receptors 3 The patient is suffering from Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD . CGD is caused by a genetic defect in the NADPH oxidase enzyme complex completely disabling the neutrophil s ability to generate the superoxide radicals required for the rapid oxidative burst killing mechanism. Consequently patients are uniquely susceptible to chronic infections by catalase-positive organisms like Aspergillus and Staphylococcus aureus. 3 A 19-year-old male is admitted to the intensive care unit with a devastating case of Herpes Simplex Virus HSV encephalitis. His medical history is notable for multiple prior unusually severe HSV outbreaks since childhood yet he mounts perfectly normal responses to bacterial and fungal challenges. A targeted genetic panel indicates a defect in a specific class of Pattern Recognition Receptors PRRs . Which of the following receptors is most likely dysfunctional in this patient? Toll-Like Receptor 3 TLR3 Toll-Like Receptor 4 TLR4 NOD-Like Receptor NLR Interferon-gamma Receptor Killer Inhibition Receptor KIR 1 Toll-Like Receptors recognize highly specific microbial molecular patterns. TLR3 is an endosomally located receptor that explicitly senses the double-stranded RNA naturally produced during viral replication. A specific isolated genetic deficiency in TLR3 renders patients uniquely remarkably susceptible to runaway infections with the Herpes Simplex Virus classically presenting as life-threatening HSV encephalitis. 4 A patient is evaluated for chronic severe gastrointestinal inflammation. A detailed family history reveals several relatives suffering from unremitting atopic eczema and profound persistent asthma. Genetic testing on the patient indicates the presence of an activating polymorphism in a specific cytosolic receptor family. This patient is at a significantly elevated risk for developing which of the following gastrointestinal conditions? Celiac Disease Peptic Ulcer Disease Crohn s Disease Familial Adenomatous Polyposis Ulcerative Colitis 3 Polymorphisms in the genes coding for cytosolic NOD-Like Receptors NLRs lead to a hyperactive dysregulated inflammasome response. This persistent inappropriate state of baseline inflammation drives a significantly higher incidence of severe allergic conditions Asthma Eczema and is one of the most firmly established high-yield genetic risk factors for the development of Crohn s disease. 5 A 4-year-old child presents with partial albinism progressive peripheral neuropathy and recurrent debilitating pyogenic skin infections. A peripheral blood smear demonstrates massive giant granules clustered within the cytoplasm of all the patient s neutrophils. The underlying molecular defect in this syndrome primarily prevents which of the following cellular processes? Phagosome-lysosome fusion Neutrophil diapedesis across the endothelium Transcription of NF-κB The oxidative burst mechanism Clearance of immune complexes by the spleen 1 This array of symptoms—partial albinism neuropathy susceptibility to pyogenic infections and the pathognomonic finding of massive giant granules on a blood smear—unmistakably points to Chediak-Higashi Syndrome. The core pathology is a microtubule polymerization defect that physically stalls all intracellular vesicular trafficking. Thus the phagosome containing the engulfed bacterium is physically incapable of trafficking to and fusing with the destructive lysosome. Which specific Toll-Like Receptor TLR detects double-stranded viral RNA and what disease does its deficiency cause? TLR3. Deficiency leads to severe susceptibility to Herpes Simplex Virus HSV encephalitis. Clinical What massive intracellular multi-protein complex is formed by NOD-Like Receptors NLRs to activate Caspase-1? The Inflammasome. Concept Genetic polymorphisms in NOD receptors strongly predispose patients to which specific Inflammatory Bowel Disease? Crohn s Disease. Clinical Which innate cell functions primarily as the shock troops during an acute response and contains granules comprised of 50% defensins? Neutrophils. Cells What specific enzyme complex is genetically deficient in Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD ? The NADPH Oxidase enzyme complex. Clinical Lack of functional microtubules leading to absent phagosome-lysosome fusion is the hallmark of which disease? Chediak-Higashi Syndrome. Clinical What is the classic presentation of a patient with a Beta-Integrin defect Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency ? Recurrent infections with absolutely no pus formation delayed umbilical cord separation and a paradoxically massive neutrophilia on blood work. Clinical Deficiencies in the early classical complement pathway C1 C2 C4 result in a failure to clear immune complexes. What is the classic clinical presentation of this? A severe Type III Hypersensitivity reaction resembling Systemic Lupus Erythematosus SLE . Complement Hereditary Angioedema is caused by unregulated bradykinin production resulting from a deficiency in what protein? C1 Inhibitor C1-INH . Clinical Unlike an allergic reaction what two classic clinical features are notably ABSENT during an attack of Hereditary Angioedema? Pain and Urticaria Hives . The swelling is completely painless and non-pruritic. Clinical B&B Immunology > Immunodeficiency > Phagocyte Disorders FirstAid Immunology > Immunodeficiencies Pathoma White Blood Cell Disorders > Leukopenia & Leukocytosis SketchyPath Immunology > Primary Immunodeficiencies Physeo Immunology > Immunodeficiencies match Chronic Granulomatous Disease & LAD resources AK Step1 v11 B&B 14 Immunology 05 Immunodeficiency 03 Phagocyte Disorders AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 12 Immunology 05 Immunodeficiency 06 Phagocyte Disorders match Chediak-Higashi Syndrome resources AK Step1 v11 Pathoma 02 Inflammation and Healing 01 Introduction to Inflammation Chediak-Higashi Syndrome match Toll-Like Receptors & Inflammasome resources AK Step1 v11 B&B 14 Immunology 01 Innate Immunity 02 Cellular Response match Hereditary Angioedema C1-INH Deficiency resources AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 12 Immunology 05 Immunodeficiency 05 Complement Disorders The NLR/Crohn s Connection Always link NOD polymorphisms hyperactive inflammasome with an astronomical risk of developing Crohn s disease Asthma and Atopic eczema. The Pus Contradiction in LAD In Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency LAD the blood will show an incredibly high neutrophil count because they are trapped in the vasculature. The infected tissue itself will have zero pus because the neutrophils cannot undergo diapedesis to reach the physical battlefield. CGD and Catalase-Positive Bugs Patients with Chronic Granulomatous Disease are distinctly susceptible to catalase-positive organisms Staph aureus Pseudomonas Serratia Nocardia Aspergillus because these specific bugs destroy the tiny amount of self-produced hydrogen peroxide the neutrophil might have otherwise hijacked. The Paradox of Hereditary Angioedema In a patient presenting with terrifying sudden facial swelling the absence of hives urticaria and pain heavily points to Hereditary Angioedema C1-INH deficiency / Bradykinin excess rather than a classic histamine-driven anaphylactic reaction. mindmap Innate Immunity Part 2 Pattern Recognition Receptors PRRs - Toll-Like Receptors TLRs Surface and endosomal sensors that activate NF-κB - TLR3 Detects dsRNA; deficiency leads to HSV Encephalitis - TLR7 Linked to HIV viral load control - Cytosolic Receptors - RLHs Sensors that trigger Type I Interferons to halt viral replication - NOD-Like Receptors NLRs Form the Inflammasome Caspase-1 -> IL-1β & Pyroptosis - Polymorphisms Asthma Eczema massively increased Crohn s Disease risk Neutrophils vs. Macrophages - Neutrophils The Shock Troops - Short-lived acute response - High defensin concentration - Primary weapon The immediate NADPH Oxidase burst - Macrophages The Generals - Long-lived chronic responders - Primary weapon Secreting IL-8 chemokines to summon neutrophils - Require a 2-key activation PAMP + IFN-γ to achieve full microbicidal strength Primary Immunodeficiencies - Phagocyte Disorders - Chediak-Higashi Microtubule defect -> Failed phagosome-lysosome fusion -> Giant granules & Albinism - Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD NADPH Oxidase defect -> Failed oxidative burst -> Susceptible to catalase-positive organisms S. aureus Aspergillus - LAD Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency Beta-integrin defect -> Failed diapedesis -> Massive neutrophilia but zero pus - IFN-γ Receptor Deficiency Severe atypical mycobacterial infections - Complement Disorders - C3 Recurrent severe encapsulated bacterial infections - C1 C2 C4 Early Classical Failed clearance of immune complexes -> SLE/Lupus-like Type III hypersensitivity - C1 Inhibitor C1-INH Unregulated Kallikrein/Bradykinin -> Hereditary Angioedema painless swelling without hives"}, {"id": "l121", "title": "DPR: History and Physical Exam of the Spine", "module": "DPR", "content": "l121 DPR History and Physical Exam of the Spine lecturer M. Heller D.O. session Lecture 121 DPR readingTime 12-18 min pdf content/L121 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L121 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 121 DPR History and Physical Exam of the Spine Lecturer M. Heller D.O. Session Lecture 121 The Subjective History and Subjective Exam Slide 2-15 The foundation of a proper physical examination lies in a highly detailed systematic subjective history. Before palpating the spine or conducting specialized maneuvers the physician must pinpoint whether the pain is mechanical inflammatory radicular or systemic. Distinguishing between acute weekend-warrior injuries and chronicity sets the tone for your diagnostic workup. Chief Complaint Dissection The OPQRST The subjective section is your standard documentation of the patient s complaints what they tell you . Use OPQRST to determine exact pain locations and characteristics. Locating the Pain Joint pain must be categorized as articular inside the joint or extra-articular outside the joint . You must define it as focal/localized suggesting simple acute trauma/injury versus diffuse suggesting systemic arthritis or widespread inflammatory change . Timing & Context Acute vs. Chronic A sudden basketball injury versus unremitting pain for 6 months points toward completely different sets of etiologies. Did the patient start a new activity after 20 years of being sedentary? Overuse injuries are incredibly common in these contexts. Associated Symptoms The presence of systemic symptoms like fevers chills or night sweats heavily implies an infectious or systemic process over simple mechanical back pain. Radicular Pain Inquire directly about pain radiating into the arms cervical or the buttocks/legs lumbar . Assess for associated neurological deficits like paresthesias numbness/tingling or weakness. Red Flag Symptoms Lecturer Emphasis You must always explicitly rule out red flag symptoms that suggest an immediate medical emergency. Cauda Equina Warning Signs Always ask the patient about new-onset bowel or bladder dysfunction incontinence urinary retention . Check for saddle anesthesia numbness on the medial aspect of the upper thighs/inner groin area and progressive lower extremity weakness. If present these suggest compression of the sacral nerve roots and require emergent imaging and decompression. Malignancy or Infection Back pain presenting in an individual younger than 20 or older than 50 with a prior history of cancer long-term steroid use IV drug use or HIV/Immunodeficiency is an immediate red flag. Unexplained Weight Loss or pain that heavily increases at night or with rest is highly suspicious for malignancy or serious infection. The Objective Exam Inspection Palpation and ROM Slide 16-25 Following a deep historical review you enter the objective physical exam. This must systematically proceed from Inspection to Palpation to Active/Passive Range of Motion ROM and finally to Special Testing. 1. Inspection The most commonly skipped step by students. Have the patient undress the relevant area. Tell the patient exactly what you are doing e.g. I am looking at your back to assess muscle symmetry . Look for obvious deformities muscle atrophy localized swelling erythema ecchymosis bruising or skin conditions. Observe the natural spinal curvature cervical lordosis thoracic kyphosis lumbar lordosis . Posture A significant cause of neck pain today is poor digital ergonomics. The head should sit directly above the shoulders; forward head posture where the head translates anteriorly due to phone usage places excessive strain on the cervical musculature. 2. Palpation and ROM Palpation Always palpate using the pads of your fingers or thumbs along the spinous processes searching for point tenderness or step-offs which indicate severe ligamentous injury spondylolisthesis or fracture . Note muscle hypotonicity spasm or somatic dysfunction using an erythema or skin drag test . Active vs Passive ROM Active Patient performs the movement. Tests the muscle tissue and their genuine functional capacity. Resisted Patient performs an isometric contraction against your resistance. Evaluates specifically for muscular weakness or tendinopathy. Cervical Spine Note 50% of flexion/extension originates at the Occipitoatlantal OA joint. 50% of rotation originates at the Atlantoaxial AA joint. The remaining segments handle the other 50% and primary lateral side-bending. Cervical Spine Special Testing and Neurovascular Screen Slide 26-40 If the patient s pain has a radicular component a comprehensive neurological exam Motor Sensory DTRs is mandatory to map the injury to a specific nerve root level. mnemonic Lower Extremity Nerve Root Mapping - L4 L4 Kick the Door Ankle Dorsiflexion medial aspect sensation Patellar reflex . - L5 Nothing for L5 Great toe dorsiflexion dorsal foot sensation no specific reflex . - S1 Achilles Tendon reflection Plantar flexion lateral foot sensation. - C7 Lecturer s Point Giving the bird to a terrible driver in New York requires extending the elbow and flexing the wrist—this is powered by the C7 nerve root triceps reflex . Cervical Impingement Special Tests Lhermitte s Sign Barber s Chair Phenomenon The seated patient actively flexes their head forward. A positive test produces a sharp electric shock sensation radiating down the spine or into the limbs. Highly indicative of cervical spinal stenosis multiple sclerosis MS lesion or significant cord compression. Cervical Axial Compression Test Push directly downward on the patient s head. The added pressure narrows the neuroforamina. If it reproduces radicular arm pain it indicates Cervical Radiculopathy or nerve root impingement. Do NOT apply heavy force if the patient is already in significant pain. Cervical Distraction Test Lifting upwards under the chin and occiput pulling the head up . This widens the neuroforamina. A positive test is indicated by the reduction or relief of radicular pain confirming an underlying impingement. Spurling s Test Extending the neck side-bending and rotating towards the affected side followed by a slight downward compressive force. This maximally closes down the neuroforamen on that specific side. Exacerbating radicular arm symptoms confirms a cervical radiculopathy. highyield NEXUS Criteria vs. Canadian C-Spine Rules Two distinct heavily validated decision aids used to clinically clear an acute trauma patient from requiring a cervical spine CT scan 1. NEXUS Criteria You can safely clear the C-spine no imaging ONLY if the patient matches NONE of the five criteria - No neurological deficit - No spinal midline tenderness - No altered mental status - No severe intoxication - No distracting painful injury e.g. a massive femur fracture drowning out the localized neck pain . 2. Canadian C-Spine Rule Evaluates trauma via risk factors. If High Risk parameters are present Age >65 dangerous mechanism paresthesias imaging is mandated. If only Low Risk factors exist e.g. simple rear-end MVA falling from sitting the patient must demonstrate the ability to actively rotate their neck 45 degrees left and right to be fully cleared. Thoracic and Lumbar Spine Special Testing Slide 45-65 The same algorithmic process of inspection palpation active ROM and neurological screening applies to the lower spine followed by specific provocative tests mapping to classic pathologies. Thoracic tests Adam s Forward Bend Test Primarily screens for scoliosis. The patient bends forward at the waist. You observe the posterior lung fields and rib cage from behind. A unilateral rib hump indicates scoliosis driven by rotational deformation of the vertebrae. Remember The spinous process points towards the concavity of the curve while the vertebral body and ribs rotate towards the convexity causing the visible hump. Adson s Test for Thoracic Outlet Syndrome The examiner abducts extends and externally rotates the patient s arm while palpating their radial pulse. The patient inhale deeply extends and rotates their head towards the tested arm. A positive test is the significant diminishment or loss of the radial pulse implying the scalene muscles are compressing the subclavian artery Thoracic Outlet Syndrome . Scapular Winging Testing Have the patient perform a wall push-up. Protrusion of the medial border indicates primary Serratus Anterior weakness Long Thoracic Nerve injury though the trapezius Spinal Accessory Nerve and rhomboids can also contribute. Lumbar tests Stork Test The patient stands on one leg and physically extends their back backward. The hyperextension forces pressure onto the posterior elements of the spine—specifically the pars interarticularis . Unilateral back pain here strongly hints at Spondylolysis a stress fracture of the pars . Straight Leg Raise SLR With the patient completely completely supine and relaxed passively elevate their straightened leg. Pain radiating past the knee between 30-70 degrees indicates significant stretching and irritation of the sciatic nerve or dural sac impingement . Braggard s Test Lower the leg slightly from the SLR pain point and sharply dorsiflex the foot. Reproducing the pain solidifies the diagnosis of L5 nerve root impingement. Well s Crossed Straight Leg Raise Raising the perfectly healthy uninvolved leg directly produces radiating pain down the opposite affected leg. A highly specific sign of a massive space-occupying disc herniation. Hoover s Test Detects malingering or psychogenic paresis. While asking the patient to lift their paralyzed or injured leg off the table cup your hand under their opposite normal heel. If the patient is genuinely trying to lift the affected leg normal biomechanics dictate they will physically push down hard with their normal heel into your hand to generate leverage. A lack of downward pressure signifies a lack of genuine effort. correlate Camptocormia Bent Spine Syndrome - Pathophysiology Characterized by a severe involuntary forward flexion kyphosis of the thoracolumbar spine that becomes deeply pronounced while standing or walking but completely resolves when the patient lies supine. It is very frequently seen as an axial muscular complication of Parkinson s Disease . - Presentation The patient walks hunched completely forward at nearly a 90-degree angle forcing them to look almost straight down at the ground. Muscle strength is otherwise preserved. - Treatment Physical therapy aggressive OMM tailored to fascial restrictions and hypertonic musculature and optimization of dopaminergic Parkinson s medications. - Lecturer s Point Through properly applied OMM muscle energy myofascial release of the OA and traps a 77-year-old Parkinson s patient presenting with chronic Camptocormia was able to stand completely upright and see the sky for the first time in years. questions 1 A 22-year-old male is brought into the emergency department restrained via backboard and C-collar following a high-speed motorcycle accident. He is agitated screaming in agony about his severely deformed open femur fracture and is actively intoxicated with a blood alcohol concentration of 0.18. According to the NEXUS criteria which of the following perfectly justifies the absolute necessity to order a cervical spine CT for this patient? The mechanism of injury was a high-speed accident over 45mph. His age inherently places him in a high-risk category. He cannot actively rotate his neck 45 degrees left and right. He is heavily intoxicated and suffering from a highly distracting painful injury. The C-collar prevents accurate assessment of his visual fields. 3 To clear a C-spine clinically without imaging via NEXUS criteria the patient MUST be completely sober fully alert free of any midline spinal tenderness free of neurological deficits AND crucially they must not have any major distracting painful injuries like a shattered femur . This patient is intoxicated and has a massive distracting injury inherently disabling the physician s ability to trust his localized pain assessment. Therefore imaging is absolutely mandated. 2 During a routine musculoskeletal physical assessment a patient complains of a chronic pins and needles numbness shooting down their left arm into their thumb. To evaluate for a cervical radiculopathy you instruct the patient to extend their neck slightly laterally side-bend and rotate their head leftward toward the affected side. You then apply a targeted downward axial compression on the top of their head. This maneuver instantly recreates the severe shooting pain. Which specific test did you perform? Spurling s Test Cervical Distraction Test Lhermitte s Sign Adson s Test Adam s Forward Bend Test 0 Spurling s test involves placing the cervical spine in a position extension ipsilateral rotation and side-bending that maximally narrows the intervertebral neuroforamen. Applying an axial downward force further squeezes the space; if a compressed nerve root radiculopathy is present this will powerfully recreate the patient s shooting radicular arm pain. 3 A 77-year-old male with a longstanding 12-year history of Parkinson s Disease presents to the osteopathic manipulative medicine clinic. He shuffles into the room demonstrating a profound abnormal severe forward flexion of his thoracolumbar spine. He is effectively bent in half while walking staring directly at his shoes. However when instructed to lie flat on his back on the examination table for treatment his spine posture almost completely normalizes. What is the most accurate clinical term for this patient s postural presentation? Severe primary structural scoliosis Ankylosing Spondylitis Spondylolysis Camptocormia Thoracic Outlet Syndrome 3 Camptocormia Bent Spine Syndrome is defined by an extreme involuntary forward-flexed kyphotic posture of the trunk that vividly appears when the patient stands or walks but dramatically disappears when they lie down supine. It is a highly recognized axial musculature side-effect heavily associated with advanced Parkinson s disease. 4 A 45-year-old male construction worker is evaluated for severe right-sided lower back pain that relentlessly shoots down his right posterior thigh wrapping past the knee and ending at his lateral right foot. You perform a classic Straight Leg Raise SLR on the patient s right leg. At 40 degrees of elevation the patient yelps as the shooting pain perfectly replicates down the leg. To further confirm the L5/S1 nerve root irritation you lower the leg to 35 degrees where the pain vanishes and then you quickly sharply dorsiflex his right foot. The pain instantly returns. This confirmatory action is strictly known as Well s Crossed-Straight Leg Raise The Stork Test Braggard s Test Hoover s Test Lhermitte s maneuver 2 Braggard s test is the standard confirmatory follow-up to a positive Straight Leg Raise. By dropping the leg a few degrees until the pain subsides and then applying sharp dorsiflexion of the foot the physician brutally stretches the sciatic nerve and its dural attachments without engaging hamstring tightness. Reproducing the pain entirely confirms a true radicular etiology dural/nerve irritation ruling out simple hamstring muscle spasms. 5 A 34-year-old female presents to the clinic complaining of an intense electric shock sensation bounding down her entire spine whenever she aggressively bends her neck forward to look down at her smartphone. Upon neurological review she also reports blurry vision in her left eye and recent episodes of profound clumsiness. This specific exam finding generated by cervical flexion—highly suggestive of conditions spanning from severe spinal stenosis to demyelinating plaques like Multiple Sclerosis—is defined as Adson s sign Positive cervical distraction Lhermitte s Sign Hoover s sign Saddle anesthesia 2 Lhermitte s sign often dubbed the Barber Chair Phenomenon is classically provoked by rapid active anterior flexion of the cervical spine. This stretches the posterior columns of the spinal cord. In the presence of highly sensitive demyelinated cervical plaques characteristic of Multiple Sclerosis or direct structural central cord compression this harmless stretch triggers a terrifying distinct electrical shock vibrating down the spine into the extremities. What test involves extending the neck rotating/side-bending towards the affected side and applying downward axial pressure to check for cervical radiculopathy? Spurling s Test. Clinical Skills When performing the Cervical Distraction Test what response technically constitutes a positive result? The reduction or complete relief of radicular arm pain as lifting the head decompresses the inflamed nerve root . Clinical Skills What is Lhermitte s Sign and what major neurologic disease is it classically associated with? A shooting electric shock sensation traversing down the spine provoked by purely flexing the neck forward. Strongly associated with Multiple Sclerosis MS or cervical stenosis. Clinical Skills What is the NEXUS Criteria primarily used for? A clinical decision aid evaluating 5 parameters to safely rule out a cervical spine injury and clear the patient without requiring a CT scan. Clinical Documentation During Adam s Forward Bend Test you see a unilateral right-sided rib hump. Which way is the vertebral body actually rotating? The vertebral body is pathologically rotating towards the convexity the right causing the ribs on that side to push outward and generate the hump. Biomechanics Adson s Test involves extending the arm while feeling the radial pulse as the patient inhales and turns their head deeply toward the tested side. What is it evaluating for? Thoracic Outlet Syndrome specifically evaluating for subclavian artery compression by hypertonic Scalene muscles . Clinical Skills A patient reporting localized severe unilateral lower back pain upon standing on one leg and hyperextending their spine backward has a positive test for what likely structural defect? The Stork Test evaluates for Spondylolysis a stress fracture of the pars interarticularis . Clinical Skills What is Camptocormia? Bent Spine Syndrome A profound involuntary forward-flexed posture while standing or walking that dramatically normalizes when the patient lies flat. Highly associated with Parkinson s Disease. Clinical In a patient presenting with an acutely herniated lumbar disc doing a Straight Leg Raise SLR on the totally healthy asymptomatic leg produces sharp radiating pain down the broken opposite leg. What test is this? Well s Test Crossed Straight Leg Raise . Clinical Skills How do you perform Braggard s Test after a positive Straight Leg Raise? Lower the leg until the shooting pain stops then rigidly acutely dorsiflex the foot. The pain returning confirms true dural/sciatic irritation rejecting hamstring tightness. Clinical Skills B&B Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Low Back Pain FirstAid Neurology > Clinical SketchyPath Neurology match Spurling / Cervical Radiculopathy resources im amboss medicalark AK Step2 v11 Subjects Neurology 05 Spinal Cord Cervical Spine Radiculopathy match Cauda Equina Syndrome resources AK Step1 v11 B&B 14 Musculoskeletal 03 Pathology 05 Low Back Pain match Straight Leg Raise resources im amboss medicalark AK Step2 v11 Subjects Neurology 05 Spinal Cord Sciatica match Multiple Sclerosis / Lhermitte s Sign resources AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Neurology 03 Pathology 24 Demyelinating Disorders Multiple Sclerosis Presentation Pathophysiology Hoover s Trick for Genuine Effort When evaluating potential malingering a patient who is genuinely trying to lift a paralyzed or severely pained leg off the table via a Straight Leg Raise will completely involuntarily push down hard on the table with their opposite healthy heel in order to lever themselves up. Muscle Spasms Don t Cause Anesthesia A patient with terrible back pain localized strictly to the paraspinal muscles from lifting a heavy box should NOT present with saddle anesthesia bowel or bladder incontinence or dense dermatomal weakness. If they do ignore the muscle strain; they legitimately have a massive red-flag quarter equina compression that needs surgical imaging. The Dural Tug of Braggard s When separating a tight cramped hamstring from a legitimately irritated sciatic nerve in a positive Straight Leg Raise simply lowering the leg and aggressively dorsiflexing the foot Braggard s test perfectly isolates the sciatic nerve dural sheath. If pain fires back down the leg without hip flexion changing it s a nerve root irritation not a hamstring spasm. The NEXUS Mandate When you have an agonizingly painful distraction injury like an open broken femur it completely overrides the brain s ability to accurately process baseline structural neck pain. You absolutely cannot trust the patient s subjective assessment that my neck perfectly doesn t hurt and you MUST legally order C-spine imaging. mindmap History and Physical Exam of the Spine Subjective Evaluation - OPQRST Analysis Differentiating articular acute localized or inflammatory qualities. - Red Flag Symptoms - Cauda Equina Bowel/bladder incontinence saddle anesthesia severe lower motor neuron deficit. Requires an emergency MRI and decompression. - Cancer/Infection Age anomalies <20 or >50 unexplained weight loss IV drug use pain increasing aggressively at night/rest. Objective Assessment - Inspection & Sensation Checking ergonomics assessing proper C7 triceps L4 medial ankle L5 great toe and S1 lateral ankle/Achilles reflexes. - Cervical Clearance Tools - NEXUS Criteria Missing ALL 5 Neuro deficit Tenderness Altered mental status Intoxication Distracting injury allows clearance without imagery. - Canadian C-Spine Age/mechanism heavy. Rotation capacity allows clearance of low-risk factors. Specific Provocative Tests - Cervical Compression - Axial Compression Pushing down recreating pain = Positive Radiculopathy. - Distraction Lifting up reducing pain = Positive decompression Validates radiculopathy or impingement . - Spurling s Side bent rotated to affected side with axial compression. - Lhermitte s Sign Electric shock down spine upon severe forward flexion MS lesion or spinal stenosis . - Thoracic/Lumbar Testing - Adam s Bend Screens for structural rotational scoliosis rib hump on the convexity . - Adson s Inhaling and head turning loses radial pulse Thoracic Outlet Syndrome by the scalenes . - Stork Test Single-leg standing extension highlighting pars interarticularis breakdown Spondylolysis . - Straight Leg Raise Severe radicular pain = Sciatic/dural interference. - Braggard s Dorsiflexing the foot validates a positive SLR. - Well s Positive pain on the un-lifted broken leg = Large central space-occupying lesion. - Hoover s Sign A lack of compensatory downward drive on the non-examined leg exposes poor exertion/malingering. Documented Pathologies - Camptocormia Bent spine syndrome characterized by extreme uncontrolled orthostatic flexion typically masking a Parkinsonian movement disorder treated drastically through structural OMM."}];